window.FL_BANK=[{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"NEC 760.130(B)(1) covers exposed power-limited fire alarm cables within 7 ft of the floor. How often must they be secured?","o":["At intervals not exceeding 3 ft","At intervals not exceeding 18 in.","At each stud","At intervals not exceeding 4 1/2 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Exposed cables within 7 ft of the floor must be secured at intervals of no more than 18 in. so they are protected from damage. [NEC 760.130(B)(1)]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 760.130(B)(1)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which statement about wiring in ducts used to transport dust, loose stock, or flammable vapors is correct?","o":["Only EMT is permitted","Any metal raceway is permitted","No wiring system of any type is permitted","Wiring is permitted if it is sealed"],"a":2,"e":"300.22(A) bars wiring systems in these ducts, and in ducts or shafts used for commercial cooking exhaust. [NEC 300.22(A) (2026: 300.25(A))]","r":"NEC 2023 300.22(A)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A schedule of values assigns $38,000 to rough-in. The work is 65 percent complete, and the contract withholds 5 percent retainage. What is the net amount payable for this line item this period (assuming no prior billing)?","o":["$24,700","$23,465","$1,235","$36,100"],"a":1,"e":"Earned = $38,000 x 0.65 = $24,700. Retainage = $24,700 x 0.05 = $1,235. Net = $24,700 - $1,235 = $23,465. $24,700 forgets retainage. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A business owner wants a cash reserve equal to three months of fixed overhead. Fixed overhead is $38,000 per month. What reserve target results?","o":["$38,000","$76,000","$456,000","$114,000"],"a":3,"e":"3 x 38,000 = 114,000. [Cash reserve planning]","r":"Cash reserve planning"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 12,000 sq ft retail store is supplied by a 208Y/120-V, 3-phase, 4-wire feeder. The general lighting load is calculated at 3 VA/sq ft and the lighting is a continuous load. What feeder current should be used for the lighting load, including the continuous-load factor and ignoring all other loads?","o":["173 A","125 A","216 A","100 A"],"a":1,"e":"Lighting = 12,000 x 3 = 36,000 VA; x 1.25 for a continuous load = 45,000 VA; I = 45,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 124.9 A, or 125 A. Without the 125% factor the current would be 99.9 A. [NEC 220.12, 215.2(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 220.12, 215.2(A)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A certified electrical contractor who also performs alarm system work must complete how many additional hours of continuing education on a specific subject?","o":["1 hour of fire sprinkler design","4 hours of burglar alarm installation","2 hours of false alarm prevention","6 hours of low-voltage wiring"],"a":2,"e":"Section 489.517(4)(b)2 and DBPR's FAQ require electrical contractors doing alarm system contracting to also complete 2 hours of false alarm prevention. [F.S. 489.517(4)(b)2]","r":"F.S. 489.517(4)(b)2"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In CSI MasterFormat, which division contains the electrical work such as panelboards, wiring devices, and lighting?","o":["Division 25 - Integrated Automation","Division 27 - Communications","Division 26 - Electrical","Division 33 - Utilities"],"a":2,"e":"Division 26 is titled Electrical. Division 27 covers Communications, Division 28 covers Electronic Safety and Security, and Division 25 covers Integrated Automation. [CSI MasterFormat Division 26]","r":"CSI MasterFormat Division 26"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum voltage allowed for portable lighting used in wet or conductive locations such as drums, tanks and vessels, unless the 120-volt lights are GFCI protected?","o":["24 volts","12 volts","50 volts","30 volts"],"a":1,"e":"Portable lighting for wet, damp or conductive locations must operate at 12 volts or less, or be 120-volt lights protected by a GFCI. [29 CFR 1926.405(a)(2)(ii)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.405(a)(2)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The emergency disconnect for a one- or two-family dwelling must be installed in:","o":["The attic near the service mast","A readily accessible outdoor location","Any location inside the dwelling","The garage, behind the panelboard"],"a":1,"e":"A readily accessible outdoor emergency disconnect is required for one- and two-family dwellings. [NEC 230.85]","r":"NEC 230.85"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A fixed multioutlet assembly 20 ft long is installed in a laboratory where several appliances will be used simultaneously. What load is calculated for it?","o":["180 VA","720 VA","1,800 VA","3,600 VA"],"a":3,"e":"Where appliances are likely to be used simultaneously, each 1 ft of assembly is counted as 180 VA: 20 x 180 = 3,600 VA. Counting 180 VA per 5 ft would give 720 VA. [NEC 220.14(H)(2)]","r":"NEC 220.14(H)(2)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A crew costs $2,160 per day and installs 90 LF of conduit per day. The estimator adds 12% overhead to direct cost, then 8% profit on cost plus overhead. What unit price per LF should be bid (rounded to the cent)?","o":["$28.80","$24","$30.48","$29.03"],"a":3,"e":"Direct = 2160/90 = 24.00; x 1.12 = 26.8800; x 1.08 = 29.0304, or $29.03. Adding 20% flat gives $28.80. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A supplier quotes $28,400 for switchgear, valid for 30 days. The bid is awarded on day 41 and the supplier raises prices 4% on expired quotes. What will the contractor probably pay?","o":["$28,400","$29,536","$39,760","$27,307.69"],"a":1,"e":"The quote expired after 30 days, so the new price applies: 28,400 x 1.04 = $29,536. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"No point in an exit access corridor may be farther than what distance from the nearest visible exit sign, or the listed viewing distance, whichever is less?","o":["100 ft","150 ft","50 ft","75 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Exit signs must be located so no point is more than 100 ft or the sign's listed viewing distance away, whichever is less. [NFPA 101, 7.10.1]","r":"NFPA 101 7.10.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 15 kVA, single-phase transformer has a 240-volt primary and is protected on the primary side only. What is the maximum standard primary overcurrent device?","o":["80 A","70 A","90 A","100 A"],"a":0,"e":"Primary current = 15,000 / 240 = 62.5 A. At 125% = 78.1 A, and the next higher standard size, 80 A, is allowed for primary-only protection. [NEC 450.3(B), 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 450.3(B), 240.6(A)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For which of these items does the employer NOT have to pay under 29 CFR 1910.132(h) and 1926.95(d)?","o":["Rubber insulating gloves required for work near energized parts","Fall protection harnesses and lanyards required for roof work","Non-specialty safety-toe footwear that employees may wear off the job","Safety glasses with side shields required for all grinding work"],"a":2,"e":"The employer must pay for required PPE except non-specialty safety-toe footwear or prescription safety eyewear worn off the job, metatarsal-protection footwear chosen by the employee, and everyday clothing.","r":"29 CFR 1910.132(h)(2); 1926.95(d)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the standard jacket color of OS2 single-mode fiber cable?","o":["Orange","Aqua","Violet","Yellow"],"a":3,"e":"OS2 single-mode jackets are yellow; OM1 and OM2 are orange, and OM3 is aqua. [TIA-598]","r":"TIA-598 colors (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum output voltage of the power source for a non-power-limited fire alarm (NPLFA) circuit?","o":["600 volts, nominal","30 volts, nominal","150 volts, nominal","1000 volts, nominal"],"a":0,"e":"The NPLFA power source must comply with Chapters 1 through 4 and have an output of not more than 600 V nominal. [NEC 760.41(A)]","r":"NEC 2023 760.41(A)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Underground conductors installed beneath a building must be:","o":["Spliced in the soil","Direct buried","Run in cable tray","In a raceway (with limited exceptions)"],"a":3,"e":"Underground cables and conductors installed under a building must be in a raceway, with exceptions for MI cable and listed MC cable. [NEC 300.5(C)]","r":"NEC 300.5(C)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A kitchen counter has three receptacles above an uninterrupted countertop. Under 2010 Standards 205.1, how many of them must meet the 309 reach requirements?","o":["None of them need to comply","All three must comply with 309","Only the outlet nearest the sink must comply","All but one; one outlet is not required to comply"],"a":3,"e":"Where two or more outlets are provided above an uninterrupted counter, one outlet is not required to comply. The other two must be in range. [2010 Standards 205.1, Exc. 3]","r":"2010 Std 205.1 Exc. 3"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Why might a contractor's estimate include a cost for performance and payment bonds?","o":["Because the premium is refunded after bid opening","Because the owner requires the bonds and the premium is a cost of the contract","Because bonds replace the need for insurance","Because the lien law requires bonds on all private jobs"],"a":1,"e":"When bonds are required, the premium is a real job cost that must be priced into the bid. Bonds do not replace insurance, and not all private jobs require them. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.532, F.S., a contract entered into by an unlicensed electrical contractor is generally:","o":["Enforceable by the unlicensed contractor for the first $2,500","Void, so that the owner may not enforce it either","Enforceable if the owner signs a waiver after the work is done","Unenforceable by the unlicensed contractor, with no lien or bond claim"],"a":3,"e":"Section 489.532(1) makes the contract unenforceable by the unlicensed contractor, and (2) bars any lien or bond claim. Others' rights, including the surety's obligations, are unaffected. [F.S. 489.532(1)-(3)]","r":"F.S. 489.532"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's semiweekly deposit of $8,000 was made 4 calendar days after its due date. Using the IRS failure-to-deposit penalty tiers, what is the penalty (before interest and abatement)?","o":["$160.00","$400.00","$800.00","$1,200.00"],"a":0,"e":"Deposits 1 to 5 days late carry a 2% penalty: $8,000 x 2% = $160.00. 5% (6-15 days) would be $400.00 and 10% (over 15 days) would be $800.00. [IRS Failure to Deposit Penalty]","r":"IRS Failure to Deposit Penalty"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A site plan is drawn with an engineer's scale of 1 in. = 20 ft. An underground conduit route measures 6.35 in. on the drawing. What is its ground length?","o":["63.5 ft","76.2 ft","127 ft","254 ft"],"a":2,"e":"One inch equals 20 ft, so 6.35 x 20 = 127 ft. Engineer's scales use decimal feet per inch, not the architect's fractional-inch scales. [Scale calculation]","r":"Scale calculation"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1926.417, how must controls that will be deactivated while work is performed on circuits be handled?","o":["They must be locked with a single master key that is held by the building owner","They must be tagged, with tags at every point where the circuits can be energized","They must be labeled only at the main service disconnect for the entire building","They may be left unmarked if the work will last less than one work shift"],"a":1,"e":"1926.417 requires controls to be tagged, tags attached at all points where the equipment or circuits can be energized, and tags that plainly identify the circuits being worked on.","r":"29 CFR 1926.417(a)-(c)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"When leveling manpower on a schedule, which activities are the first candidates to shift?","o":["Critical activities that have zero float","Activities that have already been completed","Non-critical activities that have float","Milestones that have zero duration"],"a":2,"e":"Moving a critical activity delays the project; non-critical activities can be moved within float. [Resource leveling]","r":"Manpower leveling (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How often must a competent person inspect excavations, adjacent areas and protective systems?","o":["Only once when work starts on a new project, by the project superintendent","Daily before work, as needed during the shift, and after rainstorms","Weekly, by the safety director, on the same day of every week","Only after a cave-in or other major failure has been reported to OSHA"],"a":1,"e":"1926.651(k)(1) requires daily inspections before the start of work and as needed, and after every rainstorm or other hazard-increasing occurrence.","r":"29 CFR 1926.651(k)(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A transformer delivers 45 kW to its load and has total losses of 2.5 kW. What is its efficiency?","o":["94.7 percent","105.9 percent","94.4 percent","5.6 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Input = 45 + 2.5 = 47.5 kW; efficiency = 45 / 47.5 = 94.7 percent. Dividing losses by output gives 5.6. [Efficiency]","r":"Efficiency"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1926.302(a), how must an electric power tool be protected against shock?","o":["It may be used ungrounded when it is on a 240-volt circuit with a GFCI","It must always be battery powered when used in any construction work","No protection is needed when it is used only in the interior of buildings","It must be double insulated or grounded, and the cord may not hoist or lower it"],"a":3,"e":"Electric power-operated tools must be double-insulated or grounded per Subpart K; cords may not be used for raising or lowering tools.","r":"29 CFR 1926.302(a)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Annual demand for a part is 2,400 units, each order costs $45 to place and carrying one unit for a year costs $3. What is the economic order quantity (EOQ = sqrt(2DS/H)), rounded to the nearest unit?","o":["268 units","134 units","72000 units","200 units"],"a":0,"e":"EOQ = sqrt(2 x 2,400 x 45 / 3) = sqrt(72,000) = 268.3, about 268 units. [Inventory management]","r":"EOQ"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"At the 5 W tap on a 70.7 V speaker transformer, the impedance presented to the line is approximately:","o":["500 ohms","2500 ohms","1000 ohms","8 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Z = V squared / P = 70.7 x 70.7 / 5, about 1000 ohms. The 2 W tap would be about 2500 ohms. [Ohm's law]","r":"70.7 V impedance (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A unit-price contract pays $14.50 per linear foot of installed feeder conduit. The plans estimated 640 feet, but 712 feet were actually installed and measured. What is the amount due for the conduit?","o":["$9,280","$10,324 less 10 percent retainage","$1,044","$10,324"],"a":3,"e":"Unit-price payment uses actual measured quantity: 712 ft x $14.50 = $10,324. The estimated quantity, 640 ft x $14.50 = $9,280, is only the bid basis. Extra footage is $72 x $14.50 = $1,044. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 24 V notification appliance circuit carries 0.75 A total through 16 AWG wire. The wire is 400 ft one way (800 ft round trip) and has a resistance of 4.0 ohms per 1000 ft. What is the voltage at the last appliance?","o":["22.8 V","21.6 V","19.2 V","24.0 V"],"a":1,"e":"R = 4.0 x 800/1000 = 3.2 ohm. Drop = 0.75 x 3.2 = 2.4 V. 24 - 2.4 = 21.6 V (a 10 percent drop). Using only the one-way length gives 22.8 V. [Ohm's law; NFPA 72 (2019) 10.14 / 18 voltage drop practice]","r":"Ohm's law; NFPA 72 18 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A workers' compensation policy charges $8.50 per $100 of payroll for an electrical class code. How much is the premium on $120,000 of covered payroll?","o":["$1,020.00","$10,200.00","$102,000.00","$8,500.00"],"a":1,"e":"Premium = ($120,000 / $100) x $8.50 = $10,200.00. Misplacing the decimal gives $1,020 or $102,000. [F.S. ch. 440; workers' compensation payroll-based premium]","r":"F.S. ch. 440 premium basics (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"NFPA 72 mounting for wall-mounted visible notification appliances places the entire lens at what height above the finished floor?","o":["At least 80 in. and not more than 96 in.","At least 96 in. and not more than 108 in.","At least 48 in. and not more than 66 in.","At least 60 in. and not more than 80 in."],"a":0,"e":"NFPA 72 permits an 80 in. to 96 in. lens height, while ADAAG 4.28.3 sets a mounting at 80 in. or 6 in. below the ceiling, whichever is lower. [NFPA 72 (2019), 18.5.5]","r":"NFPA 72 18.5.5 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the \"High-Velocity Hurricane Zone\" (HVHZ) in the Florida Building Code?","o":["The entire Florida panhandle west of the Apalachicola River","Any county that is within 50 miles of the Atlantic or Gulf coast","Only the Florida Keys and the county of Monroe","Miami-Dade and Broward Counties, with stricter approval rules"],"a":3,"e":"The Florida Building Code defines the HVHZ as Miami-Dade and Broward Counties, where additional testing protocols and product approval requirements apply. [FBC Building ch. 2 definitions; ch. 16 HVHZ provisions]","r":"FBC Building ch. 2 definitions; ch. 16 HVHZ provisions (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NEC 760.33 requires what on the supply side of a fire alarm control panel?","o":["A shunt-trip breaker","A ground-fault circuit interrupter","A lighting contactor","A listed surge-protective device (SPD)"],"a":3,"e":"The 2023 NEC requires surge protection on the supply side of the fire alarm control unit under 760.33, referring to Part II of Article 242. [NEC 760.33]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 760.33"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"During the summer (June 1 through Labor Day), a 15-year-old may work until what time in the evening under the FLSA?","o":["10 p.m.","9 p.m.","7 p.m.","8 p.m."],"a":1,"e":"The usual 7 a.m. to 7 p.m. window extends to 9 p.m. from June 1 through Labor Day. [29 CFR 570.35]","r":"29 CFR 570.35"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The notice required by s. 489.1425, F.S., opens with which statement?","o":["ACCORDING TO FLORIDA'S CONSTRUCTION LIEN LAW, THOSE WHO WORK ON YOUR PROPERTY OR PROVIDE MATERIALS MAY ENFORCE CLAIMS.","THE CONTRACTOR HAS PROVIDED EVIDENCE OF WORKERS' COMPENSATION COVERAGE AS REQUIRED BY CHAPTER 440, FLORIDA STATUTES.","YOU HAVE THREE BUSINESS DAYS TO CANCEL THIS CONTRACT WITHOUT PENALTY AFTER THE DATE OF SIGNING.","PAYMENT, UP TO A LIMITED AMOUNT, MAY BE AVAILABLE FROM THE FLORIDA HOMEOWNERS' CONSTRUCTION RECOVERY FUND."],"a":3,"e":"Section 489.1425(1) sets the recovery-fund statement, followed by the board's address and telephone number. The lien law warning is the separate s. 713.015 notice. [F.S. 489.1425(1)]","r":"F.S. 489.1425(1)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which entity enforces the requirement that Florida employers secure workers' compensation coverage and issues stop-work orders?","o":["The Wage and Hour Division of the U.S. Department of Labor","The Department of Business and Professional Regulation, Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board","The Florida Commission on Human Relations in the Executive Office of the Governor","The Division of Workers' Compensation of the Department of Financial Services"],"a":3,"e":"The Division of Workers' Compensation, within the Department of Financial Services, investigates compliance and issues stop-work orders under s. 440.107. [F.S. 440.107]","r":"F.S. 440.107"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A claimant last furnished labor on a Florida public project on February 10, 2028. Assuming no notice of contest was recorded, by what date must the bond suit be filed under F.S. 255.05(10)?","o":["February 10, 2029","May 10, 2028","August 8, 2028","February 10, 2032"],"a":0,"e":"One year after February 10, 2028 is February 10, 2029. [F.S. 255.05(10)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(10)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Addendum 2 (dated June 10) changes a panelboard from 42 circuits to 30 circuits, while Addendum 1 (dated June 3) said 42 circuits. Under the typical clause that later addenda govern over earlier ones, which is bid?","o":["30 circuits, from the later addendum","42 circuits, because the first addendum was issued first","36 circuits, the average","Either, whichever is cheaper"],"a":0,"e":"A later-dated addendum supersedes an earlier one on the same item. [Typical order-of-precedence clause]","r":"Typical order-of-precedence clause"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An umbrella (excess liability) policy","o":["replaces the workers' compensation policy on the job","covers only the contractor's tools and small equipment","provides limits above the underlying general liability, auto and employer's liability policies","pays only if a payment bond is already in force"],"a":2,"e":"Umbrella coverage sits on top of underlying liability policies and pays after their limits are exhausted. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What is the smallest Schedule 40 PVC trade size for six 4 AWG THW copper conductors (0.0973 sq in. each)? The 40% fill areas of Schedule 40 PVC: 1 in. = 0.333; 1-1/4 in. = 0.581; 1-1/2 in. = 0.794; 2 in. = 1.316.","o":["2 in.","1-1/2 in.","1-1/4 in.","1 in."],"a":1,"e":"6 x 0.0973 = 0.5838 sq in. The 1-1/4 in. size allows only 0.581, while 1-1/2 in. allows 0.794. [NEC Chapter 9, Tables 1, 4, 5]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Tables 1, 4, 5"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's perpetual inventory shows 340 units on hand at the start of the week; 600 are received and 515 are issued to jobs. What should the book balance be?","o":["255 units","1,455 units","425 units","85 units"],"a":2,"e":"Ending balance = beginning + receipts - issues = 340 + 600 - 515 = 425. [Inventory control]","r":"Perpetual inventory"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign needs 4 identical round footings, each 2 ft in diameter and 6 ft deep. What is the total concrete volume in cubic yards (no waste factor), rounded to one decimal?","o":["75.4 cu yd","11.2 cu yd","1.8 cu yd","2.8 cu yd"],"a":3,"e":"Each footing = pi x 1.0^2 x 6 = 18.85 cu ft; four = 75.4 cu ft, which is 2.79 cu yd. 75.4 is the volume in cubic feet. [Volume of a cylinder; unit conversion]","r":"Volume of a cylinder; unit conversion (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"At a carnival, how must metal frames of rides, concessions and trailers that contain or support electrical equipment be treated?","o":["Bonded to the equipment grounding conductor","Isolated from the supply grounding","Grounded to a driven rod only","Left unbonded if double insulated"],"a":0,"e":"Metal parts of rides, concessions, trailers and similar equipment that contain or support electrical equipment must be bonded to the EGC of the supply. [NEC 525.30]","r":"NEC 525.30"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A foreman is on the payroll full time, spending 80% of his time on Job A and 20% on shop duties. The cost classification is","o":["80% direct labor on Job A and 20% indirect (overhead)","100% overhead, because he is salaried and not paid by the hour","100% direct labor, because he is physically on site every day","100% direct labor on whichever job is largest in a given month"],"a":0,"e":"Time that can be traced to a job is direct; time spent on general shop duties is overhead. Allocating by actual time keeps job costs accurate. [Direct vs indirect costs]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the peak value of a 120 V RMS sine wave?","o":["84.9 V","169.7 V","76.4 V","240 V"],"a":1,"e":"Peak = RMS x 1.414 = 169.7 V. 120/1.414 = 84.9 V goes the wrong way. [RMS and peak]","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which statement best describes the difference between a contract warranty period and a statute of repose?","o":["Both are set by the contractor in the contract documents","A warranty is a contract promise; repose is a statutory outer limit on suing","The warranty period is set by the Legislature and repose by the lender","They are the same period under Florida law and cannot differ"],"a":1,"e":"A warranty period comes from the agreement. A statute of repose, such as the one in s. 95.11(3)(b), is a statutory cutoff that bars claims after a set time, even if a defect has not yet been found. [F.S. 95.11(3)(b)]","r":"F.S. 95.11(3)(b)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 218.735(7), F.S., a local government and contractor dispute in good faith whether some punch-list items are done. The remaining items would cost $6,000 to complete. What is the most the government may withhold for them?","o":["$6,000","$9,000","$12,000","$600"],"a":1,"e":"The entity may withhold up to 150 percent of the cost to complete the disputed items: $6,000 x 1.5 = $9,000. [F.S. 218.735(7)]","r":"F.S. 218.735(7)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the FLSA, a bona fide meal period is not hours worked when it is generally at least:","o":["60 minutes whether or not relieved of duty","10 minutes with the employee remaining on the job","15 minutes if the employee stays at the work site","30 minutes with the employee completely relieved of duty"],"a":3,"e":"Meal periods of 30 minutes or more are not worked time when the employee is completely relieved from duty. [29 CFR 785.19]","r":"29 CFR 785.19"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A town awards a $160,000 electrical contract and the awarding official exercises the discretion to exempt the project from the bond requirement. For which dollar range does F.S. 255.05 allow a county, city or political subdivision to do this?","o":["$50,000 or less","$200,000 or less","$500,000 or less","Any amount"],"a":1,"e":"Counties, municipalities and other political subdivisions may exempt contracts of $200,000 or less. State contracts of $100,000 or less are exempt. [F.S. 255.05(1)(d)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)(d)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Activity A takes 12 days and activity B takes 9 days. B is linked start-to-start with a 5-day lag. If A starts on day 0 and there are no other constraints, on what day does the later of the two activities finish?","o":["Day 14","Day 26","Day 17","Day 21"],"a":0,"e":"B starts on day 5 and finishes day 5+9 = 14. A finishes day 12. The project finish is the larger: day 14. [Start-to-start lag]","r":"Lead and lag"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Why should the Class 2 output conductors of an LED power supply be kept as short as practicable?","o":["Because Class 2 wiring may not exceed 5 ft in length","To increase the current in the circuit","To limit voltage drop at low voltage and keep LED brightness even","To avoid the need for any bonding of the sign"],"a":2,"e":"Low-voltage circuits are sensitive to voltage drop; 600.21(B) also calls for power supplies as near to the lamps as practicable. [NEC 600.21(B)]","r":"NEC 600.21(B)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 713.23, a copy of the payment bond must be","o":["mailed only to the building inspector","attached to the recorded notice of commencement","filed with the Department of Revenue","kept private by the surety"],"a":1,"e":"The contractor furnishes the bond before construction begins and attaches a copy to the notice of commencement. [F.S. 713.23(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(1)(a)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which cost is VARIABLE with the number of jobs completed?","o":["Annual insurance premium for the shop","Monthly lease on the office","Wire and devices installed on the jobs","Salary of the full-time estimator"],"a":2,"e":"Variable costs rise and fall with job volume (materials, hourly labor); the others are fixed in the short run. [Direct vs indirect costs]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which set lists the required elements of a GHS label on a shipped hazardous chemical container under 29 CFR 1910.1200(f)?","o":["Product identifier, signal word, hazard statements, pictograms, precautionary statements and supplier information","Product identifier, expiration date, retail price, lot number and barcode symbol","Pictogram, barcode, shelf life, recycling symbol and manufacturer's phone number","Supplier name, SDS number, EPA number, fire rating and storage temperature"],"a":0,"e":"Labels must include the product identifier, signal word, hazard statements, pictograms, precautionary statements, and the name, address and phone number of the responsible party.","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200(f)(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 60 Hz transformer connected to a DC source would likely","o":["overheat and burn out because there is no induced counter voltage","deliver double the secondary voltage at no load","operate normally with lower losses than on alternating current","deliver the same secondary voltage as on 60 Hz"],"a":0,"e":"Without changing flux there is no inductive reactance; only winding resistance limits the current. [Transformer operation]","r":"Transformer operation"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A 3-person crew installs 60 feet of conduit per crew-hour. The job has 1,260 feet of conduit. How many 8-hour workdays does it take?","o":["3 days (rounded up from 2.625)","1 day (dividing by crew size too)","6 days (doubling for the crew)","21 days (1,260 / 60)"],"a":0,"e":"Output per day = 60 x 8 = 480 ft. Days = 1,260 / 480 = 2.625, so 3 days. Dividing by crew size again double counts the crew. [Productivity]","r":"Crew scheduling (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has annual revenue of $3,650,000 and year-end accounts receivable of $450,000. Using a 365-day year, what are days sales outstanding (DSO)?","o":["90 days","45 days","8 days","12 days"],"a":1,"e":"DSO = receivables / revenue x 365 = $450,000 / $3,650,000 x 365 = 45 days. 8.1 is receivables turnover, not days. [Receivables analysis]","r":"Days sales outstanding"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A resistive heating element rated 16 ohms is connected to a 240 V source. How much current does it draw?","o":["0.067 A","15 A","3,840 A","224 A"],"a":1,"e":"I = E / R = 240 / 16 = 15 A. Multiplying (3,840 A) is the common slip. [Ohm's law]","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Applicants who agree to the questionnaire in Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C. also agree to notify the board in writing of any change in answers within","o":["10 days","30 days","24 hours","90 days"],"a":0,"e":"The questionnaire includes an agreement to notify the board in writing within ten days of any change. [Rule 61G6-5.004]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C."},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A store has 24 ft of track lighting. What minimum load is added for it, per 220.43?","o":["4,800 VA","1,800 VA","900 VA","3,600 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Track lighting is calculated at 150 VA for every 2 ft of track: 24/2 x 150 = 1,800 VA. [NEC 220.43(B)]","r":"NEC 220.43(B)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Before bond cost, an estimate totals $318,000. The performance and payment bond premium is 1.2% of the final contract price. What contract price covers the estimate and the bond premium (rounded to the cent)?","o":["$321,816","$321,862.35","$314,229.25","$325,632"],"a":1,"e":"Let P be the price: P = 318,000 + 0.012P, so P = 318,000/0.988 = 321,862.35. Multiplying by 1.012 gives $321,816, which charges the premium on cost rather than on the price. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor pays a full year of liability insurance premium in advance in January. On the January 31 balance sheet, the unexpired portion is reported as","o":["revenue earned in January","a prepaid expense (current asset)","an accrued liability (current)","a reduction of owner's equity"],"a":1,"e":"Premiums paid for future coverage are assets (prepaid expenses) that are expensed as the coverage period passes. [Accrual accounting]","r":"Accrual accounting"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which cause of delay is listed in FAR 52.249-10 as potentially excusable?","o":["Rework of the contractor's defective installation","A contractor's decision to reduce its crew","Late subcontractor mobilization caused by the contractor's poor planning","Strikes"],"a":3,"e":"The clause lists acts of God, government acts, acts of another contractor, fires, floods, epidemics, quarantine, strikes, freight embargoes, unusually severe weather, and subcontractor delays that are not the fault of the contractor or subcontractor. [FAR 52.249-10(b)]","r":"FAR 52.249-10"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A portable ladder used to access an upper landing must extend at least how far above the landing?","o":["3 feet","1 foot","2 feet","5 feet"],"a":0,"e":"The side rails must extend at least 3 feet above the upper landing, or the ladder must be secured at its top with a grasping device such as a grabrail. [29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The Florida Homeowners' Construction Recovery Fund under s. 489.1401, F.S., exists to compensate a claimant who:","o":["Is a commercial landlord who lost rental income on a building","Hired an unlicensed handyman to repair a home's water heater","Contracted for work on a homeowner's residence and has a judgment, restitution, or award","Is a licensee who lost a competitive bid to another contractor"],"a":2,"e":"The claimant must have contracted for work on a homeowner's residence in Florida and obtained a final judgment, board restitution or an arbitration award for financial mismanagement, abandonment or a false statement under s. 489.129(1)(g), (j) or (k). [F.S. 489.1401(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.1401(2)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The maximum unbalanced load on a 3-phase, 4-wire wye feeder is 450 A, of which 100 A is the neutral current from nonlinear (electric-discharge lighting) loads. What neutral current should be used?","o":["450 A","325 A","350 A","405 A"],"a":3,"e":"The 70% reduction applies only to the linear portion of the unbalanced load over 200 A: 200 + 0.70 x (350 - 200) = 305 A. The nonlinear 100 A may not be reduced, so 305 + 100 = 405 A. [NEC 220.61(B), 220.61(C)(2)]","r":"NEC 220.61(B), 220.61(C)(2)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A worker dies 20 days after a work-related incident. Is this a reportable fatality?","o":["No, because only deaths that occur within 24 hours are counted","No, because only deaths at the job site itself are counted","Yes, but only if the death occurs within 7 days of the incident","Yes, because the death occurred within 30 days of the incident"],"a":3,"e":"A fatality must be reported if death occurs within 30 days of the work-related incident. [29 CFR 1904.39(b)(6)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.39(b)(6)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Where employees may be exposed to injurious corrosive materials, what must be in the work area?","o":["A written warning sign posted at the entrance to the work area only","Facilities for quick drenching or flushing of the eyes and body","A portable heater, so that the water used for flushing does not freeze","A water cooler and cups, for workers to drink water as they need it"],"a":1,"e":"1926.50(g) requires quick-drenching or flushing facilities within the work area for immediate use where corrosives are present.","r":"29 CFR 1926.50(g)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the capacitive reactance of a 100 microfarad capacitor at 60 Hz?","o":["167 ohms","53.05 ohms","0.038 ohms","26.53 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"XC = 1 / (2 x pi x 60 x 0.0001) = 26.53 ohms. [Capacitive reactance]","r":"Capacitive reactance"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 715.12, interest on a late payment begins to accrue on which day after the payment is due?","o":["The 1st day","The 30th day","The 60th day","The 14th day"],"a":3,"e":"715.12(5) starts interest on the 14th day after payment is due, at the 55.03 rate plus 12% per year; retainage withheld under (7) is excluded. [F.S. 715.12(5)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A horn/strobe is wall-mounted in a corridor with its lowest edge 74 in. above the floor and projects 5 in. from the wall. Under ADAAG 4.4.1, the installation is:","o":["noncompliant, because it must be at least 80 in. high and project 12 in.","noncompliant, because the leading edge is in the 27-80 in. zone and projects more than 4 in.","compliant, because the limit is 6 in.","compliant, because alarm devices are exempt"],"a":1,"e":"Leading edges between 27 in. and 80 in. may protrude no more than 4 in. into a corridor. This horn/strobe protrudes 5 in. at 74 in., so it must be recessed, moved, or raised above 80 in. [ADAAG 4.4.1]","r":"ADAAG 4.4.1"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Good internal control divides duties so that the same person does NOT:","o":["Open the daily mail and file the paid vendor invoices in the office","Count the stockroom inventory and prepare the written count report","Use a company vehicle and the company fuel card for a single job","Order materials, receive them, and approve payment to the vendor"],"a":3,"e":"Segregating ordering, receiving and payment authority reduces the chance of fraud, error and kickbacks. [Internal control practice]","r":"Segregation of duties"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"To establish bonding capacity, a surety typically asks the contractor for","o":["current financial statements, a work-in-progress schedule and credit information","a list of its customers' home addresses and phone numbers","only the contractor's license number and a signed bid","a signed bid and bid tabulation for every project it wants"],"a":0,"e":"Underwriters analyze financial strength and backlog; the WIP schedule shows contracts in progress and expected profit. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the BEST use of a milestone in an electrical contract schedule?","o":["Marking the date temporary power must be on for other trades","Recording the hourly cost of each journeyman on site","Showing the contractor's profit margin on each task","Listing the daily weather conditions seen on the jobsite"],"a":0,"e":"Milestones track hand-off or approval points that other parties depend on. Cost and weather data are not schedule logic. [Scheduling terminology]","r":"Milestones (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A feeder supplies a panelboard in a commercial building that serves only 20-A receptacle branch circuits in locations that require GFCI protection. Which statement is correct?","o":["The feeder GFCI must be located within 6 ft of the panelboard","A feeder may never be GFCI protected; each receptacle must have its own GFCI","The feeder may be protected by a listed GFCI installed in a readily accessible location instead of protecting at each receptacle","Feeder GFCI protection is allowed only for dwelling units"],"a":2,"e":"Feeders are permitted to be protected by a listed GFCI in a readily accessible location in lieu of the GFCI protection at each receptacle required by 210.8. [NEC 215.9]","r":"NEC 215.9"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly lists the steps of applying hazardous energy control under 29 CFR 1910.147(d)?","o":["Shutdown, apply lockout/tagout devices, preparation, isolation, verify isolation, relieve stored energy","Preparation, shutdown, isolation, apply lockout/tagout devices, relieve stored energy, verify isolation","Preparation, isolation, apply devices, shutdown, verify isolation, relieve stored energy","Apply lockout/tagout devices, preparation, shutdown, relieve stored energy, isolation, verify isolation"],"a":1,"e":"The standard orders the application as preparation, shutdown, isolation, device application, stored energy release and finally verification before work starts. [29 CFR 1910.147(d)(1)-(6)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.147(d)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.105, F.S., which contractors make up 'Division I'?","o":["Plumbing, roofing, and air conditioning contractors","Electrical, alarm, and specialty contractors","General, building, and residential contractors","Pool, solar, and underground utility contractors"],"a":2,"e":"Division I consists of general, building and residential contractors (paragraphs (3)(a)-(c)); Division II contains the other Part I categories. Electrical contractors are licensed under Part II. [F.S. 489.105(3)]","r":"F.S. 489.105(3)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Unless the contract says otherwise, under F.S. 715.12(6) a dispute on one portion of the work allows the obligor to","o":["withhold all payments on the project until the entire dispute is resolved","withhold only the disputed amount, not payment for the work that is unaffected","withhold twice the disputed amount as security until the dispute is settled","suspend payment to every subcontractor on the project in the meantime"],"a":1,"e":"The statute says a dispute does not allow withholding payment from the obligee, or other obligees, for work not affected by that dispute. [F.S. 715.12(6)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A private employer outside the construction industry must carry workers' compensation coverage when it regularly has at least how many employees?","o":["One","Two","Four","Ten"],"a":2,"e":"Chapter 440 covers private employment with four or more employees; the construction industry is the exception with a one-employee threshold. [F.S. 440.02]","r":"F.S. 440.02"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A recorded Notice of Commencement is filed by the:","o":["Electrical contractor after final inspection","Supplier, before delivering material","County clerk when a permit is issued","Owner (or the owner's agent), before the work starts"],"a":3,"e":"Under s. 713.13 the owner records a Notice of Commencement before actually commencing to improve the property, and suppliers use it to find the owner's information. [F.S. 713.13]","r":"F.S. 713.13"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A return merchandise authorization (RMA) number is used to:","o":["Identify and authorize a return so the supplier will accept and credit it","Pay a freight bill that the carrier has presented to the buyer","File a Notice to Owner on a job that the supplier has supplied","Open a new credit account with a supplier for the first time"],"a":0,"e":"An RMA ties the return to the original order and tells the supplier what credit to issue. [Returns]","r":"Returns"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A DC motor armature draws 20 A from 240 V and has 0.6 ohm armature resistance. What is the counter-EMF?","o":["12 V","228 V","252 V","240 V"],"a":1,"e":"Eb = E - Ia x Ra = 240 - 12 = 228 V. [DC motors]","r":"DC motors"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-10.002(b), F.A.C., the first-offense penalty for procuring a license by bribery or fraud is:","o":["A reprimand only","Denial or revocation plus a $1,000 fine","A $100 fine","A notice of noncompliance"],"a":1,"e":"Fraud or bribery in obtaining a license draws denial or revocation and $1,000 for a first offense, revocation and $5,000 for a second, and revocation and $10,000 for a third. [F.A.C. 61G6-10.002(b)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-10.002(b)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 255.05, a claimant who is not in privity with the contractor (other than a laborer) must give the contractor written notice that it will look to the bond. The notice is due before starting work or within how many days after starting to furnish labor or materials?","o":["10 days","30 days","45 days","90 days"],"a":2,"e":"The notice to the contractor is due before or within 45 days after first furnishing; the sworn notice of nonpayment has a separate 90-day deadline. [F.S. 255.05(2)(a)2.]","r":"F.S. 255.05(2)(a)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Workers on construction sites have the right to ask OSHA to inspect their workplace. Which statement is correct under the OSH Act?","o":["An employer may not retaliate against an employee who files an OSHA complaint","Only employees who belong to a union may file an OSHA complaint","Retaliation is allowed if the complaint was made orally rather than in writing","Employees must first get the employer's permission before filing a complaint"],"a":0,"e":"Section 11(c) prohibits discrimination against employees who exercise OSH Act rights. The complaint must be filed with OSHA within 30 days of the retaliation. [29 U.S.C. 660(c)]","r":"OSH Act Sec. 11(c)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"2010 ADA Standards 215.1 requires that where fire alarm systems provide audible alarms, they must also provide:","o":["tactile alarms in each room","telephone dialers to a TTY","visible alarms complying with 702","backup audible alarms in stairs"],"a":2,"e":"Section 215 requires fire alarm systems to include visible alarms that comply with 702, and 702 points to NFPA 72 for the appliance performance. [2010 Standards 215.1, 702]","r":"2010 Std 215.1, 702"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor withholds 5% of an employee's $2,000 gross pay for a traditional 401(k) deferral ($100). Which statement is correct?","o":["Both income tax and FICA wages are $1,900","Both income tax and FICA wages remain $2,000","Federal income tax wages are $1,900, but FICA wages remain $2,000","FICA wages are $1,900 and income tax wages remain $2,000"],"a":2,"e":"Elective 401(k) deferrals reduce income tax wages but are still subject to Social Security and Medicare tax. A Section 125 cafeteria plan deduction reduces both. FICA on $2,000 is $153.00. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A schedule (all finish-to-start, working days) is: P 5d (start); Q 4d after P; R 4d after P; S 6d after Q; T 6d after R; U 3d after S+T. Activity R is delayed 2 days. What happens to completion?","o":["It slips 4 days","It slips 2 days, since R is critical","It slips 1 day","No change; Q's path still sets the finish"],"a":1,"e":"R is on a critical path with zero float, so a 2-day delay extends completion from 18 to 20 days. [Critical path delay]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1904.35, an employer must establish a reasonable procedure for employees to report injuries and illnesses, and may not:","o":["Require employees to report all injuries within 7 days or lose their right to report","Post the annual summary in the break room for the whole calendar year","Collect employee names only on the log and keep injury details private","Discharge or discriminate against an employee for reporting a work-related injury"],"a":3,"e":"Section 1904.35(b)(1)(iv) prohibits retaliation against an employee for reporting a work-related injury or illness; policies must not deter reporting. [29 CFR 1904.35(b)(1)(iv)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.35(b)(1)(iv)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The general lighting load of a 2400 sq ft dwelling is calculated at 3 VA per sq ft. How many 120-V, 20-A branch circuits are needed to serve this general lighting load at minimum (disregard the small-appliance and laundry circuits)?","o":["5","4","2","3"],"a":3,"e":"Load = 2400 x 3 = 7200 VA. A 20-A, 120-V circuit carries 2400 VA, so 7200 / 2400 = 3.0, or 3 circuits. (With 15-A circuits at 1800 VA each, 4 would be needed.) [NEC 210.11(A)]","r":"NEC 210.11(A)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under F.S. 633.202(17), tents are exempt from the Florida Fire Prevention Code if they are up to what size?","o":["1,500 square feet","300 square feet","5,000 square feet","900 square feet"],"a":3,"e":"Tents up to 900 sq ft are exempt, including the national codes the Fire Prevention Code incorporates. Larger tents are regulated. [F.S. 633.202(17)]","r":"F.S. 633.202(17)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 218.735(1), F.S., a local governmental entity must pay a proper construction payment request within how many business days after the stamped-received date?","o":["10 business days, whether or not agent approval is required","30 business days, whether or not agent approval is required","45 calendar days, whether or not agent approval is required","20 business days, when no agent approval is required"],"a":3,"e":"Subsection (1) sets 25 business days when an agent must approve the request first and 20 business days when no agent approval is needed. [F.S. 218.735(1)]","r":"F.S. 218.735(1)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The architect answers an RFI by phone and says to run 4 in. conduit instead of 3 in. The contractor believes this adds cost. Which step is most prudent?","o":["Proceed without making any record of the phone call, since the architect is the authority","Ask for written confirmation of the direction and give notice of the change in cost","Install the 3 in. conduit as drawn and say nothing about the phone call","Wait for the inspector to decide which size to install"],"a":1,"e":"Verbal direction should be documented in writing, and changes in cost or time must be notified under the contract, not assumed. [RFI practice (AIA A201)]","r":"RFI practice (AIA A201)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Two inductors of 6 H and 3 H are connected in parallel with no mutual coupling. The equivalent inductance is","o":["4.5 H","9 H","2 H","18 H"],"a":2,"e":"Parallel inductors combine like resistors: 6 x 3 / 9 = 2 H. [Inductors]","r":"Inductors"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Beginning equity is $520,000. During the year the corporation earned $138,000, distributed $60,000 to its owner, and the owner contributed $25,000 of new capital. What is ending equity?","o":["$573,000","$658,000","$743,000","$623,000"],"a":3,"e":"$520,000 + $138,000 - $60,000 + $25,000 = $623,000. Net income and contributions increase equity; distributions reduce it. [Statement of equity]","r":"Statement of equity"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"After a local government adopts a fire code amendment under F.S. 633.202, it must send the amendment to the Florida Building Commission and the State Fire Marshal within:","o":["60 days","180 days","30 days","10 days"],"a":2,"e":"The local government must transmit the amendment within 30 days after adoption. The 180 day figure is the State Fire Marshal's advance notice before a triennial adoption. [F.S. 633.202(8)]","r":"F.S. 633.202(8)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The same three 10 ohm resistors are now delta-connected to the 208 V source. What is the line current?","o":["12.0 A","36.0 A","62.4 A","20.8 A"],"a":1,"e":"Phase current = 208 / 10 = 20.8 A; line current = 1.732 x 20.8 = 36.0 A. Total power is 12,979 W, three times the wye value. [Delta connection]","r":"Delta connection"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A differing site conditions clause allows a contractor to ask for added time or money when:","o":["The contractor underestimated the labor cost when preparing the bid","The owner dislikes the appearance of the finished installation","The inspector schedules a re-inspection of the rough-in work","Concealed physical conditions differ materially from the contract documents"],"a":3,"e":"These clauses address concealed, unknown physical conditions that differ materially from the documents. The contractor generally must give prompt written notice. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Wiring and equipment at a marina's motor fuel dispensing area must comply with:","o":["Article 511","Article 514","Article 513","Article 515"],"a":1,"e":"Motor fuel dispensing stations at marinas are classified and wired per Article 514. [NEC 555.11]","r":"NEC 555.11"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.115(6), F.S., the board's authority regarding an applicant's felony conviction is:","o":["It must deny every applicant who has ever been convicted of any felony","It may deny based on severity, relevance, or public risk, but not solely for a felony","It may never consider criminal history under any circumstance","It must deny only if the felony occurred within the last year"],"a":1,"e":"A statewide criminal history check is required, and the board cannot deny solely because of a felony or failure to show restoration of civil rights, but may consider severity, relevance and risk. [F.S. 489.115(6)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(6)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An invoice for $6,500.00 is on terms 2/10, net 30. If paid within the discount period, how much is paid?","o":["$5,850.00","$6,370.00","$6,500.00","$6,240.00"],"a":1,"e":"Discount = $6,500.00 x 0.02 = $130.00; payment = $6,500.00 - $130.00 = $6,370.00. [Terms 2/10 net 30]","r":"Vendor terms"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A cost-plus-percentage-of-cost contract is sometimes criticized by owners because:","o":["The contractor receives no fee unless the job finishes early","The fee grows as costs grow, which weakens the incentive to control costs","The price can never be audited by the owner after the work","The owner pays only for the work that has been completed"],"a":1,"e":"When the fee is a percentage of cost, higher costs produce a higher fee. A fixed fee or a GMP aligns incentives better. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor places $50,000 of 5-year equipment in service in February and $30,000 of 5-year equipment in November (nothing else all year). What fraction of total depreciable basis falls in the last 3 months, and which convention results?","o":["37.5%, which is over the limit and triggers the mid-quarter convention","60.0%, which is over the limit and triggers the mid-quarter convention","62.5%, which is over the limit and triggers the mid-month convention","37.5%, which is not over 40%, so the half-year convention applies"],"a":3,"e":"30,000 / (50,000 + 30,000) = 37.5%. The mid-quarter convention requires MORE than 40% in the last quarter. [IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)]","r":"IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"One cubic yard of concrete is equal to how many cubic feet?","o":["27","9","12","36"],"a":0,"e":"A cubic yard is 3 ft x 3 ft x 3 ft = 27 cu ft. [Unit conversion]","r":"Unit conversion"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A feeder schedule shows two parallel sets of 3/0 AWG copper THWN conductors protected by a 400 A breaker. Using the 75 degrees C column of Table 310.16, 3/0 AWG copper is rated 200 A. What do the two sets provide, and is a 400 A breaker supported by the conductors?","o":["400 A total, so the conductors support the 400 A breaker","200 A total, so the 400 A breaker is too large","300 A total, so the breaker must be reduced to 300 A","800 A total, so the breaker could be raised to 800 A"],"a":0,"e":"Parallel conductors add: 2 x 200 A = 400 A, matching the 400 A standard breaker size in 240.6(A). Under 240.4 the breaker may not exceed the conductor ampacity (400 A here, which is also a standard size under 240.6(A)). A 300 A breaker would be allowed but is not required. [NEC Table 310.16, 240.4, 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC Table 310.16, 240.4, 240.6(A)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.505, F.S., an unlimited electrical contractor is a person in the electrical trade who is qualified to:","o":["Install only residential wiring in one- and two-family dwellings and garages","Install only low-voltage circuits that do not exceed 98 volts RMS in buildings","Install, repair, alter, add to, or design any electrical system, including plants and substations","Install only alarm systems used to signal or detect fire or burglary"],"a":2,"e":"The definition covers electrical systems and equipment without a segment limit, including those within plants and substations, and also anyone who offers, bids or performs that work directly or through others. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Florida's tip credit is $3.02 per hour. With the full minimum wage at $15.00 effective September 30, 2026, what is the minimum cash wage for a tipped employee?","o":["$11.98 per hour","$9.98 per hour","$12.98 per hour","$2.13 per hour"],"a":0,"e":"$15.00 - $3.02 = $11.98. The $2.13 figure is the federal tipped cash wage, which does not control where Florida's rate is higher. [F.S. 448.110; Fla. Const. Art. X, s. 24]","r":"F.S. 448.110(3); Fla. Const. Art. X s.24"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In the standard three-part section format used with MasterFormat, which part describes the materials, products, and equipment to be furnished?","o":["Part 1 - General","Part 3 - Execution","Part 2 - Products","Part 4 - Closeout"],"a":2,"e":"A section has Part 1 General (scope, submittals, quality assurance), Part 2 Products, and Part 3 Execution (installation, testing). [CSI SectionFormat]","r":"CSI SectionFormat"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An owner cannot require a lienor to use a lien waiver form different from the forms in F.S. 713.20 as a condition of payment. If a different form is signed anyway, it","o":["is enforceable according to its own terms if it is not substantially similar to the statutory form","is void in all cases because only the statutory forms are legally valid","is enforceable only for 60 days after it has been signed and delivered","must be recorded with the clerk of court to become effective against the lienor"],"a":0,"e":"713.20(6) bars requiring a different form; subsection (8) says a non-matching waiver is enforceable by its terms. [F.S. 713.20(6),(8)]","r":"F.S. 713.20"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Aluminum conduit is to be embedded in concrete or run in direct contact with the earth. What does 300.6 require?","o":["Nothing, because aluminum does not corrode","Supplementary corrosion protection","Only a bonding jumper","A second layer of aluminum conduit"],"a":1,"e":"Aluminum raceways and fittings in direct contact with concrete or earth require supplementary corrosion protection. [NEC 300.6(B)]","r":"NEC 300.6(B)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 3-wire circuit consists of two ungrounded conductors and the neutral of a 4-wire, 3-phase wye system. For applying the adjustment factors, how many current-carrying conductors does this circuit count as?","o":["2, because the neutral is not counted","3, because the neutral carries approximately the same current as the other conductors","1, because only one phase carries current","4, because the equipment grounding conductor counts"],"a":1,"e":"On a 3-wire circuit of two phase conductors and the neutral of a 4-wire wye, the neutral carries about the same current as the line-to-neutral load and counts as current-carrying. [NEC 310.15(E)(1)]","r":"NEC 310.15(E)(1)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's depreciation deduction on equipment is $9,000. If the contractor's combined tax rate is 24%, the approximate reduction in tax (the 'tax shield') is","o":["$9,000","$6,840","$216","$2,160"],"a":3,"e":"Tax shield = deduction x tax rate = 9,000 x 24% = 2,160. Depreciation is non-cash; the benefit is the lower tax payment. [Depreciation; cash flow]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"After the $15.00 rate takes effect, how does Florida's Constitution provide for future minimum wage increases?","o":["Annual CPI-based adjustment, with the new rate effective each following January 1","Fixed at $15.00 until the voters amend the Constitution again","Automatic $1.00 increase each September 30 with no further adjustment","Adjustment only when the Legislature votes to raise the minimum wage"],"a":0,"e":"Beginning September 30, 2027, the state recalculates the rate annually based on inflation (CPI-W), and the adjusted rate takes effect the next January 1. The $1.00 annual steps ended at $15.00. [Fla. Const. Art. X, s. 24(c)]","r":"Fla. Const. Art. X s.24(c)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The Florida Prompt Payment Act sections discussed for private construction (715.12) apply to","o":["written contracts to improve real property where a chapter 713, part I lien may be claimed","verbal agreements for the repair or maintenance of tangible personal property","federal government contracts only, regardless of whether a lien is available","residential tenants' monthly rent payments to a landlord under a lease"],"a":0,"e":"715.12 is limited to written contracts to improve real property entered after December 31, 1992, where chapter 713, part I lien rights exist. [F.S. 715.12]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which financial statement reports revenues and expenses for a period of time such as a fiscal year?","o":["Aged receivables listing","Balance sheet","Schedule of values","Income statement"],"a":3,"e":"The income statement (profit and loss statement) summarizes a period's revenue and expenses and results in net income or loss. The balance sheet is a point-in-time report. [Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C."},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which ampere rating is NOT one of the standard branch-circuit ratings recognized for branch circuits other than individual branch circuits?","o":["25 A","50 A","30 A","20 A"],"a":0,"e":"The standard branch-circuit ratings are 15, 20, 30, 40 and 50 A. 25 A is a standard overcurrent device rating in 240.6(A) but is not a branch-circuit rating. [NEC 210.3]","r":"NEC 210.3"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Available fault current at a new 480-V panelboard is 15,700 A. Which is the lowest standard interrupting rating that is adequate for the main breaker (assuming no series rating)?","o":["22,000 A","14,000 A","10,000 A","18,000 A"],"a":3,"e":"Equipment intended to interrupt current must have an interrupting rating at least equal to the available fault current at its line terminals. 14,000 A is too low; 18,000 A is the lowest of the standard ratings that is adequate. [NEC 110.9]","r":"NEC 110.9"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A DC series motor operated with no load will","o":["run at a constant, safe speed set by the supply","stall because the field current becomes zero","reverse direction after a short period","race to dangerous speed (runaway)"],"a":3,"e":"With no load, armature current and field current are small, so speed rises until the motor may fly apart. [DC motors]","r":"DC motors"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the apparent power of a balanced three-phase 480 V load drawing 60 A?","o":["86.4 kVA","28.8 kVA","49.9 kVA","14.4 kVA"],"a":2,"e":"kVA = 1.732 x E x I / 1,000 = 1.732 x 480 x 60 / 1,000 = 49.9 kVA. [Three-phase power]","r":"Three-phase power"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An electrician is installing a fire alarm manual pull station in a public corridor. Under the 2010 Standards, the operable part must be located within which reach range?","o":["36 in. to 60 in. above the floor","15 in. to 48 in. above the floor","24 in. to 54 in. above the floor","42 in. to 66 in. above the floor"],"a":1,"e":"A pull station is an operable part that must comply with 205.1 and 309, so its operable part falls in the 15 in. to 48 in. range. NFPA 72 further sets it at 42 in. to 48 in. [2010 Standards 205.1, 308]","r":"2010 Std 205.1, 308"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Substantial completion generally means the work is:","o":["Complete enough that the owner can use it for its intended purpose","Fully paid for, including all retainage held by the owner","Free of every minor defect and approved by the architect","Approved for financing by the owner's construction lender"],"a":0,"e":"At substantial completion only minor items remain, so the owner can use the facility. Final completion, which follows punch-list work, is a later milestone. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An employee is admitted as an in-patient to a hospital because of a work-related injury. By when must the employer report to OSHA?","o":["Within 8 hours, by telephone call to the area office only","Within 72 hours, in writing to the area office director","At the end of the month, along with the annual OSHA summary","Within 24 hours of learning of the in-patient hospitalization"],"a":3,"e":"Each work-related in-patient hospitalization, amputation or loss of an eye must be reported within 24 hours of learning of it. [29 CFR 1904.39(a)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.39(a)(2)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Three 10 ohm resistors are wye-connected to a 208 V three-phase source. What is the total power?","o":["1,442 W","12,979 W","7,494 W","4,326 W"],"a":3,"e":"Phase voltage = 120.1 V; phase current = 12.01 A; P = 3 x 12.01 squared x 10 = 4326 W. [Three-phase power]","r":"Three-phase power"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The main purpose of a pre-bid conference is to","o":["open the bids and read each total price aloud to the room","let bidders see the project scope and ask questions before they price the work","sign the final contract with the apparent low bidder","review and approve the contractor's first change order"],"a":1,"e":"Pre-bid meetings clarify scope and site conditions; any answers that change the documents are issued in writing as addenda. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Employers that establish a qualifying drug-free workplace program in Florida are eligible for what workers' compensation benefit?","o":["A waiver of the exemption certificate fee for its officers","A premium discount on their workers' compensation insurance","An exemption from workers' compensation stop-work orders","An exemption from OSHA injury and illness recordkeeping"],"a":1,"e":"Employers with a drug-free workplace program that meets s. 440.102 can qualify for a workers' compensation premium discount (a 5 percent credit under the premium program). [F.S. 440.102; F.S. 627.0915]","r":"F.S. 440.102; 627.0915"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which action is most likely to INCREASE a contractor's bonding capacity over time?","o":["Leaving job losses off the WIP schedule given to the surety","Taking large owner distributions each year","Switching to cash-basis internal statements only","Retaining earnings and building working capital supported by CPA-prepared statements"],"a":3,"e":"Sureties lend credit based on working capital and net worth; retained earnings and reliable CPA statements strengthen both, while heavy distributions deplete them. [Surety underwriting]","r":"Bonding capacity (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum running slope of an accessible ramp in new construction under ADAAG 4.8.2?","o":["1:8","1:12","1:16","1:10"],"a":1,"e":"Ramps in new construction are limited to a 1:12 slope, with a maximum rise of 30 in. per run. Steeper slopes appear only in the alterations exceptions of 4.1.6. [ADAAG 4.8.2]","r":"ADAAG 4.8.2"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A general indemnity agreement signed with a surety generally obligates the contractor (and often its owners) to","o":["pay the owner's attorney fees in a dispute","reimburse the contractor's employees for lost wages","cover the insurer's deductible on each claim","reimburse the surety for losses and expenses it pays under the bonds"],"a":3,"e":"Sureties require indemnity because they expect to be repaid after paying a claim. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor holds money in excess of the value of the work performed and receives a written demand by certified mail. Intent to defraud may be inferred if the contractor fails to refund any owed portion within","o":["7 days","90 days","30 days","1 year"],"a":2,"e":"489.126(4) allows intent to be inferred if the owed amount is not refunded within 30 days of written demand; intent to later return the money is not a defense. [F.S. 489.126(4)]","r":"F.S. 489.126"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Semiweekly depositors use which schedule attached to Form 941 to show their tax liability by payday?","o":["Schedule C (Form 941)","Schedule D (Form 941)","Schedule SE","Schedule B (Form 941)"],"a":3,"e":"Semiweekly schedule depositors report liabilities on Schedule B (Form 941); monthly depositors enter monthly totals on Form 941 itself. [Instructions for Schedule B (Form 941)]","r":"Sched. B (Form 941) instructions"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The department determines that an employer who failed to secure coverage would have paid $14,200 in premium at approved manual rates over the preceding 12 months. What penalty under F.S. 440.107 applies (ignoring any credits)?","o":["$14,200","$1,000","$42,600","$28,400"],"a":3,"e":"The penalty is two times the premium or $1,000, whichever is greater: 2 x 14,200 = $28,400. [F.S. 440.107(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.107(7)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which IRS form can a business file to request a determination of whether a worker is an employee or an independent contractor?","o":["Form SS-8","Form W-9","Form 941","Form 8300"],"a":0,"e":"Form SS-8, Determination of Worker Status for Purposes of Federal Employment Taxes and Income Tax Withholding, asks the IRS to decide a worker's status. [IRS Topic 762]","r":"IRS Topic 762"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the practical effect of making a neon tube longer or narrower with the same gas?","o":["A lower voltage is needed to start the discharge","No change in the voltage is needed for a longer tube","A higher voltage is needed to start and sustain the discharge","The tube cannot be lit at any voltage once it is lengthened"],"a":2,"e":"The voltage needed for a discharge tube increases with tube length and decreases with larger diameter, so transformer size must match the tubing. [Gas discharge tube principles]","r":"Gas discharge tube principles (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A customer pays a contractor a deposit of $20,000 before any work has begun. Under accrual accounting, how is the deposit shown at that date?","o":["Revenue earned in full when the cash is received","A reduction of accounts payable to the vendors","A liability until the work is performed","An increase in owner's equity on the deposit date"],"a":2,"e":"The contractor owes the work (or a refund), so the deposit is a liability until earned. Recording it as revenue overstates income. [Accrual accounting / revenue recognition]","r":"Revenue recognition"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A subcontractor quotes $40,000 to the GC and the quote includes a 10% markup on the sub's own cost of $36,363.64. The GC then adds 5% to the subcontract price. What does the GC carry in its bid for that scope?","o":["$42,000","$38,181.82","$46,000","$46,200"],"a":0,"e":"The sub's price already includes its markup. The GC adds 5% to the 40,000 price: $42,000. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If a state has an outstanding federal unemployment loan balance for the required period, what happens to its employers' FUTA?","o":["The FUTA credit shrinks, raising the effective FUTA tax","The FUTA wage base is eliminated for its employers","The employees must pay the difference in their pay","The state rate automatically becomes the federal rate"],"a":0,"e":"Employers in a credit reduction state get a smaller credit against the 6.0% FUTA rate, increasing the net tax owed on Form 940. [Form 940 instructions]","r":"Form 940 instructions"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A one-line shows a 150 kVA, 208 V three-phase transformer with a nameplate impedance of 3.2%. Assuming an infinite primary source, what is the approximate available three-phase fault current at the transformer secondary terminals?","o":["13,011 A","416 A","7,512 A","22,536 A"],"a":0,"e":"FLA = 150,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 416.4 A. Isc = FLA / Z = 416.4 / 0.032 = 13,011 A. The full-load current alone is not the fault current, and multiplying by 1.732 or dividing by 1.732 again double-counts the three-phase factor. [NEC 110.9, 110.10 (available fault current)]","r":"NEC 110.9, 110.10 (available fault current)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which section of Division 26 would normally specify the grounding electrode system and equipment bonding requirements?","o":["26 05 53 - Identification for Electrical Systems","26 05 26 - Grounding and Bonding for Electrical Systems","26 28 16 - Enclosed Switches and Circuit Breakers","26 43 13 - Surge Protective Devices"],"a":1,"e":"26 05 26 is Grounding and Bonding for Electrical Systems, one of the common work results sections. [CSI MasterFormat 26 05 26]","r":"CSI MasterFormat 26 05 26"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In Florida's construction industry, the maximum number of officers of a corporation (or group of affiliated corporations) who may elect exemption from workers' compensation is","o":["one","three","five","ten"],"a":1,"e":"Each exempt officer must also own at least 10 percent of the stock and be listed as an officer with the Division of Corporations. [F.S. 440.02]","r":"F.S. 440.02"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"To use the owner-builder exemption in s. 489.503(6), F.S., the owner must:","o":["Hire a license holder to sign the permit application for the owner","Post a $75,000 bond with the clerk of the court first","Personally sign the permit application and receive the disclosure statement","Have the plans approved in writing by the adjoining neighbors"],"a":2,"e":"Subsection (6)(c) requires the owner to personally appear and sign the application, comply with local requirements, and receive the disclosure statement; an employee acting as a contractor is not exempt. [F.S. 489.503(6)(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.503(6)(c)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under IRC 460, a contract for construction that is not completed in the year it is entered into is called a","o":["long-term contract, which generally must use the percentage-of-completion method unless an exception applies","short-term contract, which must always use the cash method of accounting","hybrid contract, which must always use the completed-contract method for taxes","deferred contract, which is not taxed until the contractor decides to close it"],"a":0,"e":"IRC 460(f)(1) defines a long-term contract as a construction contract not completed in the year it is entered; IRC 460(b)(1) generally requires percentage of completion. [IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 2]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A metric-scaled drawing at 1:100 shows a cable tray segment that measures 85 mm on the sheet. What is the real length in meters, and approximately in feet?","o":["8.5 m, about 27.9 ft","0.85 m, about 2.8 ft","85 m, about 279 ft","8.5 m, about 8.5 ft"],"a":0,"e":"At 1:100, 85 mm x 100 = 8,500 mm = 8.5 m, and 8.5 x 3.28084 = 27.9 ft. The wrong choices apply 1:10 or 1:1000, or forget the scale. [Scale calculation]","r":"Scale calculation"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which is an acceptable power source for a power-limited fire alarm (PLFA) circuit?","o":["A standard 120-volt branch circuit with no listed supply","Any 24-volt transformer from a bell kit","A listed PLFA or Class 3 power supply or transformer","A listed Class 4 transmitter only"],"a":2,"e":"PLFA sources are a listed PLFA or Class 3 transformer or power supply, or listed equipment marked as a PLFA source. [NEC 760.121(A)]","r":"NEC 2023 760.121(A)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"How does a contingency differ from an allowance in an estimate?","o":["A contingency is paid to the owner; an allowance is paid to the contractor","A contingency covers unknown or unforeseen cost risk; an allowance covers a specific item whose selection is pending","A contingency is used only on cost-plus work; an allowance only on lump sum","There is no difference; the terms are interchangeable"],"a":1,"e":"Contingency is the estimator's reserve for uncertainty in the estimate. An allowance is a named line item whose final price depends on the owner's selection. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The minimum thickness of sheet aluminum or copper for a sign enclosure is:","o":["0.41 mm (0.016 in.)","0.76 mm (0.030 in.)","1.02 mm (0.040 in.)","0.51 mm (0.020 in.)"],"a":3,"e":"600.8(C): copper or aluminum 0.51 mm (0.020 in.), steel 0.41 mm (0.016 in.). [NEC 600.8(C)]","r":"NEC 600.8(C)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A person who knowingly files a false or misleading notice of election to be exempt, intending to deceive, commits a:","o":["Felony of the third degree","Misdemeanor of the second degree","Noncriminal infraction","Misdemeanor of the first degree"],"a":0,"e":"Section 440.05(4) makes it a third-degree felony to knowingly provide false or misleading information in an exemption notice, and the certificate is revoked. [F.S. 440.05(4)]","r":"F.S. 440.05(4)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's schedule of values is: Mobilization & submittals $30,000; Underground conduit $90,000; Switchgear $210,000; Feeders $150,000; Branch rough-in $180,000; Trim & devices $80,000; Testing & closeout $60,000. Work in place is: mobilization 100%, underground 100%, switchgear 50%, feeders 40%, branch rough-in 20%, others 0%. What is the total completed value for the pay application (before retainage)?","o":["$321,000","$120,000","$400,000","$381,000"],"a":0,"e":"Mobilization $30,000 + underground $90,000 + switchgear 50% x $210,000 = $105,000 + feeders 40% x $150,000 = $60,000 + rough-in 20% x $180,000 = $36,000 = $321,000. Contract total is $800,000. [Schedule of values billing]","r":"SOV vs schedule (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Secondary (standby) power for a typical fire alarm system must be capable of operating the system for:","o":["4 hours of standby followed by 5 minutes of alarm","60 hours of standby followed by 15 minutes of alarm","8 hours of standby followed by 1 hour of alarm","24 hours of standby followed by 5 minutes of alarm"],"a":3,"e":"The standard requirement is 24 h standby and 5 min alarm; voice and emergency communications use 15 min of alarm. [NFPA 72 (2019) 10.6.7.2.1]","r":"NFPA 72 10.6.7 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.008, F.A.C., if an electrical contractor's required insurance is cancelled before it expires, the insurer must mail written notice to the certificate holder at least","o":["5 days before cancellation","60 days before cancellation","30 days before cancellation","90 days before cancellation"],"a":2,"e":"The rule provides a 30-day written cancellation notice to the certificate holder, the Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier's quote dated October 12, 2026 states 'valid for 30 days.' On what date does the quote expire?","o":["November 12, 2026","November 11, 2026","October 31, 2026","November 9, 2026"],"a":1,"e":"October 12, 2026 + 30 days = November 11, 2026. October has 31 days, so the 30th day after October 12 falls in November. [Quote terms]","r":"Quote validity"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A resistor is marked 2.2 megohms. How many ohms is this?","o":["2,200,000 ohms","220,000,000 ohms","220,000 ohms","22,000 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"Mega = 1,000,000, so 2.2 x 10^6 = 2,200,000 ohms. [Conversions]","r":"Conversions"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What is the 'live-dead-live' method?","o":["Turn the breaker on, off, and on again before touching the conductors","Prove the tester on a known live source, test the circuit, then prove the tester again","Test the hot, the neutral and the ground in that order, then repeat","Test the circuit twice and average the two readings before working"],"a":1,"e":"Proving the tester before and after the test verifies the meter worked on the dead circuit. For circuits over 600 V nominal, the tester is checked before and after. [29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(iv)(B)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(iv)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The general limitations period for an action for breach of a contract for sale of goods under the Florida UCC is:","o":["4 years after the cause of action accrues","10 years after the cause of action accrues, as for written contracts","2 years after the final payment is made to the seller","1 year after the breach is discovered by the buyer"],"a":0,"e":"Section 672.725 provides four years from accrual; parties may reduce it by agreement to not less than one year but cannot extend it. [F.S. 672.725]","r":"F.S. 672.725"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor's break-even point is the volume of sales at which","o":["variable costs equal fixed costs for the period being analyzed","total revenue equals total costs, so profit is zero","gross profit is equal to 100% of revenue for the period","cash on hand equals accounts payable at the end of the month"],"a":1,"e":"At break-even, contribution margin exactly covers fixed costs. [Break-even analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum depth below the normal water level for the top of the lens of an underwater luminaire not identified for lesser depths?","o":["18 in.","12 in.","24 in.","6 in."],"a":0,"e":"The top of the lens of a wall-mounted underwater luminaire must be at least 450 mm (18 in.) below the normal water level, unless listed for less. [NEC 680.23(A)(5)]","r":"NEC 680.23(A)(5)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A general liability policy's \"general aggregate\" limit is","o":["the most it will pay for any one claim only","the most the insurer will pay for all covered claims during the policy period","the total face amount of all bonds the contractor holds","the premium the contractor owes for the year"],"a":1,"e":"Per-occurrence limits cap a single event; the aggregate caps the total for the policy period. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In ASCE 7, the basic design wind speed is generally defined as a:","o":["A 1-hour average speed measured at 100 ft above the ground","A 3-second gust speed at 33 ft above ground in open terrain","The peak instantaneous speed measured at the ground surface of the site","A 5-minute average speed measured at 10 ft above ground in open country"],"a":1,"e":"ASCE 7 wind speeds are 3-second gust speeds at 33 ft (10 m) above ground in Exposure C open terrain, not hourly averages. [ASCE 7 wind speed definition]","r":"ASCE 7 wind speed definition (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under NFPA 101, new exit enclosures (stairs) connecting four or more stories must have a fire resistance rating of at least:","o":["3 hours","30 minutes","2 hours","1 hour"],"a":2,"e":"Exit enclosures connecting four or more stories are 2-hour rated; those connecting three stories or fewer are 1-hour rated. [NFPA 101, 7.1.3.2.1]","r":"NFPA 101 7.1.3.2.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Before an owner pays a lienor other than the contractor directly under s. 713.06(3)(g), F.S., the owner must give the contractor written notice at least:","o":["3 days before paying, in any form the owner chooses","10 days before paying, unless the contractor consents in writing","30 days before paying, by certified mail only","60 days before paying, with a copy to the surety"],"a":1,"e":"The owner must give at least 10 days' written notice of the intended payment amount unless the contractor consents in writing. [F.S. 713.06(3)(g)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(3)(g)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Concrete compressive strength is normally specified and reported at what age?","o":["24 hours","28 days","7 days","90 days"],"a":1,"e":"Specified compressive strength f'c is conventionally tested on cylinders at 28 days. [PCA, concrete strength]","r":"PCA, concrete strength (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor draws $80,000 on a line of credit for 45 days at 9% annual interest (365-day year, simple interest). The interest cost is about","o":["$7,200.00","$887.67","$1,400.00","$600.00"],"a":1,"e":"80,000 x 0.09 x 45/365 = 887.67. A full year of interest would be 7,200. [Line of credit interest]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under what condition may a portable power tool used in a damp location omit the equipment grounding conductor on a construction site?","o":["It is rated less than 6 amperes and is used only on a short extension cord","It is plugged into an extension cord longer than 50 feet with a GFCI","It has a plastic housing of any kind, whether or not it is marked","It is a listed or labeled tool marked as double insulated"],"a":3,"e":"Listed or labeled portable tools and appliances protected by an approved system of double insulation, and distinctively marked, need not be grounded. Plastic housing alone is not enough. [29 CFR 1926.404(f)(7)(iv)(C)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.404(f)(7)(iv)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Before loading a powder-actuated tool, what must the operator do?","o":["Load the tool and fire one test shot into the air to confirm function","Remove the safety shield so that the barrel can be inspected visually","Test the tool's safety devices each day, per the manufacturer's procedure","Fire it against a steel plate to confirm that the charge is correct"],"a":2,"e":"Powder-actuated tools must be tested each day before loading to see that safety devices are in proper working condition. [29 CFR 1926.302(e)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.302(e)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The operating part of a manual fire alarm box (pull station) is typically mounted at what height above the floor?","o":["Between 24 in. and 36 in.","Between 54 in. and 60 in.","Not less than 42 in. and not more than 48 in.","Between 80 in. and 96 in."],"a":2,"e":"Manual stations are mounted 42 to 48 in. AFF to the operating part, consistent with ADA reach ranges. 80-96 in. is the strobe mounting range. [NFPA 72 (2019) 17.14.5; ADA Std. 308]","r":"NFPA 72 17.14.5; ADA 308 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A metal wireway is installed horizontally. What is the maximum distance between supports?","o":["5 ft (and not more than 10 ft in any case), unless listed for other intervals","15 ft","20 ft","3 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Horizontal wireways are supported at each end and at intervals not to exceed 5 ft (or at each end or joint for sections over 5 ft), with at most 10 ft between supports. [NEC 376.30(A)]","r":"NEC 376.30(A)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For a general care patient bed location, how must the minimum two branch circuits be supplied?","o":["Both circuits from the normal system","At least one circuit from emergency and one from normal","Both circuits from the emergency system","One from normal and one from optional standby"],"a":1,"e":"Each general care bed location needs at least two circuits, one from the emergency system and one from the normal system, and not a multiwire branch circuit. [NEC 517.18(A)]","r":"NEC 517.18(A)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An SPD with a short-circuit current rating of 20 kA is proposed for a panelboard where the available fault current is 35 kA. Is the installation permitted?","o":["Yes, if a main breaker is added","Yes, SPD ratings do not matter","Yes, if the SPD is Type 2","No, the SPD may not be installed where the available fault current exceeds its short-circuit current rating"],"a":3,"e":"An SPD must be marked with a short-circuit current rating and may not be installed where the available fault current exceeds that rating. [NEC 242.8]","r":"NEC 242.8"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"NEC Article 600 applies to the installation of conductors and equipment for electric signs and outline lighting. At what voltage level does Article 600 apply?","o":["Only above 1000 volts","Only at 600 volts or less","Only for neon tubing installations","Regardless of voltage"],"a":3,"e":"The scope covers signs, retrofit kits, and outline lighting regardless of voltage, and all installations using neon tubing are covered. It is not limited to high voltage or to neon. [NEC 600.1]","r":"NEC 600.1"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 218.735, after a prime contractor receives payment from the local government, it must pay its subcontractors and suppliers within","o":["7 days of receiving payment from the local government entity","10 days of receiving payment","30 days of receiving payment from the local government entity","3 business days of receiving payment from the local government entity"],"a":1,"e":"The contractor has 10 days after receipt; a subcontractor must pay its lower tiers within 7 days after it receives payment. [F.S. 218.735]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under Rule 12A-1.051, F.A.C., a contractor performing a lump sum real property contract is treated as which party with respect to materials it installs?","o":["A retailer that must collect sales tax from the owner","The ultimate consumer of the materials","An exempt purchaser buying for resale","An agent of the property owner"],"a":1,"e":"Under a lump sum contract the contractor is the ultimate consumer, pays tax to its suppliers, and does not charge the customer sales tax on the materials. [Rule 12A-1.051(3)(a) and (4), F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(3)(a), (4)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of these is normally classified as home-office (general) overhead rather than a direct job cost?","o":["The estimator's salary and the office rent","Wire pulled and terminated on the job","The building permit for the project","Rental of a scissor lift for the job"],"a":0,"e":"Home-office overhead supports the business as a whole and is spread across all jobs. Materials, permits and job equipment are charged directly to the job. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Six current-carrying 12 AWG THHN copper conductors (90°C ampacity 30 A) are installed in one conduit at a 30°C ambient. The derated ampacity is 24 A (80% of 30 A). What is the maximum overcurrent device rating permitted for these conductors under the small-conductor rule?","o":["25 A","15 A","30 A","20 A"],"a":3,"e":"The small-conductor limit applies after correction and adjustment: 12 AWG copper is limited to 20 A protection, even though the derated ampacity is 24 A. [NEC 240.4(D)]","r":"NEC 240.4(D)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which Florida form is the Annual Resale Certificate that registered dealers give suppliers to buy items for resale without paying sales tax?","o":["Form DR-15","Form DR-1","Form RT-6","Form DR-13"],"a":3,"e":"Form DR-13 is the Florida Annual Resale Certificate; DR-15 is the sales and use tax return, DR-1 is the business tax application, and RT-6 is the reemployment tax return. [Florida DOR Sales and Use Tax tutorial]","r":"Florida DOR"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A registered residential alarm system contractor under s. 489.505, F.S., is limited to:","o":["Fire alarm systems in any type of occupancy anywhere in the state of Florida","Burglar and fire alarm systems in commercial buildings in any county","Burglar alarms in single-family, quadruplex, and mobile home occupancies in its jurisdiction","Any alarm system installed in a one-story building within its jurisdiction"],"a":2,"e":"The category is limited to burglar alarm systems in single-family residential, quadruplex and mobile home occupancies, and it may contract only in its registered jurisdiction. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Power-supply conductors and fire alarm circuit conductors may occupy the same raceway only if:","o":["Both are 14 AWG or smaller","The power conductors are run in a separate sleeve of PVC","They are connected to the same equipment","The fire alarm conductors are twisted pair"],"a":2,"e":"Power-supply and fire alarm conductors can share a cable, enclosure, or raceway only where connected to the same equipment. [NEC 760.48(B)]","r":"NEC 2023 760.48(B)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.521(5), F.S., a qualifying individual who stops qualifying a business organization must notify the board and the department within:","o":["30 days","5 days","45 days","90 days"],"a":0,"e":"The individual must notify the board and the department within 30 days, and the organization must notify them of the termination if the individual was its only qualifier. [F.S. 489.521(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.521(5)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A nonexempt employee works 28 hours at one of a company's job sites and 20 hours at another site, in the same workweek, for the same employer. For overtime purposes:","o":["All 48 hours are combined; 8 are overtime hours","Each site is counted separately, so no overtime is owed","Overtime applies only if the sites are under common ownership of a third party","Only the hours at the first site count"],"a":0,"e":"Hours worked for the same employer in a workweek are totaled regardless of location. [29 CFR 778.103]","r":"29 CFR 778.103"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 1 mA, 50 ohm meter movement is to read 100 mA full-scale. What shunt resistance is needed?","o":["5.000 ohms","0.505 ohms","0.500 ohms","50.000 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Shunt current = 99 mA; R = (0.001 x 50) / 0.099 = 0.505 ohms. [Meter ranges]","r":"Meter ranges"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A low-voltage alarm cable is to be run above a suspended ceiling that is part of the return-air plenum, with no raceway. Which choice meets the code?","o":["General-purpose CM or CL2 cable","Riser-rated CMR cable","Limited-use CMX cable","A cable listed and marked for plenum use, such as CMP, CL3P, or FPLP"],"a":3,"e":"Cables in plenums must be listed for plenum use (P suffix) unless installed in a permitted metal raceway. [NEC 725.154, 760.154, 800.113 / 300.22]","r":"NEC 2023 725.154; 300.22(C) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A public construction pay request totals $84,000 for the period. What is the largest retainage a public entity may withhold from that payment under the s. 255.078, F.S., cap?","o":["$8,400","$840","$4,200","$79,800"],"a":2,"e":"5 percent of $84,000 is $4,200. A 10 percent withholding ($8,400) exceeds the cap, and $79,800 is the amount payable after the maximum retainage. [F.S. 255.078(1)]","r":"F.S. 255.078(1)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's current ratio is 1.9 and its quick ratio is 0.9. The gap most likely means","o":["much of current assets is inventory, prepaids, or underbillings","current liabilities are close to zero at year end","long-term debt is excessive compared with equity","the company has no accounts receivable on its books"],"a":0,"e":"The quick ratio removes inventory, prepaids, and underbillings from current assets. A big gap means liquidity depends on those less-liquid items. [Quick ratio]","r":"Quick ratio"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign disconnect is mounted remote from the sign, such as in a building electrical room. What does Article 600 require to help service personnel and first responders find it?","o":["Nothing, because remote disconnects are exempt from marking","The remote disconnect must be accessible and marked as the disconnect for that sign, with a label at the sign location","A sign at the street showing the circuit breaker amperage","A label inside the panel door only, showing the circuit number"],"a":1,"e":"600.6(A)(4) requires a remote disconnect to be mounted at an accessible location available to first responders and service personnel, and its location to be marked with a label at the sign. The informational note says local control is meant for service personnel and first responders. [NEC 600.6(A)]","r":"NEC 600.6(A)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1926.300(a), who is responsible for keeping hand and power tools in safe condition?","o":["Only the tool manufacturer, through its warranty program for the tool","The employer, whether the tools are supplied by the employer or the employee","Only the employee who owns the tool, whether or not it is used on site","The general contractor, but only for tools that the employer has supplied"],"a":1,"e":"Hand and power tools and similar equipment must be maintained in a safe condition whether furnished by the employer or the employee.","r":"29 CFR 1926.300(a)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In Florida, a real property contractor who installs electrical materials into a customer's building generally buys those materials:","o":["Tax-free whenever the building owner is a tax-exempt organization, always","Tax-free using a resale certificate issued by the Department of Revenue","Tax-free if the total cost of the materials is under $5,000","Paying sales tax to the supplier, as the contractor is the consumer of the materials"],"a":3,"e":"Real property contractors are consumers of materials that become part of the improvement; they pay tax at purchase and do not resell. Certain exempt-entity situations still require proper handling. [Fla. Admin. Code Rule 12A-1.051]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051, F.A.C."},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under ADAAG 4.2.5, what is the minimum low forward reach, measured from the floor to the lowest operable part?","o":["18 in.","9 in.","15 in.","12 in."],"a":2,"e":"Forward reach is limited to between 15 in. and 48 in. above the floor. The 9 in. value is the older side-reach low limit under 4.2.6. [ADAAG 4.2.5]","r":"ADAAG 4.2.5"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A conduit run leaves a Class I, Division 2 location at a fuel dispensing facility and goes into an unclassified area. What is required?","o":["Nothing, since the area beyond is unclassified","A union on each side of the boundary","A seal per 501.15 where it leaves the classified area","A drain fitting at the boundary only"],"a":2,"e":"Conduit leaving a classified area must be sealed at the boundary so vapors cannot migrate through the raceway. [NEC 514.9(B)]","r":"NEC 514.9(B)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Electrical contractors in Florida are licensed under which part of chapter 489, Florida Statutes?","o":["Part I, Contracting, for all contractors","Part III, Construction Management","Part IV, Home Inspection Services","Part II, Electrical and Alarm System Contracting"],"a":3,"e":"Part II of chapter 489 (ss. 489.501-489.538) covers electrical and alarm system contracting, administered through the Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board. Part I covers construction contractors such as general, plumbing and air conditioning. [F.S. 489.501-489.538]","r":"F.S. ch. 489, Part II"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Material for Job 214 is mistakenly coded to Job 241. What is the likely effect?","o":["Total company profit changes, but each job's own profit figure is unaffected","Job 214's cost looks too low and Job 241's too high, so both profit figures are wrong","Only the company's year-end tax return is affected, not the individual jobs","Nothing changes in any report, because the total company cost is the same"],"a":1,"e":"Miscoding shifts cost between jobs, overstating one job's profit and understating the other's even though company totals are unchanged; it also corrupts future estimates. [Job costing]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Core (iron) losses in a transformer are","o":["nearly constant from no load to full load","proportional to the square of the load current","zero at no load and increase with the load current","highest at full load and zero at no load"],"a":0,"e":"Core loss depends on applied voltage and frequency, not load current. [Transformer losses]","r":"Transformer losses"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Who may operate a powder-actuated tool on a construction site?","o":["Any person over age 18 who has watched a video about powder tools","Only employees trained in the particular tool in use","Any journeyman electrician, since they are skilled with other tools","Any employee who has signed a waiver of liability for the employer"],"a":1,"e":"Only employees who have been trained in the operation of the particular tool in use may operate a powder-actuated tool. [29 CFR 1926.302(e)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.302(e)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A job has earned revenue to date of $225,000 (cost-to-cost) and billings to date of $190,000. How is the difference reported on the balance sheet?","o":["An underbilling of $35,000, a current asset","Retainage of $35,000, shown in owner's equity","An overbilling of $35,000, a current liability","An underbilling of $35,000, a long-term liability"],"a":0,"e":"Earned $225,000 - billed $190,000 = $35,000 that has been earned but not yet billed, an asset. [WIP schedule]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under NEC 701.12, a legally required standby system must make power available within:","o":["30 seconds","10 seconds","5 minutes","60 seconds"],"a":3,"e":"Legally required standby power must be available within 60 seconds of failure of the normal supply. The faster 10 second requirement is for emergency systems. [NEC 701.12]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 701.12"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Who generally designs the structural support and foundation for a large free-standing sign in Florida?","o":["The sign painter who fabricates the sign face","A Florida-licensed engineer whose signed and sealed drawings support the permit","The electrical inspector who reviews the wiring","The utility company that serves the site"],"a":1,"e":"Structural design of larger sign structures is by a registered design professional using FBC wind provisions. The electrical contractor installs the electrical work and must follow the engineered drawings; changes to the structure need the engineer's approval. [FBC Building ch. 16; F.S. ch. 471]","r":"FBC Building ch. 16; F.S. ch. 471 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier lists a breaker panel at $500 with a chain discount of 20/10. What is the net price?","o":["$400.00","$350.00","$360.00","$405.00"],"a":2,"e":"$500 x 0.80 = $400; then x 0.90 = $360.00. A chain discount of 20/10 is not the same as 30% off ($350.00). [Purchasing math]","r":"Chain discount"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A symbol on sheet E-101 does not match the symbol you normally use for that device, but the project's electrical symbol legend on E-001 defines it. Which governs how the device is bid and installed?","o":["The project symbol legend, because it defines the symbols as used in this set of drawings","The symbol used by most electrical contractors working in the area","The symbol shown in the NEC, because the NEC is the adopted code in the jurisdiction","The symbol shown in the supplier's catalog for the product"],"a":0,"e":"The legend is part of the drawings and tells you what each symbol means on this project. Local habit, catalog symbols, or the NEC do not override it. [Drawing practice: symbol legend]","r":"Drawing practice: symbol legend (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Replacing forty 60 W incandescent lamps with 9 W LED lamps that burn 3,000 hours per year saves how much per year at $0.13 per kWh?","o":["$936.00","$795.60","$140.40","$795,600.00"],"a":1,"e":"Saved kW = 40 x 51 / 1,000 = 2.04; kWh = 2.04 x 3,000 = 6,120; savings = 6,120 x 0.13 = $795.60. [Energy cost]","r":"Energy cost"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.129(2), when a qualifying agent commits a violation on a business organization's project, the board may fine the organization up to","o":["$500 per violation","$5,000 per violation","$1,000 per violation","$25,000 per violation"],"a":1,"e":"The board may fine the organization, or an officer who participated or knew, up to $5,000 per violation. [F.S. 489.129(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(2)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under Rule 61G4-19.001, F.A.C., the citation fine for an advertisement that omits the Part I licensee's license number is:","o":["$100","$50","$250","$1,000"],"a":0,"e":"The citation schedule lists $100 for an advertisement missing the license number, the same amount as late permits and name violations. [F.A.C. 61G4-19.001]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-19.001"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How many watts are dissipated as heat in a 3.5 ohm resistance carrying 20 A?","o":["245 W","1,400 W","70 W","114 W"],"a":1,"e":"P = I squared x R = 20 x 20 x 3.5 = 1400 W. Using I x R gives volts (70). [Power formula]","r":"Power formula"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For a local government's public construction project, F.S. 255.20 requires competitive award when the estimated cost exceeds what base dollar amount (before the statute's inflation adjustment)?","o":["$50,000","$100,000","$300,000","$500,000"],"a":2,"e":"Section 255.20(1) sets the base threshold at $300,000 for construction, adjusted by the ENR Building Cost Index from January 1, 2009. $75,000 is the base for electrical work. [F.S. 255.20(1)]","r":"F.S. 255.20(1)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A state agency awards a $90,000 construction contract. Under F.S. 255.05, a payment and performance bond is","o":["required at 50 percent of the contract price","required only if the contractor is licensed in another state","not required, because state contracts of $100,000 or less are exempt","required unless the contractor pays cash"],"a":2,"e":"The statute exempts state contracts of $100,000 or less from the bond requirement. [F.S. 255.05(1)(d)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)(d)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"While working near exposed energized parts, which item is prohibited unless made nonconductive?","o":["Cotton work shirts with the sleeves rolled down","Leather work gloves rated for the voltage","Safety-toe boots with no metal shank or plate","Conductive jewelry or clothing that might contact the energized parts"],"a":3,"e":"1910.333(c)(8) prohibits wearing conductive articles of jewelry and clothing (metal watch bands, metal-threaded clothing) where they might contact energized parts, unless made nonconductive. [29 CFR 1910.333(c)(8)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(8)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What length of flexible cord is permitted for a cord-and-plug-connected kitchen waste disposer?","o":["18 to 36 in.","3 to 4 ft","6 to 8 ft","12 to 24 in."],"a":0,"e":"A garbage disposer cord must be 18 in. to 36 in. long; a dishwasher or trash compactor cord is 36 in. to 48 in. [NEC 422.16(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 422.16(B)(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What acclimatization schedule does OSHA recommend for a new worker starting work in the heat?","o":["50 percent of the usual workload on day one, rising by 10 percent daily","20 percent of the usual workload on day one, rising by no more than 20 percent daily","100 percent of the usual workload from day one with extra water breaks","10 percent of the usual workload on day one, rising by 5 percent daily"],"a":1,"e":"The recommended schedule for new workers is 20 percent on the first day, increasing by no more than 20 percent each day.","r":"OSHA heat illness fact sheet"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Where is a warning line installed on a low-slope roof when no mechanical equipment is being used?","o":["At least 6 feet from the roof edge, between 34 and 39 inches high","At least 2 feet from the roof edge, 24 inches high","At least 15 feet from the roof edge, 48 inches high","At the roof edge, 42 inches high"],"a":0,"e":"Warning lines must be erected at least 6 feet from the roof edge (when no mechanical equipment is used) and have a top height between 34 and 39 inches. [29 CFR 1926.502(f)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.502(f)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under the FLSA, nonexempt employees must be paid overtime at what minimum rate for hours worked over 40 in a workweek?","o":["One and one-half times the minimum wage","Two times the regular rate of pay","One and one-half times the regular rate of pay","One and one-quarter times the regular rate of pay"],"a":2,"e":"Overtime is 1.5 times the regular rate, not the minimum wage, for each hour over 40 in a workweek. [FLSA Sec. 7(a)]","r":"FLSA Sec. 7(a)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A straight run of exposed PVC conduit is 140 ft long between securely mounted boxes. The temperature is expected to change by 45°F. Table 352.44 gives 1.83 in. per 100 ft for a 45°F change. What is the expected length change, and is an expansion fitting required?","o":["About 1.83 in.; none is required","About 0.26 in.; none is required","About 2.56 in.; an expansion fitting is required","About 2.56 in.; none is required"],"a":2,"e":"1.83 in. per 100 ft x 1.4 = 2.56 in., which is well over the 1/4 in. threshold, so an expansion fitting is required. [NEC 352.44, Table 352.44]","r":"NEC 352.44, Table 352.44"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The wind force on the sign is 6,324 lb acting at the sign centroid 15 ft above the ground. What overturning moment must the foundation resist at the ground line?","o":["6,324 ft-lb","422 ft-lb","94,860 ft-lb","47,430 ft-lb"],"a":2,"e":"Moment = force x lever arm = 6,324 x 15 = 94,860 ft-lb. Dividing by the height or using half of it gives the wrong answer. [Statics / ASCE 7 sign loading]","r":"Statics / ASCE 7 sign loading (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a fire alarm system, closing a sprinkler control valve (tamper switch) is reported as a:","o":["supervisory signal","trouble signal","emergency voice signal","fire alarm signal"],"a":0,"e":"Valve tamper switches report supervisory signals; waterflow switches report alarms, and open or ground faults report trouble. Mixing these up misdirects the response. [NFPA 72, 3.3 / Ch. 23]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 23 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor front-loads its schedule of values by assigning too much value to mobilization. A reviewer would most likely see this as","o":["a benefit to the surety because job costs are lower","proof that the construction schedule is accurate","a risk to the owner, since billings run ahead of actual progress","something the critical path method requires of every job"],"a":2,"e":"Front-loading shifts cash forward and exposes the owner (and surety) if the contractor defaults before the work catches up. [Pay application practice]","r":"SOV vs schedule (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An electrical contractor from another state applies for Florida certification by endorsement under s. 489.511(5), F.S. Which condition applies if the applicant relies on holding a license for the required period?","o":["A license held at least 10 years; apply while active or within 2 years of lapse","A license held at least 2 years; apply within 10 years after it lapsed","A license held at least 5 years; there is no deadline to apply","A license held at least 20 years in a single state only"],"a":0,"e":"Endorsement under (5)(c) requires at least 10 years of valid licensure, with application while active or within 2 years after last active, and electrical and alarm contractors must complete a 2-hour Florida Building Code course. [F.S. 489.511(5)(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.511(5)(c)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A vendor issues a credit memo for returned goods. The credit memo:","o":["Increases the amount the buyer owes","Reduces the amount the buyer owes","Cancels the purchase order","Replaces the receiving report"],"a":1,"e":"A credit memo from the vendor reduces the buyer's accounts payable. [Accounts payable practice]","r":"Credit memo"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For public bonds issued on or after October 1, 2012, a bond term that does which of the following is unenforceable under F.S. 255.05(1)(e)?","o":["Names the owner as the obligee","States the penal sum","Lists the bond number on the front page","Restricts who is protected by the bond"],"a":3,"e":"The statute makes unenforceable bond provisions that restrict who is protected, restrict venue, change duration, or add conditions precedent beyond the statute. [F.S. 255.05(1)(e)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)(e)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On a smooth ceiling, the maximum listed spacing commonly used between spot-type smoke detectors is:","o":["15 ft (4.6 m)","50 ft (15.2 m)","30 ft (9.1 m)","60 ft (18.3 m)"],"a":2,"e":"NFPA 72 uses 30 ft as the default spot-type smoke detector spacing on smooth ceilings unless the listing or engineering design allows otherwise. 50 ft is a typical heat detector listed spacing. [NFPA 72 (2019) 17.7.3.2.3]","r":"NFPA 72 17.7.3.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Between what dates must the OSHA Form 300A annual summary be posted?","o":["February 1 through April 30 of the following year","January 1 through March 31 of the following calendar year","April 1 through June 30 of the following calendar year","Throughout the entire year in which the injuries occurred"],"a":0,"e":"The summary must be posted by February 1 of the year following the covered year and kept in place until April 30. [29 CFR 1904.32(b)(6)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.32(b)(6)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Electrical contractor certificates and registrations renew every two years by which date, according to the Department of Business and Professional Regulation?","o":["August 31 of every even-numbered year","June 30 of every odd-numbered year","December 31 of every year","The anniversary of the exam date"],"a":0,"e":"DBPR's electrical contractor FAQ states that certified and registered licenses renew by August 31 of every even-numbered year; inactive licenses renew on the same date. [DBPR Electrical Contractors FAQ; F.S. 489.517]","r":"DBPR FAQ; F.S. 489.517"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.131(10), F.S., after July 1, 1993 a local government may license contractors only if it:","o":["Charges a license fee of at least $500 per contractor","Requires a written examination for every single applicant","Exercises disciplinary control and forwards recommended orders to the board","Has a population of more than 100,000 residents"],"a":2,"e":"The statute requires disciplinary control with recommended orders forwarded to the board, and local boards need at least two consumer representatives (three if seven or more members). [F.S. 489.131(10)]","r":"F.S. 489.131(10)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What two tests must be performed on cord sets, receptacles and cord-and-plug equipment under the assured equipment grounding conductor program?","o":["An insulation resistance test at 500 volts and a polarity test of the neutral","A ground-fault trip-current test and a voltage-drop test under rated load","A continuity test of the grounding conductor and a terminal connection test","A megohm test of the cord jacket and a temperature rise test of the plug"],"a":2,"e":"The program requires a continuity test and a terminal connection test; the other tests listed are not required by the standard. [29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(D)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(D)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What reading does a clamp-on ammeter show if both the hot and neutral conductors of a cord are enclosed in the jaws?","o":["Double the load current, since both currents are added together","About zero, since the opposing magnetic fields cancel","The full load current, as it is measured through either conductor","The voltage of the circuit, expressed on the amperes scale"],"a":1,"e":"A clamp meter measures the net current; equal and opposite currents in two conductors cancel, so one conductor must be clamped alone.","r":"Clamp meter operation (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor must display the OSHA 'Job Safety and Health: It's the Law' poster:","o":["Only in the main office, and never at a job site","In a conspicuous place where notices to employees are customarily posted","Only if the contractor has 11 or more employees on the payroll","In the contractor's vehicle glove box, in case an inspector asks"],"a":1,"e":"Every covered employer must post the OSHA poster where employees can see it; at job sites with no fixed location, it is kept where employees can obtain access. [29 CFR 1903.2]","r":"29 CFR 1903.2"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Resource (manpower) leveling is used to","o":["remove the milestones from the project schedule","shorten every activity by adding more workers","raise the hourly pay rate paid to the crew","smooth labor demand by shifting non-critical work within its float"],"a":3,"e":"Leveling delays non-critical work within float so the crew size stays steady, usually without delaying completion. [Resource leveling]","r":"Manpower leveling (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A $600,000 contract has $225,000 of cost to date. The estimated total cost rises from $450,000 to $500,000. By how much does earned revenue to date (cost-to-cost) change?","o":["It is unchanged at $300,000 because the price is fixed","It falls from $300,000 to $270,000","It rises from $300,000 to $330,000 as costs increase","It falls by $50,000 to $250,000 because of added cost"],"a":1,"e":"Before: $225,000/$450,000 = 50% x $600,000 = $300,000. After: $225,000/$500,000 = 45% x $600,000 = $270,000. A higher cost estimate lowers percent complete and earned revenue (profit fade). [26 CFR 1.460-4]","r":"26 CFR 1.460-4"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The IRS groups the common-law factors for deciding whether a worker is an employee or an independent contractor into which three categories?","o":["Behavioral control, financial control, and relationship type","Wages, hours worked, and the job location","License, insurance coverage, and bond amount","Training, tools used, and pay frequency"],"a":0,"e":"The three categories are behavioral control, financial control, and the relationship of the parties. [IRS Topic 762; IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27]","r":"IRS Topic 762"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A service consists of two sets of 250 kcmil copper conductors per phase in parallel. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor under Table 250.66?","o":["2 AWG","2/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"For parallel conductors the equivalent size is the sum of the areas: 2 x 250 = 500 kcmil, which falls in the 'over 350 through 600 kcmil' row, requiring 1/0 AWG copper. [NEC 250.66, Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250.66, Table 250.66"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On a project under F.S. 218.735, a general contractor receives retainage for its subcontractor's work. What must the contractor do?","o":["Hold it until the whole project is accepted, regardless of the subcontractor's work","Apply it to the general contractor's own overhead","Promptly pass the retainage on to the subcontractor","Deposit it in escrow for 90 days"],"a":2,"e":"218.735 requires that retainage paid for subcontractor or supplier work be promptly passed on. [F.S. 218.735 (retainage)]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Sign equipment located above areas accessible to vehicles must be at least this high unless protected from physical damage:","o":["8 ft","14 ft (4.3 m)","10 ft","12 ft"],"a":1,"e":"600.9(A) requires sign or outline lighting equipment to be at least 4.3 m (14 ft) above areas accessible to vehicles unless protected from physical damage. [NEC 600.9(A)]","r":"NEC 600.9(A)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supervisor tells an employee that a promotion depends on dating him. This is an example of:","o":["Disparate impact discrimination based on a neutral policy","Constructive discharge based on a hostile work environment","Quid pro quo sexual harassment under Title VII","Permissible mentoring that is not covered by Title VII"],"a":2,"e":"When a tangible employment benefit depends on submission to sexual conduct, the employer is generally strictly liable. [29 CFR 1604.11(a)]","r":"29 CFR 1604.11"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What is the main purpose of a bid bond?","o":["To guarantee that every subcontractor will be paid in full","To cover injuries to workers during construction","To insure the owner's building against fire damage","To assure the owner that the successful bidder will sign the contract and furnish the required bonds"],"a":3,"e":"If the low bidder refuses to enter the contract, the surety pays the owner the cost difference up to the bond's penal amount. Payment bonds, not bid bonds, protect subs and suppliers. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Each area of rescue assistance space under ADAAG 4.3.11 must have what minimum clear floor space?","o":["36 in. by 60 in.","24 in. by 36 in.","48 in. by 48 in.","30 in. by 48 in."],"a":3,"e":"Each rescue space is the same size as a wheelchair clear floor space, 30 in. by 48 in. The stair next to it needs at least 48 in. clear between handrails. [ADAAG 4.3.11]","r":"ADAAG 4.3.11"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A line isolation monitor in an isolated power system serving wet procedure locations should alarm when the total hazard current exceeds:","o":["10 mA","2 mA","5 mA","30 mA"],"a":2,"e":"The LIM must not alarm for hazard current of 5 mA or less and must alarm when it exceeds 5 mA. [NEC 517.160(B)]","r":"NEC 517.160(B) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.503(2), F.S., authorized employees of a county or municipality may do routine maintenance, repair or construction on existing installations up to what cost without a license?","o":["$2,500, provided they do not hold out for hire","$200,000, provided they do not hold out for hire","$5,000, provided they do not hold out for hire","$75,000, provided they do not hold out for hire"],"a":1,"e":"Government employees are exempt for work not exceeding $200,000 on existing installations; school boards, state universities and community college boards are excluded from the exemption and may not split projects over $200,000. [F.S. 489.503(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.503(2)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The three documents compared in a three-way match before paying a vendor invoice are:","o":["Receiving report, packing slip, and bill of lading only","Purchase order, job estimate, and lien waiver","Vendor invoice, bank statement, and payroll register","Purchase order, receiving report, and vendor invoice"],"a":3,"e":"Three-way match confirms that what was ordered (PO), what arrived (receiving report) and what is billed (invoice) agree in quantity and price. [Purchasing control practice]","r":"Three-way match"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Liquidated damages in a construction contract are:","o":["A fee the owner's lender charges at closing for the loan","A pre-agreed sum, often per day, owed if completion is late","The cost of correcting defective work after final completion","Interest charged on unpaid progress payments to subcontractors"],"a":1,"e":"Liquidated damages fix in advance the compensation for a specific breach, commonly delay, so actual damages need not be proved. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"With a CPI of 0.80 and a total budget at completion of $600,000, a common forecast of the estimate at completion (BAC / CPI) is","o":["$750,000","$480,000","$630,000","$600,000"],"a":0,"e":"EAC = 600,000/0.80 = 750,000 if the current cost efficiency continues. Adding only the past overrun gives 630,000. [Variance analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Using 365 days, the approximate annualized cost of skipping a 1/10, net 30 discount is:","o":["11.0 percent","1.0 percent","36.9 percent","18.4 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Cost = (1/99) x (365/20) = 0.1843, about 18.4%. [Terms analysis]","r":"Cash discount analysis"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum thickness of sealing compound in a Class I seal fitting?","o":["Not less than the seal trade size and never under 5/8 in.","1/4 in. in all cases","Not less than 2 in. in all cases","Half the conduit trade size, any depth"],"a":0,"e":"The thickness of the compound must be at least equal to the trade size of the sealing fitting and never less than 16 mm (5/8 in.). [NEC 501.15(C)]","r":"NEC 501.15(C)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A crane has motors whose combined short-time ratings in operation together total 120 amperes. Using the 50% rule, the minimum continuous rating of the disconnecting means is:","o":["60 A","120 A","90 A","30 A"],"a":0,"e":"The disconnect must have a continuous rating of at least 50% of the combined short-time ratings of motors that can operate together: 0.5 x 120 = 60 A. [NEC 610.33]","r":"NEC 610.33 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a network-powered broadband communications system, what are the two source classifications by power level?","o":["Class 1 and Class 2","PLFA and NPLFA","Limited and unlimited","Low power and medium power"],"a":3,"e":"Table 830.15 classifies sources as low power (0-100 V) or medium power (0-150 V). [NEC 830.15]","r":"NEC 2023 830.15"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A branch circuit carries 7.5 A at 120 V to a purely resistive load. What is the load resistance?","o":["0.062 ohms","16 ohms","32 ohms","900 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"R = E / I = 120 / 7.5 = 16 ohms. 900 is E x I, which is watts, not ohms. [Ohm's law]","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"How long must an employer retain the OSHA 300 Log, the privacy case list, the annual summary and the 301 incident reports?","o":["5 years following the end of the calendar year they cover","1 year following the end of the calendar year they cover","30 years, because exposure records are involved","3 years from the date each injury occurred"],"a":0,"e":"Records are kept five years following the end of the calendar year that they cover and updated for the first five years if circumstances change. [29 CFR 1904.33(a)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.33(a)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A university weather-delay policy lists normal baseline weather days per month, and only weather days above the baseline qualify for a contract time extension. If April's baseline is 7 days and the contractor documents 11 weather days that stopped critical work in April, how many days can be claimed?","o":["18 days","4 days","11 days","7 days"],"a":1,"e":"Claimable days = documented days - baseline = 11 - 7 = 4. Baseline days are part of the work and are not eligible for extension. [TBR weather delay policy 01 26 20]","r":"Weather days"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 713.13, a recorded notice of commencement becomes void if the improvement does not actually commence within how many days after recording?","o":["30 days","45 days","90 days","1 year"],"a":2,"e":"The notice is void if work does not start within 90 days after recording; otherwise it expires 1 year after recording unless it states a later date. [F.S. 713.13]","r":"F.S. 713.13"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Four emergency lighting readings taken at floor level along a corridor immediately after a loss of power are 1.5, 1.1, 0.8, and 0.4 fc. Under NFPA 101, this path:","o":["fails, because the average of 0.95 fc is below the 1 fc minimum average","fails, because the lowest reading is under the 0.6 fc average","passes, because the max-to-min ratio is well under 40 to 1","passes, because every single reading is above the 0.1 fc minimum"],"a":0,"e":"(1.5 + 1.1 + 0.8 + 0.4) / 4 = 0.95 fc, which is below the 1 fc initial average. The minimum point (0.4 fc) and 3.75 to 1 ratio would pass. [NFPA 101, 7.9.2.1]","r":"NFPA 101 7.9.2.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On a cost-plus contract with a fee of 12% of cost, documented costs total $310,000. What is the total owed to the contractor?","o":["$37,200","$352,272.73","$384,400","$347,200"],"a":3,"e":"Fee = 310,000 x 0.12 = $37,200; total = $347,200. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A fire alarm riser shows a Class A signaling line circuit. Compared to a Class B circuit, a Class A circuit differs because it provides:","o":["A redundant return path, so devices still work after a single open fault","Wireless devices only, so no return path is needed at all","A requirement for use in every building, under the code","No supervision of the circuit conductors, unlike Class B"],"a":0,"e":"Class A pathways use a return path to the panel so a single break does not cause loss of devices downstream, whereas a Class B circuit loses devices beyond the open. [NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 12]","r":"NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 12 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The unit of magnetic flux density is the","o":["henry","weber","ampere-turn","tesla"],"a":3,"e":"Flux density B is measured in tesla (webers per square meter). The weber is total flux. [Magnetism]","r":"Magnetism"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What is the general minimum age for employment in nonagricultural, nonhazardous jobs under the FLSA, with limited exceptions?","o":["18","16","12","14"],"a":3,"e":"Fourteen is the minimum age for most non-farm work, with hour limits for 14- and 15-year-olds. [DOL Fact Sheet 43]","r":"29 CFR 570.2"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Large underbillings on a WIP schedule most often prompt a surety to ask","o":["Whether the contractor pays subs earlier than required","Whether the bond premium rate is set too high","Whether billing is late or contract values or costs are misstated","Whether the contractor is holding too much cash on hand"],"a":2,"e":"Underbillings can reflect timing under the billing terms, but they can also point to billing delays or errors in the total contract value. [WIP schedule; Withum]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which period must the OSHA Form 300A annual summary be posted in a visible workplace location?","o":["March 1 through May 31 of the year after the covered year","January 1 through March 31 of the year after the covered year","February 1 through April 30 of the year after the covered year","All year, in the same place as the OSHA safety poster"],"a":2,"e":"The summary must be posted no later than February 1 and kept in place until April 30. [29 CFR 1904.32(b)(6)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.32(b)(6)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's overhead is 18% of revenue and the desired net profit is 7% of revenue. What gross margin on price is required to cover both?","o":["18%","7%","25%","11%"],"a":2,"e":"Gross margin must cover overhead plus profit: 18% + 7% = 25% of revenue (a markup of 33.3% on direct cost). [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Is selective coordination of overcurrent devices required for optional standby systems under Article 702?","o":["Yes, in every installation","No, Article 702 has no such requirement","Yes, above 50 kW","Only at hospitals"],"a":1,"e":"Selective coordination is required for emergency (700.32) and legally required standby (701.32) but not for optional standby. [NEC Art. 702]","r":"NEC Art. 702"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A factor advances 85% of a $50,000 invoice and charges a 3% fee on the face amount. When the customer pays the factor in full, how much additional cash does the contractor receive (the rebate)?","o":["$7,500","$48,500","$6,000","$1,500"],"a":2,"e":"Advance 42,500; fee 1,500; rebate = 50,000 - 1,500 - 42,500 = 6,000. The reserve is 15% (7,500) but the fee comes out of it. [Factoring]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On a statement of cash flows, the purchase of a new service truck is reported in which section?","o":["Financing activities","Operating activities","Investing activities","Owner's equity section"],"a":2,"e":"Purchases and sales of long-term assets such as vehicles and equipment are investing activities. [Statement of cash flows, Rule 14-22.002 components]","r":"Statement of cash flows"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Where ground-fault protection of equipment is required at a hospital service or feeder under 230.95 or 215.10, what additional protection is required?","o":["None, because the main device covers it","GFCI protection on every branch circuit","A transfer switch with a ground-fault trip","A second step of ground-fault protection downstream, 6 cycles apart"],"a":3,"e":"Hospitals need two levels of GFPE, with the second step on the next level of feeder disconnecting means downstream, with a time separation of at least 6 cycles between curves. [NEC 517.17(A) and (B)]","r":"NEC 517.17(A) and (B)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In a design-build delivery method, the owner contracts with","o":["a single entity responsible for both design and construction","separate firms for design and construction, with no common contract","only the architect, who then hires the owner's inspectors","the electrical inspector for both permit and design"],"a":0,"e":"Design-build places design and construction responsibility under one contract. Design-bid-build uses separate contracts. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What does NEC 110.26(E) require of the dedicated electrical space above a panelboard installed indoors?","o":["Any plumbing may be run through the space above the equipment","The space extends 1 foot above the equipment and no more","The dedicated space is not required for equipment installed indoors","6 feet above, or to the ceiling, with no foreign piping or ducts"],"a":3,"e":"Indoor dedicated electrical space is equal to the width and depth of the equipment from floor to a height of 6 ft above the equipment or the structural ceiling, whichever is lower.","r":"NEC 110.26(E)(1) (2023) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 2-ft section of flexible metal conduit (FMC) is installed to a motor on a 20-A circuit. Under 250.118, FMC may serve as the equipment grounding conductor if:","o":["A bonding screw is not used","The FMC is over 1-1/4 in. trade size on a 30 A circuit","The length exceeds 6 ft and the circuit is 60 A","The length in the ground-return path does not exceed 6 ft and the circuit conductors are protected at 20 A or less"],"a":3,"e":"Listed FMC may be used as an EGC if the total length is 6 ft or less, the conductors are protected at 20 A or less, and terminated with listed fittings. [NEC 250.118(A)(5)]","r":"NEC 250.118(A)(5)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which of the following loads is most appropriately classified as a legally required standby load under NEC Article 701?","o":["A residential chest freezer in a garage","Exit sign illumination required in a large theater","Walk-in refrigeration in a restaurant kitchen","Smoke removal ventilation required by a governmental agency"],"a":3,"e":"Legally required standby systems serve loads like smoke removal, communications, and processes whose loss could create hazards, as mandated by authorities. Exit signs are Article 700 emergency loads; restaurant refrigeration is optional. [NEC 701.2]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 701.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A trencher costs $52,000, has a salvage value of $12,000 and a 5-year life. Using straight-line, annual depreciation is","o":["$10,400","$8,000","$2,400","$12,800"],"a":1,"e":"(52,000 - 12,000) / 5 = 8,000. Ignoring salvage gives 10,400. [Equipment cost recovery]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An accounts receivable aging report is mainly used to","o":["determine the license renewal fee","set the owner's salary for the year","compute annual straight-line depreciation","see how long customer invoices have been unpaid"],"a":3,"e":"Aging groups unpaid invoices by how long they have been outstanding so collection and bad-debt risk can be assessed. [AR aging]","r":"AR aging (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 4-wire, 3-phase delta service has the midpoint of one phase grounded to supply 120 V loads. How must the conductor having the higher phase-to-ground voltage (208 V) be marked?","o":["With a white stripe on the conductor","By an outer finish that is orange, or other effective means, at any point where a connection is made if the grounded conductor is also present","By a red outer finish only on the service drop","It needs no marking if the panel is labeled 120/240 V"],"a":1,"e":"On a 4-wire delta where the midpoint of one phase is grounded, the phase conductor with the higher voltage to ground (the high leg) is identified by an orange finish or other effective means. [NEC 230.56]","r":"NEC 230.56"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor's inland marine (equipment floater) policy would most likely cover","o":["claims by neighbors for property damage","tools and equipment while moving between job sites","wages of workers who are injured","the structure while it is under construction"],"a":1,"e":"Inland marine coverage insures movable property such as tools, equipment and materials in transit. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A rectangular smooth-ceiling room measures 50 ft by 80 ft. Using a rectangular layout at the 30 ft maximum spacing, what is the minimum number of spot-type smoke detectors?","o":["6","4","8","12"],"a":0,"e":"50 ft needs 2 rows (50/30 rounds up to 2) and 80 ft needs 3 columns (80/30 rounds up to 3): 2 x 3 = 6, giving spacing near 25 ft by 27 ft with walls under 15 ft. [NFPA 72 (2019) 17.7.3.2.3]","r":"NFPA 72 17.7.3.2.3 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What cord length is permitted for a cord-and-plug-connected built-in dishwasher?","o":["18 in. to 36 in.","3 ft to 4 ft (36 to 48 in.)","6 ft to 10 ft","Up to 15 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Built-in dishwashers and trash compactors may use a 3-ft to 4-ft cord, listed for the purpose, secured to prevent damage. Disposers are limited to 18-36 in. [NEC 422.16(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 422.16(B)(2)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"ADAAG 4.1.3(14) says that where emergency warning systems are provided, they must include:","o":["both audible and visual alarms complying with 4.28","audible alarms with a 75 dBA minimum at every door","audible alarms only, with visual alarms optional","visual alarms only in corridors"],"a":0,"e":"Where an emergency warning system is required or provided, it must have both audible and visual alarms meeting 4.28. An audible-only system leaves occupants who are deaf or hard of hearing unwarned. [ADAAG 4.1.3(14)]","r":"ADAAG 4.1.3(14)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"In a valid (3)(d) retail sale plus installation contract, on what amount does the contractor collect Florida sales tax from the customer?","o":["The sales price of the materials, not the installation charge","The total contract price including installation","Only the installation labor","Nothing; the customer pays use tax"],"a":0,"e":"In a qualifying (3)(d) contract the contractor acts as a dealer selling materials, collects tax on the materials price, and does not tax installation. [Rule 12A-1.051(3)(d), F.A.C.; F.S. 212.06(14)]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(3)(d); F.S. 212.06(14)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The final electrical inspection fails because of a missing label and the re-inspection is available only after 3 working days. This delay is most likely","o":["a weather day that earns a time extension for the work","non-excusable, because the failed item was the contractor's","an owner-directed change to the original scope of work","excusable and compensable under the usual contract terms"],"a":1,"e":"A failure caused by the contractor's own deficiency is a non-excusable delay with no time or money relief. [Delay classification]","r":"Coordination with inspections"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Using inventory turnover of 5.25, about how many days of inventory does the company hold (365-day year, rounded to the nearest day)?","o":["1916 days","52 days","70 days","35 days"],"a":2,"e":"Days = 365 / 5.25 = 69.5, about 70 days. [Inventory management]","r":"Days in inventory"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A fire alarm detection zone is generally limited to a maximum floor area of about:","o":["22,500 sq ft, with no greater than 300 ft length in any direction","5,000 sq ft","100,000 sq ft","1,000 sq ft"],"a":0,"e":"Zones are limited so responders can locate the fire; 22,500 sq ft and 300 ft in any direction are the typical limits (larger in fully sprinklered buildings under certain conditions). [NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 23]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 23 zoning (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which GHS pictogram is used for skin corrosion/burns and serious eye damage?","o":["Exclamation mark (an irritant or less severe toxicity)","Health hazard (a human silhouette with a starburst)","Skull and crossbones (acute toxicity with fatal effects)","Corrosion (liquid dripping onto a hand and a surface)"],"a":3,"e":"The corrosion pictogram applies to skin corrosion, serious eye damage and corrosive to metals; the exclamation mark is for irritation and less severe toxicity.","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200 App. C"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A balanced three-phase 480 V load draws 10 kW at 0.80 lagging power factor. What is the line current?","o":["26.04 A","12.03 A","15.04 A","9.62 A"],"a":2,"e":"I = P / (1.732 x E x PF) = 10,000 / (1.732 x 480 x 0.8) = 15.04 A. Leaving out PF gives 12.03 A. [Power factor]","r":"Power factor"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A purchasing authority limit requires a second approval on any PO over a set dollar amount. The main purpose is to:","o":["Speed up payment to suppliers by skipping the invoice review","Reduce the amount of sales tax the contractor owes on materials","Limit the risk of unauthorized or excessive commitments","Increase the early-payment discounts that vendors offer the buyer"],"a":2,"e":"Approval thresholds scale review to risk and prevent a single employee from committing large sums alone. [Purchasing control practice]","r":"Approval limits"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.117(1)(c), F.S., a registrant must report each local license or certificate of competency to the board within:","o":["30 days after obtaining it","5 days","90 days","1 year"],"a":0,"e":"The registrant must report within 30 days of obtaining it. Local jurisdictions also report licensure and discipline information to the board within 30 days. [F.S. 489.117(1)(c), (4)(b)]","r":"F.S. 489.117(1)(c)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 20 hp motor, 90 percent efficient, runs 2,000 hours a year at $0.11 per kWh. Approximate annual energy cost?","o":["$4,400","$4,012","$3,647","$3,282"],"a":2,"e":"Input kW = 20 x 0.746 / 0.90 = 16.58; kWh = 33,156; cost = 33,156 x 0.11 = $3,647. [Energy cost]","r":"Energy cost"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor paid benefits for an employee of an uninsured subcontractor. Under F.S. 440.10(1)(d), the contractor may recover those benefits plus interest from the subcontractor unless","o":["the parties agreed in writing that the contractor would provide coverage","the subcontractor earned less than the minimum wage","the claim was filed on a Friday or a holiday","the project was a private home rather than a commercial building"],"a":0,"e":"The right of recovery does not apply when the parties agreed in writing that the contractor will provide the coverage. [F.S. 440.10(1)(d)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(d)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A ceiling-mounted spot-type smoke detector must be located at least how far from an air supply diffuser?","o":["1 ft (0.3 m)","6 ft (1.8 m)","3 ft (0.9 m)","10 ft (3 m)"],"a":2,"e":"Detectors are placed at least 3 ft from supply diffusers because the air stream can dilute or blow smoke away from the detector. [NFPA 72 (2019) 17.7.1.9]","r":"NFPA 72 17.7.1.9 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Required exit signs must be:","o":["illuminated only when the fire alarm sounds in the building","powered only by a GFCI-protected general branch circuit","continuously illuminated and connected to an emergency power source","controlled by a switch in the main lobby with a key lock"],"a":2,"e":"Exit signs must be continuously illuminated, and a failure of the normal supply must bring in the emergency source. An accessible switch would allow signs to be shut off. [NFPA 101, 7.10.6.3]","r":"NFPA 101 7.10.6 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"If the voltage applied to an induction motor drops by 10 percent, the available torque drops to about","o":["73 percent of normal","100 percent of normal","90 percent of normal","81 percent of normal"],"a":3,"e":"Torque varies with the square of the voltage: 0.9 squared = 0.81. [Motor torque]","r":"Motor torque"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An owner-directed change that adds work on the critical path and extends completion would usually be","o":["a concurrent delay by default","an excusable and compensable delay","a non-excusable delay","an excusable but non-compensable delay"],"a":1,"e":"Owner-caused delays (changes, differing site conditions, design revisions) typically give the contractor both time and, if contract terms allow, money. [Delay classification]","r":"Delay claims"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An underwriter uses a rule of thumb that the largest single bonded job is 10 times working capital. A contractor has working capital of $350,000. What is the estimated single-job limit?","o":["$7,000,000","$350,000","$3,500,000","$525,000"],"a":2,"e":"350,000 x 10 = $3,500,000. The aggregate program is often a higher multiple (such as 20 times), not the single-job limit. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A Class 1 remote-control or signaling circuit that is not power limited may operate at a maximum of:","o":["30 volts, nominal","150 volts, nominal","600 volts, nominal","1000 volts, nominal"],"a":2,"e":"Class 1 remote-control and signaling circuits are not power limited but are restricted to 600 V nominal. 30 V applies only to power-limited Class 1. [NEC 725.41(B)]","r":"NEC 2023 725.41(B) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.503(4), F.S., a public utility is exempt from electrical contractor licensure when:","o":["It hires unlicensed subcontractors to wire homes for paying customers","It builds houses for sale to the general public","It uses its own forces for construction and maintenance incidental to its business","It installs customer wiring behind the meter for a fee"],"a":2,"e":"The exemption covers public utilities performing construction, maintenance and development work with their own employees incidental to the utility's business. [F.S. 489.503(4)]","r":"F.S. 489.503(4)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has $240,000 in accounts receivable and billed $600,000 over the last 90 days. What is days sales outstanding (DSO) using a 90-day period?","o":["225 days","36 days","12 days","146 days"],"a":1,"e":"DSO = AR / sales x days in period = 240,000 / 600,000 x 90 = 36 days. Inverting the ratio or using the wrong period gives the other results. [Collections metrics]","r":"Accounts receivable management (SBA/IRS Pub 334 context)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Consigned materials that a supplier leaves at the contractor's shop but still owns are generally:","o":["Included in inventory at the supplier's published list price","Excluded from the contractor's inventory until the contractor buys them","Included in inventory at zero cost until the first job uses them","Treated as a loan from the supplier that must be repaid in cash"],"a":1,"e":"The contractor does not own consigned goods, so they are not inventory and not a liability until sold or used. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A small electrical contractor pays one apprentice $52,000 in 2026 wages, all subject to FICA. What is the employer's share of FICA tax on those wages?","o":["$3,978.00","$7,956.00","$3,224.00","$754.00"],"a":0,"e":"The employer pays 7.65% (6.2% + 1.45%): $52,000 x 0.0765 = $3,978.00. $7,956.00 is the employee and employer shares combined. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A change order adds a pile of materials that were not itemized and priced in the original (3)(d) contract. What is the likely result?","o":["The contract may no longer qualify as a (3)(d) contract","The original contract is unaffected","The change order becomes tax-exempt","The owner owes use tax"],"a":0,"e":"Partial itemization disqualifies a (3)(d) contract; unitemized change orders and allowances can defeat that treatment. [Rule 12A-1.051(3)(d), F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(3)(d)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which document certifies that a newly installed fire alarm system was installed per the approved plans and the code?","o":["Record of Completion","Notice of Commencement","Certificate of Occupancy","Letter of Credit"],"a":0,"e":"Chapter 7 documentation requires a Record of Completion from the installing contractor; a certificate of occupancy comes from the building official. [NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 7 documentation]","r":"NFPA 72 7.5 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under F.S. 553.503, the federal ADA Standards for Accessible Design are made part of Florida law and must be incorporated into:","o":["local zoning ordinances adopted by each county","the National Electrical Code as an appendix","the Florida Accessibility Code for Building Construction","the Florida Fire Prevention Code and no other code"],"a":2,"e":"Section 553.503 adopts the federal ADA Standards and related regulations in 28 CFR Parts 35 and 36, to be incorporated into the Florida Accessibility Code for Building Construction by the Florida Building Commission. [F.S. 553.503]","r":"F.S. 553.503"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer with 10 or fewer employees suffers a work-related fatality. The employer's obligation is to:","o":["Record it only on the Form 300A when it is next posted","Report it to OSHA within 30 days on the annual summary","Do nothing, because employers with 10 or fewer employees are exempt from all OSHA reporting","Report it to OSHA within 8 hours even though routine recordkeeping is exempt"],"a":3,"e":"The size exemption from routine recordkeeping does not excuse employers from reporting fatalities, in-patient hospitalizations, amputations and eye losses. [29 CFR 1904.39; 1904.1]","r":"29 CFR 1904.39"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The financial responsibility questionnaire under Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C. asks whether any partner, officer, director, or trustee has ever had which of the following?","o":["A prior employer that used a different payroll vendor","A vehicle accident while driving a company truck","A change of home address within the last year","A bonding company complete work or pay a settlement for him"],"a":3,"e":"The questionnaire asks about surety completions or settlements, creditor claims, construction liens, tax liens, asset assignments, bankruptcy, unlicensed-contracting charges, and criminal convictions. [Rule 61G6-5.004]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C."},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor refuses to hire women for lineman work because 'the crews prefer men.' The best description is:","o":["Unlawful disparate treatment; customer or coworker preference is not a defense","A lawful bona fide occupational qualification based on crew preference","Protected conduct under the Equal Pay Act","A lawful business necessity because customers may object"],"a":0,"e":"BFOQ is a narrow defense, not available for stereotypes or customer/coworker preference. [29 CFR 1604.2]","r":"29 CFR 1604.2"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A single 15 kW, 240-volt single-phase fixed electric heater draws 62.5 A. What limit applies to its heating elements?","o":["No limit if the feeder circuit is 30 A","Subdivided into loads of no more than 30 A","Single elements up to 60 A are permitted","Subdivided into loads of no more than 48 A each"],"a":3,"e":"Heaters rated over 48 A must be subdivided, each subdivided load not exceeding 48 A and each protected at not more than 60 A. [NEC 424.22(B) and (C)]","r":"NEC 424.22(B) and (C)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.521(7), F.S., a certified electrical contractor's license number must appear on:","o":["Building permit applications only, and not on advertisements or proposals","Every offer of services, business proposal, and advertisement, in any medium","Business stationery only, such as letterhead and envelopes","Vehicle bumpers only, and not on proposals or contracts"],"a":1,"e":"The number must be on every offer of services, business proposal or advertisement in any medium; business stationery and promotional novelties are excluded from 'advertisement'. [F.S. 489.521(7)(b)]","r":"F.S. 489.521(7)(b)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A keyed note on a plan appears as a small hexagon containing the number 4. Where do you find the text of that note?","o":["In Division 01 of the project manual, under the general requirements section numbered 4","In the panelboard schedule on the row for circuit 4","In the keyed notes list on the same sheet, beside the hexagon numbered 4","In the addendum that is numbered 4 and issued for the bid"],"a":2,"e":"Keyed notes (or flag notes) tie a number on the plan to a list on that sheet, so the instruction applies to that sheet unless the note says otherwise. They are not specification sections, circuit numbers, or addenda. [Drawing practice: keyed notes]","r":"Drawing practice: keyed notes"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum height of a new luminaire or ceiling fan above the maximum water level when it is over the pool or within 5 ft horizontally?","o":["8 ft","10 ft","7.5 ft","12 ft"],"a":3,"e":"New luminaires, ceiling fans and similar equipment over the pool or within 5 ft horizontally must be at least 3.7 m (12 ft) above the maximum water level. [NEC 680.22(B)]","r":"NEC 680.22(B)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Absorbing or applying overhead means","o":["paying overhead costs from a separate dedicated bank account","deleting overhead costs from the books at the end of each year","deducting overhead directly from each employee's paycheck","assigning a share of indirect costs to jobs using a predetermined rate"],"a":3,"e":"A predetermined rate is multiplied by an activity base (like labor hours) to charge jobs for overhead. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which category of balanced twisted-pair cabling supports 10 Gb/s over the full 100 m channel?","o":["Category 5e","Category 6 (any length)","Category 6A","Category 3"],"a":2,"e":"Cat 6A supports 10GBASE-T to 100 m; Cat 6 is limited to about 55 m for 10G and Cat 5e is rated for 1 Gb/s. [ANSI/TIA-568.2]","r":"TIA-568.2 Cat 6A (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An office building has 15,000 sq ft and 120 receptacle outlets, each on a separate yoke. Under 220.14(K), the receptacle load for banks and offices is the larger of the 180 VA per yoke calculation or 1 VA per sq ft. What is the receptacle load before demand factors?","o":["10,800 VA","15,000 VA","21,600 VA","36,600 VA"],"a":2,"e":"180 VA x 120 = 21,600 VA, versus 1 VA x 15,000 sq ft = 15,000 VA. The larger value, 21,600 VA, is used. [NEC 220.14(K)]","r":"NEC 220.14(K)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A rigid metal conduit run carries four 1/0 AWG THHN copper conductors and one 6 AWG THHN copper equipment grounding conductor (0.1855 and 0.0507 sq in. each). The 40% fill areas are: 1-1/4 in. = 0.667; 1-1/2 in. = 0.913; 2 in. = 1.496. What is the smallest trade size?","o":["1-1/2 in.","1 in.","2 in.","1-1/4 in."],"a":0,"e":"The equipment grounding conductor counts in the fill. Total = 4(0.1855) + 0.0507 = 0.7927 sq in., which exceeds the 0.667 for 1-1/4 in. but fits within 0.913 for 1-1/2 in. [NEC Chapter 9, Table 1 Note 3, Tables 4 and 5]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Table 1 Note 3, Tables 4 and 5"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum rating of a branch circuit that supplies LED or incandescent signs (signs other than neon)?","o":["20 amperes","15 amperes","30 amperes","40 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"Branch circuits that supply signs and outline lighting other than neon tubing are limited to 20 A. [NEC 600.5]","r":"NEC 600.5"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A constructive acceleration claim typically arises when the owner","o":["denies a valid time extension and demands the original date","grants every time extension the contractor requests","pays the schedule of values on time every single month","issues the notice to proceed on the very first day"],"a":0,"e":"If a contractor is forced to speed up because a justified extension was refused, it may claim the added cost of acceleration. [Delay claims]","r":"Delay claims (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Use the optional method of 220.82 for a 2,400 sq ft dwelling (120/240-V, single-phase): lighting at 3 VA/sq ft, two small-appliance circuits, one laundry circuit, and nameplate loads of a 12 kW range, 5 kW dryer, 1.2 kW dishwasher, 0.9 kW disposal and 4.5 kW water heater, plus a 6 kVA air conditioner (no electric heat). What is the calculated demand current?","o":["86 A","109 A","94 A","146 A"],"a":1,"e":"General + appliances = 11,700 + 23,600 = 35,300 VA. First 10 kVA at 100% + 40% of the remaining 25,300 = 20,120 VA. Add the air conditioner at 100% (6,000 VA) = 26,120 VA; 26,120 / 240 = 108.8, or 109 A. [NEC 220.82]","r":"NEC 220.82"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Two circuit breakers are to be paralleled in the field to provide a 1200 A rating from two 600 A breakers. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, if the conductors are the same length","No, overcurrent devices may be connected in parallel only if factory assembled in parallel and listed as a unit","Yes, if the breakers are the same make","Yes, if the load is continuous"],"a":1,"e":"Fuses and circuit breakers may not be connected in parallel unless they are factory assembled in parallel and listed as a unit. [NEC 240.8]","r":"NEC 240.8"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A schedule of values in a construction contract is used to:","o":["List the contractor's insurance limits for the owner's file","Show the owner's financing terms to the construction lender","Record the daily weather conditions affecting the work","Break the contract sum into line items to calculate progress payments"],"a":3,"e":"The schedule of values divides the price among work items. The percent complete on each line times its value gives the amount earned for a pay request. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"When must a competent person inspect a scaffold and its components for visible defects?","o":["Before each work shift and after events that could affect it","Once a week, on the same weekday as the first erection of the scaffold","Only when it is first erected, and again when it is finally dismantled","Only after a fall of a person or material has been reported to the foreman"],"a":0,"e":"1926.451(f)(3) requires inspection by a competent person before each work shift and after any occurrence that could affect the scaffold's structural integrity.","r":"29 CFR 1926.451(f)(3)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In reviewing financial responsibility under Rule 61G6-5.005, the board considers the bankruptcy and judgment history of the applicant, its officers, and which shareholders?","o":["Any shareholder holding 10% or more of the stock","Only shareholders holding more than 50% of the stock","Only the shareholder with a majority of the stock","Every shareholder regardless of stock ownership"],"a":0,"e":"The rule extends the review to corporate officers and shareholders holding 10 percent or more of the stock. [Rule 61G6-5.005]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.005, F.A.C."},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A Schedule 40 PVC conduit is installed under a 4 in. thick concrete exterior slab with no vehicular traffic, with the slab extending at least 6 in. beyond the conduit. What is the minimum cover (raceway)?","o":["4 in.","18 in.","12 in.","6 in."],"a":0,"e":"Under a minimum 4 in. thick concrete exterior slab with no vehicular traffic that extends at least 6 in. beyond the underground installation, raceways need 4 in. of cover. [NEC Table 300.5]","r":"NEC Table 300.5"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How many BTU of heat are released by 12 kWh of electric resistance heating?","o":["12,000 BTU","8,952 BTU","40,944 BTU","43,200 BTU"],"a":2,"e":"12 x 3,412 = 40,944 BTU. [Conversions]","r":"Conversions"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The retroactive date on a claims-made policy","o":["is the date the policy expires","sets the earliest incident date that can be covered","is the date the premium is audited","is the date the first claim is made"],"a":1,"e":"Incidents before the retroactive date are excluded even if the claim is made during the policy period. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor can rent a trencher for $145 per day. Buying it costs $2,900 plus $15 per day to operate. After how many days of use does buying become cheaper?","o":["19 days","22 days","23 days","20 days"],"a":2,"e":"Break-even: 2,900 + 15d = 145d, so d = 2,900/130 = 22.3. Buying is cheaper after 23 days (round up). [Equipment analysis]","r":"Rent vs buy"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A single hermetic motor-compressor has a rated-load current of 20 A. What is the maximum standard rating of the branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault protective device before the starting-current exception is used?","o":["25 A","35 A","45 A","40 A"],"a":1,"e":"The device may not exceed 175% of the rated-load current: 20 x 1.75 = 35 A, a standard size. 45 A is the 225% value, which is permitted only if 175% will not carry the starting current. [NEC 440.22(A)]","r":"NEC 440.22(A)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum spacing between a wood surface and an incandescent or HID lamp in a sign?","o":["1/2 in.","2 in. (50 mm)","1 in.","6 in."],"a":1,"e":"600.9(C) requires at least 50 mm (2 in.) between combustible materials and incandescent or HID lamps or lampholders. [NEC 600.9(C)]","r":"NEC 600.9(C)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which written program element is required by 29 CFR 1910.1200(e)?","o":["A list of hazardous chemicals cross-referenced to SDSs, and how labels, SDSs and training are provided","An annual medical exam schedule for every employee who handles chemicals","A list of every employee's home address and emergency contact numbers","A copy of each chemical supplier's insurance policy and credit rating"],"a":0,"e":"The written program must describe label, SDS and training methods and include a list of hazardous chemicals by product identifier. [29 CFR 1910.1200(e)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200(e)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"What is the effect on a lien claim when a lienor fails to serve a timely notice to owner?","o":["The lien amount is reduced by 10% for each late day the notice is served","The lien remains valid but can only be recorded after final payment is made","Only the interest and costs on the lien amount are lost, not the principal","It is a complete defense to enforcement of the lien"],"a":3,"e":"Failure to serve a timely notice to owner is a complete defense to the lien, which can leave the lienor with only a claim against whoever hired it. [F.S. 713.06(2)]","r":"F.S. 713.06"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under which type of contract is the contractor paid for the quantities actually installed, multiplied by agreed prices per unit of work?","o":["Unit price contract","Lump sum (stipulated sum) contract","Cost-plus-fixed-fee contract","Guaranteed maximum price contract"],"a":0,"e":"A unit price contract multiplies the bid price per unit by the measured quantity installed, so the total is not fixed in advance. A lump sum contract fixes one total for a defined scope. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier's terms state a finance charge of 1.5% per month on past-due balances. Assuming simple interest, what is the charge on a $4,000 balance that is 2 months late?","o":["$1,200.00","$120.00","$720.00","$60.00"],"a":1,"e":"Charge = $4,000 x 0.015 x 2 = $120.00. 1.5% per month is 18% a year. [Credit terms]","r":"Supplier credit"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A job is expected to last 14 weeks. Weekly general conditions are superintendent $1,850, trailer $220, and temporary power $95. A one-time permit fee is $1,800. What are the total general conditions?","o":["$30,310","$32,110","$27,700","$3,965"],"a":1,"e":"Weekly = 2,165; x 14 = 30,310; + permit 1,800 = $32,110. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Individual single-pole circuit breakers with identified handle ties may serve as the overcurrent protection and disconnecting means for:","o":["A 3-phase feeder of 600 A","Any 240-V two-pole motor circuit","A multiwire branch circuit that supplies only line-to-neutral loads","A 120-V circuit with no neutral"],"a":2,"e":"Individual single-pole breakers with identified handle ties are acceptable for line-to-neutral loads in multiwire branch circuits (and certain grounded system arrangements). [NEC 240.15(B)]","r":"NEC 240.15(B)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Sheet metal auxiliary gutters must be supported and secured throughout their length at intervals not exceeding:","o":["3 ft","10 ft","6 ft","5 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Sheet metal auxiliary gutters are supported at 5 ft or less intervals. [NEC 366.30(A)]","r":"NEC 366.30(A)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"In an altered building, a proposed ramp has a 1:9 slope with a 4 in. rise. Under ADAAG 4.1.6(3)(a), this ramp is:","o":["permitted, because the rise is under the 6 in. alteration maximum","not permitted, because no alteration ramp may be steeper than 1:12","permitted, because 1:9 is flatter than the 1:8 limit in alterations","not permitted, because slopes between 1:8 and 1:10 are limited to 3 in. of rise"],"a":3,"e":"The 1:8 to 1:10 band allows a maximum rise of 3 in., and 4 in. exceeds that. The 6 in. rise allowance is for slopes between 1:10 and 1:12 only. [ADAAG 4.1.6(3)(a)]","r":"ADAAG 4.1.6(3)(a)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A bidder who submits a complete bid on time but has a record of abandoning past jobs and lacks financial capacity would most likely be rejected as","o":["not responsive","not negotiable","not bonded","not responsible"],"a":3,"e":"Responsibility is about the bidder's ability to perform. Responsiveness is about whether the bid conforms to the bid documents. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For Florida reemployment tax, which test does s. 443.1216 use to determine whether an individual is an employee of a business?","o":["Whether the worker has a Florida contractor license only","Whether the worker earns more than $7,000","The usual common-law rules for the employer-employee relationship","Whether a written contract uses the term 'independent contractor'"],"a":2,"e":"Section 443.1216(1)(a)2 treats an individual as an employee if, under the usual common-law rules applicable in determining the employer-employee relationship, the individual is an employee. [F.S. 443.1216(1)(a)2]","r":"F.S. 443.1216(1)(a)2"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Replacing a conductor with one of twice the circular mil area (same length, current, material) will make the voltage drop","o":["double","be cut to one quarter","stay the same","be cut in half"],"a":3,"e":"VD is inversely proportional to circular mil area. [Voltage drop]","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A detail drawn at 3/8 in. = 1 ft-0 in. shows a wireway that measures 4.5 in. on the sheet. How long is it in real life?","o":["18 ft","12 ft","36 ft","1.6875 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Each foot takes 3/8 in., so 4.5 / 0.375 = 12 ft (about 2.67 ft per inch). Multiplying by 3/8 goes the wrong way. [Scale calculation]","r":"Scale calculation"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.503(11), F.S., which alarm work is exempt from licensure?","o":["Installation of burglar alarms in houses","Installation of alarm systems on motor vehicles and boats","Installation of fire alarms in apartment buildings","Monitoring of alarms for paying customers"],"a":1,"e":"The statute exempts installing alarm systems on motor vehicles and boats; building alarm installation and for-fee monitoring are regulated. [F.S. 489.503(11)]","r":"F.S. 489.503(11)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"One-family dwellings require at least how many outdoor receptacle outlets, located front and back, accessible at grade level and not more than 6-1/2 ft above grade?","o":["Four, one on each side","Two, one at the front and one at the back","One for each 50 ft of exterior wall","One, at the front only"],"a":1,"e":"At least one receptacle outlet at the front and one at the back of a one-family dwelling is required, readily accessible from grade and not more than 6-1/2 ft above grade. [NEC 210.52(E)(1)]","r":"NEC 210.52(E)(1)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A nonexempt crew member drives a company van from one job site to another during the workday. This travel time is:","o":["Compensable only for the crew leader","Compensable hours worked","Not compensable because it is not productive work","Compensable only if it exceeds 30 minutes"],"a":1,"e":"Travel that is part of the employee's principal activities during the workday, such as job-to-job travel, counts as hours worked. [29 CFR 785.38]","r":"29 CFR 785.38"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under Rule 61G4-19.001, F.A.C., the citation fine for failing to maintain proof of workers' compensation or public liability insurance is:","o":["$25","$500","$100","$5,000"],"a":2,"e":"The schedule lists $100 for failure to maintain proof of workers' compensation or public liability insurance, along with late permits, missing inspections, and name violations. [F.A.C. 61G4-19.001]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-19.001"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's price for a job's materials is $13,600 at a 20% gross margin on price. What was the contractor's cost for those materials?","o":["$11,333","$17,000","$2,720","$10,880"],"a":3,"e":"Cost = price x (1 - 0.20) = $13,600 x 0.80 = $10,880. [Job costing]","r":"Margin"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Gross profit recognized to date on the same contract (revenue earned minus costs to date) is","o":["$140,000","$224,000","$56,000","$392,000"],"a":2,"e":"224,000 - 168,000 = 56,000, which is 25% of revenue earned, the same as the total expected margin (25%). [IRS Construction ATG]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Four 12 V, 100 Ah batteries are connected in series. The bank rating is","o":["48 V, 100 Ah","12 V, 400 Ah","12 V, 100 Ah","48 V, 400 Ah"],"a":0,"e":"Series connection adds voltage; amp-hour capacity stays that of one battery. [Batteries]","r":"Batteries"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A dwelling has electric heating rated 15 kW and an air conditioner rated 9 kW that cannot operate at the same time (interlocked). What load is used for these two loads in the feeder calculation?","o":["15 kW only","18 kW","24 kW","9 kW only"],"a":0,"e":"Noncoincident loads: only the largest of the loads that cannot be used simultaneously is included. [NEC 220.60]","r":"NEC 220.60"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor who is paid a deposit of $2,000 on a $30,000 residential job and then does nothing for 100 days (no just cause, no notice) risks discipline under s. 489.129(1), F.S., for:","o":["Using an unapproved logo","Abandoning the project","Failing to file a tax return","Advertising without a bond"],"a":1,"e":"Failing to perform without just cause for 90 consecutive days supports a presumption of abandonment under s. 489.129(1)(j). [F.S. 489.129(1)(j)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(1)(j)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On an equipment schedule, the electrical data '460/3/60' for a pump motor means:","o":["460 V, three-phase, 60 hertz","460 W, three conductors, 60 minutes","460 A, three starters, 60 amps","460 V, single-phase, 3 hp"],"a":0,"e":"Electrical data on schedules is typically written voltage / phase / frequency. [Drawing practice: schedules]","r":"Drawing practice: schedules"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire ferrous metal conduit carries 400-A circuits. Why must all phase conductors, the neutral and the equipment grounding conductor be in the same ferrous raceway?","o":["To keep the voltage drop at zero","To prevent heating by induced currents (eddy currents and hysteresis) in the ferrous metal","To reduce the cost of the raceway","To allow smaller conduit to be used"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors carrying alternating current in ferrous raceways must be grouped so that the net magnetic field is canceled; separating them would cause heating of the ferrous metal. [NEC 300.20(A)]","r":"NEC 300.20(A)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A sign or outline lighting system must be controlled by:","o":["An internal switch reached only by opening the sign face and removing the cover","A fuse in the grounded conductor that opens when the sign circuit is overloaded","A single-pole switch that may also control other lighting loads in the space","An externally operable switch or breaker that opens all ungrounded conductors"],"a":3,"e":"600.6 requires an externally operable disconnect that opens all ungrounded conductors, and the disconnect may not control other loads. [NEC 600.6]","r":"NEC 600.6"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A written agreement states the electrician 'is an independent contractor' even though the company controls his hours, tools and methods. How will the IRS likely treat the worker?","o":["As a contractor, because the written contract says so","As an employee, because the real relationship controls, not the label","As a contractor, because he is paid by the job","As a partner in the company, because he shares risk"],"a":1,"e":"A written contract is only one factor; the IRS looks at the actual relationship using the common-law factors. [IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27; IRS Topic 762]","r":"IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under a common clause, an item shown on the drawings but not mentioned in the specifications:","o":["Is not required","Is required as though shown in both","Is required only if it costs less than $500","Is the owner's responsibility"],"a":1,"e":"The usual language states that work shown on the drawings and not mentioned in the specifications (or the other way around) is to be provided as if it were in both documents. [Typical order-of-precedence clause]","r":"Typical order-of-precedence clause"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A small job has these activities (finish-to-start, working days): 1 2d (start); 2 3d after 1; 3 4d after 1; 4 5d after 2+3; 5 2d after 4. What is the total float of activity 2?","o":["3 days","2 days","0 days","1 day"],"a":3,"e":"Activity 2 (3 days) runs in parallel with activity 3 (4 days) between 1 and 4. The difference is 4 - 3 = 1 day of float. [CPM float]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"At year-end, goods ordered FOB shipping point were shipped December 29 and arrive January 3. For the physical count at December 31, those goods are:","o":["Excluded from inventory because the buyer has not yet counted them","Owned by the seller until the goods are delivered on January 3","Included only if the vendor's invoice is dated after the December 31 count","Owned by the buyer and included in the buyer's inventory (in transit)"],"a":3,"e":"Under FOB shipping point, title and risk pass at shipment, so the buyer reports goods in transit in inventory at year-end. [F.S. 672.401, 672.319]","r":"F.S. 672.401"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.129(10), F.S., if a business organization or its officers have been fined under s. 489.129(2) or s. 489.132, the board may, on that basis alone:","o":["Fine only the local building department that issued the permit","Waive the agent's continuing education for the next renewal","Require the agent to retake the state examination at once","Revoke, suspend, probate, or deny the qualifying agent's license"],"a":3,"e":"A fine against the organization or its personnel is itself a basis for action against the qualifying agent's or financially responsible officer's credential. [F.S. 489.129(10)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(10)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"One kilowatt-hour is the energy used by","o":["a 1,000 W load operating for one second","a 1,000 W load operating for one hour","one amp at one volt for one hour","a 100 W load operating for one minute"],"a":1,"e":"1 kWh = 1,000 watt-hours = 3.6 million joules. [Energy units]","r":"Energy units"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor and customer sign a contract to sell and install a ceiling-mounted TV and AV rack as separate tangible personal property rather than as part of new construction. How does Rule 12A-1.016 treat this?","o":["The items remain tangible personal property, so the full installed sales price is taxable","The items become real property so installation is tax-free","Only the materials are taxable","They are exempt as improvements to real property"],"a":0,"e":"Rule 12A-1.016 lists items (such as TVs and speakers outside new construction) that remain tangible personal property after installation, so tax applies to the full sales price. [Rule 12A-1.016, F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.016"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The GHS pictogram showing a flame signifies which hazard group?","o":["Flammables (and related self-reactive or pyrophoric classes)","Oxidizers only, such as chlorates and nitrates","Gases under pressure only, such as compressed cylinders","Corrosives, such as acids and strong caustic solutions"],"a":0,"e":"The flame pictogram is for flammables and related classes; the flame over circle is oxidizers; the gas cylinder is gases under pressure; corrosion is the corrosive pictogram. [29 CFR 1910.1200 App. C]","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200 App. C"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Using the indirect method, a contractor has net income of $140,000, depreciation of $22,000, an increase in accounts receivable of $35,000, and an increase in accounts payable of $18,000. What is cash provided by operating activities?","o":["$65,000","$145,000","$215,000","$109,000"],"a":1,"e":"$140,000 + $22,000 (non-cash add-back) - $35,000 (receivables increase uses cash) + $18,000 (payables increase retains cash) = $145,000. [Statement of cash flows, indirect method]","r":"Statement of cash flows"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which general rule governs the conductors of a single circuit installed in a metal raceway or cable?","o":["Equipment grounding conductors must always be run separately","The neutral may be run in a different raceway if it is the same size","Phase conductors may be run in separate raceways to reduce heating","All conductors of the same circuit, including the grounded conductor and equipment grounding conductor, must be in the same raceway, cable or trench"],"a":3,"e":"All conductors of the same circuit and, where used, the grounded conductor and all equipment grounding and bonding conductors must be contained within the same raceway, cable, trench or cord to limit inductive heating and keep impedance low. [NEC 300.3(B)]","r":"NEC 300.3(B)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has a CGL per-occurrence limit of $1,000,000 and a $4,000,000 umbrella over it. A single covered occurrence results in a $3,200,000 judgment. How much does the umbrella pay (assuming the underlying limit has not been used for anything else)?","o":["$3,200,000","$4,000,000","$0","$2,200,000"],"a":3,"e":"CGL pays 1,000,000; the umbrella pays the remaining 3,200,000 - 1,000,000 = $2,200,000, which is within the 4,000,000 umbrella limit. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A surety reviews a contractor whose balance sheet shows cash $60,000, receivables $380,000, underbillings $45,000, and other current assets $25,000. Current liabilities are $330,000. The surety excludes $70,000 of receivables over 120 days and $20,000 of other current assets that are a loan to an officer. What is adjusted working capital?","o":["$180,000","$420,000","$90,000","$110,000"],"a":2,"e":"Adjusted current assets = $60,000 + $380,000 + $45,000 + $25,000 - $70,000 - $20,000 = $420,000. Adjusted working capital = $420,000 - $330,000 = $90,000. [Surety underwriting; Rule 14-22.002 adjusted net worth concept]","r":"Working capital (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"During crane assembly/disassembly near power lines, what must be assumed about the lines?","o":["That they are energized, unless the utility confirms they are deenergized and visibly grounded","That they are de-energized if no sparks are visible during the lift","That they are de-energized if the lines are not sagging between poles","That they are safe at any distance greater than 5 feet from the boom"],"a":0,"e":"Employers must assume all lines are energized unless the utility owner/operator confirms they are de-energized and visibly grounded at the worksite. [29 CFR 1926.1407(f)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1407"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Before using a megohmmeter on a motor winding, what must be done?","o":["Leave the motor energized, and set the meter to 500 volts","Test it with the motor running, with the leads clipped to the terminals","Short the winding to the motor case before connecting the meter","De-energize, lock out, disconnect the winding and verify it is dead"],"a":3,"e":"Insulation resistance tests apply high voltage to de-energized equipment; the circuit must be isolated, tested dead, and capacitance discharged after the test. [29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2); manufacturer instructions (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In purchasing control, a purchase order (PO) is best described as:","o":["The carrier's receipt proving that the freight arrived intact at the site","The vendor's statement showing the total the buyer currently owes the vendor","The buyer's internal record of the materials actually issued to a given job","The buyer's written authorization and offer to buy specific items at stated prices and terms"],"a":3,"e":"A PO is the buyer's offer and internal spending authorization. The vendor's invoice states what is owed, and a receiving report records what arrived. [Purchasing control practice; F.S. 672.206]","r":"F.S. 672.206 (offer and acceptance)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An experience modification rate (EMR) of 1.00 means the contractor's loss experience is","o":["average compared with similar employers","twice as poor as the average employer","twice as good as the average employer","not yet rated for the current period"],"a":0,"e":"An EMR above 1.00 raises premium (worse than average) and below 1.00 lowers it. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor files a Florida reemployment tax report 75 days late. Using the $25 per 30 days or fraction rule, what is the late-report penalty?","o":["$50.00","$62.50","$75.00","$100.00"],"a":2,"e":"75 days is 3 periods of 30 days or fraction thereof (30, 60, 75): 3 x $25 = $75.00. [F.S. 443.141]","r":"F.S. 443.141"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On Form I-9, the employee must complete Section 1 no later than:","o":["Before the interview","The end of the first week","The employee's first day of employment","The third business day after hire"],"a":2,"e":"Section 1 is completed by the employee on or before the first day of employment; the employer completes Section 2 within three business days of the first day. [8 CFR 274a.2(b)(1)]","r":"8 CFR 274a.2(b)(1)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For crane disconnects, the runway contact conductors' disconnect must open all ungrounded conductors:","o":["One conductor at a time","Only the neutral conductor","Simultaneously, and be readily accessible","Only the grounded conductor"],"a":2,"e":"The runway disconnect must open all ungrounded conductors simultaneously and be readily accessible. [NEC 610.31]","r":"NEC 610.31"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The experience rating period normally used to calculate an EMR is","o":["the last 12 months of payroll only","the last ten policy years of losses","the current policy year, as it develops","three policy years, not counting the most recent year"],"a":3,"e":"The standard experience period is three years ending a year before the rating date, so the most recent policy year is not included. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has $90,000 in the bank and projects average net cash outflow of $15,000 per month with no new billings. About how many months of runway remain?","o":["9 months","4 months","6 months","12 months"],"a":2,"e":"Runway = cash / monthly net outflow = 90,000 / 15,000 = 6 months. [Cash flow basics]","r":"Cash flow basics"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Florida generally follows the 'at-will' employment doctrine. This means that, absent a contract or a legally protected reason, an employer may end the relationship:","o":["Only for documented misconduct that is reported to the Department of Commerce","Only after 90 days of written warnings and a hearing before the state","At any time and for any lawful reason, with or without cause","Only with the approval of the reemployment assistance appeals board"],"a":2,"e":"At-will employment still cannot be ended for a reason that violates discrimination, retaliation, workers' compensation or other laws. [Florida common law; F.S. 760.10; F.S. 440.205]","r":"Florida at-will doctrine; 760.10"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"ADAAG 4.27.3 exempts a wall receptacle from the 15 in. minimum mounting height when:","o":["it is not meant for occupant use, or special equipment requires another height","it is installed in a public corridor of the building","it is a duplex receptacle rather than a single","it is on a GFCI-protected branch circuit"],"a":0,"e":"The 15 in. minimum has exceptions where special equipment dictates otherwise or where receptacles are not meant for occupant use. Circuit type or receptacle style does not create an exemption. [ADAAG 4.27.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.27.3"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A corporate construction subcontractor's officer holds a valid exemption certificate. The general contractor must do what under s. 440.10?","o":["Nothing, since a valid certificate relieves the contractor of any coverage duties","Obtain a copy of the officer's exemption certificate along with insurance evidence for employees","Obtain only the subcontractor's license, since an exempt officer is not an employee","Add the exempt officer to the general contractor's own policy as an employee"],"a":1,"e":"A corporate subcontractor whose officer has elected exemption must give the contractor a copy of the certificate, and the sub's other employees still need coverage evidence. [F.S. 440.10(1)(c)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(c)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A manpower histogram shows crew demand by week of 6, 6, 12, 12, 12, 6, 6, 6 electricians. What is the average weekly demand, and what is the peak?","o":["Average 8; peak 12","Average 11; peak 12","Average 66; peak 12","Average 8; peak 18"],"a":0,"e":"Total = 66 worker-weeks over 8 weeks = 8 per week. The peak is 12. A leveled plan would aim near the average. [Manpower histogram]","r":"Manpower leveling (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The branch circuit that supplies the required sign outlet must be rated at least:","o":["15 amperes, shared with the general-use receptacles in the space","30 amperes, shared with the lighting in the tenant space","20 amperes, shared with the exterior receptacle outlets","20 amperes, and no other load except the sign controller"],"a":3,"e":"The sign outlet is supplied by a branch circuit rated at least 20 A that supplies no other load. A sign controller directly related to the sign may be on that circuit. [NEC 600.5]","r":"NEC 600.5"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A load is 50 kVA apparent power and 40 kW true power. How many kVAR of reactive power does it have?","o":["90 kVAR","45 kVAR","10 kVAR","30 kVAR"],"a":3,"e":"Q = sqrt(50 squared - 40 squared) = sqrt(900) = 30 kVAR. Subtracting directly gives 10. [Power triangle]","r":"Power triangle"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which is a general recording criterion under 29 CFR 1904.7 for an injury or illness?","o":["Any injury that the employee reports verbally to a coworker","Any visit to a doctor, even if only for a checkup or advice","Any injury that is later discussed at a toolbox safety talk","Days away from work, restricted work or transfer, or medical treatment beyond first aid"],"a":3,"e":"A work-related case is recordable if it results in death, days away, restricted work or transfer, medical treatment beyond first aid, or loss of consciousness (or a significant diagnosed injury). [29 CFR 1904.7(a)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.7(a)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 713.08(5), F.S., a claim of lien may be recorded at any time during the work or up to how long after the lienor's final furnishing of labor, services or materials?","o":["30 days","90 days","45 days","1 year"],"a":1,"e":"The claim of lien may be recorded during the work or within 90 days after final furnishing. One year is how long a recorded lien lasts unless an action is begun. [F.S. 713.08(5)]","r":"F.S. 713.08(5)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An NPLFA circuit uses 16 AWG copper conductors. What is the maximum overcurrent protection?","o":["7 amperes","15 amperes","10 amperes","20 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"NPLFA overcurrent protection may not exceed 7 A for 18 AWG and 10 A for 16 AWG. 7 A is the 18 AWG value. [NEC 760.43]","r":"NEC 2023 760.43"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.008, F.A.C., an electrical licensee who is selected in a random insurance audit must provide an original certificate of insurance within","o":["30 days of the written request","5 days of the request","60 days of the request","at the next renewal"],"a":0,"e":"The board audits at least 10 percent of certificate and registration holders and requires a certificate within 30 days of a written request. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A stockroom is out of a common 20-amp breaker, delaying a job. Which control helps most to prevent a repeat?","o":["Rely on the foreman's memory to tell the buyer when to reorder","Order the item only after the stock-out has already occurred","Set a reorder point that accounts for usage during the supplier lead time plus safety stock","Buy ten times the usual quantity of every item in the stockroom"],"a":2,"e":"A reorder point based on lead time and safety stock triggers purchases before stock-outs without overbuying. [Inventory control]","r":"Reorder point"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 713.06(3)(d)1, F.S., the contractor's final payment affidavit is:","o":["A statement from the lender that construction funds remain available","A sworn list of paid and unpaid lienors given to the owner before final payment","A certificate that the building is finished and ready for occupancy","A notice that the building permit has been closed out"],"a":1,"e":"The contractor furnishes a sworn affidavit listing lienors who have been paid and those unpaid. The owner may rely on it for final payment unless unlisted lienors gave notice; a contractor in default for not giving it has no lien. [F.S. 713.06(3)(d)1]","r":"F.S. 713.06(3)(d)1"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 100 W and a 40 W lamp, both rated 120 V, are wired in series across 120 V. The 100 W lamp has 144 ohms and the 40 W lamp 360 ohms hot. Which statement is true?","o":["The 100 W lamp dissipates more because it has the higher rating","Both dissipate equal power","The 40 W lamp dissipates more (20.4 W versus 8.2 W)","Both dissipate their full rated wattage"],"a":2,"e":"Same current (0.238 A) flows in both, so P = I squared x R is larger in the larger resistance: 20.4 W against 8.2 W. [Series circuits]","r":"Series circuits"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 70-A noncontinuous load is fed by THHN copper conductors terminating on a 70-A circuit breaker whose terminals are not marked with a temperature rating. What is the smallest copper conductor size permitted for this circuit?","o":["6 AWG","4 AWG","8 AWG","3 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"For circuits rated 100 A or less with unmarked terminals, ampacity is taken from the 60°C column. 4 AWG is the smallest copper conductor with 70 A at 60°C (6 AWG is only 55 A). [NEC 110.14(C)(1)(a), Table 310.16]","r":"NEC 110.14(C)(1)(a), Table 310.16"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A Type NM cable passes through a floor in a dwelling basement and is exposed above the floor where damage is possible. What is required?","o":["A GFCI breaker only","No protection if it is secured with staples","It must be enclosed in rigid metal conduit, IMC, EMT, Schedule 80 PVC or equivalent extending at least 6 in. above the floor","Only a 12-in. sleeve of PVC"],"a":2,"e":"Where NM cable passes through a floor in exposed work it must be protected by RMC, IMC, EMT, Schedule 80 PVC or other approved means extending at least 6 in. above the floor. [NEC 334.15(B)]","r":"NEC 334.15(B)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"To elect a construction-industry exemption as a corporate officer in Florida, the officer must own at least what share of the corporation's stock?","o":["51 percent","25 percent","5 percent","10 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Each exempt construction officer must be a shareholder owning at least 10 percent and be registered as an officer with the Division of Corporations; LLC members with at least 10 percent are treated alike. [F.S. 440.02]","r":"F.S. 440.02"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 25 hp three-phase motor on 460 V has 92 percent efficiency and 0.85 power factor. What is the approximate full-load line current?","o":["17.3 A","51.8 A","23.4 A","29.9 A"],"a":3,"e":"I = 25 x 746 / (1.732 x 460 x 0.92 x 0.85) = 29.9 A. NEC Table 430.250 values, not this, are used for conductor sizing. [Motor current]","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum clear headroom required along an accessible circulation path under ADAAG 4.4.2?","o":["80 in.","96 in.","84 in.","72 in."],"a":0,"e":"Walks, halls, and corridors must keep at least 80 in. of clear headroom. Objects mounted at or above that height do not create a protruding-object hazard. [ADAAG 4.4.2]","r":"ADAAG 4.4.2"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A new Florida employer at the 2.7% rate has 6 employees who each earn more than $7,000 in 2026. What is its total reemployment tax for the year?","o":["$2,268.00","$567.00","$42.00","$1,134.00"],"a":3,"e":"Each employee has $7,000 taxable wages: $7,000 x 2.7% = $189.00; 6 x $189.00 = $1,134.00. [F.S. 443.131(2)(a); F.S. 443.1217]","r":"F.S. 443.131; 443.1217"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.503(6)(a), F.S., an owner acting as his or her own electrical contractor on a commercial building must keep aggregate construction costs below:","o":["$75,000, for the owner's own use and not for sale or lease","$2,500, for a building that the owner intends to sell soon","$500,000, for a building that the owner will lease to others","$1 million, for a building owned by a church or nonprofit"],"a":0,"e":"The owner-builder exemption covers a single-family or duplex residence, a farm outbuilding, or a commercial building with costs under $75,000, for the owner's own use and not for sale or lease. [F.S. 489.503(6)(a)]","r":"F.S. 489.503(6)(a)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor takes $18,500 in advance payments on residential jobs and violates s. 489.126(2), F.S. The total money received falls in which penalty tier?","o":["Third-degree felony","First-degree misdemeanor","Second-degree felony","First-degree felony"],"a":0,"e":"For violations of subsection (2), under $1,000 is a first-degree misdemeanor; $1,000 to under $20,000 a third-degree felony; $20,000 to under $200,000 a second-degree felony; $200,000 or more a first-degree felony. $18,500 is in the third-degree tier. [F.S. 489.126(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.126 penalty tiers"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Beginning stock 100 units at $2.00; purchase 1 of 200 units at $2.20; purchase 2 of 150 units at $2.50; 280 units are used on jobs. Using periodic LIFO, what is the cost of the 280 units used?","o":["$661.00","$596.00","$700.00","$631.56"],"a":0,"e":"LIFO uses newest costs first: 150 x $2.50 + 130 x $2.20 = $375 + $286 = $661.00. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538 (inventories)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A claim of lien must be recorded within how many days after the lienor's final furnishing of materials?","o":["45 days","30 days","1 year","90 days"],"a":3,"e":"Section 713.08(5) requires the claim of lien to be recorded not later than 90 days after final furnishing. [F.S. 713.08(5)]","r":"F.S. 713.08(5)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A finish-to-start (FS) relationship means","o":["both activities must start at the same time","the successor must finish before the predecessor finishes","the successor cannot start until the predecessor finishes","the successor cannot finish until the predecessor starts"],"a":2,"e":"FS is the most common logic link: rough-in must finish before the inspection can begin, for example. SS, FF, and SF are the other three link types. [Precedence diagramming]","r":"Precedence relationships (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A wiring method that supplies a sign must terminate within:","o":["A lampholder or socket inside the sign body, using a pigtail","A receptacle outlet that is located right next to the sign","The flasher or controller that is mounted on the sign","A sign, outline lighting enclosure, suitable box, conduit body, or panelboard"],"a":3,"e":"600.5: the wiring method supplying signs terminates in the sign, outline lighting enclosure, suitable box, conduit body, or panelboard. [NEC 600.5]","r":"NEC 600.5"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A plan note says 'See detail 3/E-501.' Using the common detail callout convention, what does this direct you to find?","o":["Sheet 3 of the E-501 specification section","Keyed note 3 on the E-501 schedule","Revision 3 issued under sheet E-501","Detail number 3 on sheet E-501"],"a":3,"e":"The usual callout is detail number over sheet number, so '3/E-501' is detail 3 on sheet E-501. Notes, revisions, and specifications use different references. [Drawing practice: detail callouts]","r":"Drawing practice: detail callouts"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The design of neon tubing connected to a transformer must be such that the load:","o":["Exceeds the transformer rating by 20 percent for greater brightness","Does not overload the transformer or power supply continuously","Is designed around the tube with the lowest starting voltage","Is independent of the length and diameter of the tubing"],"a":1,"e":"600.41 requires that the length and design of tubing not cause a continuous overload of the transformer or electronic power supply. [NEC 600.41]","r":"NEC 600.41"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 14.4 ohm resistive heating element is connected to 208 V. To the nearest watt, what power does it produce?","o":["2,995 W","1,502 W","14 W","3,004 W"],"a":3,"e":"P = E squared / R = 208 x 208 / 14.4 = 3004 W. Dividing E by R gives amperes. [Power formula]","r":"Power formula"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the resistance of 500 ft of 4/0 AWG copper (211,600 CM) with K = 12.9?","o":["0.0305 ohms","0.0384 ohms","0.305 ohms","0.0610 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"R = 12.9 x 500 / 211,600 = 0.0305 ohms. [Conductor resistance]","r":"Conductor resistance"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An employee earns $250,000 in 2026 wages from one employer. What is the total Medicare tax (regular plus Additional) withheld from the employee for the year?","o":["$4,075.00","$3,625.00","$4,500.00","$5,975.00"],"a":0,"e":"Regular Medicare: $250,000 x 1.45% = $3,625.00. Additional: ($250,000 - $200,000) x 0.9% = $450.00. Total = $4,075.00. [IRS Topic 751]","r":"IRS Topic 751"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A task is budgeted at 400 hours at $48/hour ($19,200). Actual was 460 hours at $50/hour ($23,000). What is the labor efficiency (hours) variance, valued at the budgeted rate?","o":["$920 unfavorable","$2,880 unfavorable","$3,800 unfavorable","$2,880 favorable"],"a":1,"e":"Efficiency variance = (actual hours - budget hours) x budget rate = (460 - 400) x 48 = 2,880. The rate variance is (50 - 48) x 460 = 920; together 3,800 = total variance 3,800. [Variance analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Listed flexible metal conduit enclosing the secondary circuit conductor of a neon transformer may serve as the bonding means only if the total cumulative length of the conduit in the secondary circuit does not exceed:","o":["25 ft (7.5 m)","100 ft (30 m)","50 ft (15 m)","200 ft (60 m)"],"a":1,"e":"600.7(B) allows listed flexible metal or liquidtight flexible metal conduit as a bonding means if the total length in the secondary circuit is not more than 30 m (100 ft). [NEC 600.7(B)]","r":"NEC 600.7(B)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which Power over Ethernet standard delivers at least 30 W from the power sourcing equipment per port?","o":["IEEE 802.3af (PoE)","IEEE 802.11ac","IEEE 802.3u","IEEE 802.3at (PoE+)"],"a":3,"e":"802.3af supplies 15.4 W at the PSE, while 802.3at supplies up to 30 W. [IEEE 802.3at]","r":"IEEE 802.3af/at (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A bid has direct cost of $60,000. Overhead is recovered at 15% of direct cost, and the contractor wants a profit of 10% of the selling price. What is the bid price?","o":["$75,900","$76,667","$75,000","$66,667"],"a":1,"e":"Cost with overhead = 60,000 x 1.15 = 69,000; price = 69,000 / 0.90 = 76,667. Adding 10% to cost would make profit only 9.1% of price. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A liquidated damages clause is most likely to be enforced as written when:","o":["The sum is far greater than any likely loss, to deter delay","The clause is placed on the last page without being initialed","Delay damages were hard to estimate and the sum is a reasonable forecast","The owner suffered no actual loss from the late completion"],"a":2,"e":"Courts enforce liquidated damages when actual damages were uncertain and the sum is a reasonable pre-estimate; a grossly disproportionate sum is treated as an unenforceable penalty. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A punch list is:","o":["A schedule of values used to bill progress payments","A list of the subcontractors who were served with notices","Minor items to be finished or corrected after substantial completion","A request to extend the contract time for weather delays"],"a":2,"e":"The owner or architect prepares the punch list at the substantial-completion walkthrough. Completing it leads to final completion and final payment. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"May a contractor who buys materials for a government-owned project use the government's sales tax exemption on its own purchases?","o":["Yes, if the construction contract expressly says so","Yes, if it presents its own Annual Resale Certificate","No; only the government entity's own direct purchases are exempt","Yes, but only for the materials and not for supplies"],"a":2,"e":"Section 212.08(6) exempts direct purchases by qualifying governmental entities; contractors purchasing for government projects do not qualify. [F.S. 212.08(6)]","r":"F.S. 212.08(6)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G4-15.006, F.A.C., a Construction Industry Licensing Board applicant who lacks a qualifying credit score may satisfy the financial stability requirement by completing a course of how many hours?","o":["4 hours","14 hours","8 hours","20 hours"],"a":1,"e":"The rule asks for a FICO-derived score of 660 or higher or, alternatively, a Board-approved 14-hour financial responsibility course. [F.A.C. 61G4-15.006(2)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-15.006(2)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On a lump sum (stipulated sum) contract for a defined scope, who normally bears the risk that actual job costs exceed the estimate?","o":["The contractor, because the price is fixed","The owner, because the owner approved the plans","The surety, because the job is bonded","The architect, because the architect drew the plans"],"a":0,"e":"The contractor agrees to a fixed price, so cost overruns that are not caused by owner changes come out of the contractor's profit. The surety only steps in on default. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The special (bonus) depreciation allowance for qualified property acquired and placed in service after January 19, 2025 is","o":["40%","100%","60%","50%"],"a":1,"e":"Pub 946 (2025) states 100% for qualified property acquired and placed in service after January 19, 2025 under P.L. 119-21, with an election for 40% for the first tax year ending after that date. [IRS Pub 946 (2025)]","r":"IRS Pub 946 (2025)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A home run takeoff: a circuit with three 12 AWG current-carrying conductors plus a bare equipment ground is 62 ft horizontal, with two 4-ft vertical drops, and 6 ft of slack left at each end (the installer leaves 6 ft at the panel and 6 ft at the device end). How many feet of 12 AWG THHN are needed for the three insulated conductors (ignore the bare ground)?","o":["246 ft","210 ft","328 ft","204 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Per conductor: 62 + 2 x 4 + 2 x 6 = 82 ft. Three conductors gives 246 ft. Omitting the slack or counting the ground would change the answer. [Quantity takeoff]","r":"Quantity takeoff (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An estimate calls for 1,840 labor hours of rough-in. A crew of 4 electricians works 8-hour days. How many working days are needed (no overtime, full productivity)?","o":["230 days","57.5 days","460 days","46.0 days"],"a":1,"e":"Crew-hours per day = 4 x 8 = 32. Days = 1,840 / 32 = 57.5. Forgetting the 8-hour day gives 460. [Crew-day calculation]","r":"Crew scheduling (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A panel schedule shows the 208Y/120 V, 3-phase panel has a 100 A main and a connected load of 33,600 VA. The engineer notes 'continuous loads are included at 125 percent' in that figure. What is the three-phase current of the load, using I = VA / (1.732 x 208)?","o":["162 A","280 A","93 A","40 A"],"a":2,"e":"I = 33,600 / (1.732 x 208) = 93.3 A, which is below the 100 A main. Dividing by 208 or 120 alone ignores the three-phase factor and gives currents that are far too high. [NEC 408.30, 215.3]","r":"NEC 408.30, 215.3"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A city awards a $180,000 electrical contract. A bidder is considering whether to include a bond premium in the price. Under F.S. 255.05, what is true?","o":["A bond is always required on public work at any dollar amount","The awarding official may exempt a contract of $200,000 or less from the bond, so the bid documents control","A bond is never allowed on a public contract under $200,000","The city must require the bond from one named surety"],"a":1,"e":"For counties, cities and other political subdivisions, a contract of $200,000 or less may be exempted at the discretion of the awarding official; the bidder must read the documents. A public entity cannot require a specific agent or surety. [F.S. 255.05(1)(d)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Electronic power supplies for neon typically operate at a high frequency (around 20 to 30 kHz). Under Article 600, one result of operating above 100 Hz is:","o":["A greater spacing (1-3/4 in.) from nonmetallic conduit to the bonding conductor","A lower maximum secondary voltage, limited to 5,000 V at the output","A requirement for a 100 A disconnect at every sign location","An exemption from the bonding requirements of the Article"],"a":0,"e":"Article 600 (600.7(B) and 600.32) calls for 45 mm (1-3/4 in.) clearance at over 100 Hz compared with 38 mm (1-1/2 in.) at 100 Hz or less. [NEC 600.7(B)]","r":"NEC 600.7(B)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"NFPA 101 requires the letters of a required exit sign to be at least what height, with what stroke width?","o":["4 in. high with 1/2 in. strokes","3 in. high with 1/4 in. strokes","8 in. high with 1 in. strokes","6 in. high with 3/4 in. strokes"],"a":3,"e":"Required exit signs use letters at least 6 in. high with principal strokes at least 3/4 in. wide. Larger signs are acceptable. [NFPA 101, 7.10.5]","r":"NFPA 101 7.10.5 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A time impact analysis (TIA) is used to","o":["insert a delay into the current schedule and measure the effect","replace the baseline with the original cost estimate","total up the contractor's job overhead for the period","re-bid the project to a new set of contractors"],"a":0,"e":"A TIA models a delay or change in the updated schedule to determine whether and by how much the completion date moves. [Delay analysis]","r":"Delay claims (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of these is an example of a contract that Florida requires to be in writing under the statute of frauds (s. 725.01, F.S.)?","o":["A contract to replace a light fixture","A contract for the sale of land or an interest in land","A contract to deliver a box of fittings","A contract for a same-day service call"],"a":1,"e":"Section 725.01 lists contracts for the sale of land or interests in land, among other categories, as requiring a signed writing. [F.S. 725.01]","r":"F.S. 725.01"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An electrician earning $30.00/hour works 48 hours in a week. Under the FLSA overtime rule (1.5x the regular rate over 40 hours), the gross pay for the week is","o":["$1,440.00","$1,560.00","$2,160.00","$1,680.00"],"a":1,"e":"40 x 30 = 1,200 plus 8 x 45 = 360; total 1,560. [DOL overtime pay]","r":"DOL, Overtime Pay (FLSA)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Federal EEO laws require employers to display:","o":["The employee handbook, in a binder at the main office reception desk","Only the OSHA 300 Log, in the break room, during the posting period","An 'EEO is the Law' poster in a place visible to employees and applicants","Payroll records and time cards, available to any visitor on request"],"a":2,"e":"Covered employers must post the required EEO notices where employees and applicants can see them. [29 CFR 1601.30]","r":"29 CFR 1601.30"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 255.20, a contractor may be ineligible to bid on a local government's advertised project if the contractor is behind on an approved progress schedule for that same entity by at least","o":["2 percent","25 percent","50 percent","10 percent"],"a":3,"e":"The statute allows the local government to find a contractor ineligible if it is 10 percent or more behind schedule on the entity's other work when the project is advertised. [F.S. 255.20(1)]","r":"F.S. 255.20(1)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 715.12, can an obligee waive late-payment interest in advance of the due date?","o":["Yes, in any contract that is signed before the work begins on the job","Yes, but only when the party owed money is the project owner itself","No; interest can be waived only on or after the payment due date","Yes, as long as the written waiver has been notarized by both parties"],"a":2,"e":"715.12(6) prohibits waiver of interest before payment is due, though the obligee may waive it for late payments on or after the due date. [F.S. 715.12(6)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"When must a circuit breaker with a highest continuous current trip setting of 1200 A or higher have an arc energy reduction method?","o":["Only on systems above 600 V","Only when installed outdoors","Only in healthcare facilities","Whenever the highest continuous current trip setting for which the breaker is rated or can be adjusted is 1200 A or higher"],"a":3,"e":"240.87 requires documentation and a method to reduce clearing time (such as zone-selective interlocking or an arc-energy-reducing maintenance switch) for breakers rated or adjustable to 1200 A or higher. [NEC 240.87]","r":"NEC 240.87"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a weatherproof sign with two isolated compartments, how many drain holes are required?","o":["At least one at every low point or isolated section","One for the whole sign, at the lowest corner only","None, if the sign is gasketed at the seams","Two for the whole sign, one at each end"],"a":0,"e":"600.9(D)(2) requires at least one drain hole at every low point or isolated section of the equipment. [NEC 600.9(D)(2)]","r":"NEC 600.9(D)(2)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor learns that a transformer's delivery will be 6 weeks later than the schedule assumed. The transformer is on the critical path. The first thing the contractor should do is","o":["wait until near the completion date to raise the issue","notify the owner in writing and analyze the schedule impact","remove the transformer activity from the schedule","pay the subcontractors on the job early to keep them"],"a":1,"e":"Prompt written notice and a documented impact analysis preserve time-extension rights and let the team look for recovery options. Under FAR 52.249-10 notice is due within 10 days of the start of a delay. [Delay notice]","r":"Delivery lead times"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which of the following dwelling-unit rooms is NOT one of the areas listed in 210.12(A) as requiring AFCI protection of 120-V, 15- and 20-A branch circuits supplying outlets or devices?","o":["Bathroom","Closet","Hallway","Bedroom"],"a":0,"e":"210.12(A) lists kitchens, family rooms, dining rooms, living rooms, parlors, libraries, dens, bedrooms, sunrooms, recreation rooms, closets, hallways, laundry areas and similar rooms or areas. Bathrooms are not listed. [NEC 210.12(A)]","r":"NEC 210.12(A)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Slow-moving inventory that cannot be sold or used at a price above its cost should be:","o":["Left on the books at its original cost indefinitely","Moved to the next job at cost plus the usual markup","Written down to its lower market (net realizable) value","Ignored until the next physical count discovers the difference"],"a":2,"e":"Lower-of-cost-or-market valuation recognizes the loss when the item's value falls below cost. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Electrical penetrations through a fire-rated wall must be:","o":["Sealed with electrical tape","Left open to allow air circulation","Firestopped using approved methods to maintain the fire-resistance rating","Filled with fiberglass insulation"],"a":2,"e":"Openings around electrical penetrations through fire-resistant-rated walls, partitions, floors or ceilings must be firestopped using approved methods to maintain the fire-resistance rating. [NEC 300.21]","r":"NEC 300.21"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.513(6), F.S., local jurisdictions must report licensure, code violation and disciplinary information about locally licensed registrants to the board within:","o":["30 days","10 days","60 days","6 months"],"a":0,"e":"Local jurisdictions must report within 30 days after licensing or after any disciplinary action against a locally licensed contractor registered under Part II. [F.S. 489.513(6)]","r":"F.S. 489.513(6)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A cord-and-plug connected portable sign is plugged into a receptacle. Is a separate disconnect required by 600.6?","o":["Yes, a fused disconnect switch must be installed at the sign","No, the attachment plug is the means of disconnect","Yes, a separate breaker must be installed at the receptacle","Yes, but only when the portable sign is used outdoors"],"a":1,"e":"Cord-connected signs with an attachment plug are exempt from the 600.6 disconnect requirement. [NEC 600.6 Exception No. 2]","r":"NEC 600.6 Exception No. 2"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G4-15.006, F.A.C., a Part I applicant can meet the financial stability requirement by providing a FICO-derived credit score of at least what value, or by completing what?","o":["550, or a 4-hour board-approved financial responsibility course","660, or a 14-hour board-approved financial responsibility course","720, or a 28-hour board-approved financial responsibility course","800, or a 2-hour board-approved financial responsibility course"],"a":1,"e":"The rule asks for proof of a FICO-derived score of 660 or higher, and an applicant without that score can complete a 14-hour board-approved course. [F.A.C. 61G4-15.006(2)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-15.006(2)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Material was budgeted at 2,000 feet of wire at $0.50/foot. Actual use was 2,100 feet at $0.50. The variance is","o":["$50 favorable, a price variance because the price was unchanged","$50 unfavorable, a usage (quantity) variance","$100 unfavorable, a price variance because the wire cost more","no variance at all, because the price per foot matched the budget"],"a":1,"e":"100 extra feet x $0.50 = $50 over budget; price was on budget, so the cause is quantity. [Variance analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Using current assets of $612,000 and current liabilities of $408,000, what is working capital?","o":["$204,000","$408,000","1.50","$1,020,000"],"a":0,"e":"Working capital = current assets - current liabilities = $612,000 - $408,000 = $204,000. The ratio form (1.50) is the current ratio, not a dollar amount. [Working capital]","r":"Working capital"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A change order requires 36 labor hours at a burdened $52.40 per hour and $1,840 of material. The contract allows 10% overhead on direct cost, and then 10% profit on direct cost plus overhead. What is the change order price?","o":["$4,508.94","$4,471.68","$3,726.40","$4,099.04"],"a":0,"e":"Direct = 36 x 52.4 + 1840 = 3,726.40; overhead 372.64 gives 4,099.04; profit 409.90 gives $4,508.94. A flat 20% on direct cost gives $4,471.68, which omits the compounding. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.521(2)(a)1, F.S., changes in the required application information must be mailed to the department within:","o":["10 days","45 days","30 days","60 days"],"a":1,"e":"Changes in information such as officers or address must be sent to the department within 45 days using the correct information. [F.S. 489.521(2)(a)1]","r":"F.S. 489.521(2)(a)1"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Violating or failing to comply with an order issued under F.S. 633.228(1) or (2) is:","o":["punishable only by license suspension","a misdemeanor punishable under s. 633.124","a felony of the third degree","only a civil infraction"],"a":1,"e":"F.S. 633.228(3) makes noncompliance with these orders a misdemeanor, punishable as provided in s. 633.124. [F.S. 633.228(3)]","r":"F.S. 633.228(3)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An arc welder's branch circuit overcurrent device may not exceed what percent of the welder's rated primary current?","o":["125%","300%","150%","200%"],"a":3,"e":"The welder OCPD is limited to 200% of I1max or rated primary current. [NEC 630.12(A)]","r":"NEC 630.12(A)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 70.7 V constant-voltage speaker line has six 5 W taps and six 2 W taps. What total speaker power must the amplifier supply, and what is the minimum amplifier size with 25 percent headroom?","o":["30 W; 37.5 W","42 W; 42 W","12 W; 15 W","42 W; 52.5 W"],"a":3,"e":"6 x 5 = 30 W and 6 x 2 = 12 W for 42 W. With 25 percent headroom, 42 x 1.25 = 52.5 W. [Audio system design practice; NEC Art. 640]","r":"70.7 V sound (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor wants an approved change order paid. The usual best practice is to","o":["bill it as part of the retainage release at the end of the project only","wait until final completion before mentioning it to the owner or architect","spread it evenly across the existing SOV lines without telling the owner","add it to the schedule of values as its own line item, then bill it as the work progresses"],"a":3,"e":"Change-order work should appear on the pay application so the owner can approve and pay it with the progress billings; hiding or deferring it delays cash. [Progress billing practice]","r":"Contract billing arithmetic (progress billing practice)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An estimator is told that the combined sales tax rate on materials in the county is 7%. The bid's material subtotal before tax is $63,500. What is the material cost including tax?","o":["$4,445","$67,945","$68,279.57","$72,390"],"a":1,"e":"63,500 x 0.07 = 4,445 tax; 63,500 + 4,445 = $67,945. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 30 A neon sign branch circuit is wired with copper conductors. What is the smallest size permitted by the small-conductor rule?","o":["10 AWG","12 AWG","8 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"10 AWG copper may be protected at up to 30 A under 240.4(D), and for continuous loads the conductor also must carry 125 percent of the load (210.19(A)(1)). [NEC 240.4(D), 210.19(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 240.4(D), 210.19(A)(1)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A cumulative planned-progress S-curve compared with actual progress is mainly useful for","o":["listing the hours worked by each crew member","identifying which activities have zero total float","calculating the surety premium for the contract","seeing quickly if the job is ahead of or behind the plan"],"a":3,"e":"The S-curve plots cumulative percent complete over time; the gap between planned and actual lines shows schedule variance. It does not show float. [Progress tracking]","r":"Schedule updates (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A project network (all finish-to-start, durations in working days): A 3d (start); B 5d after A; C 4d after A; D 6d after B; E 2d after B+C; F 7d after D+E; G 3d after C; H 2d after F+G. What is the project duration?","o":["25 days","23 days","32 days","21 days"],"a":1,"e":"Forward pass: A 0-3; B 3-8; C 3-7; D 8-14; E 8-10; F 14-21; G 7-10; H 21-23. The longest path A-B-D-F-H = 3+5+6+7+2 = 23 days. Adding every duration (32) ignores parallel work. [CPM forward pass]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Drain holes are required in a weatherproof (not watertight) wet-location sign. What are the allowed hole sizes?","o":["Not larger than 1 in. and not smaller than 1/2 in.","Not larger than 1/4 in. and not smaller than 1/16 in.","Any size if screened","Not larger than 1/2 in. and not smaller than 1/4 in."],"a":3,"e":"600.9(D)(1): drain holes may not be larger than 13 mm (1/2 in.) or smaller than 6 mm (1/4 in.). Each low point or isolated section needs at least one hole, positioned without external obstructions. [NEC 600.9(D)]","r":"NEC 600.9(D)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"At final payment, the contractor must give the owner an affidavit that states:","o":["The contractor's net profit on the job and the total markup applied","Whether all lienors who served timely notices have been paid in full, listing any unpaid","Only the contractor's own insurance coverage and its expiration dates","The supplier's current price list and the discounts it has given"],"a":1,"e":"The contractor's final payment affidavit states that all lienors have been paid or lists unpaid lienors and amounts, and must be given before the owner's final payment. [F.S. 713.06(3)(d)1.]","r":"F.S. 713.06(3)(d)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A nonexempt employee drives his own vehicle from home to the first job site as he does every day. This ordinary home-to-work commute is:","o":["Generally not compensable","Compensable as hours worked","Compensable if he carries tools in the vehicle","Compensable if the site is farther than 20 miles"],"a":0,"e":"Normal travel from home to work is not hours worked under the Portal-to-Portal Act, though special one-day assignments and some other situations can differ. [29 CFR 785.35]","r":"29 CFR 785.35"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is considered first aid, and therefore not recordable, under 29 CFR 1904.7?","o":["Being treated by a physician for a diagnosed fracture","Receiving sutures (stitches) to close a laceration","Using a non-prescription medication at nonprescription strength","Taking a prescription antibiotic ordered by a physician"],"a":2,"e":"Non-prescription-strength medication use is on the first aid list; sutures, prescription medication and treatment of fractures are medical treatment beyond first aid. [29 CFR 1904.7(b)(5)(ii)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.7(b)(5)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A plan shows 48 duplex receptacles. The estimator adds 5 percent for lost, damaged, or spare devices and rounds up. How many receptacles are purchased?","o":["48 receptacles","51 receptacles","53 receptacles","55 receptacles"],"a":1,"e":"48 x 1.05 = 50.4, rounded up to 51. Buying exactly 48 leaves no allowance for waste. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Title VII, which of these is a protected characteristic?","o":["Religion","Height","Credit score","Political party"],"a":0,"e":"Title VII prohibits discrimination based on race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy, sexual orientation and gender identity) and national origin. [42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(a)]","r":"42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(a)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In the construction industry, no more than how many officers of a corporation (or affiliated corporations) may elect an exemption from workers' compensation coverage?","o":["Five","Three","One","Two"],"a":1,"e":"At most three officers of a construction-industry corporation may elect exemption, and each must own at least 10 percent of the stock and be listed as an officer with the Division of Corporations. [F.S. 440.02]","r":"F.S. 440.02"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An intentional bond for pool water requires a minimum conductive surface area of:","o":["1 square inch of any metal surface","9 square inches of corrosion-resistant surface in the water","3 square feet of deck steel","25 square inches of pool shell"],"a":1,"e":"Pool water must be bonded with at least 5800 mm2 (9 in2) of conductive surface. [NEC 680.26(C)]","r":"NEC 680.26(C) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Relying on a single supplier for a critical item creates which main purchasing risk?","o":["Lower inventory carrying cost at every one of the contractor's jobs","Supply interruption or price leverage if that supplier fails to deliver","A guaranteed lower price on every purchase made from that supplier","Better credit terms from every other supplier in the market"],"a":1,"e":"Backup sources reduce schedule risk, though consolidating spend can win better pricing. [Purchasing practice]","r":"Vendor management"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"By applying for renewal under F.S. 489.517, an electrical licensee certifies that","o":["no employees were injured during the biennium","all bids during the period were awarded to the lowest bidder","the required public liability and property damage insurance has been continuously maintained","every customer has paid in full for work performed"],"a":2,"e":"The renewal application serves as the certification of continuous insurance, and the board verifies it by random sampling. [F.S. 489.517]","r":"F.S. 489.517"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A subcontractor not in privity with the owner must serve a notice to owner (NTO) before commencing or not later than how many days after commencing to furnish labor, services or materials?","o":["10 days","30 days","90 days","45 days"],"a":3,"e":"Section 713.06(2)(a) requires service before commencing or no later than 45 days after commencing. The 90-day period is the claim of lien recording deadline. [F.S. 713.06(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(2)(a)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor earned net income after taxes of $92,500 on net sales of $1,850,000. What is the net profit margin?","o":["2.5%","10.0%","5.3%","5.0%"],"a":3,"e":"Net profit margin = net income / net sales = $92,500 / $1,850,000 = 5.0%. [Profit margin, UNL Extension G2009]","r":"Profit margin"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How often must the employer verify, by supervision or inspection, that employees comply with 1910.269 safety practices?","o":["Every 2 years","Every 5 years","Only after an injury","At least annually"],"a":3,"e":"The employer must determine through regular supervision and inspections conducted on at least an annual basis that employees comply with the safety-related work practices. [29 CFR 1910.269(a)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.269(a)(2)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is a DIRECT job cost?","o":["Office rent for the contractor's main office","The accountant's monthly retainer","Conduit and wire installed on a specific job","Advertising for the company website"],"a":2,"e":"Direct costs can be traced to a specific job; rent, accounting and marketing support the whole business and are indirect (overhead). [Direct vs indirect costs]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Liquidated damages are most likely to be reduced or avoided when","o":["the contractor finishes late because of poor planning","the contractor changes foremen during the project","the delay is excusable and a time extension has been obtained","the contractor orders its materials later than planned"],"a":2,"e":"Excusable delay, properly documented, extends the contract time, so no liquidated damages accrue for that period. [FAR 52.249-10]","r":"Liquidated damages"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's PO was issued at $4.25 per unit, but the supplier invoices $4.60 per unit for 800 units. If the contractor approves the invoice as is, which control weakness is shown?","o":["A price variance was paid without review against the PO","The sales tax on the order was calculated incorrectly by the vendor","The goods were not inspected for damage when they were delivered","The receiving count was not made blind, so quantities were overstated"],"a":0,"e":"Three-way match is meant to catch price differences between PO and invoice; paying without review defeats it. [Three-way match]","r":"Three-way match"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.533(5), F.S., what share of fines for violations of chapter 633 or State Fire Marshal rules goes to the Insurance Regulatory Trust Fund?","o":["10 percent","25 percent","100 percent","50 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Half of fines imposed for chapter 633 or Fire Marshal rule violations go to the Insurance Regulatory Trust Fund. [F.S. 489.533(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.533(5)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Payments made to a vendor solely for merchandise (materials) are generally:","o":["Reportable on Form 941 with the quarterly payroll tax return","Reportable on Form W-2 to the vendor's owner at year end","Not reportable on Form 1099-NEC, which reports nonemployee compensation for services","Reportable on Form 1099-NEC every time the annual total reaches the threshold"],"a":2,"e":"Form 1099-NEC reports payments for services by non-employees; purchases of merchandise or materials are not nonemployee compensation. [IRS Instructions for Forms 1099-MISC and 1099-NEC]","r":"IRS 1099-NEC Instructions"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"What is a maintenance (warranty) bond?","o":["A bond guaranteeing that the bidder will sign the contract","A bond guaranteeing against defects after completion","A bond protecting suppliers from nonpayment","A bond covering injuries on the site"],"a":1,"e":"Maintenance bonds secure repair of defects during a post-completion warranty period, often one year. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 25 hp, 208-volt, three-phase motor has a Table 430.250 full-load current of 74.8 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the conductors supplying this single continuous-duty motor?","o":["74.8 A","93.5 A","86.0 A","112.2 A"],"a":1,"e":"74.8 A x 1.25 = 93.5 A per 430.22. The 112.2 A figure is 150%, which is not the conductor rule. [NEC 430.22(A)]","r":"NEC 430.22(A)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On the OSHA 300 Log, a 'privacy concern case' entry shows which information instead of the employee's name?","o":["'N/A'","'Privacy case'","The employee's initials","The employee's job title"],"a":1,"e":"Cases such as intimate-body-part injuries or sexual assault are entered as 'privacy case' with the name kept on a separate confidential list. [29 CFR 1904.29(b)(6)-(9)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.29(b)(6)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A sealed bid arrives after the stated deadline, but before the bids are opened. Under typical public bidding rules the bid will be","o":["accepted if it is the lowest","accepted if the bidder pays a late fee","held and opened with an addendum","rejected as late"],"a":3,"e":"Public owners treat the deadline as firm so that all bidders compete under the same conditions. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A worker is called an independent contractor in a signed agreement but is economically dependent on the contractor, uses its tools, works its schedule and has no opportunity for profit or loss. Under the FLSA economic realities test, he is most likely:","o":["Exempt from the FLSA because he is skilled","An employee entitled to minimum wage and overtime","An independent contractor because he is paid by the job","An independent contractor because the agreement says so"],"a":1,"e":"Courts and DOL look at the economic reality of the relationship, not labels. Dependence, control and lack of profit or loss indicate employee status. [29 CFR 795; DOL WHD]","r":"29 CFR Part 795"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The certificate holder on an electrical contractor's certificate of insurance filed to meet Rule 61G6-5.008 should be","o":["the Division of Workers' Compensation","the local building department that issued the permit","the DBPR Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board","the Florida Bar's Construction Law Section"],"a":2,"e":"The rule names DBPR's Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board as the certificate holder. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A surety issued a bond for a contractor who turned out to be unlicensed. Under s. 489.128(3), F.S., can the surety refuse a claim on the bond only because the principal was unlicensed?","o":["Yes, an unlicensed principal automatically voids the surety's bond","Yes, but only if the bond claim is for less than $2,500","No, the principal's unlicensed status is not a defense to the claim","No, but only if the owner recorded a notice of commencement"],"a":2,"e":"Section 489.128(3) keeps the rights of parties other than the unlicensed contractor, and says unlicensed status is not a defense to a bond or indemnity claim. This protects subs and suppliers. [F.S. 489.128(3)]","r":"F.S. 489.128(3)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The 2010 ADA Standards for Accessible Design consist of which two parts?","o":["NFPA 101 and NFPA 72 as adopted by the Department of Justice","The 1991 Standards and the Florida Building Code, Accessibility","The Title III regulation at 28 CFR 36.201-36.608 and the 2004 ADA/ABA Guidelines","The International Building Code and ICC/ANSI A117.1 only"],"a":2,"e":"The 2010 Standards combine the Department of Justice Title III requirements at 36.201-36.608 with the 2004 ADA/ABA accessibility guidelines. They are not the 1991 Standards. [28 CFR 36.104]","r":"28 CFR 36.104"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which list contains the usual major cost TYPES tracked on a job cost report?","o":["Assets, liabilities, owner's equity, revenue and expenses for the whole company","Federal, state, county, city and special-district taxes owed on each job","Principal, interest, escrow, insurance and late fees on each equipment loan","Labor, material, equipment, subcontract and other direct costs"],"a":3,"e":"Job cost reports commonly separate labor, material, equipment, subcontract and other job-related costs so each can be compared with its budget. [Job cost accounting]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The FUTA tax after the maximum state credit is 0.6% on the first $7,000 of each employee's wages. What is the maximum net FUTA per employee for the year?","o":["$42","$420","$378","$189"],"a":0,"e":"FUTA is 6.0% less up to 5.4% credit = 0.6%; 0.6% x $7,000 = $42. [IRS Topic 759]","r":"IRS Topic 759 (Form 940)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which of the following receptacles is exempt from 2010 Standards 309 reach range requirements under 205.1?","o":["A floor electrical receptacle","A receptacle in a corridor wall","A receptacle in a lobby wall","A receptacle in a conference room wall"],"a":0,"e":"Floor electrical receptacles are one of the listed exceptions, along with dedicated-use receptacles. General-use wall receptacles must comply. [2010 Standards 205.1, Exc. 4]","r":"2010 Std 205.1 Exc. 4"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"May a metal pole used to support a sign enclose the supply conductors?","o":["No, the conductors must be in a separate conduit outside the pole","Yes, but only nonmetallic poles may do so","Yes, metal or nonmetallic poles may enclose the supply conductors","Yes, but only for circuits operating below 50 volts"],"a":2,"e":"600.5 allows poles supporting signs to enclose supply conductors, and 600.6(A)(1) addresses the disconnect at the point of entry to the pole. [NEC 600.5]","r":"NEC 600.5"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Total float of an activity is","o":["the time it can slip without delaying the project finish","the time a successor waits for a material delivery","its duration multiplied by the crew size assigned","the time between its start and its finish dates"],"a":0,"e":"Total float = latest finish - earliest finish (or late start - early start). Zero float means the activity is critical. [CPM float; Pitt CPM notes]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"After the contractor receives a proper written demand under s. 489.126(2)(b), F.S., the contractor is presumed to lack just cause if it does not apply for permits, start work, or refund within:","o":["5 days after receiving the demand","30 days after receiving the demand","15 days after receiving the demand","90 days after receiving the demand"],"a":1,"e":"Failure to cure within 30 days after receiving the written demand creates a presumption that the contractor lacked just cause. [F.S. 489.126(2)(b)2]","r":"F.S. 489.126(2)(b)2"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Rule 61G6-5.008 requires the minimum liability insurance carried by an electrical licensee to include which per-person and per-occurrence limits?","o":["$100,000 per person and $300,000 per occurrence","$10,000 per person and $20,000 per occurrence","$250,000 per person and $500,000 per occurrence","$1,000,000 per person and $2,000,000 per occurrence"],"a":0,"e":"The rule's table lists liability of $100,000 per person and $300,000 per occurrence, with property damage of $500,000 or a combined single limit of $800,000. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008(1)(h)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008(1)(h)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A loan payment of $2,000 includes $1,700 principal and $300 interest. In a cash flow forecast and in the income statement, respectively, the treatment is","o":["$300 cash outflow in the forecast, but the full $2,000 as expense on the income statement","$2,000 cash outflow in the forecast, but only $300 interest expense on the income statement","$2,000 cash outflow in the forecast and the full $2,000 as expense on the income statement","$1,700 cash outflow in the forecast, with $300 expense on the income statement"],"a":1,"e":"The forecast shows the whole $2,000 leaving the bank; principal reduces the liability and is not an expense, so only the $300 interest hits the income statement. [Cash flow basics]","r":"Cash flow basics"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Contribution margin equals","o":["revenue minus all costs including fixed","revenue minus variable costs","revenue minus only fixed costs","revenue plus variable costs"],"a":1,"e":"Contribution margin is what remains from sales after variable costs to cover fixed costs and profit. [Contribution margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A supplier's cable yields only 95% usable footage after cutting loss. If 840 ft of usable cable is required, how many feet should be ordered (round up)?","o":["882 ft","885 ft","840 ft","798 ft"],"a":1,"e":"If 95% of purchased footage is usable, purchased = 840/0.95 = 884.21, rounded up to 885 ft. Multiplying by 1.05 understates the need. [Estimating practice]","r":"Waste factor"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which RFI is written most effectively?","o":["'The electrical drawings are confusing and incomplete. Please help us understand what to bid.'","'We think the plans are wrong, so we will do it our way.'","'What would it cost to change all of the panelboards to a different brand? Please advise.'","'E-301 detail 4 shows 2 in. conduit; spec 26 05 33 requires 3 in. Which governs? Reply by Friday.'"],"a":3,"e":"A good RFI cites the sheet and specification section, states the conflict, and asks a specific question with a needed date. Price requests belong in change proposals. [RFI practice]","r":"RFI practice"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What is the Social Security wage base limit for 2026?","o":["$176,100","$200,000","$168,600","$184,500"],"a":3,"e":"Pub 15 (2026) states the Social Security wage base limit is $184,500; wages above that amount are not subject to the 6.2% tax. $200,000 is the Additional Medicare Tax withholding threshold, not the Social Security base. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has CGL limits of $1,000,000 per occurrence and $2,000,000 general aggregate. In one policy year, claims already paid total $1,750,000. A new covered occurrence results in a $600,000 loss. What is the most the CGL policy will pay for it (assuming no umbrella)?","o":["$600,000","$1,000,000","$250,000","$0"],"a":2,"e":"Remaining aggregate = 2,000,000 - 1,750,000 = $250,000, which is below both the loss and the per-occurrence limit. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The sign installation uses a Class 2 power source. What must be true of that power supply?","o":["It must be a 600 V transformer with a fused primary circuit","It must be listed for signs, or be part of a listed sign","It must be an unlisted unit approved by the installer on site","It must be fed from a dedicated 50 A circuit with a disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"Article 600 requires Class 2 power sources used for signs to be listed for that use or part of a listed sign, with metal parts grounded. [NEC 600 (Class 2 power sources)]","r":"NEC 600 (Class 2 power sources) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A single-phase 115-volt, 2 hp motor is listed at 24 A in Table 430.248. What minimum ampacity is required for the branch-circuit conductors supplying only this motor?","o":["24 A","30 A","27.6 A","36 A"],"a":1,"e":"24 A x 1.25 = 30 A. The 27.6 A value uses 115%, which applies to disconnect ratings, not conductors. [NEC 430.22, Table 430.248]","r":"NEC 430.22, Table 430.248"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor has current assets of $700,000 and current liabilities of $400,000. It uses $100,000 of cash to pay down accounts payable. What happens to the current ratio and to working capital?","o":["Ratio rises from 1.75 to 2.00; working capital stays $300,000","Ratio is unchanged; working capital falls by $100,000","Ratio rises to 2.00; working capital rises by $100,000","Ratio falls to 1.50; working capital falls to $200,000"],"a":0,"e":"Both current assets and current liabilities fall by $100,000: $600,000 / $300,000 = 2.00. Working capital stays $700,000 - $400,000 = $300,000. When the ratio exceeds 1.0, paying a current liability raises it. [Current ratio / working capital]","r":"Current ratio"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A 'pacing delay' occurs when","o":["an inspector rejects the installed work at the job","the contractor speeds up all work on the entire job","a party slows non-critical work to match a critical delay by another","the owner terminates the contract for convenience"],"a":2,"e":"Pacing means matching the pace of work to a critical delay caused by someone else; it is not a cause of the project delay. [Delay analysis]","r":"Delay claims (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.132(5), F.S., the department may deny a license to a business that associates with a person who was an officer, director, partner, trustee or manager of an organization previously disciplined by:","o":["Revocation, suspension, or a fine over $2,500","A fine of any amount","A written reprimand only","A notice of noncompliance"],"a":0,"e":"The department may suspend, revoke or deny based on association with a person found in a final order to have violated this section, or an officer of an organization disciplined by revocation, suspension, or a fine over $2,500, with reasonable cause that the person knew or should have known. [F.S. 489.132(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.132(5)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.143(2), F.S., which of these is NOT payable from the recovery fund?","o":["Attorney fees, court costs, postjudgment interest, and punitive damages","Actual damages that are specified in a final court judgment","Actual damages that are specified in a board restitution order","Actual damages that are specified in an arbitration award"],"a":0,"e":"The fund covers only actual damages up to the per-claim maximum; postjudgment interest, attorney fees, court costs, medical damages and punitive damages are excluded. [F.S. 489.143(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.143(2)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An invoice dated March 5, 2026 is on terms 2/10, net 30. What are the last day to take the discount and the net due date?","o":["March 15, 2026 and April 14, 2026","March 14, 2026 and April 3, 2026","March 15, 2026 and April 4, 2026","March 17, 2026 and April 4, 2026"],"a":2,"e":"Counting from the invoice date: March 5, 2026 + 10 days = March 15, 2026; + 30 days = April 4, 2026. [Terms 2/10 net 30]","r":"Vendor terms"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the allowable ampacity of a 4/0 AWG aluminum conductor with 75°C insulation (Table 310.16, three or fewer current-carrying conductors, 30°C ambient)?","o":["230 A","205 A","180 A","150 A"],"a":2,"e":"Table 310.16 shows 4/0 AWG aluminum at 150 A (60°C), 180 A (75°C) and 205 A (90°C). 230 A is the copper 75°C value. [NEC Table 310.16]","r":"NEC Table 310.16"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor in privity with the owner (a direct contractor) wants to preserve lien rights. Compared with a subcontractor, the contractor:","o":["Does not serve a notice to owner but must record a claim of lien","Must serve a notice to owner within 10 days of starting","Has no lien rights because the contract is direct","Must wait for the certificate of occupancy before liening"],"a":0,"e":"Lienors in privity with the owner, who contract directly with the owner, are not required to serve a notice to owner, but a lien exists only when a claim of lien is recorded. [F.S. 713.05]","r":"F.S. 713.05"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum bending radius for Type AC cable?","o":["7 times the diameter","3 times the diameter","10 times the diameter","5 times the diameter of the cable"],"a":3,"e":"The inner radius of any bend in Type AC cable may not be less than five times the cable diameter. [NEC 320.24]","r":"NEC 320.24"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a conduit schedule, the entry '3#12, 1#12 G - 3/4 in. C' typically means:","o":["Three conduits of 12 in. diameter, each with a ground wire installed between them","Three 12 AWG and one 1 AWG conductor installed in a 3/4 in. wide cable tray","Three 12 AWG current-carrying conductors plus one 12 AWG ground, in 3/4 in. conduit","Twelve conductors in a conduit that is 3/4 in. in size"],"a":2,"e":"The first number is the quantity, the number after # is the AWG size, G is the ground, and the last item is the conduit size. [Drawing practice: conduit and wire schedules]","r":"Drawing practice: conduit and wire schedules"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A supplier not in privity with the contractor first furnishes materials to a Florida public job on March 8, 2027. Under F.S. 255.05, what is the last day to deliver the written notice to the contractor that it intends to look to the bond?","o":["April 7, 2027","May 7, 2027","April 22, 2027","June 6, 2027"],"a":2,"e":"March 8, 2027 plus 45 days = April 22, 2027. [F.S. 255.05(2)(a)2.]","r":"F.S. 255.05(2)(a)2."},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Field-installed skeleton tubing readily accessible to other than qualified persons must be:","o":["Left unprotected, because the glass tubing is strong enough","Marked with a danger sign and nothing more is required","Wrapped in insulating tape along its whole length","Fitted with suitable guards or protected by other approved means"],"a":3,"e":"600.41: field-installed skeleton tubing may not be subject to physical damage and must be guarded where readily accessible to unqualified persons. [NEC 600.41]","r":"NEC 600.41"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A repeat failure to secure workers' compensation coverage within what period is treated as a \"knowing act\" under s. 440.105?","o":["5 years","1 year","2 years","10 years"],"a":0,"e":"A repeat violation within five years is treated as a knowing act. [F.S. 440.107]","r":"F.S. 440.107"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A company had 520,000 hours worked in a year and 4 cases with days away, restricted work or job transfer. Using the standard 200,000-hour base, what is its DART rate, rounded to two decimals?","o":["0.77","7.69","1.54","0.65"],"a":2,"e":"DART = 4 x 200,000 / 520,000 = 800,000 / 520,000 = 1.54. [OSHA/BLS incidence rate formula]","r":"OSHA incidence rate formula"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Unusually severe weather that delays critical work, with no fault by either party, is generally","o":["automatically waived by liquidated damages","non-excusable","excusable and non-compensable","compensable because the owner controls weather"],"a":2,"e":"The contractor gets more time but typically no extra money for events beyond both parties' control. FAR 52.249-10 lists unusually severe weather among excusable causes. [Delay classification; FAR 52.249-10]","r":"Delay claims"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 6 ohm resistor is in series with a combination in which (15 ohm + 25 ohm in series) is in parallel with a 60 ohm resistor. Total current on 120 V is","o":["1.13 A","20 A","5 A","4 A"],"a":3,"e":"Series pair = 40 ohms; 40 x 60 / 100 = 24; plus 6 = 30 ohms; I = 120 / 30 = 4 A. [Combination circuits]","r":"Combination circuits"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For 2026, the maximum Section 179 deduction (before phaseout) is","o":["$2,500,000","$1,250,000","$2,560,000","$1,000,000"],"a":2,"e":"Pub 946 (2025) states the 2026 section 179 maximum is $2,560,000, reduced dollar-for-dollar when qualifying property placed in service exceeds $4,090,000. 2025's figures were $2,500,000 and $4,000,000. [IRS Pub 946 (2025)]","r":"IRS Pub 946 (2025)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The low bid is $412,000 and the second bid is $455,000. By what percentage is the low bid below the second bid (rounded to one decimal)?","o":["10.4%","90.5%","5.0%","9.5%"],"a":3,"e":"(455,000 - 412,000) / 455,000 = 0.0945 = 9.5%. Dividing by the low bid gives 10.4%, the wrong base. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor buys a hammer drill for $2,650 on an invoice with only that item. Under the $2,500 de minimis safe harbor, the drill is:","o":["Not eligible for the safe harbor and must be capitalized and depreciated (or expensed under another provision)","Eligible, because hand tools are exempt from the dollar limit","Eligible, because a tool costing under $5,000 always qualifies","Eligible if the price shown includes sales tax and freight charges"],"a":0,"e":"The safe harbor limit is $2,500 per item or invoice, so $2,650 exceeds it. Section 179 or bonus depreciation might still allow a deduction. [Treas. Reg. 1.263(a)-1(f)]","r":"Treas. Reg. 1.263(a)-1(f)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contract allows 90 calendar days from the date of the NTP to reach substantial completion. The contract was signed March 20, and the owner issued the NTP on April 6, 2026. What is the substantial completion deadline?","o":["July 5, 2026","June 18, 2026","July 4, 2026","July 19, 2026"],"a":0,"e":"When the time runs from the NTP, count 90 calendar days from April 6, 2026, which lands on July 5, 2026. Counting from the March 20 signing would produce June 18. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Willfully exaggerating the amount of a claim of lien or including labor or material not furnished can lead to:","o":["A reduced notice period for the owner to contest the claim","A longer deadline for the lienor to enforce the claim of lien","A bonus payment to the contractor for catching the error","A lien that is invalid as a fraudulent lien, with possible liability for damages and fees"],"a":3,"e":"Section 713.31 treats willfully exaggerated or fraudulent liens as unenforceable and exposes the lienor to liability. [F.S. 713.31]","r":"F.S. 713.31"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 95.11, F.S., what is the general limitations period to sue on a WRITTEN contract in Florida?","o":["Two years","Five years","Four years","Seven years"],"a":1,"e":"An action on a written contract falls under s. 95.11(2)(b) and has a five-year limitations period. Four years applies to oral contracts. Seven years is the construction statute of repose, not the contract limitation. [F.S. 95.11(2)(b)]","r":"F.S. 95.11(2)(b)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A nonexempt electrician's helper earns $24.00 per hour and works 46 hours in one workweek with no other pay. What is the gross pay for the week?","o":["$1,176.00","$1,104.00","$1,140.00","$1,248.00"],"a":0,"e":"40 x $24 = $960; 6 overtime hours x ($24 x 1.5 = $36) = $216; total $1,176.00. Paying 46 x $24 = $1,104.00 omits the premium. [FLSA Sec. 7(a)]","r":"FLSA Sec. 7(a)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor submits a bid with no stated expiration date. Before the owner responds, the contractor tells the owner in writing that the bid is withdrawn. The next day the owner says, 'We accept.' Generally:","o":["No contract is formed because the offer was revoked before acceptance","A contract is formed because the owner accepted within 24 hours","A contract is formed only if the owner pays a deposit","A contract is formed because bids cannot be withdrawn"],"a":0,"e":"An offer without a firm-offer promise can ordinarily be revoked any time before it is accepted, so a later acceptance has nothing to accept. Rules for public bids or paid options can differ. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 713.23(1)(d), F.S., an unpaid lienor with a claim on a private payment bond must serve a sworn notice of nonpayment on the contractor, with a copy to the surety, no later than how many days after the lienor's final furnishing?","o":["10 days","45 days","180 days","90 days"],"a":3,"e":"The notice of nonpayment is due no later than 90 days after final furnishing; suit must be filed within one year after the last furnishing. [F.S. 713.23(1)(d)-(e)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(1)(d)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A small job has these activities (finish-to-start, working days): 1 2d (start); 2 3d after 1; 3 4d after 1; 4 5d after 2+3; 5 2d after 4. If work starts on a Monday (day 1) and only Monday through Friday are worked, on what day does the project finish?","o":["Monday of the third week","Thursday of the third week","Wednesday of the third week","Friday of the second week"],"a":2,"e":"The project takes 13 working days. Week 1 covers days 1-5, week 2 days 6-10, and week 3 starts with day 11 (Monday), so day 13 is Wednesday. [CPM; calendar conversion]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A round sign-pole footing is 3 ft in diameter and 8 ft deep. How many cubic yards of concrete should be ordered if 10 percent is added for waste and overdig (round up to the nearest tenth)?","o":["2.1 cu yd","2.4 cu yd","62.3 cu yd","7.0 cu yd"],"a":1,"e":"Volume = pi x 1.5^2 x 8 = 56.5 cu ft = 2.09 cu yd (27 cu ft per cu yd). Adding 10 percent gives 2.30, rounded up to 2.4. 62.3 is in cubic feet, not cubic yards. [Volume of a cylinder; unit conversion]","r":"Volume of a cylinder; unit conversion (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer with a new business has no prior-quarter liability history. Under the deposit rules, which schedule applies in its first year?","o":["Semiweekly, for the entire first year","Quarterly, with no deposits at all required","Next-day, for every payroll it runs","Monthly, unless the $100,000 next-day rule applies"],"a":3,"e":"Prior-quarter liabilities are treated as zero for a new employer, so it is a monthly depositor in its first year unless it accumulates $100,000 in a day. [IRS Topic 757]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A site plan shows a 150-ft trench for a feeder, 2 ft wide and 3 ft deep. How many cubic yards of soil must be excavated, rounded to one decimal place?","o":["900 cu yd","100.0 cu yd","33.3 cu yd","75.0 cu yd"],"a":2,"e":"Volume = 150 x 2 x 3 = 900 cu ft. One cubic yard is 27 cu ft, so 900 / 27 = 33.3 cu yd. Dividing by 9 or 12 uses the wrong conversion. [Quantity takeoff; unit conversion]","r":"Quantity takeoff; unit conversion"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has 5 employees who each earned more than $7,000 in the year. Assuming the full 5.4% credit, what is the total net FUTA tax?","o":["$2,100.00","$1,890.00","$315.00","$210.00"],"a":3,"e":"FUTA wages are 5 x $7,000 = $35,000; net rate 0.6% gives $210.00. $2,100 is 6.0% (no credit) and $1,890 is the 5.4% credit alone. [Form 940 instructions]","r":"Form 940 instructions"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What are the two signal words used on GHS labels?","o":["Caution for severe hazards, and Notice for less severe hazards","Alert for severe hazards, and Hazard for less severe hazards","Danger for severe hazards, and Warning for less severe hazards","Stop for severe hazards, and Proceed for less severe hazards"],"a":2,"e":"'Danger' is used for the more severe hazards and 'Warning' for the less severe. [29 CFR 1910.1200(c)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200(c)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor withholds $75 per paycheck from an employee under a Section 125 cafeteria plan for pre-tax health premiums. Gross pay is $1,500. What are the FICA wages for the paycheck?","o":["$1,500.00","$1,575.00","$1,425.00","$1,350.00"],"a":2,"e":"Qualified Section 125 salary reductions are exempt from FICA and income tax: $1,500 - $75 = $1,425.00. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); Pub 15-B]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a fire alarm riser diagram, a box labeled FACP with lines running to each floor and ending in labeled circles typically represents:","o":["The facility power supply that feeds the distribution panel","The fire alarm control panel, with circuits running to each floor","The firefighters' access control point at the main entrance of the building","The fan control panel for the air handling units and smoke control dampers"],"a":1,"e":"A fire alarm riser shows the FACP, its circuits, remote devices, and annunciators by floor so the installer can see circuit paths and interconnections. [NFPA 72 (2019) documentation, riser diagram practice]","r":"NFPA 72 (2019) documentation, riser diagram practice (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 15 kW load runs 8 hours at 12 cents per kWh. What is the energy cost?","o":["$1.80","$0.96","$14.40","$120.00"],"a":2,"e":"kWh = 15 x 8 = 120; cost = 120 x $0.12 = $14.40. [Energy cost]","r":"Energy cost"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"If the markings for an underground facility disappear during excavation in Florida, the excavator must:","o":["Continue and guess the location","Re-mark the facility with the excavator's own paint","Stop and request remarking before continuing","Call the sheriff's office"],"a":2,"e":"If markings are destroyed or disappear during the work, the excavator must stop and request that facilities be remarked. [F.S. 556.105]","r":"F.S. 556.105"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"If the supply frequency doubles, inductive reactance ______ and capacitive reactance ______.","o":["doubles; is cut in half","stays the same; is cut in half","doubles; doubles","is cut in half; doubles"],"a":0,"e":"XL = 2 pi f L rises with frequency; XC = 1 / (2 pi f C) falls with frequency. [Reactance]","r":"Reactance"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which fire alarm cable marking is rated for installation in a plenum?","o":["FPLR","FPLP","FPL","FPLX"],"a":1,"e":"FPLP is plenum, FPLR is riser, and FPL is general-purpose power-limited fire alarm cable. [NEC 760.179 and 760.154]","r":"NEC 2023 760.154, 760.179"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The smallest copper conductor that can carry 180 A at 75°C (Table 310.16) is:","o":["4/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","2/0 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Copper at 75°C: 2/0 AWG = 175 A (too small), 3/0 AWG = 200 A, which is the smallest size meeting 180 A. [NEC Table 310.16]","r":"NEC Table 310.16"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.505, F.S., what is the scope of an alarm system contractor II?","o":["Fire alarm systems in all occupancies and nothing else","All types of alarm systems other than fire alarm systems","Burglar alarm systems in single-family homes and nothing else","Every alarm system plus unlimited electrical work of any kind"],"a":1,"e":"An alarm system contractor I covers all alarm system types, while a contractor II covers all types other than fire alarm systems. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which board has the authority to regulate electrical contractors under Part II of chapter 489?","o":["The Construction Industry Licensing Board","The Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board","The Florida Building Commission","The Board of Professional Engineers"],"a":1,"e":"Part II defines 'board' as the Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board, which adopts rules in chapter 61G6, F.A.C. The Construction Industry Licensing Board (61G4) licenses Part I contractors. [F.S. 489.505; F.A.C. ch. 61G6]","r":"F.S. 489.505"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A takeoff requires 62 receptacle boxes. The supplier sells them in cartons of 25 and the contractor allows 4% waste/spares. How many cartons should be ordered?","o":["3 cartons","2 cartons","4 cartons","5 cartons"],"a":0,"e":"62 x 1.04 = 64.48 boxes; / 25 = 2.58 cartons, rounded up to 3. [Estimating practice]","r":"Takeoff rounding"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which sequence correctly describes reenergizing equipment after electrical work under 29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(v)?","o":["Tools and grounds removed, employees warned clear, locks and tags removed, then visual check","Remove locks and tags, close the disconnect, then warn the employees to stay clear","Warn employees, close the disconnect, then remove the locks and the tags","Remove tools and grounds only after the circuit has been energized and tested"],"a":0,"e":"The standard sequence is: tools and grounds removed, employees warned to stay clear, locks and tags removed, and a visual determination that all employees are clear. [29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(v)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(v)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.129(2), F.S., in addition to the licensee's penalty, the board may fine a business organization or participating personnel up to how much per violation?","o":["$5,000","$500","$2,500","$10,000"],"a":0,"e":"Where a qualifying agent or financially responsible officer is disciplined for a violation on the organization's project, the board may add a fine up to $5,000 per violation on the organization or on any partner, officer, director, trustee or member who participated or knew or should have known and failed to act. [F.S. 489.129(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(2)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which gas, used alone in a glass tube, produces the classic red-orange glow of a neon sign?","o":["Argon","Neon","Helium","Xenon"],"a":1,"e":"Neon gas glows red-orange. Argon with mercury makes blue light, helium gives a pinkish glow, and xenon gives a lavender-bluish light. [Neon sign gas colors]","r":"Neon sign gas colors"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.131(3)(f), F.S., a local government may refuse or condition a permit for a contractor disciplined for unlicensed contracting within what recent period?","o":["12 months","30 days","5 years","10 years"],"a":0,"e":"Permits may be refused or conditioned for contractors with repeated discipline or discipline for unlicensed contracting within the prior 12 months, subject to s. 489.113(4). [F.S. 489.131(3)(f)]","r":"F.S. 489.131(3)(f)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For one class code, total gross wages including overtime are $94,000. $6,000 of that is the premium portion of overtime (the extra half-time). The rate is 6.4 per $100 and the mod is 1.00. When overtime premium pay is excluded as the rules allow, what is the premium for this class?","o":["$5,632","$6,016","$384","$6,400"],"a":0,"e":"Payroll for premium = 94,000 - 6,000 = 88,000; /100 x 6.4 = $5,632. Including the overtime premium adds $384 of unnecessary premium. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Type TC tray cable may be installed in cable trays where:","o":["It is a residential kitchen circuit","It supplies a swimming pool","Conditions of maintenance and supervision ensure that only qualified persons service the installation (industrial settings and others as permitted)","It is a 15-A bathroom circuit"],"a":2,"e":"Type TC cable is permitted in cable trays and in industrial establishments where conditions of maintenance and supervision ensure only qualified persons service the installation. [NEC 336.10]","r":"NEC 336.10"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A fixed electric baseboard heater load totals 12 kW on a 240-volt single-phase circuit. What is the minimum branch-circuit conductor ampacity?","o":["62.5 A","50 A","57.5 A","75 A"],"a":0,"e":"I = 12,000 / 240 = 50 A. Fixed electric space-heating branch circuits are sized at 125% of the load: 50 x 1.25 = 62.5 A. [NEC 424.3(B)]","r":"NEC 424.3(B)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor uses a 25 percent markup on cost. What gross profit margin on selling price does that produce?","o":["25%","75%","20%","33.3%"],"a":2,"e":"With cost = $100, price = $125; profit $25 / $125 = 20 percent. A markup is measured on cost, a margin on price, so the margin is smaller. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.533(7), F.S., mediation of an eligible complaint: written consent is due within 15 days of notification, the department may suspend action for how many days, and a mediator's report is due within how many days of conclusion?","o":["15 days; 60 days","60 days; 5 days","45 days; 10 days","90 days; 30 days"],"a":2,"e":"The department may suspend action for 45 days, mediation must conclude within the 60-day period, and the mediator's written report is due within 10 days; the licensee pays the costs and may use mediation no more than three times without board approval. [F.S. 489.533(7)]","r":"F.S. 489.533(7)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 443.141, interest on delinquent reemployment tax may not exceed what rate?","o":["1 percent per month","10 percent per year flat","0.5 percent per month","5 percent per quarter"],"a":0,"e":"Interest is computed under s. 213.235 and may not exceed 1 percent per month from the due date. [F.S. 443.141]","r":"F.S. 443.141"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"An RFI (request for information) is a written request, normally from the contractor to the design team, to:","o":["Clarify the contract documents, such as a conflict, omission, or unclear detail","Request payment for the portion of the work that has been completed and inspected","Extend the contract completion date for weather delays","Propose a substitute product in place of the one that the specification names"],"a":0,"e":"RFIs seek clarification of design intent or point out conflicts and omissions. Payment, schedule, and substitutions follow different procedures. [RFI practice (AIA A201)]","r":"RFI practice (AIA A201)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A resistive heater is rated 4,500 W at 240 V. If the supply sags to 216 V, what power does it produce (assume R is constant)?","o":["5,556 W","3,600 W","3,645 W","4,050 W"],"a":2,"e":"Power varies with the square of voltage: 4500 x (216/240)^2 = 3645 W. A 10 percent drop in voltage is a 19 percent drop in power, not 10. [Power formula]","r":"Power formula"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A project network (all finish-to-start, durations in working days): A 3d (start); B 5d after A; C 4d after A; D 6d after B; E 2d after B+C; F 7d after D+E; G 3d after C; H 2d after F+G. What is the earliest start of activity F (project start = day 0)?","o":["Day 8","Day 21","Day 10","Day 14"],"a":3,"e":"F follows D (EF 14) and E (EF 10). Earliest start is the larger finish, day 14. [Forward pass]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A panel schedule lists a 'LP-1A' 20 A, 1-pole breaker for 'Emergency Egress Lights.' The one-line shows LP-1A fed from a normal-power panel. What should the contractor do before roughing in?","o":["Submit an RFI, because egress lighting needs an emergency source and the sheets conflict","Install it exactly as shown, because the panel schedule governs over the Code","Change the label to 'Normal Lights' on the schedule and proceed","Ignore the conflict until the inspection is scheduled and see what the inspector says"],"a":0,"e":"Egress lighting generally needs an emergency source and a method of automatic transfer under the Code, so a conflict like this should be resolved in writing through an RFI before installation. See NEC 700.12 and NEC 700.16. [NEC 700.12, 700.16]","r":"NEC 700.12, 700.16"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"How long before the end of the renewal cycle must the department send renewal notices to active and inactive licensees under s. 489.116(8), F.S.?","o":["At least 10 days","At least 60 days","At least 30 days","At least 12 months"],"a":1,"e":"The department must send renewal notification to active and inactive holders, and pending cancellation notices to delinquent holders, at least 60 days before the cycle ends. [F.S. 489.116(8)]","r":"F.S. 489.116(8)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"NEC 110.2 states that conductors and equipment required or permitted by the Code are acceptable only if:","o":["Approved","Priced below the engineer's estimate","Installed before inspection","Purchased from a licensed supplier"],"a":0,"e":"Section 110.2 requires that conductors and equipment be approved. Buying listed or labeled equipment helps meet this requirement and avoids rejected inspections. [NFPA 70 (2023) 110.2]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 110.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A property owner hires an electrician once for a small home project outside the owner's trade or business. Which Florida reemployment tax exemption is most relevant?","o":["Direct seller exemption","Casual labor not in the course of the employer's trade or business","Employee leasing exemption","Delivery messenger exemption"],"a":1,"e":"Section 443.1216 exempts casual labor not in the course of the employer's trade or business. A contractor hiring labor for its own jobs would be in the course of its business. [F.S. 443.1216]","r":"F.S. 443.1216"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For a construction contract with a public agency, s. 725.06(2), F.S., allows an indemnity requirement only to the extent the loss is caused by:","o":["The negligence of the public agency or its own employees","Any natural event or act of God occurring at the project site","Negligence, recklessness, or intentional misconduct of the indemnifying party","The conduct of any person who happens to be on the project site"],"a":2,"e":"A public agency contract may require indemnity limited to the extent caused by the indemnifying party's (or its employees' or those it uses) negligence, recklessness, or intentional wrongful misconduct. [F.S. 725.06(2)]","r":"F.S. 725.06(2)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Unless a different date is stated, a notice of commencement expires","o":["90 days after it is recorded","1 year after the date it is recorded","On final payment","2 years after it is recorded"],"a":1,"e":"713.13 sets a default expiration of 1 year after recording; when the contract period exceeds a year the notice must state it lasts 1 year plus the additional time. Payments after expiration are improper payments. [F.S. 713.13]","r":"F.S. 713.13"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor claims an excusable delay but its schedule update shows the delayed activity had 12 days of float and was delayed 9 days. What is the likely schedule result?","o":["A 21-day extension of the contract completion date","A 12-day extension of the contract completion date","No completion extension; the delay did not affect the critical path","A 9-day extension of the contract completion date"],"a":2,"e":"A delay is excusable only as to completion if it affects the critical path; here float absorbs the whole delay. The contractor bears the burden of showing the delay affected overall completion. [Delay claims; critical path]","r":"Delay claims"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's customers pay on average 30 days after invoicing, so each month's sales are collected the following month. Sales were $60,000 in January, $80,000 in February and $70,000 in March. How much cash is collected in March?","o":["$70,000","$60,000","$80,000","$75,000"],"a":2,"e":"With a 30-day lag, March collections equal February's sales, 80,000. March sales are not collected until April. [Cash flow forecasting]","r":"Cash flow forecast arithmetic"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A task of 960 labor hours is planned for 10 days with 12 electricians at straight time ($36/hour). To finish in 8 days the contractor keeps the same 12 workers but they work 10-hour days. Company policy (more generous than the FLSA) pays hours beyond 8 in a day at time and one-half. How many labor hours are needed and what is the cost?","o":["960 hours; cost $34,560","960 hours; cost $51,840","1,200 hours; cost $43,200","960 hours; cost $38,016"],"a":3,"e":"Hours worked per worker per day = 10; 12 workers x 10 hours x 8 days = 960 hours (same total). Each worker-day costs 8 x $36 + 2 x $54 = $396. 12 workers x 8 days x $396 = $38,016, versus $34,560 at straight time. Overtime adds $3,456. [Schedule acceleration cost]","r":"Overtime scheduling"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which annual return reports an employer's federal unemployment tax?","o":["Form 941","Form 940","Form W-3","Form 1096"],"a":1,"e":"Form 940 is the Employer's Annual Federal Unemployment (FUTA) Tax Return, due January 31 following the year. Form 941 is the quarterly income tax and FICA return. [IRS Form 940 instructions]","r":"Form 940 instructions"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"From the high-voltage terminal of a transformer, how long can the GTO conductor to the first neon tube electrode be if installed in metal conduit?","o":["3 m (10 ft)","15 m (50 ft)","30 m (100 ft)","6 m (20 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"600.32: up to 6 m (20 ft) in metal conduit or tubing, and 15 m (50 ft) in nonmetallic conduit. [NEC 600.32]","r":"NEC 600.32"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In scheduling terms, a 'lead' is","o":["the float that is available on a critical activity","an overlap letting a successor start before its predecessor finishes","a delay that is inserted between two linked activities","the time a supplier needs to ship material to the site"],"a":1,"e":"A lead is negative lag (for example FS-2), allowing the successor to begin before the predecessor is complete. A lag is a required waiting time. [Precedence diagramming]","r":"Lead and lag (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A concrete-encased electrode (Ufer ground) built from rebar must contain at least how much steel reinforcing bar of 1/2-in. diameter or larger, or bare copper not smaller than 4 AWG, and how thick must the concrete encasement be at minimum?","o":["10 ft of rebar, with 1 in. of concrete","20 ft of rebar or copper, with at least 2 in. of concrete","8 ft of rebar, with 2 in. of concrete","20 ft of rebar, with 6 in. of concrete"],"a":1,"e":"The concrete-encased electrode is at least 20 ft of 1/2-in. rebar, or 20 ft of bare copper of 4 AWG or larger, encased by at least 2 in. of concrete and located within a foundation or footing in direct contact with the earth. [NEC 250.52(A)(3)]","r":"NEC 250.52(A)(3)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A three-phase feeder of 6 AWG copper (26,240 circular mils) carries 30 A over 200 ft one way. Using K = 12.9, the voltage drop is","o":["2.95 V","5.11 V","10.22 V","5.90 V"],"a":1,"e":"VD = 1.732 x K x I x L / CM = 1.732 x 12.9 x 30 x 200 / 26,240 = 5.11 V. Using the factor 2 gives the single-phase value (5.90 V). [Voltage drop]","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A detached garage is fed from the house by a 3-wire feeder (two ungrounded conductors and a neutral) with no equipment grounding conductor, and a ground rod at the garage. Under 250.32(B), is this acceptable for new work?","o":["Yes, if the feeder is in PVC conduit","Yes, the ground rod is all that is required","No, an equipment grounding conductor must be run with the supply conductors and the neutral must not be bonded at the garage","Yes, if the neutral is bonded at the garage panel"],"a":2,"e":"A building supplied by a feeder must have an EGC run with the supply conductors; the grounded conductor is not connected to the EGC or electrodes at the second building. [NEC 250.32(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 250.32(B)(1)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Two jobs: Job X has revenue $600,000 and direct costs $510,000. Job Y has revenue $200,000 and direct costs $150,000. Company overhead for the period is $90,000 and these are the only two jobs. What is the combined net income before taxes and which job has the higher gross margin percentage?","o":["$140,000; Job Y at 25.0%","$50,000; Job Y at 25.0%","$50,000; Job X at 15.0%","$230,000; Job X at 15.0%"],"a":1,"e":"Combined revenue $800,000 - direct costs $660,000 = gross profit $140,000; minus overhead $90,000 = $50,000. Job X margin = $90,000/$600,000 = 15.0%; Job Y = $50,000/$200,000 = 25.0%. [Income statement; profit margin]","r":"Profit margin"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On a floor plan drawn at 1/4 in. = 1 ft-0 in., a conduit run scales 3.875 in. (3-7/8 in.) long on the sheet. What is its horizontal length in the building?","o":["7.75 ft","31 ft","46.5 ft","15.5 ft"],"a":3,"e":"At 1/4 in. per foot each inch represents 4 ft, so 3.875 x 4 = 15.5 ft. Using 2 or 8 ft per inch applies the wrong scale. [Scale calculation]","r":"Scale calculation"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 60-A circuit uses 6 AWG copper conductors that are upsized to 3 AWG for voltage drop. Wire-type EGCs must be increased proportionately by circular mil area. What minimum copper EGC size results? (Circular mils: 10 AWG 10,380; 8 AWG 16,510; 6 AWG 26,240; 3 AWG 52,620.)","o":["6 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG","3 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The table EGC for 60 A is 10 AWG (10,380 cmil). Ratio of conductor increase: 52,620/26,240 = 2.01. Required EGC = 10,380 x 2.01 = 20,815 cmil. 8 AWG (16,510) is too small, so 6 AWG (26,240) is the smallest that works. [NEC 250.122(B)]","r":"NEC 250.122(B)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 212.054, a sale delivered to a county that does not impose a discretionary surtax has which result?","o":["The surtax of the seller's county applies","Only the 6% state tax applies; no surtax is due","The statewide average surtax applies","The sale is exempt"],"a":1,"e":"A sale is not subject to the surtax if the property is delivered to a county without a discretionary surtax. [F.S. 212.054(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 212.054(2)(a)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A 40% gross margin is equivalent to what markup on cost?","o":["40.0%","60.0%","25.0%","66.7%"],"a":3,"e":"Markup = 0.40 / 0.60 = 66.7%. A 40% markup would be only a 28.6% margin. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which of these is required for the sign transformer located in an attic or soffit space?","o":["A battery-powered lamp with a pull chain located at the transformer","A fire-rated door at the point of entry to the attic or soffit space","A louvered vent at least 4 ft wide at the access point to the space","A lighting outlet switched at the usual point of entry, and a 12 in. walkway"],"a":3,"e":"600.21(E) requires at least one lighting outlet controlled by a wall switch, with a point of control at the usual point of entry, and a walkway of at least 300 mm (12 in.). [NEC 600.21(E)]","r":"NEC 600.21(E)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An ohmmeter reading of infinity between two ends of a conductor indicates","o":["a short circuit","a good conductor","an open circuit","a ground fault"],"a":2,"e":"Infinite resistance means there is no continuous path. [Meters]","r":"Meters"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor places $20,000 of equipment in service in March and $30,000 in December (nothing else). Which convention applies?","o":["Mid-quarter, because 60% of the basis is placed in service in the last 3 months","Half-year, because the larger purchase was made in December","Mid-month, because the equipment is business personal property","Neither; depreciation cannot begin until the following January"],"a":0,"e":"30,000/50,000 = 60%, which is more than 40%, so the mid-quarter convention applies to all that year's MACRS property in those classes. [IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)]","r":"IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor pays an uninsured subcontractor $120,000. The carrier assumes 40% of subcontract price is payroll and charges 6.2 per $100 on it (mod 1.00). What additional premium results?","o":["$7,440","$4,464","$297.60","$2,976"],"a":3,"e":"Assumed payroll = 120,000 x 0.40 = 48,000; /100 x 6.2 = $2,976. Charging the rate on the full subcontract price overstates it. [Insurance practice; F.S. 440.10(1)(b)]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The contractor's written bid is $18,400. The owner writes back, 'We accept, but only at $17,000.' What is the legal effect of the owner's reply?","o":["It is an acceptance, and the price becomes $17,000","It is an acceptance, and the price stays $18,400","It has no effect, and the original bid remains open","It is a counteroffer that rejects the original bid"],"a":3,"e":"Under the mirror-image rule, an acceptance that changes a material term such as price is a counteroffer and ends the original offer. The contractor may then accept or reject the $17,000 figure. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor returns $2,400 of undamaged, stock materials to a supplier that charges a 15% restocking fee. What credit does the contractor receive?","o":["$2,760.00","$2,400.00","$2,040.00","$360.00"],"a":2,"e":"Fee = $2,400 x 0.15 = $360; credit = $2,400 - $360 = $2,040. [Returns]","r":"Returns"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An existing 2,000 sq ft dwelling (120/240-V, single-phase) with a 12 kW range, 5 kW dryer, 1.2 kW dishwasher and 4.5 kW water heater, two small-appliance circuits and one laundry circuit, is evaluated with the existing-dwelling method (no air conditioning or electric heat is being added). The lighting is 3 VA/sq ft. What is the calculated load in volt-amperes?","o":["18,080 VA","33,200 VA","16,560 VA","19,480 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Total connected = 6,000 + 3,000 + 1,500 + 12,000 + 5,000 + 1,200 + 4,500 = 33,200 VA. First 8,000 VA at 100% + 40% of 25,200 = 18,080 VA. Using 10,000 as the first block would be the 220.82 figure. [NEC 220.83(A)]","r":"NEC 220.83(A)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"F.S. 489.505(7) describes a certified alarm system contractor's scope as including alarm circuits from the alarm control panel and certain low-voltage wiring. The statute defines low-voltage wiring there as no more than:","o":["24 volts","50 volts","98 volts RMS","120 volts"],"a":2,"e":"The statute describes low-voltage wiring of 98 volts RMS or less for data, proprietary video, central vacuum, or electric locks within the alarm contractor scope. [F.S. 489.505(7)]","r":"F.S. 489.505(7)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Operating income (income from operations) is best described as","o":["net income plus the owner's distributions for the year","revenue minus direct job costs, before overhead","cash received from customers minus cash paid to vendors","gross profit minus overhead, before interest and income taxes"],"a":3,"e":"Operating income measures profit from the core contracting business before financing costs and taxes. [Income statement]","r":"Income statement"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A construction-industry employer must be able to provide proof of coverage. Which is acceptable evidence from a subcontractor on a job?","o":["A current certificate of insurance showing active workers' compensation coverage","A copy of the subcontractor's state contractor license showing active status","The subcontractor's local business tax receipt for the current year","A written note on the invoice that the sub 'carries its own insurance'"],"a":0,"e":"A license or tax receipt does not prove workers' compensation coverage; evidence of a policy or an exemption certificate does. The Division's online proof-of-coverage database can verify it. [F.S. 440.10(1)(c)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(c)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under the Department of Justice's safe harbor, an element in an existing facility that fully complied with the 1991 Standards before March 15, 2012 is:","o":["not required to be modified to meet the 2010 Standards unless it is altered","required to be replaced at the next permit","required to be updated by March 15, 2013","required to meet the 2010 reach range at the next inspection"],"a":0,"e":"The safe harbor says elements complying with the 1991 Standards before March 15, 2012 need not be modified to follow the 2010 Standards, until they are altered. [28 CFR 36.304(d)(2)]","r":"28 CFR 36.304(d)(2)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"If the current through a fixed resistance is doubled, the power dissipated","o":["increases to four times","is cut in half","doubles","is unchanged"],"a":0,"e":"P = I squared x R, so doubling I quadruples P. Doubling would be true only if voltage were constant and R varied. [Power formula]","r":"Power formula"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign controller (flasher) switches a neon transformer with a marked primary current of 4.2 A. What is the minimum current rating required of the controlling switch if it is not rated for inductive loads?","o":["4.2 A, the same as the transformer current","5.25 A, which is 125 percent of the current","8.4 A (twice the transformer current)","12.6 A, which is three times the current"],"a":2,"e":"A switch or flasher controlling a transformer must be rated for inductive loads or have a current rating not less than twice the transformer's current: 2 x 4.2 = 8.4 A. [NEC 600.6(B)]","r":"NEC 600.6(B)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has a $25,000 self-insured retention on its liability policy. A covered claim is $140,000. Assuming the policy limit is high enough, what does the insurer pay?","o":["$140,000","$25,000","$115,000","$165,000"],"a":2,"e":"140,000 - 25,000 = $115,000; the contractor pays the first 25,000. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A delivery arrives with a crushed carton and visibly damaged fixtures. Before signing the carrier's delivery receipt the receiver should:","o":["Refuse the entire delivery without making any notation on the receipt","Wait about a week before reporting the visible damage to the carrier","Note the damage and shortage on the delivery receipt and photograph the goods","Sign the receipt clean to avoid delaying the driver and report problems later"],"a":2,"e":"Notation on the delivery receipt preserves the buyer's claim against the carrier or seller; a clean signature can weaken it. [F.S. 672.513, 672.602]","r":"F.S. 672.513 (inspection)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor on a $250,000 project has a 5 percent retainage clause in a private-sector contract. The first $50,000 progress payment is made. What retainage is held from that payment, and how much is the net check?","o":["$12,500 held; net $37,500","$2,500 held; net $50,000","$5,000 held; net $45,000","$2,500 held; net $47,500"],"a":3,"e":"5 percent of the $50,000 payment is $2,500, leaving $47,500. The $12,500 figure applies the 5 percent to the whole $250,000 contract. Private retainage terms come from the contract, not the public statute. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Fire alarm circuits must be identified at terminal and junction locations in a manner that helps to:","o":["Allow the fire alarm and security systems to share wiring","Prevent unintentional signals on the fire alarm circuits during testing and servicing of other systems","Distinguish PLFA from NPLFA by conductor gauge","Satisfy the 6-foot rule between detectors"],"a":1,"e":"Identification keeps technicians working on other systems from inadvertently initiating fire alarm signals. [NEC 760.30]","r":"NEC 2023 760.30"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The insulation of high-voltage GTO conductors leaving the end of metal conduit must extend beyond the conduit by at least:","o":["1/2 in.","2-1/2 in. (65 mm)","1 in.","6 in."],"a":1,"e":"600.32: the insulation on all conductors must extend not less than 65 mm (2-1/2 in.) beyond the metal conduit or tubing. [NEC 600.32]","r":"NEC 600.32"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A motor draws 600 A across the line at start. With a wye-delta starter, the starting line current is about","o":["600 A","200 A","1,039 A","346 A"],"a":1,"e":"Wye start reduces line current to one third: 600 / 3 = 200 A. 346 would be 600/1.732, which is the phase-voltage reduction only. [Starting methods]","r":"Starting methods"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which statement about the Medicare tax wage base is correct for 2026?","o":["There is no wage base limit for the 1.45% Medicare tax","Medicare tax stops at the Social Security wage base","Medicare tax applies only to the first $200,000","Medicare tax applies only to the first $7,000"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike Social Security, Medicare tax applies to all covered wages with no cap. The $200,000 figure only triggers the extra 0.9% withholding. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); IRS Topic 751]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A receiving-station outdoor antenna of hard-drawn copper has a maximum open span of 100 ft. What is the minimum size conductor per Table 810.16(A)?","o":["14 AWG","19 AWG","12 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"For hard-drawn copper the table lists 19 AWG under 35 ft, 14 AWG for 35 to 150 ft, and 12 AWG over 150 ft. A 100 ft span falls in the middle range. [NEC Table 810.16(A)]","r":"NEC 2023 Table 810.16(A)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor adds a 25% markup to a material cost of $200. What gross margin percentage on the selling price does this produce?","o":["25%","20%","75%","33%"],"a":1,"e":"Price = $200 x 1.25 = $250; margin = ($250 - $200) / $250 = 20%. A 25% markup is not a 25% margin. [Job costing]","r":"Markup vs margin"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the types of drug tests an employer may conduct under the Florida Drug-Free Workplace program (s. 440.102)?","o":["Reasonable-suspicion testing","Random testing of retirees","Pre-employment testing","Follow-up testing after rehabilitation"],"a":1,"e":"Section 440.102 recognizes job applicant, reasonable-suspicion, routine fitness-for-duty and follow-up testing programs for employers. Retirees are not employees. [F.S. 440.102(1), (2)]","r":"F.S. 440.102"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A feeder (not a 100% rated assembly) supplies a 140 A continuous load and a 55 A noncontinuous load. What is the minimum standard rating of the feeder overcurrent device?","o":["300 A","200 A","250 A","225 A"],"a":2,"e":"Minimum OCPD = 55 + 1.25 x 140 = 230 A. 230 A is not a standard rating, and 240.4(B) permits the next higher, 250 A. [NEC 215.3, 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 215.3, 240.6(A)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A specification states 'Provide a 200 A panelboard.' Under the usual definitions in Division 01, what does 'provide' require of the contractor?","o":["Furnish and install it complete and ready for use","Only furnish the panelboard and deliver it to the site for others to install","Only install a panelboard that the owner supplies at the project site","Only coordinate with the trade that installs it"],"a":0,"e":"In typical spec conventions 'furnish' means supply, 'install' means set in place and connect, and 'provide' means furnish and install. [Specification conventions]","r":"Specification conventions"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the scale ratio of a drawing marked 1/8 in. = 1 ft-0 in.?","o":["1:48","1:96","1:24","1:12"],"a":1,"e":"One foot is 12 in., and 12 / (1/8) = 96, so 1/8 in. = 1 ft is 1:96. 1/4 in. = 1 ft is 1:48, and 1/2 in. = 1 ft is 1:24. [Scale ratio conversion]","r":"Scale ratio conversion"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum insulation rating of the individual conductors in network-powered broadband medium-power cables?","o":["150 volts","600 volts","1000 volts","300 volts"],"a":3,"e":"Network-powered broadband medium-power cable conductors must have insulation rated for at least 300 V. [NEC 830.2(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 2023 830.2(A)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In Class I, Division 1 and 2 locations, bonding through locknut-and-bushing contacts of a metal raceway is:","o":["Acceptable when two locknuts are used","Acceptable for trade sizes over 2 in.","Not relied on; bonding jumpers or equivalent are needed","Required only in Division 1 locations"],"a":2,"e":"Locknuts and bushings or double locknuts cannot be depended on for bonding in Class I locations; bonding jumpers with proper fittings are needed. [NEC 501.30(A)]","r":"NEC 501.30(A)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 18 AWG conductors of a Class 1 circuit?","o":["5 amperes","7 amperes","10 amperes","15 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"Class 1 circuit overcurrent protection is limited to 7 A for 18 AWG and 10 A for 16 AWG. 10 A is the 16 AWG value. [NEC 725.43]","r":"NEC 2023 725.43 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A $820,000 contract has a performance bond with a penal sum of $820,000. The owner has paid $500,000 when the contractor defaults. It costs $410,000 to complete the work. How much must the surety pay to cover the shortfall?","o":["$90,000","$410,000","$820,000","$500,000"],"a":0,"e":"Unpaid contract balance = 820,000 - 500,000 = 320,000; completion cost 410,000 exceeds it by $90,000, which is within the penal sum. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What reemployment tax rate applies to a new Florida employer for 2026?","o":["5.4%","0.1%","2.7%","6.0%"],"a":2,"e":"Employers whose records are chargeable with benefits for less than 8 calendar quarters pay the initial rate of 2.7%. [F.S. 443.131(2)(a); Florida DOR]","r":"F.S. 443.131(2)(a)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A project has earned value of $120,000 and actual cost of $150,000. The cost performance index (CPI = earned value / actual cost) is","o":["1.25","-0.25","1.00","0.80"],"a":3,"e":"CPI = 120,000/150,000 = 0.80. A CPI under 1.0 means the job is over budget for the work completed. [Variance analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The permit fee for a single job is paid by the contractor. For costing purposes it is generally","o":["an overhead cost allocated to all jobs by labor hours","a direct job cost, because it is incurred only for that job","a financing cost","a depreciation expense"],"a":1,"e":"A cost that exists only because of one job is traced directly to it. [Direct vs indirect costs]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The standard cost of a connector is $3.10; the contractor paid $3.34 each for 500 units. What is the purchase price variance?","o":["$1,670.00 unfavorable","$120.00 favorable","$120.00 unfavorable","$1,550.00 unfavorable"],"a":2,"e":"Variance = ($3.34 - $3.10) x 500 = $120.00; actual exceeds standard, so unfavorable. [Cost accounting]","r":"Purchase price variance"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A hangar floor is otherwise unclassified. What area is Class I, Division 2 within an aircraft hangar that stores aircraft with fuel in the tanks?","o":["Only the interior of the aircraft","Floor to 18 in. up, plus 5 ft around engines and fuel tanks","Everything within 10 ft above the floor","Only pits, sumps and trenches below floor"],"a":1,"e":"The whole hangar floor to 18 in. above the floor is Class I, Division 2, and the area within 5 ft horizontally of aircraft engines and fuel tanks is also Division 2. Pits below the floor are Division 1. [NEC 513.3(B)(1) and (C)]","r":"NEC 513.3(B)(1) and (C) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in ducts specifically fabricated to transport environmental air, in lengths not exceeding 4 ft, to connect physically adjustable equipment?","o":["Flexible metal conduit","Nonmetallic-sheathed cable","Liquidtight flexible nonmetallic conduit","Type NM-B in a PVC sleeve"],"a":0,"e":"Only specific metal methods are allowed in fabricated ducts; FMC is permitted up to 4 ft for adjustable devices. [NEC 300.22(B) (2026: 300.25(B))]","r":"NEC 2023 300.22(B)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Activity A (conduit rough-in) takes 10 days. Activity B (wire pull) takes 6 days and is linked finish-to-start with a 4-day lag. If A starts on day 0, on what day does B finish?","o":["Day 16","Day 14","Day 20","Day 24"],"a":2,"e":"A finishes day 10. The 4-day lag adds a wait, so B starts day 14 and finishes day 14+6 = 20. [Lag]","r":"Lead and lag"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which extension cord set is acceptable for use with a portable electric tool on a construction site?","o":["A two-wire lamp cord rated for light duty","A three-wire cord rated for junior hard service only","A three-wire cord set rated for hard or extra-hard usage","A flat two-conductor cord with a molded plug"],"a":2,"e":"Extension cord sets used with portable tools and appliances must be of the three-wire type and rated for hard or extra-hard usage. [29 CFR 1926.405(g)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.405(g)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On a site plan, closely spaced curved lines that show the shape of the ground are called:","o":["Contour lines","Section lines","Easement lines","Setback lines"],"a":0,"e":"Contour lines connect points of equal elevation. Steep slopes show closely spaced contours, which matters for trenching and pole placement. [Civil drawing conventions]","r":"Civil drawing conventions"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under the FLSA, a nonexempt employee must be paid overtime at what rate for hours worked over 40 in a workweek?","o":["Double the regular rate","Straight time plus $1.00 per hour","At least one and one-half times the regular rate of pay","One and one-quarter times the regular rate"],"a":2,"e":"The FLSA requires overtime pay of at least 1.5 times the regular rate for hours over 40 in a workweek. [29 U.S.C. 207; DOL Fact Sheet 23]","r":"FLSA 29 U.S.C. 207"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Who may hold a construction lien under chapter 713, F.S.?","o":["Any person who has an unpaid account with the owner of the property","Only persons in the statutory classes, such as contractors, subs, and materialmen","Any lender that has advanced funds toward the improvement","Any building official who inspected the work during construction"],"a":1,"e":"Section 713.01 limits lienors to defined classes (contractor, subcontractor, sub-subcontractor, laborer, materialman, professional lienor). Unlicensed contractors have no lien under s. 713.02. [F.S. 713.01]","r":"F.S. 713.01"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1926.1204(e), in what order are atmospheres in a permit space tested?","o":["Toxic contaminants first, then oxygen, then flammable gases and vapors","Flammable gases first, then toxic contaminants, then oxygen last of all","Oxygen, then flammable gases and vapors, then potential toxic contaminants","Any order is acceptable if the instrument is calibrated within 30 days"],"a":2,"e":"Test oxygen first because combustible gas meters may not work correctly in oxygen-deficient atmospheres, then combustibles, then toxics.","r":"29 CFR 1926.1204(e)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contract includes a $6,500 fixture allowance. The owner selects fixtures costing $8,120. The contract says overages above the allowance carry a 15% markup. What additional amount is added to the contract?","o":["$1,863","$1,620","$9,338","$243"],"a":0,"e":"Overage = 8,120 - 6,500 = 1,620; plus 15% = $1,863. The allowance itself is already in the contract price. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 75 kVA, 208 V three-phase transformer has a 5 percent impedance. Assuming an infinite primary source, the available secondary short-circuit current is about","o":["4,164 A","1,041 A","208 A","7,212 A"],"a":0,"e":"FLA = 75,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 208.2 A; ISC = FLA / 0.05 = 4,164 A. [Short-circuit current]","r":"Short-circuit current"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under NEC 700.12, the branch circuit feeding emergency unit equipment must be:","o":["a dedicated circuit protected by a GFCI breaker for the unit","the same branch circuit serving the normal lighting in the area, connected ahead of local switches","connected after the local lighting switch so it follows the room lights","a Class 2 circuit supplied from the fire alarm control unit"],"a":1,"e":"Unit equipment must monitor the normal lighting circuit, so it is fed from that branch circuit ahead of the local switches. If it were after the switch, turning off the lights would defeat the unit. [NEC 700.12(I)]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 700.12(I) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A listed 12 V Class 2 low-voltage lighting power supply is rated 60 W. How many 10 W lamps can be connected, and what is the maximum output current?","o":["5 lamps; 6 A","6 lamps; 5 A","8 lamps; 7.5 A","12 lamps; 12 A"],"a":1,"e":"60 W / 10 W = 6 lamps. I = P / V = 60 / 12 = 5 A. [NEC Art. 411; arithmetic]","r":"411 power supply (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Crashing activity X shortens it by 3 days at an added cost of $900 per day; crashing activity Y shortens it by 2 days at $600 per day. Both are on the critical path, and no other path becomes critical. The owner wants the project shortened by 3 days at the lowest added cost. What should the contractor do?","o":["Crash Y by 2 days and X by 2 days, for $3,000","Crash X by 3 days, for a total of $2,700","Crash Y by 2 days and X by 1 day, total $2,100","Crash Y by 3 days, for a total of $1,800"],"a":2,"e":"Use the cheapest per-day option first: Y gives 2 days at $600 = $1,200 (its limit); the third day comes from X at $900. Total = $1,200 + $900 = $2,100. Y cannot be crashed by 3 days (limit 2). [Schedule compression / crashing]","r":"Schedule compression (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An unbalanced bid is risky for the owner mainly because","o":["it always lowers the owner's final cost on the project","it breaks the workers' compensation rules for the job","some line items may be priced well above or below true cost, which can shift money toward early work or distort quantity overruns","it makes it impossible to add any alternates to the base bid"],"a":2,"e":"Front-loaded prices pull cash forward and if quantities change the owner can pay more than the apparent low bid implied. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A semiweekly depositor pays wages on a Monday. By which day must the deposit be made, ignoring holidays?","o":["The following Wednesday","The same Monday","The next Tuesday","The following Friday"],"a":3,"e":"Paydays falling on Saturday, Sunday, Monday, or Tuesday require a deposit by the following Friday. [IRS Topic 757]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A scope-of-work description in a contract can be read two reasonable ways. The contractor wrote the contract form. If the dispute goes to court, an ambiguity is most often construed:","o":["Against the party who drafted the language","In favor of the party who drafted the language","In favor of the party who signed first","In favor of the party with the lower bid"],"a":0,"e":"The contra proferentem rule resolves a genuine ambiguity against the drafter, who had the chance to be clear. Careful, specific scope wording and exclusions protect the contractor. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The statutory late-payment interest rate in F.S. 715.12 is","o":["a flat 1% per month in all cases, regardless of the contract's own rate","the federal funds rate in effect on the date payment was due, plus 2%","6% per year unless the owner and contractor agree to a different rate","the s. 55.03 rate plus 12 percent per year, or the contract rate if greater"],"a":3,"e":"715.12(5)-(6) provide the 55.03 rate plus 12% per annum, or the contract rate if greater, but not both. [F.S. 715.12(5),(6)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Using the first break-even example (fixed costs $288,000, variable costs 62% of revenue), what revenue is needed to earn a target profit of $60,000?","o":["$817,895","$132,240","$915,789","$561,290"],"a":2,"e":"Required revenue = (fixed + target profit)/CM ratio = (288,000 + 60,000)/0.38 = 915,789. Adding profit after dividing understates the revenue needed. [Break-even analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The limited energy systems specialty electrical contractor under Rule 61G6-7.001, F.A.C., may work on circuits up to what voltage?","o":["24 volts RMS","50 volts RMS","98 volts RMS","120 volts RMS"],"a":2,"e":"The limited energy specialty covers wiring, fixtures, thermostats, apparatus, raceways, conduit and fiber optics up to 98 volts RMS, excluding work that is part of an alarm system. [F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(4)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(4)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A PoE switch has a total power budget of 370 W. Each camera is allocated 30 W (802.3at Type 2). What is the maximum number of cameras the switch can power?","o":["12","13","24","10"],"a":0,"e":"370 / 30 = 12.33; only whole cameras can be powered, so 12. Using 15.4 W gives 24, which is the 802.3af count. [IEEE 802.3at; arithmetic]","r":"PoE budget (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Conduit fittings are priced at $38.50 per C (per 100 pieces). What is the cost of 650 pieces?","o":["$25,025.00","$231.00","$2,502.50","$250.25"],"a":3,"e":"650/100 = 6.5 C; 6.5 x $38.50 = $250.25. 'C' means hundred. [Purchasing math]","r":"Unit pricing"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"If the trencher is only used 600 hours instead of 1,000, the hourly rate needed to recover the same annual costs becomes","o":["$17.32","$18.03","$12.99","$24.53"],"a":3,"e":"Fixed ownership cost 10,820 / 600 = 18.03 plus operating 6.50 = 24.53. Lower utilization raises the ownership cost per hour. [Equipment cost recovery]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A separate communications ground rod is driven. What bonding connection is required to the power grounding electrode system?","o":["A 14 AWG copper jumper","A bonding jumper not smaller than 6 AWG copper","No connection; the two electrodes must remain isolated","A 10 AWG aluminum jumper"],"a":1,"e":"Separate electrodes must be bonded together with a jumper of at least 6 AWG copper to limit potential differences. [NEC 800.100(D)]","r":"NEC 2023 800.100(D)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A construction exemption notice becomes effective on the earlier of the date the department issues the certificate or how many days after the department receives the application?","o":["5 days","60 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":2,"e":"The notice takes effect on the earlier of issuance or 30 days after receipt. [F.S. 440.05]","r":"F.S. 440.05"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"In a rated wall, steel outlet boxes on opposite sides are normally separated by what horizontal distance unless listed or protected with putty pads?","o":["24 in.","12 in.","6 in.","36 in."],"a":0,"e":"Membrane penetrations by boxes in fire-rated walls must be separated horizontally by at least 24 in. unless listed or protected with approved putty pads or other means. [IBC 714; FBC Building 714.4.2]","r":"IBC/FBC-B 714.4.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A subcontractor schedules these activities (all finish-to-start, working days): S 4d (start); T 6d after S; U 3d after S; V 8d after T; W 5d after U+T; X 4d after W; Y 6d after V+X. What is the minimum project duration?","o":["19 days","36 days","24 days","25 days"],"a":3,"e":"S 0-4; T 4-10; U 4-7; V 10-18; W 10-15; X 15-19; Y 19-25. Y waits for X (finish 19), the later of its two predecessors. [CPM forward pass]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A claims-made policy's annual premium is $14,800. The insurer prices a tail endorsement at 175% of the annual premium. What is the cost of the tail?","o":["$11,100","$40,700","$25,900","$14,800"],"a":2,"e":"14,800 x 1.75 = $25,900. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A bidder prices a job but fails to acknowledge a required addendum on the bid form. The owner's most likely response is to","o":["add the addendum cost to the bid automatically","award the job at the original price","treat the bid as nonresponsive","hold the bid until the bidder pays a fee"],"a":2,"e":"Acknowledging all addenda shows the bidder priced the revised documents; a bid that omits the acknowledgment may be rejected as nonresponsive. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 6-pole, 60 Hz induction motor runs with 4 percent slip. What is the rotor speed?","o":["1,728 rpm","1,152 rpm","1,196 rpm","1,248 rpm"],"a":1,"e":"Ns = 1,200 rpm; Nr = 1,200 x (1 - 0.04) = 1152 rpm. [Slip]","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A surety reviewing a work-in-progress schedule sees a contractor with large overbillings on most jobs. The most likely concern is","o":["Too much retainage receivable has built up on the jobs","The contractor is reporting on the accrual method","Earned revenue is being understated on every job","Cash from billed jobs may be funding other jobs (job borrowing)"],"a":3,"e":"Overbillings larger than the jobs' remaining gross profit can indicate cash being borrowed from one job to pay for another, which can snowball into a cash shortage. [WIP schedule; Withum]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Nonresidential real property (such as a commercial office building) is depreciated under MACRS over how many years, using straight-line?","o":["27.5 years","20 years","39 years","31.5 years"],"a":2,"e":"Nonresidential real property is the 39-year class and uses straight line with the mid-month convention. 27.5 years is for residential rental property. [IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)]","r":"IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A grounding diagram shows the grounding electrode conductor running to a single driven ground rod only, while the service conductors are 500 kcmil copper (Table 250.66 would normally call for 1/0 AWG copper). What is the minimum copper GEC size needed for that connection to the rod?","o":["6 AWG copper","1/0 AWG copper","4 AWG copper","8 AWG copper"],"a":0,"e":"NEC 250.66(A) says that where the GEC connects only to a rod, pipe, or plate electrode, the portion that is the sole connection to it is not required to be larger than 6 AWG copper. Concrete-encased electrodes in 250.66(B) are limited to 4 AWG. [NEC 250.66(A)]","r":"NEC 250.66(A)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C. lists required components of the financial statements for FDOT qualification. Which is NOT among them?","o":["An income statement","A balance sheet with notes","A schedule of employee pay rates","A statement of cash flows"],"a":2,"e":"The rule requires a balance sheet, income statement, retained earnings or equity statement, cash flow statement, and notes; payroll rate schedules are not part of the statement set. [Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C."},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A county awards a $2,150,000 public building contract. Under F.S. 255.05, what is the general amount of the payment and performance bond the contractor must record before starting work?","o":["$2,150,000","$215,000","$1,075,000","$250,000"],"a":0,"e":"The bond amount generally equals the contract price. The exceptions (for example contracts over $250 million) do not apply here. [F.S. 255.05(1)(g)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)(g)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The estimated total cost rises from $420,000 to $480,000 with costs to date still $168,000 and no change in contract price. Revenue earned to date under cost-to-cost becomes","o":["$224,000","$168,000","$256,000","$196,000"],"a":3,"e":"New % complete = 168,000/480,000 = 35%; revenue = 560,000 x 0.35 = 196,000, which is 28,000 less than before, so profit to date is reduced. [IRS Construction ATG]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Sequencing electrical work on a multi-story building commonly places which activity first?","o":["Final inspection, before the rough-in is done","Fixture installation, before the ceiling grid","Finish trim, before the drywall is hung","Underground and slab sleeves, before the concrete pour"],"a":3,"e":"Embedded conduit and sleeves must be in place before the pour; trim and fixtures follow wall and ceiling work. [Construction sequencing]","r":"Coordination with trades (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The department finds that 4 workers were misclassified as independent contractors. What is the maximum penalty under F.S. 440.10(1)(f) for this misclassification (not counting any other penalties)?","o":["$5,000","$4,000","$40,000","$20,000"],"a":3,"e":"Up to $5,000 for each misclassified employee: 4 x 5,000 = $20,000. [F.S. 440.10(1)(f)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(f)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Two parallel sets of conductors run in separate raceways to a switchboard from an 800-A overcurrent device. What is the minimum copper EGC required in each raceway?","o":["3 AWG","2 AWG","1/0 AWG","4/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"When conductors are run in parallel in multiple raceways, an EGC sized per Table 250.122 for the OCPD rating (800 A, 1/0 AWG copper) is installed in each raceway. [NEC 250.122(F)]","r":"NEC 250.122(F)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"FAR 52.236-15 (Schedules for Construction Contracts) requires the contractor to submit what type of schedule within five days after work commences, unless the contracting officer sets a different period?","o":["A schedule of values that has no dates at all","A weekly manpower histogram with no dates shown","A cost-loaded network with full float calculations","A practicable schedule on a progress chart of suitable scale"],"a":3,"e":"The clause calls for a practicable schedule showing the order of work and planned start and finish dates for key features, drawn on a progress chart, submitted within five days after the work commences. [FAR 52.236-15]","r":"FAR 52.236-15"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Resistors of 6, 9 and 15 ohms are in series across 120 V. What is the total power?","o":["4,960 W","480 W","960 W","432,000 W"],"a":1,"e":"Rt = 30 ohms; P = E squared / Rt = 14,400 / 30 = 480 W. Summing individual E squared / R values is the parallel method. [Series circuits]","r":"Series circuits"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Where an accessible route is less than 60 in. wide, ADAAG 4.3.4 requires passing spaces of what size at intervals of what maximum length?","o":["60 in. by 60 in., at intervals not exceeding 400 ft","30 in. by 48 in., at intervals not exceeding 200 ft","60 in. by 60 in., at intervals not exceeding 200 ft","48 in. by 48 in., at intervals not exceeding 100 ft"],"a":2,"e":"A 60 in. by 60 in. passing space is needed at intervals of no more than 200 ft where the route is narrower than 60 in. The 30 in. by 48 in. dimension is the single wheelchair footprint, not a passing space. [ADAAG 4.3.4]","r":"ADAAG 4.3.4"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.515, an applicant for an electrical contractor certificate must furnish evidence of","o":["three consecutive years of audited financial statements","a minimum net worth of $1 million in company assets","ownership of at least one commercial work vehicle","financial responsibility, credit, and business reputation"],"a":3,"e":"Section 489.515 requires evidence of financial responsibility, credit, and business reputation for the applicant or the business organization to be qualified; the dollar standards are set by board rule. [F.S. 489.515]","r":"F.S. 489.515"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2), who verifies that a circuit is deenergized before work, and how?","o":["A qualified person, using test equipment and considering induced voltage and backfeed","Any employee, by confirming the breaker handle is in the off position","The electric utility, by phone confirmation that the shutdown has been made","The owner's representative, by visual inspection of the panel schedule on site"],"a":0,"e":"A qualified person must operate the equipment controls or otherwise verify it cannot restart, then use test equipment to test the circuit elements and parts, considering induced voltage and stored energy. [29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(iv)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(iv)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A nonexempt worker earns $22.00 per hour, works 45 hours in the week, and receives a $180.00 nondiscretionary production bonus for the week. What is total gross pay for the week, including overtime?","o":["$1,235.00","$1,225.00","$1,365.00","$1,170.00"],"a":0,"e":"The bonus must be included in the regular rate: ($990 + $180) / 45 = $26.00. Straight time covers all 45 hours ($1,170); the extra half-time premium is 5 x $13.00 = $65, for $1,235.00. Leaving the bonus out of the rate yields $1,225.00. [29 CFR 778.208-.209; Fact Sheet 56A]","r":"29 CFR 778.209"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Paying suppliers later (extending days payable) while keeping the same customer collection time will generally","o":["worsen short-term cash flow with no downsides for the contractor's suppliers","improve short-term cash flow but may cost early-pay discounts or strain supplier relationships","reduce the contractor's days sales outstanding by the same number of days","convert accounts payable into retained earnings on the balance sheet"],"a":1,"e":"Delaying payments retains cash longer, but suppliers may withdraw credit terms or discounts, and lienors may be left unpaid. [Cash cycle]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Laminated iron cores are used in AC transformers and motors mainly to","o":["increase the number of turns","raise the frequency","reduce eddy-current losses","reduce copper resistance"],"a":2,"e":"Thin insulated laminations break up circulating eddy currents, which would otherwise cause heating. [Core losses]","r":"Core losses"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A lump sum contract is accompanied by a proposal listing material costs, and the invoice splits the lump sum into materials and labor. How does the Department treat it?","o":["It becomes a (3)(d) retail sale plus installation contract","It becomes a time and materials contract","It becomes exempt from sales and use tax","It remains a lump sum contract; the breakdown only explains the price"],"a":3,"e":"Proposals and invoices that break down a lump sum price only explain or justify the price and do not convert the contract. [Rule 12A-1.051(2)(h)4, F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(2)(h)4"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A recessed incandescent luminaire with a completely enclosed light source is installed in a clothes closet. What is the minimum clearance to the nearest point of the closet storage space?","o":["12 in.","3 in.","6 in.","18 in."],"a":2,"e":"A recessed incandescent luminaire with a completely enclosed lamp requires 6 in. from the storage space; a surface-mounted enclosed incandescent luminaire requires 12 in. [NEC 410.16(C)(2)]","r":"NEC 410.16(C)(2)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a mobile home panelboard, what must be done with the main bonding jumper?","o":["Installed in the home's panelboard","Doubled with a second jumper","Removed, since the neutral is isolated from the EGC","Bonded to the chassis"],"a":2,"e":"In a mobile home the neutral is isolated from the equipment grounds in the home, so there is no neutral-to-ground bond in the home's panel. [NEC 550.16(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 550.16(A)(2)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Cycle counting is a method in which a business:","o":["Counts only the items that have been reported as damaged or lost","Counts a rotating portion of inventory throughout the year instead of one annual count","Counts the entire stockroom once a year at the fiscal year end","Counts inventory only when an outside auditor requests a count"],"a":1,"e":"Cycle counts spot errors earlier and focus on high-value items. [Inventory management]","r":"Cycle counting"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"How long does an employer have to produce requested business records after receiving the Division's written request under s. 440.107?","o":["10 days","30 days","21 days","72 hours"],"a":2,"e":"The employer must produce the records within 21 days of receipt of the written request, or the Division may issue a stop-work order. [F.S. 440.107]","r":"F.S. 440.107"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An angle pull box has three raceway entries in the same row on one wall: two 2-in. conduits and one 1-1/2 in. conduit, with conductors 2/0 AWG. The conductors leave through the wall at a right angle. What is the minimum distance from those entries to the opposite wall?","o":["9 in.","15-1/2 in.","12 in.","18 in."],"a":1,"e":"Six times the largest raceway (6 x 2 = 12 in.) plus the sum of the other entries in the same row (2 + 1.5 = 3.5 in.) = 15.5 in. [NEC 314.28(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 314.28(A)(2)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor who is profitable but short of cash most likely has a problem with","o":["too little revenue being recorded on the income statement for the year","timing, such as slow collections or paying costs before customers pay","a lack of depreciation expense being deducted from the company's profit","an overstated owner's equity balance on the year-end balance sheet"],"a":1,"e":"Profit is measured on an accrual basis; cash shortages usually come from the gap between paying labor and materials and being paid. [Cash flow basics]","r":"Cash flow basics"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under NEC Article 725, a Class 1 power-limited circuit is limited to what voltage and power?","o":["60 volts and 1,000 volt-amperes","30 volts and 100 volt-amperes","600 volts and 1,000 volt-amperes","30 volts and 1,000 volt-amperes"],"a":3,"e":"Class 1 power-limited circuits are limited to 30 V and 1,000 VA. Class 1 remote-control and signaling circuits may go to 600 V. [NEC 725.41(A)]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 725.41 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor reports operating income of $210,000 on revenue of $2,800,000. If interest expense is $18,000 and income taxes are $42,000, what is net income after taxes?","o":["$186,000","$150,000","$192,000","$168,000"],"a":1,"e":"$210,000 - $18,000 - $42,000 = $150,000. Leaving out either deduction overstates net income. [Income statement]","r":"Income statement"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 600-A switch enclosure has feed-through conductors passing through its wiring space. For the splices and taps permitted in the wiring space, what are the maximum fill limits of the cross-sectional area at any cross section?","o":["Conductors 40% of the space, and conductors plus splices and taps 75% of the space","Conductors 53%, splices and taps 60%","Conductors 20%, splices and taps 40%","Conductors 75%, splices and taps 90%"],"a":0,"e":"The wiring space of switch and overcurrent device enclosures may hold feed-through, spliced or tapped conductors if the conductors fill no more than 40% of the cross section and conductors plus splices and taps no more than 75%. [NEC 312.8]","r":"NEC 312.8"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A homeowner and a contractor made only an oral agreement for a kitchen rewire. Under Florida's general limitations statute, how long does the homeowner have to sue for breach of that oral contract?","o":["Five years","One year","Ten years","Four years"],"a":3,"e":"Florida allows five years for written contracts and four years for oral contracts under s. 95.11. The shorter period for oral deals is one more reason to put the agreement in writing. [F.S. 95.11(3)(j)]","r":"F.S. 95.11(3)(j)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which areas does ADAAG 4.28.1 name as places where signal appliances are required at a minimum?","o":["Mechanical rooms, attics, and crawl spaces","Stairwells and elevator hoistways only","Private offices and storage closets only","Restrooms, general-use areas, hallways, and lobbies"],"a":3,"e":"At minimum, signal appliances go in restrooms, general-use areas (meeting rooms), hallways, lobbies, and other common areas. Mechanical rooms are not listed. [ADAAG 4.28.1]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.1"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An electrician earns $30 per hour. Payroll taxes are 10.2% of wages, workers' compensation is 9.4%, general liability allocated to labor is 2.1%, and fringe benefits cost $3.20 per hour. What is the burdened hourly labor cost?","o":["$36.26","$33.20","$39.71","$36.51"],"a":2,"e":"Burden percentages = 0.217; 30.0 x 1.217 = 36.51; plus 3.2 fringes = $39.71. Leaving out the fringe cost or the insurance percentages understates labor. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A synchronous motor that is overexcited will operate at","o":["a lagging power factor","unity only","a leading power factor","zero torque"],"a":2,"e":"Overexcited synchronous motors supply reactive power to the system, which is why they are used for power factor correction. [Synchronous motors]","r":"Synchronous motors"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A bidder who submits a bid without acknowledging an addendum that adds 3 panelboards should expect:","o":["The bid will be accepted at the original price, since the added scope is only a suggestion","The addendum will be ignored when the bids are compared at the bid opening","The bid price will be reduced by the cost of the addendum","The bid may be rejected as nonresponsive, and the added scope binds the awarded contract"],"a":3,"e":"Addenda become part of the contract documents, and most bid forms require acknowledgment. Failure to acknowledge can disqualify a bid. [Bidding procedures]","r":"Bidding procedures"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A hermetic refrigerant motor-compressor has a nameplate rated-load current of 26 A. What is the minimum ampere rating of its disconnecting means, based on current?","o":["29.9 A","26.0 A","32.5 A","40.0 A"],"a":0,"e":"The disconnect must be rated at least 115% of the nameplate rated-load current: 26 x 1.15 = 29.9 A. The 32.5 A answer applies 125%, the conductor rule. [NEC 440.12(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 440.12(A)(1)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor bills labor at the employee's hourly wage plus overhead only, ignoring labor burden. The likely result is","o":["over-recovery of labor costs, which increases profit on every job","no effect at all, because the burden is paid by the employee","a lower overhead rate for the company in the following year","under-recovery of labor costs, reducing or eliminating profit"],"a":3,"e":"Employer taxes and insurance are a real cost of each paid hour; leaving them out of the bill rate understates cost. [Labor burden]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which record best identifies each major piece of equipment for maintenance and insurance?","o":["A list of vendor telephone numbers sorted by supplier name","A list of the job addresses on which each crew has worked","A payroll summary showing hours worked by each employee","An equipment register with serial numbers, purchase dates and costs"],"a":3,"e":"An asset register supports depreciation, insurance, recovery of stolen property and maintenance scheduling. [Equipment control]","r":"Equipment control"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A trench in Type C soil is 8 feet deep and is protected by sloping at the maximum allowed angle. What is the minimum horizontal distance from the bottom edge to the top edge on each side? (Type C max slope is 1.5 horizontal to 1 vertical.)","o":["8 feet","12 feet","10 feet","16 feet"],"a":1,"e":"At 1.5H:1V, 8 ft depth x 1.5 = 12 ft of horizontal run on each side. [29 CFR 1926.652(b)(1); Appendix B]","r":"29 CFR 1926.652(b)(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An employee is injured on a job site and receives emergency room treatment only, then goes home. What is the reporting status?","o":["Reportable within 8 hours of the incident, because it is a work injury","Not reportable under 1904.39, since ER treatment alone is not in-patient hospitalization","Reportable within 24 hours, because the worker visited a hospital","Reportable within 72 hours, if the worker misses the next workday"],"a":1,"e":"Only formal admission to a hospital or clinic for care or treatment counts as in-patient hospitalization; ER treatment alone does not. It may still be recordable on the OSHA 300 log. [29 CFR 1904.39]","r":"29 CFR 1904.39"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the Table A minimum clearance for a crane working near a 69 kV power line (over 50 kV to 200 kV)?","o":["10 feet","15 feet","20 feet","25 feet"],"a":1,"e":"Table A: up to 50 kV = 10 ft; over 50 to 200 kV = 15 ft; over 200 to 350 kV = 20 ft; over 350 to 500 kV = 25 ft. [29 CFR 1926.1408 Table A]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1408 Table A"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which scheduling presentation shows activities as horizontal bars against a calendar, typically without explicit logic links?","o":["Critical path matrix","Precedence network","Arrow diagram","Bar chart (Gantt chart)"],"a":3,"e":"A bar chart shows start, finish, and duration against time and is easy to read, but without links it does not show how a delay in one activity affects others. A CPM network shows the logic. [Scheduling methods]","r":"Bar charts vs networks (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Lenz's law states that an induced current","o":["aids the change that produced it","opposes the change that produces it","is always direct current","is independent of the magnetic field"],"a":1,"e":"The direction of the induced EMF produces a field that opposes the change in flux. [Lenz's law]","r":"Lenz's law"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Completed operations coverage in a CGL policy responds to","o":["theft of tools and equipment from a job site","delays in completing the job by the contract date","bodily injury or property damage caused by the contractor's finished work after it has been turned over","injuries to the contractor's employees during construction"],"a":2,"e":"Defective finished work can cause damage long after the job ends; completed operations coverage addresses that exposure. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Before seeking payment from the recovery fund, s. 489.141(1), F.S., requires the claimant to first:","o":["Pay a $500 filing fee with the board before any other step","Obtain the contractor's written consent to the fund claim","Exhaust the limits of available bonds, sureties, warranties, and insurance","Wait five years after the judgment before filing a claim"],"a":2,"e":"A claimant may seek recovery only after making a claim and exhausting any available bond, cash bond, surety, guarantee, warranty, letter of credit or insurance policy. [F.S. 489.141(1)]","r":"F.S. 489.141(1)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"An addendum is a written change to the bidding documents that is issued:","o":["After the final inspection of the completed building","After the certificate of occupancy has been issued","During the bidding period, before the contract is awarded","Only when the building department requires an addendum for a permit"],"a":2,"e":"Addenda modify the drawings, specifications, or bidding conditions before bids are due and become part of the contract documents. Changes after award are made by change orders or similar. [Bidding procedures]","r":"Bidding procedures"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A business holds only a Florida registration as an Alarm System Contractor II. Which job is outside what that license class covers?","o":["Installing a fire alarm system in a new commercial building","Installing a burglar alarm system in a retail store","Installing a robbery hold-up alarm in a bank branch","Servicing a burglar alarm system in a residence"],"a":0,"e":"Under 489.505 a Type I alarm system contractor may work on all types of alarm systems, while a Type II contractor may work on all types except fire. Burglar and robbery systems are within Type II. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505(2)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Commercial general liability (CGL) insurance for a contractor primarily protects against claims for","o":["injuries to the contractor's own employees on the job","loss of the contractor's tools and equipment to theft","the contractor's failure to finish a job on time","bodily injury or property damage suffered by third parties"],"a":3,"e":"CGL responds to third-party bodily injury and property damage. Employee injuries are handled by workers' compensation; tools are an inland marine item. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A general contractor receives a $74,000 electrical subcontractor quote and adds a 6% markup for coordination and bonding. What is the GC's price for that item?","o":["$78,723.40","$4,440","$78,440","$82,880"],"a":2,"e":"74,000 x 1.06 = $78,440. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Nonconductive optical fiber cables are permitted to occupy the same raceway or cable tray as which conductors?","o":["None of those; fiber must always be in its own raceway","Only Class 2 and Class 3 conductors","Only medium-voltage conductors over 1000 volts","Electric light, power, Class 1, and non-power-limited fire alarm conductors operating at 1000 volts or less"],"a":3,"e":"Nonconductive fiber carries no current, so it may share a raceway or tray with light, power, Class 1, and NPLFA circuits of 1000 V or less. [NEC 770.133 (2026: 722.164)]","r":"NEC 2023 770.133(A)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A drawing note specifies a 'NEMA 14-50R' receptacle for a range or EV charger. What are the characteristics of this configuration?","o":["125 V, 50 A, three-wire with a ground and no separate neutral conductor","250 V, 50 A, three-wire with two hots and a ground but no neutral","125/250 V, 50 A, four-wire: two hots, a neutral, and a ground","480 V three-phase, 50 A, four-wire"],"a":2,"e":"NEMA 14-50R is a four-wire 125/250 V, 50 A configuration. The 10-50 is a three-wire older range type. [NEMA WD 6 configurations]","r":"NEMA WD 6 configurations (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor wants a 15 percent gross margin on selling price. What markup on cost is required (rounded to two decimals)?","o":["17.65%","15.00%","12.75%","18.50%"],"a":0,"e":"Markup = margin / (1 - margin) = 0.15 / 0.85 = 0.1765, or 17.65%. Using 15% as the markup yields only a 13.04% margin. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The AHU-1 nameplate shows MCA 28.5 A and MOCP 45 A. Using the 75 degrees C column of Table 310.16 for copper, which is the smallest conductor that meets the MCA (12 AWG = 25 A, 10 AWG = 35 A, 8 AWG = 50 A)?","o":["12 AWG copper","8 AWG copper","14 AWG copper","10 AWG copper"],"a":3,"e":"The conductor must have ampacity of at least 28.5 A: 12 AWG (25 A) is too small, and 10 AWG (35 A) is the smallest that meets it. 8 AWG would also work but is larger than needed. [NEC 440.35, Table 310.16]","r":"NEC 440.35, Table 310.16"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 448.095, a licensed electrical contractor engages a sole proprietor electrician as a true independent contractor for a specified portion of work. For E-Verify purposes, that independent contractor is:","o":["An employee who must be verified within 3 days","An employee only after 90 days","Subject to a double I-9","Not an 'employee' to be run through E-Verify"],"a":3,"e":"Independent contractors as defined by federal law who perform a specified portion of labor are excluded from the E-Verify employee definition. Misclassification remains a risk. [F.S. 448.095]","r":"F.S. 448.095"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under the ADA, an employer who has not yet made a job offer to an applicant may NOT:","o":["Require a valid driver license","Ask disability-related questions or require a medical examination","Ask the applicant to describe how they would perform the job","Ask whether the applicant can perform the essential job functions"],"a":1,"e":"Before a conditional offer, employers may not ask disability-related questions or require medical exams, but can ask whether the applicant can perform essential functions with or without accommodation. [29 CFR 1630.13(a)]","r":"29 CFR 1630.13(a)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A schedule of values line for feeders and gear is $120,000. At this month's cut-off the line is 70% complete, and $60,000 was billed on it in previous applications. How much is billed on this line in the current application (before retainage)?","o":["$24,000","$84,000","$60,000","$42,000"],"a":0,"e":"Work completed to date = 120,000 x 70% = 84,000; less previously billed 60,000 = 24,000. Billing the full completed amount again would double-bill the earlier work. [Progress billing practice]","r":"Contract billing arithmetic (progress billing practice)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A drawing note says 'NOT TO SCALE' (NTS) under a riser diagram. The riser shows two panels with a feeder between them. How do you determine the feeder length for pricing?","o":["Measure the riser with an architect's scale and apply the title block scale","Assume 100 ft of feeder for each floor in the building and add a waste factor","Use the dimensioned floor plans or a field measurement, since an NTS drawing cannot be scaled","Use the longest dimension that appears on any sheet in the electrical set"],"a":2,"e":"NTS means the drawing is diagrammatic, so scaling it gives false lengths. Use dimensioned plans, elevations, or field verification. [Drawing practice: NTS]","r":"Drawing practice: NTS"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.129(11), F.S., the Department may offer binding arbitration of a legally sufficient consumer complaint. Within how many days after notification may the parties give written consent?","o":["15 days","5 days","30 days","60 days"],"a":0,"e":"The parties may consent in writing within 15 days after notification; the department may then suspend action for 45 days. [F.S. 489.129(11)(b)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(11)(b)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which atmospheric reading makes a confined space atmosphere hazardous by oxygen content?","o":["Oxygen below 21 percent or above 25 percent of the air by volume","Oxygen below 16 percent only, with no upper limit specified","Oxygen above 25 percent only, with no lower limit specified","Oxygen below 19.5 percent or above 23.5 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Hazardous atmospheres include oxygen concentrations below 19.5 percent or above 23.5 percent, flammable gas above 10 percent of the LFL, and toxics above PEL. [29 CFR 1926.1202]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1202 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has earnings before interest and taxes of $186,000 and interest expense of $31,000. What is the times-interest-earned ratio?","o":["6.0","5.0","0.9","0.17"],"a":0,"e":"Times interest earned = EBIT / interest = $186,000 / $31,000 = 6.0. [Coverage ratio]","r":"Interest coverage (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-10.008, F.A.C., which of the following complaints is eligible for mediation?","o":["Practicing on a revoked license","A missing license number in an advertisement","Obtaining a license by fraud","Failing to carry workers' compensation insurance"],"a":1,"e":"Eligible areas are a missing license number (s. 489.533(1)(q)), non-completion because a final inspection was not done when the permit was properly obtained (1)(r), and failing to pay a judgment within 18 months (1)(m)4. [F.A.C. 61G6-10.008]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-10.008"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under ADAAG 4.5.2, a change in level on an accessible route that is between 1/4 in. and 1/2 in. high must be:","o":["separated from the route by a handrail","beveled with a slope no steeper than 1:2","finished with a ramp no steeper than 1:12","left vertical, because only 1/2 in. or more matters"],"a":1,"e":"Changes in level up to 1/4 in. may be vertical, from 1/4 in. to 1/2 in. must be beveled at no steeper than 1:2, and anything over 1/2 in. requires a ramp, elevator, or lift. [ADAAG 4.5.2, 4.3.8]","r":"ADAAG 4.5.2"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Marina signs must warn of which hazard near shore power pedestals?","o":["High-pressure fuel lines","Electric current may be present in the water","Fire hazard from fuel vapors","Radio frequency radiation"],"a":1,"e":"Marinas must post a warning that electric current may be present in the water and that swimming is dangerous near the dock. [NEC 555.10]","r":"NEC 555.10 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a 208Y/120 V system, what is the phase (line-to-neutral) voltage?","o":["208 V","120 V","240 V","360 V"],"a":1,"e":"Ep = EL / 1.732 = 208 / 1.732 = 120 V. [Wye connection]","r":"Wye connection"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum sound level that an audible alarm signal may produce under ADAAG 4.28.2?","o":["100 dBA","120 dBA","90 dBA","140 dBA"],"a":1,"e":"ADAAG 4.28.2 states that sound levels for alarm signals shall not exceed 120 dBA. NFPA 72 sets a lower public-mode ceiling of 110 dBA, so both limits matter. [ADAAG 4.28.2]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.2"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor's balance sheet shows total equity of $418,000 and total assets of $1,260,000. What are total liabilities?","o":["$1,678,000","$842,000","$630,000","$418,000"],"a":1,"e":"Liabilities = Assets - Equity = $1,260,000 - $418,000 = $842,000. Adding them is the common slip. [Accounting equation; SBA financial statement guidance]","r":"Accounting equation"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The party who owns the float in a construction schedule is usually determined by","o":["the weather service reports for the area of the job","the building inspector who is assigned to the project","whichever contractor submitted the lowest price bid","the contract; without terms, it is often a shared project resource"],"a":3,"e":"Many contracts state that float belongs to the project; the first party to use it may consume it. [Float ownership]","r":"Critical path method (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum bending radius for Type MC cable with interlocked-type armor?","o":["10 times the external diameter","5 times the external diameter","7 times the external diameter of the metallic sheath","12 times the external diameter"],"a":2,"e":"Interlocked-type armor or corrugated-sheath MC cable has a minimum bending radius of seven times the external diameter of the sheath. Smooth-sheath cable needs 10 times (up to 3/4 in. diameter). [NEC 330.24(B)]","r":"NEC 330.24(B)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In a construction defect case, the 2023 amendment to s. 95.11(3)(b), F.S., says each building in a multi-building project is treated as:","o":["Part of one improvement whose period starts when the last building is done","Exempt from the statute of repose when the buildings share one permit","A separate improvement for determining when its repose period starts","Subject to a repose period of 15 years instead of seven years"],"a":2,"e":"For repose purposes the starting date is determined building by building, so an early building's period can start before the last building is complete. [F.S. 95.11(3)(b)]","r":"F.S. 95.11(3)(b)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The IRS says that the officers of a corporation who perform services for it are generally treated as what for employment tax purposes?","o":["Independent contractors","Employees","Statutory nonemployees","Exempt from FICA"],"a":1,"e":"A corporate officer who performs services and receives pay is an employee of the corporation for employment tax purposes (Pub 15). The exemption is for qualifying statutory exceptions only. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A $8,000 invoice on terms 2/10, net 30 is partly returned: $1,000 of goods are sent back and a credit memo is issued before payment. If the buyer pays inside the discount window, what is paid?","o":["$7,840.00","$6,860.00","$7,000.00","$6,840.00"],"a":1,"e":"The discount applies to the net purchase after the return: ($8,000 - $1,000) x 0.98 = $6,860.00. Taking the discount on the full invoice and then subtracting the return would pay $6,840.00. [Terms 2/10 net 30]","r":"Vendor terms"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A Florida contractor under contract with a public employer must do what under s. 448.095?","o":["Use E-Verify only if the contractor has more than 100 employees","Use E-Verify only for workers in the electrical trade, not for office staff","Register with and use E-Verify, and require its subcontractors to provide an affidavit of E-Verify use","Use only Form I-9, since E-Verify applies only to the public employer itself"],"a":2,"e":"Contractors with public employers must use E-Verify for their employees and obtain subcontractor affidavits. [F.S. 448.095]","r":"F.S. 448.095"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A builder's risk policy has an 80% coinsurance clause. The completed value is $800,000, so $640,000 of coverage is required, but only $480,000 is carried. A covered loss is $100,000 with a $5,000 deductible. The insurer pays (carried / required) x loss, less the deductible. What is the payment?","o":["$95,000","$55,000","$70,000","$75,000"],"a":2,"e":"Ratio = 480,000/640,000 = 0.75; x 100,000 = 75,000; less 5,000 = $70,000. Ignoring the penalty pays $95,000. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"What is the usual difference between substantial completion and final completion?","o":["Final completion occurs before the owner can use the work","They are always the same date under every standard contract","Substantial completion requires that final payment be made","Final completion follows the punch list and closeout documents"],"a":3,"e":"Substantial completion allows use; final completion means the contract work, including punch-list items and closeout paperwork, is complete and final payment can be made. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A retail store has 60 single receptacle outlets, each on its own yoke, and the actual connected load is unknown. Calculating each at 180 VA and applying the demand factors for non-dwelling receptacle loads, what is the receptacle demand load?","o":["10,400 VA","10,800 VA","5,400 VA","10,000 VA"],"a":0,"e":"60 x 180 = 10,800 VA. Table 220.44 gives the first 10 kVA at 100% and the remainder at 50%: 10,000 + 0.5 x 800 = 10,400 VA. [NEC 220.14(I), 220.44]","r":"NEC 220.14(I), 220.44"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"On a one-line diagram, an automatic transfer switch (ATS) is shown between the normal service and a generator, feeding a life-safety panel. Which check does the engineer's one-line help the contractor confirm?","o":["That the generator neutral must be bonded to the frame of every downstream panelboard","That the ATS may supply only motor loads, and no lighting or receptacle circuits","That the life-safety loads are separate from other loads and fed through the transfer equipment","That the emergency feeder may share a raceway with normal feeders"],"a":2,"e":"The one-line shows source arrangement and which loads sit behind each transfer switch. Emergency circuits must be kept independent of normal wiring under NEC 700.10(B), so a shared raceway is not allowed. [NEC 700.10(B), 700.5]","r":"NEC 700.10(B), 700.5"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The neon secondary conductor must be supported not more than ______ from the electrode connection to the tubing.","o":["1 in.","6 in. (150 mm)","3 in.","12 in."],"a":1,"e":"600.42: neon secondary conductors are supported not more than 150 mm (6 in.) from the electrode connection to the tubing. [NEC 600.42]","r":"NEC 600.42"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Outlets supplying pool pump motors on 15- or 20-amp, single-phase branch circuits rated 120 through 240 volts must have:","o":["AFCI protection","No special protection","A disconnect at the pool edge only","GFCI protection"],"a":3,"e":"These pump circuits need GFCI protection whether cord-and-plug or hardwired. [NEC 680.21(C)]","r":"NEC 680.21(C)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-10.002, F.A.C., the penalty range for failing to display the registration or certification number is:","o":["$500-$1,000 for a first offense, and $1,000-$2,500 for a second offense","$1,000-$2,500 for a first offense, and revocation for a second","A reprimand only for the first offense, and $100 for a second","$100-$250 for a first offense, and $250-$400 for a second offense"],"a":3,"e":"The guideline for violating s. 489.533(1)(q) is $100-$250 first, $250-$400 second, and reprimand plus $500-$1,000 for a third offense. [F.A.C. 61G6-10.002(o)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-10.002(o)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum thickness of sheet steel used for a sign enclosure under 600.8?","o":["0.51 mm (0.020 in.)","0.81 mm (0.032 in.)","0.41 mm (0.016 in.)","1.5 mm (0.060 in.)"],"a":2,"e":"Sheet steel must be at least 0.41 mm (0.016 in.) thick; sheet copper or aluminum must be at least 0.51 mm (0.020 in.). Enclosures must be metal or listed. [NEC 600.8(C)]","r":"NEC 600.8(C)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.103, F.S. (Part I), casual, minor or inconsequential work is exempt from licensure when the aggregate contract price is less than:","o":["$250, regardless of how the work is advertised to customers","$2,500, if not part of a larger job and the person does not advertise","$25,000, if the work is advertised as a contracting service","$75,000, if the work is divided into several smaller contracts"],"a":1,"e":"The minor-work exemption applies below $2,500 in aggregate and is lost if the work is part of a larger operation, divided to evade the part, or advertised. [F.S. 489.103]","r":"F.S. 489.103"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A bid bond is written for 5% of the bid amount. What is the penal sum on a $1,350,000 bid?","o":["$675,000","$67,500","$202,500","$1,350,000"],"a":1,"e":"1,350,000 x 0.05 = $67,500. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What does the Class I Zone marking 'AEx' signify on equipment?","o":["Built to US standards for the Zone system","Approved for Class II dust locations","Permitted only in Zone 2 locations","Approved for outdoor use only"],"a":0,"e":"AEx identifies equipment for Class I, Zone locations built to the recognized US standards. [NEC 505.9(C)]","r":"NEC 505.9(C)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Listing labels and markings that identify the input voltage and current rating of a sign must be:","o":["Visible after installation, or in a place visible before servicing","Hidden under the paint so that the sign looks clean from the street","Printed on the invoice that is kept by the sign owner","Only on the shipping carton the sign arrived in at the site"],"a":0,"e":"600.4: listing labels and markings with input voltage and current must be visible after installation and permanently applied in a location visible prior to servicing. [NEC 600.4]","r":"NEC 600.4 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A box has four 14 AWG and two 12 AWG insulated conductors spliced or terminated in it, one receptacle on a 12 AWG yoke connection, internal cable clamps, and 12 AWG equipment grounding conductors. What is the minimum box volume?","o":["23.75 cu in.","17.75 cu in.","21.5 cu in.","19.25 cu in."],"a":2,"e":"Conductors: 4 x 2.00 + 2 x 2.25 = 12.5; device 2 x 2.25 = 4.50; clamps (one, based on the largest conductor) 2.25; EGCs (one, largest) 2.25. Total = 21.5 cu in. [NEC 314.16(B)]","r":"NEC 314.16(B)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An electrician is paid on a cost-plus-percentage contract: actual job costs of $86,500 plus a 12 percent fee. What is the total owed to the contractor?","o":["$86,512","$10,380","$96,880","$98,300"],"a":2,"e":"Fee = $86,500 x 0.12 = $10,380. Total = $86,500 + $10,380 = $96,880. A fee of $10,380 alone omits the costs. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.503(6)(a), F.S., what is prima facie evidence that an owner-builder's construction was for sale or lease?","o":["Selling a single structure after owning it for ten years","Painting the structure shortly before listing it for sale","Hiring a licensed subcontractor to perform part of the work","Selling or leasing more than one such structure within a year of completion"],"a":3,"e":"The statute treats the sale or lease of more than one such structure within one year after completion as prima facie evidence the construction was for sale or lease, which defeats the exemption. [F.S. 489.503(6)(a)]","r":"F.S. 489.503(6)(a)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A portable generator connected to a panel with a double-ended cord and no transfer equipment is:","o":["Not permitted; transfer equipment is required","Allowed if the generator is under 3 kW","Allowed if used outdoors","Allowed with a 2-pole breaker"],"a":0,"e":"Optional standby systems require transfer equipment that prevents inadvertent interconnection of sources. [NEC 702.5]","r":"NEC 702.5"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under the FLSA, how long must an employer keep payroll records such as names, hours, rates and wages paid?","o":["1 year","3 years","7 years","2 years"],"a":1,"e":"Payroll records must be kept 3 years; supplementary records such as time cards and wage-rate tables for 2 years. [29 CFR 516.5-.6]","r":"29 CFR 516.5"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"After a cited person requests a hearing under s. 489.127(5), F.S., the enforcement board or special magistrate may impose a civil penalty of not more than:","o":["$2,500 per day for each violation","$500 total per case","$2,000 total per case","$10,000 total per case"],"a":0,"e":"The penalty must be at least the citation amount and may be up to $2,500 per day per violation, considering gravity, corrective action and prior violations; each day of a willful, knowing violation is a separate offense. [F.S. 489.127(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.127(5)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor expects annual overhead of $216,000 and direct labor cost of $480,000. What is the overhead rate as a percentage of direct labor cost?","o":["222%","45%","31.0%","-55%"],"a":1,"e":"Rate = overhead / allocation base = 216,000 / 480,000 = 45% of direct labor. Inverting gives 222%. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An inherently limited Class 2 ac power source has Vmax of 18 volts (within the 0 through 20 V range). What is the maximum nameplate rating in VA per Table 11(A), where the limit is 5.0 x Vmax?","o":["100 VA","90 VA","144 VA","250 VA"],"a":1,"e":"5.0 x 18 V = 90 VA. 100 VA applies to the higher voltage ranges; 144 VA would multiply Vmax by the 8.0 A Imax, which is a current limit, not the nameplate rating. [NEC Ch. 9 Table 11(A)]","r":"NEC Ch. 9 Table 11(A)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A project network (all finish-to-start, durations in working days): A 3d (start); B 5d after A; C 4d after A; D 6d after B; E 2d after B+C; F 7d after D+E; G 3d after C; H 2d after F+G. What is the free float of activity C?","o":["11 days","5 days","0 days","4 days"],"a":2,"e":"C finishes on day 7. Its successor G can start on day 7 (its early start), so C can slip 0 days without delaying any successor's early start. Free float = min(ES of successors) - EF = 7 - 7 = 0, even though total float is 5. [Free float vs total float]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The cost of a project-specific bond or permit is direct, but liability insurance covering all work is","o":["direct, because insurance is a requirement for every job","a revenue item","indirect, because it supports all jobs and must be allocated","a financing cost"],"a":2,"e":"Insurance that covers the whole operation is allocated to jobs through the overhead rate; job-specific bonds can be traced to the job. [Direct vs indirect costs]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under 2010 Standards 307.2, objects with leading edges between 27 in. and 80 in. above the finish floor may protrude no more than how far into circulation paths?","o":["4 in.","10 in.","2 in.","6 in."],"a":0,"e":"Section 307.2 limits protrusion to 4 in. for wall-mounted objects with leading edges between 27 in. and 80 in. Vertical clearance in 307.4 is 80 in. minimum. [2010 Standards 307.2, 307.4]","r":"2010 Std 307.2"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which cost is FIXED in the short run?","o":["Monthly rent on the contractor's shop","Hourly wages of crew on the job","Wire purchased for a job","Fuel used by service vans"],"a":0,"e":"Fixed costs stay constant within a range of activity; rent does not change with job volume in the short run. [Direct vs indirect costs]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A bonding conductor is run outside the sign body or raceway. What protection is required?","o":["It must be bare and exposed","It must be stapled every 12 in.","It must be protected from physical damage","It must be run only in rigid nonmetallic conduit"],"a":2,"e":"Bonding conductors installed outside of a sign or raceway must be protected from physical damage. [NEC 600.7(B)]","r":"NEC 600.7(B)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An equipment schedule lists for AHU-1: 'MCA 28.5 A, MOCP 45 A, 208/3/60.' What does MCA stand for, and what is its use?","o":["Maximum circuit amps, used to size the breaker","Minimum circuit ampacity, used to size the supply conductors","Motor control amperes, used to size the starter","Minimum conductor area, used to size the raceway"],"a":1,"e":"MCA is the minimum circuit ampacity marked on the nameplate and used to size branch-circuit conductors (see 440.35 for hermetic motor-compressors), while MOCP is the maximum overcurrent protection. 208/3/60 means 208 V, three-phase, 60 Hz. [NEC 440.4(B), 440.35]","r":"NEC 440.4(B), 440.35"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor will stop work on a residential job because the owner has not paid. To avoid an abandonment finding, the contractor should:","o":["Simply stop work and wait 30 days before telling the owner","Remove the permit sign from the job site and lock the panel","Send written notice stating the reason, following the contract's notice terms","Refund the full contract price to the owner immediately"],"a":2,"e":"Abandonment under s. 489.129(1)(j) turns on the absence of just cause or proper notice to the owner, including the reason. A written notice that follows the contract protects the contractor. [F.S. 489.129(1)(j)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(1)(j)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is listed as a prohibited basis for employment discrimination under the Florida Civil Rights Act (s. 760.10) but not under Title VII?","o":["Marital status","Religion","Color","National origin"],"a":0,"e":"Florida's Act includes race, color, religion, sex, pregnancy, national origin, age, handicap and marital status. Marital status is not a Title VII category. [F.S. 760.10(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 760.10(1)(a)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A PO was issued for 20 spools of wire at $182.00 each. Receiving reports show 16 spools received. The vendor invoices all 20. Under the three-way match, what amount should accounts payable approve now?","o":["$3,640.00","$2,912.00","$728.00","$3,276.00"],"a":1,"e":"Pay only for goods received and matching the PO: 16 x $182.00 = $2,912.00. The 4 missing spools ($728.00) are held until delivered or credited. [Three-way match]","r":"Three-way match"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A nonexempt helper earning $22.50 per hour works 52 hours in a workweek with no bonuses. What is the gross pay?","o":["$1,305.00","$1,170.00","$1,462.50","$1,350.00"],"a":0,"e":"Regular: 40 x $22.50 = $900.00. Overtime: 12 x $33.75 = $405.00. Total = $1,305.00. [29 U.S.C. 207; DOL Fact Sheet 23]","r":"FLSA 29 U.S.C. 207"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NFPA 101 requires emergency lighting to turn on automatically upon:","o":["manual activation from the fire alarm control panel only","activation of a sprinkler waterflow switch only","failure of the utility supply only, not a tripped breaker","any interruption of normal lighting, including accidental opening of a switch"],"a":3,"e":"Emergency lighting must come on automatically in case of any interruption of normal lighting, such as utility failure, a tripped breaker, or accidental switching. It cannot depend on a manual act. [NFPA 101, 7.9.2]","r":"NFPA 101 7.9.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under Chapter 9, Table 1, what percent fill is permitted for exactly two conductors in a conduit?","o":["40%","31%","25%","53%"],"a":1,"e":"Two conductors are limited to 31%, one conductor to 53%, and three or more conductors to 40%. [NEC Chapter 9, Table 1]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Table 1"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"In NFPA 70E concepts, what is the arc flash boundary?","o":["Distance at which an unprotected person would get a second-degree burn (1.2 cal/cm2)","Distance at which a worker may safely touch the equipment bare-handed","Distance in front of a panel marked by tape on the floor","Maximum length of the insulated glove cuff that is worn at the work"],"a":0,"e":"The arc flash boundary is where incident energy equals 1.2 cal/cm2, the onset of a second-degree burn; inside it, arc-rated PPE is required.","r":"NFPA 70E concepts (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the maximum pressure at which compressed air may be used for cleaning under 29 CFR 1926.302(b)?","o":["Less than 10 psi, with chip guarding and a face shield","Less than 60 psi, with chip guarding and eye protection","Less than 100 psi, with chip guarding and hearing protection","Less than 30 psi, with chip guarding and PPE"],"a":3,"e":"Compressed air may be used for cleaning only if reduced to less than 30 psi and used with effective chip guarding and PPE.","r":"29 CFR 1926.302(b)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What type of insulated conductor is specified for neon secondary-circuit wiring over 1000 volts?","o":["Listed GTO cable rated 5, 10, or 15 kV, 105 degrees C, 18 AWG minimum","Type THHN conductors rated 600 V and 90 degrees C","Type NM-B cable with an equipment grounding conductor included","Type SO flexible cord rated 600 V and 90 degrees C, 18 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"600.32: conductors must be insulated, listed as gas tube sign and ignition cable (GTO), rated 5, 10, or 15 kV, not smaller than 18 AWG, with a minimum 105 degrees C rating. [NEC 600.32]","r":"NEC 600.32"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For Division I contracts on or after July 1, 2024, the aggregate limit payable from the fund per licensee under s. 489.143(6), F.S., is:","o":["$2 million per Division I licensee","$250,000 per Division I licensee","$500,000 per Division I licensee","$10 million per Division I licensee"],"a":0,"e":"The per-licensee aggregate caps are $2 million for Division I and $600,000 for Division II for contracts on or after July 1, 2024, effective January 1, 2025. [F.S. 489.143(6)]","r":"F.S. 489.143(6)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A plan shows a written dimension of 24 ft-0 in. between two panelboards, but when you measure with a scale rule it comes out to about 22 ft. Which value should you rely on for ordering conduit, and what should you do?","o":["Use the scaled dimension, because drawings are always exactly to scale","Use the average of the two and note it on the shop drawing","Use the shorter length so that less material is purchased","Use the written dimension and submit an RFI if the difference affects the work"],"a":3,"e":"Figured (written) dimensions take precedence over scaled dimensions under typical contract language, and scale distortion in copies is common. If the difference matters, document it with an RFI. [Typical order-of-precedence clause on dimensions]","r":"Typical order-of-precedence clause on dimensions"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The interior of an underground raceway is considered:","o":["A wet location","A hazardous location","A dry location","A damp location only"],"a":0,"e":"The interior of enclosures or raceways installed underground is considered a wet location, so conductors must be listed for wet locations. [NEC 300.5(B)]","r":"NEC 300.5(B)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Overhead is $216,000 per year. If direct labor hours fall to 7,200, the new overhead rate per hour is","o":["$22.50","$15.00","$30.00","$12.86"],"a":2,"e":"216,000 / 7,200 = 30.00, up from 22.50 at 9,600 hours. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On a plan drawn at 1/4 inch = 1 foot, a branch circuit run measures 9 1/2 inches. How many feet of conduit does the run represent (before adding drops and offsets)?","o":["38 feet","76 feet","114 feet","19 feet"],"a":0,"e":"At 1/4 inch per foot, 1 inch = 4 feet; 9.5 x 4 = 38 feet. [Estimating practice, takeoff]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On December 31 an electrical contractor reports total assets of $840,000 and total liabilities of $515,000. Using the accounting equation, what is owner's equity?","o":["$325,000","$515,000","$1,355,000","$420,000"],"a":0,"e":"Assets = Liabilities + Equity, so equity = $840,000 - $515,000 = $325,000. Adding the two figures gives a number larger than assets, which cannot be equity. [Accounting equation; SBA financial statement guidance]","r":"Accounting equation"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 212.054, how does Florida's discretionary sales surtax apply to the sale of a single item of tangible personal property?","o":["It applies only to the first $5,000 of the item's sales price","It applies to the full price of the item","It applies only to items under $5,000","It never applies to tangible personal property"],"a":0,"e":"The sales amount above $5,000 on any single item of tangible personal property is not subject to the surtax. [F.S. 212.054(2)(b)1]","r":"F.S. 212.054(2)(b)1"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the purpose of a duct smoke detector on an air-handling system?","o":["To provide early warning for the occupants of a room","To replace area smoke detectors in the space","To measure airflow velocity","To detect smoke in the air stream and shut down fans or close dampers to limit smoke spread"],"a":3,"e":"Duct detectors protect the HVAC system and prevent distribution of smoke; they are not substitutes for open-area detection. [NFPA 72 (2019) 17.7.5, NFPA 90A]","r":"NFPA 72 17.7.5 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A fire alarm panel draws 0.3 A in standby and 2.4 A in alarm. The system is tone-only (24 h standby plus 5 min alarm). What is the minimum required battery capacity before any derating?","o":["7.4 Ah","7.2 Ah","9.6 Ah","19.2 Ah"],"a":0,"e":"Standby: 0.3 A x 24 h = 7.2 Ah. Alarm: 2.4 A x (5/60) h = 0.2 Ah. Total 7.4 Ah. Using 5 hours or adding currents directly gives the larger wrong values. [NFPA 72 (2019) 10.6.7.2.1; arithmetic]","r":"NFPA 72 10.6.7 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A cable is installed parallel to a wood stud, concealed in the wall cavity, with the nearest edge of the cable 3/4 in. from the face of the stud. What is required?","o":["Protection by a steel plate, sleeve or equivalent at least 1/16 in. thick, because it is closer than 1-1/4 in. to the nearest edge","Nothing, because the cable is concealed","A bushing at each end of the run","Only a cable tie every 4-1/2 ft"],"a":0,"e":"A cable or raceway running parallel to framing must be at least 1-1/4 in. from the nearest edge of the framing, or protected by a steel plate, sleeve or equivalent at least 1/16 in. thick. [NEC 300.4(D)]","r":"NEC 300.4(D)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For a county project estimated at more than $500,000, F.S. 255.0525 requires the invitation to bid to be advertised at least once in a county newspaper no later than how many days before bid opening?","o":["30 days","7 days","14 days","21 days"],"a":0,"e":"Over $200,000 the minimum is 21 days; over $500,000 it is 30 days. Both also require advertising at least 5 days before any scheduled prebid conference. [F.S. 255.0525(2)]","r":"F.S. 255.0525(2)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A neon power supply produces 12,000 volts open-circuit between its two output terminals, and its secondary is arranged so that one output terminal is 12,000 V above ground (not center-tapped). Does it meet the voltage limits of 600.23(C)?","o":["Yes, because 12,000 V is below the 15,000 V maximum","Yes, because the secondary current is limited to 300 mA","No, because an output terminal is at more than 7,500 volts to ground","No, because 12,000 V exceeds the 10,000 V limit for dry locations"],"a":2,"e":"Two limits apply: not over 15,000 V total and not over 7,500 V from any output terminal to ground. A center-tapped 12,000 V unit gives 6,000 V to ground and complies, but this arrangement gives 12,000 V to ground and does not. [NEC 600.23(C)]","r":"NEC 600.23(C)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Are auxiliary grounding electrodes permitted for signs under Article 600?","o":["No, they are prohibited for all electric signs","Yes, and they must meet 250.54 but do not replace the EGC","Yes, but only for signs that use neon tubing","Yes, and they take the place of the equipment grounding conductor"],"a":1,"e":"600.7(A) permits auxiliary grounding electrodes and refers to 250.54, but they do not replace the EGC, because the earth is not an effective fault-current path (250.4(A)(5)). [NEC 600.7(A), 250.54]","r":"NEC 600.7(A), 250.54"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Actual annual revenue is $900,000 and break-even revenue is $757,895. What is the margin of safety in dollars?","o":["$900,000","$757,895","$1,657,895","$142,105"],"a":3,"e":"Margin of safety = actual - break-even = 900,000 - 757,895 = 142,105 (15.8% of actual sales). [Break-even analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"29 CFR 1910.269 applies primarily to which type of work?","o":["Interior wiring of commercial office buildings by licensed contractors","Residential service upgrades performed by electrical contractors","Fire alarm and low-voltage installations in schools and hospitals","Operation and maintenance of power generation, transmission and distribution systems"],"a":3,"e":"1910.269 covers power generation, transmission and distribution (utilities, line work); building wiring falls under Subpart S and construction rules.","r":"29 CFR 1910.269(a)(1)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"An argon-mercury tube with no phosphor coating produces what color of light?","o":["Red","Green","Yellow","Blue (violet-blue)"],"a":3,"e":"Argon with a drop of mercury glows blue, and phosphor coatings can convert the ultraviolet to other colors such as green, white, or pink. [Neon sign gas colors]","r":"Neon sign gas colors"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"For the same series string (22, 33 and 47 ohms) on 120 V, what is the voltage drop across the 47 ohm resistor?","o":["25.9 V","55.3 V","102.5 V","40.0 V"],"a":1,"e":"I = 120 / 102 = 1.1765 A; drop = I x 47 = 55.3 V. The largest resistor takes the largest share. [Series circuits]","r":"Series circuits"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which action is prohibited when using a powder-actuated fastening tool?","o":["Wearing the eye and face protection recommended by the manufacturer","Using the shield recommended by the manufacturer for the application","Keeping hands clear of the open barrel end while the tool is loaded","Firing into very hard or brittle material such as cast iron or glass block"],"a":3,"e":"Fasteners must not be driven into very hard or brittle materials; operators must be trained and may not point the tool at anyone. [29 CFR 1926.302(e)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.302(e)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An RL circuit has L = 2 H and R = 10 ohms. What is the time constant?","o":["5.0 s","12 s","0.2 s","20 s"],"a":2,"e":"tau = L / R = 2 / 10 = 0.2 s. [Time constants]","r":"Time constants"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Earth is relied upon by itself to clear a ground fault on a 120/240-V system with no equipment grounding conductor. Which NEC principle does this violate?","o":["The earth may not be considered an effective ground-fault current path","The neutral must be bonded at every panel","Equipment must be grounded only at the load end","Grounding electrodes must be at least 10 ft long"],"a":0,"e":"An effective ground-fault current path must be a low-impedance circuit capable of carrying the maximum fault current, and the earth is not to be considered such a path. [NEC 250.4(A)(5)]","r":"NEC 250.4(A)(5)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Per Table 312.6(A), what is the minimum wire-bending space for one 250 kcmil copper conductor per terminal, where the conductors do not enter or leave through the wall opposite the terminals?","o":["6 in.","4-1/2 in.","8 in.","3 in."],"a":1,"e":"Table 312.6(A) lists 4-1/2 in. for a single 250 kcmil conductor per terminal; 400–500 kcmil needs 6 in. and 600–700 kcmil needs 8 in. [NEC Table 312.6(A)]","r":"NEC Table 312.6(A)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On a one-line diagram, the standard symbol of a circle with a smaller circle inside, or two overlapping circles, in series with a power line most commonly represents a:","o":["Generator","Capacitor bank","Motor starter","Transformer"],"a":3,"e":"Two coupled or overlapping circles represent the primary and secondary windings of a transformer on a one-line (ANSI/IEEE 315-style). A generator is a circle with a G, and a motor starter is usually a rectangle with a contact symbol. [ANSI/IEEE Std 315 symbols]","r":"ANSI/IEEE Std 315 symbols (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"As an alternative to a listed IBT, communications bonding conductors may terminate on a copper or aluminum busbar not smaller than:","o":["6 mm thick x 50 mm wide (1/4 in. x 2 in.)","3 mm thick x 25 mm wide (1/8 in. x 1 in.)","6 mm thick x 25 mm wide (1/4 in. x 1 in.)","13 mm thick x 100 mm wide (1/2 in. x 4 in.)"],"a":0,"e":"The busbar must be at least 1/4 x 2 in., accessible, securely fastened, with room for at least three terminations. [NEC 250.94(B)]","r":"NEC 2023 250.94(B)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A raceway contains two circuits. Circuit A is protected at 40 A and circuit B at 100 A. What is the minimum copper EGC if one EGC serves both circuits?","o":["10 AWG","8 AWG","12 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"A single EGC may be installed for multiple circuits in the same raceway and is sized from Table 250.122 for the largest OCPD (100 A), so 8 AWG copper. [NEC 250.122(C)]","r":"NEC 250.122(C)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the maximum 5.4% Florida rate, how much reemployment tax is due on an employee who earned $41,000 in 2026?","o":["$2,214.00","$189.00","$7.00","$378.00"],"a":3,"e":"Only the first $7,000 is taxable: $7,000 x 5.4% = $378.00. $2,214 would incorrectly apply the rate to the full wages. [Florida DOR Reemployment Tax Rate page]","r":"Florida DOR rate page"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"According to Subpart K construction rules, what minimum clear working distance is required in front of electrical equipment rated 151 to 600 volts to ground when exposed live parts are on both sides of the workspace (operator between)?","o":["3 feet","4 feet","3 feet 6 inches","6 feet"],"a":1,"e":"Table K-1 gives 3 ft (condition a), 3.5 ft (condition b) and 4 ft (condition c, live parts on both sides) for 151-600 V. [29 CFR 1926.403(i)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.403(i)(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which SDS section would an electrician check to find the recommended PPE for a cleaner or solvent?","o":["Section 2, hazard(s) identification","Section 7, handling and storage","Section 10, stability and reactivity","Section 8, exposure controls/personal protection"],"a":3,"e":"Section 8 covers exposure limits, engineering controls and recommended PPE; Section 2 gives hazard classification and label elements.","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(2)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which statement about part-time work and worker status is consistent with the IRS fact sheet?","o":["Anyone working under 20 hours is a contractor","Anyone earning under $600 is automatically an employee","Part-time workers can never be employees","Working part time does not by itself make someone a contractor"],"a":3,"e":"The IRS says the number of hours worked and part-time status do not by themselves determine whether a worker is an employee or a contractor. [IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27]","r":"IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's annual fixed overhead is $360,000 and its gross margin averages 25% of revenue. What annual revenue is needed to break even (net income of zero, ignoring interest and taxes)?","o":["$288,000","$90,000","$1,440,000","$450,000"],"a":2,"e":"Break-even revenue = fixed overhead / gross margin % = $360,000 / 0.25 = $1,440,000. Multiplying by 1.25 gives $450,000, which is not a break-even formula. [Break-even analysis]","r":"Break-even (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Rule 61G6-5.005, F.A.C. directs the Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board to look back how many years for bankruptcy or court judgments arising from the applicant's contracting operations?","o":["One year","Three years","Five years","Ten years"],"a":2,"e":"The board considers bankruptcies and judgments for failing to pay materialmen, laborers, and suppliers within the five years before the application. [Rule 61G6-5.005]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.005, F.A.C."},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor buys equipment for $24,000, expects a salvage value of $3,600 after 6 years, and expects 1,200 hours of use per year. Using straight-line depreciation only, what is the ownership cost per operating hour?","o":["$3.33","$3,400","$17","$2.83"],"a":3,"e":"(24,000 - 3,600) = 20,400; hours = 7,200; 20,400/7,200 = $2.83. Ignoring salvage gives $3.33. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 90°C-rated 10 AWG NM cable carries a load after applying correction and adjustment factors. What is the maximum ampacity permitted for the cable?","o":["The 60°C ampacity (30 A), though the 90°C value may be used for derating calculations","The 75°C ampacity (35 A)","The 90°C ampacity (40 A) under all conditions","The ampacity of 8 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The ampacity of NM cable may not exceed that of a 60°C rated conductor, although the 90°C rating may be used for adjustment and correction calculations. [NEC 334.80]","r":"NEC 334.80"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor receives an initial payment of more than 10 percent of a residential contract price on March 3, 2026, and a permit is required. By what date must the contractor apply for the permit unless there is just cause or a written agreement for a longer time?","o":["April 2, 2026","March 18, 2026","May 1, 2026","June 1, 2026"],"a":0,"e":"Section 489.126(2)(a)1 requires application for permits within 30 days after payment: March 3 plus 30 days is April 2, 2026. The 90-day clock to begin work starts only after permits are issued. [F.S. 489.126(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 489.126(2)(a)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.533(1)(m)4, F.S., a contractor who fails to pay a related judgment or to follow a repayment schedule within what time commits financial mismanagement?","o":["18 months","30 days","6 months","5 years"],"a":0,"e":"The judgment must be against the contractor or a business the contractor qualified and relate to contracting, and unpaid for 18 months. Rule 61G6-10.008 makes this violation eligible for mediation. [F.S. 489.533(1)(m)4]","r":"F.S. 489.533(1)(m)4"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The circuit disconnecting means for a fire alarm branch circuit must have what color identification?","o":["Orange","Yellow","Blue","Red"],"a":3,"e":"The disconnect must have red identification, be accessible only to qualified personnel, and be marked \"FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT.\" [NEC 760.121(B)]","r":"NEC 2023 760.121(B)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A neon sign has a marked load of 14.5 A on a 120 V branch circuit. Treating it as a continuous load, what is the minimum branch-circuit rating (conductor and OCPD sized at 125 percent), choosing the next standard size?","o":["20 A","15 A","25 A","30 A"],"a":0,"e":"14.5 A x 1.25 = 18.12 A. The next standard breaker rating at or above is 20 A. A 15 A circuit cannot carry 18.1 A continuously. [NEC 600.5, 210.20(A), 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 600.5, 210.20(A), 240.6(A)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which party pays the 0.9% Additional Medicare Tax on a worker's wages above $200,000?","o":["The employer alone","The employer and employee equally, 0.45% each","The employee for 0.9% and the employer for 1.45%","The employee alone; there is no employer match"],"a":3,"e":"The Additional Medicare Tax is imposed only on the employee and is not matched by the employer, although the employer must withhold it. [IRS Topic 751; IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Topic 751"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A scissor lift rents for $450 per day for 6 days, with $275 delivery and $275 pickup charges. What is the total rental cost?","o":["$2,700","$2,975","$3,700","$3,250"],"a":3,"e":"6 x 450 = 2,700; plus 275 + 275 = 550; total $3,250. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Using the same current assets of $420,000 and current liabilities of $310,000, the current ratio is","o":["0.74","0.26","0.58","1.35"],"a":3,"e":"Current ratio = 420,000 / 310,000 = 1.35. [Working capital]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A project network (all finish-to-start, durations in working days): A 3d (start); B 5d after A; C 4d after A; D 6d after B; E 2d after B+C; F 7d after D+E; G 3d after C; H 2d after F+G. What is the total float of activity G?","o":["11 days","0 days","9 days","5 days"],"a":0,"e":"G (3 days) has EF = 10. H must start by day 21, so G's late finish is 21. Float = 21 - 10 = 11 days (path A-C-G-H is only 12 days against 23). [CPM total float]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A through-penetration firestop system is tested to ASTM E814 / UL 1479. What does its \"T rating\" indicate?","o":["The time the system stays free of flames on the unexposed side","The time the penetrating cable keeps carrying rated current","The time the system resists a water hose-stream after the fire test","The time the unexposed side of the assembly stays below a 325 deg F rise"],"a":3,"e":"The F rating is the time to flame passage; the T rating adds a temperature-rise limit of 325 deg F (above ambient) on the unexposed side. Neither relates to circuit integrity. [UL 1479 / ASTM E814]","r":"UL 1479 / ASTM E814 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Equipment that is billed to jobs at too low an internal rate will cause","o":["equipment costs to be over-recovered, so job profit looks smaller than it is","no change in reported profit, since equipment is not a job cost","a decrease in the company's insurance costs on the equipment","equipment costs to be under-recovered, so jobs look more profitable than they are"],"a":3,"e":"If the rate does not cover ownership and operating cost, the shortfall appears as a company loss instead of being priced into jobs. [Equipment cost recovery]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A photovoltaic-powered sign's battery compartment must:","o":["Be open to the weather","Be unlocked","Require a tool to open","Have a window"],"a":2,"e":"Article 600 requires battery compartments in PV powered signs to require a tool to open. [NEC 600 (PV powered signs)]","r":"NEC 600 (PV powered signs) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Chapter 9, Table 1 limits the cross-sectional area of three or more conductors in a raceway to what percent of the raceway's cross-sectional area?","o":["53%","60%","31%","40%"],"a":3,"e":"Table 1 allows 53% for one conductor, 31% for two conductors, and 40% for over two conductors. [NEC Chapter 9, Table 1]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Table 1"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A wattmeter measures","o":["reactive power","peak voltage","apparent power","true power"],"a":3,"e":"A wattmeter has a voltage coil and a current coil and responds to V x I x cos(angle). [Meters]","r":"Meters"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 12,000 volt neon transformer with a grounded center tap has secondary terminals. What is the voltage from each terminal to ground, ignoring other factors?","o":["6,000 volts","12,000 volts","3,000 volts","24,000 volts"],"a":0,"e":"With a grounded midpoint, each terminal is half the total open-circuit voltage to ground: 12,000 / 2 = 6,000 V. That is under the 7,500 V limit to ground. [NEC 600.23(C)]","r":"NEC 600.23(C)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A balanced three-phase panel should keep phase loading close. A 208Y/120 V panel schedule shows the highest phase at 5,900 VA and the lowest phase at 5,400 VA. What is the percent by which the highest phase exceeds the lowest (rounded to the nearest whole percent)?","o":["8%","109%","3%","9%"],"a":3,"e":"(High - Low) / Low = (5,900 - 5,400) / 5,400 = 9.3 percent. The other results divide by the wrong base or report a ratio instead of an excess. [Panel balancing practice; NEC 408]","r":"Panel balancing practice; NEC 408 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How many sections does a safety data sheet have under 29 CFR 1910.1200, and what is Section 4?","o":["Sixteen sections; Section 4 is first-aid measures","Ten sections; Section 4 is fire-fighting measures","Twelve sections; Section 4 is disposal","Sixteen sections; Section 4 is composition"],"a":0,"e":"SDSs have 16 sections in a set order; Section 1 identification, 2 hazard identification, 3 composition, 4 first-aid measures, 5 fire-fighting, 8 exposure controls/personal protection. [29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(2)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Neon tubing that is readily accessible to pedestrians must be:","o":["Painted a bright color so that pedestrians can see it at night","Covered with clear tape to contain glass fragments if it breaks","Installed less than 3 ft above the finished floor level","Protected from physical damage, unless a listed dry-location portable sign"],"a":3,"e":"600.9(B) requires protection of neon tubing accessible to pedestrians, other than listed dry-location portable signs. 600.41 adds guard requirements for skeleton tubing. [NEC 600.9(B)]","r":"NEC 600.9(B)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 60 kVA, 208-volt, three-phase generator supplies its output conductors to the first overcurrent device. Using nameplate current, what minimum conductor ampacity applies?","o":["166.5 A","208.2 A","191.5 A","183.1 A"],"a":2,"e":"I = 60,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 166.5 A; x 1.15 = 191.5 A. [NEC 445.13(A)]","r":"NEC 445.13(A)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a fire alarm riser, a cable shown as FPLR is rated for installation in:","o":["Plenums used for environmental air only, where plenum-rated cable is required","Wet locations only, such as outdoors under a roof canopy or overhang","Underground installations only, such as direct burial under a concrete slab","Vertical shafts (risers), where fire spread between floors must be limited"],"a":3,"e":"NEC Article 760 cable types: FPL general use, FPLR riser, FPLP plenum. The 'R' stands for riser-rated. [NEC 760.179]","r":"NEC 760.179"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How much energy is stored in the magnetic field of a 0.5 H inductor carrying 4 A?","o":["16.0 J","2.0 J","4.0 J","8.0 J"],"a":2,"e":"E = 1/2 L I squared = 0.5 x 0.5 x 16 = 4.0 J. [Inductors]","r":"Inductors"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.115(7), F.S., the board's financial stability guidelines for initial Part I applicants may require minimum net worth, cash, and bonding of no more than what amounts?","o":["$5,000 for Division I and $2,500 for Division II","$20,000 for Division I and $10,000 for Division II","$50,000 for Division I and $25,000 for Division II","$100,000 for Division I and $50,000 for Division II"],"a":1,"e":"Subsection (7) caps the financial stability requirements at $20,000 (Division I) and $10,000 (Division II), and a 14-hour board-approved financial responsibility course can satisfy 50 percent. [F.S. 489.115(7)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(7)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier first delivers materials on April 1 but serves its Notice to Owner on June 1, which is after the 45-day period. Under Florida law, this failure is:","o":["Only a reduction in the amount recoverable","Not a defense if the owner had actual notice","A complete defense to enforcement of the supplier's lien","Cured by recording the claim of lien"],"a":2,"e":"Failure to serve the notice timely is a complete defense to lien enforcement, and the time requirements are strictly construed. [F.S. 713.06(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(2)(a)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"For sizing motor branch-circuit conductors and short-circuit protection, which full-load current value is used for a standard three-phase induction motor?","o":["The full-load current stamped on the nameplate","The locked-rotor current marked on the nameplate","Values from Tables 430.247-430.250, not the nameplate","The average of table and nameplate values"],"a":2,"e":"The NEC tables are used for conductor and branch-circuit device sizing; the nameplate current is used for separate overload protection. [NEC 430.6(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 430.6(A)(1)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contract specifies 60 working days (Monday through Friday, no holidays in the period). About how many calendar weeks is that?","o":["12 weeks","60 weeks","8.6 weeks","10 weeks"],"a":0,"e":"60 working days / 5 days per week = 12 weeks. Dividing by 7 treats weekends as workdays. [Contract time]","r":"Contract time (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An electrical contractor buys $8,400 of materials subject to Florida sales tax at 6% state plus a 1% county surtax. What is the total cost including tax (assume the surtax applies to the whole amount)?","o":["$8,904.00","$14,280.00","$8,484.00","$8,988.00"],"a":3,"e":"Tax = $8,400 x 0.07 = $588.00; total = $8,988.00. [F.S. 212.05, 212.054]","r":"F.S. 212.05; 212.054"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under 2010 Standards 308.3.2, what is the maximum height for a side reach over an obstruction up to 34 in. high if the reach depth is more than 10 in. (to 24 in. maximum)?","o":["54 in.","48 in.","44 in.","46 in."],"a":3,"e":"Side reach over an obstruction is 48 in. for depths up to 10 in., and 46 in. for depths greater than 10 in. up to 24 in. The 44 in. figure applies to forward reach over deeper obstructions. [2010 Standards 308.3.2]","r":"2010 Std 308.3.2"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Conductors installed in parallel must be at least what size?","o":["1 AWG","2/0 AWG","4 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Aluminum, copper-clad aluminum or copper conductors of 1/0 AWG and larger may be connected in parallel (with limited exceptions). [NEC 310.10(G)(1)]","r":"NEC 310.10(G)(1)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under a claims-made liability policy, a claim is covered only if","o":["the injury happened during the policy period, even if the claim was made years later","the policy has been renewed for at least five consecutive years","the claim is first made in the policy period and the incident follows the retro date","the employer has paid the claim first"],"a":2,"e":"Claims-made coverage requires both a claim made while the policy (or extended reporting period) is in force and an incident after the retro date. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An unqualified person is working on an elevated platform near an unguarded overhead line energized at 40 kV. What minimum distance must the person and their longest conductive object keep from the line (29 CFR 1910.333(c)(3))?","o":["3 feet","6 feet","10 feet","15 feet"],"a":2,"e":"Unqualified persons working in elevated positions must stay at least 10 feet from unguarded energized lines of 50 kV or below, with 4 inches added for each 10 kV over 50 kV.","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(3)(i)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contract price is $420,000 with estimated total cost $350,000. Costs to date are $140,000. Using cost-to-cost, how much gross profit has been earned to date?","o":["$28,000","$23,333","$70,000","$168,000"],"a":0,"e":"Percent complete = $140,000 / $350,000 = 40%. Earned revenue = $168,000. Gross profit to date = $168,000 - $140,000 = $28,000 (equivalently 40% of the $70,000 total profit). [26 CFR 1.460-4; WIP schedule]","r":"26 CFR 1.460-4"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under 2010 Standards 205.1, which operable part is NOT required to comply with Section 309?","o":["A general-use receptacle in an accessible classroom","A thermostat in an accessible hotel guest room","One intended only for use by service or maintenance personnel","A light switch in an accessible office space"],"a":2,"e":"Operable parts intended only for service or maintenance personnel are exempt. Light switches and general-use receptacles are explicitly covered. [2010 Standards 205.1, Exc. 1]","r":"2010 Std 205.1 Exc. 1"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the Family and Medical Leave Act, an eligible employee may take up to how much unpaid, job-protected leave in a 12-month period for qualifying reasons?","o":["6 workweeks","12 workweeks","4 workweeks","26 workweeks"],"a":1,"e":"FMLA covers employers with 50 or more employees within 75 miles and gives eligible employees up to 12 workweeks of unpaid leave. [29 U.S.C. 2612(a)(1)]","r":"29 U.S.C. 2612(a)(1)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent device rating protecting the input of a transformer that supplies power-limited fire alarm circuits?","o":["15 amperes","30 amperes","7 amperes","20 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"Transformers or other devices supplying PLFA circuits from power-supply conductors must be protected by an OCPD not exceeding 20 A. The 7 A figure is the NPLFA limit for 18 AWG. [NEC 760.127]","r":"NEC 2023 760.127"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size conductor for neon secondary-circuit wiring of 1000 volts or less?","o":["22 AWG","16 AWG","18 AWG","14 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"600.31: conductors must be listed, insulated, and not smaller than 18 AWG. [NEC 600.31]","r":"NEC 600.31"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under the Florida minimum wage schedule, what hourly rate took effect on September 30, 2026?","o":["$14.00","$13.50","$16.00","$15.00"],"a":3,"e":"Florida's Amendment 2 schedule raised the rate by $1.00 each September 30 until it reached $15.00 on September 30, 2026; $14.00 was the rate from September 30, 2025. [Fla. Const. Art. X, s. 24; F.S. 448.110]","r":"Fla. Const. Art. X s.24; F.S. 448.110"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A Class 2 circuit is installed where wet contact is likely. Per the Chapter 9 Table 11(A) notes, which option applies?","o":["Use Class 3 wiring methods, or limit Vmax to 15 volts for sinusoidal ac","No change; Class 2 limits apply identically in wet locations","Limit Vmax to 30 volts sinusoidal ac and use Class 2 wiring","Overcurrent protection must be raised to 8.0 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"Note 2 to Tables 11(A)/(B) requires Class 3 wiring methods, or reduced voltage (15 V sinusoidal ac, 21.2 V peak nonsinusoidal), where wet contact is likely. [NEC Ch. 9 Table 11(A), Note 2]","r":"NEC Ch. 9 Table 11(A) Note 2"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Because Florida has a state fair employment agency, the deadline to file a Title VII charge with the EEOC in Florida is generally:","o":["30 days from the discriminatory act","2 years from the discriminatory act","300 days from the discriminatory act","180 days from the discriminatory act, with no extension"],"a":2,"e":"The EEOC deadline is 180 days, extended to 300 days where a state or local agency enforces a law prohibiting the same discrimination. [EEOC: Time Limits for Filing a Charge]","r":"42 U.S.C. 2000e-5(e)(1)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A dwelling EVSE has a nameplate of 32 A at 240 V. What load is used in the service calculation?","o":["7,200 VA","6,144 VA","9,600 VA","7,680 VA"],"a":3,"e":"EVSE load is the larger of 7,200 VA or the nameplate: 32 x 240 = 7,680 VA. [NEC 220.57]","r":"NEC 220.57"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Willfully filing a fraudulent lien under s. 713.31, F.S., is a:","o":["Second-degree misdemeanor","Second-degree felony","Noncriminal infraction","Third-degree felony"],"a":3,"e":"A willfully exaggerated lien, or one for work not done or material not furnished, is a fraudulent lien; filing one willfully is a third-degree felony and also forfeits the lienor's rights. [F.S. 713.31(3)]","r":"F.S. 713.31"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A cash-method contractor receives and uses a December supplier shipment of wire but pays the invoice on January 10. Under the cash method the cost is generally deducted in","o":["the year the wire was received and installed","the year it is paid, which is the following year","the year the invoice is dated","the year the job is billed to the customer"],"a":1,"e":"Cash-method taxpayers generally deduct expenses in the year they actually pay them. Matching to the job's billing date is accrual or percentage-of-completion thinking. [IRS Pub 334 (2023), Cash method]","r":"IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 218.735, a subcontractor who has just received payment from the prime contractor must pay its own subcontractors and suppliers within","o":["7 days of receiving payment","10 days of receiving payment from the prime contractor above it","14 days of receiving payment from the prime contractor above it","30 days of receiving payment from the prime contractor above it"],"a":0,"e":"The prime contractor has 10 days; sub-tier parties have 7 days. [F.S. 218.735]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An electrician's annual payroll is $62,400. Workers' compensation costs $6.50 per $100 of payroll. What is the annual premium for this employee?","o":["$406","$4,056","$405,600","$624"],"a":1,"e":"Premium = payroll/100 x rate = 62,400/100 x 6.5 = 4,056. [Labor burden]","r":"Workers' comp premium formula (invented rate)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A lighting load of 4,300 VA at 120 V is all continuous. Branch circuits are 20 A, loaded to no more than 80 percent. How many 20 A circuits are needed, rounded up?","o":["2 circuits","4 circuits","5 circuits","3 circuits"],"a":3,"e":"A 20 A circuit at 80 percent carries 16 A x 120 V = 1,920 VA. 4,300 / 1,920 = 2.24, so 3 circuits. Without the continuous-load limit it would be 2 circuits. [NEC 210.19(A)(1), 210.20(A)]","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1), 210.20(A)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Same contract: completion due August 14, liquidated damages $850 per calendar day, finished September 2. The owner approved a change order that added 6 calendar days. What are the liquidated damages?","o":["$11,050","$16,150","$5,100","$21,250"],"a":0,"e":"The extended deadline is August 20, and September 2 is 13 days later, so 13 x $850 = $11,050. Ignoring the extension gives $16,150, and subtracting 6 days' worth ($5,100) from the wrong base is a mistake. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A PV system's rapid shutdown requires conductors outside the array boundary to drop to what level within 30 seconds?","o":["80 volts","120 volts","0 volts","30 volts"],"a":3,"e":"Controlled conductors outside the array boundary must be limited to 30 V within 30 seconds; inside the array, 80 V within 30 seconds. [NEC 690.12(B)]","r":"NEC 690.12(B)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which type of sign installation is not required by Article 600 to be listed when it is installed in accordance with the Code?","o":["A wall-mounted channel-letter sign","Field-installed skeleton tubing","A portable sign with an attachment plug","A section sign supplied by a Class 2 power source"],"a":1,"e":"Field-installed skeleton tubing (neon tubing that is itself the sign, not mounted in a sign body) is not required to be listed if it complies with the Code. Other signs and section signs must be listed. [NEC 600 (field-installed skeleton tubing)]","r":"NEC 600 (field-installed skeleton tubing) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An electrical contractor using the accrual method generally reports income when","o":["the customer's check clears the contractor's bank account at the end of the month","all events have occurred that fix the right to receive it and the amount can be determined with reasonable accuracy","the contractor signs the bid proposal and the owner's authorization to proceed","the contractor deposits the check at the end of the calendar year in all cases"],"a":1,"e":"Accrual-method income is recognized when the all-events test is met, regardless of when cash arrives. Waiting for the check to clear describes the cash method. [IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods]","r":"IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A feeder (not a 100%-rated assembly) serves a 90 A continuous load and a 60 A noncontinuous load. All terminations are rated 75°C. What is the smallest copper feeder conductor (Table 310.16, 75°C) permitted, before adjustment?","o":["2/0 AWG","1 AWG","1/0 AWG","3/0 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Minimum ampacity = 1.25 x 90 + 60 = 172.5 A. At 75°C, 1/0 AWG carries 150 A (too small) and 2/0 AWG carries 175 A, which meets the 172.5 A requirement. [NEC 215.2(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 215.2(A)(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1910.333(a)(1), which energized parts need not be deenergized before work?","o":["All parts of 120-volt branch circuits that are in dry locations only","Any part that the employer considers inconvenient to shut down for the job","Parts under 50 volts to ground when there is no increased burn or arc exposure","Any part inside a panel that has a main breaker in the off position"],"a":2,"e":"Live parts must be deenergized unless the employer shows deenergizing adds hazards or is infeasible; parts under 50 volts to ground may stay energized when no added burn or arc-blast exposure exists.","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(a)(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 208 V three-phase load draws 40 A at 0.90 power factor. What is the true power?","o":["7.49 kW","14.41 kW","22.46 kW","12.97 kW"],"a":3,"e":"P = 1.732 x 208 x 40 x 0.90 / 1,000 = 12.97 kW. [Three-phase power]","r":"Three-phase power"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Live parts of a sign, other than lamps and neon tubing, must be:","o":["Enclosed","Painted","Grounded through the neutral","Left open for ventilation"],"a":0,"e":"600.8 requires enclosure of live parts other than lamps and neon tubing. Transformers and power supplies with integral enclosures do not need an additional enclosure. [NEC 600.8]","r":"NEC 600.8"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Who must implement the written assured equipment grounding conductor program on a construction site?","o":["One or more competent persons","The general contractor's insurance carrier","Any employee holding a journeyman electrician license","The local authority having jurisdiction"],"a":0,"e":"The program must be implemented by one or more competent persons as defined in 1926.32(f); a license is not the criterion. [29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(B)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(B)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The employer must complete Section 2 of Form I-9 within how long after the employee's first day of work for pay?","o":["7 calendar days","24 hours","30 days","3 business days"],"a":3,"e":"The employer examines the documents and completes Section 2 within three business days after the employee starts work for pay. [8 CFR 274a.2(b)(1)(ii)]","r":"8 CFR 274a.2(b)(1)(ii)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum cover for rigid metal conduit (RMC) buried in the general location, such as under a lawn?","o":["12 in.","6 in.","24 in.","18 in."],"a":1,"e":"Table 300.5, Column 2: RMC and IMC require 6 in. of cover in all locations not specified. [NEC Table 300.5]","r":"NEC Table 300.5"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the synchronous speed of a 4-pole, 60 Hz induction motor?","o":["1,750 rpm","3,600 rpm","1,200 rpm","1,800 rpm"],"a":3,"e":"Ns = 120 x f / P = 120 x 60 / 4 = 1,800 rpm. 1,750 rpm is a typical full-load rotor speed. [Synchronous speed]","r":"Synchronous speed"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A contract states that the order of precedence is: (1) change orders, (2) addenda, (3) specifications, (4) drawings. A drawing shows a 100 A breaker, but the specification says 125 A. Which governs?","o":["100 A, because the drawing is more visual than the text","125 A, because the specification ranks above the drawings","Neither, because the engineer must redesign the panel","The larger of the two values plus 25 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Where the contract states the ranking, the higher-ranked document governs. Always read the contract's own precedence clause because it varies. [Contract order of precedence]","r":"Contract order of precedence"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of the following assets generally cannot be depreciated?","o":["Land","A service van","Office furniture","A building used in business"],"a":0,"e":"Land is not depreciable because it does not wear out; the cost of clearing and grading it is generally added to land cost. [IRS Pub 946 (2025)]","r":"IRS Pub 946 (2025)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Rigid PVC conduit of 3/4 in. size must be supported at maximum intervals of:","o":["3 ft","6 ft","10 ft","5 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Table 352.30: 1/2 through 1 in., 3 ft; 1-1/4 through 2 in., 5 ft; 2-1/2 through 3 in., 6 ft; 3-1/2 through 5 in., 7 ft; 6 in., 8 ft. [NEC 352.30, Table 352.30]","r":"NEC 352.30, Table 352.30"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which motor uses a rotor with no external electrical connection and is the most common industrial type?","o":["Squirrel-cage induction motor","Wound-rotor induction motor with slip rings","Universal motor with carbon brushes","Series-wound DC motor with a commutator"],"a":0,"e":"The squirrel-cage rotor has bars shorted by end rings and no brushes or slip rings. [Motor types]","r":"Motor types"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A battery with 12.6 V open-circuit EMF and 0.1 ohm internal resistance supplies a 2.0 ohm load. What is the terminal voltage under load?","o":["11.0 V","12.5 V","12.0 V","12.6 V"],"a":2,"e":"I = 12.6 / 2.1 = 6.0 A; terminal V = 12.6 - (6.0 x 0.1) = 12.0 V. [Batteries]","r":"Batteries"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What is the federal minimum wage under the Fair Labor Standards Act for covered nonexempt employees?","o":["$10.00 per hour","$7.25 per hour","$8.50 per hour","$7.00 per hour"],"a":1,"e":"The FLSA sets the federal minimum wage at $7.25 per hour; Florida's higher state minimum applies where it exceeds the federal rate. [DOL Handy Reference Guide to the FLSA]","r":"FLSA Sec. 6(a)(1)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A run of EMT contains three 4 AWG, three 8 AWG and three 10 AWG THHN copper conductors (0.0824, 0.0366 and 0.0211 sq in. each). What is the smallest EMT trade size permitted?","o":["1 in.","1-1/2 in.","2 in.","1-1/4 in."],"a":3,"e":"Total = 3(0.0824) + 3(0.0366) + 3(0.0211) = 0.4203 sq in. At 40% fill, 1 in. EMT allows 0.346 (too small); 1-1/4 in. allows 0.598 (adequate). [NEC Chapter 9, Tables 1, 4, 5]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Tables 1, 4, 5"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A mobile home has six fixed appliances, not counting heating or air-conditioning, with nameplate loads totaling 14,400 VA. What demand load applies to them?","o":["14,400 VA","7,200 VA","10,800 VA","12,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Four or more appliances get a 75% demand: 14,400 x 0.75 = 10,800 VA. [NEC 550.18(B)(6)]","r":"NEC 550.18(B)(6)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"How many neon secondary conductors over 1000 V may be run in a single conduit or tubing?","o":["Two","Three","As many as will fit","Only one"],"a":3,"e":"600.32: conduit or tubing shall contain only one conductor. Minimum size is metric designator 16 (trade size 1/2). [NEC 600.32]","r":"NEC 600.32"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which RV site receptacles require GFCI protection in a park?","o":["Only the 50-ampere receptacles","125-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles","Only the 30-ampere receptacles","None, since the pedestal breaker covers it"],"a":1,"e":"GFCI protection is required for the 125-volt 15- and 20-ampere receptacles at RV sites. [NEC 551.71(F)]","r":"NEC 551.71(F)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 240-V, 3-phase corner-grounded delta system is to receive a surge-protective device that is not listed for corner-grounded delta systems. May it be installed?","o":["Yes, if the SPD has a status light","No, SPDs may not be installed on corner-grounded delta systems unless specifically listed for that use","Yes, all SPDs may be installed on any system","Yes, if the SPD is Type 3"],"a":1,"e":"SPDs may not be installed on ungrounded, impedance-grounded or corner-grounded delta systems unless listed for those systems. [NEC 242.12]","r":"NEC 242.12"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A nonexempt employee earns $18.00 per hour and works 50 hours in a week. The employer also pays a nondiscretionary $100 production bonus for that week. What is the total gross pay?","o":["$1,090.00","$1,120.00","$1,000.00","$1,100.00"],"a":3,"e":"The bonus must be included in the regular rate: ($900 + $100) / 50 = $20.00. Straight time for all hours plus the half-time premium: $1,000 + (0.5 x $20 x 10 = $100) = $1,100.00. $1,090 ignores the bonus in the regular rate. [29 CFR 778.209; DOL Fact Sheet 23]","r":"29 CFR 778.209; FLSA"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Who pays FUTA tax?","o":["The employee alone, through payroll deduction","The employer alone; it is not withheld from pay","The employer and employee equally, half each","The independent contractor, through estimated tax"],"a":1,"e":"FUTA is imposed on the employer and is not deducted from employees' wages. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); Form 940 instructions]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the FLSA recordkeeping rules, how long must an employer keep time cards and wage computation records?","o":["2 years","5 years","3 years","6 months"],"a":0,"e":"Basic payroll records are kept 3 years, while time cards and records used to compute wages are kept at least 2 years. [29 CFR 516.6]","r":"29 CFR 516.6"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Equipment in a hazardous location is marked T3. What is the maximum surface temperature of that equipment?","o":["135 degrees C","100 degrees C","200 degrees C","300 degrees C"],"a":2,"e":"Temperature classes: T1 450 C, T2 300 C, T3 200 C, T4 135 C, T5 100 C, T6 85 C. [NEC 500.8(C), Table 500.8(C)]","r":"NEC 500.8(C), Table 500.8(C)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The State Fire Marshal must decide an expedited declaratory statement petition about the Florida Fire Prevention Code within:","o":["90 days","30 days","14 days","45 days"],"a":3,"e":"F.S. 633.202(12) sets 45 days. The petition must come from the project owner or representative and ask a single yes-or-no question about a specific provision. [F.S. 633.202(12)]","r":"F.S. 633.202(12)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Revenue is $2,400,000, direct job costs $1,800,000, general overhead $390,000, and interest expense $30,000. Before income taxes, what is net income?","o":["$180,000","$570,000","$240,000","$210,000"],"a":0,"e":"Gross profit $600,000 - overhead $390,000 = operating income $210,000; minus interest $30,000 = $180,000. Stopping at $210,000 leaves out interest. [Income statement]","r":"Income statement"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under Article 645, a flexible power-supply cord connecting information technology equipment to a branch circuit may not exceed:","o":["1.8 m (6 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","7.5 m (25 ft)","4.5 m (15 ft)"],"a":3,"e":"Power-supply cords for ITE are limited to 15 ft in length. [NEC 645.6(B)]","r":"NEC 2023 645.6"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A neon transformer is rated 15,000 V and 30 mA on its secondary. What is the secondary volt-ampere rating?","o":["0.5 VA","450,000 VA","450 VA","500,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"VA = V x I = 15,000 x 0.03 = 450 VA. Changing mA to amperes matters: 30 mA is 0.03 A. [Basic power calculation; NEC 600.23]","r":"Basic power calculation; NEC 600.23"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A GTO conductor from a transformer terminal to the first electrode is 24 ft long and is installed in nonmetallic conduit. Does it comply with 600.32?","o":["No, because the maximum is 20 ft in nonmetallic conduit","Yes, because nonmetallic conduit permits up to 50 ft","No, because nonmetallic conduit is not allowed for this use","No, because the maximum for any conduit is 12 ft"],"a":1,"e":"The limits are 20 ft in metal conduit or tubing and 50 ft in nonmetallic conduit. 24 ft is allowed in nonmetallic conduit but would exceed the 20 ft limit in metal conduit. [NEC 600.32]","r":"NEC 600.32"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For a serialized piece of equipment that is bought for a specific job, which valuation method matches cost to the actual item?","o":["FIFO","LIFO","Weighted average","Specific identification"],"a":3,"e":"Specific identification tracks the actual cost of each unique item, suited to high-value serialized goods. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"After the general contractor is awarded a job, the GC tells a subcontractor that another sub has quoted a lower price and asks the first sub to match it, using the quote as leverage. This practice is commonly called","o":["bid bonding","unit pricing","bid shopping","bid leveling"],"a":2,"e":"Bid shopping uses one sub's price to pressure another after award. Bid leveling is the legitimate comparison of quotes for equal scope. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 480-V branch circuit supplies a 28 A continuous load and an 8 A noncontinuous load. The conductors terminate on 75°C-rated terminals and the OCPD is not listed for 100% operation. What is the smallest copper conductor (Table 310.16, 75°C column) for this circuit before any adjustment factors?","o":["4 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Minimum ampacity = 1.25 x 28 + 8 = 43 A. At 75°C, 10 AWG is 35 A (too small) and 8 AWG is 50 A, which is the smallest that works. [NEC 210.19(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 210.19(A)(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A motor draws 24 kW and 18 kVAR. What is its apparent power?","o":["42 kVA","6 kVA","432 kVA","30 kVA"],"a":3,"e":"S = sqrt(24 squared + 18 squared) = sqrt(900) = 30 kVA. [Power triangle]","r":"Power triangle"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum rating of the service disconnecting means for a one-family dwelling?","o":["125 A","60 A","200 A","100 A"],"a":3,"e":"For a one-family dwelling the service disconnect must be rated not less than 100 A (3-wire). [NEC 230.79(C)]","r":"NEC 230.79(C)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A baseline schedule is","o":["the approved original plan used to measure progress and delay","the final as-built record of the completed project work","a weekly list of the crew assigned to every task","the schedule of values used for the pay applications"],"a":0,"e":"The baseline records the approved plan; updates compare actual progress to it, and delay analyses rely on it. [Scheduling terminology]","r":"Schedule updates (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Who normally may remove a lockout or tagout device under 29 CFR 1910.147?","o":["The supervisor on duty, at any time that the equipment is needed in production","Any employee who has a key that happens to fit the lock that was applied","The authorized employee who applied it, with narrow exceptions under a documented procedure","The first employee to arrive on the next shift after the work is complete"],"a":2,"e":"Each device must be removed by the employee who applied it, with a narrow exception only under a documented procedure that verifies the applier is off-site and reasonable efforts are made to contact them. [29 CFR 1910.147(e)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.147(e)(3)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 713.07, F.S., liens of persons under ss. 713.05 and 713.06 take priority from what date?","o":["The date the claim of lien is recorded","The date of the building permit","The date the notice of commencement is recorded","The date of the certificate of occupancy"],"a":2,"e":"These liens relate back to the recording of the notice of commencement; without a notice of commencement they attach when the claim of lien is recorded. [F.S. 713.07]","r":"F.S. 713.07"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An unqualified person on an elevated platform must stay clear of an unguarded 130 kV line. What minimum clearance applies under 29 CFR 1910.333(c)(3)(i) (10 feet plus 4 inches for every 10 kV over 50 kV)?","o":["12 feet 8 inches","10 feet 8 inches","11 feet 4 inches","13 feet 4 inches"],"a":0,"e":"130 kV is 80 kV over 50 kV: 8 increments x 4 in = 32 in. 10 ft (120 in) + 32 in = 152 in = 12 ft 8 in.","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(3)(i)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A Florida contractor buys materials from an out-of-state supplier that does not charge Florida tax. The contractor generally:","o":["Owes only the county surtax","Owes nothing, because the vendor did not charge it","Owes tax only on freight","Owes Florida use tax on the materials"],"a":3,"e":"Florida imposes use tax on taxable items brought into the state when sales tax was not paid. [F.S. ch. 212]","r":"F.S. ch. 212"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier invoice for $18,400 carries terms 2/10, net 30. If the contractor pays on day 9, what amount is paid?","o":["$18,032","$18,400","$17,671.36","$18,775.51"],"a":0,"e":"2% of 18,400 = 368; 18,400 - 368 = $18,032. The discount applies when paid within 10 days. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A 120 V resistive load draws 12 A. What is its power?","o":["1,440 W","10 W","720 W","2,494 W"],"a":0,"e":"P = E x I = 120 x 12 = 1440 W. The square root of 3 factor applies only to three-phase. [Power formula]","r":"Power formula"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier invoices $12,000 with terms '2/10, net 30.' The buyer pays on day 8. What is the correct payment?","o":["$12,000","$11,400","$240","$11,760"],"a":3,"e":"Payment within 10 days earns a 2 percent discount: $12,000 x 0.02 = $240, so $12,000 - $240 = $11,760. Net 30 is the due date if the discount is not taken. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under Florida's contractor licensing law, a \"specialty contractor\" in electrical contracting is one whose scope:","o":["Is limited to a particular segment of electrical or alarm work set by board rule, such as signs","Includes only lighting maintenance work performed in single-family homes","Is unlimited and covers every kind of electrical work in the state","Is set by the local building official in each county where work is done"],"a":0,"e":"Section 489.505 defines a specialty contractor by a limited scope tied to a category set in board rule, and gives electrical and nonelectrical advertising sign work as one example. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The 30 ohm resistance and 40 ohm inductive reactance in series are connected to 120 V. What is the current?","o":["3.0 A","1.71 A","4.0 A","2.4 A"],"a":3,"e":"I = E / Z = 120 / 50 = 2.4 A. Using R only (4.0 A) ignores the reactance. [Impedance]","r":"Impedance"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is required for a PV system DC arc-fault protection when the max PV voltage is 80 volts or more on or in a building?","o":["A GFCI device on the array","A listed PV arc-fault interrupter or equivalent","An AFCI breaker at the main","Nothing for rooftop arrays"],"a":1,"e":"PV circuits operating at 80 V dc or greater on or inside a building need DC arc-fault circuit protection. [NEC 690.11]","r":"NEC 690.11"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An area of rescue assistance is a place where occupants who cannot use stairs can wait for assistance. Under ADAAG 4.3.11, openings within 20 ft of a rescue area (other than the exit door) must have what fire protection rating?","o":["1 1/2 hours","3/4 hour","20 minutes","3 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Openings within 20 ft of the rescue area, except for the exit enclosure doors, must be protected with assemblies having a 3/4 hour fire protection rating. [ADAAG 4.3.11]","r":"ADAAG 4.3.11"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A van costs $38,000, has a salvage value of $5,000 and a 6-year useful life. Straight-line annual depreciation is","o":["$5,500","$6,333","$833","$7,167"],"a":0,"e":"(38,000 - 5,000)/6 = 5,500. Book value after 2 years = 38,000 - 2 x 5,500 = 27,000. [Depreciation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under ADAAG 4.2.6, what is the maximum high side reach where the clear floor space allows a parallel approach (no obstruction)?","o":["46 in.","54 in.","60 in.","48 in."],"a":1,"e":"The 1994 ADAAG allows a high side reach of 54 in. and a low side reach of 9 in. above the floor for parallel approach. The 2010 Standards later changed the side-reach range to 15 in. to 48 in. [ADAAG 4.2.6]","r":"ADAAG 4.2.6"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A fixed 24 ohm resistor passes 2 A at 48 V. What current flows when the voltage is raised to 72 V?","o":["26.0 A","4.5 A","2.0 A","3.0 A"],"a":3,"e":"I = 72 / 24 = 3.0 A. Current rises in direct proportion (72/48 = 1.5), not with the square. [Ohm's law]","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A $500,000 electrical contract has estimated total cost of $400,000 and costs to date of $200,000. Using cost-to-cost percentage of completion, revenue earned to date is","o":["$200,000","$290,000","$250,000","$300,000"],"a":2,"e":"% complete = 200,000 / 400,000 = 50%; earned revenue = 50% x 500,000 = 250,000. [IRS Construction ATG, percentage-of-completion]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What defines a competent person under the OSHA construction standards?","o":["A person with ten years of experience in the same trade or craft","A person who can identify hazards and is authorized to take prompt corrective measures","A person who is licensed by the state in the specialty trade","A person who has passed an OSHA 30-hour course in construction"],"a":1,"e":"A competent person can identify existing and predictable hazards in the surroundings or working conditions that are unsanitary, hazardous or dangerous to employees, and has authorization to take prompt corrective measures. [29 CFR 1926.32(f)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.32(f)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A supplier uses INCREMENTAL pricing: the first 100 units at $2.00, the next 400 units at $1.70, and all units above 500 at $1.50. What is the total cost of 700 units?","o":["$1,150.00","$1,050.00","$1,180.00","$1,190.00"],"a":2,"e":"100 x $2.00 = $200; 400 x $1.70 = $680; 200 x $1.50 = $300; total $1,180.00. Applying $1.50 to all 700 units would give $1,050.00. [Bulk discount math]","r":"Quantity discount"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An order to vacate issued by the State Fire Marshal under F.S. 633.228(2)(a) during an inspection, for an immediate danger, is:","o":["effective immediately as an immediate final order","stayed automatically for 30 days to allow an appeal","advisory until a court issues an injunction","effective only after a formal administrative hearing"],"a":0,"e":"If a violation poses an immediate danger to public health, safety, or welfare, the order to vacate takes effect immediately and is an immediate final order under s. 120.569(2)(n). [F.S. 633.228(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 633.228(2)(a)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The one-line diagram for a school shows a 112.5 kVA, 480 V delta to 208Y/120 V transformer. What is the secondary full-load current at 208 V three-phase?","o":["135 A","541 A","938 A","312 A"],"a":3,"e":"FLA = 112,500 / (1.732 x 208) = 312.3 A. The 480 V value is the primary current, and the single-phase divisors do not apply to a three-phase secondary. [Three-phase current formula]","r":"Three-phase current formula"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Terminals of neon tubing electrodes must not be accessible to:","o":["Pedestrians only","Maintenance staff","Unqualified persons","Persons with a cell phone"],"a":2,"e":"600.42: terminals of the electrode must not be accessible to unqualified persons, and connections are made in listed enclosures. [NEC 600.42]","r":"NEC 600.42"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A sole proprietor electrical contractor hires his 16-year-old son to sweep the shop and file paperwork. Which treatment is correct under federal employment tax rules?","o":["The wages are fully subject to FICA and FUTA","The wages are generally exempt from FICA and FUTA for a child under 18","Only Medicare tax applies to the son","Only FUTA applies to the son"],"a":1,"e":"Wages paid by a sole proprietor to his child under age 18 are exempt from Social Security, Medicare and FUTA (income tax withholding still applies if required). The exemption does not apply when the business is a corporation or a partnership with non-parent partners. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A project network (all finish-to-start, durations in working days): A 3d (start); B 5d after A; C 4d after A; D 6d after B; E 2d after B+C; F 7d after D+E; G 3d after C; H 2d after F+G. Which sequence is the critical path?","o":["A-C-E-F-H","A-C-G-H","A-B-E-F-H","A-B-D-F-H"],"a":3,"e":"A-B-D-F-H totals 23 days. A-B-E-F-H is 19, A-C-E-F-H is 18, and A-C-G-H is 12, so only the first has zero float. [CPM critical path]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the failure-to-deposit penalty, which circumstance triggers the highest 15% rate?","o":["Tax still unpaid over 10 days after the first IRS notice","A deposit made 3 calendar days after the due date","A deposit made 12 calendar days after the due date","A deposit made 16 calendar days after the due date"],"a":0,"e":"The 15% tier applies to amounts unpaid more than 10 days after the date of the first notice (or on a notice demanding immediate payment). Late deposits without notice max at 10%. [IRS Failure to Deposit Penalty]","r":"IRS Failure to Deposit Penalty"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Nonconductive optical fiber may NOT occupy the same cabinet or outlet box as terminations of electric light and power circuits unless:","o":["The cable is yellow","The enclosure has a single conduit opening","The fiber is functionally associated with the electric light or power circuit","The box is larger than 4 inches square"],"a":2,"e":"For enclosures that hold the electrical terminations, fiber is allowed only under listed conditions such as functional association with the power circuit. [NEC 770.133 (2026: 722.164(B))]","r":"NEC 2023 770.133(B)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Activity A takes 8 days and activity B takes 5 days. B is linked finish-to-start with a 2-day lead (FS-2). If A starts on day 0, on what day does B finish?","o":["Day 6","Day 11","Day 13","Day 15"],"a":1,"e":"With a 2-day lead, B starts 2 days before A finishes: day 8-2 = 6. B ends day 6+5 = 11. [Lead]","r":"Lead and lag"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"During construction, a contractor re-routes a feeder around a beam and does not record it. At closeout, the as-built set shows the original route. What is the likely consequence?","o":["A later electrician or excavator may rely on wrong information, causing delay or damage","No consequence, because as-builts are only a courtesy copy and are never used again","The inspector will order the feeder removed and reinstalled along the original route","The permit will automatically expire"],"a":0,"e":"Record drawings are meant to show actual conditions. Incorrect information can create hazards during future work. [Project closeout practice]","r":"Project closeout practice"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If the contract sets no deadline for the owner to submit a punchlist, under F.S. 715.12(7) the punchlist deadline is how many days from the triggering event (certification, certificate of occupancy, or possession)?","o":["30 days","10 days","60 days","15 days"],"a":3,"e":"The statute supplies a default of 15 days when the contract is silent; if no punchlist is given, interest begins 14 days after the triggering event. [F.S. 715.12(7)(a)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which fact most strongly supports independent contractor status under the IRS financial control category?","o":["The worker is paid a regular hourly wage rate","The business reimburses all of the worker's expenses","The worker receives paid vacation and health benefits","Significant investment, services offered to the public, and profit or loss risk"],"a":3,"e":"Independent investment in tools, marketing services to the public, and the opportunity for profit or loss indicate an independent business. Regular wages and employee benefits point to employee status. [IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27]","r":"IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 6 AWG copper feeder with 75°C insulation and terminals (65 A) supplies a fixed motor-driven load and is not a multiple-receptacle cord-and-plug branch circuit. What is the largest standard OCPD that may protect it under 240.4(B)?","o":["60 A","80 A","70 A","65 A"],"a":2,"e":"65 A is not a standard rating (240.6(A)), so the next higher standard rating, 70 A, is permitted when the other 240.4(B) conditions are met. [NEC 240.4(B)]","r":"NEC 240.4(B)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor abandons a job after being paid more than the percentage completed. Under s. 489.533(1)(m)2, F.S., the excess must be refunded within:","o":["Within 24 hours after the abandonment, whatever the contract says","Within 90 days after the abandonment, whatever the contract says","Within 1 year after the abandonment, whatever the contract says","Within 30 days after abandonment, unless the contract allows retention"],"a":3,"e":"Financial mismanagement includes abandoning a job with completion below the percentage of price paid and not refunding the excess within 30 days of abandonment, unless the contract permits retention. [F.S. 489.533(1)(m)2]","r":"F.S. 489.533(1)(m)2"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A pipe-bending machine cost $24,000 with an estimated salvage value of $3,000 and a useful life of 7 years. What is annual straight-line depreciation?","o":["$1,500","$3,429","$429","$3,000"],"a":3,"e":"Annual depreciation = (cost - salvage)/life = ($24,000 - $3,000)/7 = $3,000. [Pub 946]","r":"IRS Pub 946"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A hospital has 100,000 sq ft of floor area calculated at 2 VA per sq ft. What is the lighting demand under Table 220.42 (first 50,000 VA at 40%, remainder at 20%)?","o":["40,000 VA","80,000 VA","50,000 VA","200,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Connected = 200,000 VA. First 50,000 VA x 40% = 20,000; remaining 150,000 VA x 20% = 30,000; total demand 50,000 VA. [NEC 220.42]","r":"NEC 220.42"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which contract fee arrangement gives the contractor an incentive to keep costs low?","o":["A fee equal to a percentage of costs","A fixed fee that does not rise with cost","A fee that increases when materials are bought at higher prices","A fee based only on hours worked"],"a":1,"e":"A percentage fee grows as costs grow, so the contractor earns more by spending more. A fixed fee stays the same, rewarding efficiency. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under ADAAG 4.13.5, a door in an accessible route must provide a minimum clear opening width of how many inches (door open 90 degrees)?","o":["30 in.","34 in.","32 in.","36 in."],"a":2,"e":"Doorways need at least 32 in. clear width, measured between the face of the door and the stop with the door open 90 degrees. Thirty-six inches is the width of the accessible route, not the door. [ADAAG 4.13.5]","r":"ADAAG 4.13.5"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Beginning stock 100 units at $2.00; purchase 1 of 200 units at $2.20; purchase 2 of 150 units at $2.50; 280 units are used on jobs. Using the weighted-average cost method, what is the cost of the 280 units used?","o":["$661.00","$631.56","$625.33","$596.00"],"a":1,"e":"Total cost = $200 + $440 + $375 = $1,015 for 450 units, so average = 2.2556; 280 x 2.2556 = $631.56. A simple average of the three prices is not the weighted average. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538 (inventories)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which part of a hospital essential electrical system supplies a nurse call system?","o":["The life safety branch","The critical branch","The equipment system","The optional standby system"],"a":1,"e":"Nurse call systems belong on the critical branch. Life safety supplies egress lighting, exit signs and similar loads. [NEC 517.33]","r":"NEC 517.33"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's manual workers' compensation premium is $92,000. Safety improvements lower its EMR from 1.15 to 0.92. By how much does the premium fall?","o":["$84,640","$13,800","$98,440","$21,160"],"a":3,"e":"92,000 x 1.15 = $105,800; 92,000 x 0.92 = $84,640; difference = $21,160. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An underwriter uses the rule of thumb that a contractor's work program should not exceed a multiple of working capital. If a contractor has $350,000 of adjusted working capital and the underwriter uses 10 times working capital, what is the indicated aggregate bonding capacity?","o":["$35,000,000","$3,500,000","$700,000","$35,000"],"a":1,"e":"$350,000 x 10 = $3,500,000. Multiples vary by surety and are only a guide; character and job experience also matter. [Surety underwriting rule of thumb]","r":"Bonding capacity (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"At a marina, ground-fault protection of equipment at a feeder or main supplying shore power must be set to trip at no more than:","o":["30 mA","100 mA","6 mA","500 mA"],"a":1,"e":"Feeder and branch circuits supplying marina power outlets require GFPE set at no more than 100 mA at the main or feeder; each individual shore power receptacle gets 30 mA GFPE. [NEC 555.35(A)]","r":"NEC 555.35(A)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A neon tube penetrates the wall of a building at a wet location. What is required at the opening between the tubing and the receptacle?","o":["A listed cap closing the opening, or a seal where a bushing penetrates","Nothing, because the glass tubing is an excellent insulator in the opening","Silicone caulk applied only on the outside of the wall","A 2 in. air gap between the tubing and the edge of the wall opening"],"a":0,"e":"600.42 requires a listed cap to close the opening between neon tubing and a receptacle that penetrates a building, and the opening between tubing and bushing to be sealed. [NEC 600.42]","r":"NEC 600.42"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In CSI MasterFormat, structured cabling and data communications work is found in which division?","o":["Division 26 - Electrical","Division 28 - Electronic Safety and Security","Division 23 - HVAC","Division 27 - Communications"],"a":3,"e":"Division 27 covers Communications, including Structured Cabling (27 10 00) and Data Communications (27 20 00). [CSI MasterFormat Division 27]","r":"CSI MasterFormat Division 27"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Transformers are rated in kVA rather than kW because","o":["the rating depends on voltage and current, not on the load power factor","they can supply only resistive loads at unity power factor","kilowatts are never used to describe alternating-current power","the copper loss depends on the frequency of the supply"],"a":0,"e":"Heating comes from current, and core flux from voltage, so the VA product limits the transformer regardless of PF. [Transformer ratings]","r":"Transformer ratings"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Article 518 applies to buildings or portions of buildings designed or intended for the assembly of at least how many persons?","o":["50","300","1,000","100"],"a":3,"e":"Assembly occupancies are those intended for the gathering of 100 or more persons for amusement, worship, deliberation and similar purposes. [NEC 518.2(A)]","r":"NEC 518.2(A)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Applying NFPA 101 occupant load factors, a 7,200 ft^2 gross business occupancy area (150 ft^2 gross per person) has what occupant load?","o":["24","720","72","48"],"a":3,"e":"7,200 gross ft^2 / 150 ft^2 per person = 48 persons. Using 100 gives 72, and misplacing the decimal gives 720. [NFPA 101, Table 7.3.1.2]","r":"NFPA 101 Table 7.3.1.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A claim of lien must be recorded no later than how many days after the lienor's final furnishing of labor, services or materials?","o":["30 days","90 days","45 days","1 year"],"a":1,"e":"713.08(5) allows recording during the work or not later than 90 days after final furnishing. The 45-day figure is for the notice to owner; 1 year is the lien's life after recording. [F.S. 713.08(5)]","r":"F.S. 713.08"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What does 29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(ii) say about using control circuit devices such as push buttons and interlocks to deenergize equipment for electrical work?","o":["They may not be the sole means of deenergizing a circuit or equipment","They are never reliable at any voltage above 120 volts to ground","They must be defeated before any lockout may begin on the equipment","They cause the disconnect itself to become energized when it is opened"],"a":0,"e":"1910.333(b)(2)(ii) specifies that control circuit devices such as push buttons, selector switches and interlocks may not be used as the sole means of deenergizing circuits or equipment.","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(ii)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"After learning of a recordable injury, how soon must the employer enter it on the OSHA 300 log?","o":["Within 7 calendar days","Within 24 hours","Within 30 days","By the end of the quarter"],"a":0,"e":"Each recordable case must be entered on the 300 log and the incident report (301 or equivalent) completed within 7 calendar days of receiving information. [29 CFR 1904.29(b)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.29(b)(3) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A metal sign pole is set in a concrete footing with reinforcing steel. The installer proposes to omit the equipment grounding conductor from the supply raceway because the pole is 'grounded by the footing.' Is this acceptable?","o":["Yes, concrete-encased steel in the footing is a sufficient equipment ground","Yes, if the footing is deeper than 6 ft in the native soil","Yes, if the metal sign pole is galvanized steel pipe","No, the EGC must still be run with the supply conductors"],"a":3,"e":"Earth and footings are not an effective fault-current path back to the source (250.4(A)(5)). Metal sign equipment must be connected to the EGC of the supply circuit (600.7(A)); an auxiliary electrode may be added (600.7(A)) but never replaces it. [NEC 600.7(A), 250.4(A)(5)]","r":"NEC 600.7(A), 250.4(A)(5)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A job has an estimated total cost of $240,000 and $150,000 has been spent. Assuming the original estimate is still valid, the estimated cost to complete is","o":["$90,000","$150,000","$240,000","$390,000"],"a":0,"e":"Cost to complete = estimated total - cost to date = 240,000 - 150,000 = 90,000. [Job costing]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An accounts receivable aging report groups unpaid invoices by","o":["how long each invoice has been outstanding, such as current, 31-60, 61-90 and over 90 days","the profit margin earned on each job that was billed during the last quarter","the payroll classification of the crew that performed the work on each job","the date the claim of lien was recorded for each unpaid customer account"],"a":0,"e":"Aging buckets show which receivables are late and need collection action. [Collections metrics]","r":"Accounts receivable management (SBA/IRS Pub 334 context)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The purpose of a performance bond is to","o":["pay the contractor's workers on each payday","guarantee the owner's construction financing","insure the contractor's tools and equipment","protect the owner if the contractor fails to complete the contract as agreed"],"a":3,"e":"If the contractor defaults, the surety may complete the work, finance the contractor, or pay the owner up to the penal sum. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A personal fall arrest system is being set up for an electrician working at the edge of a roof. Which combination meets 29 CFR 1926.502(d)?","o":["Non-locking snaphooks, an anchorage of 3,000 pounds, free fall up to 10 feet, and 6 feet of deceleration","Locking snaphooks, an anchorage supporting 5,000 pounds per attached employee, free fall limited to 6 feet, and deceleration distance no more than 3.5 feet","Locking snaphooks, an anchorage of 2,000 pounds, free fall up to 6 feet, and 6 feet of deceleration","A body belt, an anchorage of 5,000 pounds, free fall up to 12 feet, and 3.5 feet of deceleration"],"a":1,"e":"Anchorages must support 5,000 lb per employee (or be designed under a qualified person with a safety factor of at least two), free fall must not exceed 6 ft, deceleration distance 3.5 ft, and snaphooks must be the locking type.","r":"29 CFR 1926.502(d)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which device is a \"personal emergency response system\" as that term is used in F.S. 489.505?","o":["A hard-wired intrusion alarm with keypad, door contacts, and a siren","A wireless smoke alarm network interconnected throughout a residence","A pendant-activated unit plugged into an outlet and phone jack that calls for help in a medical emergency","A hard-wired fire alarm pull station tied to a central station"],"a":2,"e":"The statute describes a PERS as a device plugged into a phone jack or outlet that summons help, and it specifically excludes hard-wired or wireless intrusion and fire alarms. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505(26)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"How many hours of continuing education must a certified electrical contractor complete each two-year renewal cycle?","o":["11 hours","7 hours","14 hours","24 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Per DBPR and s. 489.517, certified and registered electrical contractors need 11 classroom hours per biennium; alarm and specialty licensees need 7. The 14-hour requirement applies to Part I contractors. [F.S. 489.517(3)(b); DBPR FAQ]","r":"F.S. 489.517(3)(b)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A rising DSO generally means that","o":["customers are paying faster","the contractor's billing frequency has increased","overhead has decreased","customers are taking longer to pay, which slows cash"],"a":3,"e":"DSO measures the average days to collect; an increase signals slower collection and more cash tied up in receivables. [Collections metrics]","r":"Accounts receivable management (SBA/IRS Pub 334 context)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 218.735, the retainage provisions do not apply to a local-government construction contract whose total is","o":["$50,000 or less","$1,000,000 or less","$200,000 or less","$10 million or less"],"a":2,"e":"The statute's retainage rules, including the 5% cap, do not apply to contracts totaling $200,000 or less (per the 2022 statute text). The $10 million figure relates to the final-completion list timing, not retainage. [F.S. 218.735 (retainage)]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On a time-and-material job, the contractor's invoice is built from","o":["a fixed lump sum that is agreed in advance regardless of how many hours are worked","only the percentages shown on the schedule of values for each line item","hours worked at agreed billing rates plus materials at cost with an agreed markup","the retainage percentage applied to the original contract amount"],"a":2,"e":"T&M billing charges documented labor hours and materials per the contract's rates; it does not use a fixed price. [Billing methods]","r":"Contract billing arithmetic (progress billing practice)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Special-order or custom-cut materials are usually:","o":["Non-returnable or subject to a high restocking charge","Always fully returnable for a complete credit within 90 days","Credited at double the original purchase cost if returned","Returnable at any time without any restocking charge"],"a":0,"e":"Suppliers often refuse returns of items made to order; the buyer should confirm return terms before ordering. [Purchasing practice]","r":"Returns"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For illustration, assume a statewide average weekly wage of $1,200.00. A contractor with no records used 2 uncovered employees for 10 weeks. At a class rate of $6.00 per $100 of payroll, what is the base penalty (2 times premium)?","o":["$4,320.00","$2,880.00","$2,160.00","$5,320.00"],"a":0,"e":"Imputed weekly payroll = 1.5 x $1,200.00 = $1,800.00; payroll = $1,800.00 x 2 x 10 = $36,000.00; premium = $36,000.00/100 x 6.00 = $2,160.00; penalty = 2 x $2,160.00 = $4,320.00. Using the 1.0 average wage gives $2,880.00. [F.S. 440.107]","r":"F.S. 440.107"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A homeowner signs a $9,500 direct contract for electrical work that requires a building permit. Under s. 713.135(1), F.S., what must be filed before the first inspection?","o":["A copy of the recorded notice of commencement","A claim of lien signed by the contractor","A final payment affidavit from the contractor","A notice of contest of lien recorded by the owner"],"a":0,"e":"For direct contracts greater than $5,000 the applicant must file a copy of the notice of commencement before the first inspection. The permitting authority may not require recording as a condition of the permit. [F.S. 713.135(1)]","r":"F.S. 713.135(1)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"ADAAG 4.28.3 limits flash pulse duration to 0.2 s and duty cycle to 40 percent. For a visual alarm flashing at 3 Hz, what is the maximum allowable pulse duration?","o":["0.20 s","About 0.13 s","About 0.08 s","0.40 s"],"a":1,"e":"At 3 Hz the period is 1/3 = 0.333 s, and 40 percent of that is 0.133 s, which is shorter than the 0.2 s cap, so the duty cycle limit governs. At 1 Hz the 0.2 s cap would govern instead. [ADAAG 4.28.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.3"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.1195, a financially responsible officer designated by a business organization with board approval","o":["is the primary qualifying agent for the field work","handles financial matters but may not be the primary agent","is responsible only for the company's bonds","is chosen by the property owner for each job"],"a":1,"e":"The FRO supervises the financial aspects; the primary qualifying agent is then responsible for all construction activities. [F.S. 489.1195]","r":"F.S. 489.1195"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A feeder run measures 5.5 in. on a plan drawn at 1/8 in. = 1 ft-0 in. What is the actual length?","o":["44 ft","22 ft","66 ft","0.6875 ft"],"a":0,"e":"At 1/8 in. = 1 ft each inch represents 8 ft, so 5.5 x 8 = 44 ft. 4 ft per inch is the 1/4 in. scale. [Scale calculation]","r":"Scale calculation"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How often must first-aid supplies at a construction site be checked?","o":["Before each job, and at least weekly on each job","Daily, by the foreman, before the start of work each morning","Monthly, by the safety director, on the first working day of the month","Quarterly, by the employer's insurance carrier, during a loss-control visit"],"a":0,"e":"First-aid supplies must be checked before being sent out on each job and at least weekly on each job to ensure expended items are replaced. [29 CFR 1926.50(d)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.50(d)(2)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An employing unit has no payroll records. What does s. 443.1215(3) presume?","o":["That it is an employer subject to the chapter, whatever its headcount","That it has no employees during the period","That it is exempt from the chapter for the year","That it is a new employer assigned the maximum rate"],"a":0,"e":"An employing unit that fails to keep required employment records is presumed to be an employer under s. 443.1215(3). [F.S. 443.1215(3)]","r":"F.S. 443.1215(3)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 715.12(7), a written punchlist is timely submitted and the contractor substantially completes it. The owner must pay the remaining balance, including retainage, within how many days after the architect certifies substantial completion, a certificate of occupancy issues, or the owner takes possession?","o":["14 days","30 days","7 days","45 days"],"a":0,"e":"715.12(7)(a) requires payment of the balance, including retainage, within 14 days after the triggering event if the punchlist is submitted and substantially completed. [F.S. 715.12(7)(a)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under Rule 61G4-18.001, F.A.C., a Part I licensee may earn up to how many continuing education hours by attending a Construction Industry Licensing Board meeting where disciplinary cases are heard?","o":["1 hour","7 hours","14 hours","4 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Attendance at a board meeting hearing disciplinary cases earns hour-for-hour credit up to 4 hours per renewal cycle, with 7 days' notice; the first hour satisfies the laws-and-rules requirement. [F.A.C. 61G4-18.001(1)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-18.001(1)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A non-dwelling feeder has a receptacle load of 28,800 VA. Applying NEC 220.44 (first 10 kVA at 100 percent, remainder at 50 percent), what is the demand load?","o":["14,400 VA","19,400 VA","28,800 VA","24,400 VA"],"a":1,"e":"First 10,000 VA at 100 percent = 10,000 VA. The remaining 18,800 VA at 50 percent = 9,400 VA. Total = 19,400 VA. Applying 50 percent to everything, or to the whole load after adding 10 kVA, is wrong. [NEC 220.44]","r":"NEC 220.44"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A WIP schedule uses cost-to-cost. Job A: contract $900,000, estimated cost $720,000, cost to date $360,000, billed $500,000. Job B: contract $450,000, estimated cost $360,000, cost to date $288,000, billed $350,000. Job C: contract $300,000, estimated cost $270,000, cost to date $81,000, billed $60,000. What are total overbillings and total underbillings across the three jobs?","o":["Overbillings $50,000; underbillings $40,000","Net overbilling $10,000; no underbillings","Overbillings $40,000; underbillings $50,000","Overbillings $90,000; underbillings $0"],"a":0,"e":"Earned: A = 50% x $900,000 = $450,000 (billed $500,000, over $50,000). B = 80% x $450,000 = $360,000 (billed $350,000, under $10,000). C = 30% x $300,000 = $90,000 (billed $60,000, under $30,000). Over = $50,000; under = $10,000 + $30,000 = $40,000. Overbillings and underbillings are reported separately, not netted across jobs. [WIP schedule; ASC 606 presentation]","r":"WIP schedule (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Three resistors of 8, 12 and 24 ohms are in parallel. The equivalent resistance is","o":["4 ohms","2.0 ohms","8 ohms","44 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"1/R = 1/8 + 1/12 + 1/24 = 6/24, so R = 4 ohms. It must be below the smallest branch (8). [Parallel circuits]","r":"Parallel circuits"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A certificate of insurance is best described as","o":["a contract that automatically adds the holder as an insured","a full replacement for any policy endorsement","evidence that a policy exists on a given date, which does not by itself change the policy","a guarantee that the policy cannot be cancelled"],"a":2,"e":"Certificates are informational; coverage is defined by the policy and its endorsements. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.1195, a secondary qualifying agent is responsible for","o":["all financial supervision of the business as a whole","only the company's liability insurance program","field supervision at sites where the agent's license was used to pull the permit, but not financial matters","nothing at all; the role is honorary and carries no duties"],"a":2,"e":"A secondary qualifying agent supervises field work for permits pulled under that license and has no responsibility for financial supervision. [F.S. 489.1195]","r":"F.S. 489.1195"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under ADAAG 4.27.3, what is the minimum height above the floor for a wall-mounted electrical receptacle that must be accessible?","o":["12 in.","18 in.","24 in.","15 in."],"a":3,"e":"Wall-mounted electrical and communications receptacles must be at least 15 in. above the floor, which matches the low forward reach. Twelve inches is a common legacy mounting height that fails the requirement. [ADAAG 4.27.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.27.3"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An employee was hired March 1, 2024 and terminated August 15, 2025. Using the I-9 retention rule (3 years after hire or 1 year after termination, whichever is later), until what date must the employer keep the I-9?","o":["August 15, 2028","March 1, 2027","August 15, 2026","March 1, 2030"],"a":1,"e":"3 years after hire = March 1, 2027; 1 year after termination = August 15, 2026; the later date, March 1, 2027, controls. [8 CFR 274a.2(b)(2)(i)(A)]","r":"8 CFR 274a.2(b)(2)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's CM ratio is 40%. If revenue increases by $50,000 with no change in fixed costs, operating profit increases by","o":["$50,000","$20,000","$30,000","$40,000"],"a":1,"e":"Each additional sales dollar adds the CM ratio, so profit rises 40% x 50,000 = $20,000. [Contribution margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor who keeps a customer's advance payment argues, 'I always intended to pay it back.' Under s. 489.126(4)(c), F.S., this argument is:","o":["A complete defense to the offense once the money is repaid","A defense only if the refund was made within 90 days","Not a defense, because intent to repay is irrelevant","A defense only on commercial construction jobs"],"a":2,"e":"The statute says that an intent to repay the money is not a defense to the offense. Criminal intent may be shown when the contractor appropriates the money for its own use. [F.S. 489.126(4)]","r":"F.S. 489.126(4)(c)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies three 460-volt, three-phase squirrel-cage motors with Table 430.250 full-load currents of 34 A (25 hp), 14 A (10 hp) and 7.6 A (5 hp). What is the minimum feeder conductor ampacity?","o":["55.6 A","64.1 A","71.0 A","80.0 A"],"a":1,"e":"Feeder ampacity is 125% of the largest motor plus the other motors: (34 x 1.25) + 14 + 7.6 = 42.5 + 21.6 = 64.1 A. Adding 125% to every motor gives 80 A, which overstates the load. [NEC 430.24]","r":"NEC 430.24"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Knowingly aiding or conspiring to present a false or fraudulent claim against the recovery fund under s. 489.143(10), F.S., is a:","o":["Noncriminal infraction","Second-degree misdemeanor","First-degree felony","Third-degree felony"],"a":3,"e":"The offense is a third-degree felony, with a fine of up to $30,000 or up to double the fraud's value if greater. [F.S. 489.143(10)]","r":"F.S. 489.143(10)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NEC Article 700 requires overcurrent devices in emergency systems to be:","o":["fusible types only, with no circuit breakers","of the same trip rating throughout the entire system","located inside the generator set enclosure only","selectively coordinated with all supply-side overcurrent protective devices"],"a":3,"e":"Selective coordination is required so a fault clears only the nearest device and does not interrupt other emergency loads. [NEC 700.32]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 700.32 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Chapter 9 Note 4 allows a raceway nipple of what maximum length to be filled to 60% of its cross-sectional area?","o":["24 in.","18 in.","12 in.","36 in."],"a":0,"e":"A conduit or tubing nipple not exceeding 24 in. in length may be filled to 60% of its total cross-sectional area; the 310.15(C)(1) adjustment factors need not be applied to such a nipple. [NEC Chapter 9, Table 1 Note 4]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Table 1 Note 4"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Billing a contract at stages, such as 30% at rough-in, 40% at trim, 30% at final, is known as","o":["cost-plus billing","milestone (stage) billing","time-and-material billing","retainage billing"],"a":1,"e":"Milestone billing ties invoices to completed stages rather than to a monthly percentage-of-completion estimate. [Progress billing practice]","r":"Contract billing arithmetic (progress billing practice)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A semiweekly depositor with a Wednesday deposit due date finds that Wednesday is a federal holiday in D.C. How does this change the deadline?","o":["The deposit becomes due the prior Tuesday","Each weekday that is a D.C. legal holiday adds one day to the deadline","The deposit is waived until the next quarter","The deposit becomes due on the 15th of next month"],"a":1,"e":"Semiweekly depositors have at least 3 business days after the semiweekly period closes, and weekdays that are D.C. legal holidays do not count as business days, extending the deadline. [IRS Topic 757]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Terms of '2/10 EOM' on an invoice dated June 12 mean the discount may be taken through:","o":["June 22","July 10","July 31","June 30"],"a":1,"e":"EOM (end of month) terms start the discount period at the end of the invoice month: 10 days after June 30 is July 10. [Purchasing practice]","r":"Vendor terms"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A task budgeted at 400 labor hours is 50% complete, and 260 hours have been used. If the same productivity continues, the estimated total hours at completion are","o":["400 hours","660 hours","120 hours","520 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Hours used / percent complete = 260 / 0.50 = 520, which is 120 hours over budget. Using the original budget ignores the overrun. [Job costing forecasts]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Sign transformers are installed in an attic. What minimum access door size does 600.21(E) require?","o":["36 in. x 22-1/2 in.","24 in. x 24 in.","30 in. x 30 in.","18 in. x 18 in."],"a":0,"e":"Attics and soffits require an access door of at least 900 mm x 562.5 mm (36 in. x 22-1/2 in.) and a passageway at least 3 ft high and 2 ft wide with a walkway at least 12 in. wide. [NEC 600.21(E)]","r":"NEC 600.21(E)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An employer that reported $50,000 or less in employment taxes during its lookback period is which kind of Form 941 depositor?","o":["A monthly schedule depositor","A semiweekly schedule depositor","A next-day depositor","A quarterly-only depositor"],"a":0,"e":"Employers reporting $50,000 or less in the lookback period deposit monthly; those above $50,000 deposit semiweekly. [IRS Topic 757; IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Boxes installed in noncombustible walls (tile, plaster, concrete) with flush-type covers may have the front edge set back from the finished surface by no more than:","o":["3/8 in.","1/2 in.","1/4 in.","1/8 in."],"a":2,"e":"In noncombustible surfaces the box front edge may not be set back more than 1/4 in. from the finished surface. [NEC 314.20]","r":"NEC 314.20"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under F.S. 633.216, fire safety inspectors must complete a department-approved training program of at least how many hours?","o":["200 hours","54 hours","24 hours","120 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Inspections must be performed by a certified person who passed the certification exam and completed at least 200 hours of approved training. Firefighters under supervision need only 24 hours. [F.S. 633.216(2)]","r":"F.S. 633.216(2)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A single-job limit and an aggregate limit are terms used by sureties to describe","o":["the largest bond for one contract and for all open bonded work","the total number of field employees a firm may hire","the maximum deductible on liability insurance policies","the cap on a contractor's weekly payroll and overtime"],"a":0,"e":"Surety bonding programs are usually stated as a single-project limit and a total (aggregate) work-on-hand limit. [Surety bonding capacity]","r":"Bonding capacity (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 25 kVA single-phase transformer has a 480 V primary and 240 V secondary. What is the full-load secondary current?","o":["104.2 A","6.0 A","60.1 A","52.1 A"],"a":0,"e":"Is = VA / E = 25,000 / 240 = 104.2 A (primary: 52.1 A). [Transformer current]","r":"Transformer current"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Where must SDSs be kept?","o":["In the owner's office, to be requested in writing by each employee","Locked in the supervisor's truck, with a key given to the foreman","Readily accessible to employees in their work areas during each shift","At company headquarters only, with a copy mailed on request"],"a":2,"e":"SDSs must be readily accessible during each shift; electronic access is permitted if it doesn't create barriers to immediate access. [29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(8)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(8)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Job cost accounting for an electrical contractor means","o":["recording only the company's total annual expenses in one general account","tracking labor, material, equipment and subcontract costs against each individual job","tracking only the income taxes that the company owes on each job","valuing the company's tools and equipment for resale at the end of each year"],"a":1,"e":"Job costing assigns actual costs to the job that caused them so profit, estimates and bids can be checked job by job. [Job cost accounting]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A surety bond involves three parties: the principal, the obligee, and the","o":["subrogee","claimant","underwriter's agent","surety"],"a":3,"e":"The principal (contractor) is bound to the obligee (usually the owner), and the surety guarantees the principal's obligation. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A very small employer expects its annual employment taxes to be $1,000 or less. Which annual return may the IRS permit it to file instead of quarterly Forms 941?","o":["Form 940","Form 943","Form 944","Form 945"],"a":2,"e":"Employers whose employment taxes for the year will be $1,000 or less may be notified by the IRS to file Form 944 annually. Form 940 is the FUTA return. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Substantial completion typically means","o":["every single punch list item has been closed out","the owner can occupy or use the work as intended","final payment has been released to the contractor","the building permit for the work has just been issued"],"a":1,"e":"Substantial completion allows beneficial use even though minor punch-list work may remain; it often stops liquidated damages. [Contract time]","r":"Contract time (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A subcontractor's work is delayed because the general contractor failed to provide access. The subcontractor should","o":["leave the site for good without telling anyone","keep working silently and claim the costs later on","bill the owner directly for the delay with no notice","give prompt written notice of the delay and impact per the subcontract"],"a":3,"e":"Timely written notice preserves time and cost rights; FAR 52.249-10 uses a 10-day notice requirement as an example. [Delay notice]","r":"Delay claims"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Cord-and-plug-connected equipment operated above what voltage to ground must be grounded on a construction site (guarded motors and insulated appliance frames excepted)?","o":["50 volts","120 volts","300 volts","150 volts"],"a":3,"e":"Both fixed and cord-and-plug equipment must be grounded when operated over 150 volts to ground, subject to listed exceptions. [29 CFR 1926.404(f)(7)(iv)(B)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.404(f)(7)(iv)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 713.015, F.S., the mandatory notice does NOT apply when the owner is:","o":["A contractor licensed under chapter 489","A retired person","A first-time homebuyer","A church"],"a":0,"e":"The section does not apply where the owner is a licensed contractor or a person who creates or offers parcels for sale or lease in the ordinary course of business. [F.S. 713.015(2)(c)]","r":"F.S. 713.015"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which cost is a typical job-site overhead item (general conditions) rather than a labor or material item?","o":["Temporary power and the project superintendent's trailer","Conduit, wire and fittings installed in the building","Journeyman electrician installation labor on the job","The lighting fixtures called for on the drawings"],"a":0,"e":"General conditions are job-related support costs not tied to installing a specific item, such as temporary facilities and supervision. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In an intrinsically safe system, where color coding is used for raceways, cable trays and junction boxes, which color identifies intrinsically safe wiring?","o":["Orange","Red","Light blue","Yellow"],"a":2,"e":"Intrinsically safe wiring may be identified by light blue, and no other color may be used for other purposes where this is used. [NEC 504.80(C)]","r":"NEC 504.80(C) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which statement about Class 3 circuits is correct?","o":["Class 3 allows higher voltage and current than Class 2, so additional shock safeguards are specified","Class 3 circuits are limited to the same energy levels as Class 2 but need larger conductors","Class 3 circuits are always non-power-limited and may reach 600 volts","Class 3 circuits are permitted only for fire alarm systems"],"a":0,"e":"The Article 100 informational note says a Class 3 circuit allows higher levels of voltage and current than Class 2, so extra safeguards apply for shock protection. [NEC Art. 100, Class 3 Circuit]","r":"NEC Art. 100 (Class 3 Circuit)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Section 713.015, F.S., requires a mandatory lien-law notice in direct contracts between an owner and a contractor for improvements to one- to four-unit residential property when the contract is greater than:","o":["$500","$5,000","$15,000","$2,500"],"a":3,"e":"The notice applies to direct contracts greater than $2,500 for single or multiple family dwellings of up to and including four units. $5,000 is the notice-of-commencement copy threshold in s. 713.135. [F.S. 713.015(1)]","r":"F.S. 713.015(1)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a drawing, a revision cloud with a triangle containing the number 2 around a relocated panelboard means:","o":["It marks a change made in revision 2 of the drawing set","The panelboard is rated for two separate services","The panelboard is described in specification Part 2","The panelboard is supplied from the feeder on sheet 2"],"a":0,"e":"Revision clouds with a delta tag mark where a drawing changed under a numbered revision, bulletin, or addendum. Compare with the earlier issue and the revision list in the title block to see what changed in price or scope. [Drawing practice: revisions]","r":"Drawing practice: revisions"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which MasterFormat division contains the Procurement and Contracting Requirements, such as the invitation to bid and instructions to bidders?","o":["Division 01","Division 00","Division 02","Division 10"],"a":1,"e":"Division 00 holds procurement and contracting requirements (bidding documents and contract forms), while Division 01 contains General Requirements that apply to all trades. [CSI MasterFormat Division 00]","r":"CSI MasterFormat Division 00"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contract has 180 calendar days. The owner grants a 17-day extension for an excusable delay. What is the adjusted contract time?","o":["163 days","214 days","197 days","180 days"],"a":2,"e":"180 + 17 = 197 days. A time extension adds days to the contract time. [FAR 52.249-10]","r":"Time extensions"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under the FDOT manual, how long does a contractor have to appeal a weather or holiday time extension notice with supporting facts?","o":["24 hours from receipt","60 days from receipt","1 year from receipt","10 days from receipt"],"a":3,"e":"The manual gives ten days from receipt to appeal with supporting facts; otherwise the right to appeal is waived. [FDOT CPAM 7.2]","r":"Weather days"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under NEC 700.10(D), fire protection of feeder-circuit wiring for emergency systems applies in assembly occupancies with an occupant load of at least:","o":["2,500 persons","1,000 persons","300 persons","500 persons"],"a":1,"e":"Feeder-circuit wiring to emergency loads must be fire-protected in assembly occupancies for 1,000 or more persons (and certain tall buildings). Smaller assemblies are not covered. [NEC 700.10(D)]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 700.10(D) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier sells directly to the property owner under a direct contract. Is a Notice to Owner needed to preserve lien rights?","o":["Yes, within 10 days","Yes, within 45 days","Yes, but only if the contract exceeds $2,500","No, because the supplier is in privity with the owner"],"a":3,"e":"The Notice to Owner is a prerequisite for lienors not in privity with the owner; a supplier contracting directly with the owner does not serve one. [F.S. 713.06(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(2)(a)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor on a lump sum job gives its supplier an Annual Resale Certificate for conduit that is then installed in the job. What is the contractor's obligation?","o":["None; resale certificates make the purchase exempt","Report and pay use tax on the materials it used, since it is the consumer","Collect sales tax from the owner on the materials","Only charge surtax to the owner"],"a":1,"e":"A dealer who gives a resale certificate but uses materials on lump sum, cost-plus, guaranteed price, or time-and-materials jobs must accrue use tax on them and report it on its return. [Rule 12A-1.051(4), F.A.C.; TIP 03A01-03]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(4); TIP 03A01-03"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A motor delivers 18.65 kW. How many horsepower is that?","o":["50 hp","25 hp","14 hp","35 hp"],"a":1,"e":"hp = kW / 0.746 = 18.65 / 0.746 = 25 hp. [Conversions]","r":"Conversions"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A motor controller is installed in a machine room and its motor is 80 ft away behind a wall, out of sight of the controller. Which statement about the motor-circuit disconnect is correct?","o":["Anywhere in the building if it is labeled","Not required for motors operating below 480 volts","Within sight of the controller, and a lockable disconnect at the motor if the motor is out of sight","A panel breaker in a different room, always"],"a":2,"e":"Controller disconnects must be within sight of the controller. The motor itself also needs a disconnect within sight, or an individual lockable disconnect that is capable of being locked in the open position, when it is out of sight. [NEC 430.102(A) and (B)]","r":"NEC 430.102(A) and (B) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A supplier offers terms of 2/10, net 30 on a $12,500 invoice. The contractor pays on day 10. What is the cash discount, and what is the approximate annualized cost of NOT taking it (365-day basis)?","o":["$250 discount; about 2% annualized","$250 discount; about 37.2% annualized","$12,250 discount; about 37.2% annualized","$250 discount; about 24.8% annualized"],"a":1,"e":"Discount = 12,500 x 2% = 250. Skipping it means paying 2/98 = 2.04% extra to hold the money 20 more days: 2.04% x 365/20 = 37.2% per year. [Payment terms]","r":"Payment terms math"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Capacitors connected in parallel have a total capacitance equal to","o":["the reciprocal of the sum of the individual capacitances","the sum of the individual capacitances","the value of the smallest capacitor in the group","the product divided by the sum of the capacitances"],"a":1,"e":"Parallel plates add area, so capacitances add. [Capacitors]","r":"Capacitors"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"For 2026 wages, what is the maximum Florida reemployment tax rate?","o":["5.4%","2.7%","6.0%","7.0%"],"a":0,"e":"The maximum contribution rate is 5.4% ($378 per employee on a $7,000 base). [F.S. 443.131(3)(e); Florida DOR]","r":"F.S. 443.131(3)(e)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Using the NEC Chapter 9 Table 8 note (copper, alpha = 0.00323), a conductor with 0.50 ohm at 75 degrees C has about what resistance at 90 degrees C?","o":["0.5242 ohms","0.6453 ohms","0.4758 ohms","0.5750 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"R2 = R1[1 + alpha(T2 - T1)] = 0.50 x (1 + 0.00323 x 15) = 0.5242 ohms. [NEC Chapter 9, Table 8 Note 2]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Table 8 Note 2"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A project network (all finish-to-start, durations in working days): A 3d (start); B 5d after A; C 4d after A; D 6d after B; E 2d after B+C; F 7d after D+E; G 3d after C; H 2d after F+G. Activity C takes 8 days longer than planned (12 days instead of 4). What is the new project duration?","o":["28 days","23 days","26 days","31 days"],"a":2,"e":"C has 5 days of total float, so only the excess counts: 8 - 5 = 3 days. New duration = 23 + 3 = 26 days (path A-C-E-F-H becomes 3+12+2+7+2 = 26). [Float consumption]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"If a payee fails to give a TIN, the payer generally must begin backup withholding on reportable payments at what rate?","o":["15%","10%","28%","24%"],"a":3,"e":"The backup withholding rate is 24% of reportable payments when a payee does not furnish a correct TIN. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); IRS Topic 307]","r":"IRS Topic 307"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The U.S. Small Business Administration's Surety Bond Guarantee Program can support contracts up to what amount for non-federal projects?","o":["$250,000","$2 million","$9 million","$50 million"],"a":2,"e":"SBA states contract limits of up to $9 million for non-federal contracts and up to $14 million for federal contracts. [SBA Surety Bonds]","r":"SBA Surety Bonds"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Mediation differs from arbitration because in mediation:","o":["A neutral issues a binding award that the parties must accept","A neutral helps the parties negotiate but does not impose a decision","A judge enters a final judgment after a short bench trial","The parties must waive all claims they may have against each other"],"a":1,"e":"Section 489.505 defines mediation as an informal, nonadversarial process where a neutral helps parties reach an agreement; the mediator does not dictate the outcome. Arbitration produces a decision. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the purpose of an end-of-line (EOL) resistor or device on an initiating circuit?","o":["To increase the audible sound level","To allow the panel to supervise the integrity of the circuit wiring","To limit battery charge current","To prevent ground faults from occurring"],"a":1,"e":"The EOL element provides a known resistance so the panel detects opens as trouble, distinct from a short (alarm). [NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 12 / manufacturer practice]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 12 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For straight pulls of conductors 4 AWG or larger, the length of a pull box must be at least how many times the trade diameter of the largest raceway?","o":["8","12","4","6"],"a":0,"e":"Straight pulls require a box length of at least 8 times the trade size of the largest raceway; angle or U pulls require 6 times plus the other entries. [NEC 314.28(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 314.28(A)(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a delta connection, the line voltage is","o":["1.732 times the phase voltage","half the phase voltage","equal to the phase voltage","0.577 times the phase voltage"],"a":2,"e":"Each delta winding is connected across two lines, so line and phase voltage are equal. [Delta connection]","r":"Delta connection"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's foreman asks a new hire which religion he practices and removes him from a crew because of it. The employer's liability is best described as:","o":["Not liable, since the foreman acted alone and without owner approval","The employer may be liable for religious discrimination, because supervisors' acts are attributed to the employer","Liable only if the owner personally approved the foreman's decision","Liable only for back wages, because religion is not covered by Title VII"],"a":1,"e":"Title VII holds employers liable for tangible employment actions taken by supervisors. [42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(a); Faragher/Ellerth line of cases]","r":"42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(a)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor records a notice of contest and the claimant is served on October 1, 2027. Under F.S. 255.05(2)(a)1., what is the last day to file suit on the bond?","o":["October 31, 2027","November 30, 2027","December 30, 2027","September 30, 2028"],"a":1,"e":"October 1, 2027 plus 60 days = November 30, 2027. [F.S. 255.05(2)(a)1.]","r":"F.S. 255.05(2)(a)1."},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A pole footing is checked using the IBC nonconstrained-pole formula d = 0.5A{1 + [1 + (4.36h/A)]^0.5} with A = 2.34P/(S1 b). Given P = 3,000 lb, h = 20 ft, b = 3 ft, and S1 = 400 psf, what is the required embedment depth d (to the nearest tenth of a foot)?","o":["14.6 ft","5.8 ft","10.0 ft","21.9 ft"],"a":0,"e":"A = 2.34 x 3,000 / (400 x 3) = 5.85. d = 0.5 x 5.85 x (1 + sqrt(1 + 4.36 x 20/5.85)) = 14.59 ft. A alone is not the depth, and the 1.5 factor is not part of the formula. [IBC/FBC 1807.3.2.1 embedded posts and poles]","r":"IBC/FBC 1807.3.2.1 embedded posts and poles (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor continues to work for 18 days under a stop-work order. What is the penalty for operating in violation of the order under F.S. 440.107?","o":["$9,000","$1,000","$90,000","$18,000"],"a":3,"e":"$1,000 per day x 18 days = $18,000. [F.S. 440.107(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.107(7)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If a Part I (CILB) applicant cannot show a qualifying credit score, Rule 61G4-15.006 allows the applicant to satisfy financial stability by","o":["passing the business and finance exam a second time","obtaining three written trade references from suppliers","completing a board-approved 14-hour financial responsibility course","posting a $5,000 cash deposit with the local county"],"a":2,"e":"The rule's alternative to a 660 FICO-derived score is a board-approved 14-hour financial responsibility course. [Rule 61G4-15.006]","r":"Rule 61G4-15.006, F.A.C."},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The penal sum of a bond is","o":["the maximum amount the surety can be required to pay under the bond","the annual premium the contractor pays the surety","the contractor's net worth on its balance sheet","the retainage withheld on the construction contract"],"a":0,"e":"The surety's liability is limited to the penal sum, as F.S. 713.23(1) states for payment bonds. [F.S. 713.23(1)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(1)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Outdoor antennas and lead-in conductors must not be attached to:","o":["A chimney","A masonry wall","The electric service mast","A pole carrying only telephone cable"],"a":2,"e":"Antennas and lead-ins may not be attached to the electric service mast, nor to poles carrying open power conductors over 250 V. [NEC 810.12]","r":"NEC 2023 810.12"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A local government must reject a noncompliant payment request in writing, identifying the deficiency and the correction needed, within","o":["20 business days of the stamped received date","4 business days of the stamped received date on the request","10 days after the contract award or the notice to proceed is issued","45 calendar days after the request is stamped as received"],"a":0,"e":"218.735 requires rejection within 20 business days of the stamped receipt date. The 4-business-day period relates to a contractor's overdue notice being deemed accepted. [F.S. 218.735]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a wye connection, the line current is","o":["equal to the phase current","1.732 times the phase current","0.577 times the phase current","three times the phase current"],"a":0,"e":"Each line conductor connects directly to one winding, so the line and phase currents are the same. [Wye connection]","r":"Wye connection"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"NEC Article 640 does NOT cover which of the following?","o":["Distributed public address systems in schools","The installation and wiring of fire and burglary alarm signaling devices","Wiring of audio equipment racks","Temporary audio installations"],"a":1,"e":"Article 640 covers audio signal generation, amplification, and distribution but excludes fire and burglary alarm signaling devices. [NEC 640.1(B)]","r":"NEC 2023 640.1(B)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the Form 941 rules, an employer may pay the quarter's employment taxes with its timely filed return instead of depositing when the liability for the preceding or current quarter is less than what amount?","o":["$1,000","$2,500","$500","$5,000"],"a":1,"e":"If the liability for the preceding quarter or current quarter is under $2,500 (and no $100,000 next-day obligation arose), the tax may be paid with the return. $500 is the FUTA deposit trigger and $1,000 is the Form 944 eligibility figure. [IRS Topic 757]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A cost code is best described as","o":["a standardized number used to classify each cost by job phase or work type","a code the building department requires on every permit application it issues","a password used by the office to approve every purchase order and invoice","the license number the state assigns to each registered contractor"],"a":0,"e":"Cost codes let labor hours and purchases be sorted by task (for example rough-in or fixtures) so actual costs can be compared with the estimate. [Job cost accounting]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A nonexempt electrician earning $24.00 per hour works 46 hours in one workweek. Using the FLSA overtime rule, what is the gross pay for the week?","o":["$1,104.00","$1,656.00","$1,272.00","$1,176.00"],"a":3,"e":"Regular: 40 x $24 = $960.00. Overtime: 6 x ($24 x 1.5 = $36) = $216.00. Total = $1,176.00. $1,104.00 pays all 46 hours at straight time. [29 U.S.C. 207; DOL Fact Sheet 23]","r":"FLSA 29 U.S.C. 207"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For 2026, a new Florida employer's reemployment tax rate is 2.7% on the first $7,000 of each employee's wages. What is the maximum per employee for the year?","o":["$42","$189","$1,890","$7,189"],"a":1,"e":"2.7% x $7,000 = $189. [Florida DOR reemployment tax rate page, 2026]","r":"Florida Dept. of Revenue, Reemployment Tax rates"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 300 A feeder OCPD supplies a transformer tap (primary plus secondary not over 25 ft) from 480 V to 208 V. Applying 240.21(B)(3), what minimum ampacity is required for the secondary conductors?","o":["208 A","300 A","231 A","100 A"],"a":2,"e":"Primary conductors need at least 300/3 = 100 A. The secondary needs the primary-to-secondary voltage ratio times that value: (480/208) x 100 = 230.8, or 231 A. [NEC 240.21(B)(3)]","r":"NEC 240.21(B)(3)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A 125-volt, 15-ampere receptacle in an elevator machine room or pit must be:","o":["GFCI-protected","AFCI-protected","Hospital-grade","Dedicated only"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles in pits, machine rooms and hoistways are required to be GFCI-protected. [NEC 620.85]","r":"NEC 620.85"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An electrical contractor has an annual payroll of $312,500 in the governing class code with a rate of $4.80 per $100 of payroll and an experience modification factor of 0.92. What is the estimated annual premium?","o":["$13,800.00","$16,304.35","$15,000.00","$1,380.00"],"a":0,"e":"Premium = ($312,500 / 100) x $4.80 x 0.92 = $15,000.00 x 0.92 = $13,800.00. Ignoring the mod gives $15,000.00; dividing by 0.92 applies it backwards. [F.S. 440.381; NCCI/FL rating rules]","r":"F.S. 440.381"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For F.S. 715.12 interest purposes, how is retainage withheld under subsection (7) treated?","o":["It bears interest from the day of billing at the statutory interest rate","It bears interest at the contract rate plus 18% from the day it is withheld","It is excluded from the interest calculation on late payments while it is properly withheld","It is treated the same as a disputed amount and bears interest after 60 days"],"a":2,"e":"715.12(5) excludes retainage withheld under subsection (7) from the interest accrual rule; retainage interest runs only after the release deadlines. [F.S. 715.12(5), (7)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Where must a contractor on a Florida public building project record the payment and performance bond before beginning work?","o":["With the Florida Department of State only","With the contractor's insurance agent","With the Department of Financial Services in Tallahassee","In the county where the improvement is located"],"a":3,"e":"Section 255.05(1) requires recording in the county where the improvement is located; a certified copy goes to the public entity. [F.S. 255.05(1)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a commercial kitchen, which receptacles require GFCI protection for personnel under 210.8(B)?","o":["All 125-V through 250-V receptacles in the kitchen","Only receptacles over a countertop","None, if the kitchen is in a restaurant","Only receptacles within 3 ft of a sink"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles installed in kitchens of other-than-dwelling occupancies require GFCI protection. [NEC 210.8(B)]","r":"NEC 210.8(B)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A service contract is billed at $85 per labor hour plus the cost of materials, with a stated 'not to exceed' amount. This is a:","o":["Lump-sum contract","Unit-price contract","Design-build contract","Time-and-material contract with a cap"],"a":3,"e":"Time-and-material pricing charges labor hours and materials as incurred. A not-to-exceed clause limits the owner's exposure and requires approval before the cap is passed. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A county assessed liquidated damages for late completion even though it had full use of the completed project for over two years before final acceptance. The appellate court reversed the assessment because","o":["the forfeiture was grossly disproportionate to any loss the county faced","the contract time had been stated in calendar days only","liquidated damages are never allowed in any public contract","the contractor had furnished a performance and payment bond"],"a":0,"e":"The article reports the court found the assessment grossly disproportionate because the county had beneficial use of the project during the delay. [Florida liquidated damages]","r":"Liquidated damages"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum operating force for operable parts under 2010 ADA Standards 309.4?","o":["10 lbf","8.5 lbf","5 lbf","3 lbf"],"a":2,"e":"Operable parts must be usable with one hand, without tight grasping, pinching, or wrist twisting, and need no more than 5 lbf. Closed fist operation is a recommended test, not a requirement. [2010 Standards 309.4]","r":"2010 Std 309.4"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A detail calls for a NEMA 3R enclosure for a disconnect outdoors. Which condition is it intended to protect against?","o":["Submersion in water for a limited time without damage to the enclosure contents","Corrosive washdown with a high-pressure hose used for cleaning in food plants","Hazardous (classified) gas or vapor","Falling rain, sleet, and external ice formation; it is not submersible"],"a":3,"e":"NEMA 3R is rain-tight (rainproof), good for outdoor use. NEMA 4 or 4X is watertight, and Class I locations require enclosures identified for hazardous locations. [NEMA 250]","r":"NEMA 250 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A public owner cannot divide a large construction project into several smaller projects in order to","o":["avoid the advertising and competitive bid requirements","lower the number of subcontractors needed on the job","reduce the owner's insurance and bond costs","increase the retainage held on each payment"],"a":0,"e":"Both F.S. 255.0525(4) and 255.20 prohibit splitting a project to dodge the bidding and advertising thresholds. [F.S. 255.0525(4); F.S. 255.20]","r":"F.S. 255.0525(4); 255.20"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the Florida UCC (ch. 672), a buyer who has received nonconforming goods must reject them:","o":["At any time before final payment, regardless of use","Within 5 years, as the statute of limitations","Within a reasonable time after delivery, and notify the seller","Within 24 hours, with no exceptions"],"a":2,"e":"Section 672.602 requires rejection within a reasonable time after delivery or tender, and the buyer must seasonably notify the seller. [F.S. 672.602]","r":"F.S. 672.602"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In the forward pass of CPM, the earliest start of an activity with several predecessors equals","o":["the average of its predecessors' finishes","the latest finish of its successors","the smallest earliest finish among its predecessors","the largest earliest finish among its predecessors"],"a":3,"e":"The forward pass takes the maximum of predecessor finishes because the activity cannot start until all of them are done. [CPM forward pass; Pitt CPM notes]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A neon sign's secondary circuit uses three separate pieces of listed flexible metal conduit measuring 40 ft, 35 ft, and 30 ft. The installer wants to rely on them for bonding. What is the result?","o":["Permitted, because each piece is under 100 ft","Permitted, because the longest piece is under 50 ft","Not permitted, because flexible metal conduit can never be used for bonding","Not permitted as the bonding means, because the total of 105 ft exceeds 100 ft"],"a":3,"e":"The limit applies to the total accumulative length in the secondary circuit. 40 + 35 + 30 = 105 ft, which is over 100 ft, so a separate bonding conductor is required. [NEC 600.7(B)]","r":"NEC 600.7(B)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under F.S. 489.505, which description best fits an \"alarm system\"?","o":["Any electrical device that detects only unauthorized entry into a building and sounds a local siren","A low-voltage network of devices installed solely to relay fire signals to a municipal fire department","A set of devices that records video and audio for later review by a property owner or insurer","An electrical or signaling device, or a combination of them, that detects or signals a burglary, fire, robbery, or medical emergency"],"a":3,"e":"The Part II definition is broad: it covers devices that detect or signal burglary, fire, robbery, or medical emergency, not just burglary. Limiting it to intrusion or to fire misstates the statute. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which edition of NFPA 101 is the base for the Florida-specific edits in the 9th Edition Florida Fire Prevention Code (effective 12/31/2026)?","o":["2024","2012","2021","2018"],"a":0,"e":"The Florida-specific edits published by the State Fire Marshal apply to the 2024 edition of NFPA 101. The NFPA 101 edition on the DBPR exam booklet is 2018. [Rule 69A-60.004, F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 69A-60.004 F.A.C."},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A transformer has 1.2 kW of copper loss at full load. At half load, the copper loss is approximately","o":["0.6 kW","0.3 kW","4.8 kW","1.2 kW"],"a":1,"e":"Copper loss varies as the square of the load current: 1.2 x 0.5 squared = 0.3 kW. Core loss stays nearly constant. [Transformer losses]","r":"Transformer losses"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Estimated hours for a task are 920 at normal conditions. Expected overtime and crowding reduce productivity by 12%, meaning the crew produces only 88% as much per hour. How many hours are required (rounded to one decimal)?","o":["1030.4","1045.5","809.6","920.0"],"a":1,"e":"If output per hour falls to 88%, hours needed = 920/0.88 = 1045.5. Multiplying by 1.12 gives 1030.4, which understates the loss. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Compared with copper conductors of the same size and length, aluminum conductors (K = 21.2 versus 12.9) have a voltage drop approximately","o":["2.0 times as great","1.64 times as great","0.61 times as great","the same"],"a":1,"e":"21.2 / 12.9 = 1.64; aluminum is more resistive. [Conductor properties]","r":"Conductor properties"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which estimate normally gives the most accurate price for a bid?","o":["A square-foot estimate from a similar past job","A rough order-of-magnitude estimate for budgeting","A detailed estimate built from a complete quantity takeoff and current prices","A guess based on the owner's stated budget"],"a":2,"e":"Detailed estimates price each quantity with current material and labor costs. Square-foot and order-of-magnitude estimates are used for early budgeting. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which statement describes the surge-protection requirement for the service of a new one-family dwelling?","o":["A Type 1 or Type 2 surge-protective device is required at the service","SPDs are required only on services over 400 A","A Type 3 SPD at a receptacle satisfies the requirement","No SPD is permitted at a dwelling service"],"a":0,"e":"Services supplying dwelling units must have a surge-protective device (Type 1 or Type 2), integral to the service equipment or immediately adjacent to it. [NEC 230.67]","r":"NEC 230.67"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A compilation of financial statements by a CPA provides","o":["limited assurance based on analytics and inquiry only","a guarantee that no errors or fraud exist in the figures","no assurance; management's figures are presented untested","reasonable assurance based on detailed testing of records"],"a":2,"e":"A compilation assembles statements from management's records without verifying them. [Accountant engagement levels]","r":"Audit/review/compilation"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If a contractor's work volume (direct labor hours) falls but fixed overhead stays the same, the overhead rate per labor hour will","o":["increase, because the same overhead is spread over fewer hours","decrease, because fewer hours are worked and less overhead is incurred","stay the same, because overhead is not related to the hours worked","become negative until the next billing cycle in which volume recovers"],"a":0,"e":"Overhead is largely fixed, so lower volume raises the rate per hour; estimates that use an old rate under-recover overhead. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How long must an employer retain the OSHA 300 log, 300A summaries and 301 incident reports?","o":["5 years following the end of the covered calendar year","1 year following the end of the covered calendar year","3 years following the end of the covered calendar year","10 years following the end of the covered calendar year"],"a":0,"e":"Records must be kept for five years following the end of the calendar year they cover and be updated during that time. [29 CFR 1904.33(a)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.33(a) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the required height of the top edge of a guardrail system from the walking/working level under the construction fall protection rules?","o":["36 inches, plus or minus 3 inches","48 inches, plus or minus 3 inches","42 inches, plus or minus 3 inches","30 inches, plus or minus 6 inches"],"a":2,"e":"Top edge height must be 42 inches plus or minus 3 inches; the top rail must withstand 200 pounds applied within 2 inches of the top edge. [29 CFR 1926.502(b)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.502(b)(1)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Spot-type heat detector spacing is primarily determined by:","o":["The detector's listed spacing, reduced for ceiling heights above 10 ft or for obstructions","The 30 ft default used for smoke detectors","The cable gauge on the initiating device circuit","The detector's operating voltage"],"a":0,"e":"Heat detectors are installed at or below listed spacing (e.g., 50 ft x 50 ft); spacing is reduced as ceiling height rises above 10 ft. [NFPA 72 (2019) 17.6.3]","r":"NFPA 72 17.6.3 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What demand factor applies to a feeder supplying two elevators?","o":["1.00","0.90","0.85","0.95"],"a":3,"e":"Table 620.14: 1 elevator 1.00; 2 elevators 0.95; 3 elevators 0.90. [NEC 620.14, Table 620.14]","r":"NEC 620.14, Table 620.14"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"All initiating devices on a fire alarm system must be functionally tested at what minimum frequency?","o":["Every 5 years","Annually","Monthly","Every 3 years"],"a":1,"e":"Initiating devices are tested annually; smoke detector sensitivity is checked one year after installation and every alternate year thereafter. [NFPA 72 (2019) Table 14.4.3.2]","r":"NFPA 72 Table 14.4.3.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In multiwire branch circuits, which statement about the grounded conductor at a receptacle is correct?","o":["It may be omitted if the receptacle is GFCI protected","Its continuity must not depend on the receptacle terminals, so a pigtail splice must be used","It may be continued through the screw terminals if rated 20 A","It may be continued through the receptacle terminals"],"a":1,"e":"In multiwire branch circuits the continuity of the grounded conductor may not depend on device connections where removing the device would interrupt it. [NEC 300.13(B)]","r":"NEC 300.13(B)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A termination for convenience clause lets the owner:","o":["End the contract without proving breach, paying for work done and agreed costs","Withhold all payment for work the contractor already performed in place","Keep all retainage without any limit or accounting to the contractor","Assign the contract to the owner's lender without the contractor's consent"],"a":0,"e":"Convenience terminations do not require fault. The contractor is generally paid for work in place and stated costs, but not for anticipated profit on unperformed work. Check the contract's wording. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A plan shows a lowercase letter 'a' as a subscript beside a single-pole switch symbol 'S'. Using the usual plan convention, what does the lowercase letter indicate?","o":["The switch is mounted at the height given in keyed note a","The switch is on circuit a of the panelboard schedule","The switch controls the luminaires tagged with the same lowercase letter","The switch is an item changed in revision a of the set"],"a":2,"e":"A lowercase letter next to a switch is the common way to tie that switch to the luminaires carrying the same letter, so the installer knows which fixtures it controls. It does not give a circuit, a note, or an addendum. [Drawing practice: switch/luminaire designators]","r":"Drawing practice: switch/luminaire designators (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"On a project, a critical activity is delayed 5 days by an owner-caused change and 5 of the same days by the contractor's late material order (concurrent delay). In most contracts the likely outcome is","o":["time but no delay damages for the overlapping days","full compensation for all five of the delay days","no time at all and full liquidated damages owed","liquidated damages only against the owner of the job"],"a":0,"e":"When both parties cause the same critical delay, the contractor commonly receives time but not compensation because neither party can show its delay was the only cause. [Concurrent delay]","r":"Delay claims (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier's quantity price schedule is: 1-99 units at $1.20; 100-499 units at $1.05; 500 or more at $0.92, with the price for the quantity ordered applying to ALL units. What is the cost of an order of 650 units?","o":["$598.00","$682.50","$780.00","$677.72"],"a":0,"e":"Under all-units pricing the 500+ price applies to every unit: 650 x $0.92 = $598.00. The incremental method would be $677.72. [Bulk discount math]","r":"Quantity discount"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.521(2)(a)1, F.S., a certificate issued to qualify a business organization is issued:","o":["In the name of the business only, with no individual named","In the name of the owner's spouse, who must be a stockholder","In the qualifying agent's name, with the business name noted on it","In the name of the local building official for the county"],"a":2,"e":"The certificate is in the agent's name with the organization's name noted, which is why the license shows the individual and the business. [F.S. 489.521(2)(a)1]","r":"F.S. 489.521(2)(a)1"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 440.02, work is considered 'casual' when it is expected to take no more than how long and cost less than how much in total labor?","o":["3 days and less than $100","5 working days and less than $250","30 days and less than $1,000","10 working days and less than $500"],"a":3,"e":"Casual employment means work expected to finish in 10 or fewer working days with total labor cost under $500. [F.S. 440.02]","r":"F.S. 440.02"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What is the employer's share of the Medicare tax rate for 2026 on a non-highly-paid employee's wages?","o":["2.90%, since the employer pays double","1.45%, matched to the employee's share","0.90%, the Additional Medicare rate","6.20%, the same as Social Security"],"a":1,"e":"The employer and employee each pay 1.45% for Medicare (2.9% combined). The 0.9% Additional Medicare Tax is paid by the employee only. [IRS Topic 751; IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Topic 751; Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which switch type is required for hand-held circular saws, chain saws and percussion tools without a positive accessory holding means?","o":["A lock-on switch that holds the power on until it is pushed again","A constant pressure switch that shuts off power when released","A two-position toggle switch with a safety guard over the lever","An automatic timer switch that cuts power after a preset interval"],"a":1,"e":"These tools must have a constant pressure switch that will shut off power when the pressure is released. [29 CFR 1926.300(d)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.300(d)(3)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"When a bonded contractor defaults on a project, which is NOT an option normally available to the surety?","o":["Require the owner to pay a higher contract price","Finance the contractor so it can finish","Arrange for another contractor to complete the work","Pay the owner up to the penal sum"],"a":0,"e":"A surety may complete, finance, tender a replacement contractor, or pay damages. It cannot require the owner to raise the price. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the Florida enforceability test reported in Sarasota County v. Southern Underground Industries, a liquidated damages clause is enforceable only if","o":["the amount equals exactly ten percent of the total contract price","the contractor agrees to the sum in writing at final payment","the owner has not yet used any part of the finished project","damages were hard to fix in advance and the sum is not grossly disproportionate"],"a":3,"e":"The two-part test requires damages that are hard to determine in advance and a sum that is not so grossly disproportionate to reasonably expected damages as to be a penalty. [Florida liquidated damages]","r":"Liquidated damages"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A grounding electrode conductor of aluminum is being installed. Where may it NOT be terminated?","o":["On a listed lug","On a ground rod","Within 18 in. of the earth, or where in direct contact with masonry or earth","On a busbar"],"a":2,"e":"Bare aluminum or copper-clad aluminum GECs may not be used where in direct contact with masonry or the earth or where subject to corrosive conditions, and terminations must be at least 18 in. above earth. [NEC 250.64(A)]","r":"NEC 250.64(A)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In the SBA Surety Bond Guarantee Program, which bond type carries no SBA guarantee fee?","o":["Bid bonds","Performance bonds","Payment bonds","All types"],"a":0,"e":"SBA lists a 0.6 percent fee for performance and payment bonds; bid bonds have no fee. [SBA Surety Bonds]","r":"SBA Surety Bonds"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"During I-9 verification, which statement about acceptable documents is correct?","o":["The employer may demand a specific document, such as a driver license, from noncitizens","The employer must require both a driver license and a Social Security card","The employee chooses which acceptable documents to present from the Lists of Acceptable Documents","The employer must require a U.S. passport from every new hire"],"a":2,"e":"Employers may not specify which documents to present; doing so may be discrimination. A U.S. passport is a List A document establishing both identity and work authorization. [8 USC 1324b(a)(6)]","r":"8 USC 1324b(a)(6)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 240 V heater circuit draws 20 A. If the element is replaced with one of half the resistance on the same supply, what is the new current?","o":["20 A","40 A","80 A","10 A"],"a":1,"e":"I = E / R = 240 / 6 = 40 A. Halving R doubles current at constant voltage. [Ohm's law]","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Practicing under a revoked, suspended, inactive, or delinquent certificate or registration is grounds for discipline under which paragraph of s. 489.533(1), F.S.?","o":["Paragraph (b)","Paragraph (h)","Paragraph (m)","Paragraph (q)"],"a":1,"e":"Paragraph (1)(h) covers practicing on a revoked, suspended, inactive or delinquent credential. Paragraph (b) is fraud in procuring a license and (q) is failing to display the number. [F.S. 489.533(1)(h)]","r":"F.S. 489.533(1)(h)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A GMP contract is $1,200,000. The actual cost of the work is $1,110,000. The contract says savings below the GMP are shared 60% to the owner and 40% to the contractor. How much of the savings does the contractor keep?","o":["$54,000","$90,000","$36,000","$444,000"],"a":2,"e":"Savings = 1,200,000 - 1,110,000 = 90,000; contractor share 40% = $36,000. The 60% figure is the owner's share. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.116(6), F.S., the board may not require an inactive license holder returning to active status to complete more continuing education than:","o":["One renewal cycle's worth","Two renewal cycles' worth","One half of a renewal cycle's worth","Four renewal cycles' worth"],"a":0,"e":"To reactivate, the holder must complete required CE, but no more than one renewal cycle of CE can be required. [F.S. 489.116(3), (6)]","r":"F.S. 489.116(6)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.131(3)(e), F.S., a local government may require each contractor to post a single bond of up to what amount for compliance with the Florida Building Code?","o":["$500, payable to the local government that issues the permit","$50,000, payable to the owner of the property being improved","$250,000, payable to the Department of Business and Professional Regulation","$5,000, payable to the Florida Homeowners' Construction Recovery Fund"],"a":3,"e":"Local governments may require one bond per contractor, up to $5,000, tied to Florida Building Code compliance and payable to the recovery fund; a contractor providing a third-party insured warranty for a new building is exempt. [F.S. 489.131(3)(e)]","r":"F.S. 489.131(3)(e)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"When the federal and Florida minimum wage rates differ, which rate must a covered employer pay?","o":["The higher of the two rates","The Florida rate, even if the federal rate is higher","The average of the two rates","The federal rate, because it preempts state law"],"a":0,"e":"The FLSA does not preempt a higher state minimum; the employee is entitled to the higher standard. [FLSA Sec. 18(a); F.S. 448.110]","r":"FLSA Sec. 18; F.S. 448.110"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 713.23, a lienor who gives a lien waiver and release under s. 713.20","o":["keeps its full bond rights","also waives its bond claim in a like amount","can sue only the owner","must also record a notice of contest"],"a":1,"e":"Section 713.23(5) states that the waiver of a lien also waives a claim against the bond to the same extent. [F.S. 713.23(5)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(5)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In NFPA 72, a signal that indicates a fault in the wiring or a power source, such as a ground fault or open circuit, is called a:","o":["Alarm signal","Supervisory signal","Pre-signal","Trouble signal"],"a":3,"e":"Trouble signals indicate a fault condition in the system; supervisory signals indicate an off-normal condition of a protected system. [NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 3]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 3 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A 125-V, 15-A receptacle is installed on the outside wall at the back of a dwelling. What protection does 210.8(A) require for that receptacle?","o":["Arc-fault circuit-interrupter protection only","No special protection if it is in a weatherproof in-use cover","Surge protection only","Ground-fault circuit-interrupter (GFCI) protection for personnel"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles installed outdoors at dwelling units require GFCI protection for personnel, and a weatherproof cover does not substitute for that. [NEC 210.8(A)]","r":"NEC 210.8(A)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A schedule of values (SOV) differs from a construction schedule because the SOV","o":["sets the start and finish dates of every single activity","shows the critical path through the whole network","lists the size of the crew working on each day","divides the price into pay items; the schedule sets dates and logic"],"a":3,"e":"The SOV allocates the contract sum among work items for progress billing; the schedule sequences the work in time. They should be reconciled so that billing follows progress. [Pay application / schedule]","r":"SOV vs schedule (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor has DSO of 45 days, inventory days of 10 and pays suppliers in 30 days on average. What is the cash conversion cycle?","o":["25 days","85 days","5 days","65 days"],"a":0,"e":"CCC = DSO + days inventory - days payable = 45 + 10 - 30 = 25 days; the contractor funds about 25 days of costs. [Cash cycle]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A supplier of materials to an electrical contractor (not in privity with the owner) who wants to preserve lien rights must serve a Notice to Owner no later than:","o":["45 days after first furnishing materials","1 year after the final delivery of materials to the job","10 days after the first delivery of materials to the job","90 days after the first delivery of materials to the job"],"a":0,"e":"Section 713.06(2)(a) requires the notice before commencing or within 45 days after commencing to furnish materials, and in any event before the owner's final payment after the contractor's final affidavit. [F.S. 713.06(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(2)(a)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Overhead feeder conductors operating at 480Y/277 V are run over a county road. For voltage and location reasons, what is the minimum clearance from final grade?","o":["18 ft","10 ft","15 ft","12 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Over public streets, alleys, roads, parking areas subject to truck traffic and driveways on other than residential property, 18 ft is required. [NEC 225.18(4)]","r":"NEC 225.18(4)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"F.S. 633.202(13) exempts which buildings from the manual fire alarm requirement in Section 9.6 of the Life Safety Code?","o":["Condominiums, cooperatives, and multifamily buildings under four stories with exterior egress corridors","Hotels and motels that are more than four stories high","All business buildings under 10,000 sq ft with one story","Any building that has a fire sprinkler system installed"],"a":0,"e":"The exemption is limited to condominiums, cooperatives, and multifamily residential buildings under four stories with exterior egress corridors. Sprinklered buildings are not exempt from Section 9.6 on that basis. [F.S. 633.202(13)]","r":"F.S. 633.202(13)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Rigid metal conduit that enters a box and relies on locknuts for bonding at a service must instead use:","o":["A single plastic bushing","Standard locknuts on both sides","A threadless connector with no bonding means","Bonding-type bushings or other approved means; standard locknuts and bushings alone are not permitted for service bonding"],"a":3,"e":"At service equipment, bonding must use threaded hubs/bosses, bonding-type locknuts or bushings; standard locknuts and bushings may not be the sole means of bonding. [NEC 250.92(B)]","r":"NEC 250.92(B)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A local enforcement body may discipline a locally licensed (registered) contractor under s. 489.131(7), F.S. What is the maximum local fine, and how long does the contractor have to challenge the recommended penalty at the state board?","o":["$500; 10 days","$10,000; 30 days","$25,000; 6 months","$5,000; 60 days"],"a":3,"e":"Local bodies may order restitution, suspend or revoke local licenses, assess costs and fine up to $5,000, and a recommended penalty may be challenged to the board within 60 days, creating a presumption of probable cause. [F.S. 489.131(7)(b), (d)]","r":"F.S. 489.131(7)(b), (d)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A metal underground water pipe that is in direct contact with the earth for 10 ft or more is used as a grounding electrode. What else does 250.53 require?","o":["Nothing further is required if the pipe is copper","It must be supplemented by an additional electrode of a type listed in 250.52(A)(2) through (A)(8)","It must be wrapped in insulating tape at the meter","It must be replaced by a rod within 12 months"],"a":1,"e":"A metal underground water pipe electrode must be supplemented by an additional electrode, and the supplemental electrode may not be the water pipe itself. [NEC 250.53(D)(2)]","r":"NEC 250.53(D)(2)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Open service conductors are run alongside a window designed to be opened. What clearance from the window is required?","o":["6 ft, in all cases","1 ft, in all cases","No clearance is required if the conductors are bare","3 ft, except conductors run above the top level of the window may be closer"],"a":3,"e":"Open conductors or multiconductor cable without an overall outer jacket must be at least 3 ft from windows designed to be opened, doors, porches and similar locations; conductors above the top level of a window are excepted. [NEC 230.9(A)]","r":"NEC 230.9(A)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 218.735, when a local government's payment request requires approval by an agent, payment is due how many business days after the date the request is stamped as received?","o":["10 business days","30 calendar days","25 business days","45 business days"],"a":2,"e":"With agent approval required, the due date is 25 business days after receipt; without agent approval it is 20 business days. [F.S. 218.735]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A recorder stores video from 8 IP cameras, each streaming 4 Mb/s continuously. How much storage is needed for 30 days (use 1 GB = 1000 MB, 8 bits per byte)?","o":["About 1.3 TB","About 86 GB","About 83 TB","About 10.4 TB"],"a":3,"e":"4 Mb/s = 0.5 MB/s = 43.2 GB per day per camera. 8 cameras x 43.2 = 345.6 GB/day; x 30 = 10,368 GB, about 10.4 TB. Forgetting to divide by 8 gives roughly 83 TB. [Bitrate arithmetic]","r":"CCTV storage (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An update shows activity C, planned to be 60% complete this week, is only 40% complete. The best next step is to","o":["add the missing percentage into next week's report","delete the late activity from the project schedule","change the planned dates so they match actual progress","find the cause, check the critical path impact, and plan recovery"],"a":3,"e":"The update's purpose is to expose variances early and trigger recovery or notice. Editing the baseline to hide variance undermines claims. [Schedule updates]","r":"Schedule updates"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Before opening an excavation, if utility companies cannot respond to a request to locate underground installations within 24 hours (unless state or local law requires a longer period), what may the employer do?","o":["Proceed with mechanical excavation at normal speed, since utilities were notified","Wait indefinitely until the utility arrives, with no work allowed in the meantime","Request a written variance from the OSHA area office before any digging begins","Proceed with caution, using detection equipment or other safe means to locate them"],"a":3,"e":"1926.651(b)(1) allows the employer to proceed if utilities cannot respond within 24 hours, provided caution and detection equipment are used.","r":"29 CFR 1926.651(b)(1)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A job's estimated material cost was $18,500 and actual material cost was $20,350. What is the material cost variance as a percentage of the estimate?","o":["9.1% over","10.0% under","110.0% over","10.0% over"],"a":3,"e":"Variance = $20,350 - $18,500 = $1,850; / $18,500 = 10.0% over. The base is the estimate, not actual cost. [Job costing]","r":"Job cost variance"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1910.132(f), when is retraining of employees on PPE required?","o":["Only once every ten years, regardless of any change in the workplace","Only when the PPE costs more than fifty dollars per item purchased","When the employer has reason to believe an employee lacks needed knowledge or skill","Every time that a new employee joins the crew on any project"],"a":2,"e":"Retraining is required when workplace changes, PPE changes, or inadequate employee knowledge or use of PPE shows the earlier training is not sufficient. [29 CFR 1910.132(f)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.132(f)(3)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The circuit disconnect for the branch circuit supplying a fire alarm control unit must be identified in:","o":["orange, labeled \"CRITICAL\"","white, labeled \"ALARM\"","red, labeled \"FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT\"","yellow, labeled \"EMERGENCY\""],"a":2,"e":"760.121(B)(4) requires red identification marked \"FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT,\" accessible only to qualified personnel, and lockable in the closed position. [NEC 760.121(B)]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 760.121(B)(4)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 443.141, what is the penalty for a late Florida reemployment tax report?","o":["A flat $100 for each late report filed","10% of the tax due for each month it is late","$5 per day of delay, capped at $150 per report","$25 for each 30 days or fraction thereof of lateness"],"a":3,"e":"The statute sets $25 for each 30 days or fraction of delinquency for a late report. [F.S. 443.141]","r":"F.S. 443.141"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Why do foremen record labor hours by cost code on daily time sheets?","o":["So the owner can avoid paying employer payroll taxes on those crew hours","So actual hours can be compared with estimated hours for each phase of work","So the general ledger will show a higher bank balance at the month-end","So the building inspector can verify that the permit was properly closed"],"a":1,"e":"Hours by cost code allow phase-level productivity variances to be found while there is still time to correct them. [Job costing]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a balanced three-phase, four-wire wye system with linear loads, the neutral current is","o":["1.732 times phase current","equal to one phase current","three times phase current","approximately zero"],"a":3,"e":"Balanced phase currents 120 degrees apart cancel in the neutral. [Neutral current]","r":"Neutral current"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Why can a neon transformer be shorted at its secondary terminals without producing a large fault current like a utility transformer would?","o":["It has a built-in 100 A fuse in the secondary circuit of the unit","It is always wired in series with the neon tube to carry the load","It uses a ground rod at the end of the tube to drain the current","It has high leakage reactance that limits the secondary current"],"a":3,"e":"Neon transformers have high leakage reactance, so the short-circuit secondary current is limited to a safe value (typically similar to the rating, not hundreds of amperes). [Neon transformer principles]","r":"Neon transformer principles (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a commercial repair garage, an area within a lubritorium or major repair bay without special ventilation is classified up to what height above the floor as Class I, Division 2?","o":["18 in.","6 in.","3 ft","8 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Where vehicles are repaired and the area is not specially ventilated, the area up to 18 in. above the floor is Class I, Division 2, and any pit or depression below floor level is Class I, Division 1 (or 2 if ventilated). [NEC 511.3(B) and (C)]","r":"NEC 511.3(B) and (C) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For 2026, the Section 179 deduction begins to be reduced dollar-for-dollar when the cost of section 179 property placed in service exceeds","o":["$2,560,000","$4,090,000","$4,000,000","$5,000,000"],"a":1,"e":"Pub 946 (2025) gives $4,090,000 as the 2026 phaseout threshold ($4,000,000 was the 2025 threshold). [IRS Pub 946 (2025)]","r":"IRS Pub 946 (2025)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A review engagement relies mainly on which procedures?","o":["Analytical procedures and inquiries of management","Re-performing every payroll","Confirming every receivable with customers","Physical observation of all jobsites"],"a":0,"e":"A review gives limited assurance through analytics and inquiry; extensive testing is characteristic of an audit. [Accountant engagement levels]","r":"Audit/review/compilation"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Type NM cable passes through a hole punched in a metal stud. What protection is required for the cable?","o":["A coat of insulating paint on the hole","Electrical tape wrapped around the cable","None, since the stud is smooth steel","Listed bushings or listed grommets covering all metal edges, securely fastened in the opening before the cable is installed"],"a":3,"e":"NM cable passing through slots or holes in metal members must be protected by listed bushings or grommets covering all metal edges. [NEC 300.4(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 300.4(B)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"To be admitted to a specialty electrical contractor examination under Rule 61G6-7.001, F.A.C., an applicant must show how much comprehensive training, technical education, or broad experience in the relevant type of work?","o":["2 years","3 years","10 years","6 years"],"a":3,"e":"The rule requires 6 years of comprehensive training, technical education, or broad experience in the type of electrical or alarm work for the specialty. [F.A.C. 61G6-7.001]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-7.001"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.503(20), F.S., a licensed real estate broker acting as the owner's agent may arrange repair or improvement work without an electrical license if all contracted work is done by licensed contractors and the aggregate price is less than:","o":["$5,000","$500","$2,500","$10,000"],"a":0,"e":"The exemption applies when the aggregate contract for labor, materials and all other items is under $5,000; it fails if the work is divided to evade the part or if the broker advertises as a contractor. [F.S. 489.503(20)]","r":"F.S. 489.503(20)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A Class A GFCI is designed to trip when the ground-fault current reaches about:","o":["About 30 mA, the level at which most people can still let go","About 100 mA, a level that might cause ventricular fibrillation","About 1 amp, which would be a typical breaker trip current","About 5 mA (trips at 6 mA, must not trip below 4 mA)"],"a":3,"e":"NEC defines Class A GFCI as tripping when current to ground is 6 mA or higher and not tripping when below 4 mA.","r":"NEC Article 100 (2023) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the ADA, an applicant who is currently engaging in the illegal use of drugs is:","o":["Not protected as a 'qualified individual' on that basis","Entitled to a reasonable accommodation of continued drug use at work","Protected as having a disability in all cases, whatever the substance involved","Protected if the drug used is marijuana but not other substances"],"a":0,"e":"Individuals currently using illegal drugs are not protected by the ADA, and a drug test for illegal drugs is not a prohibited medical exam. [42 U.S.C. 12114(a), (d)]","r":"42 U.S.C. 12114"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A fire pump motor has a full-load current of 52 A, its pressure maintenance pump 6 A and accessory equipment 2.5 A, all on the same supply conductors. What is the minimum conductor ampacity?","o":["60.5 A","88.0 A","66.0 A","75.0 A"],"a":3,"e":"125% of (52 + 6) = 72.5 A, plus 100% of accessories 2.5 A = 75.0 A. [NEC 695.6(B)]","r":"NEC 695.6(B)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which billing approach best protects cash flow on a long project with large material purchases?","o":["One single invoice sent after final completion once all punch list items are closed","Billing only after the owner's punch list is closed and the final inspection passes","Quarterly billing with a flat percentage and no schedule of values attached","Monthly progress billing with a firm cut-off date and stored-materials billing allowed in the contract"],"a":3,"e":"Frequent, documented progress billing keeps receipts close to costs; single invoices at completion force the contractor to finance the whole job. [Progress billing practice]","r":"Contract billing arithmetic (progress billing practice)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A metal box is used with a grounded system. What does the NEC require for metal boxes?","o":["They need not be grounded if fed by NM cable","They must be bonded only at the service","They must have a green screw only","They must be grounded and bonded to the equipment grounding conductor"],"a":3,"e":"Metal boxes, cabinets and fittings must be grounded in accordance with Article 250. [NEC 314.4]","r":"NEC 314.4"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 218.735(2), F.S., a local governmental entity that wants to reject a payment request must do so within how long?","o":["Within 20 business days, in writing, identifying the deficiency","Within 5 business days, orally, at the next site meeting","Within 45 calendar days, by notice sent to the surety","Only at the next regular meeting of the governing body"],"a":0,"e":"A non-conforming request must be rejected in writing within 20 business days of the stamped-received date, stating the deficiency and the corrective action needed. [F.S. 218.735(2)]","r":"F.S. 218.735(2)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under F.S. 633.228(1), which of the following may the State Fire Marshal order a violator to do?","o":["Stop the violation, correct a hazardous condition, stop occupancy, or vacate","Forfeit the building to the State of Florida","Pay a civil penalty of $50,000 per violation","Surrender their contractor license to the department"],"a":0,"e":"The order may require the violator to stop the violation, correct a hazardous condition, stop occupancy of the building, or vacate. Penalties for noncompliance are criminal, not civil license actions. [F.S. 633.228(1)]","r":"F.S. 633.228(1)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Overhead service conductors of 120/240 V cross a commercial parking area subject to truck traffic. What is the minimum clearance from final grade?","o":["12 ft","18 ft","10 ft","15 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Over public streets, alleys, roads, parking areas subject to truck traffic and non-residential driveways, 18 ft is required. [NEC 230.24(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 230.24(B)(4)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A feeder supplies seven household electric dryers, each with a 5.5 kW nameplate, in a multifamily building. What is the dryer demand load under Table 220.54 (65% for seven dryers)?","o":["34.7 kW","38.5 kW","25.0 kW","19.3 kW"],"a":2,"e":"7 x 5.5 = 38.5 kW connected; 65% gives 25.025 kW, or about 25.0 kW. [NEC 220.54, Table 220.54]","r":"NEC 220.54, Table 220.54"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Signs on a 20 A, 120 V branch circuit are each marked 3.2 A. If the signs operate continuously, what is the maximum number of signs the circuit can serve without exceeding the continuous-load limit?","o":["6 signs","5 signs","7 signs","4 signs"],"a":1,"e":"Continuous loads may not exceed 80 percent of 20 A = 16 A. 16 / 3.2 = 5.00, so only 5 full signs. Dividing 20 A by 3.2 A gives 6, which ignores the 125 percent rule. [NEC 600.5, 210.20(A)]","r":"NEC 600.5, 210.20(A)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What must an employer do with a tool or piece of equipment that does not comply with Part 1926?","o":["Keep using it until the tool breaks and then discard it from service","Allow it for only the least complex tasks until a new tool arrives","Mark it with a note, but leave it on the shelf where it is stored","Tag or lock the controls as unsafe, or remove it from the work area"],"a":3,"e":"1926.20(b)(3) prohibits using noncompliant machinery, tools, materials or equipment, which must be tagged or locked out as unsafe or physically removed.","r":"29 CFR 1926.20(b)(3)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employee never gives the employer a Form W-4. How should the employer figure federal income tax withholding?","o":["Treat the employee as single with no other adjustments","Withhold nothing until a form is received","Treat the employee as married filing jointly","Withhold a flat 24% backup withholding rate"],"a":0,"e":"Under Pub 15, if an employee does not provide a Form W-4, the employer withholds as if the employee were single (or married filing separately) and made no other W-4 entries. The 24% backup withholding rate applies to payees, not employees. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"F.S. 255.20 allows a local government to require prequalification of contractors for bridge, road, street, highway or railroad projects costing more than","o":["$25,000","$300,000","$750,000","$250,000"],"a":3,"e":"Prequalification is allowed over $250,000 for those transportation project types. [F.S. 255.20(1)]","r":"F.S. 255.20(1)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A journeyman earns $36 per hour. Under the Fair Labor Standards Act, he works 50 hours in a week at the regular rate with overtime for hours over 40 at time and one-half (no other pay elements). What is his gross pay for the week?","o":["$1,800.00","$1,980.00","$4,140.00","$2,160.00"],"a":1,"e":"Regular: 40 x $36 = $1,440.00. Overtime: 10 x $54 = $540.00. Total = $1,980.00. [FLSA overtime, 29 CFR 778]","r":"Overtime scheduling"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A Part I contractor relies on a building official's interpretation of the building code, and later it turns out to be incorrect. Under s. 489.129(1), F.S., is this a violation?","o":["Yes, always a violation, because the contractor must know the code","Yes, but only if the work was commercial rather than residential","No violation, even if the contractor committed fraud or deceit","No violation, absent fraud, deceit, or the official's gross or repeated negligence"],"a":3,"e":"The statute provides that no violation occurs when a contractor relies on a building code interpretation by an authorized building official, absent fraud, deceit, or gross or repeated official negligence. [F.S. 489.129(1)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which is a required property of a tagout device attachment under 29 CFR 1910.147(c)(5)?","o":["Non-reusable, self-locking and non-releasable with a minimum unlocking strength of 50 pounds","Reusable and releasable with a tool, with a minimum strength of 10 pounds","Made of metal wire only, with no minimum strength specified","Releasable by hand so the tag can be removed quickly in an emergency"],"a":0,"e":"Tag attachments must be non-reusable, self-locking and non-releasable with at least 50 pounds of unlocking strength. [29 CFR 1910.147(c)(5)(ii)(B)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.147(c)(5)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor buys a $1,200 box of small tools used across all jobs. How should the cost be classified for job costing?","o":["As a direct cost of the first job that happens to use the tools","As a direct cost split evenly among all open jobs at month-end","As additional compensation paid to the owner of the business","As overhead (small tools) allocated to jobs through the overhead rate"],"a":3,"e":"Items shared by many jobs cannot be traced to a single one, so they are recovered through overhead allocation. [Direct vs indirect costs]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1910.147, when is lockout/tagout NOT required for servicing equipment that is cord-and-plug connected?","o":["Unplugging fully controls the hazard and the plug is under the worker's exclusive control","The cord is shorter than 6 feet and the equipment is rated below 20 amperes","The equipment is rated below 240 volts and sits on a bench in the shop","A second employee is standing by the outlet while the work is being done"],"a":0,"e":"The exception applies only when unplugging de-energizes the equipment and the plug stays under the exclusive control of the person servicing it. [29 CFR 1910.147(a)(2)(iii)(A)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.147(a)(2)(iii)(A)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In rising-price conditions, which statement about FIFO is correct?","o":["FIFO produces the same result as LIFO","Cost of goods used is lower and ending inventory is higher than under LIFO","Cost of goods used is higher and profit is lower than under LIFO","Ending inventory is always lower than under LIFO"],"a":1,"e":"FIFO assigns older, lower costs to goods used, so profit is higher and ending inventory reflects recent prices. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"To shorten the billing and collection cycle for progress billings, the contractor should","o":["wait to batch several months of billing into a single large application","submit complete pay applications promptly after cut-off, with the required waivers and documents","submit incomplete applications on time and supply the missing documents later","skip the schedule of values on each application to avoid disputes with the owner"],"a":1,"e":"Incomplete applications can be returned and restart the payment clock (see F.S. 715.12(5)); prompt, complete submissions move payment forward. [Billing and collection cycle]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A bank lends up to 80% of eligible receivables (invoices under 90 days). Receivables total $450,000, of which $70,000 are over 90 days old and ineligible. What is the maximum borrowing base?","o":["$360,000","$56,000","$342,000","$304,000"],"a":3,"e":"Eligible = 450,000 - 70,000 = 380,000; 80% = 304,000. Including the stale invoices overstates the base. [Asset-based lending]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"After a notice of protest is filed under F.S. 120.57(3), the bidder must file the formal written protest within how many days?","o":["72 hours","20 days","10 days","60 days"],"a":2,"e":"The formal written protest is due within 10 days after the notice of protest; the notice itself has a 72-hour clock. [F.S. 120.57(3)]","r":"F.S. 120.57(3)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What fee does the Division of Workers' Compensation charge for a construction-industry certificate of election to be exempt, or its renewal?","o":["$10","$100","$250","$50"],"a":3,"e":"Section 440.05(8) sets a $50 fee per request for a construction-industry exemption certificate or renewal. [F.S. 440.05(8)]","r":"F.S. 440.05(8)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A change order is best defined as:","o":["A monthly request for payment submitted by the contractor","A notice from the owner authorizing the work to begin","A signed written modification of the contract price, time, or scope","A list of minor items remaining after substantial completion"],"a":2,"e":"A change order records an agreed change in the contract sum, the contract time, or the scope. A pay application, a notice to proceed and a punch list are different documents. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"SBA CAPLines are designed to finance","o":["only the purchase of real estate for the owner's long-term investment","short-term and cyclical working capital needs of the business","only long-term equipment replacement for the contractor's fleet","only personal vehicle loans for the owner and the owner's employees"],"a":1,"e":"CAPLines are an umbrella of revolving or non-revolving lines for seasonal, contract, builders and working capital needs. [SBA 7(a) loan types]","r":"SBA 7(a) CAPLines"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The three basic components of a means of egress in NFPA 101 are:","o":["alarm, lighting, and exit","exit, assembly point, and refuge","entrance, corridor, and stair","exit access, exit, and exit discharge"],"a":3,"e":"A means of egress consists of the exit access, the exit, and the exit discharge. The exit is the separated portion that provides a protected path to the exit discharge. [NFPA 101, 7.1.3]","r":"NFPA 101 7.1.3 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"RMC is run under the driveway of a one-family dwelling used only for dwelling-related purposes. What is the minimum cover?","o":["12 in.","6 in.","18 in.","24 in."],"a":2,"e":"Table 300.5 requires 18 in. of cover for RMC under driveways and outdoor parking areas of one- and two-family dwellings used only for dwelling-related purposes. [NEC Table 300.5]","r":"NEC Table 300.5"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A panelboard has a 16-week manufacturing lead time. The pad and rough-in will be ready for it in week 20 of the schedule. If the submittal and approval process takes 5 weeks and shipping takes 1 week, what is the latest week the submittal can be sent?","o":["Week 4, after the pad and rough-in are ready","Week -1, one week before the schedule start","Week 20, the same week the panel is needed","Week -2, two weeks before the schedule start"],"a":3,"e":"Delivery is needed in week 20, so manufacturing and shipping (16 + 1 = 17 weeks) must begin by week 3; approval takes 5 weeks, so the submittal must go out in week 3 - 5 = week -2, before the project starts. This is a procurement problem the schedule must account for. [Procurement lead time]","r":"Delivery lead times (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A building has a prevailing equivalent sound level of 58 dBA and no sound that lasts 60 seconds above that level. Under ADAAG 4.28.2, what is the minimum audible alarm level needed to comply?","o":["73 dBA","78 dBA","63 dBA","120 dBA"],"a":0,"e":"The alarm must exceed the prevailing equivalent sound level by 15 dBA: 58 + 15 = 73 dBA. The 120 dBA figure is the upper limit, not the required level. [ADAAG 4.28.2]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.2"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A one-line diagram uses ANSI/IEEE C37.2 device numbers. A relay labeled '51' next to a circuit breaker labeled '52' indicates:","o":["An AC time overcurrent relay used with an AC circuit breaker","An undervoltage relay mounted on a transfer switch","A differential relay across a transformer winding","A lockout relay used for a generator set that has tripped"],"a":0,"e":"Device 52 is the AC circuit breaker, 51 is the AC time overcurrent relay, 27 is undervoltage, 87 is differential, and 86 is lockout. [ANSI/IEEE C37.2]","r":"ANSI/IEEE C37.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An arbitrator's order under s. 489.129(11), F.S., becomes a final board order unless it is challenged within how many days after filing?","o":["30 days","10 days","15 days","60 days"],"a":0,"e":"Subsection (11)(d) makes the arbitrator's order a final order of the board unless challenged within 30 days after filing; review is not de novo. The 15-day figure is the consent window. [F.S. 489.129(11)(d)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(11)(d)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A multimeter is rated CAT III 600 V. Where is it designed to be used?","o":["On distribution circuits such as panelboards, feeders and branch circuits to 600 V","Only on low-energy battery circuits and electronic signal circuits","On the utility side of the service entrance, including the drop and meter","Only on plug-in appliance outlets and small cord-connected loads"],"a":0,"e":"CAT III covers building distribution (panels, feeders, branch circuits); CAT IV covers the service origin and utility connection; CAT II is for plug-in loads. [IEC 61010-1 categories]","r":"IEC 61010-1 measurement categories (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An audio amplifier output circuit may be wired with Class 2 or Class 3 methods only if:","o":["The amplifier is rated over 100 watts","The speakers are wired in series","The amplifier is listed and marked for use with that class of wiring","The cable is red"],"a":2,"e":"A listed amplifier marked for Class 1, 2, or 3 wiring ensures the energy output is equivalent to that class. [NEC 640.9(C)]","r":"NEC 2023 640.9(C)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the nominal voltage of one lead-acid cell?","o":["2 volts","1.5 volts","6 volts","12 volts"],"a":0,"e":"A 12 V lead-acid battery consists of six 2 V cells in series. [Batteries]","r":"Batteries"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 489.115(7), the financial stability guideline for a Division II applicant may not exceed","o":["$1,000","$10,000","$25,000","$75,000"],"a":1,"e":"Division II is capped at $10,000. Half of the requirement may be satisfied by completing a 14-hour financial responsibility course. [F.S. 489.115(7)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(7)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"F.S. 489.129(1) allows the board to impose an administrative fine of up to how much per violation?","o":["$1,000","$2,500","$50,000","$10,000"],"a":3,"e":"The statute authorizes fines up to $10,000 per violation, in addition to other penalties. [F.S. 489.129(1)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(1)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Job 214 has a contract price of $500,000, estimated total cost of $400,000, and cost incurred to date of $180,000. Using the cost-to-cost percentage of completion method, what is the revenue earned to date?","o":["$280,000","$225,000","$180,000","$250,000"],"a":1,"e":"Percent complete = $180,000 / $400,000 = 45%. Earned revenue = 45% x $500,000 = $225,000. [26 CFR 1.460-4(b) cost-to-cost; WIP schedule]","r":"26 CFR 1.460-4"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which document alone establishes both identity and employment authorization on Form I-9?","o":["A state driver license","A voter registration card","A Social Security card","A U.S. passport"],"a":3,"e":"A passport is a List A document. Driver license is List B (identity) and a Social Security card is List C (work authorization), which must be paired. [Form I-9 Lists of Acceptable Documents]","r":"Form I-9 Lists of Acceptable Documents"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On a liability policy with a self-insured retention (SIR) of $25,000, the contractor","o":["is paid $25,000 by the insurer before any other loss","pays the first $25,000 of a covered loss before the insurer pays","receives a $25,000 premium credit on every claim","has no responsibility for any part of a covered loss"],"a":1,"e":"An SIR is an amount the insured must absorb before the insurer pays. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On a standard 120/240 V single-phase panelboard schedule, a 2-pole breaker serving a 240 V load is shown spanning which circuit positions?","o":["Two positions on the same phase, such as circuits 1 and 2","Any two positions in the panelboard, since the breaker handle ties may be changed","Two vertically adjacent positions on opposite phases, such as circuits 1 and 3","Two positions on the neutral bus and the ground bus of the panelboard"],"a":2,"e":"Panelboard positions alternate phases down each column, so a 2-pole breaker takes two vertically adjacent spaces on different phases to deliver 240 V. A 3-pole breaker takes three adjacent spaces. [NEC 408.36; panelboard practice]","r":"NEC 408.36; panelboard practice"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A 'materialman' under chapter 713, F.S., is a person who:","o":["Installs materials that the owner purchased and supplied","Furnishes materials under contract and does not install them","Inspects delivered materials on behalf of the building department","Finances the purchase of materials for the contractor"],"a":1,"e":"Chapter 713 defines a materialman as one who furnishes materials under contract and performs no labor in installing them. [F.S. 713.01]","r":"F.S. 713.01"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On a state contract of $190,000, retainage is held at up to 5% as allowed by F.S. 255.05(1)(f). A payment request is $46,000. What is the maximum amount that may be retained?","o":["$4,600","$6,900","$2,300","$43,700"],"a":2,"e":"46,000 x 0.05 = $2,300. [F.S. 255.05(1)(f)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)(f)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which lamp type and light color does ADAAG 4.28.3 specify for a visual alarm?","o":["Incandescent lamp with a red unfiltered lens","Xenon strobe or equivalent, clear or white light","LED of any color as long as intensity is met","Neon lamp with an amber unfiltered lens"],"a":1,"e":"The standard calls for a xenon strobe type or equivalent lamp with clear or white unfiltered light. Colored lenses reduce intensity and are not allowed. [ADAAG 4.28.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.3"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What is an electrically safe work condition?","o":["The breaker handle is in the off position and the panel cover is closed","The panel cover is on, and the area is barricaded with caution tape","Disconnected, locked out, tested for absence of voltage, and grounded if needed","A tag is attached to the panel door and the foreman has been told"],"a":2,"e":"It requires determining all sources, interrupting load, opening the disconnects, applying LOTO, testing for absence of voltage, and grounding where appropriate.","r":"NFPA 70E concepts (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A fidelity (employee dishonesty) bond is different from a contract bond because it","o":["guarantees that the contractor will finish the job","protects the business against fraud by its own employees","guarantees that the bidder will sign a contract","covers workers' compensation benefits for employees"],"a":1,"e":"Contract bonds protect obligees; fidelity coverage protects the business owner from employee dishonesty. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Four 12 V, 100 Ah batteries are connected in parallel. The bank rating is","o":["12 V, 400 Ah","48 V, 400 Ah","48 V, 100 Ah","3 V, 100 Ah"],"a":0,"e":"Parallel connection keeps voltage and adds amp-hour capacity. [Batteries]","r":"Batteries"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"At resonance, an ideal parallel LC circuit presents","o":["a constant 90 degree leading current","very low impedance, drawing maximum current from the source","very high impedance to the source","zero reactance with maximum line current"],"a":2,"e":"Parallel resonance: circulating current between L and C makes the line current minimum and impedance maximum. [Resonance]","r":"Resonance"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the characteristic impedance of RG-6 coaxial cable used for CATV and satellite distribution?","o":["50 ohms","100 ohms","300 ohms","75 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"RG-6 and RG-59 video/CATV coax are 75-ohm; 50 ohm coax is used in RF transmitting. [CATV practice / NEC Art. 820]","r":"RG-6 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a burglar alarm system, a 24-hour tamper zone:","o":["Remains armed and will alarm even when the system is disarmed","Only alarms when the system is armed away","Is bypassed whenever the keypad is used","Reports only to the keypad"],"a":0,"e":"24-hour zones such as tamper, panic, and fire are not disarmed with the system. [Burglar alarm practice / NTC Blue Book]","r":"Burglar zones (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size of the bonding conductor or grounding electrode conductor for a communications primary protector or coaxial cable ground?","o":["18 AWG","10 AWG","14 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The conductor may be insulated, covered or bare copper, no smaller than 14 AWG, with ampacity at least equal to the protected cable's metal members. The 6 AWG figure is the electrode-to-electrode bonding jumper. [NEC 800.100(A)(3), 820.100 (2026: 750.100)]","r":"NEC 2023 800.100(A)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which action would LOWER a contractor's break-even point?","o":["Reducing fixed overhead such as unused office space","Increasing variable costs per job","Adding a new full-time salaried manager with no new work","Lowering the billing rate without changing costs"],"a":0,"e":"Lower fixed costs reduce the revenue needed to cover them; higher variable costs or lower prices shrink the contribution margin. [Break-even analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What type of fluid must be used in hydraulic power tools under the construction tool standard?","o":["Any mineral-based hydraulic oil sold by an industrial supplier","Water mixed with antifreeze, to prevent freezing in cold weather","Engine oil that is recommended by the manufacturer of the vehicle","Fire-resistant fluid approved under Bureau of Mines Schedule 30"],"a":3,"e":"Only fire-resistant fluids approved under Schedule 30 and retaining operating characteristics at temperature extremes may be used in hydraulic power tools. [29 CFR 1926.302(d)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.302(d)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A drawing note reads: 'Mount all wall receptacles at 18 in. AFF UNO.' What does 'UNO' tell the installer?","o":["The height applies only to receptacles in unoccupied spaces","The 18 in. height applies everywhere unless a different height is noted","The height applies unless the engineer is notified in writing","The height applies only to the first receptacle in each room"],"a":1,"e":"UNO means 'unless noted otherwise,' so the general note is the default and any specific note or detail that gives a different height controls at that location. AFF means above finished floor. [Drawing practice: abbreviations]","r":"Drawing practice: abbreviations"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign disconnect marking must identify:","o":["The date on which the sign was originally installed and energized","The permit number issued by the building department for the sign","The maximum wind speed rating for which the sign is designed","The sign, outline lighting system, or controller it controls"],"a":3,"e":"600.5 requires disconnecting means to be marked to identify the sign, outline lighting system, or controller it controls (with an exception for external disconnects mounted on the sign). [NEC 600.5]","r":"NEC 600.5"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum use voltage for Class 0 rubber insulating gloves?","o":["500 volts AC","7,500 volts AC","17,000 volts AC","1,000 volts AC"],"a":3,"e":"Class 0 is 1,000 V AC; Class 1 is 7,500 V; Class 2 is 17,000 V. [29 CFR 1910.137 Table I-5]","r":"29 CFR 1910.137 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which container does NOT require a workplace label under the hazard communication standard?","o":["A portable container filled for the immediate use of the employee who made the transfer","A drum of solvent that is stored in the shop for more than one shift","A container that is left unattended for a full shift after being filled","A spray bottle that is handed to another employee for later use"],"a":0,"e":"Portable containers into which hazardous chemicals are transferred from labeled containers and intended only for the immediate use of the employee who performs the transfer do not need labels. [29 CFR 1910.1200(f)(7)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200(f)(7)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Product data submittals for lighting typically include:","o":["The installer's payroll records and the training certificates of the crew","The electrical permit and inspection record issued by the building department","The contractor's insurance certificates and bond forms","The manufacturer's catalog sheets, photometrics, and electrical ratings"],"a":3,"e":"Product data are the manufacturer's published information on the exact products to be furnished. Payroll and insurance are not product data. [Submittal practice]","r":"Submittal practice"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which SBA CAPLine helps small general contractors build or rehabilitate property for resale?","o":["Builders CAPLine","Seasonal CAPLine","Contract CAPLine","Working CAPline"],"a":0,"e":"The Builders CAPLine is an exception to the rule against financing investment property and covers on-site construction or rehabilitation. [SBA 7(a) loan types]","r":"SBA 7(a) CAPLines"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"29 CFR 1926.21 requires an employer to instruct each employee in what?","o":["First aid and CPR procedures, and nothing about hazard recognition","Recognition and avoidance of unsafe conditions and the regulations applicable to the work environment","The employer's pay policies, benefit options and holiday schedule only","The use and maintenance of company vehicles, and nothing else"],"a":1,"e":"Section 1926.21(b)(2) requires employers to instruct each employee on recognizing and avoiding unsafe conditions and the regulations applicable to the work environment to control or eliminate hazards. [29 CFR 1926.21(b)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.21(b)(2)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Cord-and-plug-connected IT equipment in a data room may use a power-supply cord no longer than:","o":["6 ft","25 ft","15 ft","10 ft"],"a":2,"e":"Information technology equipment cords are limited to 4.5 m (15 ft). [NEC 645.5(B)]","r":"NEC 645.5(B)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum clearance of a ventilated dry-type transformer rated 112.5 kVA or less from combustible material, unless it is separated by a fire-resistant, heat-insulating barrier or is completely enclosed?","o":["6 in.","3 ft","12 in.","36 in."],"a":2,"e":"Dry-type transformers of 112.5 kVA or less must be at least 12 in. from combustible material unless a fire-resistant, heat-insulating barrier is used or the unit is totally enclosed. [NEC 450.21(A)]","r":"NEC 450.21(A) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 713.22(1), F.S., a recorded lien remains valid for how long if no action to enforce it is started?","o":["90 days after it is recorded","2 years after final furnishing","5 years after it is recorded","1 year after the claim of lien is recorded"],"a":3,"e":"The lien lasts one year from recording, or from recording an amended claim showing a later final-furnishing date, if an enforcement action is begun in that period. [F.S. 713.22(1)]","r":"F.S. 713.22(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which describes a confined space under OSHA's construction standard?","o":["Any room with a single door that is used for storage by the contractor","Any space below grade, whether or not it is made for human occupancy","Large enough to enter, limited means of entry or exit, and not made for continuous occupancy","Any space smaller than 100 square feet, regardless of its configuration"],"a":2,"e":"A confined space is large enough for bodily entry, has limited or restricted means for entry or exit, and is not designed for continuous occupancy. [29 CFR 1926.1202]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1202"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Manual fire alarm boxes should be located within what distance of the entrance to each exit, and so that travel distance to the nearest box does not exceed what?","o":["Within 20 ft of each exit; travel not exceeding 50 ft","Within 5 ft of each exit doorway; travel distance not exceeding 200 ft","Within 10 ft of each exit; travel not exceeding 100 ft","Within 5 ft of each exit; travel not exceeding 400 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Manual boxes are located within 5 ft of exit doorways on each floor, with a maximum 200 ft travel distance to the nearest box. [NFPA 72 (2019) 17.14.8]","r":"NFPA 72 17.14 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.115(7) (Part I, certified contractors), the board may set financial stability guidelines for Division I certificate holders that include minimum net worth, cash, and bonding requirements of no more than","o":["$20,000","$2,000","$1 million","$100,000"],"a":0,"e":"Section 489.115(7) caps the financial stability requirements at $20,000 for Division I and $10,000 for Division II. [F.S. 489.115(7)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(7)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Multiconductor cables smaller than 4/0 AWG with a total cross-sectional area of 12 sq in. are to be installed in a ladder cable tray. Using Column 1 of Table 392.22(A) (6 in. = 7.0 sq in.; 9 in. = 10.5; 12 in. = 14.0; 18 in. = 21.0), what is the smallest standard tray width?","o":["9 in.","12 in.","6 in.","18 in."],"a":1,"e":"For cables smaller than 4/0 AWG in a ladder or ventilated trough tray, the total cable area may not exceed the Column 1 fill area. 9 in. permits only 10.5 sq in.; 12 in. permits 14.0 sq in. [NEC 392.22(A)(1)(b), Table 392.22(A)]","r":"NEC 392.22(A)(1)(b), Table 392.22(A)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A PV source circuit has a module short-circuit current of 10 A. Before any ambient correction or conductor adjustment, what is the minimum conductor ampacity?","o":["15.6 A","12.5 A","10.0 A","20.0 A"],"a":0,"e":"The maximum circuit current is Isc x 1.25 = 12.5 A; conductors must carry 125% of that: 12.5 x 1.25 = 15.6 A. [NEC 690.8(A) and (B)]","r":"NEC 690.8(A) and (B)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Twelve current-carrying 10 AWG THHN copper conductors (90°C ampacity 40 A) are in one conduit at 30°C ambient. What is the adjusted ampacity of each conductor?","o":["12 A","20 A","32 A","28 A"],"a":1,"e":"Table 310.15(C)(1): 10 to 20 conductors take a 50% adjustment. 40 x 0.50 = 20 A. [NEC 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.15(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 310.15(C)(1), Table 310.15(C)(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Why should an electrician avoid pushing a steel fish tape into a panel or box that may contain energized conductors?","o":["Steel tape is too stiff to push through boxes with tight bends","Steel tape is too heavy to be pushed by hand for any long distance","Steel tape cannot be pushed around the bends in a conduit run","The conductive tape can contact energized parts, creating shock and arc hazards"],"a":3,"e":"A steel fish tape is conductive and can bridge energized parts; use nonconductive fish tapes or de-energize first. [29 CFR 1926.416(a); 1910.333(c)(6)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(6)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under NEC Article 725, a Class 1 power-limited circuit is supplied from a source limited to what maximum?","o":["24 volts and 100 volt-amperes","60 volts and 250 volt-amperes","150 volts and 1000 volt-amperes","30 volts and 1000 volt-amperes"],"a":3,"e":"Class 1 power-limited circuits are limited to 30 V and 1000 VA. The 100 VA and 250 VA figures belong to Class 2/3 sources. [NEC 725.41(A)]","r":"NEC 2023 725.41(A) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A network (finish-to-start, working days): A 4d (start); B 7d after A; C 6d after A; D 5d after B; E 5d after C; F 3d after D+E. Activity C takes 3 days longer than planned. What is the new project duration?","o":["20 days","19 days","21 days","22 days"],"a":2,"e":"A-C-E-F becomes 4+9+5+3 = 21 days, which exceeds the old 19-day critical path by 2 days; C's 1 day of float is used up first. [Float consumption]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which employer is partially exempt from routinely keeping OSHA injury and illness records?","o":["One with 10 or fewer employees at all times during the last calendar year","One with fewer than 50 employees at all times during the last year","One with fewer than 100 employees at all times during the last year","Any contractor with less than $1 million in annual gross revenue"],"a":0,"e":"Employers with 10 or fewer employees are exempt from routine recordkeeping but must still report fatalities and hospitalizations. [29 CFR 1904.1]","r":"29 CFR 1904.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor has contract revenue of $2,400,000 and direct job costs (labor, materials, subs, job equipment) of $1,800,000. What is gross profit?","o":["$4,200,000","$210,000","$1,800,000","$600,000"],"a":3,"e":"Gross profit = revenue - direct job costs = $2,400,000 - $1,800,000 = $600,000. Overhead has not yet been deducted. [Income statement]","r":"Income statement"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For a wet procedure location, which protection is required for 15- and 20-ampere branch circuits?","o":["AFCI protection","A transient voltage surge protector","None; hospital-grade receptacles suffice","A GFCI or an isolated power system"],"a":3,"e":"Wet procedure locations must have the branch circuits protected by a GFCI or served by an isolated power system. [NEC 517.20]","r":"NEC 517.20"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's DSO rose from 38 days to 62 days over two years while revenue stayed flat. For a surety or lender, the most likely implication is","o":["slower collections that tie up cash and raise write-off risk","stronger working capital and an improving quick ratio","lower overhead costs and a stronger profit margin","faster turnover of receivables than the prior period"],"a":0,"e":"A rising DSO means cash is tied up longer in receivables, weakening liquidity and raising doubts about collectability. [Receivables analysis]","r":"Days sales outstanding (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Power sources for premises-powered broadband (power over communications cables) are limited per Chapter 9 Table 11(B) for voltage sources up to what value?","o":["30 volts ac","60 volts dc","100 volts dc","150 volts ac"],"a":1,"e":"Article 840 premises-powered sources are limited by Table 11(B) for sources up to 60 V dc. [NEC 840.160 (2026: 721.60)]","r":"NEC 2023 840.160 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Fourteen- and fifteen-year-olds may work no more than how many hours on a school day under the FLSA?","o":["6 hours","8 hours","3 hours","4 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Hours are limited to 3 on a school day, 18 in a school week, 8 on a non-school day and 40 in a non-school week. [29 CFR 570.35]","r":"29 CFR 570.35"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Who must certify the OSHA 300A annual summary?","o":["Any employee who was injured or ill during the covered year","A DBPR or OSHA compliance officer during the next inspection","A company executive, such as an owner, officer or highest ranking official at the establishment","The safety coordinator or safety committee chair, and no one else"],"a":2,"e":"A company executive must certify that he or she has examined the 300 Log and that the 300A is correct and complete. [29 CFR 1904.32(b)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.32(b)(3)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which cable may be used in place of FPLR cable in a riser under the fire alarm cable substitution rules?","o":["FPL","FPLP","CM","CL2X"],"a":1,"e":"A higher-rated plenum fire alarm cable can substitute for riser or general-purpose, but FPL cannot be used where FPLR is required. [NEC Table 760.154(D)]","r":"NEC 2023 760.154(D)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 112.5 kVA, three-phase, 480-volt to 208-volt transformer has overcurrent protection on the secondary at the maximum permitted rating. What is the maximum standard-size secondary overcurrent device?","o":["350 A","450 A","400 A","300 A"],"a":2,"e":"Secondary current = 112,500 / (1.732 x 208) = 312.3 A. At 125% = 390.3 A, so the next higher standard size, 400 A, is permitted. [NEC 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B)]","r":"NEC 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"What is Florida's late filing or payment penalty for a sales tax return?","o":["2% of tax owed per month, with no minimum","10% of tax owed, minimum $50, even when no tax is due","$25 for each 30 days late, with a $300 cap","1% of tax owed per day, up to a 25% maximum"],"a":1,"e":"A late return or payment carries a 10% penalty with a $50 minimum, even if no tax is due. [Florida DOR Sales and Use Tax tutorial; F.S. 212.12]","r":"Florida DOR; F.S. 212.12"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On a panel schedule, a breaker position marked 'SPACE' differs from one marked 'SPARE' because:","o":["A space is a 2-pole breaker and a spare is a 1-pole breaker","A space has no breaker installed, while a spare is an installed breaker with no load","A space is on the neutral bus and a spare is on a phase","A space is reserved for emergency circuits only, while a spare is for normal circuits"],"a":1,"e":"A spare is an installed overcurrent device with nothing connected, while a space is an open position that can accept a breaker later. Unused openings must be closed per 408.7. [NEC 408.4(A), 408.7]","r":"NEC 408.4(A), 408.7"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The Notice to Owner is best described as:","o":["A recorded lien against the owner's title to the property","A notice that preserves lien rights but is not itself a lien on the property","A bond filed with the county clerk to guarantee payment of suppliers","A lien waiver signed by the supplier after it receives payment"],"a":1,"e":"The notice is not a lien or encumbrance; it must be followed by a timely recorded claim of lien to enforce. [F.S. 713.06(2)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(2)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In MasterFormat, the six-digit number 26 24 16 identifies which type of specification section?","o":["Panelboards","Switchboards","Interior lighting","Low-voltage conductors and cables"],"a":0,"e":"26 24 16 is Panelboards. Interior Lighting is 26 51 00, and Switchboards are 26 24 13. [CSI MasterFormat 26 24 16]","r":"CSI MasterFormat 26 24 16"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum secondary open-circuit voltage permitted for a neon transformer or electronic power supply?","o":["7,500 volts","15,000 volts, nominal","10,000 volts","20,000 volts"],"a":1,"e":"600.23(C): secondary voltage may not exceed 15,000 volts, nominal, under any load condition, and the voltage to ground of any output terminal may not exceed 7,500 volts. [NEC 600.23(C)]","r":"NEC 600.23(C)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An approved change order adds $9,000 to a contract. For job costing, the contractor should","o":["ignore the change until the final invoice is sent at the end of the job","charge the extra work to the overhead pool instead of to the job","add the extra amount to the retainage account until final completion","add the change order revenue and its budgeted cost to the job so profit tracking stays accurate"],"a":3,"e":"Unbudgeted extra work shows up as a cost overrun unless the matching revenue and budget are added to the job. [Job costing]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The board finds an applicant ineligible for lacking good moral character. Under s. 489.511, F.S., such a finding requires:","o":["Any past arrest, whatever the outcome of the criminal case","A substantial connection to a contractor's duties, shown by clear and convincing evidence","A preponderance of the evidence of any past misconduct at all","A customer complaint filed against the applicant within five years"],"a":1,"e":"The board may find ineligibility on this basis only if there is a substantial connection to the professional responsibilities and the finding is supported by clear and convincing evidence, with written findings and notice of rehearing and appeal rights. [F.S. 489.511(1)(b), (3)]","r":"F.S. 489.511"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor bids $250,000 and expects an 8% gross margin on the price. What is the expected cost of the work?","o":["$231,481.48","$230,000","$20,000","$270,000"],"a":1,"e":"Profit = 250,000 x 0.08 = 20,000; cost = 250,000 - 20,000 = $230,000. Dividing by 1.08 treats 8% as a markup on cost. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An electrical subcontractor first furnishes labor on April 3, 2026. By what date must its notice to owner be served to be timely under s. 713.06(2)(a), F.S.?","o":["May 18, 2026","May 3, 2026","June 2, 2026","July 2, 2026"],"a":0,"e":"Forty-five days after April 3, 2026 is May 18, 2026. May 3 is 30 days, and July 2 is 90 days. [F.S. 713.06(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(2)(a)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Builder's risk insurance covers","o":["the structure and materials being built or installed, during construction","the contractor's own office building and its contents","claims by a neighbor for bodily injury on the site","wages lost by workers who are injured on the job"],"a":0,"e":"Builder's risk is first-party property insurance on the work in progress. Third-party injury is a CGL matter. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"When an employer's payroll records are inadequate, s. 440.107 imputes each employee's weekly payroll at:","o":["The statewide average weekly wage exactly","2 times the employee's last reported wage","1.5 times the statewide average weekly wage","The state minimum wage x 40 hours"],"a":2,"e":"For employees without adequate records, imputed weekly payroll equals the statewide average weekly wage x 1.5, used to compute the premium and penalty. [F.S. 440.107]","r":"F.S. 440.107"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contract includes a $4,000 allowance for light fixtures. The owner selects fixtures that cost $5,350 installed material price. Assuming the contract adds no markup on overages, how is the difference handled?","o":["The contractor absorbs the $1,350","The contract price stays unchanged because it is a lump sum","The owner pays the $1,350 overage, usually through a change order","The owner pays $5,350 in addition to the $4,000"],"a":2,"e":"An allowance is a placeholder amount in the price. When actual cost exceeds it, the contract price is adjusted by the difference: $5,350 - $4,000 = $1,350. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Liquidated damages in a construction contract are","o":["a pre-agreed daily amount the contractor owes for late completion","a bonus paid to the contractor for early completion","a refund of the retainage after final payment","damages that are awarded only after a jury trial"],"a":0,"e":"Liquidated damages fix the cost of delay in advance. The estimator must weigh the schedule risk when pricing the bid. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-10.002, F.A.C., the first-offense penalty for practicing on a DELINQUENT license (as opposed to an inactive, suspended or revoked license) is:","o":["$250 and/or probation or revocation","Reprimand plus $1,000-$5,000","Revocation plus $10,000","A notice of noncompliance with no fine"],"a":0,"e":"For paragraph (h), a delinquent license draws $250 and/or probation or revocation for a first offense, whereas an inactive, suspended or revoked license draws a reprimand plus $1,000-$5,000. [F.A.C. 61G6-10.002(h)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-10.002(h)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Can the secondary outputs of two neon transformers be connected in parallel to increase current capacity?","o":["Yes, if both transformers have exactly the same secondary rating","Yes, when the tubing being supplied is less than 10 ft long","Yes, if one of the transformers is held as a spare unit","No, the secondary outputs may not be paralleled or put in series"],"a":3,"e":"600.23(E) prohibits paralleling or series connection of secondary circuit outputs. [NEC 600.23(E)]","r":"NEC 600.23(E)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If no approval by an agent is required, a proper payment request submitted to a local government is due within","o":["5 business days after the request is stamped as received by the entity","20 business days after the request is stamped as received","14 calendar days after the work described in the request is completed","60 calendar days after the request is stamped as received by the entity"],"a":1,"e":"218.735 gives 20 business days when no agent approval is needed. [F.S. 218.735]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which of these commonly occurs at substantial completion under standard contract forms?","o":["The statute of limitations is waived by both of the parties","The contract automatically terminates for the owner's convenience","Warranties begin and the owner assumes utilities and security","All lien rights of the contractor and subs end immediately"],"a":2,"e":"Substantial completion is frequently the date that warranties start and that responsibilities for insurance, utilities and security shift to the owner. Lien deadlines run from final furnishing, not from this date. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On $1,560.00 of weekly gross pay, the employer's Social Security and Medicare tax is","o":["$238.68","$96.72","$119.34","$110.16"],"a":2,"e":"1,560.00 x 7.65% = 119.34. Overtime premium pay is also subject to FICA. [IRS Pub 15 (2026), Sec. 1]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor carries an average of $85,000 in inventory and estimates carrying cost at 22% a year (storage, insurance, shrink and capital). What is the annual carrying cost?","o":["$386,363.64","$18,700.00","$1,870.00","$66,300.00"],"a":1,"e":"Carrying cost = $85,000 x 0.22 = $18,700.00. [Inventory management]","r":"Carrying cost"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The NFPA 101 Life Safety Code requires emergency lighting for means of egress to operate for a minimum of:","o":["1 1/2 hours (90 minutes)","3 hours (180 minutes)","1 hour (60 minutes)","30 minutes (1/2 hour)"],"a":0,"e":"Emergency illumination must last at least 1 1/2 hours after normal power fails. Thirty minutes was the old figure for some existing buildings. [NFPA 101, 7.9.2.1]","r":"NFPA 101 7.9.2.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A county code enforcement officer issues a citation under s. 489.127(5), F.S. What is the maximum civil penalty on the citation?","o":["$2,000","$500","$5,000","$10,000"],"a":0,"e":"The local governing body sets a penalty schedule, but the citation's maximum civil penalty may not exceed $2,000. The person must respond within 10 days, excluding weekends and holidays. [F.S. 489.127(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.127(5)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A covered private Florida employer must use E-Verify within how many business days after a new employee begins work for pay?","o":["1 business day","3 business days","5 business days","10 calendar days"],"a":1,"e":"The employer verifies each new employee through E-Verify no later than three business days after the first day of work for pay. [F.S. 448.095]","r":"F.S. 448.095"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Rule 61G4-15.006, F.A.C. refers to which minimum FICO-derived credit score as proof of financial stability?","o":["660","500","580","720"],"a":0,"e":"The Board may refuse an applicant who does not provide proof of a FICO-derived score of 660 or higher (or an approved alternative). [F.A.C. 61G4-15.006(2)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-15.006(2)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.532(1)(b), F.S., does failing to hold a chapter 205 local business tax receipt, by itself, make a contractor 'unlicensed'?","o":["Yes, any missing local receipt makes the contractor unlicensed","Yes, but only for contracts with a price over $2,500","Yes, but only if the contractor was previously disciplined","No, lacking a chapter 205 occupational license does not make one unlicensed"],"a":3,"e":"The statute says that failure to hold an occupational license certificate under chapter 205 does not make a person unlicensed for purposes of enforceability. [F.S. 489.532(1)(b)]","r":"F.S. 489.532(1)(b)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A start-to-start relationship with a 3-day lag between pulling wire and terminating devices means","o":["terminations may start 3 days after pulling starts","terminations must finish 3 days after pulling finishes","terminations start 3 days before pulling ends","terminations start 3 days before pulling starts"],"a":0,"e":"In an SS+3 link, the successor starts no sooner than 3 days after the predecessor starts, letting the activities overlap. [Precedence diagramming, lag]","r":"Lead and lag (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A stockroom uses 12 units per day, the supplier's lead time is 9 days, and the contractor wants a safety stock of 30 units. What is the reorder point?","o":["108 units","42 units","138 units","150 units"],"a":2,"e":"Reorder point = daily usage x lead time + safety stock = 12 x 9 + 30 = 138. [Inventory management]","r":"Reorder point"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A normally closed magnetic reed door contact in a burglar alarm zone produces an alarm when:","o":["The door is closed","The magnet moves away from the switch, opening the circuit","Power is applied to the contact","The panel is armed"],"a":1,"e":"A closed loop opens when the door opens, which the panel sees as a violation. [Burglar alarm practice]","r":"Door contacts (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employee's injury occurs entirely at the employer's job site and requires work restrictions assigned by a physician. Recording is based on:","o":["Whether it is work-related, a new case, and meets a general recording criterion","Whether OSHA later cites the employer for a violation related to it","Whether the employee files a workers' compensation claim for the injury","Whether the employer was at fault for the unsafe condition"],"a":0,"e":"Recordability depends on the criteria in 1904.4, work-relatedness (1904.5), and new case (1904.6), not on fault or workers' comp filings. [29 CFR 1904.4-.7]","r":"29 CFR 1904.4-.7"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Wet (plastic) concrete and cement are caustic to skin. What is the best precaution for workers placing concrete?","o":["Wear waterproof gloves and boots, and rinse exposed skin promptly","Rinse the skin with vinegar after the pour is finished","Wear thin cotton gloves so the concrete can be felt","Work barehanded so that the finish can be sensed"],"a":0,"e":"Fresh cement is alkaline and can cause chemical burns, so skin contact should be avoided and any exposure rinsed with clean water promptly. [PCA safety guidance on wet concrete]","r":"PCA safety guidance on wet concrete (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Interior metal water piping located more than 5 ft from the point of entrance to the building:","o":["May serve as the grounding electrode conductor if painted","Must be at least 2 in. in diameter to be used","May not be used as part of the grounding electrode system or as a conductor to interconnect electrodes","May be used as the sole grounding electrode"],"a":2,"e":"Interior metal water piping beyond 5 ft from the point of entrance may not be used as part of the grounding electrode system or as a conductor to interconnect electrodes. [NEC 250.68(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 250.68(C)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A secondary qualifying agent under s. 489.1195(2)(e), F.S., is responsible for:","o":["Field work at sites where its license pulled the permit, but not finances","All financial matters of the entire business organization","Every job site of the business, regardless of whose license pulled the permit","Only the corporate tax filings and annual reports of the business"],"a":0,"e":"A secondary qualifying agent is responsible only for field work at sites where its license was used to obtain a building permit and for other work it accepts; it has no financial supervision responsibility. [F.S. 489.1195(2)(e)]","r":"F.S. 489.1195(2)(e)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Many contractors add 30% to their costs and believe they earn a 30% margin. In reality, the gross margin on price is","o":["23.1%","30.0%","33.3%","70.0%"],"a":0,"e":"Margin = markup / (1 + markup) = 0.30 / 1.30 = 23.1%. To net 30% margin the markup must be 42.9%. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which receptacle type is required for a 100-ampere shore power receptacle at a marina?","o":["Standard straight-blade","NEMA 5-20R","Twist-lock 20 A","Pin-and-sleeve type"],"a":3,"e":"Receptacles rated over 60 A must be pin-and-sleeve type. [NEC 555.33(A)(3)]","r":"NEC 555.33(A)(3) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Overhead applied to jobs for the year totals $216,000 but actual overhead incurred was $225,000. The overhead is","o":["under-applied by $9,000","over-applied by $9,000","under-applied by $225,000","properly applied; no adjustment is needed"],"a":0,"e":"Applied 216,000 < actual 225,000, so overhead was under-recovered by 9,000; job costs were understated. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 1,000 W, 120 V resistive element is mistakenly connected to 240 V. Ignoring burnout, the power it would try to dissipate is","o":["2,000 W","4,000 W","1,732 W","500 W"],"a":1,"e":"Current doubles and voltage doubles, so P = 1000 x (240/120)^2 = 4000 W. That is why the element fails quickly. [Power formula]","r":"Power formula"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A project network (all finish-to-start, durations in working days): A 3d (start); B 5d after A; C 4d after A; D 6d after B; E 2d after B+C; F 7d after D+E; G 3d after C; H 2d after F+G. Activity E takes 3 days longer than planned. What is the effect on the project completion date?","o":["A 3-day delay to the project finish","No delay; E has 4 days of total float","A 4-day delay to the project finish","A 1-day delay to the project finish"],"a":1,"e":"E has total float of 4 days (A-B-E-F-H is 19 days vs. 23), so a 3-day slip leaves 1 day of float and does not move the finish. [Float consumption]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How long is the information provided in a Florida one-call locate notice valid?","o":["10 business days after the notice, and then it must be renewed by phone only","60 calendar days after the notice, or until the project is half complete","One full year after the notice, if the original excavator remains on site","30 calendar days after it is provided"],"a":3,"e":"The excavation notice information is valid for 30 calendar days after it is provided; work continuing past that requires a new notice. [F.S. 556.105]","r":"F.S. 556.105"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a typical architectural or electrical plan set, the abbreviation 'MLO' on a panel schedule means:","o":["Maximum load operating, the panel's design demand","Multi-level overcurrent protection","Main lugs only, so the panel has no main breaker","Metering location outside"],"a":2,"e":"MLO means main lugs only. The panel is fed through lugs and relies on the upstream feeder overcurrent device, not an integral main breaker. NEC 408.36 still requires panelboard overcurrent protection, which may be provided at the feeder source. [NEC 408.36]","r":"NEC 408.36"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An electrician installs a wall receptacle in a public lobby with the bottom of the receptacle opening 13 in. above the finished floor. Under ADAAG 4.27.3, this installation is:","o":["noncompliant, since wall receptacles must be at least 15 in. above the floor","compliant, since 12 in. is the accepted minimum for wall receptacles","noncompliant, since the required minimum height is 24 in. above the floor","compliant, since receptacles are exempt from accessible reach ranges"],"a":0,"e":"4.27.3 requires wall-mounted receptacles to be at least 15 in. above the floor, so 13 in. is too low. The receptacle is not exempt unless it is for special equipment or not meant for occupant use. [ADAAG 4.27.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.27.3"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A homeowners association representing 120 parcels serves a Chapter 558 notice. Under s. 558.004(5), F.S., the written response from the served party is due within:","o":["45 days of service","60 days of service","75 days of service","120 days of service"],"a":2,"e":"For an association of more than 20 parcels the response period is 75 days instead of 45. The 120-day figure is the pre-suit notice period for such an association. [F.S. 558.004(1)(a), (5)]","r":"F.S. 558.004(1)(a), (5)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A dealer sells a single taxable item for $7,200 delivered to a county with a 1% discretionary surtax. Using the state rate of 6%, what is the total tax?","o":["$504.00","$432.00","$504.00 with no cap","$482.00"],"a":3,"e":"State tax is 6% x $7,200 = $432.00; surtax applies only to the first $5,000: 1% x $5,000 = $50.00. Total = $482.00. [F.S. 212.05; F.S. 212.054(2)(b)1]","r":"F.S. 212.05; 212.054(2)(b)1"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"When a buyer returns defective goods and wants to reduce what it owes, the buyer may send the vendor a:","o":["Debit memo","Credit memo","Packing slip","Lien waiver"],"a":0,"e":"The buyer issues a debit memo to reduce its payable; the vendor issues a credit memo. [Accounts payable practice]","r":"Debit memo vs credit memo"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Unlisted outside plant communications cable entering a building may extend into spaces other than risers and plenums for a maximum of:","o":["15 m (50 ft) from the point of entrance","3 m (10 ft)","30 m (100 ft)","6 m (20 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"Unlisted outside plant cable is limited to 50 ft inside the building, measured from the point of entrance, and must terminate in an enclosure or on a listed primary protector. [NEC 800.48 (2026 text confirms)]","r":"NEC 2023 800.48"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"If the sign disconnect is not within sight of every section of the sign that can be energized, what additional requirement applies?","o":["It must be lockable open, with its location marked on the sign","It must be a fused switch that is rated for the sign load current","It must be mounted on a separate pole located right next to the sign","It must be mounted at least 6 ft above the floor or grade level"],"a":0,"e":"600.6(A)(2) requires lockable-open capability where the disconnect is out of line of sight, together with a permanent field-applied marking identifying its location. [NEC 600.6(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 600.6(A)(2)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which ANSI/IEEE C37.2 device number is conventionally used for an instantaneous overcurrent relay?","o":["27","86","50","59"],"a":2,"e":"Device 50 is instantaneous overcurrent. 59 is overvoltage, 27 is undervoltage, and 86 is a lockout relay. [ANSI/IEEE C37.2]","r":"ANSI/IEEE C37.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum channel length for horizontal twisted-pair copper cabling under TIA-568?","o":["90 m (295 ft), including patch cords","150 m (492 ft), including patch cords","55 m (180 ft), including patch cords","100 m (328 ft), including patch cords"],"a":3,"e":"The 100 m channel is made of a 90 m permanent link plus up to 10 m of patch and equipment cords. 90 m is the permanent link only. [ANSI/TIA-568.2]","r":"TIA-568.2 horizontal cabling (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a purely resistive DC circuit, one watt is equal to","o":["one ohm times one ampere","one volt times one ampere","one volt divided by one ampere","one ampere divided by one ohm"],"a":1,"e":"Power in watts = volts x amperes. Volts divided by amperes is ohms. [Power formula]","r":"Power formula"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under 300.3(C), ac and dc circuits of 1000 V ac / 1500 V dc or less may be in the same raceway if:","o":["The raceway is nonmetallic","The dc circuits use only aluminum conductors","All conductors have insulation rated at least equal to the maximum circuit voltage applied to any conductor in the raceway","The ac circuits are less than 120 V"],"a":2,"e":"Conductors of ac and dc circuits within these limits may occupy the same wiring enclosure, cable or raceway provided all have insulation rating at least equal to the maximum voltage applied to any conductor within it. [NEC 300.3(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 300.3(C)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 443.1216, certain agent-drivers, commission-drivers, and traveling salespeople are covered employees only if which three conditions are met?","o":["Weekly pay, a company vehicle, and a written contract","Ten or more employees, $1,500 payroll, and 20 weeks","A Florida license, a surety bond, and liability insurance","Personal services, no large facilities investment, and a continuing relationship"],"a":3,"e":"The statute requires that substantially all services be performed personally, no substantial investment in facilities other than transportation, and that the services be part of a continuing relationship rather than a single transaction. [F.S. 443.1216(1)(a)3-4]","r":"F.S. 443.1216(1)(a)3-4"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"How far (one way) can 16 A be carried on 10 AWG copper (10,380 CM, K = 12.9) at 240 V single-phase and still hold the drop to 3 percent?","o":["314 ft","91 ft","362 ft","181 ft"],"a":3,"e":"L = VD x CM / (2 x K x I) = 7.2 x 10,380 / (2 x 12.9 x 16) = 181 ft. [Voltage drop]","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 48 V, 200 Ah battery bank stores approximately how much energy?","o":["0.24 kWh","248 kWh","9,600 kWh","9.6 kWh"],"a":3,"e":"Energy = V x Ah = 9,600 Wh = 9.6 kWh. [Batteries]","r":"Batteries"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Using a target margin of 22% on a $3,200 job cost, the price is","o":["$4,102.56","$3,904.00","$2,496.00","$2,622.95"],"a":0,"e":"3,200 / 0.78 = 4,102.56. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, 20-ampere receptacle is installed outdoors at a commercial storefront to serve a temporary plug-in sign. Besides the sign requirements of Article 600, what protection does NEC 210.8 require for this outdoor receptacle?","o":["Ground-fault circuit-interrupter protection for personnel","Arc-fault circuit-interrupter protection only","Surge-protective device protection only","No additional protection is required"],"a":0,"e":"NEC 210.8 requires GFCI protection for personnel for 125-volt through 250-volt receptacles installed outdoors in the locations it lists (for other than dwellings, this includes outdoor receptacles). Portable signs also need the integral GFCI of 600.10(C) in wet or damp locations. [NEC 210.8, 600.10(C)]","r":"NEC 210.8, 600.10(C)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A load draws 50 kVA and delivers 40 kW of true power. What is its power factor?","o":["0.64","1.25","0.80","0.60"],"a":2,"e":"PF = kW / kVA = 40 / 50 = 0.80. 1.25 is inverted and impossible. [Power factor]","r":"Power factor"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A riser diagram differs from a one-line diagram in that the riser diagram primarily:","o":["Shows only the layout of the grounding electrode system","Shows the vertical arrangement of equipment and feeders between floors","Lists the branch-circuit loads on each floor in detail","Shows relay settings and device time-current curves"],"a":1,"e":"A riser diagram shows how the feeders, panels and equipment relate floor-to-floor in a building. A one-line shows electrical connectivity and ratings but not necessarily the vertical physical layout. [Drawing practice: riser diagrams]","r":"Drawing practice: riser diagrams"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.515, an applicant seeking a workers' compensation exemption must file an affidavit promising to obtain the exemption within how many days after the certificate or registration is first issued?","o":["10 days","30 days","60 days","120 days"],"a":1,"e":"The affidavit commits the applicant to obtain the exemption within 30 days of initial issuance. [F.S. 489.515]","r":"F.S. 489.515"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A small storage room has a ceiling exactly 7 ft above the floor. Under the ADAAG 4.28.3 mounting rule, the visual alarm lens must be mounted no higher than:","o":["84 in., which is at the ceiling line","72 in., which is 12 in. below the ceiling","78 in., which is 6 in. below the ceiling","80 in., which is 4 in. below the ceiling"],"a":2,"e":"The rule is 80 in. above the floor or 6 in. below the ceiling, whichever is lower. A 7 ft ceiling is 84 in., and 84 - 6 = 78 in., which is lower than 80 in. [ADAAG 4.28.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.3"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Which item is NOT acceptable on a certificate of insurance filed for an electrical license under Rule 61G6-5.008?","o":["A binder number in place of the policy number","The exact legal name of the insured","The policy effective and expiration dates","The agent's signature"],"a":0,"e":"The rule requires the policy number; binder numbers are not accepted. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A Class 2 power source for sign illumination is a power supply that:","o":["Is listed, limits its output power, and has reduced shock and fire hazard","Has an output above 600 V so that long runs can be used","Is unlisted and is protected by a 100 A fuse on its primary","Provides only an alternating-current output at 120 V"],"a":0,"e":"Class 2 circuits are power-limited by a listed source, so they present reduced fire and shock initiation hazard; Article 600 requires the source to be listed for sign use or part of a listed sign. [NEC Art. 725 Class 2; Art. 600]","r":"NEC Art. 725 Class 2; Art. 600 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A Florida sales tax return is due on the 1st of the month after the reporting period and is late after which day?","o":["The 10th","The 15th","The last day of the month","The 20th"],"a":3,"e":"Returns and payments are due on the 1st and are late after the 20th (moved to the next business day if it falls on a weekend or holiday for the permitted filing methods). [Florida DOR Sales and Use Tax tutorial; F.S. 212.11]","r":"Florida DOR; F.S. 212.11"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In the cost-to-cost percentage-of-completion method, which item is used to measure progress?","o":["Cash collected to date compared with the contract price","Months elapsed compared with total months","Costs incurred to date compared with total estimated costs","Invoices sent compared with invoices planned"],"a":2,"e":"IRC 460(b)(1) generally measures completion by comparing costs allocated to the contract and incurred before year-end with the total estimated contract costs. [IRS Construction ATG, Ch. 2]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A 125-V, 20-A receptacle is installed on the roof of a commercial building to service rooftop equipment. What does 210.8(B) require for it?","o":["GFCI protection for personnel","No protection beyond overcurrent protection","A dedicated 30-A circuit","AFCI protection"],"a":0,"e":"Receptacles on rooftops of other-than-dwelling occupancies require GFCI protection for personnel. [NEC 210.8(B)]","r":"NEC 210.8(B)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 255.05(2), a contractor may record a notice of contest of a claim. The claimant then has how many days after service to sue before the claim is extinguished?","o":["20 days","60 days","90 days","1 year"],"a":1,"e":"A recorded notice of contest shortens the time to sue to 60 days after service. [F.S. 255.05(2)(a)1.]","r":"F.S. 255.05(2)(a)1."},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Temporary power for the other trades must be energized by a milestone date. The electrical contractor should","o":["include it as a predecessor to the dependent activities","treat it as owner-furnished and exclude it from the logic","schedule it after the finish trim has been completed","leave it off the schedule since it is only temporary"],"a":0,"e":"Dependent activities cannot start until power is available; showing it as a predecessor makes the dependency visible. [Schedule logic]","r":"Coordination with trades (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A service truck stocking plan uses min 4 and max 12 for a part. On-hand is 3 and none are on order. How many should be added to reach the max?","o":["8 units","4 units","12 units","9 units"],"a":3,"e":"Replenish up to the max: 12 - 3 = 9. [Inventory control]","r":"Min/max"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.126, after all necessary permits are issued, work must begin on residential work (where an initial payment above 10 percent was taken) within","o":["30 days","60 days","6 months","90 days"],"a":3,"e":"489.126(2) requires starting work within 90 days of permit issuance absent just cause or a longer written period. [F.S. 489.126(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.126"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A coworker is in contact with an energized conductor and cannot let go. What should you do first?","o":["Grab the victim's arm firmly and pull the victim free of the conductor","Pour water on the victim to cool the burns, then move the victim","Shut off power or separate with a dry nonconductive object, then call 911","Move the victim at once, without checking the energy source first"],"a":2,"e":"Never touch a victim in contact with a live source; de-energize or separate with a dry nonconductive object, then call emergency services and begin first aid.","r":"General first aid practice (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A submittal for a switchboard is returned stamped 'Approved as Noted - make corrections noted and proceed.' What should the contractor do?","o":["Make the noted corrections, then order and install; resubmit only if the notes require it","Wait for a formal change order to be signed by the owner before proceeding with the order","Resubmit and wait for another review of every page","Ignore the notes whenever they conflict with what the supplier prefers to ship"],"a":0,"e":"'Approved as noted' lets the contractor proceed once corrections are incorporated. 'Revise and resubmit' or 'Rejected' require another submittal first. [Typical submittal review actions]","r":"Typical submittal review actions"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 20 A sign branch circuit uses 12 AWG copper. Is the conductor adequately protected by the 20 A breaker?","o":["No, 12 AWG copper requires a 15 A breaker in a sign circuit","No, signs require at least 10 AWG copper conductors for the circuit","Yes, but only when the breaker is rated 25 A or less","Yes, 12 AWG copper may be protected at up to 20 A under 240.4(D)"],"a":3,"e":"240.4(D) limits small conductors: 14 AWG copper to 15 A, 12 AWG copper to 20 A, 10 AWG copper to 30 A. [NEC 240.4(D)]","r":"NEC 240.4(D)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The main difference between an autotransformer and an isolation transformer is that the autotransformer","o":["has windings that are electrically connected","never needs any grounding or bonding connection","has no magnetic core and uses an air gap instead","produces a higher output frequency than the input"],"a":0,"e":"An autotransformer shares part of one winding between primary and secondary, so there is no electrical isolation. [Autotransformer]","r":"Autotransformer"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of the following would typically be included in a contractor's overhead pool?","o":["Wire and conduit that were installed on one specific job","Office salaries, insurance, shop rent and vehicle costs not charged to a job","Subcontractor invoices for trenching on one specific project","Permit fees paid to the city for one specific project"],"a":1,"e":"The overhead pool collects indirect costs of running the business that are not traceable to one job. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A project network (all finish-to-start, durations in working days): A 3d (start); B 5d after A; C 4d after A; D 6d after B; E 2d after B+C; F 7d after D+E; G 3d after C; H 2d after F+G. Activity D takes 4 days longer than planned. What is the new project duration?","o":["29 days","27 days","25 days","23 days"],"a":1,"e":"D is critical (zero float), so the delay passes straight to the finish: 23 + 4 = 27 days. [Critical path delay]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under FAR 52.236-15, if the contractor falls behind the approved schedule, it must","o":["take steps to improve progress at no added cost to the Government","stop all work until a new notice to proceed is issued","ask for an automatic and unconditional time extension","wait for the owner to resequence the remaining work"],"a":0,"e":"The clause requires the contractor to take recovery steps (more shifts, overtime, days of work, plant) at no additional cost when it falls behind. [FAR 52.236-15]","r":"FAR 52.236-15"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The Age Discrimination in Employment Act (ADEA) protects employees who are at least:","o":["40 years old","21 years old","18 years old","65 years old"],"a":0,"e":"The ADEA protects individuals 40 and older from age discrimination, and applies to employers with 20 or more employees. [29 U.S.C. 631, 630(b)]","r":"29 U.S.C. 631"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"At what depth must an excavation have a protective system against cave-ins, unless it is in stable rock or a competent person finds no cave-in potential?","o":["3 feet or deeper","5 feet or deeper","4 feet or deeper","6 feet or deeper"],"a":1,"e":"Employees must be protected by a protective system in excavations 5 feet or deeper unless in stable rock; shallower excavations still require a competent person's examination. [29 CFR 1926.652(a)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.652(a)(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Placing a soft iron core inside a coil will","o":["convert alternating current to direct current","greatly increase flux because iron has high permeability","reduce the inductance of the coil to near zero","eliminate the magnetic field of the coil"],"a":1,"e":"Permeability of iron is hundreds or thousands of times that of air. [Magnetism]","r":"Magnetism"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which Class I group includes propane and gasoline vapors?","o":["Group A","Group D","Group B","Group C"],"a":1,"e":"Group A is acetylene, Group B is hydrogen, Group C is ethylene, and Group D includes propane and gasoline. [NEC 500.6(A)]","r":"NEC 500.6(A)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor buys one switchboard section for $12,000. Florida state tax is 6% and the county surtax is 1.5%. Surtax applies only to the first $5,000 of the price of a single item. What is the total tax?","o":["$900.00","$825.00","$720.00","$795.00"],"a":3,"e":"State tax = $12,000 x 6% = $720.00; surtax = $5,000 x 1.5% = $75.00; total tax = $795.00. [F.S. 212.054(2)(b)1.]","r":"F.S. 212.054"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under the construction confined space rule, who identifies confined spaces and decides which are permit spaces before work begins?","o":["The building owner's insurance auditor","A competent person","The local fire marshal","The first employee to enter"],"a":1,"e":"Before work begins, a competent person must identify all confined spaces and determine which are permit spaces. [29 CFR 1926.1203(a)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1203(a)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign contractor wishes to change the footing depth shown on the signed and sealed sign drawings because the soil is rocky. What is the correct step?","o":["Change it and note the difference on the invoice","Ask the sign supplier to approve it in writing","Get written approval from the engineer of record for the change","Make it shallower, since rocky soil is stronger"],"a":2,"e":"Deviations from the engineered design must be approved by the engineer of record, then resubmitted to the building department if required. [FBC Building ch. 16, ch. 18]","r":"FBC Building ch. 16, ch. 18 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Unit prices listed in the bid form (for example, a price per added receptacle) are mainly used to","o":["set the contractor's overhead rate","price additions or deletions of work after award","determine the retainage held on each payment","calculate the amount of the bid bond"],"a":1,"e":"Quoting unit prices in advance sets a predictable price for quantity changes during construction. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 2,400 sq ft dwelling has a general lighting load of 7,200 VA, two small-appliance circuits and one laundry circuit. What is the demand-factored lighting, small-appliance and laundry load under Table 220.42?","o":["7,200 VA","4,095 VA","11,700 VA","6,045 VA"],"a":3,"e":"Total = 7,200 + 3,000 + 1,500 = 11,700 VA. First 3,000 VA at 100% plus the remaining 8,700 VA at 35%: 3,000 + 3,045 = 6,045 VA. [NEC 220.42, 220.52]","r":"NEC 220.42, 220.52"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An estimate requires 1,260 labor hours. A crew of 4 works 8 hours per day, 5 days per week. How many weeks does the job take (round up to a whole week)?","o":["40 weeks","7 weeks","8 weeks","63 weeks"],"a":2,"e":"Crew capacity = 4 x 8 x 5 = 160 hours per week; 1,260/160 = 7.88, rounded up to 8 weeks. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A 'flow-down' clause in a subcontract does what?","o":["Gives the owner a lien on the contractor's equipment","Limits the sub's profit to the profit in the prime contract","Binds the sub to the prime contract terms that apply to its work","Eliminates the sub's need to carry any insurance"],"a":2,"e":"Flow-down provisions pass the relevant obligations of the prime contract, such as scope, schedule and dispute resolution, to the subcontractor. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"An emergency system must make power available within what time after normal power fails?","o":["10 seconds","60 seconds","2 minutes","5 seconds"],"a":0,"e":"Emergency systems must restore power within 10 seconds; legally required standby within 60 seconds. [NEC 700.12]","r":"NEC 700.12"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A fire alarm drawing shows an 'EOL' symbol at the last device of an initiating device circuit. What does the end-of-line device provide?","o":["Higher alarm sensitivity of all the detectors that are installed on the circuit","A backup battery that powers the loop during a loss of normal power","Supervision of the circuit, so that an open or a short is detected","A means of silencing the alarm at the end of the circuit"],"a":2,"e":"An end-of-line resistor or device enables the panel to monitor circuit integrity. NFPA 72 requires supervision of the installation wiring. [NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 12 circuits and pathways]","r":"NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 12 circuits and pathways (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which action most directly speeds collection of a contractor's final payment under Florida lien law?","o":["Wait for the owner to ask for lien waivers before preparing any paperwork","Record a claim of lien before final furnishing of labor and materials","Submit the final payment affidavit and the releases the owner needs to pay safely","Cancel the notice of commencement that the owner recorded with the clerk"],"a":2,"e":"An owner may need the contractor's final payment affidavit (713.06(3)(d)) and lien releases (713.20) before paying; providing them promptly avoids delays. [F.S. 713.06, 713.20]","r":"F.S. 713.06"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Rule 61G6-5.005, F.A.C. says the board may review a bankruptcy involving the applicant, officers or 10 percent shareholders if it occurred within the past","o":["six months","five years","two years","ten years"],"a":1,"e":"The rule also reviews judgments in the last five years for failing to pay materialmen or laborers, and tax liens of record. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.005]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.005"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the FLSA, an employer who discharges an employee for filing a wage complaint has committed:","o":["No violation if a reason is later stated in writing","A lawful at-will termination in Florida","Unlawful retaliation under the Act","A violation only if the employee had worked over a year"],"a":2,"e":"FLSA Section 15(a)(3) prohibits discharging or discriminating against an employee who files a complaint or takes part in a proceeding. [29 U.S.C. 215(a)(3)]","r":"FLSA Sec. 15(a)(3)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Branch circuits serving receptacles in patient care spaces must have what equipment grounding arrangement?","o":["A metal raceway alone","An insulated green conductor alone","A bare copper conductor alone","A qualifying metal raceway or armor plus an insulated copper EGC"],"a":3,"e":"Patient care spaces require a redundant ground path: a qualifying metal raceway or cable armor and an insulated copper EGC sized per 250.122. [NEC 517.13(A) and (B)]","r":"NEC 517.13(A) and (B)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The voltages of a balanced three-phase system are displaced from one another by","o":["180 electrical degrees","120 electrical degrees","90 electrical degrees","60 electrical degrees"],"a":1,"e":"Three equal voltages 120 degrees apart sum to zero. [Phase displacement]","r":"Phase displacement"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Before work begins on a construction job, what must the employer do about energized circuits that could contact a person, tool or machine?","o":["Obtain written permission from the electric utility to work within 20 feet of any circuit","Determine whether energized circuits exist, post warning signs, and inform employees of the hazards","Have every exposed circuit tested and certified by an independent third-party engineer","Require every employee near the circuits to hold a valid master electrician license"],"a":1,"e":"1926.416(a)(3) requires the employer to determine whether energized lines exist, post and maintain warning signs, and advise employees of the location, hazards and protective measures.","r":"29 CFR 1926.416(a)(3)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Two resistors of 30 and 45 ohms are connected in parallel. What is the equivalent resistance?","o":["1,350 ohms","37.5 ohms","75 ohms","18 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Product over sum: 30 x 45 / 75 = 18 ohms. 75 is the series value. [Parallel circuits]","r":"Parallel circuits"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A newly uncovered employer's calculated premium for the unsecured period is only $350. Under s. 440.107, what is the base penalty?","o":["$700","$2,000","$1,000","$350"],"a":2,"e":"Twice $350 is $700, but the statute sets the penalty at the greater of 2 times the premium or $1,000. [F.S. 440.107]","r":"F.S. 440.107"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A favorable variance on a revenue line means","o":["actual revenue was lower than budgeted","costs were higher than budgeted","actual revenue was higher than budgeted","the budget was not approved"],"a":2,"e":"Revenue above budget is favorable; costs above budget are unfavorable. [Variance analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 6-pole alternator is driven at 1,200 rpm. What is the output frequency?","o":["50 Hz","60 Hz","120 Hz","30 Hz"],"a":1,"e":"f = P x N / 120 = 6 x 1,200 / 120 = 60 Hz. [Generators]","r":"Generators"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.511(6), F.S., what happens to a contractor's earlier registered licenses in the same classification when a certificate is issued?","o":["They remain valid in the registered jurisdiction","They convert to inactive status","They must be renewed separately every year","The earlier registered licenses are voided"],"a":3,"e":"Issuing a certificate voids any previously issued registered licenses in the same classification, because the certificate is geographically unlimited. [F.S. 489.511(6)]","r":"F.S. 489.511(6)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In the critical path method, the critical path is the","o":["shortest path from the start node to the end node","path made up of the most expensive activities","longest path, which sets the minimum project duration","path that has the greatest number of activities"],"a":2,"e":"The critical path is the longest chain of dependent activities; any delay on it delays the finish. The Pitt CPM notes define critical activities as those with zero total float. [CPM]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The main disconnecting means for an elevator may NOT be provided with:","o":["A lock-out provision","Remote operation, except automatic opening for sprinklers","A permanent label","A wall-mounting enclosure"],"a":1,"e":"The main disconnect cannot be operated remotely, except automatic opening per sprinkler/fire protection. [NEC 620.51(B)]","r":"NEC 620.51(B)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"In a balanced delta-connected load, each phase winding carries 20 A. What is the line current?","o":["11.5 A","34.6 A","60.0 A","20.0 A"],"a":1,"e":"In delta, line current = 1.732 x phase current = 34.6 A. [Delta connection]","r":"Delta connection"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A civil plan places a new handhole in a sidewalk and shows 'CALL 811.' Under Florida's underground facility damage prevention law, when must an excavator notify the one-call system before beginning excavation outside state waters?","o":["At least 2 full business days before starting","At least 10 full business days before starting","On the same day, before digging begins","At least 5 calendar days before starting"],"a":0,"e":"F.S. 556.105 requires notice not less than 2 full business days before beginning excavation, with 10 full business days for excavation beneath state waters. The ticket info is valid for 30 calendar days. [F.S. 556.105]","r":"F.S. 556.105"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A job priced at $85,000 has actual costs of $28,500 labor, $31,200 material, $4,100 subcontract and $2,300 equipment. What is the job gross profit and gross margin (before company overhead)?","o":["$18,900 and 28.6%","$66,100 and 77.8%","$18,900 and 22.2%","$25,300 and 29.8%"],"a":2,"e":"Cost = 28,500 + 31,200 + 4,100 + 2,300 = 66,100; gross profit = 85,000 - 66,100 = 18,900; margin = 18,900/85,000 = 22.2%. Dividing by cost gives markup (28.6%), not margin. [Job costing]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Which statement about the $1 million figure in s. 725.06(1), F.S., is correct?","o":["It is a ceiling on the liability of every subcontractor on private projects","It is a default minimum cap for indemnity running to the owner from its contracting party","It is the maximum liability insurance a contractor can be required to carry","It is a minimum that applies only to public agency construction contracts"],"a":1,"e":"The $1 million floor applies to indemnification the owner of real property receives from a party in privity of contract with the owner, unless the parties agree otherwise. It is not a universal cap. [F.S. 725.06(1)]","r":"F.S. 725.06(1)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A raceway passes from a heated building into a cold storage area where condensation is a problem. What is required by 300.7(A)?","o":["The raceway must be replaced with aluminum conduit","A separate grounding conductor must be added","The raceway must be sealed (with sealants identified for the purpose) or arranged to prevent the circulation of warm air to the colder section","No action, because condensation is not covered by the NEC"],"a":2,"e":"Where portions of a raceway are exposed to different temperatures and condensation is a problem, the raceway must be sealed to prevent circulation of warm air to the colder section. [NEC 300.7(A)]","r":"NEC 300.7(A)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Operation of a sprinkler waterflow switch in a building with a fire alarm system should cause:","o":["a trouble signal only, with no notification appliances","an alarm signal and activation of the occupant notification appliances","a local bell at the riser only, with no signal at the panel","a supervisory signal only, with no horns or strobes"],"a":1,"e":"A waterflow switch is an alarm-initiating device. The fire alarm control unit must indicate an alarm and activate the occupant notification appliances. [NFPA 72, Ch. 17 / NFPA 101, 9.7]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 17; NFPA 101 9.7 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The average job generates $18,000 in revenue and $11,700 in variable costs. Annual fixed costs are $189,000. How many jobs are needed to break even?","o":["30 jobs","10.5 jobs","16.2 jobs","60 jobs"],"a":0,"e":"Contribution per job = 18,000 - 11,700 = 6,300; jobs = 189,000/6,300 = 30. [Break-even analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier delivers materials to a subcontractor on a job. Whom must the supplier serve with the Notice to Owner?","o":["The subcontractor only","The owner and the general contractor","The county building department","The owner only"],"a":1,"e":"A materialman to a subcontractor serves the owner and also serves a copy on the contractor. [F.S. 713.06(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(2)(a)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A qualifying agent who wants to qualify an additional business organization under s. 489.521(8), F.S., must:","o":["Pay a fee equal to the original fee and show the ability to supervise each","Pay no new fee, because the agent already holds a license","Obtain a second exam score of at least 90 percent first","Give up the first business's qualification before qualifying another"],"a":0,"e":"The agent pays an amount equal to the original certification fee for each additional organization, and the board reviews the agent's supervisory ability and financial responsibility; inadequate supervision can lead to revocation of the additional qualification. [F.S. 489.521(8)]","r":"F.S. 489.521(8)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A $24,000 machine with a 5-year life is depreciated for books using the double-declining-balance method (rate = 2 x 1/5 = 40% of beginning book value). Year-2 depreciation is","o":["$9,600","$3,840","$5,760","$4,800"],"a":2,"e":"Year 1 = 24,000 x 40% = 9,600; beginning book value year 2 = 14,400; year 2 = 14,400 x 40% = 5,760. [Depreciation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor served with a Chapter 558 notice believes its subcontractor caused the defect. Within how many days of being served may the contractor forward the notice to the subcontractor under s. 558.004(3) (homeowner claim, not an association of more than 20 parcels)?","o":["5 days","10 days","30 days","60 days"],"a":1,"e":"The served party may forward the notice to others it believes responsible within 10 days of service (30 days for a large association claim). A forwarded party then has 15 days to respond. [F.S. 558.004(3)-(4)]","r":"F.S. 558.004(3)-(4)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which workers may work on exposed energized parts under 29 CFR 1910.333(c)?","o":["Any employee who has completed a general company safety orientation course","Any employee supervised by a licensed apprentice who is on the job site","Only qualified persons trained in the safe techniques, PPE and insulated tools","Any employee wearing rubber gloves rated for the circuit voltage"],"a":2,"e":"Only qualified persons may work on or near exposed energized parts, and they must know the safe techniques, PPE, insulating and shielding materials and insulated tools. [29 CFR 1910.333(c)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(2)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Switchgear must be on site by March 15, 2027. Submittal preparation takes 10 calendar days, engineer review 14 calendar days, fabrication 84 calendar days after approval, and shipping 7 calendar days. By what date must the submittal effort begin (ignoring weekends and holidays)?","o":["November 06, 2026","December 14, 2026","November 20, 2026","December 04, 2026"],"a":2,"e":"Total lead = 10 + 14 + 84 + 7 = 115 days. Counting back 115 days from March 15, 2027 gives November 20, 2026. Ordering only after approval ignores the submittal and review time. [Procurement lead time]","r":"Delivery lead times (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Retainage in a construction contract is","o":["a portion of each progress payment withheld by the payer until a later stage of the project","a deposit that the contractor pays to the owner in order to obtain the contract","interest charged to the owner by the contractor on late progress payments","the contractor's expected profit margin as a percentage of the contract sum"],"a":0,"e":"Retainage withholds a percentage of progress payments as security for completion; the ATG notes it is usually 10% of billed amounts, and the general contractor withholds a corresponding portion from subs. [IRS Construction ATG, Ch. 1]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Ground-fault protection of equipment is required for which feeder disconnect under 215.10?","o":["A 1200 A disconnect on a 208Y/120-V system","An 800 A disconnect on a 480Y/277-V solidly grounded wye system","A 1200 A disconnect on an ungrounded 480-V delta system","A 1200 A disconnect on a 480Y/277-V solidly grounded wye system"],"a":3,"e":"GFPE is required for each feeder disconnect rated 1000 A or more on solidly grounded wye systems of more than 150 V to ground but not exceeding 1000 V phase-to-phase. 208Y/120 V is only 120 V to ground. [NEC 215.10]","r":"NEC 215.10"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.1425, F.S., a contract for repair, restoration, improvement or construction on residential real property must include the Florida Homeowners' Construction Recovery Fund statement unless the total value of all labor and materials does not exceed what amount?","o":["$2,500","$500","$1,000","$5,000"],"a":0,"e":"The recovery-fund statement is required in each residential agreement except where the total value of labor and materials does not exceed $2,500. [F.S. 489.1425(1)]","r":"F.S. 489.1425(1)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For new dwelling construction, the NEC requires what minimum communications outlet provision?","o":["At least one communications outlet in an accessible area of the dwelling unit, cabled to the service provider demarcation point","One outlet in every bedroom","One outlet in the garage only","None; communications outlets are never required by the NEC"],"a":0,"e":"A minimum of one communications outlet, accessible and cabled to the service provider's demarcation point, is required in new dwellings. [NEC 2023 Art. 800 (2026: 720.9)]","r":"NEC 2023 Art. 800 dwelling outlet (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"EMT must be securely fastened at intervals not exceeding 10 ft and within what distance of each outlet box, junction box, cabinet or conduit body?","o":["1 ft","6 in.","3 ft","5 ft"],"a":2,"e":"EMT is fastened at intervals not to exceed 10 ft and within 3 ft of each termination; the distance may increase to 5 ft where structural members do not readily permit 3 ft. [NEC 358.30]","r":"NEC 358.30"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 713.16, a lienor who is asked by the owner for a sworn statement of account must respond within:","o":["60 days","30 days of the demand","10 days","5 days"],"a":1,"e":"An owner's written demand for a statement of account under s. 713.16 must be answered under oath within 30 days, or the lien may be lost. [F.S. 713.16]","r":"F.S. 713.16"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which practice is correct when using a hydraulic crimping tool on a lug?","o":["Use the die and lug the manufacturer specifies, and confirm a complete crimp","Use any die that fits the lug, since all dies make the same crimp","Crimp the lug while the conductor is energized, to save time","Re-crimp the same spot repeatedly until the lug will not turn"],"a":0,"e":"Wrong die or incomplete crimps cause high-resistance connections that overheat; always follow the lug and tool manufacturer's instructions. Hydraulic tool pressure ratings must not be exceeded. [29 CFR 1926.302(d)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.302(d); manufacturer instructions (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the recommended drinking pattern for workers in the heat?","o":["About one cup of cool water every 15 to 20 minutes","One large glass of water, taken only at each of the scheduled breaks","Water only when thirsty, since thirst is a reliable early indicator","Sports or energy drinks only, since plain water can cause cramps"],"a":0,"e":"OSHA recommends roughly a cup every 15-20 minutes; waiting to drink until thirsty means already dehydrated.","r":"OSHA heat illness fact sheet"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 150 kVA three-phase transformer is rated 480 V primary, 208Y/120 V secondary. What is its full-load primary current?","o":["104.2 A","416.4 A","180.4 A","312.5 A"],"a":2,"e":"Primary FLA = 150,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 180.4 A; the secondary FLA is 416.4 A. [Transformer current]","r":"Transformer current"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's lookback liabilities by quarter were $12,800, $13,400, $13,100 and $13,900. Which deposit schedule applies?","o":["Monthly, because no quarter exceeded $50,000","Semiweekly, because the total exceeds $100,000","Semiweekly, because the total is $53,200","Monthly, because the total is $51,200"],"a":2,"e":"$12,800 + $13,400 + $13,100 + $13,900 = $53,200, which exceeds $50,000, so semiweekly deposits are required. Quarters are added together, not tested separately. [IRS Topic 757]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 255.078, F.S., how much may a public entity withhold as retainage from each progress payment on a construction services contract?","o":["Up to 2 percent","Up to 5 percent","Up to 10 percent","Up to 15 percent"],"a":1,"e":"The statute caps retainage at 5 percent of each progress payment, though the entity may withhold less, reduce the rate on a schedule, or release it early. [F.S. 255.078(1)-(2)]","r":"F.S. 255.078(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A company tells a worker when to arrive, supplies the tools, requires a specific order of tasks, and trains the worker in its methods. Which IRS category does this evidence mainly address?","o":["Financial control, which points toward contractor status","Type of relationship, which points toward contractor status","Behavioral control, which points toward employee status","None; these facts are irrelevant to worker status"],"a":2,"e":"Instructions on when, where and how work is done, required tools, and training are behavioral control facts that suggest an employer-employee relationship. [IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27; IRS Topic 762]","r":"IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A feeder breaker shown on a one-line diagram must be a standard rating. Which of these is a standard ampere rating for an inverse-time circuit breaker in NEC Table 240.6(A)?","o":["225 A","235 A","245 A","255 A"],"a":0,"e":"225 A is listed in Table 240.6(A) (between 200 and 250). The other three values are not standard ratings. [NEC 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 240.6(A)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1926.652, an employer using a trench shield may excavate no more than how far below the bottom of the shield?","o":["5 feet, if the shield is designed for the full depth and no soil loss is evident","2 feet, if the shield is designed for the full depth and no soil loss is evident","6 inches, if the shield is designed for the full depth and no soil loss is evident","10 feet, if the shield is designed for the full depth and no soil loss is evident"],"a":1,"e":"Excavation of material to a level no greater than 2 feet below the bottom of a shield is permitted if the shield is designed for the full depth and there is no sign of soil loss. [29 CFR 1926.652(g)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.652(g)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has L = 0.1 H and C = 50 microfarads. What is the resonant frequency?","o":["142.4 Hz","0.0022 Hz","71.2 Hz","31,831 Hz"],"a":2,"e":"f = 1 / (2 pi sqrt(LC)) = 1 / (6.2832 x sqrt(5e-6)) = 71.2 Hz. [Resonance]","r":"Resonance"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under IRC 460, qualifying home construction contracts are","o":["required to use percentage of completion in all cases","required to use cash method","exempt from the percentage-of-completion requirement","taxed only after the first anniversary"],"a":2,"e":"IRC 460(e)(6)(A) exempts qualifying home construction contracts from the PCM requirement. [IRS Construction ATG, Ch. 2]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which statement about profit and overhead is correct for estimating?","o":["Profit is a cost; overhead is the reward above cost","Overhead is a cost of doing business; profit is the reward above all costs including overhead","Overhead and profit are the same item","Profit is paid before overhead from each progress payment"],"a":1,"e":"Overhead recovers business operating costs. Profit is what remains after all direct and overhead costs are covered. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under the 1994 ADAAG (28 CFR Part 36, App. A, 4.2.5), what is the maximum height of the highest operable part for an unobstructed forward reach?","o":["44 in.","60 in.","54 in.","48 in."],"a":3,"e":"Section 4.2.5 sets the maximum high forward reach at 48 in. and the minimum low forward reach at 15 in. The 54 in. figure is the side-reach limit for a parallel approach, not a forward reach. [ADAAG 4.2.5]","r":"ADAAG 4.2.5"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On the balance sheet, retainage that a contractor has billed but not yet collected is typically reported as","o":["accounts payable, shown as a current liability on the contractor's balance sheet","accounts receivable, shown as retainage receivable","owner's equity, shown as an increase in retained earnings in the current year","a depreciation reserve, shown as a reduction of the contractor's equipment"],"a":1,"e":"Retainage withheld by customers is money owed to the contractor, so it appears as a receivable (retainage receivable); the same amount held from subs is a payable. [IRS Construction ATG, Ch. 1]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A feeder runs in two parallel raceways and is protected by a 400 A breaker. The EGC in each raceway is sized from Table 250.122 using the 400 A device rating. What is the minimum copper EGC size in each raceway?","o":["1 AWG copper, because a 400 A breaker requires 1 AWG","3 AWG copper","6 AWG copper, because the load is divided","1/0 AWG copper, because the two raceways together are 800 A"],"a":1,"e":"Per 250.122(F), when conductors are in parallel in multiple raceways, an EGC sized per 250.122 based on the overcurrent device ahead of the feeder is run in each raceway. Table 250.122 gives 3 AWG copper for a 400 A device. 1 AWG corresponds to 600 A. [NEC Table 250.122; 250.122(F)]","r":"NEC Table 250.122; 250.122(F)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An electrical contractor pays a licensed subcontractor (a sole proprietor) $9,400 during 2026 for labor. Which form does the contractor generally file with the IRS, and what is its general due date?","o":["Form W-2, due April 15 of the next year","Form 1099-NEC, due January 31 of the next year","Form 941, due the last day of the next month","Form 1099-NEC, due March 31 of the next year"],"a":1,"e":"Payments of $2,000 or more (2026) to a non-employee for services are reported on Form 1099-NEC, due to the recipient and the IRS by January 31. W-2 is for employees and 941 is the quarterly employment tax return. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A voltmeter is connected ______ with the load being measured, and an ammeter is connected ______.","o":["in parallel; in series","in series; in series","in parallel; in parallel","in series; in parallel"],"a":0,"e":"Voltmeters read potential difference across a component; ammeters must carry the circuit current. [Meters]","r":"Meters"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The accessible portion of abandoned fire alarm cables must be:","o":["Removed","Left in place if tagged","Cut flush at the panel","Covered with a listed cap"],"a":0,"e":"Abandoned fire alarm cable must be removed; a durable tag applies only to cables identified for future use. [NEC 760.25]","r":"NEC 2023 760.25"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the FDOT Construction Project Administration Manual, a weather day can be granted when rain or related soil conditions prevent productive work on controlling items such that the contractor","o":["has no personnel at all present on the site that day","has already submitted a request for a bonus payment","cannot work for even the smallest part of the day at all","cannot work at least half a normal workday on controlling items"],"a":3,"e":"The FDOT manual says a day is eligible when the contractor cannot work at least half a normal workday on predetermined controlling work items. [FDOT CPAM 7.2]","r":"Weather days"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A panel schedule lists these connected VA values by phase (each column is one phase): Phase A circuits total 5,400 VA, Phase B total 5,500 VA, Phase C total 5,900 VA. The panel is 208Y/120 V three-phase, 4-wire. What is the total connected load of the panel in kVA?","o":["17.7 kVA","5.9 kVA","29.1 kVA","16.8 kVA"],"a":3,"e":"Total connected VA is the sum of all phases: 5,400 + 5,500 + 5,900 = 16,800 VA = 16.8 kVA. Taking the highest phase times three overstates the load, and the 1.732 factor is applied only when converting to current. [NEC 408.30; 220 load calculations]","r":"NEC 408.30; 220 load calculations"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The grounding electrode conductor for a 600 kcmil copper service connects only to a concrete-encased electrode. What is the largest size copper GEC required?","o":["4 AWG","2/0 AWG","1/0 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Where the GEC connects to a concrete-encased electrode and extends to no other type of electrode requiring a larger conductor, it need not be larger than 4 AWG copper. [NEC 250.66(B)]","r":"NEC 250.66(B)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"For a portable sign in a dry location, the maximum length of the cord is:","o":["4.5 m (15 ft)","1.8 m (6 ft)","3 m (10 ft)","9 m (30 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"600.10(D) requires SP-2, SPE-2, SPT-2, or heavier, not exceeding 4.5 m (15 ft) in length. [NEC 600.10(D)]","r":"NEC 600.10(D)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A sole-owner corporation began the year with equity of $400,000. It earned net income of $96,000 and distributed $40,000 to the owner, with no other equity changes. Using average equity, what is ROE?","o":["24.0%","13.1%","22.4%","21.1%"],"a":2,"e":"Ending equity = $400,000 + $96,000 - $40,000 = $456,000. Average = ($400,000 + $456,000)/2 = $428,000. ROE = $96,000/$428,000 = 22.4%. [UNL Extension G2009]","r":"Return on equity"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Through the prior pay period, a supervisor has $182,000 in 2026 wages. This period's gross is $6,500. Using the 2026 Social Security wage base of $184,500, how much Social Security tax is withheld from this paycheck?","o":["$403.00","$155.00","$94.25","$0.00"],"a":1,"e":"Only $184,500 - $182,000 = $2,500 of this paycheck is still under the wage base: $2,500 x 6.2% = $155.00. $403.00 taxes the whole $6,500, and $94.25 is the Medicare tax on the full check. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In what excavations must the atmosphere be tested before entry when oxygen deficiency or hazardous substances could be present?","o":["Excavations deeper than 2 feet","Excavations deeper than 8 feet","Excavations deeper than 12 feet","Excavations deeper than 4 feet"],"a":3,"e":"Atmospheres in excavations over 4 feet deep must be tested before entry where hazardous atmospheres could exist; oxygen below 19.5 percent is deficient. [29 CFR 1926.651(g)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.651(g)(1)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For the same $45,000 truck, second-year MACRS depreciation using the 32% table rate is","o":["$9,000","$14,400","$8,640","$18,000"],"a":1,"e":"45,000 x 32% = 14,400. Rates for the 5-year class under half-year: 20%, 32%, 19.2%, 11.52%, 11.52%, 5.76% (sum 100%). [Pub 946 Table A-1]","r":"IRS Pub 946 (2025)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Service-entrance conductors supply a 120 A noncontinuous load and an 80 A continuous load. The equipment is rated 75°C and not listed for 100% operation. What is the smallest copper conductor size (Table 310.16, 75°C) permitted?","o":["250 kcmil","2/0 AWG","4/0 AWG","3/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Minimum ampacity = 120 + 1.25 x 80 = 220 A. At 75°C, 3/0 AWG is only 200 A; 4/0 AWG carries 230 A, which is the smallest that meets 220 A. [NEC 230.42(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 230.42(A)(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a construction site with no nearby infirmary, clinic or hospital, what does 29 CFR 1926.50 require?","o":["A registered nurse must be on site at all times during each shift","A person with a valid first-aid training certificate must be available on the site","A first-aid kit must be locked in the supervisor's vehicle on the site","Nothing is required if the site has fewer than twelve workers present"],"a":1,"e":"Where infirmary, clinic or hospital is not reasonably accessible in terms of time and distance, a person with a valid first-aid certificate must be available. [29 CFR 1926.50(c)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.50(c)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A vendor's written quote that has not been accepted by the buyer is generally:","o":["A lien notice that the vendor must file with the property owner","A binding contract on the buyer as soon as the quote is received","A request for payment that falls due within 30 days of receipt","An offer that does not bind the buyer until the buyer issues a purchase order"],"a":3,"e":"A quote is the vendor's price proposal; the contract is formed when the buyer accepts it, usually by issuing a PO. Check the quote's expiration date. [F.S. 672.206]","r":"F.S. 672.206"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"29 CFR 1910.269(c) requires a job briefing at which times?","o":["Only once at the beginning of a project, with no further briefings needed","Only after an incident or near miss has occurred on the job","Only when the voltage on the equipment exceeds 600 volts","Before each job, and for repetitive work once per day or shift"],"a":3,"e":"The employer must ensure the employee in charge conducts a job briefing before each job covering hazards, procedures, precautions, energy controls and PPE; repetitive work needs one per shift.","r":"29 CFR 1910.269(c)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under NEC 110.26(A)(3), what is the minimum height of the working space?","o":["6 1/2 feet, or the equipment height if greater","5 feet, or the equipment height if greater","6 feet, or the equipment height if greater","7 feet, or the equipment height if greater"],"a":0,"e":"Working space headroom is measured from the grade, floor or platform and must be 6.5 feet minimum or the equipment height, whichever is greater.","r":"NEC 110.26(A)(3) (2023) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Straight-line depreciation spreads the cost of an asset, less salvage value,","o":["more heavily in the first years","only in the years the asset is used","evenly over its useful life","over 39 years in all cases"],"a":2,"e":"Annual straight-line depreciation = (cost - salvage)/useful life. [Depreciation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Proceeding on a job without obtaining required local permits and inspections is a ground for discipline under which paragraph of s. 489.533(1), F.S.?","o":["Paragraph (c)","Paragraph (g)","Paragraph (r)","Paragraph (j)"],"a":2,"e":"Paragraph (1)(r) lists proceeding on a job without required local permits and inspections. Paragraph (j) is assisting unlicensed persons, and (g) is violating chapter 633. [F.S. 489.533(1)(r)]","r":"F.S. 489.533(1)(r)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An approved disconnecting means for an information technology equipment room must disconnect power to the electronic equipment and also:","o":["Power to dedicated HVAC systems and cause required fire/smoke dampers to close","Only the lighting circuits","The fire alarm system","The branch-circuit panelboard feeder only"],"a":0,"e":"The remote disconnect must shut down equipment power and dedicated HVAC and close required dampers. [NEC 645.10]","r":"NEC 2023 645.10"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Which task is excluded from the two-way radio communications enhancement systems specialty under Rule 61G6-7.001, F.A.C.?","o":["Installing antennae for the system","Connecting the system to a fire alarm panel","Installing amplifiers for the system","Testing the system after installation"],"a":1,"e":"That specialty covers installation, repair, alteration, addition, testing, replacement and design of wiring, antennae and amplifiers up to 98 volts RMS, but it excludes connecting the system to a fire alarm panel. [F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(6)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(6)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer with enterprise coverage under the FLSA generally has what annual gross volume of business?","o":["At least $5 million","At least $100,000","At least $1 million","At least $500,000"],"a":3,"e":"Enterprise coverage generally applies to businesses with annual gross volume of $500,000 or more, plus other covered enterprises such as hospitals and schools. Individual coverage can still apply below it. [DOL Handy Reference Guide]","r":"FLSA Sec. 3(s)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A feeder run needs 840 ft of conductor. Add a 5% waste allowance to the net quantity. How many feet should be ordered?","o":["882 ft","888 ft","1260 ft","840 ft"],"a":0,"e":"840 x 1.05 = 882 ft. Waste is added to the net requirement. [Estimating practice]","r":"Waste factor"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In NFPA 101, doors in the means of egress must swing in the direction of exit travel when serving an area with an occupant load of:","o":["50 persons or more, or a high hazard area","300 persons or more, or any assembly room","100 persons or more, or a high hazard area","10 persons or more, or any storage area"],"a":0,"e":"Egress doors swing in the direction of travel for rooms with 50 or more occupants or high-hazard contents. Smaller rooms may use swing-in doors. [NFPA 101, 7.2.1.4.2]","r":"NFPA 101 7.2.1.4.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A subcontractor not in privity with the owner must serve a notice to owner no later than how many days after first furnishing labor, services or materials?","o":["15 days","60 days","45 days","90 days"],"a":2,"e":"713.06(2) requires the notice before commencing or within 45 days after starting; it must also be served before the owner's final payment. The 90-day figure is the claim-of-lien deadline. [F.S. 713.06(2)]","r":"F.S. 713.06"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A resistor and an inductor are in parallel. The resistor branch draws 8 A and the inductor branch draws 6 A. What is the total line current?","o":["2 A","14 A","10 A","48 A"],"a":2,"e":"Branch currents are 90 degrees apart: I = sqrt(8 squared + 6 squared) = 10 A, not 14 A. [Parallel AC circuits]","r":"Parallel AC circuits"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.119(4), F.S., when a qualifying agent applies for a county or municipal business tax receipt for the business organization, the application is made in:","o":["The agent's name only, and the receipt is issued to the agent","The business name only, and no agent certificate is required","The names of both the business and the agent; it issues to the business","The name of the local building official who reviews permits"],"a":2,"e":"The application is in both names, the receipt is issued to the business after the fee is paid and the agent's valid department certificate is presented, and state license numbers must be noted. [F.S. 489.119(4)]","r":"F.S. 489.119(4)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which ladder is required where the ladder or employee could contact exposed energized electrical equipment on a construction site?","o":["A ladder with nonconductive side rails","A ladder with aluminum rails and rubber feet","Any ladder if the electrician wears dielectric gloves","A wooden ladder painted with conductive paint for visibility"],"a":0,"e":"Ladders must have nonconductive side rails if used where the employee or the ladder could contact exposed energized equipment. [29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(12)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(12)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contract sets liquidated damages of $500 per calendar day. The completion date was day 100. The contractor finished on day 137, and the owner approved a 9-day excusable time extension. What are the liquidated damages?","o":["$23,000","$14,000","$4,500","$18,500"],"a":1,"e":"Adjusted completion date = day 109. Days late = 137 - 109 = 28. LD = 28 x $500 = $14,000. Ignoring the extension charges $18,500. [Liquidated damages; FAR 52.249-10]","r":"Liquidated damages"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A new accessible ramp must climb 24 in. at the maximum 1:12 slope. What is the minimum horizontal run?","o":["30 ft","12 ft","24 ft","20 ft"],"a":2,"e":"At 1:12 each inch of rise requires 12 in. of run, so 24 in. x 12 = 288 in. = 24 ft. This is within the 30 in. maximum rise per run. [ADAAG 4.8.2]","r":"ADAAG 4.8.2"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Break-even analysis assumes that, within the relevant range,","o":["all costs are fixed no matter how much work the company performs","fixed costs stay constant in total and variable costs change in proportion to volume","all costs are variable and change with every job that is completed","prices fall automatically as sales volume rises throughout the year"],"a":1,"e":"The standard cost-volume-profit model uses constant fixed costs and a constant variable cost per unit or per dollar of sales. [Break-even analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A single ground rod used as the grounding electrode measures 40 ohms to earth. What does 250.53 require?","o":["Nothing, since 40 ohms is acceptable","A 6 AWG aluminum conductor to the water pipe","Replace the rod with a 4 ft rod","A supplemental electrode, because a single rod, pipe or plate electrode with resistance over 25 ohms must be supplemented"],"a":3,"e":"A single rod, pipe or plate electrode must be supplemented by an additional electrode unless it has a resistance to earth of 25 ohms or less. [NEC 250.53(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 250.53(A)(2)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 713.23, an owner, contractor or surety must provide a copy of the bond to a lienor who requests it. The lienor may be charged","o":["a 10 percent administration fee","reproduction costs","a deposit equal to the lien","nothing; copies are never provided"],"a":1,"e":"The statute provides that a copy be furnished at the cost of reproduction. [F.S. 713.23(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(1)(a)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Allocating a disproportionately large share of the contract sum to early-work line items (mobilization, permits, rough-in material) on the schedule of values is called","o":["back-charging","front-loading","retainage stacking","overhead absorption"],"a":1,"e":"Front-loading speeds the contractor's cash receipts but can be challenged by owners and lenders and increases risk if the contractor defaults. [Progress billing practice]","r":"Contract billing arithmetic (progress billing practice)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A restaurant kitchen has five pieces of commercial electric cooking equipment rated 8, 6, 5, 4 and 3 kW. Using Table 220.56, what is the demand load?","o":["14.0 kW","26.0 kW","20.8 kW","18.2 kW"],"a":3,"e":"Total = 26 kW. For five units the demand factor is 70%: 26 x 0.70 = 18.2 kW, which exceeds the minimum of the two largest units (14 kW). [NEC 220.56, Table 220.56]","r":"NEC 220.56, Table 220.56"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Ground-fault protection of equipment is required at a service disconnect when the service is a:","o":["480Y/277-V solidly grounded wye service with a disconnect rated 1000 A or more","120/240-V single-phase service rated 400 A","480-V ungrounded delta service rated 1200 A","208Y/120-V service with a disconnect rated 600 A"],"a":0,"e":"GFPE is required for solidly grounded wye services of more than 150 V to ground but not over 1000 V phase-to-phase, for each service disconnect rated 1000 A or more. [NEC 230.95]","r":"NEC 230.95"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 713.20, a right to claim a lien","o":["may be waived in the contract before work starts if the owner requires it","may be waived verbally by the foreman if the owner's agent is present","may not be waived in advance; a waiver is valid only for work already furnished","is waived automatically as soon as the contractor issues its first invoice"],"a":2,"e":"713.20(2) states a lien right may not be waived in advance and that a waiver is enforceable only to the extent of labor, services or materials already furnished. [F.S. 713.20(2)]","r":"F.S. 713.20"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On a contractor's balance sheet, billings in excess of costs and estimated earnings (overbillings) are reported as","o":["a long-term asset","a current liability","a current asset","owner's equity"],"a":1,"e":"Overbillings represent billings collected or billable ahead of work performed; the contractor owes the work, so it is a liability. [WIP schedule]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Direct costs for a job total $150,070. The estimator adds a 3% contingency to direct cost. What is the contingency amount?","o":["$4,641.34","$154,572.10","$4,370.97","$4,502.10"],"a":3,"e":"150,070.00 x 0.03 = $4,502.10. The total with contingency would be $154,572.10. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A revolving line of credit differs from a term loan because","o":["the borrower may draw, repay and redraw up to a limit, paying interest on the amount drawn","it is fully funded on the first day and repaid in equal monthly installments","it can only be used to purchase real estate or other long-term capital assets","it has no interest cost as long as the balance is repaid within the year"],"a":0,"e":"SBA describes a revolving line as one that stays open indefinitely; businesses draw as needed up to the credit limit. [SBA working capital options]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Where is an equipotential plane required in a livestock confinement area?","o":["Concrete floors where accessible metal equipment may be energized","Any area with a gravel floor","Only poultry houses with dirt floors","Only outdoor feed lots"],"a":0,"e":"Equipotential planes are needed in concrete-floored livestock confinement areas with accessible metallic equipment that may become energized; they are bonded with an 8 AWG solid copper conductor. Poultry is excluded from the plane requirement. [NEC 547.10]","r":"NEC 547.10 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The unit of inductance is the","o":["farad","ohm","henry","weber"],"a":2,"e":"Inductance is measured in henrys; the farad is capacitance and the weber is magnetic flux. [Electrical units]","r":"Electrical units"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A service department has fixed costs of $14,000 per month. It bills $95 per labor hour and variable cost is $58 per hour. How many billable hours per month are needed to break even (round up)?","o":["148 hours","242 hours","379 hours","92 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Contribution per hour = 95 - 58 = 37; hours = 14,000/37 = 378.4, rounded up to 379. [Break-even analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What limitation of a tag used without a lock must employees be trained to understand?","o":["A tag cannot be used on any electrical equipment, only on mechanical equipment","A tag is only a warning that does not physically restrain, and may give a false sense of security","A tag lasts only 24 hours before it must be replaced with a new one","A tag is stronger than a lock and therefore does not need to be inspected"],"a":1,"e":"Training must cover that tags are warning devices, not physical restraints, and that they may give a false sense of security. [29 CFR 1910.147(c)(7)(ii)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.147(c)(7)(ii)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under NEC Article 600, fixed, mobile, or portable electric signs and outline lighting systems shall be:","o":["Inspected and approved by the manufacturer's field representative only","Approved only by the sign designer who laid out the work","Listed and labeled, with instructions, and installed per the listing","Labeled only when the sign is to be installed outdoors"],"a":2,"e":"Article 600 requires signs and outline lighting to be listed and labeled and installed per the listing and instructions. Field-installed skeleton tubing is a stated exception. [NEC 600 (listing requirement)]","r":"NEC 600 (listing requirement) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"At month-end, the total of the job cost ledger for open jobs should agree with the","o":["the contractor's bank statement balance at the end of the month","the quarterly payroll tax deposit report filed with the IRS","the work-in-process (job cost control) account in the general ledger","the owner's personal tax return for the year in which the jobs close"],"a":2,"e":"Reconciling the job subledger to the control account catches posting errors, unrecorded invoices and miscoded time. [Job costing]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Liquidated damages in a construction contract are","o":["a fee charged by the building department each day","the cost of builder's risk insurance on the whole project","a bonus paid to the contractor for finishing early on","a daily amount set in advance as compensation for late completion"],"a":3,"e":"FAR 52.211-12 requires payment of a stated amount for each calendar day of delay until the work is completed or accepted. [FAR 52.211-12]","r":"Liquidated damages"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which location is generally unsuitable for a smoke detector due to nuisance alarms?","o":["A corridor outside an office","An elevator lobby","The ceiling of a storage room with ordinary stock","Directly above a cooking range in a commercial kitchen"],"a":3,"e":"Cooking aerosols and steam cause nuisance alarms; heat detectors are typically used in kitchens. [NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 17 and annex guidance]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 17 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.129(1)(p), F.S., intimidating, threatening, coercing or discouraging the service of a notice to owner under chapter 713 is:","o":["Permitted if the owner consents","Permitted for jobs under $5,000","A ground for discipline","A requirement for lien waivers"],"a":2,"e":"Paragraph (p) makes it a disciplinary ground to intimidate, threaten, coerce or discourage service of a notice to owner under part I of chapter 713, or a notice to contractor under chapter 255 or part I of chapter 713. [F.S. 489.129(1)(p)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(1)(p)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor collects 40% of each month's billings in the month billed and 60% the next month. Billings were $100,000 in April and $120,000 in May. What are projected May cash receipts?","o":["$120,000","$112,000","$48,000","$108,000"],"a":3,"e":"May receipts = 40% x 120,000 + 60% x 100,000 = 48,000 + 60,000 = 108,000. The other choices apply the percentages to the wrong month. [Cash flow forecasting]","r":"Cash flow forecast arithmetic"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"IMC must be securely fastened at intervals not exceeding:","o":["15 ft","20 ft","5 ft","10 ft"],"a":3,"e":"IMC is fastened within 3 ft of each termination and supported at intervals not exceeding 10 ft. [NEC 342.30]","r":"NEC 342.30"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A rigid metal conduit enters an explosionproof enclosure containing a switch that can make or break arcs, in a Class I, Division 1 location. Within what distance of the enclosure must a seal be installed?","o":["12 in.","24 in.","36 in.","18 in."],"a":3,"e":"Seals are required within 450 mm (18 in.) of the enclosure when it contains arcing devices. [NEC 501.15(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 501.15(A)(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"If employees provide their own PPE on a construction job, what is the employer's responsibility under 29 CFR 1926.95(b)?","o":["Nothing, because employee-owned PPE is exempt from OSHA","Reimburse the employee, then retain ownership","Require it to be replaced every year","Ensure it is adequate, properly maintained and sanitary"],"a":3,"e":"Where employees provide their own protective equipment, the employer is responsible for ensuring its adequacy, including proper maintenance and sanitation.","r":"29 CFR 1926.95(b)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A change order for added circuits states, 'Contract time is unchanged.' The contractor later claims delay due to the added work. The change order language most likely means:","o":["The contractor reserved the right to claim added time later","The owner agreed to pay liquidated damages for the added work","The contractor agreed to no time extension for that change","The change order is not binding on either of the parties"],"a":2,"e":"A signed change order that states the time is unchanged generally waives a later time-extension claim for that change. Contractors should state any time impact before signing. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A Florida private employer subject to E-Verify certifies its compliance to the state by:","o":["Mailing a letter to DBPR each January stating that it uses E-Verify","Posting a notice of E-Verify participation inside the shop or office","Filing a certification with the county tax collector each October","Signing a certification on its first reemployment tax return filed each calendar year"],"a":3,"e":"Covered employers certify E-Verify use on the first return of the year filed with the Department of Revenue, usually the fourth-quarter return filed in January. [F.S. 448.095]","r":"F.S. 448.095"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Service equipment at a carnival that is accessible to unqualified persons must be:","o":["Weatherproof only","Painted yellow","Lockable","Located at least 10 ft high"],"a":2,"e":"Service equipment must be lockable when accessible to unqualified persons. [NEC 525.10(A)]","r":"NEC 525.10(A)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which tool may be equipped with a momentary-contact on-off switch that may have a lock-on feature if released by one motion of the same finger?","o":["Hand-held circular saw","Hand-held chain saw","Hand-held drill","Hand-held percussion tool"],"a":2,"e":"Hand-held drills, tappers, fastener drivers, grinders with wheels over 2 inches, disc and belt sanders, reciprocating saws and saber saws can have momentary-contact switches with a lock-on if one motion of the same finger turns it off. [29 CFR 1926.300(d)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.300(d)(2)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a series-parallel circuit, if the series element opens, then","o":["only the parallel branches lose power while the series path remains active","the total circuit voltage doubles across the open point","the parallel branches each carry double their normal current","all current stops, because every path passes through it"],"a":3,"e":"The series element carries the total current, so opening it interrupts every branch. [Combination circuits]","r":"Combination circuits"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A common internal control for significant purchases is to require at least how many competing quotes, where practical?","o":["Three","Seven","Two","One"],"a":0,"e":"Obtaining several competitive quotes supports fair pricing and documents the buyer's due diligence; the exact number is set by company policy. [Purchasing control practice]","r":"Competitive quotes"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The point of attachment of overhead service conductors to a building must in no case be less than what height above finished grade?","o":["12 ft","15 ft","10 ft","8 ft"],"a":2,"e":"The point of attachment must provide the 230.24 clearances and may never be lower than 10 ft above finished grade. [NEC 230.26]","r":"NEC 230.26"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A one-line diagram shows a 225 kVA, 480 V three-phase transformer secondary feeding a main breaker. Using full-load amperes = VA / (1.732 x V), what is the transformer secondary full-load current?","o":["469 A","271 A","625 A","156 A"],"a":1,"e":"FLA = 225,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 270.6 A. The wrong choices come from using single-phase math, a 208 V base, or dividing by 3. [Three-phase current formula]","r":"Three-phase current formula"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A tubular LED lamp is installed in existing sign sockets. What label is required by Article 600?","o":["A label showing the date of the most recent relamping of the sign","A label stating the sign was modified, and warning not to install fluorescent lamps","A label showing the design wind speed of the sign structure","No label is required for LED lamps used in the existing sockets"],"a":1,"e":"Signs equipped with tubular LED lamps powered by the existing sockets require a label alerting service personnel that the sign has been modified, with a warning not to install fluorescent lamps. [NEC 600.4]","r":"NEC 600.4 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A feeder is protected by a 600 A overcurrent device. A 25-ft tap is made with conductors that terminate in a single circuit breaker limiting the load to the tap conductor ampacity, with the tap protected from physical damage. What is the minimum ampacity of the tap conductors?","o":["300 A","60 A","200 A","400 A"],"a":2,"e":"A 25-ft tap needs an ampacity of at least one-third of the feeder OCPD rating: 600/3 = 200 A. [NEC 240.21(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 240.21(B)(2)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1910.333(b)(1), how must electrical parts that are deenergized but not locked out or tagged be treated?","o":["As deenergized parts if the breaker handle is off","As grounded parts if the circuit is only 120 volts","As if they were still energized parts","As safe parts if a tester was used once"],"a":2,"e":"Parts that have been deenergized but not locked or tagged out must be treated as energized. [29 CFR 1910.333(b)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(b)(1)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A transformer or power supply that has secondary-circuit ground-fault protection must be:","o":["Marked to show that it has secondary ground-fault protection","Installed only in indoor dry locations","Wired with 10 AWG GTO conductors on its secondary","Protected by a 30 A fuse on its primary"],"a":0,"e":"600.23(F) requires transformers and power supplies equipped with secondary ground-fault protection to be so marked. [NEC 600.23(F)]","r":"NEC 600.23(F)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A lienor signs a conditional waiver in exchange for a check. The waiver becomes effective","o":["when the check is signed","when the claim of lien is recorded","only when the check is paid by the bank","30 days after signing"],"a":2,"e":"713.20 allows a waiver given for a check to be conditioned on payment of the check. A lienor should not release rights on a check that may not clear. [F.S. 713.20]","r":"F.S. 713.20"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Electrical nonmetallic tubing (ENT) may be used in any building not exceeding how many floors above grade (for both exposed and concealed work, subject to 362.12)?","o":["Two","Three","Five","One"],"a":1,"e":"ENT is permitted in buildings not exceeding three floors above grade; in taller buildings it must be concealed behind a 15-minute thermal barrier or in a building with an automatic fire protective system. [NEC 362.10]","r":"NEC 362.10"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor builds a customer's service upgrade under a lump sum contract. When the contractor buys conduit and panels from a Florida supplier, what should the contractor do?","o":["Pay sales tax to the supplier and not charge the customer sales tax on the materials","Give the supplier a resale certificate and charge the customer tax","Pay no tax because installation labor is exempt","Charge the customer tax only on the labor"],"a":0,"e":"A contractor on a lump sum contract is the consumer of the materials, so it pays tax on purchases and does not collect tax from the customer. Giving a resale certificate would be improper. [Rule 12A-1.051(3)(a) and (4), F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 300-A feeder supplies the entire load of one dwelling unit. Using 310.12 (83% of the feeder rating, no adjustment or correction factors, 75°C columns), what is the smallest copper conductor size?","o":["250 kcmil","300 kcmil","4/0 AWG","350 kcmil"],"a":0,"e":"83% of 300 A = 249 A. 4/0 AWG at 75°C carries only 230 A; 250 kcmil carries 255 A and meets the requirement (Table 310.12(A) lists 250 kcmil for 300 A). [NEC 310.12, Table 310.12(A)]","r":"NEC 310.12, Table 310.12(A)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Ceiling-fan outlet boxes that are listed and marked for support of a fan weighing more than 35 pounds must also show what?","o":["The date of manufacture","The wire gauge allowed","The maximum weight to be supported","The maximum fan blade length"],"a":2,"e":"A box used for a fan over 35 lb must be marked with the maximum weight to be supported; boxes listed for fans may support up to 70 lb. [NEC 314.27(C), 422.18]","r":"NEC 314.27(C), 422.18"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A cash flow forecast for a contracting business is primarily used to","o":["calculate the exact taxable income that will be reported on the annual return","determine the market value of the business for a prospective buyer or lender","predict when cash will come in and go out so shortfalls can be planned for","record the annual depreciation expense on equipment and vehicles for the books"],"a":2,"e":"A forecast projects the timing of receipts and disbursements, not tax or valuation figures; it highlights months when a line of credit or collections push is needed. [SBA, manage your finances]","r":"SBA - Manage your business"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.533(1)(m)1, F.S., a valid lien for supplies or services for which the contractor received customer funds is financial mismanagement if it is not removed by payment or bond within:","o":["10 days after recording","75 days after recording","30 days after recording","180 days after recording"],"a":1,"e":"A valid lien that remains unremoved 75 days after recording, despite customer funds having been received, is financial mismanagement or misconduct. [F.S. 489.533(1)(m)1]","r":"F.S. 489.533(1)(m)1"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the low-voltage contact limit for sinusoidal ac under Article 680?","o":["30 volts","50 volts","15 volts (rms)","6 volts"],"a":2,"e":"The low voltage contact limit is 15 V (rms) for sinusoidal ac, 21.2 V peak for non-sinusoidal. [NEC 680.2]","r":"NEC 680.2"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A dwelling living room has a single unbroken wall 13 ft long. Considering only the floor-line spacing rule of 210.52(A), what is the fewest number of receptacle outlets that can serve that wall?","o":["Two","Three","One","Four"],"a":0,"e":"No point along the floor line may be more than 6 ft from a receptacle, so one outlet can cover at most 12 ft of wall. A 13-ft wall cannot be covered by a single outlet, so two are required. [NEC 210.52(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 210.52(A)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.533(1)(q), F.S., failing to display the registration or certification number as required by s. 489.521(7) is:","o":["Allowed if the contractor is a sole proprietor","A ground for discipline","Allowed for jobs under $5,000","Allowed during hurricane season"],"a":1,"e":"Failing to display the number is a disciplinary ground, but under Rule 61G6-10.005 an initial violation is designated minor and generally handled with a notice of noncompliance. [F.S. 489.533(1)(q); 489.521(7)]","r":"F.S. 489.533(1)(q)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A project requirement for how and when submittals must be prepared and delivered is usually found in which Division 01 section?","o":["01 78 00 - Closeout Submittals","01 25 00 - Substitution Procedures","01 21 00 - Allowances","01 33 00 - Submittal Procedures"],"a":3,"e":"Submittal Procedures is 01 33 00. Closeout Submittals (01 78 00) covers record documents and manuals, and 01 25 00 covers substitutions. [CSI MasterFormat 01 33 00]","r":"CSI MasterFormat 01 33 00"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A firesafety inspector certificate in Florida lasts 4 years. Renewal requires at least how many hours of continuing education in the preceding 4 years?","o":["24 hours","40 hours","200 hours","54 hours"],"a":3,"e":"F.S. 633.216(4) sets the certificate term at 4 years with 54 hours of continuing education. The 200 hours is initial training. [F.S. 633.216(4)]","r":"F.S. 633.216(4)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor recorded 6 recordable cases in a year and its employees worked 420,000 hours. What is the Total Recordable Case Incidence Rate (cases x 200,000 / hours worked), rounded to one decimal?","o":["14.3","0.3","1.4","2.9"],"a":3,"e":"TRIR = 6 x 200,000 / 420,000 = 1,200,000 / 420,000 = 2.9. The 200,000 base equals 100 full-time employees working 2,000 hours a year. [29 CFR 1904, Appendix; OSHA/BLS]","r":"OSHA incidence rate formula"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Conductors in a vertical raceway must be supported if the vertical rise exceeds the values in Table 300.19(A). What is the maximum spacing for 6 AWG through 1/0 AWG copper conductors?","o":["200 ft","60 ft","40 ft","100 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Table 300.19(A) allows 100 ft for copper 6 AWG through 1/0 AWG (200 ft for aluminum of those sizes). [NEC 300.19(A), Table 300.19(A)]","r":"NEC 300.19(A), Table 300.19(A)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum width of the clear working space in front of electrical equipment rated 600 volts or less under the OSHA construction electrical standard?","o":["24 inches","36 inches","30 inches","42 inches"],"a":2,"e":"1926.403(i)(1) requires working space at least 30 inches wide in front of the electric equipment. [29 CFR 1926.403(i)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.403(i)(1)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NEC 700.16 requires emergency illumination to be arranged so that:","o":["each luminaire has its own separate battery source","failure of a single lighting element does not leave a space in total darkness","luminaires are controlled by a wall switch in the room","the lighting is controlled by a dimmer to save energy at night"],"a":1,"e":"Emergency illumination must be designed so a lamp or driver failure does not leave any space dark, typically by overlapping coverage. Wall switches and dimmers are restricted for such lighting. [NEC 700.16]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 700.16 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An information technology equipment room qualifies for the alternative wiring methods of 645.5 only if which condition is met?","o":["The room is separated from other occupancies by fire-resistant-rated construction and has an approved disconnecting means","The room is below grade","The room contains no HVAC equipment of any kind","The room is open to the public for tours"],"a":0,"e":"Conditions include disconnecting means per 645.10, an HVAC arrangement, listed equipment, restricted occupancy, fire-resistant separation, and only associated equipment in the room. [NEC 645.5]","r":"NEC 2023 645.5"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A junction box for a wet-niche pool luminaire, not separated from the pool by a fence or wall, must be at least how far from the inside wall of the pool?","o":["10 ft","4 ft","1 ft","6 ft"],"a":1,"e":"The box must be 1.2 m (4 ft) from the inside wall (unless separated by a solid barrier) and elevated at least 4 in. above ground or 8 in. above the maximum water level. [NEC 680.24(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 680.24(A)(2)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A series RLC circuit has R = 12 ohms, XL = 25 ohms and XC = 20 ohms. What is the impedance?","o":["17 ohms","13 ohms","46.6 ohms","27.7 ohms"],"a":1,"e":"Net reactance = 25 - 20 = 5 ohms; Z = sqrt(12 squared + 5 squared) = 13 ohms. [Impedance]","r":"Impedance"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor pays a supplier in full for materials on a job. To protect the owner and itself against duplicate payment, the contractor should obtain:","o":["A copy of the supplier's insurance","A release (waiver) of lien from the supplier","A new purchase order","The supplier's catalog"],"a":1,"e":"Release of lien forms provide evidence of payment and protection against later lien claims. [F.S. 713.20]","r":"F.S. 713.20"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's current ratio is 1.80 with current assets of $900,000 and current liabilities of $500,000. Management uses $150,000 of cash to buy a service truck. What are the new current ratio and working capital?","o":["1.80 and $400,000","1.50 and $250,000","2.14 and $400,000","2.10 and $550,000"],"a":1,"e":"Cash (a current asset) falls $150,000 and the truck is a noncurrent asset. Current assets = $750,000; working capital = $750,000 - $500,000 = $250,000; ratio = 1.50. [Current ratio / working capital]","r":"Current ratio"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor violates s. 489.126(2), F.S., after receiving exactly $20,000. Under the penalty tiers, the offense is a:","o":["Third-degree felony","First-degree misdemeanor","First-degree felony","Second-degree felony"],"a":3,"e":"The tier of $20,000 to under $200,000 is a second-degree felony; exactly $20,000 is the start of that tier, not the top of the third-degree tier. [F.S. 489.126]","r":"F.S. 489.126 penalty tiers"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"An industrial control panel must be marked with its:","o":["Maximum allowed conduit size","Ground resistance in ohms","Short-circuit current rating","Insulation resistance"],"a":2,"e":"Industrial control panels must be marked with their short-circuit current rating; the marking must not be less than the available fault current at the panel. [NEC 409.110]","r":"NEC 409.110"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Which change would DECREASE cash from operating activities for a contractor, all else equal?","o":["Adding back depreciation","An increase in billings in excess of costs and estimated earnings (overbillings)","An increase in accounts payable","An increase in costs and estimated earnings in excess of billings (underbillings)"],"a":3,"e":"Underbillings mean costs and profit were recognized but not yet billed or collected, which uses cash. Overbillings and higher payables retain cash. [Statement of cash flows; WIP analysis]","r":"Cash flow analysis"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An allowance in a contract is","o":["a discount the owner receives for paying early","a percentage that is added to every change order","a set dollar amount for an item not yet selected or fully defined, adjusted to actual cost","a reserve the contractor keeps if it is not spent"],"a":2,"e":"Allowances carry placeholder costs for items such as light fixtures. The contract is adjusted up or down when actual cost is known. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What is the maximum sound pressure level NFPA 72 permits for audible public mode notification at the minimum hearing distance?","o":["100 dBA","90 dBA","110 dBA","120 dBA"],"a":2,"e":"NFPA 72 limits audible public mode appliances to 110 dBA, while the ADAAG ceiling is 120 dBA. The more restrictive limit applies in the design. [NFPA 72 (2019), 18.4]","r":"NFPA 72 18.4 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"For a series circuit with R = 30 ohms and XL = 40 ohms, the current lags the applied voltage by about","o":["36.9 degrees","0 degrees","53.1 degrees","90 degrees"],"a":2,"e":"Angle = arctan(XL / R) = arctan(1.333) = 53.1 degrees. 36.9 would result from inverting the ratio. [Phase angle]","r":"Phase angle"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The labor unit for 3/4-inch EMT is 14.5 labor hours per 100 feet. How many labor hours should be estimated for 3,800 feet?","o":["551 hours","55 hours","55,100 hours","1102 hours"],"a":0,"e":"3,800/100 = 38 units x 14.5 = 551 hours. Dividing by 1,000 instead of 100 gives the wrong decimal place. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor bills 35% of a $480,000 schedule of values this period and the owner withholds 5% retainage on the amount billed. Previous billings were zero. What net payment is due?","o":["$159,600","$456,000","$168,000","$8,400"],"a":0,"e":"Gross billing = $480,000 x 35% = $168,000. Retainage = $8,400. Net = $159,600. [Schedule of values billing]","r":"Pay application (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Metal parts of a sign must be connected to which conductor of the supply circuit?","o":["The equipment grounding conductor","The grounded (neutral) conductor","The secondary return conductor","A metal part of the building"],"a":0,"e":"Metal equipment of signs is connected to the equipment grounding conductor of the supply branch circuit or feeder per 600.7(A). [NEC 600.7(A)]","r":"NEC 600.7(A)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under ADAAG 4.27.4, what is the maximum force allowed to operate controls required to be accessible?","o":["2.5 lbf (11 N)","5 lbf (22.2 N)","8.5 lbf (38 N)","15 lbf (67 N)"],"a":1,"e":"Controls must be operable with one hand, without tight grasping, pinching, or twisting of the wrist, and with no more than 5 lbf. The 8.5 lbf figure is not the control limit; it is a common wrong guess. [ADAAG 4.27.4]","r":"ADAAG 4.27.4"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A surety adjusts a contractor's balance sheet by removing assets of doubtful value such as slow receivables, loans to officers, and personal-use property. This adjusted figure is usually called","o":["book depreciation adjusted for salvage","gross receipts adjusted for sales tax","adjusted net worth (or underwriting net worth)","cost of revenue adjusted for waste"],"a":2,"e":"Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C. uses adjusted net worth in its qualification formulas; sureties similarly discount nonoperating or doubtful assets. [Rule 14-22.002; surety practice]","r":"Adjusted net worth (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which IRS form does a business request from a new vendor or subcontractor to obtain its taxpayer identification number?","o":["Form W-9","Form W-4","Form I-9","Form 940"],"a":0,"e":"Form W-9 collects the payee's name, TIN and certification, used to prepare Forms 1099 when required. [IRS Form W-9]","r":"IRS Form W-9"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The project manual includes a specification section whose Part 1 states 'Installer Qualifications: Fire alarm installer shall be factory-trained and hold a Florida license.' Which statement is most accurate about where this requirement is found and how the contractor should treat it?","o":["It is advisory only, because Part 1 is general information","It applies only if the owner requests it after award","It is a Part 1 quality assurance requirement the contractor must meet and may have to document","It is replaced by the stricter Part 3 execution article, which controls whenever the two parts differ"],"a":2,"e":"Part 1 often includes quality assurance and submittal requirements that are enforceable contract requirements. Part 3 covers installation and testing. [CSI SectionFormat]","r":"CSI SectionFormat"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which optical fiber cable marking indicates a nonconductive cable suitable for use in a plenum?","o":["OFCP","OFNR","OFNP","OFNG"],"a":2,"e":"The N means nonconductive and the P means plenum. OFCP is conductive plenum, and OFNR is nonconductive riser. [NEC Art. 770 / listing and marking]","r":"NEC 2023 770.179 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A workers' compensation premium audit is performed","o":["to compare actual payroll by classification to the estimate used to set the premium","to inspect the contractor's tools and equipment","to approve the contractor's bid before it is submitted","to set the contractor's license renewal fee"],"a":0,"e":"The estimated premium is based on projected payroll; the audit adjusts it to actual payroll after the policy period. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under 215.4(A), a common neutral is permitted for up to how many sets of 3-wire feeders?","o":["Six","Two","Three","Four"],"a":2,"e":"Up to three sets of 3-wire feeders, or two sets of 4-wire or 5-wire feeders, may utilize a common neutral. [NEC 215.4(A)]","r":"NEC 215.4(A)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Florida's Uniform Commercial Code, a contract for the sale of goods for a price of $500 or more is generally not enforceable unless:","o":["There is a writing sufficient to show a contract, signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought","The goods are delivered to the buyer within 30 days of the order","The buyer paid the Florida sales tax on the goods in advance","The seller is a Florida corporation registered with the Secretary of State"],"a":0,"e":"Section 672.201 sets the statute of frauds for goods at $500 or more, with exceptions such as specially manufactured goods and accepted or paid-for goods. [F.S. 672.201]","r":"F.S. 672.201"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor owns a trencher that cost $120,000. Accumulated depreciation is $45,000. What is the trencher's book value on the balance sheet?","o":["$75,000","$45,000","$165,000","$120,000"],"a":0,"e":"Book value = cost - accumulated depreciation = $120,000 - $45,000 = $75,000. Accumulated depreciation is a contra-asset that reduces the asset. [Depreciation / balance sheet]","r":"Depreciation"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contract sets liquidated damages of $500 per calendar day. The completion date was day 100 and the work was actually completed on day 137, with no time extensions. What are the liquidated damages?","o":["$9,250","$19,000","$18,500","$68,500"],"a":2,"e":"Days late = 137 - 100 = 37. LD = 37 x $500 = $18,500. [FAR 52.211-12 per calendar day]","r":"Liquidated damages"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A balanced three-phase 15 kW resistive heater is connected to 480 V. What is the line current?","o":["54.13 A","31.25 A","18.04 A","10.42 A"],"a":2,"e":"I = P / (1.732 x E) = 15,000 / 831.4 = 18.04 A (unity PF). [Three-phase power]","r":"Three-phase power"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer must keep employment tax records for at least how long?","o":["1 year","2 years","7 years","4 years"],"a":3,"e":"Employers must keep employment tax records (payroll, TINs, W-4s, deposit records) for at least 4 years after the tax is due or paid, whichever is later. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Using the ASCE 7-style equation q = 0.00256 x Kz x Kzt x Kd x Ke x V^2 (q in psf, V in mph), find q for Kz = 0.85, Kzt = 1.0, Kd = 0.85, Ke = 1.0, and V = 150 mph.","o":["57.6 psf","0.28 psf","41.6 psf","62.4 psf"],"a":2,"e":"V^2 = 22,500. q = 0.00256 x 0.85 x 1.0 x 0.85 x 1.0 x 22,500 = 41.62 psf. Leaving out the factors gives 57.6 psf, and forgetting to square the speed gives a tiny number. [ASCE 7 velocity pressure equation]","r":"ASCE 7 velocity pressure equation (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A dwelling unit requires a minimum of how many 20-A small-appliance branch circuits for the receptacle outlets in the kitchen, pantry, breakfast room and dining room?","o":["Three","Four","One","Two"],"a":3,"e":"210.11(C)(1) requires two or more 20-A small-appliance branch circuits for these areas, and they are rated 1500 VA each in the load calculation. [NEC 210.11(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 210.11(C)(1)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The warning that the permitting authority prints on a permit card under s. 713.135, F.S., about paying twice and recording a notice of commencement must appear in:","o":["At least 8-point type in any style","At least 14-point, capitalized, boldface type","Any type size that is legible to the owner","At least 12-point type in lowercase letters"],"a":1,"e":"The permit-card warning is required in at least 14-point capitalized boldface type. The owner's contract notice under s. 713.015 is 12-point. [F.S. 713.135(1)]","r":"F.S. 713.135(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A business buys the assets of another Florida business. How does s. 443.1215 treat the acquirer?","o":["It is always exempt as a new employer","It must wait 8 quarters before liability","It can become an employer subject to the chapter as a successor","It is liable only for federal unemployment tax"],"a":2,"e":"Section 443.1215(1)(e)-(f) makes successor employing units employers subject to the chapter when they acquire a business or its assets. [F.S. 443.1215(1)(e)-(f)]","r":"F.S. 443.1215(1)(e)-(f)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under NFPA 72, a fire alarm system's secondary power supply must be capable of operating the system in standby for how long, followed by alarm operation for 5 minutes (non-voice systems)?","o":["12 hours","8 hours","24 hours","60 hours"],"a":2,"e":"The secondary supply must supply standby for 24 hours and then 5 minutes of alarm. Emergency voice systems need 15 minutes of alarm. [NFPA 72 (2019), 10.6.7]","r":"NFPA 72 10.6.7 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A semiweekly depositor pays employees on a Thursday. By which day must the related employment tax deposit be made?","o":["The following Friday","The same Friday","The following Wednesday","The 15th of the next month"],"a":2,"e":"For paydays Wednesday, Thursday, or Friday, the deposit is due the following Wednesday. For Saturday through Tuesday paydays it is due the following Friday. [IRS Topic 757]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 500 kVA, 480-V, 3-phase transformer with 4.5% impedance supplies a switchboard. Assuming an infinite primary source, what is the lowest standard interrupting rating adequate for the main breaker? Available ratings: 10, 14, 18, 22, 25, 35 and 42 kA.","o":["14 kA","18 kA","22 kA","10 kA"],"a":0,"e":"I(FL) = 500,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 601.4 A. Isc = 601.4 / 0.045 = 13,365 A. A 10 kA rating is below that value; 14 kA is the lowest rating that equals or exceeds it. [NEC 110.9]","r":"NEC 110.9"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Bidder A: base $480,000, Alternate 1 add $40,000. Bidder B: base $495,000, Alternate 1 add $20,000. The owner will award to the low bidder on the base bid plus Alternate 1. Who is low and by how much?","o":["Bidder B, by $5,000","Bidder A, by $15,000","Bidder B, by $15,000","Bidder A, by $5,000"],"a":0,"e":"With the alternate: A = 520,000, B = 515,000. B is lower by 5,000. A is lower on the base bid alone, but the award basis includes the alternate. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A transformer has 480 V on the primary and 120 V on the secondary. What is the turns ratio (primary : secondary)?","o":["4 : 1","16 : 1","1 : 4","2 : 1"],"a":0,"e":"Turns ratio = Ep / Es = 480 / 120 = 4. The secondary has one-fourth the turns. [Turns ratio]","r":"Turns ratio"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Per the FDOT manual, who must request weather days from the Department?","o":["No one; the Project Administrator monitors weather and recommends them","The subcontractor, after the final payment is received","The contractor, within 24 hours of each rainfall event","The surety, at the time of substantial completion"],"a":0,"e":"The manual says the contractor does not have to request weather days; the Project Administrator monitors weather and recommends them. [FDOT CPAM 7.2]","r":"Weather days"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A banquet room has 2,100 ft^2 net floor area with tables and chairs (a less concentrated use, 15 ft^2 net per person). What is the occupant load?","o":["210","300","140","1,400"],"a":2,"e":"2,100 net ft^2 / 15 = 140 persons. Using 7 ft^2 per person (concentrated use) would give 300. [NFPA 101, Table 7.3.1.2]","r":"NFPA 101 Table 7.3.1.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For Part I contractors (such as general contractors), s. 489.115(4)(b)1, F.S., requires how many classroom continuing education hours per two-year renewal, and how many on laws and rules?","o":["14 hours, including 1 hour on laws and rules","11 hours, including 3 hours on laws and rules","7 hours, including 1 hour on laws and rules","24 hours, including 8 hours on laws and rules"],"a":0,"e":"Part I licensees renew every two years with at least 14 classroom hours of at least 50 minutes each, one of which must cover laws and rules. [F.S. 489.115(4)(a)-(b)1]","r":"F.S. 489.115(4)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"For an angle pull, the distance between a raceway entry and the raceway entry that encloses the same conductors on another wall must be at least:","o":["3 times the trade size of the smaller raceway","8 times the trade size of the larger raceway","12 in., regardless of raceway size","6 times the trade size of the larger raceway"],"a":3,"e":"The distance between raceway entries enclosing the same conductor must be at least six times the trade size of the larger raceway. [NEC 314.28(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 314.28(A)(2)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which taxes are the 'trust fund' portion of the taxes reported on Form 941?","o":["The employer's share of Social Security and Medicare","FUTA tax and state unemployment tax","Additional Medicare Tax only","Withheld income tax and the employee's share of Social Security and Medicare"],"a":3,"e":"Taxes withheld from employees' pay are held in trust for the government; responsible persons who willfully fail to collect, account for, or pay them over can be held personally liable for the trust fund recovery penalty equal to the unpaid amount. [IRC 6672; IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRC 6672; Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The magnetic field strength around a straight conductor","o":["decreases when the current increases","is independent of the current","increases when the current increases","is strongest at an infinite distance from it"],"a":2,"e":"Field strength is proportional to current and decreases with distance. [Magnetism]","r":"Magnetism"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The voltage drop on a 120 V sign branch circuit using 10 AWG copper (10,380 circular mils) with a 20 A load and 150-ft one-way length is calculated using VD = 2 K I L / CM with K = 12.9. What is the percent voltage drop?","o":["3.73 V, or 3.1 percent","4.69 V, or 3.9 percent","3.1 percent","7.46 V, or 6.2 percent"],"a":3,"e":"VD = 2 x 12.9 x 20 x 150 / 10,380 = 7.46 V, which is 6.2 percent of 120 V. That is above the 3 percent recommendation in the informational note to 210.19(A), so a larger conductor is advisable. Leaving out the factor of 2 halves the result. [NEC 210.19(A) Informational Note; Chapter 9 Table 8]","r":"NEC 210.19(A) Informational Note; Chapter 9 Table 8"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor owes $10,000 on terms 2/10, net 30. To take the discount it borrows the discounted amount for 20 days at 9% simple interest (365-day year). What is its net saving?","o":["$200.00","$248.33","$151.67","$48.33"],"a":2,"e":"Discount = $10,000 x 2% = $200. Interest = $9,800 x 0.09 x 20/365 = $48.33. Net = $200 - $48.33 = $151.67. [Cash discount analysis]","r":"Cash discount analysis"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"To earn a 30% gross margin on selling price for materials that cost $700, what price should the contractor charge?","o":["$490","$910","$1,000","$1,190"],"a":2,"e":"Price = cost / (1 - margin) = $700 / 0.70 = $1,000. Adding 30% to cost gives only $910, a 23.1% margin. [Job costing]","r":"Markup vs margin"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which task is prohibited for a 17-year-old employee under the FLSA Hazardous Occupations Orders?","o":["Working on or about a roof","Answering the office phone","Stocking small parts on shelves","Cleaning the shop with a broom"],"a":0,"e":"Roofing and all work on or about a roof is a hazardous occupation closed to workers under 18, along with excavation deeper than 4 feet and power-driven hoisting equipment. [29 CFR 570.55]","r":"29 CFR 570.55"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NEC 760.121(B)(2) says the branch circuit supplying a power-limited fire alarm (PLFA) system must:","o":["be GFCI protected","not be GFCI or AFCI protected","be a multiwire branch circuit","be AFCI protected"],"a":1,"e":"The fire alarm branch circuit must not have GFCI or AFCI protection, so nuisance tripping cannot disable the system. It must also supply no other loads. [NEC 760.121(B)]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 760.121(B)(2)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 633.202(3), how long does a local fire code amendment remain effective?","o":["Until the local governing body votes to repeal it","Until the next triennial edition takes effect, when it is adopted or rescinded","For one year after the local government adopts it","Permanently, once the local government adopts it"],"a":1,"e":"Local amendments expire when the next triennial edition takes effect; the State Fire Marshal then adopts or rescinds them, and the local government may readopt a rescinded one. [F.S. 633.202(3)]","r":"F.S. 633.202(3)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum age to apply to take the electrical contractor certification examination under s. 489.511, F.S.?","o":["16 years","19 years","21 years","18 years"],"a":3,"e":"Section 489.511(1)(a) requires applicants to be at least 18. [F.S. 489.511(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 489.511(1)(a)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer must begin withholding Additional Medicare Tax from an employee's wages once the employee's wages from that employer exceed which amount in the calendar year?","o":["$250,000 for married filing jointly","$200,000, regardless of filing status","$184,500, the Social Security wage base","$150,000 for a head of household"],"a":1,"e":"Employers withhold the 0.9% Additional Medicare Tax on wages over $200,000 in a calendar year regardless of the employee's filing status; the employee settles any difference on the income tax return. The employer does not match it. [IRS Topic 751]","r":"IRS Topic 751"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor has total liabilities of $690,000 and owner's equity of $460,000. What is the debt-to-equity ratio?","o":["0.67","0.50","1.50","0.60"],"a":2,"e":"Debt-to-equity = total debt / owner's equity = $690,000 / $460,000 = 1.50. Debt divided by total assets would be 0.60. [UNL Extension G2009]","r":"Debt-to-equity"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A subcontract is a contract between:","o":["A contractor and a subcontractor","An owner and a lender","An owner and a subcontractor","A lender and a surety"],"a":0,"e":"The prime contract is between the owner and the contractor. A subcontract is between the contractor and a subcontractor; the subcontractor typically has no contract with the owner. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Why does retainage withheld by the customer NOT reduce revenue recognized under percentage of completion?","o":["Retainage is a tax that is paid to the government and never repaid","Retainage is always forfeited by the contractor at the end of the job","Retainage is recorded as an expense of the contractor in the period withheld","Retainage only delays collection; the work is earned and the withheld amount is a receivable"],"a":3,"e":"Revenue is earned as work is performed; retainage only delays collection and appears as retainage receivable. [IRS Construction ATG]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A bidder's total is much lower than all other bids on a public project, about 25 percent below the next. The owner's best step is generally to","o":["ask the bidder to confirm the bid and review the scope included, as allowed by the bid documents","award immediately, since the lowest price always best serves the public","reject every bid and re-advertise the project the same day","raise the low bidder's price to the average of the other bids"],"a":0,"e":"A large spread can signal an error or scope omission. Owners often seek confirmation to avoid a later default or claim. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 448.095, a private employer first fails to comply with E-Verify and is notified by the Department of Commerce. What happens next?","o":["The employer has 30 days to cure, and fines of $1,000 per day apply after three violations in 24 months","The employer is fined $1,000 per day starting with the first violation","The employer is barred from hiring any new employees for 90 days","The employer's licenses are revoked permanently on the first violation"],"a":0,"e":"The employer gets a 30-day cure period; if it fails three times within 24 months, a fine of $1,000 per day applies until compliance. License suspension may also be imposed. [F.S. 448.095]","r":"F.S. 448.095"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An unlicensed person commits a violation of s. 489.127(1), F.S., while the Governor's executive order declaring a state of emergency is in effect. The offense is a:","o":["First-degree misdemeanor","Third-degree felony","Second-degree felony","First-degree felony"],"a":1,"e":"Section 489.127(2)(c) makes the violation a third-degree felony during a state of emergency declared by the Governor. [F.S. 489.127(2)(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.127(2)(c)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Ballasts, transformers, electronic power supplies and Class 2 power sources must be located:","o":["Behind a finished wall or ceiling, sealed from any access","Accessible, securely fastened, and near the lamps or tubing","On the roof, with no enclosure or protection from the weather","At the service equipment, as far from the lamps as practicable"],"a":1,"e":"600.21 requires them to be accessible, securely fastened, and as near to the lamps or tubing as practicable so that secondary conductors are short. [NEC 600.21(A), (B)]","r":"NEC 600.21(A), (B)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A string has 12 modules, each with Voc 38.0 V at 25 C and a Voc temperature coefficient of -0.28%/C. The record low is -10 C. What is the maximum system voltage?","o":["456.0 V","500.7 V","416.0 V","532.8 V"],"a":1,"e":"Correction factor = 1 + 0.0028 x 35 = 1.098. 12 x 38.0 = 456 V; 456 x 1.098 = 500.7 V. [NEC 690.7(A)]","r":"NEC 690.7(A)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"To enforce an order to vacate, the State Fire Marshal may seek an injunction in which court?","o":["The county court of the county where the Fire Marshal sits","The circuit court of the county where the building is located","The Florida Supreme Court in Tallahassee","The federal district court for the Northern District of Florida"],"a":1,"e":"The Fire Marshal may seek an injunction in the circuit court of the county where the building is located. [F.S. 633.228(2)(b)]","r":"F.S. 633.228(2)(b)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A surety's \"aggregate\" bonding capacity for a contractor refers to","o":["the maximum total of uncompleted bonded work the surety will support at one time","the premium charged for one project","the sum of the contractor's insurance limits","the total number of bids the contractor submits in a year"],"a":0,"e":"Single-job limits cap one project; aggregate limits cap the total backlog under bond. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"When a lienor records a lien and the contractor then records a verified notice of bond under F.S. 713.23(2), the lien","o":["is extinguished and the lienor must start over","remains on the property","becomes a claim against the owner personally","transfers from the property to the bond"],"a":3,"e":"A recorded notice of bond with the bond attached transfers liens to the bond, as the statute provides. [F.S. 713.23(2)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(2)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Standard method, 2,000 sq ft dwelling, 120/240-V single-phase: 3 VA/sq ft lighting, two small-appliance circuits, one laundry circuit, a 12 kW range, a 5.5 kW dryer, a 1.2 kW dishwasher, 0.9 kW disposal, 4.5 kW water heater and 1.1 kW trash compactor, and a 5 kW electric heating unit (larger than the 3.6 kW air conditioner). What is the calculated demand current?","o":["152 A","139 A","100 A","125 A"],"a":3,"e":"Lighting/SA/laundry 10,500 VA demand = 5,625; range 8,000; dryer 5,500; four fixed appliances 7,700 x 75% = 5,775; larger of heat or A/C = 5,000. Total 29,900 VA / 240 = 124.6, or 125 A. [NEC 220.42, 220.53, 220.54, 220.55, 220.60]","r":"NEC 220.42, 220.53, 220.54, 220.55, 220.60"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under FAR 52.236-15, failure to submit the required schedule on time may result in the Contracting Officer","o":["cutting the contract price by half","withholding approval of progress payments","assessing a tax lien against the contractor","canceling the contractor's bid bond right away"],"a":1,"e":"The clause states that failure to submit the schedule on time may lead to withholding approval of progress payments, and that serious noncompliance can support termination for lack of diligence. [FAR 52.236-15]","r":"FAR 52.236-15"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A residential repair contract is for $48,000. Under s. 489.126(2)(a), F.S., which initial payment obligates the contractor to apply for permits within 30 days and begin work within 90 days after permits issue?","o":["$4,900","$4,800","$4,000","$2,400"],"a":0,"e":"The duties apply to an initial payment of MORE than 10 percent of the contract price. 10 percent of $48,000 is $4,800, so $4,800 does not trigger them, while $4,900 does. [F.S. 489.126(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 489.126(2)(a)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which method reduces the starting current of an induction motor by connecting the stator windings first in wye and then in delta?","o":["Wye-delta starting","Capacitor start","Plugging","Reversing starting"],"a":0,"e":"Wye connection applies 1/1.732 of line voltage to each winding, cutting line starting current to about one third. [Starting methods]","r":"Starting methods"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A forecast shows net cash flow for Jan-May of $-12,000, $-18,000, $6,000, $-9,000, $21,000, starting from zero cash with no borrowing. What is the minimum line of credit needed to avoid a negative balance?","o":["$33,000","$39,000","$18,000","$12,000"],"a":0,"e":"Cumulative cash by month: $-12,000, $-30,000, $-24,000, $-33,000, $-12,000. The lowest point is $-33,000, so the line must cover $33,000. Adding only the negative months ($-39,000) ignores the positive month in between. [Cash flow forecasting]","r":"Cash flow forecast arithmetic"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which hazardous (classified) location class covers combustible dusts?","o":["Class I","Class III","Class II","Class IV"],"a":2,"e":"Class I is gases and vapors, Class II is combustible dust, and Class III is ignitible fibers and flyings. [NEC 500.5]","r":"NEC 500.5"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For a workplace injury, how many days of disability must pass before compensation is also paid retroactively for the first 7 days (the waiting period)?","o":["More than 14 days","More than 60 days","More than 7 days","More than 21 days"],"a":3,"e":"No indemnity is paid for the first 7 days of disability, but if disability exceeds 21 days, compensation is allowed from the commencement of disability. [F.S. 440.12(1)]","r":"F.S. 440.12(1)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The architect's approval stamp appears on a luminaire submittal that lists a 2x2 fixture, but the plans call for a 2x4 fixture. After approval, who is responsible if the 2x2 fixtures are installed?","o":["The architect, because the approval stamp covered the submittal and its contents","The contractor, unless the deviation was flagged and approved in writing as a change","The owner, because the owner accepted the finished building from the contractor","The supplier, because the supplier prepared the submittal"],"a":1,"e":"A201 3.12.8 requires work to conform to approved submittals, but the contractor remains responsible for compliance with the contract documents unless the deviation was specifically identified and approved. [AIA A201 3.12.8]","r":"AIA A201 3.12.8"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"What must a contractor obtain from each subcontractor under s. 440.10 to protect itself on coverage?","o":["A verbal assurance from the subcontractor, noted in the daily job log","Evidence of workers' compensation insurance (or a copy of an exempt officer's certificate)","A copy of the subcontractor's OSHA 300 Log for the prior calendar year","A statement of the subcontractor's net worth and recent bank balances"],"a":1,"e":"Section 440.10(1)(c) requires the contractor to obtain evidence of workers' compensation insurance from each subcontractor, plus a copy of any exemption certificate for an electing officer. [F.S. 440.10(1)(c)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(c)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What is the gross federal unemployment (FUTA) tax rate on the first $7,000 of each employee's wages, before credits?","o":["0.6%","5.4%","6.0%","7.65%"],"a":2,"e":"The FUTA rate is 6.0% of the first $7,000 of each employee's annual wages; most employers receive up to a 5.4% credit, for a net 0.6%. [Form 940 instructions; IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"Form 940 instructions"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Any conduit or cable installed beneath a public street, highway or road must have a minimum cover of:","o":["12 in.","24 in.","30 in.","18 in."],"a":1,"e":"Under streets, highways, roads, alleys, driveways and parking lots, all columns of Table 300.5 require 24 in. [NEC Table 300.5]","r":"NEC Table 300.5"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A foreman sees that a phase is 30% over its labor budget at the 40% complete point. The best response is to","o":["wait until the job is complete before looking at any numbers","ignore it, since overruns on one phase usually average out later","investigate the cause promptly and update the forecast instead of waiting for completion","lower the hourly pay of the crew until the budget is recovered"],"a":2,"e":"Variances are most useful when found early enough to correct productivity or change orders. [Variance analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor employs 8 workers at $41,600 each for the year. Using the employer FICA rate of 7.65%, FUTA net 0.6% and Florida new employer reemployment tax of 2.7% (both on the first $7,000 per worker), what are the employer's total payroll taxes?","o":["$27,307.20","$25,459.20","$25,795.20","$29,640.00"],"a":0,"e":"Employer FICA: $332,800 x 7.65% = $25,459.20. FUTA: 8 x $7,000 x 0.6% = $336.00. Reemployment: 8 x $7,000 x 2.7% = $1,512.00. Total = $27,307.20. $25,459.20 is FICA only. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); Form 940 instructions; F.S. 443.131(2)(a)]","r":"IRS Pub 15; Form 940; F.S. 443.131"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Service conductors are installed under 3 in. of concrete beneath a building. For the purpose of the service rules, these conductors are:","o":["Considered inside the building and require a disconnect at the point of entry","Considered inside only if rigid conduit is used","Prohibited under buildings","Considered outside the building"],"a":3,"e":"Conductors installed under not less than 2 in. of concrete beneath a building are considered outside the building. [NEC 230.6]","r":"NEC 230.6"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.129(9), F.S., a person whose license was revoked may not serve as an officer, director or partner of a business organization, or reapply, for how long?","o":["1 year","5 years","2 years","10 years"],"a":1,"e":"The person is barred from those roles and from reapplying for 5 years after the revocation's effective date. [F.S. 489.129(9)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(9)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is a current liability on a contractor's balance sheet?","o":["Accounts payable to suppliers due in 30 days","Equipment owned outright and used on jobs","Retained earnings from prior years","A five-year loan balance due after the next twelve months"],"a":0,"e":"Obligations due within twelve months (or the operating cycle) are current liabilities. The long-term loan balance is noncurrent, and the other two items are an asset and an equity item. [Balance sheet classification]","r":"Balance sheet classification"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum total degree of bends permitted in a run of RMC between pull points?","o":["180 degrees","360 degrees (four quarter bends)","540 degrees","270 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"The total of all bends between pull points (outlet, fitting, conduit body, or box) may not exceed 360 degrees. [NEC 344.26]","r":"NEC 344.26"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The 310.15(C)(1) adjustment factors for current-carrying conductors in a metal wireway apply only when:","o":["The wireway is longer than 24 in.","More than three conductors are present","The number of current-carrying conductors at any cross section exceeds 30","More than 10 conductors are present"],"a":2,"e":"In wireways, adjustment factors apply only where the number of current-carrying conductors exceeds 30 at any cross section; signal and motor-starting control conductors are not counted. [NEC 376.22(B)]","r":"NEC 376.22(B)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Planned value (budgeted work scheduled to date) is $240,000 and earned value (budgeted value of work actually done) is $192,000. What is the schedule performance index (SPI), and is the job ahead or behind?","o":["0.80; behind schedule","1.25; ahead of schedule","1.25; behind schedule","0.80; ahead of schedule"],"a":0,"e":"SPI = EV / PV = $192,000 / $240,000 = 0.80. A value below 1.0 means less work has been accomplished than planned. Inverting the ratio gives 1.25. [Earned value scheduling]","r":"Schedule updates (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A subcontractor schedules these activities (all finish-to-start, working days): S 4d (start); T 6d after S; U 3d after S; V 8d after T; W 5d after U+T; X 4d after W; Y 6d after V+X. Activity V has 1 day of float. A supplier problem makes V take 3 extra days. By how much does the project finish later?","o":["1 day","3 days","2 days","0 days"],"a":2,"e":"V's float absorbs 1 day, so the net delay to the end date is 3 - 1 = 2 days (S-T-V-Y becomes 27). [Float consumption; near-critical activity]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Fire detection and alarm systems and access control systems fall under which MasterFormat division?","o":["Division 26 - Electrical power and lighting","Division 28 - Electronic Safety and Security","Division 27 - Communications and data","Division 21 - Fire Suppression systems"],"a":1,"e":"Division 28 is Electronic Safety and Security (access control, intrusion detection, surveillance, detection and alarm). Division 21 covers fire suppression such as sprinklers. [CSI MasterFormat Division 28]","r":"CSI MasterFormat Division 28"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum overcurrent protection for 10 AWG copper conductors (general rule for small conductors)?","o":["40 A","30 A","20 A","25 A"],"a":1,"e":"Small conductors: 14 AWG Cu 15 A, 12 AWG Cu 20 A and 10 AWG Cu 30 A, applied after correction and adjustment factors. [NEC 240.4(D)]","r":"NEC 240.4(D)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C. says the financial statement for a business organization must be","o":["a verbal summary of finances given by the qualifier","a copy of the bank statement for the latest quarter","prepared under GAAP for the previous fiscal year","prepared on the tax basis for the current month only"],"a":2,"e":"The rule calls for a comprehensive statement of financial condition for the previous fiscal year prepared under GAAP. [Rule 61G6-5.004]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C."},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Which statement best contrasts a surety bond with an insurance policy?","o":["A bond protects the contractor from its own losses, while insurance protects the owner from the contractor","A bond has two parties, while an insurance policy always has three","A bond expects repayment from the principal after a claim; insurance does not expect repayment from the insured","A bond pays regardless of fault, while insurance never pays"],"a":2,"e":"A bond is a credit instrument backed by the principal's indemnity agreement. Insurance spreads risk and pays covered losses without expecting repayment. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a one-family dwelling, smoke alarms are required in which locations?","o":["Inside each sleeping room, outside each sleeping area, and on every level including basements","Only in the kitchen","Only in the hallway of the main floor","Only in the garage and attic"],"a":0,"e":"Household fire alarm requirements place alarms in each sleeping room, outside each separate sleeping area in the vicinity of bedrooms, and on each additional level. [NFPA 72 (2019) 29.8.3.4]","r":"NFPA 72 29.8.3.4 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A purely resistive load has a power factor of","o":["0.5","1.0 (unity)","0.0","0.707"],"a":1,"e":"In a resistive circuit voltage and current are in phase, so true power equals apparent power. [Power factor]","r":"Power factor"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A channel-letter sign has 150 W of 24 V DC LED modules. What is the load current?","o":["3,600 A","0.16 A","6.25 A","7.81 A"],"a":2,"e":"I = P / V = 150 / 24 = 6.25 A. [Basic power calculation]","r":"Basic power calculation"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"When listed nonmetallic conduit encloses the secondary neon conductor and a bonding conductor is required, how far must the bonding conductor be installed from the conduit for a circuit operating at 100 Hz or less?","o":["At least 1-1/2 in. (38 mm)","At least 1/2 in.","At least 3 in.","At least 6 in."],"a":0,"e":"600.7(B): spaced at least 38 mm (1-1/2 in.) from the conduit when the circuit operates at 100 Hz or less, or 45 mm (1-3/4 in.) when over 100 Hz. [NEC 600.7(B)]","r":"NEC 600.7(B)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What protection is required for electric heating cable embedded in a bathroom floor?","o":["GFCI protection for personnel","AFCI protection only","Ground-fault protection of equipment at 30 mA","None if the cable is listed"],"a":0,"e":"Electric heating cables in bathroom and hydromassage tub floors require GFCI protection for personnel. [NEC 424.44(E)]","r":"NEC 424.44(E) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.005, F.A.C., 'financial responsibility' for an electrical contractor applicant means the ability to ensure that","o":["the contractor can afford the premium for a surety bid bond on any job","the public, laborers, and suppliers will not lose money because the contractor cannot pay its obligations","the contractor will never need to borrow money to start any project","the contractor's license renewal fee is paid on its due date each cycle"],"a":1,"e":"The rule defines financial responsibility by protection of the public, laborers, materialmen, suppliers, and others from losses due to the contractor's inability to pay. [Rule 61G6-5.005]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.005, F.A.C."},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"At a junction, three branch conductors carry 12 A, 8.5 A and 4.75 A away from the node. What current flows into the junction?","o":["8.42 A","20.50 A","-1.25 A","25.25 A"],"a":3,"e":"Kirchhoff's current law: current in = current out = 12 + 8.5 + 4.75 = 25.25 A. [Kirchhoff's current law]","r":"Kirchhoff's current law"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A small employer who failed to secure coverage would have paid $300 in premium over the preceding 12 months. Ignoring credits, the penalty is","o":["$1,000","$600","$300","$2,000"],"a":0,"e":"Two times the premium is 600, which is below $1,000, so the $1,000 minimum applies. [F.S. 440.107(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.107(7)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"At higher frequencies, alternating current tends to flow near the conductor surface. This effect is called","o":["skin effect","corona","Faraday effect","hysteresis"],"a":0,"e":"Skin effect increases effective AC resistance of large conductors; hysteresis is a magnetic core loss. [Skin effect]","r":"Skin effect"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 12 V dc door strike draws 0.25 A. The cable has 16.0 ohms per 1000 ft and the run is 150 ft one way (300 ft of conductor in the loop). What is the voltage at the strike?","o":["11.4 V","12.0 V","10.8 V","9.6 V"],"a":2,"e":"R = 16.0 x 300/1000 = 4.8 ohm. Drop = 0.25 x 4.8 = 1.2 V. 12 - 1.2 = 10.8 V (a 10 percent drop). Using 150 ft only gives 11.4 V. [Ohm's law; access control practice]","r":"Access control VD (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A series-rated combination of a 14 kA branch breaker downstream of a current-limiting 100 kA fuse is proposed for a panel that also has motors connected between the two devices. The motors' full-load currents total 160 A. Is the series rating permitted?","o":["No, because the motor full-load currents exceed 1% of the interrupting rating of the lower-rated breaker (140 A)","Yes, because the fuse is rated 100 kA","Yes, because motors do not contribute fault current","No, because series ratings are never permitted in panelboards"],"a":0,"e":"Series ratings may not be used where motors are connected between the two devices and the sum of their full-load currents exceeds 1% of the lower-rated breaker's interrupting rating: 1% of 14,000 A = 140 A, and 160 A is more. [NEC 240.86(C)]","r":"NEC 240.86(C)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What clearance applies under Table A for a crane near a 230 kV transmission line?","o":["15 feet","25 feet","20 feet","35 feet"],"a":2,"e":"230 kV falls in the over 200 to 350 kV band, which is 20 feet; over 350 to 500 kV is 25 feet. [29 CFR 1926.1408 Table A]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1408 Table A"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A sign specialty electrical contractor under Rule 61G6-7.001, F.A.C., may provide the electrical entrance for a remote billboard independent of any structure if the load does not exceed:","o":["10 kilowatts at 120 volts maximum","50 kilowatts at 480 volts maximum","25 kilowatts at 250 volts maximum","100 kilowatts at 600 volts maximum"],"a":2,"e":"The sign specialty excludes electrical work beyond the last disconnect or terminal points, with an exception for metering and a main disconnect for remote billboards or signs needing no more than 25 kW at 250 V maximum. [F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(2)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(2)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The utility line electrical contractor specialty covers transmission and distribution circuits and substation construction for:","o":["Residential subdivisions of any size built by private developers","Commercial buildings taller than three stories in urban areas","Telecommunications companies and cable television providers only","Investor-owned utilities, municipal utilities, and rural electric cooperatives"],"a":3,"e":"The scope runs from the point of origin to the point of delivery for investor-owned utilities, city municipal utilities and rural electric cooperatives. [F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(5)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(5)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Liquidtight flexible metal conduit (LFMC) must be securely fastened within what distance of each box or termination, and at what maximum interval?","o":["12 in., and every 4-1/2 ft","24 in., and every 6 ft","3 ft, and every 10 ft","6 in., and every 3 ft"],"a":0,"e":"LFMC (like FMC) is securely fastened within 12 in. of each termination and supported at intervals not exceeding 4-1/2 ft, with listed exceptions. [NEC 350.30]","r":"NEC 350.30"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An owner serves a notice of contest of lien on a lienor. If the lienor does not file suit to enforce the lien within how many days after service, the lien is extinguished?","o":["30 days","60 days","90 days","1 year"],"a":1,"e":"713.22(2) lets the owner shorten the enforcement period to 60 days from service of the notice of contest. [F.S. 713.22(2)]","r":"F.S. 713.22"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Edison-base fuses may be used only:","o":["As replacements in existing installations where there is no evidence of overfusing or tampering","In any new 120/240-V panelboard","For motor branch circuits over 30 A","In new installations up to 60 A"],"a":0,"e":"Edison-base fuses are classified at 125 V and 30 A or less and are limited to replacements in existing installations. [NEC 240.51]","r":"NEC 240.51"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.126, a contractor who receives an initial payment of more than 10 percent of the contract price for residential work must apply for the necessary permits within","o":["10 days after payment","30 days after payment","60 days after payment","90 days after payment"],"a":1,"e":"489.126(2) requires permit applications within 30 days after the payment, unless no permit is needed. [F.S. 489.126(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.126"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under MACRS, salvage value is","o":["subtracted from basis in every year of the recovery period","always assumed to be 10% of the original cost of the asset","added to the basis before the first year's deduction is figured","ignored when figuring depreciation, since the full basis is recovered"],"a":3,"e":"MACRS tables recover the full depreciable basis; salvage value matters for straight-line book depreciation. [IRS Pub 946 (2025)]","r":"IRS Pub 946 (2025)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the FDOT manual, weekend days are counted as weather days only if","o":["the contractor submits a written request for the day","they are scheduled workdays on the accepted schedule","the owner is holding the keys to the job site","rain fell for more than one hour on the day"],"a":1,"e":"Days are granted per contract day, and weekend days count only if they are scheduled workdays on the accepted schedule. [FDOT CPAM 7.2]","r":"Weather days"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Service-entrance conductors are 500 kcmil copper per phase, with a neutral that carries only unbalanced load. What is the minimum size copper grounded (neutral) conductor brought to the service equipment under 250.24(C)?","o":["1/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","2/0 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The grounded conductor brought to service equipment may not be smaller than the Table 250.102(C)(1) size for the largest ungrounded conductor (500 kcmil gives 1/0 AWG copper), and 220.61 loading must also be met. [NEC 250.24(C), Table 250.102(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 250.24(C), Table 250.102(C)(1)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in an assembly occupancy of fire-rated construction?","o":["Type NM cable","Type UF cable","Type MC cable","Type NMC cable"],"a":2,"e":"In fire-rated assembly buildings, wiring must be in rigid metal conduit, EMT, MC, AC or similar metal methods. NM and ENT are permitted only in non-fire-rated buildings of limited type. [NEC 518.4(A)]","r":"NEC 518.4(A)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under AIA A201 section 3.12.4, shop drawings, product data, and samples:","o":["Are always part of the contract documents","Are not contract documents","Replace the drawings when they differ","Automatically create a change order"],"a":1,"e":"A201 states shop drawings, product data, samples, and similar submittals are not contract documents. Approval does not change the contract unless the deviation is specifically called out and accepted. [AIA A201 3.12.4]","r":"AIA A201 3.12.4"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Open overhead conductors at 13.2 kV to ground cross near an in-ground residential pool. What is the minimum clearance to the water level?","o":["25 ft","22.5 ft","12 ft","16 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Open overhead conductors from 0 to 15 kV to ground need 7.5 m (25 ft) clearance in any direction to the water level. [NEC 680.9(A)]","r":"NEC 680.9(A)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For the executive, administrative and professional exemptions, the federal salary level restored effective May 15, 2026 is how much per week?","o":["$455","$844","$684","$1,128"],"a":2,"e":"A 2026 technical amendment restored the 2019 standard of $684 per week ($35,568 per year) after courts vacated later increases. $844 and $1,128 were thresholds from the vacated rules. [29 CFR 541.600; DOL WHD]","r":"29 CFR 541.600"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The output voltage of a generator can be increased by","o":["adding a resistive load to the generator terminals","decreasing the number of armature conductors in series","reducing the field current and slowing the prime mover","strengthening the magnetic field (field current) or increasing speed"],"a":3,"e":"Induced EMF depends on flux, speed and number of conductors. [Generators]","r":"Generators"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Why is a conduit seal installed in a Class I location?","o":["Keep water from entering the conduit","Support conductors in vertical runs","Limit gas and vapor passage and stop flame travel in the raceway","Reduce voltage drop in long runs"],"a":2,"e":"Seals limit migration of gases and vapors and contain explosion pressures; they are not drainage or support fittings. [NEC 501.15]","r":"NEC 501.15"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A price escalation clause in a contract is intended to","o":["adjust the contract price for specified changes in material cost","raise the price whenever the contractor is late","convert a lump sum contract to a unit price contract","waive the owner's right to inspect the work"],"a":0,"e":"Escalation clauses share the risk of volatile material prices by tying price adjustments to defined indexes or documented changes. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size copper main bonding jumper for a service with 400 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors?","o":["2/0 AWG","1 AWG","2 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.102(C)(1): copper ungrounded conductors over 350 through 600 kcmil require a 1/0 AWG copper bonding jumper. [NEC 250.28(D), Table 250.102(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 250.28(D), Table 250.102(C)(1)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Credit terms of '2/10, net 30' mean:","o":["A 30% discount if paid within 10 days; due in 2 months","A 10% discount if paid within 2 days; the full amount is due in 30 days","A 2% penalty if paid after 10 days; due in 30 days","A 2% discount if paid within 10 days; the full amount is due in 30 days"],"a":3,"e":"'2/10, net 30' offers a 2% cash discount for payment within 10 days and requires full payment within 30 days of the invoice date. [Purchasing practice]","r":"Vendor terms"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The employer's share of Social Security and Medicare tax on wages (below the Social Security wage base) in 2026 is","o":["15.3% (employer pays both halves)","6.2% only","1.45% only","7.65% (6.2% + 1.45%)"],"a":3,"e":"Pub 15 states the employer pays 6.2% Social Security and 1.45% Medicare; the 15.3% figure is the combined employer-plus-employee amount for self-employed persons. [IRS Pub 15 (2026), Sec. 1]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size of bonding conductor used for an equipotential plane at a marina?","o":["12 AWG stranded copper","6 AWG aluminum","10 AWG solid copper","8 AWG solid copper"],"a":3,"e":"Equipotential plane bonding conductors must be solid copper, insulated, covered or bare, not smaller than 8 AWG. [NEC 555.14]","r":"NEC 555.14"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 100 Ah battery supplies a constant 10 A load. If only 80 percent of the capacity may be used, how long can it run?","o":["1.25 hours","12.5 hours","8 hours","10 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Usable Ah = 100 x 0.8 = 80; hours = 80 / 10 = 8 hours. Ignoring the limit gives 10. [Batteries]","r":"Batteries"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"ADAAG 4.4 limits how far wall-mounted objects may protrude into a corridor when their leading edges are between 27 in. and 80 in. above the floor. What is the limit?","o":["2 in.","4 in.","12 in.","6 in."],"a":1,"e":"Objects with leading edges between 27 in. and 80 in. may protrude no more than 4 in. into walks, halls, corridors, and aisles. Twelve inches is the allowance for free-standing objects mounted on posts or pylons. [ADAAG 4.4.1]","r":"ADAAG 4.4.1"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under NEC 110.26(C)(2), equipment rated 1200 amperes or more and over 6 feet wide requires:","o":["A single entrance, with a width of at least 36 inches","An entrance at each end of the working space","Only an access door equipped with panic hardware","No special entrance, because equipment width is not a factor"],"a":1,"e":"Large equipment needs an entrance at each end of the working space for escape, unless a continuous unobstructed way of exit travel is provided or the working space is doubled.","r":"NEC 110.26(C)(2) (2023) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The only qualifying agent of a business organization leaves. Under s. 489.521(5), F.S., the organization has how long to qualify another person?","o":["10 days","30 days","180 days","60 days"],"a":3,"e":"The organization has 60 days from the termination to qualify another person or the board will find it no longer qualified. A temporary certificate may allow completion of incomplete contracts only. [F.S. 489.521(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.521(5)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer must deposit FUTA tax for a quarter if the undeposited FUTA liability exceeds which amount?","o":["$100","$1,000","$500","$2,500"],"a":2,"e":"If the accumulated undeposited FUTA tax is over $500 at quarter end, deposit by the last day of the month following the quarter; otherwise carry it to the next quarter. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the time for one complete cycle (the period) of a 60 Hz supply?","o":["16.67 ms","1.67 ms","8.33 ms","60 ms"],"a":0,"e":"T = 1 / f = 1 / 60 = 16.67 ms. 8.33 ms is a half cycle. [Frequency and period]","r":"Frequency and period"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Flour, starch and grain dusts belong to which Class II group?","o":["Group E","Group G","Group F","Group D"],"a":1,"e":"Group E is metal dust, Group F is carbonaceous dust such as coal and carbon black, and Group G is flour, starch, grain and plastic dusts. [NEC 500.6(B)]","r":"NEC 500.6(B)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Annual ownership cost for the trencher is straight-line depreciation $8,000, interest at 6% on average investment ((52,000 + 12,000)/2) and insurance $900. Operating cost is $6.50 per hour and expected use is 1,000 hours per year. What hourly rate recovers all costs?","o":["$17.32","$10.82","$25.32","$14.50"],"a":0,"e":"Interest = 32,000 x 6% = 1,920; ownership = 8,000 + 1,920 + 900 = 10,820; per hour 10.82; plus operating 6.50 = 17.32. [Equipment cost recovery]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Type NM cable must be secured within 12 in. of each box and at intervals not exceeding:","o":["8 ft","6 ft","3 ft","4-1/2 ft"],"a":3,"e":"NM cable is supported and secured at intervals not exceeding 4-1/2 ft and within 12 in. of every cable entry into enclosures. [NEC 334.30]","r":"NEC 334.30"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In ABC inventory analysis, 'A' items are:","o":["The items that never need to be counted during cycle counts","The small number of items that represent the largest share of inventory dollars","The items purchased from only one vendor in the entire year","The items with the lowest dollar value in the stockroom"],"a":1,"e":"ABC analysis ranks items by annual dollar usage; A items get the tightest control and the most frequent counts. [Inventory management]","r":"ABC analysis"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which statement about splicing the grounding electrode conductor is correct?","o":["Splices are permitted with solder only","It must be installed in one continuous length without a splice, except for listed irreversible compression connectors, exothermic welding, or busbar connections","Splices with wire nuts are permitted","Any splice is permitted in a junction box"],"a":1,"e":"A GEC is installed without splice or joint, except for irreversible compression-type connectors listed as grounding and bonding equipment, exothermic welding, and busbars. [NEC 250.64(C)]","r":"NEC 250.64(C)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"NFPA 72 requires the operable part of a manual fire alarm box to be mounted at what height above the floor?","o":["Not less than 48 in. and not more than 54 in.","Not less than 54 in. and not more than 60 in.","Not less than 30 in. and not more than 40 in.","Not less than 42 in. and not more than 48 in."],"a":3,"e":"NFPA 72 places the operable part 42 in. to 48 in. above the floor. The 48 in. maximum agrees with the ADA forward reach limit. [NFPA 72 (2019), 17.15]","r":"NFPA 72 17.15 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A committed cost on a job report is","o":["a cost the company has agreed to pay, such as a purchase order or subcontract, whether invoiced or not","a cost that has already been paid in cash from the company's bank account","a cost that the estimator does not expect to occur on the job","the contractor's profit target for the job as set in the original bid"],"a":0,"e":"Committed costs show future obligations so the forecast of final cost includes work ordered but not yet billed. [Job costing]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 20 ohm and a 30 ohm resistor are in parallel across 120 V. What is the total current drawn from the source?","o":["24 A","2.4 A","10 A","6 A"],"a":2,"e":"6 A + 4 A = 10 A. The equivalent resistance is 12 ohms, and 120 / 12 = 10 A. [Parallel circuits]","r":"Parallel circuits"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.115(5)(b), the board defines 'financial responsibility' by rule, based on the applicant's","o":["the number of jobs completed during the prior year","the applicant's estimated annual contract revenue","credit history, bondability, and any bankruptcy history","ten years of personal tax returns and bank statements"],"a":2,"e":"The statute directs the board to define financial responsibility by rule using credit history, bondability, and bankruptcy or receivership history. [F.S. 489.115(5)(b)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(5)(b)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"One household electric range is rated 16 kW. Using Table 220.55 Column C and the note for ranges over 12 kW, what is its demand?","o":["9.6 kW","12.8 kW","11.2 kW","8.0 kW"],"a":0,"e":"Column C gives 8 kW for one range; for each kW above 12 kW the value is increased 5%. 4 kW over = +20% so 8 x 1.20 = 9.6 kW. [NEC Table 220.55]","r":"NEC Table 220.55"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Using the small-business gross receipts test described in IRS Pub 334 (2023) (average annual gross receipts of $29 million or less for the 3 prior years), a contractor with prior-year receipts of $24.0M, $31.0M and $30.0M has an average of 28.33M. What is the result?","o":["Qualifies, because the three-year average is under $29 million even with one year over it","Does not qualify, because one of the three years was above the $29 million threshold","Does not qualify, because only the most recent year ($30.0M) is tested against the threshold","Qualifies only if the contractor also keeps an inventory of materials for resale"],"a":0,"e":"The test uses the average of the 3 prior tax years: (24+31+30)/3 = 28.33M, below $29M. A single year over the threshold does not disqualify. Thresholds are adjusted yearly. [IRS Pub 334 (2023)]","r":"IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A building has a calculated occupant load of 620 persons. What is the minimum number of exits for the story under NFPA 101?","o":["4","2","3","5"],"a":2,"e":"Two exits are required up to 500 occupants, three for 501 to 1000, and four for more than 1000. 620 falls in the 501-1000 band. [NFPA 101, 7.4.1.2]","r":"NFPA 101 7.4.1.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An owner records a notice of contest of lien under s. 713.22(2), F.S. How long does the lienor have, after service, to sue before the lien is extinguished?","o":["20 days","30 days","60 days","1 year"],"a":2,"e":"A notice of contest shortens the enforcement period; the lienor has 60 days after service to sue, or the lien is automatically extinguished. [F.S. 713.22(2)]","r":"F.S. 713.22(2)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which alternative test makes an employing unit a Florida reemployment tax employer without meeting the $1,500 payroll test?","o":["At least one employee in each of 20 different calendar weeks","At least 5 employees on any single day of the year","Annual gross receipts of at least $100,000 for the year","One employee working for 52 consecutive days in a row"],"a":0,"e":"The 20 weeks need not be consecutive and need not involve the same person. [F.S. 443.1215(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 443.1215(1)(a)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Can metal parts of a building be used as the equipment grounding conductor or as the secondary return conductor for a sign?","o":["Yes, if they are bonded to the service equipment of the building","Yes, but only if the sign is installed outdoors on the building","Yes, if the metal members are 1/2 in. or thicker in section","No, metal building parts may not be used for that"],"a":3,"e":"600.7(A) prohibits using metal parts of a building as a secondary return or equipment grounding conductor, and 600.7(B) prohibits using them to bond sign parts. [NEC 600.7(A), (B)(3)]","r":"NEC 600.7(A), (B)(3)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For 2026, an employee earns $200,000. The Social Security wage base is $184,500. What is the employer's total Social Security plus Medicare tax on this employee?","o":["$15,300.00","$14,114.25","$14,339.00","$11,439.00"],"a":2,"e":"Social Security = 184,500 x 6.2% = 11,439.00; Medicare = 200,000 x 1.45% = 2,900.00; total 14,339.00. Medicare has no wage base limit. [IRS Pub 15 (2026), Sec. 1]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A loaded labor cost of $39.00 per productive hour plus overhead of $22.50 per hour gives a full cost of $61.50. To earn a 10% profit on price, the hourly billing rate must be","o":["$68.33","$67.65","$65.40","$72.35"],"a":0,"e":"Price = cost / (1 - 0.10) = 61.50/0.90 = 68.33. Multiplying by 1.10 gives 67.65, a profit of only 9.1% of price. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An amateur transmitting-station antenna of hard-drawn copper has a maximum open span of 180 ft. What is the minimum conductor size per Table 810.52?","o":["14 AWG","12 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"Table 810.52 lists 14 AWG for hard-drawn copper under 150 ft and 10 AWG over 150 ft. [NEC Table 810.52]","r":"NEC 2023 Table 810.52"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A new educational occupancy has maximum travel distance of 150 ft in an unsprinklered building. If the building is fully sprinklered, the limit becomes:","o":["175 ft","200 ft","250 ft","300 ft"],"a":1,"e":"New educational occupancies allow 150 ft unsprinklered and 200 ft sprinklered. [NFPA 101, 14.2.6]","r":"NFPA 101 14.2.6 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A lighting-control subcontractor first furnishes materials on February 10, 2026. Counting 45 days from that date, the last day to serve a timely notice to owner (assuming no earlier final payment) is","o":["March 12, 2026","April 11, 2026","March 27, 2026","May 11, 2026"],"a":2,"e":"Feb 10 + 45 days = Mar 27, 2026. Serving later is not timely, and failure to serve a timely notice is a complete defense to the lien. [F.S. 713.06(2)]","r":"F.S. 713.06"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 440.10(1)(b), F.S., a general contractor who sublets part of a contract is liable for workers' compensation coverage of which employees?","o":["Only its own direct employees","All subcontractor employees even if the sub has coverage","Those of a subcontractor who has not secured coverage","No subcontractor employees"],"a":2,"e":"The contractor is liable for, and must secure payment of, compensation to employees of a subcontractor, except employees of a subcontractor who has secured such payment. [F.S. 440.10(1)(b)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(b)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"If one lamp in a series string of lamps burns out (opens), the other lamps","o":["become brighter","stay lit on the same current","light at half brightness","all go out because the circuit is broken"],"a":3,"e":"A series circuit has one path; an open anywhere stops all current. [Series circuits]","r":"Series circuits"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What type of eye and face protection is required when using an abrasive wheel grinder?","o":["Prescription glasses without side shields, since they correct vision","Tinted sunglasses with a wraparound frame, if the grinding is outdoors","None, if the wheel guard is installed and is adjusted correctly","Eye protection meeting ANSI Z87.1, plus a face shield as needed"],"a":3,"e":"Employees exposed to flying objects from grinders must have appropriate eye and face protection meeting the referenced ANSI Z87.1 standard. [29 CFR 1926.102(b)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.102 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Within a spray booth (the interior of the booth) where flammable finishes are applied, how is the location classified?","o":["Class I, Division 2","Unclassified","Class I, Division 1","Class III, Division 1"],"a":2,"e":"The interior of a spray booth or spray room is Class I, Division 1 (and Class II as applicable). Areas outside an open face are Division 2. [NEC 516.5(B)]","r":"NEC 516.5(B) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"During bidding, a contractor finds a conflict between the electrical plans and the specifications. The best action is to","o":["submit a written request for information so an addendum can clarify it","price the cheaper option and say nothing to anyone","call the inspector and rely on a verbal answer","wait until after award to raise the issue"],"a":0,"e":"Written questions let the designer answer all bidders equally through an addendum. Verbal answers are not binding on the owner. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In a private-job payment bond claim under F.S. 713.23, suit on the bond must be filed within","o":["30 days after the notice of commencement is recorded","2 years after substantial completion","1 year after the lienor's last furnishing","6 months after the owner's final payment"],"a":2,"e":"The one-year period runs from the last furnishing; a notice of contest can shorten it to 60 days. [F.S. 713.23(1)(e)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(1)(e)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 2-1/2 in. PVC conduit run is supported on a wall. What is the maximum distance between supports?","o":["3 ft","5 ft","10 ft","6 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Table 352.30: 2-1/2 through 3 in. PVC requires support at 6 ft maximum. [NEC Table 352.30]","r":"NEC Table 352.30"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The unit of electrical resistance is the","o":["farad","ohm","weber","henry"],"a":1,"e":"Resistance is measured in ohms (symbol omega). The farad is capacitance and the henry is inductance. [Electrical units]","r":"Electrical units"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 455.225(3)(a), F.S., a licensee who receives notice of a minor violation has how many days to correct it?","o":["5 days","15 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":1,"e":"Subsection (3)(a) gives 15 days after notice to correct a minor violation; Rule 61G6-10.005 treats an initial missing-number violation as minor. [F.S. 455.225(3)(a)]","r":"F.S. 455.225(3)(a)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Rule 61G4-15.003, F.A.C. sets minimum public liability and property damage coverage for a certified general contractor at","o":["$25,000 public liability and $10,000 property damage","$100,000 public liability and $25,000 property damage","$300,000 public liability and $50,000 property damage","$1,000,000 public liability and $500,000 property damage"],"a":2,"e":"General and building contractors need $300,000 and $50,000; residential and most specialty contractors need $100,000 and $25,000. [F.A.C. 61G4-15.003]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-15.003"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a room with an 8 ft (96 in.) ceiling, wall-mounted visual alarm appliances must be mounted no higher than which dimension under ADAAG 4.28.3?","o":["80 in. above the highest floor level in the space","90 in. above the highest floor level in the space","96 in. above the highest floor level in the space","6 in. above the ceiling line at the lens top"],"a":0,"e":"The appliance is mounted 80 in. above the highest floor level or 6 in. below the ceiling, whichever is lower. With a 96 in. ceiling, 6 in. below is 90 in., so the lower value, 80 in., governs. [ADAAG 4.28.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.3"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under a common clause, if one drawing is drawn at 1/8 in. = 1 ft and another at 1/2 in. = 1 ft shows the same equipment layout differently, which governs?","o":["The smaller-scale drawing, because it shows the whole plan","The one issued last without regard to the scale","The larger-scale drawing, because it is more detailed","The one with the thicker lines"],"a":2,"e":"Typical precedence language states that larger-scale drawings take precedence over smaller-scale drawings. [Typical order-of-precedence clause]","r":"Typical order-of-precedence clause"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 12,000 sq ft retail store on a 208Y/120-V, 3-phase, 4-wire service has: general lighting at 3 VA/sq ft (continuous), 60 receptacles on separate yokes (180 VA each, demand per 220.44), and 22 kVA of fixed HVAC (continuous). What is the calculated service current?","o":["281.2 A","207.3 A","230.1 A","184.3 A"],"a":2,"e":"Lighting 36,000 x 1.25 = 45,000 VA; receptacles 60 x 180 = 10,800 VA, demand 10,000 + 0.5 x 800 = 10,400 VA; HVAC 22,000 x 1.25 = 27,500 VA. Total 82,900 VA / (1.732 x 208) = 230.1 A. [NEC 220.12, 220.14(I), 220.44, 230.42(A)]","r":"NEC 220.12, 220.14(I), 220.44, 230.42(A)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Approximately how much torque does a 10 hp motor develop at 1,750 rpm (hp = torque x rpm / 5,252)?","o":["360 lb-ft","15.0 lb-ft","3.33 lb-ft","30.0 lb-ft"],"a":3,"e":"Torque = 10 x 5,252 / 1,750 = 30.0 lb-ft. Lower speed gives higher torque at the same horsepower. [Motor torque]","r":"Motor torque"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.505, F.S., a specialty contractor is one whose scope is:","o":["Limited to work on state-owned buildings and public schools only","Limited to a segment of electrical or alarm contracting set by board rule","Unlimited in scope, but valid only within a single county","Limited to jobs with a total price under $2,500 in any year"],"a":1,"e":"Specialty contractors are limited to a segment defined by board rule, such as residential electrical contracting, fixture maintenance, or advertising sign work. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What minimum clearance must a scaffold keep from an uninsulated power line carrying less than 50 kV?","o":["3 feet","10 feet","6 feet","15 feet"],"a":1,"e":"Scaffolds may not be erected closer than 10 feet to uninsulated power lines of 50 kV or less; insulated lines under 300 V need only 3 feet. [29 CFR 1926.451(f)(6)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.451(f)(6)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A chronically underbilled job usually signals","o":["a strong cash position because work is done ahead of what has been invoiced","that excess retainage has been released early to the contractor by the owner","that the lien rights on the job have expired early under chapter 713","the contractor is financing the owner's project, so billing should be accelerated"],"a":3,"e":"Underbillings mean costs have been paid but not yet billed; speeding up pay applications recovers cash. [Billing practice]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A subcontractor who is not exempt refuses to give the contractor a TIN on Form W-9. What federal backup withholding rate may apply to the payments?","o":["10%","22%","24%","37%"],"a":2,"e":"Backup withholding is 24% on reportable payments when the payee fails to furnish a TIN. 22% is the flat supplemental wage rate and 37% is the supplemental rate above $1 million. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A contractor receives an RFI response that increases the work beyond the contract. What should the contractor do before proceeding?","o":["Proceed, because an RFI response is a final and binding change to the contract price and time","Proceed with the work and invoice the extra at the end","Ignore the response, since RFIs never change the contract","Notify the architect in writing that the response affects cost or time and request a change order"],"a":3,"e":"RFI responses generally do not change cost or time unless incorporated by change order or other formal change. Notify promptly and get written direction. [RFI practice (AIA A201)]","r":"RFI practice (AIA A201)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Eight current-carrying 250 kcmil THHN copper conductors (90°C ampacity 290 A; 75°C ampacity 255 A) are in a conduit in a 43°C ambient. What is the maximum ampacity each conductor may be used at?","o":["255.0 A","145.0 A","176.6 A","202.6 A"],"a":2,"e":"Corrected ampacity = 290 x 0.87 (41–45°C, 90°C) x 0.70 (7–9 conductors) = 176.6 A, which is below the 75°C terminal value of 255 A, so 176.6 A governs. [NEC 310.14, 310.15(B)(1), 310.15(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 310.14, 310.15(B)(1), 310.15(C)(1)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Between 'pay-when-paid' and an unconditional payment obligation, which is better for a subcontractor's cash flow?","o":["Pay-when-paid, because it guarantees the subcontractor is paid faster than the prime","The unconditional obligation, because payment does not depend on the general contractor being paid first","They are identical in effect, since both depend on the owner's payment history","Pay-when-paid, because it removes the need for a written payment request"],"a":1,"e":"Under F.S. 715.12(4) payment to an obligee is due when the obligor has been paid by the party above it, among other conditions; a subcontractor may wait for upstream payment. [F.S. 715.12(4)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A completed 2,400-square-foot office electrical system cost $82,800. Using the same cost per square foot, what is the budget price for a similar 3,150-square-foot office?","o":["$46,994.59","$108,675","$25,875","$34.50"],"a":1,"e":"82,800/2,400 = 34.50 per sq ft; x 3,150 = $108,675. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A cash flow budget differs from an income statement because it shows","o":["only the depreciation expense recorded on the contractor's vehicles and tools","only the owner's salary and the draws taken from the business during the year","only the sales tax and payroll taxes that the contractor owes to the state","actual or planned cash receipts and payments, not revenue and expenses when earned"],"a":3,"e":"The income statement is accrual-based; the cash flow budget is about the timing of money in and out. [Cash flow basics]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Outside a bar magnet, the lines of force travel from","o":["the south pole to ground","south to north","the center to the south","north pole to south pole"],"a":3,"e":"By convention, magnetic flux lines leave the north pole and enter the south pole. [Magnetism]","r":"Magnetism"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A single-phase 240 V circuit draws 25 A at a 0.85 power factor. What is the true power?","o":["5.10 kW","7.06 kW","3.16 kW","6.00 kW"],"a":0,"e":"Apparent power = 240 x 25 = 6.0 kVA; true power = 6.0 x 0.85 = 5.10 kW. [Power triangle]","r":"Power triangle"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A subcontractor schedules these activities (all finish-to-start, working days): S 4d (start); T 6d after S; U 3d after S; V 8d after T; W 5d after U+T; X 4d after W; Y 6d after V+X. Which activity is NOT on the critical path?","o":["V","W","X","T"],"a":0,"e":"The critical path is S-T-W-X-Y (25 days). V has 1 day of float (S-T-V-Y = 4+6+8+6 = 24 days). [CPM critical path]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which statement about lien waivers and cash flow is correct?","o":["Lien waivers speed up payment because they replace the owner's notice of commencement","Lien waivers are never used on Florida projects, since the lien law forbids them","A contractor should sign final waivers before issuing the final invoice for the job","Requiring unconditional releases before payment can delay cash, so conditional waivers exchanged for payment are common"],"a":3,"e":"713.20 allows waivers to be conditioned on payment, which protects the lienor against giving up rights for a check that does not clear. [F.S. 713.20]","r":"F.S. 713.20"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which additional article applies to a theater stage and its audience area in an assembly occupancy?","o":["Article 520","Article 525","Article 530","Article 540"],"a":0,"e":"Article 520 covers theaters, audience areas of motion picture and TV studios, performance areas and similar locations. [NEC 518.3, 520.1]","r":"NEC 518.3, 520.1"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Within what time after an employee starts work must the employer complete Section 2 of Form I-9?","o":["Before the first paycheck is issued","Within 3 business days of the first day of employment","Within 30 calendar days","By January 31 of the next year"],"a":1,"e":"Employers must examine documents and complete Section 2 of Form I-9 within three business days after the employee's first day of work. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); USCIS Form I-9 instructions]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026) / USCIS I-9"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor deducts the cost of a lost tool from a nonexempt employee's final paycheck so that the employee receives less than the minimum wage for that week. Under the FLSA, this deduction is:","o":["Always allowed in Florida because it is an at-will employment state","Not allowed to the extent it cuts wages below the minimum wage or cuts into overtime due","Allowed only if the tool cost is under $100 and the employee was careless","Allowed up to the full cost of the tool if the employee signed a tool policy"],"a":1,"e":"Deductions for items that primarily benefit the employer cannot reduce pay below the minimum wage or into required overtime, even with employee consent. [29 CFR 531.35]","r":"29 CFR 531.35"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a construction site, which receptacle outlets must be protected by a GFCI (when the employer does not use an assured equipment grounding conductor program for them)?","o":["120-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere receptacle outlets in use that are not part of the permanent wiring","Only receptacle outlets installed on the exterior of the building under construction","All 240-volt receptacle outlets, whether or not they are part of the permanent wiring","Only 30-ampere and larger receptacle outlets used for welders and heavy equipment"],"a":0,"e":"1926.404(b)(1)(ii) requires GFCI protection for 120-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere temporary receptacles in use by employees. Location outdoors or amperage above 20 is not the test.","r":"29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(ii)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What unit lighting load in VA per square foot does Table 220.12 assign to a dwelling unit?","o":["3 VA","3.5 VA","2 VA","1 VA"],"a":0,"e":"Dwelling units are calculated at 3 VA per sq ft of outside-dimension floor area (excluding open porches, garages and unused spaces). [NEC 220.12, Table 220.12]","r":"NEC 220.12, Table 220.12"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which metal parts of a fountain must be bonded?","o":["Only the pump casing","Only the lighting fixtures","None, if the circuit is GFCI-protected","All metal piping and metal fittings in or on it"],"a":3,"e":"All metal piping, fittings and parts within or attached to the fountain must be bonded. [NEC 680.53]","r":"NEC 680.53"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An electrician pulls conductors through a 2-hour fire-rated wall in conduit leaving an annular gap. The opening must be:","o":["firestopped with a listed system that restores the wall's 2-hour rating","left open as long as the gap is smaller than 4 in.","packed with fiberglass insulation compressed in the gap","sealed with silicone caulk applied from one side only"],"a":0,"e":"Penetrations of rated assemblies must be firestopped with a listed system tested for the wall's rating; NEC 300.21 and NFPA 101 8.3.5 require this. Insulation and common caulk are not listed firestop systems. [NEC 300.21]","r":"NEC 300.21; NFPA 101 8.3.5 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"How must a hydromassage bathtub be supplied?","o":["By a shared bathroom circuit","By a 20 A AFCI circuit","By an isolated circuit with no protection","By an individual branch circuit protected by a GFCI"],"a":3,"e":"Hydromassage tub circuits must be individual and GFCI-protected. [NEC 680.71]","r":"NEC 680.71"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1910.333(c)(4), what must exist before an employee enters a space containing exposed energized parts?","o":["A second worker holding a flashlight outside the space for the entire job","A written permit signed by the building owner before any work begins","A fire watch stationed at the door of the space during the work","Adequate illumination, and no blind reaching into areas that may be energized"],"a":3,"e":"Employees may not enter spaces with exposed energized parts unless adequate illumination is provided, and may not reach blindly into areas that may be energized. [29 CFR 1910.333(c)(4)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(4)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which pair of factors most directly raises a contractor's EMR?","o":["Frequent claims and large claims in the experience period","A large number of employees and a high payroll","Many bids submitted but few of them won","Holding more than one contractor license"],"a":0,"e":"The experience rating compares actual losses (frequency and severity) with expected losses for the classifications. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A payment bond protects","o":["the owner against errors in the design documents","subs, laborers, and suppliers who go unpaid","the surety against its own underwriting mistakes","the contractor against nonpayment by the owner"],"a":1,"e":"A payment bond guarantees the principal will pay those who furnish labor and materials; a performance bond protects the owner if the contractor defaults. [Surety bonds]","r":"Surety bonds (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor expects annual direct costs of $2,600,000, annual overhead of $416,000, and wants net profit of 6% of sales. What sales price should a job with $180,000 of direct cost carry, if all jobs use the same ratio of price to direct cost?","o":["$219,600","$208,800","$222,127.66","$191,489.36"],"a":2,"e":"Required sales = (2,600,000 + 416,000) / 0.94 = 3,208,510.64; ratio to direct cost = 1.2340; 180,000 x 1.2340 = $222,127.66. Adding 6% to the cost ratio ignores that profit is a share of price. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A new Florida employer pays one employee $1,176.00 this week (annual wages still under $7,000). Counting the wages plus employer FICA (7.65%), FUTA (net 0.6%) and Florida reemployment tax (2.7%), what is the employer's total cost for the week?","o":["$1,265.04","$1,347.50","$1,304.77","$1,176.00"],"a":2,"e":"FICA $89.96 + FUTA $7.06 + reemployment tax $31.75 = $128.77; plus $1,176.00 wages = $1,304.77. $1,265.04 leaves out the employer's FUTA and reemployment taxes. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); Form 940 instructions; F.S. 443.131]","r":"IRS Pub 15; Form 940; F.S. 443.131"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Where must the GFCI be located for a portable sign used in a wet or damp location, in addition to 210.8 requirements?","o":["At the receptacle only, as a feature of the building","Inside the sign enclosure, next to the transformer","In the attachment plug or in the cord within 12 in. of the plug","In the cord, within 6 ft of the sign itself"],"a":2,"e":"600.10(C)(2): the manufacturer shall provide listed GFCI protection for personnel, integral to the attachment plug or in the supply cord within 300 mm (12 in.) of the plug. [NEC 600.10(C)(2)]","r":"NEC 600.10(C)(2)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which national standards does the State Fire Marshal adopt under F.S. 633.202 as the base of the Florida Fire Prevention Code, subject to Florida modifications?","o":["NFPA 70 and NFPA 72","NFPA 13 and NFPA 25","NFPA 1 and NFPA 101","IFC and IBC"],"a":2,"e":"The statute adopts the current editions of NFPA 1 (Fire Code) and NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code) with state modifications. The IBC is not adopted by the State Fire Marshal. [F.S. 633.202(2)]","r":"F.S. 633.202(2)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which power is measured in volt-amperes reactive (VAR)?","o":["Peak power","Reactive power","True power","Apparent power"],"a":1,"e":"Watts = true power, VA = apparent power, VAR = reactive power. [Power units]","r":"Power units"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Using the figures in a burden study, an installer is paid $30 per hour and costs the company $39.71 per hour fully burdened. What is the total burden as a percentage of base wage (rounded to one decimal)?","o":["21.7%","132.4%","32.4%","10.7%"],"a":2,"e":"(39.71 - 30.00) / 30.00 = 0.3237, or 32.4%. The percentage items alone are 21.7% and omit the fringe cost. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"After an employer learns of a work injury, how soon must it report the injury to its workers' compensation carrier?","o":["Within 90 days","Within 24 hours","Within 30 days","Within 7 days"],"a":3,"e":"The employer must report the injury to the carrier within 7 days after learning of it; the carrier then reports to the Division. [F.S. 440.185(2)]","r":"F.S. 440.185(2)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Cords for portable signs in wet or damp locations must be:","o":["Lamp cord Type SPT-1 with a two-wire attachment plug","Extension cord Type SPT-2 without an equipment grounding conductor","Junior hard-service or hard-service cord with an equipment grounding conductor","Any listed cord that is rated for the load of the sign"],"a":2,"e":"600.10(C)(1) requires junior hard-service or hard-service cords, per Table 400.4, with an equipment grounding conductor. [NEC 600.10(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 600.10(C)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.513(3), F.S., where a local jurisdiction requires no examination, a registration applicant may qualify by scoring at least what percent on a board-approved exam and showing how much technical experience?","o":["70 percent and at least 1 year of technical experience","80 percent and at least 6 years of technical experience","75 percent and at least 3 years of technical experience","100 percent and at least 2 years of technical experience"],"a":2,"e":"The statute allows qualification by scoring at least 75 percent on a substantially equivalent board-approved exam and having at least 3 years of technical experience in the trade. [F.S. 489.513(3)]","r":"F.S. 489.513(3)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which luminaires may NOT be installed in a clothes closet?","o":["Surface-mounted fluorescent luminaires","Recessed luminaires with enclosed lamps","Pendant luminaires and open-lamp incandescent luminaires","Listed LED luminaires identified for the closet"],"a":2,"e":"Incandescent luminaires with open or partially enclosed lamps and pendant luminaires or lampholders are prohibited in closets because the lamp could contact combustible storage. [NEC 410.16(B)]","r":"NEC 410.16(B)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which measurement category is required for testing at the service entrance, such as the utility drop and the meter?","o":["CAT I","CAT II","CAT IV","CAT III"],"a":2,"e":"CAT IV is for the origin of the installation, including service entrance conductors and the meter; CAT III is for distribution downstream of the service.","r":"IEC 61010-1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"How much energy is stored in a 100 microfarad capacitor charged to 400 V (E = 1/2 C V squared)?","o":["2.0 J","0.04 J","16.0 J","8.0 J"],"a":3,"e":"E = 0.5 x 0.0001 x 400 squared = 8.0 joules. C x V (0.04) is charge in coulombs. [Capacitors]","r":"Capacitors"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which item should be left OUT of a cash-basis cash flow forecast because it does not involve a cash payment?","o":["Depreciation expense on a van","Principal payment on an equipment loan","Payroll tax deposit","Owner's draw"],"a":0,"e":"Depreciation is a non-cash expense that allocates cost over time; the cash left when the van was bought or when loan payments are made. Loan principal, tax deposits and draws are all cash outflows. [Cash flow forecasting]","r":"Cash flow basics"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A takeoff shows 1,240 feet of 3/4-inch EMT. The estimator adds 5 percent for waste and cuts. EMT comes in 10-foot lengths. How many lengths should be ordered?","o":["131","124","1302","141"],"a":0,"e":"1,240 x 1.05 = 1,302 ft; divided by 10 ft = 130.2, rounded up to 131 lengths. Ordering without waste gives 124, which leaves no allowance. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"After 2 years of straight-line depreciation on the same van, its book value (cost minus accumulated depreciation) is","o":["$32,500","$25,333","$5,000","$27,000"],"a":3,"e":"Annual depreciation 5,500 x 2 = 11,000; book value 38,000 - 11,000 = 27,000. [Depreciation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the key advantage of a Class A initiating device circuit over a Class B circuit?","o":["Class A circuits do not require supervision","A single open fault does not prevent devices from operating, because a return path exists","Class A circuits can be wired with one conductor","Class A circuits eliminate the need for a battery"],"a":1,"e":"Class A circuits are looped back to the panel so one open can be tolerated, while on a Class B circuit an open disables devices beyond the break. [NFPA 72 (2019) 12.3 / 12.4]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 12 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor wants a 30% gross margin on a job costing $8,400. What selling price is needed?","o":["$12,000","$10,920","$5,880","$8,400"],"a":0,"e":"Price = cost / (1 - margin) = 8,400 / 0.70 = 12,000. Cost x 1.30 = 10,920 gives only a 23.1% margin. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A monthly schedule depositor must deposit the employment taxes from payments made in one month by which date?","o":["The last day of the same month","The 15th day of the following month","The 15th day of the second following month","Within 3 business days of each payday"],"a":1,"e":"Monthly depositors deposit by the 15th of the following month; if the 15th is a weekend or D.C. legal holiday, the deposit is due the next business day. [IRS Topic 757]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A free-standing object mounted on a post or pylon may overhang a circulation path by no more than how much between 27 in. and 80 in. above the floor (ADAAG 4.4.1)?","o":["12 in.","8 in.","4 in.","18 in."],"a":0,"e":"Free-standing objects on posts or pylons may overhang by up to 12 in. in the 27 in. to 80 in. zone. The 4 in. limit applies to wall-mounted objects. [ADAAG 4.4.1]","r":"ADAAG 4.4.1"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which of the following is one of the encroachment prevention measures required when using Option 2 or Option 3 near power lines?","o":["Keep a fire watch on the boom during every lift near the line","Use non-conductive tag lines, if tag lines are used","Paint the boom yellow so that it can be seen by the line crew","Disable the crane's brakes while the load is positioned near the line"],"a":1,"e":"1926.1408(b) requires a planning meeting, non-conductive tag lines (if used), an elevated warning line or barricade at the limit, and at least one of: proximity alarm, dedicated spotter, range control warning device, range limiting device or insulating link.","r":"29 CFR 1926.1408(b)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A single-mode link is 2 km long with 0.35 dB/km attenuation, two mated connector pairs at 0.75 dB each, and one fusion splice at 0.3 dB. What is the total expected loss?","o":["2.5 dB","1.0 dB","2.2 dB","3.0 dB"],"a":0,"e":"Fiber: 2 x 0.35 = 0.70 dB. Connectors: 2 x 0.75 = 1.50 dB. Splice: 0.30 dB. Sum = 2.50 dB. [Link-loss budget method; TIA-568.3]","r":"Link loss budget (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"After recording a claim of lien, the lienor must serve a copy on the owner","o":["within 45 days after recording, matching the notice to owner period","within 5 days before final furnishing of labor or materials on the job","only after a lawsuit to enforce the lien has been filed with the court","before recording or within 15 days after recording"],"a":3,"e":"713.08 requires service before recording or within 15 days afterward; untimely service makes the claim voidable only to the extent it prejudices someone relying on it. [F.S. 713.08]","r":"F.S. 713.08"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A general contractor sublets electrical work to a subcontractor who has no workers' compensation coverage. If the subcontractor's employee is hurt on the job, who must secure compensation for that employee?","o":["Nobody, because subcontractors are independent businesses","Only the injured employee's own health insurer","The property owner","The general contractor, as the statutory employer"],"a":3,"e":"Under s. 440.10(1)(b), a contractor that sublets work is liable for compensation for the subcontractor's employees unless the subcontractor has secured coverage. [F.S. 440.10(1)(b)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(b)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In a Florida public bid, a bidder who wishes to protest a notice of intended award must first file a notice of protest within how long after the agency posts its decision?","o":["24 hours","10 days","30 days","72 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Section 120.57(3) requires a written notice of protest within 72 hours of the posting; the formal written protest follows within 10 days. [F.S. 120.57(3)]","r":"F.S. 120.57(3)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If a contractor's fixed costs increase while the contribution margin ratio stays the same, break-even revenue will","o":["decrease","increase","stay the same","become negative"],"a":1,"e":"Break-even = fixed costs / CM ratio, so higher fixed costs raise it. [Break-even analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Federal employment tax deposits are generally required to be made by what method?","o":["Electronic funds transfer, such as through EFTPS","Mailing a check to the IRS office","Paying cash at the employer's bank","Mailing Form 941 with the payment each payday"],"a":0,"e":"Pub 15 requires federal tax deposits to be made by electronic funds transfer (EFTPS or other approved means). [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"An intersystem bonding termination (IBT) must provide terminals for connecting at least how many intersystem bonding conductors?","o":["One","Two","Three","Six"],"a":2,"e":"The IBT must be accessible and consist of terminals able to connect not fewer than three intersystem bonding conductors. [NEC 250.94(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 2023 250.94(A)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under F.S. 489.129(1)(j), a project may be presumed abandoned if the contractor fails to perform work, without just cause, for how long?","o":["90 consecutive days","10 consecutive days with no inspections","30 days after the last progress payment","180 days after the contract date"],"a":0,"e":"The statute permits a presumption of abandonment after 90 consecutive days without work or just cause (or termination without just cause or proper notice). [F.S. 489.129(1)(j)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(1)(j)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor expects a job to cost $250,000 on a contract price of $280,000. What is the estimated gross profit and estimated gross margin?","o":["$250,000 and 89.3%","$30,000 and 10.7%","$30,000 and 12.0%","$180,000 and 64.3%"],"a":1,"e":"Gross profit = $280,000 - $250,000 = $30,000; margin = $30,000 / $280,000 = 10.7%. [WIP schedule]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.127(4), F.S., a certified contractor who lets an unqualified person use the contractor's certification number commits:","o":["A noncriminal infraction","A first-degree misdemeanor on a first offense","A second-degree misdemeanor","A first-degree felony"],"a":1,"e":"Subsection (4) bars agreements allowing unqualified use of one's number; a first violation is a first-degree misdemeanor and a violation after a prior guilty finding is a third-degree felony. [F.S. 489.127(4)(d)]","r":"F.S. 489.127(4)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For a Form 941 filer depositing for calendar year 2027, the lookback period is which span?","o":["January 1, 2026 through December 31, 2026","July 1, 2026 through June 30, 2027","January 1, 2025 through December 31, 2025","July 1, 2025 through June 30, 2026"],"a":3,"e":"The lookback period is the 12 months starting July 1 of the second preceding year and ending June 30 of the prior year (four quarters). [IRS Topic 757]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Metal-clad cable (Type MC) is run as a cable assembly of 12 AWG conductors. Adjustment factors under 310.15(C)(1) do not apply when:","o":["The cables are in a raceway","The cable is longer than 24 in.","The cables are 10 AWG","There is no overall jacket, each cable has no more than three current-carrying 12 AWG conductors, and no more than 20 current-carrying conductors are bundled"],"a":3,"e":"Adjustment factors do not apply to Type AC or MC cable without an overall outer jacket, with no more than three current-carrying 12 AWG copper conductors per cable and not more than 20 current-carrying conductors bundled. [NEC 310.15(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 310.15(C)(1)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NEC Article 411 applies to lighting systems operating at what maximum voltage?","o":["50 volts ac or 100 volts dc","120 volts ac","12 volts ac only","30 volts ac or 60 volts dc or less"],"a":3,"e":"Article 411 covers listed low-voltage lighting systems operating at 30 V ac (42.4 V peak) or 60 V dc or less. [NEC 411.1]","r":"NEC 2023 411.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A step-up transformer has","o":["more secondary turns than primary turns, and less secondary current","fewer secondary turns than primary turns, and more secondary current","equal primary and secondary turns, with equal currents","more primary turns than secondary turns, and a higher secondary voltage"],"a":0,"e":"More secondary turns raises voltage; power is conserved, so current falls. [Step-up transformer]","r":"Step-up transformer"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A specification names a lighting fixture \"or equal.\" The contractor may","o":["install any fixture the contractor likes without approval","charge the owner extra for the specified fixture","propose a substitute that meets the specified performance, subject to designer approval","delay the bid opening until a substitute is found"],"a":2,"e":"\"Or equal\" lets the bidder propose an equivalent product; the designer decides whether it is equal. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Rungs, cleats and steps on portable and fixed ladders must be spaced how far apart, measured center to center?","o":["Between 10 and 14 inches","Between 6 and 10 inches","Between 12 and 18 inches","Between 14 and 20 inches"],"a":0,"e":"Rungs, cleats and steps of portable and fixed ladders must be uniformly spaced 10 to 14 inches apart, center to center. [29 CFR 1926.1053(a)(3)(i)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1053(a)(3)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A $30,000 customer deposit received before work begins is best recorded as","o":["a liability (customer deposit or deferred revenue) until the work is performed","revenue earned in full on the date the deposit is received from the customer","an operating expense of the contractor in the month the deposit is received","an increase to owner's equity in the month the deposit is received"],"a":0,"e":"Deposits for work not yet done are obligations to deliver; the cash helps cash flow but is not yet earned revenue under accrual accounting. [Billing practice; IRS Pub 334 advance payments]","r":"IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.008, the insurance must be carried in the name of","o":["the property owner on each job","the general contractor on each job","the licensee or the business name on the license","the insurance agent who wrote the policy"],"a":2,"e":"The rule requires coverage to be in the name of the licensee or the business entity named on the license. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008(3)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008(3)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which marking identifies Class 2 cable suitable for use in vertical runs through more than one floor (riser)?","o":["CL2P","CL2X","CL2","CL2R"],"a":3,"e":"CL2R is the riser-rated Class 2 cable. CL2P is plenum, CL2X is limited use, and CL2 is general purpose and not riser rated. [NEC 725.179 and 725.154]","r":"NEC 2023 725.154, 725.179 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What are the minimum clear floor space dimensions for one stationary wheelchair and occupant under ADAAG 4.2.4?","o":["30 in. by 48 in.","36 in. by 60 in.","30 in. by 60 in.","36 in. by 48 in."],"a":0,"e":"ADAAG 4.2.4 requires at least 30 in. by 48 in. of clear floor space. 36 in. by 60 in. is not the standard minimum for a single stationary position. [ADAAG 4.2.4]","r":"ADAAG 4.2.4"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Is an emergency shutoff switch for spas required at a single-family dwelling?","o":["Yes, at every spa","No, not at a single-family dwelling","Only for outdoor spas","Only for spas over 300 gallons"],"a":1,"e":"Emergency shutoff is required for spas at other than single-family dwellings, within sight and at least 5 ft away. [NEC 680.41]","r":"NEC 680.41"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On a plan set, a drawing that is titled 'Enlarged Electrical Room Plan' and drawn at a larger scale than the floor plan is mainly used to show:","o":["The architectural finish schedule for the room and its trim","Equipment layout and clearances in more detail than the floor plan allows","The occupancy and egress calculations required by the code","The site utilities that enter the building from the street"],"a":1,"e":"Enlarged plans show congested areas such as electrical rooms at a scale that lets the installer see equipment locations, working space and conduit stub-up locations. They do not show finishes or site utilities. [Drawing practice: enlarged plans]","r":"Drawing practice: enlarged plans"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor reports cash $95,000, accounts receivable $420,000, costs and estimated earnings in excess of billings $38,000, inventory $60,000, and prepaid expenses $12,000. Current liabilities are $430,000. Using the strict definition (cash plus receivables over current liabilities), what is the quick ratio?","o":["0.22","1.20","1.34","1.45"],"a":1,"e":"Quick ratio = ($95,000 + $420,000) / $430,000 = $515,000 / $430,000 = 1.20. Inventory, prepaids, and underbillings are excluded; using all current assets gives 1.45. [Quick (acid-test) ratio]","r":"Quick ratio"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For threaded conduit joints in hazardous (classified) locations, what minimum number of full threads must be engaged?","o":["Three","Two","Seven","Five"],"a":3,"e":"Threaded joints must be made up with at least five threads fully engaged, to keep the joint explosion-proof. [NEC 500.8(E)(1)]","r":"NEC 500.8(E)(1)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The working space at a sign ballast, transformer or power supply (not in the sign) must be at least:","o":["2 ft x 2 ft x 2 ft","30 in. wide x 6.5 ft high x 3 ft deep","4 ft x 4 ft x 4 ft","3 ft high x 3 ft wide x 3 ft deep"],"a":3,"e":"600.21(D) requires a working space at least 900 mm (3 ft) high, wide, and deep at each ballast, transformer, power supply, or its enclosure where not installed in a sign. [NEC 600.21(D)]","r":"NEC 600.21(D)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Two parallel strings each have four 6 V, 200 Ah batteries in series. What is the bank voltage and capacity?","o":["48 V, 200 Ah","12 V, 800 Ah","24 V, 400 Ah","24 V, 200 Ah"],"a":2,"e":"Each string is 4 x 6 = 24 V at 200 Ah; two strings in parallel give 24 V at 400 Ah. [Batteries]","r":"Batteries"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Hourly field workers such as electrician apprentices and helpers are normally classified as:","o":["Nonexempt, because they are paid hourly and do not meet the duties tests","Exempt, because manual work performed outdoors is excluded from overtime","Exempt, because skilled-trade employees qualify for the professional exemption","Exempt once they work more than 40 hours in the same workweek"],"a":0,"e":"Hourly pay fails the salary-basis test, and manual-trade work is not exempt executive, administrative or professional work. [29 CFR 541.3(a)]","r":"29 CFR 541.3"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The essential electrical system of a hospital consists of:","o":["A normal system and an optional standby system","An emergency system and an equipment system","A life safety system only","A legally required standby system only"],"a":1,"e":"The essential system has an emergency system (life safety and critical branches) and an equipment system. [NEC 517.30(B)]","r":"NEC 517.30(B)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An employee of a certified unlimited electrical contractor spends the week installing fire alarm devices for pay. Under F.S. 489.505 this employee is a:","o":["Burglar alarm system agent","Secondary qualifying agent","Registered alarm system contractor II","Fire alarm system agent"],"a":3,"e":"The statute defines a fire alarm system agent as an employee of a licensed fire alarm contractor or certified unlimited electrical contractor who performs fire alarm work for pay. The burglar alarm agent class does not cover fire work. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505(28)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On an exterior elevation, a note says 'WALL PACK AT 12 FT AFF.' What does AFF tell the installer?","o":["The luminaire is mounted above the final fixture finish on the building elevation","The luminaire is supplied by an arc-fault protected feeder circuit","The height is measured to the top of the fixture housing at the wall","The height is measured from the finished floor or finished grade at that location"],"a":3,"e":"AFF is 'above finished floor,' and for exterior work typically the finished grade or walkway. The note does not say whether it is centerline or top, so confirm with the details. [Drawing practice: abbreviations]","r":"Drawing practice: abbreviations"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Increasing the length of a conductor while holding area constant ______ its resistance, and increasing its cross-sectional area ______ it.","o":["raises; raises","lowers; raises","lowers; lowers","raises; lowers"],"a":3,"e":"R = K x L / A. [Conductor properties]","r":"Conductor properties"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under the Department's Voluntary Disclosure Program, when must a business apply to limit lookback and obtain penalty relief?","o":["Within 30 days after an audit notice","Only after a warrant is filed","At any time before the case goes to court","Before the Department contacts the business about the tax"],"a":3,"e":"Voluntary disclosure can limit lookback periods and generally waive most penalties, but only if the business applies before the Department contacts it. [Florida DOR Voluntary Disclosure Program]","r":"Florida DOR VDP"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A one-family dwelling has a 200-A, 120/240-V single-phase service. Using Table 310.12(A) with no adjustment or correction factors, what is the smallest copper service-entrance conductor (75°C or higher)?","o":["4/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","1/0 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 310.12(A) permits 2/0 AWG copper (or 4/0 AWG aluminum) for a 200-A single-phase dwelling service. [NEC 310.12]","r":"NEC 310.12"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A packing slip differs from an invoice in that a packing slip:","o":["Lists the items shipped but is not a bill requesting payment","Replaces the purchase order as the buyer's authorization to purchase","States the total amount due and the payment terms for the shipment","Authorizes the buyer to deduct a cash discount from the payment"],"a":0,"e":"A packing slip accompanies the shipment and is compared to the PO; the invoice requests payment and states terms. [Purchasing control practice]","r":"Receiving control practice"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On a commercial electrical job, the schedule of values (SOV) is","o":["a list of the lien waivers that each subcontractor and supplier is required to sign","the contractor's summary of insurance certificates collected for the owner's file","an itemized breakdown of the contract price by work component, used to support billings","a record of all employee hours kept for payroll and workers' compensation audits"],"a":2,"e":"The SOV divides the total contract sum into line items so each pay application can show the percent complete and dollars earned for each. [Progress billing practice; IRS Construction ATG Ch. 1 on progress billings]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"When may a lock alone, without a tag, be used to deenergize a circuit under 29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(iii)?","o":["Only one circuit is involved, it lasts within the work shift, and exposed employees know of it","The lock is a numbered padlock and the key is kept by the supervisor","More than one crew is working on the same panel at the same time","The circuit voltage is below 120 volts to ground at the disconnect"],"a":0,"e":"A lock without a tag is allowed only if all three conditions are met: one circuit, within the shift, and employees know the circuit is locked. [29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(iii)(B)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)(iii)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A milestone in a project schedule is","o":["the lowest-cost task found in the entire project","a delay that was caused by weather conditions","a long activity that takes the most labor of any task","an event of zero duration marking a key point, such as energization"],"a":3,"e":"Milestones have no duration and mark important dates, such as notice to proceed, rough-in complete, or substantial completion. [Scheduling terminology]","r":"Milestones (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under F.S. 255.20, the base competitive-award threshold that applies to a local government's electrical work (before inflation adjustment) is","o":["$75,000","$300,000","$200,000","$25,000"],"a":0,"e":"Electrical work has its own lower threshold of $75,000; other construction uses $300,000. [F.S. 255.20(1)]","r":"F.S. 255.20(1)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Job 301 has an original contract of $800,000 and an approved change order of $50,000. Estimated total cost is now $680,000, cost to date is $340,000, and billings are $450,000. What is the over- or underbilling using cost-to-cost?","o":["Overbilled by $50,000","Overbilled by $25,000","Overbilled by $110,000","Underbilled by $85,000"],"a":1,"e":"Revised contract = $850,000. Percent complete = $340,000 / $680,000 = 50%. Earned = $425,000. Billed $450,000 - earned $425,000 = $25,000 overbilled. [WIP schedule]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Using the same contract at 40% complete, revenue earned to date is","o":["$224,000","$168,000","$196,000","$392,000"],"a":0,"e":"560,000 x 0.40 = 224,000. [IRS Construction ATG]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On a time-and-materials contract with a not-to-exceed (NTE) amount, the contractor","o":["is paid a fixed lump sum regardless of the actual cost","may bill any amount above the NTE at the contractor's choice","is paid for time and materials used but not more than the NTE without an approved change","must refund all of the profit if the work finishes early"],"a":2,"e":"The NTE caps what the owner must pay unless the owner authorizes more. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's tools and equipment are most commonly insured against theft away from the premises through:","o":["An inland marine (contractors equipment) policy","A commercial auto liability policy only","A surety bond","A workers' compensation policy"],"a":0,"e":"Inland marine coverage protects tools and equipment while in transit or at job sites; auto liability and WC policies do not cover tool theft. [Insurance practice]","r":"Equipment insurance"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A written policy applies equally to everyone but disproportionately screens out members of a protected group, and is not job-related or consistent with business necessity. This describes:","o":["Retaliation","Disparate impact discrimination","Quid pro quo","Disparate treatment"],"a":1,"e":"Facially neutral practices that disproportionately exclude a protected group violate Title VII unless justified by job-relatedness and business necessity. [42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(k)]","r":"42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(k)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"After determining that an employer failed to secure workers' compensation, the Department of Financial Services must issue a stop-work order within","o":["24 hours","72 hours","10 days","30 days"],"a":1,"e":"Section 440.107 requires a stop-work order within 72 hours after the determination. [F.S. 440.107(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.107(7)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.116(1), F.S., what is required before a certificateholder or registrant may contract?","o":["A local business tax receipt only","A bond posted with the county","A letter from the lender","An active-status certificate or registration"],"a":3,"e":"A holder may not contract without an active status; doing so is grounds for discipline under ss. 455.227 and 489.129. Holders may choose active or inactive status at renewal. [F.S. 489.116(1)-(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.116(1)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NEC 600.5 requires a sign outlet at the entrance of commercial occupancies accessible to pedestrians. How many such outlets are required?","o":["At least one outlet at each entrance to each tenant space","One outlet for the whole building, near the main entrance","One outlet for every 100 ft of frontage on the street","One outlet only when the tenant requests it in writing"],"a":0,"e":"Each commercial building and occupancy accessible to pedestrians must have at least one accessible outlet at each entrance to each tenant space for sign or outline lighting use. [NEC 600.5]","r":"NEC 600.5"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under Rule 61G4-19.001, F.A.C., a contractor with an inactive or delinquent license who contracts, and who renews or reactivates within 30 days of the citation, pays a citation of:","o":["$100 with no fees","$1,000 with no fees","$5,000 plus costs","$250, plus applicable fees"],"a":3,"e":"Contracting on an inactive or delinquent license is cited at $250 plus fees if the license is renewed or reactivated within 30 days of the citation. [F.A.C. 61G4-19.001]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-19.001"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Each platform on a scaffold must be at least how wide (except specific scaffold types)?","o":["12 inches","24 inches","18 inches","30 inches"],"a":2,"e":"Each platform unit must be at least 18 inches wide; ladder jack, top plate bracket, roof bracket and pump jack scaffolds need at least 12 inches. [29 CFR 1926.451(b)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.451(b)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A fail-safe electric lock, such as a magnetic lock, behaves how on loss of power?","o":["It remains locked","It locks permanently","It unlocks","It sounds the burglar alarm"],"a":2,"e":"Fail-safe locks require power to stay locked; fail-secure locks stay locked when power is lost. [NTC Blue Book / access control practice]","r":"Access control (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor treats an electrician as a 1099 worker, but the IRS later determines the worker was an employee. Which consequence is most accurate?","o":["Only the worker owes any additional tax at filing","The business may owe withholding, both FICA shares, FUTA and penalties","Only the employer share of Medicare tax is owed","The business owes nothing since it filed Form 1099-NEC"],"a":1,"e":"A misclassified employee leaves the employer liable for the withholding it failed to make, the employer and employee shares of FICA, FUTA, and penalties and interest. Filing a Form 1099-NEC does not remove that liability. [IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27; IRS Pub 15-A]","r":"IRS Fact Sheet 2007-27"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A network (finish-to-start, working days): A 4d (start); B 7d after A; C 6d after A; D 5d after B; E 5d after C; F 3d after D+E. The contractor shortens activity B by 3 days. What is the new project duration?","o":["16 days","18 days","17 days","19 days"],"a":1,"e":"A-B-D-F drops from 19 to 16, but A-C-E-F is 4+6+5+3 = 18 days, so it becomes critical. The project can only improve by 1 day. [Schedule compression]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which is a requirement for a cover over a hole in a floor or walking surface on a construction site?","o":["Support at least the weight of one employee and be painted bright yellow","Be made of plywood at least 1 inch thick and be nailed on every 4 feet","Be removed whenever the area is occupied by a qualified person or crew","Support twice the expected load, be secured, and be marked HOLE or COVER"],"a":3,"e":"Covers must support twice the expected load (twice the maximum axle load on roadways), be secured against displacement, and be color-coded or marked HOLE or COVER. [29 CFR 1926.502(i)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.502(i)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's equity is $520,000 and total assets are $1,300,000. What percentage of assets is financed by owners' equity?","o":["60%","67%","150%","40%"],"a":3,"e":"$520,000 / $1,300,000 = 40%. The remaining 60% is financed by liabilities. [Equity ratio]","r":"Equity ratio"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Why should an electrical contractor schedule rough-in inspections before the walls are closed in?","o":["Wall board is cheaper to buy before the inspection is done","Inspectors work only before noon on weekdays and holidays","Concealed work cannot be inspected once covered and must be uncovered","The NEC requires all of the work to be done in one single trip"],"a":2,"e":"Rough-in inspections must be passed before covering; scheduling the inspection before the drywall crew arrives avoids tear-out and delay. [Inspection coordination]","r":"Coordination with inspections (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In a lump-sum (fixed-price) contract for a defined scope, who normally bears the risk that the actual cost of the work exceeds the estimate?","o":["The owner","The inspector","The contractor","The material supplier"],"a":2,"e":"A lump-sum contract fixes the price for the stated scope, so cost overruns come out of the contractor's profit unless a change order adjusts the price. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire feeder serves only linear 120-V line-to-neutral loads. The maximum unbalanced load is 320 A. What neutral current may be used for the feeder neutral under 220.61?","o":["224 A","200 A","284 A","320 A"],"a":2,"e":"The first 200 A is taken at 100% and the portion over 200 A at 70%: 200 + 0.70 x (320 - 200) = 284 A. [NEC 220.61(B)]","r":"NEC 220.61(B)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A sign shop plans to put neon transformers, each marked 3.8 A input, on one 30 A neon branch circuit. Treating the load as continuous, what is the maximum number of transformers allowed on the circuit?","o":["7 transformers","8 transformers","5 transformers","6 transformers"],"a":3,"e":"Continuous limit = 0.8 x 30 = 24 A. 24 / 3.8 = 6.32, so 6 transformers. Using 30 A directly gives 7. [NEC 600.5, 210.20(A)]","r":"NEC 600.5, 210.20(A)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 489.505, the person who supervises a business's electrical or alarm contracting work on one specific permitted job is the:","o":["Primary qualifying agent","Certificateholder of record for the department","Secondary qualifying agent","Local board representative"],"a":2,"e":"The primary qualifying agent supervises the business's contracting overall, while a secondary qualifying agent is the certified person supervising work on a specific permitted job. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505(14), (15)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 5 hp single-phase motor on 230 V has an efficiency of 85 percent and a 0.90 power factor. What is the full-load current?","o":["12.2 A","19.1 A","21.2 A","16.2 A"],"a":2,"e":"I = hp x 746 / (E x eff x PF) = 3,730 / (230 x 0.85 x 0.90) = 21.2 A. [Motor current]","r":"Motor current"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The OSHA hazard communication standard designates how many pictograms, and are SDS Sections 12-15 enforced by OSHA?","o":["Nine pictograms; OSHA enforces all sections","Eight pictograms; OSHA does not enforce SDS Sections 12 through 15","Six pictograms; OSHA enforces Sections 1 through 10 only","Eight pictograms; OSHA enforces only Section 1"],"a":1,"e":"1910.1200 designates eight pictograms. OSHA will not enforce sections 12-15 (ecological, disposal, transport, regulatory) because they fall under other agencies.","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200(f)(5), (g)(2)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 460-volt, three-phase Design B motor on an instantaneous-trip breaker (motor circuit protector) is being sized. What is the general maximum setting of the instantaneous-trip breaker as a percentage of full-load current?","o":["250% of full-load current","175% of full-load current","300% of full-load current","800% of full-load current (1100% for Design B energy-efficient)"],"a":3,"e":"An instantaneous-trip breaker is limited to 800% of FLC (1100% for Design B energy-efficient motors where necessary to start). The 250% and 175% figures are for inverse-time breakers and dual-element fuses. [NEC Table 430.52, 430.52(C)(3)]","r":"NEC Table 430.52, 430.52(C)(3) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What is the tolerance zone for locating a buried facility in Florida, and what care is required within it?","o":["12 inches from the facility centerline; normal machine excavation is fine","48 inches from the outer edge; no digging of any kind is permitted","36 inches from the centerline; a backhoe may be used at low speed","24 inches from the facility's outer edge; hand dig or vacuum excavate"],"a":3,"e":"Member operators mark within 24 inches of the outer edge, and within the tolerance zone the excavator must use increased caution (hand digging, vacuum excavation) with mechanized equipment supervised. [F.S. 556.105]","r":"F.S. 556.105"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A location where ignitible concentrations of flammable gases or vapors can exist under normal operating conditions is classified as:","o":["Class I, Division 1","Class I, Division 2","Class II, Division 1","Class III, Division 1"],"a":0,"e":"Division 1 covers areas where hazard exists under normal operation, frequent repair or leakage, or equipment breakdown that also causes electrical failure. Division 2 is abnormal conditions only. [NEC 500.5(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 500.5(B)(1)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor signs a statutory progress payment lien waiver with a 'through date' of June 30. Which work does that waiver cover?","o":["All work on the project, including retainage and all work furnished after June 30","Work furnished through June 30, but not retainage or work after that date","Work furnished after June 30 as well as work before it, if it is the same project","Only the contractor's overhead and profit portion on the project"],"a":1,"e":"The 713.20(4) form waives lien rights only for work through the stated date and does not cover retainage or later work. [F.S. 713.20(4)]","r":"F.S. 713.20"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A megohmmeter (megger) is used to","o":["measure the line voltage under load","measure the power factor of a motor","measure the frequency of the supply","test insulation resistance"],"a":3,"e":"A megger applies a high DC voltage to test insulation resistance. [Meters]","r":"Meters"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A material requisition (or job issue ticket) is used to:","o":["Request a lien waiver from a supplier after payment is made","Record the hours worked by each employee on a given job","Charge materials taken from stock to the specific job that uses them","Pay a vendor invoice that has been matched with the PO"],"a":2,"e":"Requisitions tie inventory issues to job numbers so material costs are charged to the correct job. [Job costing]","r":"Job costing"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A solid freestanding sign is 20 ft wide and 8 ft tall. Use F = q x G x Cf x A with q = 30 psf, G = 0.85, and Cf = 1.55. What is the design wind force on the sign face?","o":["4,800 lb","4,080 lb","1,107 lb","6,324 lb"],"a":3,"e":"Area A = 20 x 8 = 160 sq ft. F = 30 x 0.85 x 1.55 x 160 = 6,324 lb. The wrong choices leave out the factors or add the sign dimensions instead of multiplying them. [ASCE 7 Ch. 29 sign wind force]","r":"ASCE 7 Ch. 29 sign wind force (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the hot resistance of a 1,500 W, 120 V resistive heater?","o":["12.5 ohms","19.2 ohms","0.080 ohms","9.6 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"R = E squared / P = 120 x 120 / 1,500 = 9.6 ohms. 12.5 is the current, not the resistance. [Power formula]","r":"Power formula"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 489.505(28), a \"fire alarm system agent\" is employed by:","o":["a licensed plumbing contractor working on sprinkler piping","a licensed fire alarm contractor or a certified unlimited electrical contractor","a municipal building department as an inspector","any registered specialty electrical contractor in the county"],"a":1,"e":"A fire alarm system agent works for a licensed fire alarm contractor or a certified unlimited electrical contractor and performs fire alarm work for compensation. [F.S. 489.505(28)]","r":"F.S. 489.505(28)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In the NEC, which Chapter 9 tables set the power source limitations for Class 2 and Class 3 circuits?","o":["Table 11(A) for ac sources and Table 11(B) for dc sources","Table 9 for ac sources and Table 10 for dc sources","Table 12(A) for ac sources and Table 12(B) for dc sources","Table 5 for ac sources and Table 8 for dc sources"],"a":0,"e":"Chapter 9 Tables 11(A) and 11(B) are the Class 2 and Class 3 source limitation tables. Tables 12(A) and 12(B) are for power-limited fire alarm sources. [NEC Chapter 9, Tables 11(A), 11(B)]","r":"NEC Ch. 9 Tables 11(A), 11(B)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign shop produces green, gold, and white tubing mostly by:","o":["Using argon and mercury with phosphors or colored glass","Changing the transformer frequency to 400 Hz for the green tubes","Painting the outside of the clear glass tubing with enamel","Raising the gas pressure far above atmospheric pressure"],"a":0,"e":"Phosphors and glass color shift the argon-mercury discharge to many colors; neon alone gives red-orange. [Neon sign gas colors]","r":"Neon sign gas colors"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"In an alteration where space limits prevent a 1:12 ramp, ADAAG 4.1.6(3)(a) allows a slope between 1:10 and 1:12 up to what maximum rise?","o":["12 in.","9 in.","6 in.","3 in."],"a":2,"e":"A slope between 1:10 and 1:12 is permitted for a maximum rise of 6 in. A slope between 1:8 and 1:10 is limited to 3 in. of rise, and anything steeper than 1:8 is not allowed. [ADAAG 4.1.6(3)(a)]","r":"ADAAG 4.1.6(3)(a)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Each shore power receptacle at a marina must have individual ground-fault protection of equipment set to trip at:","o":["30 mA or less","100 mA or less","5 mA or less","300 mA or less"],"a":0,"e":"Each shore power receptacle must have a 30 mA or less GFPE device; a feeder-level device alone is not enough. [NEC 555.35(B)]","r":"NEC 555.35(B)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What range of flash rate must visible notification appliances (strobes) meet under ADA/NFPA 72 guidance?","o":["0.1 Hz to 0.5 Hz","Not less than 1 Hz and not more than 2 Hz","3 Hz to 5 Hz","10 Hz to 15 Hz"],"a":1,"e":"The ADA Standards (702) and NFPA 72 limit strobe flash to 1-2 Hz to avoid seizure triggering and ensure visibility. [28 CFR Part 36 App. A / ADA Std. 702]","r":"ADA Std. 702 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum size copper equipment grounding conductor for a circuit protected by a 60-A overcurrent device (Table 250.122)?","o":["12 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.122 lists 10 AWG copper for OCPDs rated 60 A. [NEC 250.122, Table 250.122]","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If a contractor misses the permit or start-of-work deadlines in s. 489.126(2), F.S., how must the contractee make the written demand?","o":["By certified mail, return receipt requested, to the contract address","By text message sent to the contractor's cell phone number","By a recorded phone call witnessed by the county clerk","By posting the written demand on the job site door"],"a":0,"e":"Section 489.126(2)(b)1 requires a written demand by certified mail, return receipt requested, to the address in the contract. [F.S. 489.126(2)(b)1]","r":"F.S. 489.126(2)(b)1"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.126(3)(a), F.S., a contractor who holds money in excess of the value of work performed may not refuse or fail to perform work for what period, unless the contract states another?","o":["Any 30-day period","Any 60-day period","Any 90-day period","Any 180-day period"],"a":2,"e":"The statute bars failing to perform for any 90-day period (or a period the parties state) while holding excess funds. Failure without just cause or proper notice is prima facie evidence of holding excess money. [F.S. 489.126(3)]","r":"F.S. 489.126(3)(a)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A city estimates its own direct costs for an in-house project: employee pay and benefits $120,000, equipment $20,000, insurance $8,000, and direct materials $130,000. Under F.S. 255.20, what is the project's estimated cost after the statute's 20 percent factor for management, overhead and indirect costs?","o":["$278,000","$333,600","$283,560","$347,500"],"a":1,"e":"Direct costs = 120,000 + 20,000 + 8,000 + 130,000 = 278,000; x 1.20 = $333,600. The statute adds a 20% factor, not a divisor. [F.S. 255.20(1)]","r":"F.S. 255.20(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.129(7), F.S., the board may not issue or renew a certificate or registration for a person who owes:","o":["A personal income tax debt owed to the state of Florida","A traffic citation issued in any county of the state","A fine, costs, or restitution that is unpaid under a final order","A local business tax of any amount owed to a city"],"a":2,"e":"No certificate may be issued or renewed while fines, interest, costs or restitution from a final order remain unpaid, until paid in full or the order's terms are satisfied. [F.S. 489.129(7)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(7)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Who may temporarily defeat an electrical safety interlock, and under what condition?","o":["Any employee, as long as the equipment is labeled with a warning sign","Only the owner, and never at any time during maintenance on the equipment","A qualified person following paragraph (c), and the interlock must be restored afterward","Any employee, if the interlock is bypassed with a jumper for the full shift"],"a":2,"e":"Only a qualified person following 1910.333(c) may defeat an interlock temporarily, and it must be returned to service immediately after the work. [29 CFR 1910.333(c)(10)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(10)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Where may a Type 2 SPD be connected in a building supplied by a service?","o":["Only at the receptacle","Only on the line side of the service disconnect","Anywhere on the load side of the service disconnect overcurrent device","Only between the transformer and the meter"],"a":2,"e":"Type 2 SPDs are connected anywhere on the load side of the service disconnect OCPD. [NEC 242.14]","r":"NEC 242.14"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A cost-plus (fixed fee) real property contract is treated under Rule 12A-1.051 in which way regarding sales tax on materials?","o":["The contractor collects tax on the cost of materials from the owner","The owner must pay use tax directly","The materials are exempt","The contractor is the consumer and pays tax to suppliers"],"a":3,"e":"Lump sum, cost-plus or fixed fee, guaranteed price, and time and materials contracts all treat the contractor as the consumer of the materials. [Rule 12A-1.051(3)(a)-(c), (e), F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(3)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"How many officers of a Florida construction-industry corporation may be exempt from workers' compensation coverage by certificate of election to be exempt?","o":["Unlimited","Only one","As many as the corporation has","No more than three"],"a":3,"e":"Construction industry corporations may exempt no more than three officers through a Certificate of Election to be Exempt. [F.S. 440.02 and 440.05]","r":"F.S. 440.02, 440.05"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Florida's Drug-Free Workplace program, a post-injury positive test for drugs that were not prescribed generally creates:","o":["No effect on benefits, because intoxication is never a defense in Florida","An absolute bar to all benefits, with no way for the employee to rebut it","A presumption that the injury was occasioned primarily by drug use, which the employee may rebut","A requirement that the employer pay double benefits to the employee"],"a":2,"e":"Section 440.09(7)(b) presumes that an injury was caused by intoxication or drug use when the employee had a positive confirmed test; the employee may overcome it by evidence. [F.S. 440.09(7)(b)]","r":"F.S. 440.09(7)(b)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Two resistive heaters (3,000 W and 2,000 W) are connected in parallel on 240 V. What current does the smaller heater draw?","o":["8.33 A","16.67 A","20.83 A","12.50 A"],"a":0,"e":"I = P / E = 2000 / 240 = 8.33 A. Total circuit current would be 20.83 A. [Parallel circuits]","r":"Parallel circuits"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A Part I contractor places the license on inactive status and later asks to return to active status. Under Rule 61G4-18.001, F.A.C., what proof of continuing education is required for activation?","o":["None, because inactive licensees owe no continuing education","7 hours for the biennium before the requested activation","28 hours covering the two biennia before activation","14 hours for the biennium before the requested activation"],"a":3,"e":"No CE is required while inactive, but returning to active status requires proof of 14 hours for the biennium before the requested activation. [F.A.C. 61G4-18.001(11)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-18.001(11)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A pull box has a straight pull from a 3-in. conduit to a 3-in. conduit with 250 kcmil conductors. What is the minimum length of the box?","o":["18 in.","12 in.","24 in.","30 in."],"a":2,"e":"Straight pull: 8 x 3 in. = 24 in. [NEC 314.28(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 314.28(A)(1)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A Florida employer is found to have failed to secure coverage for a period during which the premium at approved manual rates would have been $18,400.00. What is the base penalty?","o":["$1,000.00","$18,400.00","$27,600.00","$36,800.00"],"a":3,"e":"The base penalty is 2 x $18,400.00 = $36,800.00, which is greater than the $1,000 minimum. [F.S. 440.107]","r":"F.S. 440.107"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"At month end, materials were received and counted, but the vendor's invoice has not arrived. Proper accounting records the receipt:","o":["As an expense of the following month, when the invoice is dated","As sales tax payable to the Florida Department of Revenue","As inventory or job cost with an accrued liability for the amount owed","Not at all, until the vendor's invoice has been received and paid"],"a":2,"e":"Goods received not invoiced (GRNI) should be accrued so costs and liabilities appear in the period of receipt. [Accrual accounting]","r":"Cutoff and accruals"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A private-sector Florida contractor offers an hourly electrician time off in a later week instead of paying cash overtime. Under the FLSA, this practice is:","o":["Permitted if time off is given at 1.5 hours per overtime hour within 90 days","Permitted whenever the employee agrees in writing","Not permitted for private employers except in limited cases under a recognized exception","Permitted only for employees earning under $20 per hour"],"a":2,"e":"Compensatory time off in place of overtime pay is generally reserved for public agencies. Private employers must pay overtime in the pay period in which it is earned. [FLSA Sec. 7(o)]","r":"FLSA Sec. 7(o)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"How does F.S. 489.505 define \"alarm system\"?","o":["Only burglar alarms installed in single-family residences","Only devices that detect fire and notify the fire department","Any electrical or signaling device, or combination, used to signal or detect a burglary, fire, robbery, or medical emergency","Any device that is supplied by a Class 2 power source"],"a":2,"e":"The definition includes fire alarms as well as burglar, robbery, and medical emergency devices. It is not limited to residences. [F.S. 489.505(1)]","r":"F.S. 489.505(1)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A space has a prevailing equivalent sound level of 50 dBA, and a maximum sound level of 72 dBA that lasts at least 60 seconds. Under ADAAG 4.28.2, the alarm must exceed which minimum level?","o":["77 dBA","87 dBA","72 dBA","65 dBA"],"a":0,"e":"The alarm must exceed the prevailing level by 15 dBA (50 + 15 = 65) or any maximum level lasting 60 seconds by 5 dBA (72 + 5 = 77), whichever is louder. The louder result, 77 dBA, governs. [ADAAG 4.28.2]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.2"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which is the due date for the Form 941 for the second quarter (April through June)?","o":["June 30","July 31","August 15","September 30"],"a":1,"e":"Quarterly Forms 941 are due April 30, July 31, October 31, and January 31 for the preceding quarters. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which lienor is exempt from the requirement to serve a notice to owner under F.S. 713.06?","o":["A sub-subcontractor","A materialman supplying a subcontractor","An engineer retained by the owner's tenant","A laborer"],"a":3,"e":"Laborers are not required to serve a notice to owner; materialmen and subs not in privity with the owner must. [F.S. 713.06(2)]","r":"F.S. 713.06"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An 18-unit apartment building qualifies for the optional multifamily method. Table 220.84 gives 38% for 18 units. If the connected load per unit is 30,000 VA, what is the demand load of the 18 units?","o":["270,000 VA","114,000 VA","205,200 VA","540,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Connected = 18 x 30,000 = 540,000 VA; at 38% the demand is 205,200 VA. [NEC 220.84]","r":"NEC 220.84"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Annual revenue of $1,200,000 and average receivables of $150,000 give a receivable turnover of","o":["46 times per year","96 times per year","8 times per year","4 times per year"],"a":2,"e":"Turnover = revenue / average AR = 1,200,000 / 150,000 = 8. Dividing 365 by that gives DSO (45.6 days), not turns. [Collections metrics]","r":"Accounts receivable management (SBA/IRS Pub 334 context)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 489.115(7), the financial stability guideline the board adopts for Division I applicants (cash, net worth or bonding) may not exceed","o":["$5,000","$50,000","$20,000","$100,000"],"a":2,"e":"The cap is $20,000 for Division I and $10,000 for Division II; half may be met by a 14-hour financial responsibility course. [F.S. 489.115(7)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(7)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Three employees earned $5,000, $12,000 and $7,000 during the year. With the full credit, what is the contractor's net FUTA liability?","o":["$174.00","$144.00","$42.00","$114.00"],"a":3,"e":"Taxable wages are $5,000 + $7,000 + $7,000 = $19,000; x 0.6% = $114.00. Using total wages of $24,000 would give $144.00, which ignores the $7,000 cap. [Form 940 instructions]","r":"Form 940 instructions"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"At a motor fuel dispensing facility, where must the emergency electrical shutoff device for dispensers be located?","o":["Inside each dispenser enclosure","Within 3 ft of each dispenser","At the utility meter only","Remote from dispensers, 20 to 100 ft away, accessible"],"a":3,"e":"Emergency shutoffs must be at a location remote from the dispensing devices and not less than 20 ft nor more than 100 ft from the devices they serve. [NEC 514.11(A)]","r":"NEC 514.11(A) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 489.505, which activity falls within \"monitoring\"?","o":["Installing sensors and wiring for a burglar alarm system","Annual testing of fire alarm initiating devices","Receiving signals from alarms, cameras, or access-control systems in order to trigger a response","Programming a keypad at the customer's premises"],"a":2,"e":"Monitoring is defined by receipt of signals from alarms, cameras, access control, or similar systems to trigger a response, not by installing or testing the equipment. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505(27)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 75 kVA, three-phase transformer has a 480-volt primary and 208-volt secondary. It is protected only on the primary side. What is the maximum standard-size primary overcurrent device?","o":["110 A","125 A","100 A","150 A"],"a":1,"e":"Primary current = 75,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 90.2 A. At 125% = 112.8 A, and with primary-only protection the next higher standard size, 125 A, is permitted. 110 A is not a standard size. [NEC 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B) Note 1]","r":"NEC 450.3(B), Table 450.3(B) Note 1"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Using the same task, what is the labor rate variance (actual hours times the rate difference)?","o":["$920 unfavorable","$2,880 unfavorable","$800 unfavorable","$920 favorable"],"a":0,"e":"(50 - 48) x 460 actual hours = 920. It is unfavorable because the actual rate exceeded the budget rate. [Variance analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What zone around a bathtub requires luminaires to be marked suitable for damp or wet locations (unless they are otherwise protected)?","o":["5 ft horizontally and 12 ft vertically","1 ft horizontally and 6 ft vertically","6 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically","3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically from the tub rim"],"a":3,"e":"The zone is 3 ft horizontally and 8 ft vertically measured from the top of the bathtub rim or shower stall threshold. [NEC 410.10(D)]","r":"NEC 410.10(D)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Notes to the financial statements are important to a surety because they","o":["replace the need for a balance sheet entirely","disclose policies, contingencies, and debt terms","are optional marketing material for customers","reduce the company's income tax liability"],"a":1,"e":"Footnotes disclose revenue recognition policies, long-term commitments, litigation, and related-party transactions; FDOT guidance requires complete footnote disclosures. [FDOT PreQual Financial Statements]","r":"Financial statement notes"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.119(7)(a), F.S., a business may avoid applying for qualification only if it, among other requirements, maintains a minimum net worth of:","o":["$20 million, held by it or its wholly owning parent","$75,000, held by it or its wholly owning parent","$200,000, held by it or its wholly owning parent","$2 million, held by it or its wholly owning parent"],"a":0,"e":"The exemption requires employing licensed contractors who pull permits and supervise all contracting, contracting only on property it or its affiliates own, and a net worth of at least $20 million. [F.S. 489.119(7)(a)]","r":"F.S. 489.119(7)(a)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A cost-plus contract has a not-to-exceed (NTE) amount of $240,000 for costs, with a 10% fee on cost. Actual documented costs reach $248,000 with no approved change to the NTE. What is the most the owner must pay (cost plus fee)?","o":["$272,800","$264,000","$272,000","$240,000"],"a":1,"e":"The NTE caps reimbursable costs at 240,000; fee on that = 24,000; total $264,000. The excess 8,000 is the contractor's cost. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A toeboard used for falling object protection must be at least how high and withstand how much force?","o":["2 inches high, withstanding 25 pounds in any downward or outward direction","3.5 inches high, withstanding 50 pounds in any downward or outward direction","4 inches high, withstanding 75 pounds in any downward or outward direction","6 inches high, withstanding 100 pounds in any downward or outward direction"],"a":1,"e":"Toeboards must be at least 3-1/2 inches in vertical height from the top edge to the walking level and withstand a 50-pound force. [29 CFR 1926.502(j)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.502(j)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The nameplate on a condensing unit shows Minimum Circuit Ampacity 24.8 A and Maximum Overcurrent Protective Device 40 A. Which installation is correct?","o":["Conductors rated 40 A with a 24.8 A breaker","Conductors rated at least 24.8 A with a 40 A or smaller breaker","Conductors rated at least 40 A with a 50 A breaker","Conductors rated 24.8 A with a 60 A breaker"],"a":1,"e":"Conductors are sized from the marked minimum circuit ampacity and the protective device must not exceed the marked maximum. A larger breaker than the nameplate maximum is a violation. [NEC 440.35, 440.22, 110.3(B)]","r":"NEC 440.35, 440.22, 110.3(B)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A feeder supplies a 25 hp motor (Table 430.250 FLC 34 A) protected by a 90 A inverse-time breaker, plus a 10 hp motor (14 A) and a 5 hp motor (7.6 A). What is the maximum standard-size feeder overcurrent device?","o":["110 A","125 A","90 A","100 A"],"a":0,"e":"Feeder protection is the largest branch-circuit device (90 A) plus the other motors' FLC (14 + 7.6 = 21.6 A) = 111.6 A. The result is rounded down to the next lower standard size, 110 A; 125 A would exceed the limit. [NEC 430.62(A), 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 430.62(A), 240.6(A)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The minimum spacing between neon secondary conductors over 1000 V (not in metal conduit) and between them and other objects is:","o":["1/2 in.","3/4 in.","1-1/2 in. (38 mm)","3 in."],"a":2,"e":"600.32: secondary conductors must be separated from each other and from all objects other than insulators or neon tubing by at least 38 mm (1-1/2 in.). GTO cable in metal conduit does not need spacing from the conduit. [NEC 600.32]","r":"NEC 600.32"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under which condition is a second service permitted for a building?","o":["Whenever the owner prefers separate meters","Whenever the building is larger than 10,000 sq ft","Only if the first service is underground","Where the capacity requirements exceed 2000 A at a supply voltage of 1000 V or less"],"a":3,"e":"230.2 permits additional services where capacity requirements exceed 2000 A at 1000 V or less, for different characteristics, by special permission and in other listed cases. [NEC 230.2(C)]","r":"NEC 230.2(C)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The center of the grip of the operating handle of a circuit breaker or fused switch, in its highest position, may not be more than what height above the floor?","o":["6 ft 0 in.","5 ft 0 in.","7 ft 6 in.","6 ft 7 in."],"a":3,"e":"Readily accessible circuit breakers and switches must have the grip of the operating handle no higher than 2.0 m (6 ft 7 in.). [NEC 240.24(A)]","r":"NEC 240.24(A)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Overhead of $216,000 and annual revenue of $1,800,000 give an overhead-to-revenue ratio of","o":["833%","10.7%","12%","88%"],"a":2,"e":"216,000 / 1,800,000 = 12% of revenue. This ratio is what the markup must recover before any profit is earned. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A supplier's last delivery on a job was February 10, 2026. What is the latest date to record the claim of lien under the 90-day rule?","o":["April 11, 2026","March 27, 2026","May 11, 2026","June 10, 2026"],"a":2,"e":"February 10, 2026 + 90 days = May 11, 2026. [F.S. 713.08(5)]","r":"F.S. 713.08(5)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G4-15.006, F.A.C., the 'financial responsibility' ground for denial focuses on a current consumer credit report showing","o":["no more than one employer during the last five years of work","no unsatisfied judgments or liens against the applicant or related entities","no outstanding vehicle loans or equipment leases on the books","a minimum bank balance of $20,000 on the date of the report"],"a":1,"e":"The board may refuse to qualify an applicant whose credit report does not show the absence of unsatisfied judgments or liens, including against entities previously qualified. [Rule 61G4-15.006]","r":"Rule 61G4-15.006, F.A.C."},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If an employee's injury is caused primarily by intoxication or illegal drug use, Chapter 440 generally provides that compensation is:","o":["Payable in all cases; only medical care is denied","Payable if the employee enters rehab","Payable at half the normal rate","Not payable, with the employer having the burden to prove it"],"a":3,"e":"Section 440.09(3) bars compensation when the injury was occasioned primarily by intoxication or by the influence of drugs not prescribed by a physician; the employer must prove it. [F.S. 440.09(3)]","r":"F.S. 440.09(3)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The lighting maintenance specialty electrical contractor under Rule 61G6-7.001, F.A.C., is NOT allowed to:","o":["Provide or work beyond the last supplying source, outlet, or disconnecting means","Replace lighting fixtures located in parking lots and similar areas","Install lighting fixtures in or on buildings, signs, and billboards","Repair or alter lighting fixtures along roadways and streets"],"a":0,"e":"The category covers installing, repairing, altering or replacing fixtures in or on buildings, signs, billboards, roadways, streets and parking lots, but excludes anything beyond the last supplying source, outlet or disconnecting means. [F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(1)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(1)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"NEC 300.21 requires openings around electrical penetrations through fire-resistance-rated walls, floors, or ceilings to be:","o":["firestopped using approved methods that maintain the fire resistance rating","covered by a metal plate fastened on one side of the wall","packed with loose mineral wool only, without a listing","left open if the cable is limited-energy wiring"],"a":0,"e":"300.21 requires electrical circuits and equipment to be installed so spread of fire and products of combustion is not substantially increased, and penetrations to be firestopped. Wool is not a listed system by itself. [NEC 300.21]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 300.21 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A luminaire schedule lists Type A: 40 W LED troffer, Type B: 30 W LED strip, Type C: 55 W LED wall pack. The plan counts 24 Type A, 10 Type B, and 6 Type C. What is the total connected lighting load in watts?","o":["5,000 W","1,260 W","1,590 W","1,600 W"],"a":2,"e":"Multiply each count by its wattage: 24 x 40 = 960, 10 x 30 = 300, 6 x 55 = 330; the total is 1,590 W. Using one wattage for every fixture or leaving out a type gives the other results. [Quantity takeoff; luminaire schedule]","r":"Quantity takeoff; luminaire schedule"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C., a CPA's adverse opinion or disclaimer of opinion on an applicant's statements results in","o":["disapproval of the application","a requirement to add a compilation only","no effect if the balance sheet balances","automatic approval at half capacity"],"a":0,"e":"The rule says an adverse opinion or disclaimer of opinion results in disapproval, while a qualified opinion may lead to adjustments. [Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C."},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which statement about enclosures integral to a sign used as pull or junction boxes is correct?","o":["They may be used as pull boxes for any conductors in the building at any voltage","Listed, labeled integral sign enclosures up to 600 V, for adjacent signs or sign floodlights","They may be used only for high-voltage neon conductors above 1000 V","They may be used only for Class 2 wiring in the sign body"],"a":1,"e":"600.5 permits listed and labeled sign enclosures up to 600 V as pull or junction boxes for the specified conductors, and they may contain both branch and secondary circuit conductors. [NEC 600.5]","r":"NEC 600.5"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Four electricians each earn $28 per hour and each worked 52 hours in one workweek. Overtime is paid at 1.5 times the regular rate for hours over 40. What is the total gross wages for the crew?","o":["$8,736","$6,496","$5,824","$7,168"],"a":1,"e":"Each: 40 x 28 = 1120; 12 x 42 = 504; total 1624. Four people = $6,496. Paying all hours at 1.5 gives $8,736. [Estimating practice; FLSA overtime]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The submittal review states 'Reviewed for general conformance with design intent only; contractor remains responsible for dimensions and quantities.' The switchboard submittal shows the board is 8 in. deeper than the electrical room allows with required working space. Who must resolve the conflict?","o":["The architect, because the approval stamp confirmed that the dimensions fit the space","The inspector, who will approve the clearances at final","The contractor, who is responsible for coordinating dimensions and fit in the space","The manufacturer, because it made the equipment and certified the overall dimensions"],"a":2,"e":"Design-team review is typically for general conformance and does not relieve the contractor of responsibility for dimensions and coordination. NEC 110.26 working space must be maintained. [AIA A201 3.12; NEC 110.26]","r":"AIA A201 3.12; NEC 110.26"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum secondary-circuit current rating of a neon transformer or electronic power supply?","o":["30 milliamperes","60 milliamperes","1 ampere","300 milliamperes"],"a":3,"e":"600.23(D): the secondary circuit current rating may not exceed 300 mA. Typical neon units are 15, 30, or 60 mA. [NEC 600.23(D)]","r":"NEC 600.23(D)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"By what date must an employer furnish Form W-2 to employees for wages paid in the prior calendar year?","o":["March 15","April 15","January 31","February 28"],"a":2,"e":"Copies of Form W-2 must be given to employees (and filed with the SSA) by January 31 of the following year. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); IRS General Instructions for Forms W-2 and W-3]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A job's budgeted labor cost was $45,000 and the actual cost was $49,500. The variance is","o":["$4,500 favorable (10% under budget)","$49,500 unfavorable","$45,000 favorable","$4,500 unfavorable (10% over budget)"],"a":3,"e":"Actual - budget = 49,500 - 45,000 = 4,500 over; 10% of budget. Higher-than-budget costs are unfavorable. [Variance analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What is the purpose of IRS Form W-4?","o":["It reports the employee's annual wages to the Social Security Administration","It requests the contractor's taxpayer identification number for payments","It tells the employer how much federal income tax to withhold from the employee's pay","It reports nonemployee compensation to the IRS"],"a":2,"e":"Employees complete Form W-4 so the employer can figure federal income tax withholding. Form W-2 reports wages, and Form W-9 collects a payee's TIN. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"NEC 760.25 requires what to be done with abandoned fire alarm cables?","o":["They may be left if tagged","They must be capped and left in place","They must be energized at 12 V","They must be removed"],"a":3,"e":"Abandoned fire alarm cable must be removed unless contained within a raceway. Left cable adds fuel load and makes mapping circuits harder. [NEC 760.25]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 760.25"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A firm with $150,000 average receivables and $1,200,000 annual revenue has a DSO (365-day year) of about","o":["22.5 days","8.0 days","12.5 days","45.6 days"],"a":3,"e":"150,000 / 1,200,000 x 365 = 45.6 days. The figure 8 is the receivable turnover (times per year), not days. [Collections metrics]","r":"Accounts receivable management (SBA/IRS Pub 334 context)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's cost is $156,000. To earn a 22% gross profit margin on the selling price, what price must be bid?","o":["$190,320","$200,000","$127,868.85","$34,320"],"a":1,"e":"Price = cost / (1 - margin) = 156,000 / 0.78 = $200,000. Multiplying cost by 1.22 produces only a 18.0% margin. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier may require a personal guarantee from a business owner for a new credit account. This means the owner:","o":["Cannot be sued by the supplier under any circumstances","Is protected from personal liability if the business fails to pay the account","Becomes a co-owner of the supplier's business through the guarantee","Becomes personally liable for the account if the business does not pay"],"a":3,"e":"A personal guarantee makes the individual liable for the debt in addition to the business. [Supplier credit practice]","r":"Supplier credit"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"For the same circuit (a 28 A continuous load plus an 8 A noncontinuous load, standard 100%-not-rated OCPD), what is the minimum standard OCPD rating that may be selected?","o":["40 A","50 A","45 A","35 A"],"a":2,"e":"210.20(A) requires an OCPD of at least the noncontinuous load plus 125% of the continuous load = 43 A. Because that is not a standard size, 240.4(B) permits the next higher standard rating of 45 A (a 40 A device would be below the required value). [NEC 210.20(A)]","r":"NEC 210.20(A)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Legally required standby systems must make power available within:","o":["10 seconds","60 seconds","5 minutes","30 minutes"],"a":1,"e":"Legally required standby power must be available within 60 seconds. [NEC 701.12]","r":"NEC 701.12"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Fixed costs are $288,000 per year and variable costs are 62% of revenue. What annual revenue is needed to break even?","o":["$464,516","$109,440","$466,560","$757,895"],"a":3,"e":"Contribution margin ratio = 1 - 0.62 = 0.38; break-even sales = 288,000 / 0.38 = 757,895. [Break-even analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's revenue is $3,150,000 and gross profit is $693,000. What is the gross profit margin?","o":["78%","22%","28.2%","12%"],"a":1,"e":"Gross margin = gross profit / revenue = $693,000 / $3,150,000 = 22.0%, i.e. 22%. Dividing by cost of revenue instead gives 28.2%. [Profit margin, UNL Extension G2009]","r":"Profit margin"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Underwriters often say a surety evaluates the contractor on the \"three Cs\". These are","o":["cost, completion and credit","contract, claims and compliance","character, capacity and capital","cash, collateral and calendar"],"a":2,"e":"The SBA says a business must meet the surety's credit, capacity and character requirements; capital and credit are the financial side of the review. [SBA Surety Bonds]","r":"SBA Surety Bonds"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Dry-type transformers installed indoors must have what kind of case or enclosure?","o":["Any enclosure listed for outdoor use only","Noncombustible and moisture-resistant, guarding live parts","A sealed oil-filled steel tank","A wire guard with an open top"],"a":1,"e":"Dry-type transformers must have a noncombustible, moisture-resistant case or enclosure that gives protection from accidental contact. [NEC 450.8(B)]","r":"NEC 450.8(B)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Vertical runs of metal wireway must be securely supported at intervals not exceeding:","o":["15 ft","20 ft","5 ft","10 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Vertical wireway runs are supported at intervals not exceeding 15 ft with no more than one joint between supports. [NEC 376.30(B)]","r":"NEC 376.30(B)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The same supplier's last delivery is June 21, 2027. Under F.S. 255.05, what is the last day to serve the sworn notice of nonpayment?","o":["September 19, 2027","August 5, 2027","August 20, 2027","October 19, 2027"],"a":0,"e":"June 21, 2027 plus 90 days = September 19, 2027. The notice also cannot be served sooner than 45 days after first furnishing. [F.S. 255.05(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(2)(a)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A properly recorded payment bond under s. 713.23, F.S. gives the owner what benefit?","o":["The owner is exempt from liens under the direct contract; lienors look to the bond","The owner may withhold final payment from the contractor indefinitely","The owner is not required to record a notice of commencement","The owner becomes liable to pay lienors twice for the same work"],"a":0,"e":"A payment bond furnished before construction and attached to the recorded notice of commencement exempts the owner from liens by lienors under the direct contract, and the lienors' claims transfer to the bond. [F.S. 713.23]","r":"F.S. 713.23"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.006(3), F.A.C., a qualifying agent applying to take the exam as secondary qualifier for an already certified business:","o":["Does not require the business to resubmit its financial portion","Must resubmit the business's tax returns for the last year","May not serve as a secondary qualifier for a certified business","Must post a new $50,000 bond for the added qualifier"],"a":0,"e":"A secondary qualifier applying for a business already certified by a certified qualifying agent does not need that business to resubmit the financial portion. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.006(3)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.006(3)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Why is depreciation added back to net income in the operating section of an indirect-method cash flow statement?","o":["It is a financing activity that must be repaid","It represents an increase in accounts receivable","It reduced net income but did not use cash in the period","It is a cash payment made to equipment vendors this year"],"a":2,"e":"Depreciation is a non-cash expense; adding it back converts accrual net income toward cash flow. [Statement of cash flows]","r":"Statement of cash flows"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"When comparing three subcontractor quotes, the estimator discovers the lowest quote excludes the fire alarm scope that the other two include. The best step is to","o":["accept the lowest quote because it is the cheapest price","average the three quotes and use the result","discard the two higher quotes without any review","level the quotes by adding the missing scope before comparing prices"],"a":3,"e":"Bid leveling puts all quotes on an equal-scope basis so the real low price is identified. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier offers a 5% rebate on annual purchases over $60,000, calculated on the amount over $60,000 only. If the contractor buys $92,000, what rebate is earned?","o":["$3,000.00","$1,600.00","$16,000.00","$4,600.00"],"a":1,"e":"Rebate = ($92,000 - $60,000) x 5% = $32,000 x 0.05 = $1,600.00. A rebate on the full amount would be $4,600.00. [Purchasing math]","r":"Volume rebate"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which part of the sign installation is covered by the NEC, as opposed to the structural code?","o":["The pole footing and the anchor bolts that are cast into the concrete","The electrical conductors, equipment, disconnects, grounding, and power supplies","The wind-load design and engineering of the sign structure","The graphics, lettering, and color of the sign face"],"a":1,"e":"The NEC covers electrical installations. The structural support, foundation, and wind loads are covered by the building code and engineering design. [NEC 90.2; FBC Building ch. 16]","r":"NEC 90.2; FBC Building ch. 16"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A $12,000 employment tax deposit was made 9 calendar days after the due date. What failure-to-deposit penalty applies?","o":["$240.00","$600.00","$1,200.00","$1,800.00"],"a":1,"e":"Deposits 6 to 15 days late are penalized 5%: $12,000 x 5% = $600.00. 2% applies to 1-5 days, 10% to more than 15 days, and 15% to amounts still unpaid more than 10 days after a first notice demanding payment. [IRS Failure to Deposit Penalty]","r":"IRS Failure to Deposit Penalty"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"What must be true to qualify as a 'retail sale plus installation' contract under Rule 12A-1.051(3)(d)?","o":["The invoice lists materials after the job is complete","All materials incorporated are itemized and priced in the contract before work begins","The contractor gives a lump sum with a materials estimate","Materials are described by general category only"],"a":1,"e":"The rule requires the contract to itemize and price all materials incorporated into the work before the work begins; partial itemization disqualifies it. [Rule 12A-1.051(3)(d), F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(3)(d)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 633.202(19), areas of refuge are provided when:","o":["the local fire marshal prefers to have them in the building","a building has more than three stories above grade","an elevator in the building is out of service","the Florida Building Code's accessibility provisions require them"],"a":3,"e":"Areas of refuge are provided where the Florida Building Code accessibility chapter requires them, and must be reachable by an accessible means of egress. [F.S. 633.202(19)]","r":"F.S. 633.202(19)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Four multiconductor power cables, each 2.0 in. in outside diameter and each 4/0 AWG or larger, are to be placed in a single layer in a ladder-type cable tray. The tray widths available are 6, 9, 12 and 18 in. What is the smallest tray that can be used?","o":["9 in.","12 in.","18 in.","6 in."],"a":0,"e":"For cables all 4/0 AWG or larger, the sum of the cable diameters may not exceed the tray width and the cables must be in a single layer. 4 x 2.0 = 8.0 in., so a 9 in. tray is the smallest. [NEC 392.22(A)(1)(a)]","r":"NEC 392.22(A)(1)(a)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor orders switchgear online from an out-of-state seller who charges no Florida tax and installs it on a lump sum job. What is the contractor's obligation?","o":["No tax is due because the seller was out of state","The owner owes the use tax","Only the surtax is due","Accrue and pay Florida use tax on the purchase"],"a":3,"e":"Materials delivered into Florida for use are taxable; when the seller does not collect, the contractor owes use tax, generally at the combined state and county surtax rate. [F.S. 212.05; Rule 12A-1.091, F.A.C.]","r":"F.S. 212.05; Rule 12A-1.091"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"After a box is set in a plaster wall, gaps around the box edge must be repaired so there are no gaps or open spaces greater than:","o":["1/8 in.","1/2 in.","1/16 in.","1/4 in."],"a":0,"e":"Noncombustible surfaces that are broken or incomplete around boxes must be repaired so no gaps greater than 1/8 in. remain at the edge of the box. [NEC 314.21]","r":"NEC 314.21"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Materials issued to a job during the month total $12,430, and $1,280 of unused materials were returned to stock. What is the net material cost charged to the job?","o":["$13,710","$11,150","$12,430","$1,280"],"a":1,"e":"Net material cost = $12,430 - $1,280 = $11,150. Returns are credited to the job at the cost at which they were issued. [Job costing]","r":"Job costing"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In general, an employee's exclusive remedy against a covered employer for a work-related injury is:","o":["OSHA damages","A civil suit for pain and suffering","A claim under the FLSA","Workers' compensation benefits"],"a":3,"e":"Section 440.11 makes workers' compensation liability exclusive and in place of all other employer liability, absent narrow intentional-tort exceptions. [F.S. 440.11]","r":"F.S. 440.11"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a project that uses a standard discipline-designator convention in sheet numbers, the sheet number 'E-201' most likely belongs to which discipline?","o":["Elevations","Engineering (civil)","Electrical","Equipment"],"a":2,"e":"In the U.S. National CAD Standard sheet-identification scheme, the discipline letter E designates Electrical (T is Telecommunications, F is Fire Protection, and Q is Equipment). The first digit commonly indicates the sheet type, such as 2 for plans. [U.S. National CAD Standard sheet identification]","r":"U.S. National CAD Standard sheet identification"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum length of the cord for a cord-and-plug-connected pool pump motor?","o":["6 ft","25 ft","3 ft","10 ft"],"a":2,"e":"A flexible cord on cord-and-plug-connected permanently installed pool pump equipment may not exceed 3 ft (900 mm). Longer cords (10 ft, 6 ft, 25 ft) are not permitted for this use. [NEC 680.7(A)]","r":"NEC 680.7(A)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A base bid is $925,000. Alternate 1 adds $38,400, Alternate 2 deducts $17,250, and Alternate 3 adds $9,800. The owner accepts Alternates 1 and 2 only. What is the contract amount?","o":["$946,150","$955,950","$980,650","$963,400"],"a":0,"e":"925,000 + 38,400 - 17,250 = $946,150. Alternate 3 was not accepted. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 150-A noncontinuous load is supplied by THHN copper conductors terminating on equipment with unmarked terminals. What is the smallest copper conductor size?","o":["2 AWG","1 AWG","1/0 AWG","2/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"For circuits over 100 A, unmarked terminals are taken as 75°C. Copper 1/0 AWG is 150 A in the 75°C column; 1 AWG is only 130 A. [NEC 110.14(C)(1)(b), Table 310.16]","r":"NEC 110.14(C)(1)(b), Table 310.16"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of these is normally part of the contract documents for a construction project once the agreement is signed?","o":["The contractor's personal income tax return filings","The building inspector's handwritten field notes","The lender's internal credit approval memorandum","Addenda issued before the bid and any signed change orders"],"a":3,"e":"The contract documents commonly include the agreement, general and special conditions, drawings, specifications, addenda and modifications such as change orders. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Conductors for lighting circuits are to be supported above a suspended ceiling. Which is correct?","o":["They may be tied to the ceiling grid wires if bundled with plastic ties","Support wires of any color may be used","They may rest on ceiling tiles","They may not be secured to the ceiling support wires; independent support must be provided, and independent support wires must be distinguishable from the ceiling system's wires"],"a":3,"e":"Wiring may not be secured to or supported by the ceiling grid or its support wires; independent support wires are permitted if they are distinguishable by color, tagging or other means, are in addition to ceiling wires and are secured at both ends. [NEC 300.11(B)]","r":"NEC 300.11(B)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's last day of furnishing labor was March 12, 2026. What is the last day to record the claim of lien under s. 713.08, F.S.?","o":["April 11, 2026","June 10, 2026","April 26, 2026","September 8, 2026"],"a":1,"e":"Ninety days after March 12, 2026 is June 10, 2026. April 26 is 45 days, the notice-to-owner period, not the recording period. [F.S. 713.08(5)]","r":"F.S. 713.08(5)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 60-ft straight horizontal run of EMT is installed between two boxes. NEC 358.30 requires support within 3 ft of each box and at intervals not exceeding 10 ft. What is the minimum number of straps for the run?","o":["6 straps","8 straps","9 straps","7 straps"],"a":3,"e":"Place one strap 3 ft from each box. That leaves 54 ft between them, which needs ceil(54/10) = 6 intervals, so 6 + 1 = 7 straps. Counting 60/10 = 6 ignores the 3-ft rule. [NEC 358.30(A)]","r":"NEC 358.30(A)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which MasterFormat section number is conventionally used for Fire Detection and Alarm?","o":["28 46 00","26 05 53","27 10 00","26 36 00"],"a":0,"e":"28 46 00 is Fire Detection and Alarm. 26 05 53 is Identification for Electrical Systems, 27 10 00 is Structured Cabling, and 26 36 00 is Transfer Switches. [CSI MasterFormat 28 46 00]","r":"CSI MasterFormat 28 46 00"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a one- or two-family dwelling, the communications bonding conductor or grounding electrode conductor should be as short as practicable and not exceed:","o":["3 m (10 ft)","6.0 m (20 ft)","15 m (50 ft)","30 m (100 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"Dwellings are limited to 20 ft; if that is impracticable, a separate ground rod bonded to the power electrode system is used. [NEC 800.100(A)(4)]","r":"NEC 2023 800.100(A)(4)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A taxpayer values an item at lower of cost or market. The item cost $48 and its current replacement cost is $41. The inventory value is:","o":["$7","$41","$48","$44.50"],"a":1,"e":"Under the lower-of-cost-or-market method, the item is valued at the lower figure: $41. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538 (lower of cost or market)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Before filing a lawsuit under Florida's minimum wage law, an employee must first give the employer written notice identifying the minimum wage to which the employee claims entitlement and the actual or estimated work dates. How long must the employer be given to pay or object?","o":["30 calendar days","5 business days","15 calendar days","60 calendar days"],"a":2,"e":"F.S. 448.110(6)(a) requires written notice and gives the employer 15 calendar days to pay the amount or dispute it before suit may be filed. [F.S. 448.110(6)]","r":"F.S. 448.110(6)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If an employer fails to produce required business records within a set time after a written request, the department must issue a stop-work order. The records must be produced within","o":["3 days","21 days","10 days","45 days"],"a":1,"e":"Failure to provide requested records within 21 days of the written request is a ground for a stop-work order. [F.S. 440.107(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.107(7)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For a contractor's income statement, which cost belongs in general and administrative overhead rather than in cost of revenue (direct job costs)?","o":["Journeyman wages on a specific job","Office manager salary and shop rent","Permit fees for a specific job","Wire and conduit installed on a specific job"],"a":1,"e":"Costs that cannot be traced to a specific job (office salaries, general rent) are overhead below gross profit; job-specific labor, material, and permits are direct costs. [Income statement classification]","r":"Income statement"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A fixed storage-type water heater rated 5,500 W at 240 volts, with a capacity of 80 gallons, is installed. What is the smallest standard branch-circuit overcurrent device that meets the sizing rule?","o":["25 A","30 A","20 A","40 A"],"a":1,"e":"I = 5,500 / 240 = 22.9 A. The branch-circuit rating must be at least 125%: 22.9 x 1.25 = 28.6 A, so a 30 A device is the smallest standard size. [NEC 422.13, 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 422.13, 240.6(A)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"For wages paid in 2026, what is the Social Security tax rate withheld from an employee's pay?","o":["7.65% of covered wages","1.45% of covered wages","6.2% of covered wages","12.4% of covered wages"],"a":2,"e":"The employee's share of Social Security tax is 6.2%, matched by a 6.2% employer share. 7.65% is the combined Social Security plus Medicare employee rate, and 12.4% is the combined employee-plus-employer Social Security rate. [IRS Topic 751; IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Topic 751; Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A subcontractor submits a pay application for $250,000 on a contract with 10% retainage. How much cash does it expect to collect on this application, and how is the withheld amount carried?","o":["$25,000 cash; $225,000 retainage receivable","$250,000 cash; no retainage receivable","$225,000 cash; $25,000 as revenue not yet earned","$225,000 cash; $25,000 retainage receivable"],"a":3,"e":"Retainage withheld = $250,000 x 10% = $25,000. Cash due = $250,000 - $25,000 = $225,000. The withheld amount stays on the books as retainage receivable. [Retainage]","r":"Retainage (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The accessible portion of abandoned Class 2 and Class 3 cables must be:","o":["Tagged and left in place permanently","Cut and capped at each end","Painted red and left in the ceiling","Removed"],"a":3,"e":"NEC 725.25 requires that the accessible portion of abandoned cables be removed; a tag is only for cable identified for future use. [NEC 725.25]","r":"NEC 2023 725.25 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A low-voltage landscape lighting circuit limited to 30 V is wired with Type UF cable. The minimum cover is:","o":["6 in.","24 in.","18 in.","12 in."],"a":0,"e":"Column 5 of Table 300.5 permits 6 in. for irrigation and landscape lighting circuits limited to 30 V installed with UF or other identified cable or raceway. [NEC Table 300.5]","r":"NEC Table 300.5"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Using the NFPA 101 capacity factor of 0.3 in. per person for stairs, how many persons can a stair with 66 in. of clear width accommodate?","o":["220","66","330","132"],"a":0,"e":"66 in. / 0.3 in. per person = 220 persons. The 0.2 in. per person factor is for level components and ramps, which would give 330. [NFPA 101, 7.3.3.1]","r":"NFPA 101 7.3.3.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Costs and estimated earnings in excess of billings (underbillings) appear on a contractor's balance sheet as","o":["a deduction from retained earnings","a long-term liability","a current asset","a current liability"],"a":2,"e":"Work has been performed and earned but not yet billed, so it is an asset, and a surety will scrutinize it. [WIP schedule]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 440.10(1)(c), a contractor must require each subcontractor to provide","o":["a personal guarantee of the contractor","a copy of the sub's tax return","a performance bond","evidence of workers' compensation insurance"],"a":3,"e":"The contractor must obtain evidence of coverage (or a copy of an officer's exemption certificate) from each sub. [F.S. 440.10(1)(c)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(c)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Hospital emergency generator transfer: how soon must the life safety and critical branches be restored after loss of normal power?","o":["Within 10 seconds","Within 60 seconds","Within 2 minutes","Within 30 seconds"],"a":0,"e":"Life safety and critical branches must be automatically restored within 10 seconds; the equipment system may be delayed or manual. [NEC 517.31, 700.12]","r":"NEC 517.31, 700.12"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A payment bond has a penal sum of $450,000. Valid claims from unpaid claimants total $520,000. What is the most the surety must pay in total under the bond?","o":["$520,000","$450,000","$70,000","$225,000"],"a":1,"e":"The surety's liability cannot exceed the penal sum, so the cap is $450,000; the remaining $70,000 must be pursued against the contractor. [F.S. 713.23(1)(f)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(1)(f)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor asks a new subcontractor to complete Form W-9. What does that form collect?","o":["The subcontractor's income tax withholding allowances","The subcontractor's name and taxpayer identification number","The subcontractor's workers' compensation class code","The subcontractor's Florida reemployment tax rate"],"a":1,"e":"Form W-9 provides the payee's correct name and TIN so the payer can file information returns such as Form 1099-NEC. Form W-4 sets income tax withholding for employees. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); IRS Form W-9 instructions]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A nonexempt employee paid $20.00 per hour works 52 hours in the first week and 28 hours in the second week of a biweekly pay period. What is the correct gross pay for the two weeks?","o":["$1,600.00","$1,360.00","$1,840.00","$1,720.00"],"a":3,"e":"Each workweek stands alone: Week 1 = 40 x $20 + 12 x $30 = $1,160; Week 2 = 28 x $20 = $560; total $1,720.00. Averaging the two weeks to 80 hours ($1,600.00) is not permitted. [29 CFR 778.104]","r":"29 CFR 778.104"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 713.06(3)(d)1, F.S., a contractor must deliver the final payment affidavit at least how many days before suing the owner to enforce a lien?","o":["5 days","10 days","20 days","45 days"],"a":0,"e":"The affidavit must be delivered at least 5 days before instituting an action, and a contractor in default for failing to deliver it has no lien. [F.S. 713.06(3)(d)1]","r":"F.S. 713.06(3)(d)1"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contract states its effective date as January 5. The contractor's required license was not issued until January 20, and the contractor began work on February 1. Under s. 489.128(1)(c), F.S., is the contractor treated as unlicensed?","o":["No, because the contractor was licensed when work began","No, because the license was issued within 30 days","Yes, but only for work performed before January 20","Yes, because status is judged on the contract's stated effective date"],"a":3,"e":"Unlicensed status is tested on the stated effective date, or the date the last party signed if none is stated, and only then on the first day of work. Here January 5 controls, so the contract is unenforceable by the contractor. [F.S. 489.128(1)(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.128(1)(c)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For three 480-ft pulls (one for each of three phase conductors) a contractor adds 6 ft of tail per end at both ends of each conductor. How many total feet of wire are required for all three conductors?","o":["1476 ft","1452 ft","1446 ft","1458 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Each conductor = 480 + 6 + 6 = 492 ft; three conductors = 1476 ft. The tail allowance applies to each conductor at each end. [Estimating practice]","r":"Takeoff"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The Working CAPline is best described as","o":["a non-revolving term line used only for buying equipment and heavy vehicles","a government grant for new contractors that does not need to be repaid","a bridge loan intended for the owner's personal home purchase","an asset-based revolving line repaid as short-term assets such as receivables convert to cash"],"a":3,"e":"It is an asset-based revolving line that requires ongoing collateral monitoring and is repaid from collections on receivables. [SBA 7(a) loan types]","r":"SBA 7(a) CAPLines"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum rating of a branch circuit that supplies a neon tubing installation?","o":["15 amperes","20 amperes","30 amperes","50 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"Branch circuits supplying neon tubing may not exceed 30 A. Branch circuits that supply all other signs and outline lighting are limited to 20 A. [NEC 600.5]","r":"NEC 600.5"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor reports revenue $5,000,000; direct costs $4,100,000; overhead $520,000; interest $60,000; and a 21% income tax rate on pre-tax income. What are net income after tax and the net profit margin?","o":["$300,200 and 6.00%","$67,200 and 1.34%","$320,000 and 6.40%","$252,800 and 5.06%"],"a":3,"e":"Pre-tax income = $5,000,000 - $4,100,000 - $520,000 - $60,000 = $320,000. Tax = 21% x $320,000 = $67,200. Net income = $252,800; margin = $252,800 / $5,000,000 = 5.06%. [Income statement; profit margin]","r":"Profit margin"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.505, a \"burglar alarm system agent\" is best described as:","o":["A property owner who installs a burglar alarm in his or her own home","An employee of a licensed alarm or electrical contractor who performs burglar alarm work for pay","A qualifying agent who supervises all permitted jobs for the contractor","A central station operator who dispatches police after an intrusion signal"],"a":1,"e":"A burglar alarm system agent is an employee working under a licensed contractor; the qualifying agent is the certified supervisor, not the employee class. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505(25)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A written promise that work will be free of defects for one year after substantial completion is an example of:","o":["An implied warranty of habitability","An express warranty","A statute of repose","A liquidated damages clause"],"a":1,"e":"Express warranties are stated in the contract or other writing. Implied warranties arise by law without being written. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Temporary wiring for construction must be removed:","o":["Immediately when the purpose is completed","Within 30 days of completion","At the next inspection","After the certificate of occupancy"],"a":0,"e":"Temporary wiring must be removed immediately upon completion of the purpose for which it was installed. [NEC 590.3(D)]","r":"NEC 590.3(D)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A long-lead item is best managed on a schedule by","o":["showing submittal, approval, fabrication, and delivery in the network","listing the item in the specifications section only","adding the item to the schedule at installation time only","ignoring the item until a purchase order is signed"],"a":0,"e":"When the procurement chain is on the schedule, a late submittal or release becomes visible and its float can be measured. [Procurement scheduling]","r":"Delivery lead times (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A luminaire schedule lists Type D as 'Recessed 2x2 LED, 120/277 V, 0-10 V dimming driver.' The branch circuit is 277 V. What does this tell the contractor?","o":["The fixture needs both a 120 V neutral and a 277 V ungrounded conductor","The fixture can be used only on a 120 V branch circuit and never at 277 V","The driver accepts either voltage, and a separate 0-10 V dimming control is needed","The fixture draws a maximum of 10 amperes when dimmed"],"a":2,"e":"120/277 V means universal input, and 0-10 V is a Class 2 control signal for dimming. The control wiring must be separate from the line-voltage conductors unless the manufacturer permits otherwise. [Luminaire schedule reading; NEC 725]","r":"Luminaire schedule reading; NEC 725 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A project manager receives $230,000 in 2026 wages from one employer. How much Additional Medicare Tax should the employer withhold for the year?","o":["$2,070.00","$3,335.00","$270.00","$0.00"],"a":2,"e":"Additional Medicare Tax is 0.9% of wages over $200,000: ($230,000 - $200,000) x 0.9% = $270.00. $2,070.00 is 0.9% of the full wages. [IRS Topic 751]","r":"IRS Topic 751"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a purely capacitive AC circuit, the current","o":["leads the voltage by 90 degrees","lags the voltage by 90 degrees","is 180 degrees out of phase with the voltage","is in phase with the voltage"],"a":0,"e":"Capacitor current leads voltage by 90 degrees (ICE). [Phase relationships]","r":"Phase relationships"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The maximum loan amount for an SBA 7(a) loan is","o":["$500,000","$50 million","$150,000","$5 million"],"a":3,"e":"SBA states the 7(a) maximum is $5 million; SBA Express tops out at $500,000. [SBA 7(a) loans]","r":"SBA 7(a) loans"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An order imposing a local civil penalty under s. 489.127(5), F.S., has gone unpaid. After how long may the board or magistrate authorize foreclosure, and on what property is foreclosure barred?","o":["After 10 days; not on commercial property","After 12 months; not on rental property","After 30 days; not on any real property","After 3 months of nonpayment; not on a homestead"],"a":3,"e":"A certified penalty order becomes a lien that accrues until compliance or foreclosure, and after 3 months of nonpayment foreclosure may be authorized, but not against a homestead. [F.S. 489.127(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.127(5)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A wall-mounted strobe has its lowest edge at 82 in. above the floor and projects 6 in. into a corridor. Under ADAAG 4.4, the protrusion is:","o":["a violation, because the lens must be below 80 in.","compliant only with a cane-detectable barrier","not limited by the 4 in. rule, because the object is above 80 in.","a violation, because everything over 4 in. is prohibited"],"a":2,"e":"The 4 in. protrusion limit applies to leading edges between 27 in. and 80 in. above the floor. An object whose lowest edge is at 82 in. leaves the required 80 in. headroom. [ADAAG 4.4.1, 4.4.2]","r":"ADAAG 4.4"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A shop recovers overhead as a percentage of direct labor. Annual overhead is $240,000 and annual direct labor is $600,000. How much overhead is added to a job with $38,500 of direct labor?","o":["$11,000","$3,850","$15,400","$9,240"],"a":2,"e":"Overhead rate = 240,000/600,000 = 40% of labor; 38,500 x 0.40 = $15,400. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A mechanical schedule lists three 460 V three-phase motors with full-load currents (from Table 430.250) of 14 A (10 hp), 7.6 A (5 hp), and 7.6 A (5 hp). What is the minimum feeder ampacity for the three motors according to NEC 430.24?","o":["29.2 A","36.5 A","17.5 A","32.7 A"],"a":3,"e":"125 percent of the largest motor plus the other motors: 1.25 x 14 = 17.5, plus 7.6 + 7.6 = 15.2, for 32.7 A. Adding no factor gives 29.2 A. [NEC 430.24; Table 430.250]","r":"NEC 430.24; Table 430.250"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A transformer primary has 800 turns, the secondary 100 turns. With 480 V applied to the primary, what is the secondary voltage?","o":["380 V","240 V","3,840 V","60 V"],"a":3,"e":"Es = Ep x Ns / Np = 480 x 100 / 800 = 60 V. [Turns ratio]","r":"Turns ratio"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which MACRS convention applies if more than 40% of the year's depreciable bases of MACRS property (other than real property) is placed in service in the last 3 months?","o":["Half-year convention","Mid-quarter convention","Mid-month convention","Full-month convention"],"a":1,"e":"The mid-quarter convention applies when property placed in service in the last 3 months exceeds 40% of the year's total depreciable bases (excluding real property classes). [IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)]","r":"IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The 'as-built' schedule is","o":["the schedule showing the lowest-cost approach","the baseline schedule that was set before the award","a record of the actual start and finish dates","a forecast of the weather during construction"],"a":2,"e":"As-built schedules document what actually happened and are often used to analyze delay after the fact. [Scheduling terminology]","r":"Schedule updates (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"When may the State Fire Marshal or authorized agents inspect buildings covered by F.S. chapter 633?","o":["Only after a fire has occurred in the building","Only with a warrant issued by a circuit judge","At any reasonable hour when there is reasonable cause to suspect a violation","Only during business hours on weekdays"],"a":2,"e":"F.S. 633.216 authorizes inspection at any reasonable hour when reasonable cause exists to suspect a violation of the chapter or a minimum firesafety code. [F.S. 633.216]","r":"F.S. 633.216"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Resistors of 22, 33 and 47 ohms are connected in series across 120 V. What is the circuit current?","o":["0.85 A","1.18 A","2.55 A","11.64 A"],"a":1,"e":"Rt = 22 + 33 + 47 = 102 ohms; I = 120 / 102 = 1.18 A. [Series circuits]","r":"Series circuits"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Which is NOT one of the three conditions in F.S. 715.12(4) that must be met before an obligor must pay an obligee?","o":["The obligee is entitled to payment and submitted a written payment request","The obligee has substantially completed the entire project","The obligor (other than an owner) has been paid for that work by the party above it","The obligee has provided affidavits or waivers needed for the owner to make proper payments under s. 713.06"],"a":1,"e":"The three conditions are entitlement plus written request, upstream payment, and required affidavits or waivers. Completion of the whole project is not a condition. [F.S. 715.12(4)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NEC 408.4(B) requires a panelboard fed by a feeder (in other than one- and two-family dwellings) to be marked with:","o":["The date of the most recent torque check of the terminals","The name of the licensed contractor who installed the panel","The maximum number of circuits the panel may contain","Where the power originates, on a permanent non-handwritten label"],"a":3,"e":"408.4(B) requires identification of the source and physical location of the supply in a durable, non-handwritten way which helps the next person find the disconnecting means. [NEC 408.4(B)]","r":"NEC 408.4(B) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A coil has 20 ohms of resistance and draws 6 A. How much true power does it consume?","o":["720 W","120 W","1,247 W","1,800 W"],"a":0,"e":"Only resistance consumes true power: P = I squared x R = 36 x 20 = 720 W, regardless of the reactance. [True power]","r":"True power"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A worker earns $1,200 in a week (well below the wage base). The employer's share of Social Security and Medicare tax is","o":["$183.60","$74.40","$17.40","$91.80"],"a":3,"e":"1,200 x 7.65% = 91.80. [IRS Pub 15 (2026), Sec. 1]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum headroom required for working spaces about service equipment, switchboards, panelboards and motor control centers rated 600 volts or less under 29 CFR 1926.403?","o":["5 feet 6 inches","6 feet 3 inches","6 feet 6 inches","7 feet"],"a":1,"e":"1926.403(i)(1) requires working space headroom of at least 6 feet 3 inches for these assemblies (the higher 6 ft 6 in figure is for over 600 volts). [29 CFR 1926.403(i)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.403(i)(1)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Once bids have been publicly opened and the bid period has started to run, a bidder's price normally","o":["can be changed by a phone call before the award","remains firm for the number of days stated in the bid documents","drops automatically by the amount of the retainage","converts to a cost-plus price"],"a":1,"e":"Bid documents require the bid to remain irrevocable for a stated number of days after opening. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On a construction contract bond, the principal is the","o":["owner who is protected","contractor whose performance or payment is guaranteed","insurance company that issues the policy","subcontractor's supplier"],"a":1,"e":"The contractor is the party who must perform; the owner is the obligee and the surety guarantees the contractor's obligation. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is acceptable in an agricultural building where excessive dust, moisture and corrosive vapors are present?","o":["Type NM cable","EMT with set-screw fittings","Open wiring on cleats","Type UF, NMC, jacketed MC, PVC or liquidtight conduit"],"a":3,"e":"Corrosive/damp agricultural buildings need corrosion-resistant methods such as UF, NMC, jacketed MC, PVC and liquidtight nonmetallic or metal conduit. Type NM cable is not listed for these areas. [NEC 547.5(A)]","r":"NEC 547.5(A) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A dwelling has a mudroom (not a laundry area, basement or garage) with a hand sink. A 125-V, 15-A receptacle is mounted 5 ft from the top inside edge of the sink bowl. Does 210.8(A) require GFCI protection?","o":["No, because the room is not a bathroom, garage or basement","Yes, because it is within 6 ft of the sink bowl in an area other than a kitchen","Yes, but only if the sink is a laundry tub","No, because only receptacles within 3 ft of a sink need GFCI protection"],"a":1,"e":"Dwelling-unit receptacles within 6 ft of the top inside edge of a sink bowl (outside kitchens, where countertop receptacles are covered separately) require GFCI protection. [NEC 210.8(A)]","r":"NEC 210.8(A)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which statement best describes the SBA Surety Bond Guarantee program?","o":["SBA pays the entire bond premium for any licensed electrical contractor","SBA guarantees the contractor's bank line of credit for the project","SBA writes and issues the bonds to the contractor directly","SBA guarantees a surety against part of the loss on bonds to eligible small businesses"],"a":3,"e":"SBA guarantees the surety, not the contractor, and only where a bond is required and the applicant cannot obtain it on reasonable terms without the guarantee. [SBA Surety Bond Guarantee; CRS R42037]","r":"SBA Surety Bond Guarantee"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor has current assets of $840,000 and current liabilities of $480,000. It borrows $120,000 on a 90-day note and holds the proceeds as cash. What are the new current ratio and working capital?","o":["2.00 and $480,000","1.75 and $360,000","1.60 and $360,000","1.60 and $480,000"],"a":2,"e":"Both sides rise by $120,000: ($840,000 + $120,000) / ($480,000 + $120,000) = 1.60 (down from 1.75). Working capital is still $360,000. [Current ratio / working capital]","r":"Current ratio"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.129(11)(c), F.S., when a consumer complaint is otherwise eligible and the damages are LESS than a stated amount, the Department must refer it to mandatory arbitration. What is the amount?","o":["$2,500","$1,000","$5,000","$10,000"],"a":0,"e":"If damages are less than $2,500 and the other criteria are met, the complaint is referred to mandatory arbitration. $10,000 is the maximum fine per violation, a different number. [F.S. 489.129(11)(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(11)(c)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A small job has these activities (finish-to-start, working days): 1 2d (start); 2 3d after 1; 3 4d after 1; 4 5d after 2+3; 5 2d after 4. What is the project duration in working days?","o":["16 days","13 days","11 days","12 days"],"a":1,"e":"Path 1-3-4-5 = 2+4+5+2 = 13; path 1-2-4-5 = 2+3+5+2 = 12. The longer path controls: 13 days. [CPM forward pass]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The sum of the cross-sectional areas of all conductors in a sheet metal auxiliary gutter may not exceed what percent of its interior cross-sectional area?","o":["40%","25%","20%","30%"],"a":2,"e":"Sheet metal auxiliary gutters are limited to 20% fill at any cross section. [NEC 366.22(A)]","r":"NEC 366.22(A)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Where must the primary protector for a communications circuit be located?","o":["Within 100 feet of the telephone closet","Only at the service provider's pole","As close as practicable to the point of entrance of the cable","At the farthest outlet from the entrance"],"a":2,"e":"The primary protector must be listed and located as close as practicable to the point of entrance so the cable's exposure inside the building is limited. [NEC 805.90 (2023)]","r":"NEC 2023 805.90"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Using Table 220.12, what is the general lighting load for a 40,000 sq ft storage warehouse?","o":["100,000 VA","10,000 VA","40,000 VA","20,000 VA"],"a":1,"e":"Warehouses (storage) are calculated at 1/4 VA per sq ft: 40,000 x 0.25 = 10,000 VA. [NEC 220.12, Table 220.12]","r":"NEC 220.12, Table 220.12"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Overbilling on a job is generally favorable to cash flow but","o":["has no effect on the balance sheet because the cash is already received","creates risk if the contractor must later finish the work with cash already collected","reduces the contractor's liabilities by the amount of the overbilling","is always prohibited by Florida law on every private construction contract"],"a":1,"e":"An overbilled job means billing is ahead of earned revenue; the excess should be tracked as a liability and not spent as profit. [Billing practice]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.533(1)(p), F.S., a project is considered abandoned after how many days of termination without just cause or proper notice, or of failing to perform work without just cause?","o":["10 days","90 consecutive days","30 days","180 days"],"a":1,"e":"A project is abandoned after 90 days of termination without just cause or proper notice, or after 90 consecutive days of failing to perform work without just cause. [F.S. 489.533(1)(p)]","r":"F.S. 489.533(1)(p)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 489.115(4) (Part I), a certificateholder's license renewal cycle and continuing education requirement are","o":["three years, with 20 classroom hours each cycle","two years, with at least 14 classroom hours each biennium","one year, with 8 classroom hours each year","two years, with no continuing education required"],"a":1,"e":"Subsection (4) requires renewal every two years with at least 14 hours of continuing education per biennium, including topics such as workers' compensation, business practices, and workplace safety. [F.S. 489.115(4)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(4)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which SBA CAPLine finances one or more specific contracts, including allocable overhead or G&A costs?","o":["Seasonal CAPLine","Builders CAPLine","Contract CAPLine","Working CAPline"],"a":2,"e":"The Contract CAPLine finances specific contracts; the Seasonal CAPLine finances seasonal increases in receivables and inventory. [SBA 7(a) loan types]","r":"SBA 7(a) CAPLines"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A grounding diagram shows the neutral-to-ground bonding jumper made in the service disconnect and again at the downstream distribution panel. What is the issue?","o":["No issue, because every panelboard must bond its neutral to ground","The jumper may not be installed in the service disconnect, only at the transformer","The neutral is bonded in more than one place, putting neutral current on grounding paths","A second bond is required when the downstream panel is more than 100 ft away"],"a":2,"e":"The grounded conductor is bonded to the equipment grounding conductor only at the service (or at a separately derived system). A second bond downstream makes parallel neutral paths. See 250.24(A)(5) and 250.142(B). [NEC 250.24(A)(5), 250.142]","r":"NEC 250.24(A)(5), 250.142"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The financial statement and credit report submitted with a business organization's electrical application under Rule 61G6-5.004 must be dated within what period before the application?","o":["6 months","12 months","5 years","30 days"],"a":1,"e":"The rule requires the financial statement and credit report to be dated within 12 months of the application. [Rule 61G6-5.004]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C."},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 633.202, any local amendment to the Florida Fire Prevention Code must:","o":["cite an older edition of a national code","strengthen the code's minimum firesafety requirements","be approved by the local school board","reduce the life safety requirements for cost savings"],"a":1,"e":"Local amendments must strengthen the minimum firesafety requirements, and the State Fire Marshal checks that they do not lower the level of life safety or cite another edition of a national code. [F.S. 633.202(3),(7)]","r":"F.S. 633.202(7)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A $5,000 federal tax deposit is made 20 calendar days late, before any IRS notice is received. What is the failure-to-deposit penalty rate and amount?","o":["5%, which is $250.00","2%, which is $100.00","15%, which is $750.00","10%, which is $500.00"],"a":3,"e":"A deposit paid more than 15 calendar days late incurs 10%: $5,000 x 10% = $500.00. The 15% rate applies only if the tax remains unpaid more than 10 days after the first IRS notice demanding payment. [IRS Failure to Deposit Penalty]","r":"IRS Failure to Deposit Penalty"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A fluorescent sign is converted to LED using a retrofit kit. Which of these does Article 600 require?","o":["The kit must be listed with instructions, and the sign marked as retrofitted","The kit may be any unlisted LED strip bought from a retail store","Only the installer's name needs to be marked on the sign afterward","No marking of the sign is required once the retrofit is done"],"a":0,"e":"Retrofit kits must be listed and labeled and come with instructions; Article 600 also requires marking showing the illumination system was replaced with the kit provider's and installer's identification. [NEC 600 (retrofit kits)]","r":"NEC 600 (retrofit kits) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"If the contract documents call for something that would violate the NEC, what should the contractor do?","o":["Notify the design team in writing before proceeding, and comply with the Code","Follow the drawings as issued and let the inspector decide any conflict later","Install it as specified and then correct the work only if it is cited","Follow the specification only if the owner agrees in writing to the change"],"a":0,"e":"Contractors are expected to comply with applicable codes and should raise conflicts with the design professional promptly, usually through an RFI. [Contract practice; NEC 90.4]","r":"Contract practice; NEC 90.4"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier regularly ships late, causing crew downtime. Which measure helps the contractor compare it with other vendors?","o":["A vendor scorecard tracking on-time delivery, accuracy and price","The supplier's overall size, regardless of how it performs for you","The number of different items that the supplier lists in its catalog","The supplier's company logo and how well known its brand name is"],"a":0,"e":"Tracking delivery reliability, quality and price per vendor supports objective sourcing decisions. [Purchasing practice]","r":"Vendor evaluation"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 24-in. EMT nipple contains 6 3/0 AWG THHN copper conductors (0.2679 sq in. each). What is the smallest trade size EMT permitted using the 60% nipple fill? (40% areas in sq in.: 1-1/2 in. = 0.814; 2 in. = 1.342; 2-1/2 in. = 2.343.)","o":["3 in.","2-1/2 in.","2 in.","1-1/2 in."],"a":2,"e":"Total area = 6 x 0.2679 = 1.6074 sq in. The 60% area is 1.5 times the 40% value: 1-1/2 in. = 1.221 (too small), 2 in. = 2.013 (adequate). [NEC Chapter 9, Table 1 Note 4, Table 4, Table 5]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Table 1 Note 4, Table 4, Table 5"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A technician is billed at $120/hour and variable cost is $75/hour. Contribution margin per hour and CM ratio are","o":["$45 and 60.0%","$75 and 62.5%","$45 and 37.5%","$195 and 100.0%"],"a":2,"e":"CM = 120 - 75 = 45; ratio = 45/120 = 37.5%. [Contribution margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"After how many unsuccessful attempts at the examination may the board require additional college-level or technical courses in the areas of deficiency?","o":["One","Two","Three","Five"],"a":2,"e":"Under s. 489.511(2) the board sets by rule how often an applicant may test, and after three unsuccessful attempts it may require additional courses in the deficient areas. [F.S. 489.511(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.511(2)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Labor burden is best described as","o":["the physical strain that heavy tools and long hours place on workers","the overhead that a subcontractor pays to the general contractor","the employer's added cost of payroll taxes, insurance and benefits on top of wages","the amount of overtime worked by a crew in a given month"],"a":2,"e":"Labor burden includes employer payroll taxes, workers' compensation, benefits and paid time off, and must be added to the wage to know the true labor cost. [Labor burden]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which ratio is most useful for judging how well management turns the owner's invested capital into profit?","o":["Quick ratio","Current ratio","Return on equity","Days sales outstanding"],"a":2,"e":"ROE = net income / average owner's equity; it measures profit generated on owners' capital. Current and quick ratios measure liquidity, and DSO measures collection speed. [UNL Extension G2009]","r":"Return on equity"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A sign shop installs a 12,000 V, 60 mA neon transformer on a 120 V primary. The nameplate shows input current 6.3 A. Six identical transformers are on one 30 A neon branch circuit (continuous loads). Is this acceptable?","o":["No. Six units draw 37.8 A, above the 24 A continuous limit","Yes. 37.8 A is under the 30 A rating of the circuit","Yes. Transformers are not continuous loads in signs","No. A 30 A circuit is not allowed for neon signs"],"a":0,"e":"6 x 6.3 = 37.8 A. Branch circuits for signs are continuous loads, so the limit is 80 percent of 30 A = 24 A. Neon circuits can be 30 A (600.5), but this load is too high. [NEC 600.5, 210.20(A)]","r":"NEC 600.5, 210.20(A)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A portable or mobile sign must be provided with:","o":["A permanent hardwired connection to the building","An attachment plug, and be movable without tools","A pole-mounted disconnect within sight of the sign","A 480 V supply with a locking disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"600.10 requires adequate support, ready movement without tools, and an attachment plug for each portable or mobile sign. [NEC 600.10(A), (B)]","r":"NEC 600.10(A), (B)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An owner withholds 10% retainage on a $50,000 progress billing. What is the net payment due on this application, ignoring prior retainage?","o":["$45,000","$5,000","$55,000","$50,000"],"a":0,"e":"Retainage = 50,000 x 10% = 5,000; net = 50,000 - 5,000 = 45,000. [Retainage]","r":"IRS Construction ATG, Ch. 1 (retainage)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 1,200 W and a 600 W resistive load are connected in parallel on a 120 V circuit. What is the total current?","o":["8 A","15 A","10 A","216 A"],"a":1,"e":"Total watts = 1,800; I = 1,800 / 120 = 15 A. Branch currents (10 A and 5 A) add. [Parallel circuits]","r":"Parallel circuits"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a 240 V delta four-wire system with a center-tapped winding, the 'high leg' (wild leg) to neutral measures about","o":["240 V","277 V","208 V","120 V"],"a":2,"e":"Vhigh = 1.732 x 120 = 208 V (commonly stated as 208 V). Phase B is the high leg and must be marked orange. [High-leg delta]","r":"High-leg delta"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.131(7)(g), F.S., local governments cannot discipline which class of contractor?","o":["Registered contractors","Unlicensed persons","Certified contractors","Out-of-state visitors"],"a":2,"e":"Localities may discipline locally licensed or registered contractors but not certified contractors, who are disciplined by the state board. [F.S. 489.131(7)(g)]","r":"F.S. 489.131(7)(g)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under MACRS, a light general-purpose truck is classified as property with a recovery period of","o":["3 years","7 years","10 years","5 years"],"a":3,"e":"Form 4562 instructions list automobiles and light general-purpose trucks in the 5-year class. [IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025), Line 19]","r":"IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the first step in the hierarchy of risk controls for electrical hazards?","o":["Using PPE rated for the incident energy at the work location","Posting warning signs and labels at the equipment","Elimination, by creating an electrically safe work condition","Training employees in the safe work practices for the job"],"a":2,"e":"Elimination (de-energizing and lockout) is the most effective control; PPE is the last line of defense.","r":"NFPA 70E concepts (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A county awards a $1,742,000 building contract. What amount is the contractor's payment and performance bond under F.S. 255.05(1)?","o":["$871,000","$174,200","$2,613,000","$1,742,000"],"a":3,"e":"The bond generally equals the contract price. [F.S. 255.05(1)(g)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)(g)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A continuous-duty motor has a nameplate current of 30 A, a service factor of 1.0, and a 40 degrees C temperature rise marking. It uses a separate overload device. What is the maximum trip setting permitted by the general rule?","o":["37.5 A","42.0 A","30.0 A","34.5 A"],"a":0,"e":"The nameplate shows a 40 C temperature rise, so the 125% setting applies: 30 x 1.25 = 37.5 A. The 115% setting (34.5 A) is for motors with a service factor under 1.15 and no 40 C rise marking. [NEC 430.32(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 430.32(A)(1)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A luminaire weighing 65 lb is to be hung from a ceiling outlet box in a commercial building. What does the NEC require?","o":["The luminaire must be independently supported, unless the box is listed and marked for that weight; boxes are otherwise suitable for luminaires up to 50 lb","Any listed box may support it","The box need not be secured to the structure","A plastic box is acceptable if the cable is secured"],"a":0,"e":"Outlet boxes at ceiling luminaire locations are required to support luminaires up to 50 lb; heavier luminaires must be supported independently or the box must be listed and marked for the greater weight. [NEC 314.27(A)]","r":"NEC 314.27(A)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor on the accrual method finishes a service job and invoices $48,000 on December 20. The customer pays on January 15. Which statement is correct?","o":["The income belongs in January, because that is the month in which the customer's cash arrives","Half of the income belongs in each year, since the work and payment straddle the year-end","The income can be reported in either year, at the contractor's choice on each job","The income belongs in the December tax year because the right to the money was fixed then"],"a":3,"e":"Under accrual accounting the all-events test was met when the work was done and billed, so the income is earned in December. Cash timing only matters for the cash method. [IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accrual method]","r":"IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's estimated electrical payroll is $640,000. The manual rate is 7.85 per $100 of payroll, and the experience modification factor is 1.18. What is the estimated workers' compensation premium?","o":["$50,240","$5,928,320","$50,358","$59,283.20"],"a":3,"e":"Manual premium = 640,000/100 x 7.85 = 50,240.00; x 1.18 = $59,283.20. Omitting the modification gives the manual premium only. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What effect does a current of about 100 mA to 200 mA through the chest typically cause?","o":["A mild tingle, with no lasting effect on the body","Ventricular fibrillation, which can be fatal","No effect, since the body resistance limits the current","A slight warming of the skin only, with no internal effects"],"a":1,"e":"Currents of about 1 mA are perceptible, 10-20 mA can cause loss of muscle control, and 100 mA or more may cause fibrillation. Time and path matter.","r":"OSHA/NIOSH electrical shock effects (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.115(4)(a), F.S. (Part I), how often do certificates and registrations renew?","o":["Every year","Every 3 years","Every 5 years","Every 2 years"],"a":3,"e":"Part I licenses renew biennially, and the department mails renewal applications. Electrical contractors under Part II also renew every two years, by August 31 of even-numbered years. [F.S. 489.115(4)(a)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(4)(a)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"On a performance bond for a public project, the obligee is normally","o":["the contractor who signed the contract","the public owner that awarded the contract","the surety's reinsurer or underwriter","each subcontractor who supplied labor"],"a":1,"e":"The obligee is the party protected by the bond. F.S. 255.05(3) uses the owner as the party to whom the bond runs. [F.S. 255.05(3)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(3)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Using the same figures (wages $62,400, burden $14,460.60), the labor burden rate as a percentage of wages is about","o":["18.8%","7.65%","6.5%","23.2%"],"a":3,"e":"14,460.60/62,400 = 23.2%. Dividing by wages-plus-burden (18.8%) is the wrong base. [Labor burden]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A policy has two classifications: electrician payroll $420,000 at 6.2 per $100 and clerical payroll $85,000 at 0.18 per $100. The experience modification is 0.88. What is the total premium?","o":["$26,193","$27,552.80","$23,049.84","$22,915.20"],"a":2,"e":"Electrician: 26,040.00; clerical: 153.00; sum 26,193.00; x 0.88 = $23,049.84. Charging all payroll at the electrician rate overstates it. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If an employee's supplemental wages from one employer exceed $1 million in a calendar year, what flat withholding rate must the employer use on the excess?","o":["37%","22%","24%","32%"],"a":0,"e":"Mandatory flat withholding applies at 37% on supplemental wages over $1 million paid in the year. The 22% optional rate applies only up to $1 million. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A box contains three 12/2 NM cables with ground (six insulated 12 AWG conductors that terminate or splice in the box, one equipment grounding conductor group, no clamps) and one receptacle on a yoke. What is the minimum box volume required?","o":["15.75 cu in.","22.50 cu in.","18.00 cu in.","20.25 cu in."],"a":3,"e":"Conductors: 6; one device: 2; equipment grounding conductors: 1 = 9 allowances x 2.25 = 20.25 cu in. [NEC 314.16(B)]","r":"NEC 314.16(B)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What is the maximum number of 12 AWG conductors permitted in a 4 x 1-1/2 in. round/octagonal box (15.5 cu in.) containing no devices, clamps or fittings?","o":["6","8","7","5"],"a":0,"e":"Table 314.16(B): 12 AWG requires 2.25 cu in. each; 15.5 / 2.25 = 6.89, so 6 conductors. (Table 314.16(A) also shows 6.) [NEC 314.16(A), 314.16(B)]","r":"NEC 314.16(A), 314.16(B)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For PVC conduit, expansion fittings must be provided where the length change in a straight run between securely mounted items is expected to be at least:","o":["1/2 in.","1/4 in.","1/8 in.","1 in."],"a":1,"e":"Expansion fittings are required where the expected change in length, from Table 352.44, is 1/4 in. or greater between securely mounted items. [NEC 352.44]","r":"NEC 352.44"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which property is generally 'listed property' with special depreciation substantiation rules?","o":["Passenger automobiles weighing 6,000 pounds or less","Wire spools on a job","Computer used exclusively at the business's regular office","Hearses"],"a":0,"e":"Form 4562 instructions define listed property to include passenger automobiles of 6,000 pounds or less; some items such as hearses and computers used exclusively at a regular business establishment are excluded. [IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)]","r":"IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor performing a true (3)(d) contract buys the materials tax-exempt for resale. What must the contractor have?","o":["A Florida contractor license and nothing else at all","Dealer registration and an Annual Resale Certificate","A signed letter of authority from the property owner","A tax-exempt organization number issued to the owner"],"a":1,"e":"Contractors doing genuine (3)(d) contracts must register as dealers and use their Annual Resale Certificate when buying the materials to be resold. [Rule 12A-1.051(5), F.A.C.; F.S. 212.18]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(5); F.S. 212.18"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the standard temporal pattern used for the audible evacuation signal required for fire alarm systems?","o":["Continuous steady tone","One pulse per second","Two long tones then silence","Temporal-3 (three pulses, pause, repeat)"],"a":3,"e":"The Temporal-3 pattern (ANSI S3.41) is the standard evacuation signal. [NFPA 72 (2019) 18.4.2]","r":"NFPA 72 18.4.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Temporary wiring for holiday decorative lighting may remain installed for no more than:","o":["90 days","30 days","6 months","1 year"],"a":0,"e":"Temporary electrical power for decorative lighting, carnivals and similar purposes is permitted for not more than 90 days. [NEC 590.3(B)]","r":"NEC 590.3(B)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What protection is required for outlets that supply boat hoists at a marina rated not over 240 volts?","o":["GFCI protection for personnel","AFCI protection","GFPE at 100 mA only","No protection if the hoist is permanently wired"],"a":0,"e":"GFCI protection is required for outlets not exceeding 240 volts that supply boat hoists at docking facilities; the rule does not extend to higher voltage circuits. [NEC 555.9, 555.35]","r":"NEC 555.9, 555.35"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 218.735(6), F.S., a contractor on a local-government project must pay its subcontractors and suppliers within how many days after receiving payment?","o":["7 days","10 days","20 days","45 days"],"a":1,"e":"The contractor must pay subs and suppliers within 10 days of receiving payment; a subcontractor must pay its own subs and suppliers within 7 days. [F.S. 218.735(6)]","r":"F.S. 218.735(6)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"F.S. 218.735 governs timely payment for construction services purchased by","o":["private homeowners","federal agencies","private commercial landlords","local governmental entities"],"a":3,"e":"218.735 sets payment deadlines for local government construction purchases; private contracts fall under 715.12. [F.S. 218.735]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A year-end statement shows revenue $800,000, variable costs $520,000 and fixed costs $210,000. What is net operating profit?","o":["$70,000","$280,000","$590,000","$-140,000"],"a":0,"e":"Contribution margin = 800,000 - 520,000 = 280,000; profit = 280,000 - 210,000 = 70,000. [Contribution margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under Chapter 440, F.S., how many employees trigger the workers' compensation coverage requirement for a private employer in the construction industry?","o":["One or more employees","Two or more employees","Four or more employees","Six or more employees"],"a":0,"e":"Non-construction private employers need coverage at four or more employees, but construction employers must cover from the first employee. [F.S. 440.02]","r":"F.S. 440.02"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A specification section number 26 51 00 would contain requirements for:","o":["Exterior lighting","Lighting controls","Transfer switches","Interior lighting"],"a":3,"e":"26 51 00 is Interior Lighting, 26 56 00 is Exterior Lighting, and 26 36 00 is Transfer Switches. Lighting control devices are in 26 09 23. [CSI MasterFormat 26 51 00]","r":"CSI MasterFormat 26 51 00"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer fires a worker for filing a workers' compensation claim. Chapter 440 treats the discharge as:","o":["Lawful if the worker had less than one year of service with the company","Lawful, because Florida employers may end at-will employment for any reason","Prohibited coercion or retaliation that can give rise to a civil action","Lawful if the employer has fewer than 15 employees and no union contract"],"a":2,"e":"Section 440.205 prohibits coercing, threatening or discharging an employee because of a valid claim. [F.S. 440.205]","r":"F.S. 440.205"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 255.05(1)(a), before a public entity may pay a contractor on a bonded project, the contractor must give the entity","o":["the contractor's tax returns","a certified copy of the recorded bond","an unconditional bond from a personal friend","a certificate of workers' compensation exemption"],"a":1,"e":"The entity may not pay until the contractor provides a certified copy of the bond recorded in the county where the improvement is located. [F.S. 255.05(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)(a)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A job is priced at $1,000 with a cost of $750 (25% margin). The customer negotiates a 5% price reduction and the cost does not change. The new gross margin is about","o":["20.0%","21.1%","23.8%","25.0%"],"a":1,"e":"New price = 950; profit = 200; margin = 21.1%. A 5% discount cuts profit by 20% because cost stays fixed. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which wire color is the first pair of the 25-pair telephone cable color code?","o":["White/orange","Red/blue","White/blue","White/green"],"a":2,"e":"Premises telephone cable color code starts with the white/blue pair (tip white, ring blue). [Telecommunications color code]","r":"25 pair code (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under Rule 61G4-19.001, F.A.C., after how many citations for the same offense will the Department prosecute later violations under s. 455.225, F.S.?","o":["One","Two","Three","Ten"],"a":2,"e":"Three citations for the same offense lead to prosecution for later violations; for minor violations, the Department may cite if corrective action is not begun within 15 days or the offense is not the first. [F.A.C. 61G4-19.001]","r":"F.A.C. 61G4-19.001"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An RV park has 6 sites, each with a 50-ampere supply facility calculated at 9,600 VA per site. The Table 551.73(A) demand factor for 6 sites is 60%. What is the calculated load?","o":["57,600 VA","34,560 VA","28,800 VA","36,000 VA"],"a":1,"e":"6 x 9,600 = 57,600 VA, times 0.60 = 34,560 VA. [NEC 551.73(A), Table 551.73(A)]","r":"NEC 551.73(A), Table 551.73(A)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1904.35, when must an employer provide a copy of the OSHA 300 Log to an employee or former employee who requests it?","o":["Within 7 days","Within 30 days","By the end of the next business day","Within 90 days"],"a":2,"e":"Employees, former employees, their personal representatives and authorized representatives have access; the employer must give a copy by the end of the next business day. [29 CFR 1904.35(b)(2)(iii)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.35(b)(2)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"At a status date, an activity planned for 10 days has an actual duration to date of 6 days with 7 days remaining. The updated duration and the effect on a critical path are","o":["10 days total, with no effect on the project finish","13 days total, and the critical path shortens by 3 days","16 days total, and the critical path extends by 6 days","13 days total; the critical path extends by 3 days if no other change"],"a":3,"e":"Updated duration = 6 actual + 7 remaining = 13 days, 3 more than planned. If the activity is critical, the finish moves 3 days. [Schedule updates]","r":"Schedule updates (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The drawings call for a 20 A GFCI receptacle at an outdoor location, but the specification does not mention GFCI. The contract has no clause on drawing/spec differences other than requiring compliance with all applicable codes. Which action is most correct?","o":["Install a standard receptacle, because the specification is silent","Install GFCI only if the owner asks for it later in the project","Provide the GFCI protection shown on the drawings and required by the NEC","Delete the receptacle from the work, since it is not in the specifications"],"a":2,"e":"Items shown on the drawings are required, and NEC 210.8 independently requires GFCI protection for outdoor outlets in the listed conditions. [NEC 210.8; contract documents]","r":"NEC 210.8; contract documents"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Concrete for a transformer pad must cure 7 calendar days before the transformer is set. This is best modeled as","o":["a finish-to-finish relationship with no lag","a milestone with a 7-day duration","a start-to-start relationship with a 7-day lead","a finish-to-start relationship with a 7-day lag"],"a":3,"e":"A mandatory waiting period between the end of one activity and the start of the next is a lag on an FS link. [Lag]","r":"Lead and lag (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The contract requires written authorization before any change in scope is priced. An owner's representative says in the hallway, 'Go ahead and add six more outlets.' The contractor adds them without a signed change order. What is the main risk?","o":["The owner must pay double the normal price for the added outlets","The extra work is automatically free and the notice to owner is void","The contractor's license is suspended for the unapproved change","The contractor may be unable to collect for the extra work"],"a":3,"e":"Many contracts make a signed change order a condition of extra payment, and a court may enforce that. The contractor should get the written change authorization before doing the work. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which of the following may be connected to the supply side of the service disconnecting means?","o":["A cable limiter or a listed Type 1 surge-protective device","A 120-V GFCI receptacle","A lighting panelboard","A branch-circuit breaker for the HVAC unit"],"a":0,"e":"Only the equipment listed in 230.82 (cable limiters, meters and sockets, meter disconnects, SPDs, instrument transformers, and similar) may be connected ahead of the service disconnect. [NEC 230.82]","r":"NEC 230.82"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A dual-voltage 230/460 V three-phase motor is connected for the higher voltage by connecting its winding groups","o":["in series","in parallel","in open delta","through a capacitor"],"a":0,"e":"Series groups share the 460 V; parallel groups each see 230 V. Winding voltage stays the same in both. [Dual-voltage motors]","r":"Dual-voltage motors"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which action by an employer is unlawful retaliation under Title VII?","o":["Assigning ordinary job duties","Investigating a complaint promptly","Demoting an employee after she filed an EEOC charge","Documenting a performance issue that pre-dated the complaint"],"a":2,"e":"Section 704(a) prohibits discrimination against employees who oppose practices or participate in an investigation or proceeding. [42 U.S.C. 2000e-3(a)]","r":"42 U.S.C. 2000e-3(a)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under the NEC, which type of system is intended to supply power for convenience or to protect property, with no required automatic transfer time?","o":["Legally required standby (Article 701)","Optional standby (Article 702)","Critical operations (Article 708)","Emergency (Article 700)"],"a":1,"e":"Optional standby systems protect property or provide convenience and have no required automatic response time. Emergency and legally required systems have 10 and 60 second limits. [NEC 702.1]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 702"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"After receiving a Chapter 558 notice of claim from a homeowner, a contractor wants to inspect the property. Within what time may the contractor inspect under s. 558.004(2)?","o":["Within 10 days of service","Within 45 days of service","Within 30 days of service of the notice","Within 90 days of service"],"a":2,"e":"Section 558.004(2) allows inspection within 30 days of service, or 50 days for an association of more than 20 parcels. Destructive testing needs agreement. [F.S. 558.004(2)]","r":"F.S. 558.004(2)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.006, F.A.C., applications for transfers or additional business entities must be completed and received how long before a board meeting?","o":["7 calendar days","60 calendar days","180 calendar days","30 calendar days"],"a":3,"e":"The rule requires applications for transfers or additional business entities to be received 30 calendar days before a board meeting. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.006(2)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.006(2)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A work truck classed as 5-year MACRS property costs $30,000. Using the half-year convention table, depreciation percentages are 20%, 32%, 19.2%, 11.52%, 11.52% and 5.76%. What is the depreciation for the second year?","o":["$9,600.00","$6,000.00","$5,760.00","$3,456.00"],"a":0,"e":"Year 2 = $30,000 x 32% = $9,600.00. [IRS Pub 946, Table A-1]","r":"IRS Pub 946, Table A-1"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A sign transformer is installed above a suspended ceiling. Which statement is correct?","o":["It may hang from the ceiling grid support wires if the load is under 20 lb","It must be fastened independent of the grid and not use flexible cord","It may use flexible cord if the cord is shorter than 6 ft","It must be bonded to the T-bar grid, which can also support it"],"a":1,"e":"600.21(F) permits location above suspended ceilings if enclosures are securely fastened independent of the grid and not connected by flexible cord. [NEC 600.21(F)]","r":"NEC 600.21(F)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The labor unit for installing a fixture is 0.9 hour each. A crew of 3 works 8-hour days. How many whole workdays are needed to install 180 fixtures?","o":["6 days","21 days","8 days","7 days"],"a":3,"e":"Labor = 180 x 0.9 = 162 hours; crew capacity 24 hours per day; 162/24 = 6.75, so 7 days. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 5,000 W, 240 V single-phase resistive heater draws how much current?","o":["20.8 A","41.7 A","12.0 A","10.4 A"],"a":0,"e":"I = P / E = 5,000 / 240 = 20.8 A. Using 120 V (41.7 A) ignores the actual supply. [Power formula]","r":"Power formula"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A percent variance is calculated as","o":["(budget - actual) divided by actual","(actual - budget) divided by budget","actual divided by actual","budget plus actual divided by 2"],"a":1,"e":"Dividing the difference by the budget shows the size of the miss relative to the plan. [Variance analysis]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which entrances are exempt from the required sign outlet?","o":["Entrances on the north side of a building away from the street","Entrances used only by service personnel or employees","Entrances that are less than 6 ft wide at the threshold","Entrances at residential occupancies with a single tenant"],"a":1,"e":"The exception applies to entrances for deliveries, service corridors, or service hallways intended only for service personnel or employees. [NEC 600.5 Exception No. 1]","r":"NEC 600.5 Exception No. 1"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An electrical contractor performs work on a time-and-materials basis, listing each material and the labor separately on the final invoice. How are the materials taxed?","o":["The customer owes sales tax on the listed materials","The materials become tax-exempt","The contractor is the consumer; separately listing materials does not change this","The contractor must collect surtax only"],"a":2,"e":"Under Rule 12A-1.051(3)(e), time-and-materials contracts treat the contractor as the consumer, and itemizing on an invoice after the fact does not make the contract a retail sale. [Rule 12A-1.051(3)(e), F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(3)(e)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Generally, employers with how many employees or fewer at all times during the prior calendar year are partially exempt from routine OSHA recordkeeping requirements?","o":["25","10","50","5"],"a":1,"e":"Employers with 10 or fewer employees (and certain low-hazard industries) need not keep injury and illness records unless OSHA or BLS asks, but must still report fatalities and the other severe events. [29 CFR 1904.1]","r":"29 CFR 1904.1"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"When must employers train employees on hazardous chemicals in their work area?","o":["Only when an OSHA inspector arrives to review the written program","Once every ten years, at the time the written program is updated","Only for employees who handle more than one gallon of any chemical","At initial assignment, and when a new chemical hazard is introduced"],"a":3,"e":"Employees must be trained at initial assignment and when a new physical or health hazard is introduced that they have not been trained on. [29 CFR 1910.1200(h)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.1200(h)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A person who is not certified or registered runs an advertisement claiming to be a certified electrical contractor. Under s. 489.521(7)(c), F.S., this is a:","o":["Noncriminal infraction","Third-degree felony","Second-degree misdemeanor","First-degree felony"],"a":2,"e":"Falsely claiming to be certified or registered in an advertisement without a valid state credential is a second-degree misdemeanor under ss. 775.082 or 775.083. [F.S. 489.521(7)(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.521(7)(c)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A retail sale of a $3,000 taxable item is delivered to a county with a 1.5% surtax. What total tax (6% state plus surtax) is due?","o":["$180.00","$225.00","$270.00","$202.50"],"a":1,"e":"State: $3,000 x 6% = $180.00; surtax: $3,000 x 1.5% = $45.00 (under the $5,000 cap). Total = $225.00. [F.S. 212.05; F.S. 212.054]","r":"F.S. 212.05; 212.054"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor cancels a claims-made policy and wants protection for claims that arise later from past work. The contractor should buy","o":["a payment bond for the completed job","an additional insured endorsement","a higher experience modification factor","an extended reporting period (tail) endorsement"],"a":3,"e":"A tail extends the time to report claims after the policy ends. Bonds and endorsements for other parties do not fill that gap. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A subcontractor wants to receive its retained funds early. Under F.S. 715.12(7)(c), it may withdraw retainage by depositing which of the following?","o":["U.S. Treasury securities, Florida bonds or notes, or insured certificates of deposit","A personal guaranty from the subcontractor's owner secured by a second mortgage","A lien waiver signed by all of the subcontractor's own material suppliers","A verbal promise to the general contractor to complete the punchlist quickly"],"a":0,"e":"715.12(7)(c) permits substitution of listed securities for retainage, up to the lesser of market or par value, and only to the extent the obligor itself has withdrawn funds for that work. [F.S. 715.12(7)(c)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"For FLSA purposes, a workweek is defined as:","o":["The calendar week from Sunday to Saturday, with no other choice allowed","Any seven-day period that the employer may change from week to week","A biweekly pay period of 80 hours that may be averaged for overtime","A fixed, recurring period of 168 hours (seven consecutive 24-hour periods)"],"a":3,"e":"An employer may choose when the workweek starts, but it must be fixed and recurring. Overtime is computed per workweek, not per pay period. [29 CFR 778.105]","r":"29 CFR 778.105"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.115(5)(c), F.S., if an applicant does not provide evidence of required insurance within what time after notice of qualification must the applicant seek an extension?","o":["10 days","30 days","180 days","60 days"],"a":3,"e":"If evidence is not provided within 60 days after notice of qualification, the applicant must seek an extension, granted for just cause. The 30-day figure applies to workers' compensation exemptions. [F.S. 489.115(5)(c)-(d)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(5)(c)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The sum of the cross-sectional areas of all conductors at any cross section of a metal wireway may not exceed what percent of the interior cross-sectional area?","o":["30%","20%","40%","50%"],"a":1,"e":"Metal wireways are limited to a 20% conductor fill at any cross section. [NEC 376.22(A)]","r":"NEC 376.22(A)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Base labor for a conduit run is 551 hours at a burdened rate of $41.50 per hour. The area is congested and the estimator applies a 1.2 difficulty factor to hours. What is the labor cost?","o":["$22,866.50","$23,323.83","$27,439.80","$32,927.76"],"a":2,"e":"551 x 1.2 = 661.2 hours; 661.2 x 41.5 = $27,439.80. Applying the factor twice, or not at all, gives the wrong figure. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A job requires 470 ft of conduit. Conduit comes in 10-ft sticks and the estimator adds 8% waste. How many sticks should be ordered?","o":["55 sticks","51 sticks","47 sticks","49 sticks"],"a":1,"e":"470 x 1.08 = 507.6 ft; / 10 = 50.76, rounded up to 51 sticks. [Estimating practice]","r":"Waste factor"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A load schedule shows continuous lighting of 8,000 VA and noncontinuous receptacle load of 6,000 VA on one feeder. What is the load used to size the feeder, applying 125 percent to the continuous load only?","o":["17,500 VA","14,000 VA","16,000 VA","15,500 VA"],"a":2,"e":"Continuous: 8,000 x 1.25 = 10,000 VA. Add noncontinuous 6,000 VA to get 16,000 VA. Applying 125 percent to everything overstates the load, and adding no factor understates it. [NEC 215.2(A)(1), 210.20(A)]","r":"NEC 215.2(A)(1), 210.20(A)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A feeder is protected by a 225-A circuit breaker. What is the minimum size copper EGC for the feeder?","o":["8 AWG","3 AWG","4 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"225 A falls between the 200 A and 300 A rows; the OCPD rating not exceeding 300 A requires 4 AWG copper. [NEC 250.122, Table 250.122]","r":"NEC 250.122, Table 250.122"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which cord sets and receptacles may be tested at intervals not exceeding 6 months under the assured equipment grounding conductor program?","o":["Any extension cord that has a molded plug on both ends","Fixed cord sets and receptacles that are not exposed to damage","All receptacles on a portable generator regardless of use","Cord sets used outdoors if they are marked as weather resistant"],"a":1,"e":"The 3-month interval is the rule; only fixed cord sets and receptacles that are not exposed to damage get the 6-month interval. Being weatherproof or molded does not change this. [29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(E)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(E)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A run of EMT has an offset of 30 degrees, a 90-degree bend, a second 90-degree bend and a 45-degree bend between two pull boxes. The total degrees of bends is:","o":["360 degrees, which is not permitted","300 degrees, which is permitted","255 degrees, which is permitted","450 degrees, which is not permitted"],"a":2,"e":"30 + 90 + 90 + 45 = 255 degrees, which is under the 360 degree maximum for bends between pull points. [NEC 358.26]","r":"NEC 358.26"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A worker is paid every other week, 26 times a year. If the annual salary is $52,000, what is the gross pay per pay period?","o":["$4,333.33","$2,166.67","$1,000.00","$2,000.00"],"a":3,"e":"$52,000 / 26 = $2,000.00. Dividing by 12 would give $4,333.33 (a monthly figure) and by 24 would give $2,166.67 (semimonthly). [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An electrician earns $31.00 per hour and works 46 hours (overtime at 1.5x after 40), for gross pay of $1,519.00. A 5% pre-tax 401(k) deferral and $142.50 federal income tax withholding apply. FICA is figured on the full gross. What is the net pay?","o":["$1,260.29","$1,184.34","$1,185.00","$1,259.56"],"a":1,"e":"401(k): 5% x $1,519 = $75.95. FICA: $94.18 + $22.03 = $116.21 (on full gross). Net = $1,519.00 - $75.95 - $116.21 - $142.50 = $1,184.34. $1,260.29 reduces FICA for the deferral. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor has total liabilities of $800,000 and equity of $400,000. It uses $200,000 of cash to retire a long-term loan. What is the debt-to-equity ratio after the payment?","o":["1.50","2.50","2.00","3.00"],"a":0,"e":"Liabilities fall to $800,000 - $200,000 = $600,000. Paying debt with cash does not change equity, so D/E = $600,000 / $400,000 = 1.50 (down from 2.00). [Debt-to-equity]","r":"Debt-to-equity"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A Florida contractor that has used E-Verify as required faces an allegation that it knowingly employed an unauthorized worker under s. 448.09. What does E-Verify use provide?","o":["A rebuttable presumption and affirmative defense","Total immunity from all state and federal enforcement actions","Nothing, because E-Verify participation is only advisory","A guarantee that no federal penalties can ever be imposed"],"a":0,"e":"Employers who use E-Verify (or Form I-9 when E-Verify is unavailable) get a rebuttable presumption and affirmative defense against violating s. 448.09. [F.S. 448.095]","r":"F.S. 448.095"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A thermostat has its highest operable part 52 in. above the floor, with clear floor space that allows a parallel (side) approach. Which statement is correct?","o":["It fails both documents, because side reach is limited to 44 in. in each","It meets the 1994 ADAAG side reach (54 in.) but not the 2010 Standards (48 in.)","It fails the 1994 ADAAG side reach but meets the 2010 Standards side reach","It meets both the 1994 ADAAG side reach and the 2010 Standards side reach"],"a":1,"e":"ADAAG 4.2.6 allowed a 54 in. high side reach, but 2010 Standards 308.3 limit side reach to 48 in. maximum, so the control passes the old test and fails the new one. [ADAAG 4.2.6; 2010 Standards 308.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.2.6; 2010 Std 308.3"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's employee drives a company truck to a job site and causes a crash. Which policy normally responds first for injuries to the other driver?","o":["The workers' compensation policy","The builder's risk policy","The commercial auto liability policy","The payment bond"],"a":2,"e":"Vehicle liability is covered by the auto policy, not by CGL, which typically excludes autos. Workers' comp covers the employee's own injuries. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum spacing between neon tubing and the nearest surface (other than its support)?","o":["1/16 in.","1/2 in.","1/4 in. (6 mm)","1 in."],"a":2,"e":"600.41: a spacing of not less than 6 mm (1/4 in.) is maintained between the tubing and the nearest surface other than its support. [NEC 600.41]","r":"NEC 600.41"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Using the right-hand rule for a straight conductor, if the thumb points in the direction of conventional current, the curled fingers show the direction of the","o":["polarity of the voltage across the conductor","magnetic field around the conductor","resistance gradient along the conductor","charge stored on the conductor surface"],"a":1,"e":"Thumb = conventional current, fingers = magnetic field circulation. [Magnetism]","r":"Magnetism"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A takeoff counts 642 device boxes. The estimator adds 4% for waste and breakage. How many boxes should be priced (round up to a whole box)?","o":["642","668","694","669"],"a":1,"e":"642 x 1.04 = 667.68, rounded up to 668. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A technician measures a horizontal Cat 6 permanent link from the telecommunications room to the wall outlet at 310 ft. What does that mean?","o":["It is acceptable because the channel limit is 328 ft","It is acceptable because Cat 6 allows 150 m","It exceeds the 90 m (about 295 ft) permanent link limit and must be shortened or re-routed","It is acceptable if the cable is shielded"],"a":2,"e":"90 m x 3.281 = 295 ft for the permanent link. The 328 ft figure is the full channel including patch cords. [ANSI/TIA-568.2]","r":"TIA-568.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A 200-A, 120/240-V single-phase service uses 3/0 AWG copper ungrounded conductors. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor to a ground ring, concrete-encased electrode and rod system (Table 250.66)?","o":["4 AWG","2 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"From Table 250.66, copper ungrounded conductors of 2/0 or 3/0 AWG require a 4 AWG copper GEC. [NEC 250.66, Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250.66, Table 250.66"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Abandoned audio distribution cables must be:","o":["Capped and left in place","Removed from the accessible portion","Retagged every year","Left in place if in conduit"],"a":1,"e":"The accessible portion of abandoned audio distribution cables must be removed; cables for future use must be tagged with dates and purpose. [NEC 640.6(B)]","r":"NEC 2023 640.6(B)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What cover is required for rigid PVC conduit buried at a gasoline dispensing facility in a Class I location?","o":["24 in. cover, threaded RMC or IMC for the last 24 in.","6 in. cover with RMC risers","12 in. cover with EMT risers","18 in. cover with PVC risers"],"a":0,"e":"PVC may be used underground in Class I locations with at least 24 in. of cover, and threaded RMC or IMC must be used on the last 24 in. of run to emerge from the ground. [NEC 514.8]","r":"NEC 514.8 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A weekly nonexempt employee receives time-and-a-half for overtime. Which of these must be included in the 'regular rate' when computing overtime?","o":["Reimbursement for business expenses incurred for the employer's benefit","Payments for vacation days and holidays on which no work was performed","A nondiscretionary bonus promised for meeting production goals","Gifts, such as holiday gift certificates not tied to hours or production"],"a":2,"e":"Nondiscretionary bonuses are part of the regular rate; expense reimbursements, true gifts and pay for time not worked are excluded. [FLSA Sec. 7(e)]","r":"FLSA Sec. 7(e)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-7.001, F.A.C., a residential electrical contractor may work on residences of up to how many families and stories, and with what service size?","o":["1- to 2-family residences up to 3 stories, with 200-ampere service","1- to 4-family residences up to 2 stories, with single-phase 400-ampere service","Any residential building of any size, with three-phase 800-ampere service","1- to 4-family residences of any height, with 100-ampere service"],"a":1,"e":"The residential electrical contractor scope covers wiring and equipment in one-, two-, three- or four-family residences not exceeding two stories, plus accessory structures, limited to single-phase, 400-ampere single service. [F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(3)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-7.001(3)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor lets a 16-year-old helper operate a power-driven hoisting apparatus, such as a forklift or aerial lift, on a job. This is:","o":["Permitted during summer vacation only","Permitted if the helper has completed OSHA 10","Permitted for 16- and 17-year-olds with parental consent","A violation of the Hazardous Occupations Orders for minors under 18"],"a":3,"e":"Operating power-driven hoisting apparatus is prohibited under HO 7 for workers under 18 regardless of consent or training. [29 CFR 570.58]","r":"29 CFR 570.58"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A pay application shows an original contract of $800,000 plus an approved change order of $40,000. Work completed to date is 45% of the adjusted contract, retainage is 5% of work completed, and $260,000 has already been paid (net of retainage). What is the current payment due?","o":["$118,000","$82,000","$99,100","$18,900"],"a":2,"e":"Adjusted contract = 840,000; earned to date = 378,000; less retainage 18,900 = 359,100; less previous payments 260,000 = 99,100. Ignoring the change order or the retainage gives a wrong number. [G702/G703-style pay application]","r":"Contract billing arithmetic (progress billing practice)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contract has 120 calendar days for substantial completion and a $500 per day liquidated damages clause. A change order adds 9 calendar days of time. The work is substantially complete on day 138. What liquidated damages apply?","o":["$9,000","$4,500","$0","$500"],"a":1,"e":"The adjusted time is 120 + 9 = 129 days. The project is 138 - 129 = 9 days late, so 9 x $500 = $4,500. Ignoring the time extension gives 18 days, or $9,000. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Florida's usury law generally sets a maximum rate of simple interest for loans of $500,000 or less of how much per year?","o":["25%","18%","12%","6%"],"a":1,"e":"Section 687.02 and 687.071 set 18% per year simple interest as the ceiling for loans of $500,000 or less. A 1.5% monthly service charge equals 18% a year. [F.S. 687.02, 687.071]","r":"F.S. 687.02"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A state agency's policy sets the base daily liquidated damages at the construction amount multiplied by 0.06 and divided by 365 (before adding other delay-related costs). For a $2,190,000 construction contract, what is the base daily amount?","o":["$6,000.00","$131,400.00","$360.00","$36.00"],"a":2,"e":"$2,190,000 x 0.06 = $131,400 per year; / 365 = $360.00 per day. [FSU liquidated damages guideline]","r":"Liquidated damages"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"RMC is installed with threaded couplings in a straight run. Where permitted by the support table, what is the maximum distance between supports for 1-inch RMC?","o":["16 ft","14 ft","10 ft","12 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Table 344.30(B)(2) allows 12 ft for 1 in. RMC with threaded couplings (10 ft for 1/2–3/4 in., 14 ft for 1-1/4–1-1/2 in.), provided supports prevent stress at terminations. [NEC 344.30(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 344.30(B)(2)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign site sits directly on an open-water shoreline in a hurricane-prone region. Which exposure category in ASCE 7 is the most conservative?","o":["Exposure D: flat, unobstructed shoreline facing open water","Exposure A, the large city center category","Exposure B, urban and suburban terrain","Exposure E, a category for hurricane coastlines"],"a":0,"e":"Exposure D applies to flat, unobstructed areas and water surfaces; for a coastal site, using a more open exposure raises the design pressure. Exposure A is no longer used in current editions, and there is no Exposure E. [ASCE 7 exposure categories]","r":"ASCE 7 exposure categories (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under NEC 700.12, an emergency system must be able to supply the emergency load within what time after normal power fails?","o":["5 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds","10 seconds"],"a":3,"e":"Emergency systems must have power available within 10 seconds of normal power loss. The 60 second period belongs to legally required standby systems under 701.12. [NEC 700.12]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 700.12"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Class 2 cable conductors may be installed in the same outlet box as electric light conductors if:","o":["Both circuits are protected by 20-ampere breakers","A permanent barrier or listed divider separates the two groups of conductors","The Class 2 cable is listed CL2P","The Class 2 conductors are twisted around the lighting conductors"],"a":1,"e":"Separation of Class 2/3 from light and power conductors is achieved by a permanent barrier or listed divider (among other listed exceptions). The cable rating or breaker size does not change the separation rule. [NEC 725.136(B)]","r":"NEC 2023 725.136 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A capacitor in a DC circuit, once fully charged, behaves like","o":["a resistor","a battery of double voltage","an open circuit","a short circuit"],"a":2,"e":"Once charged, no more current flows through the dielectric. [Capacitors]","r":"Capacitors"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Use this simplified experience rating formula: Mod = (Ap + W x Ae + (1 - W) x Ee + B) / (E + B). Actual primary losses Ap = 22,000; actual excess losses Ae = 48,000; expected excess losses Ee = 60,000; total expected losses E = 100,000; weighting W = 0.2; ballast B = 30,000. What is the modification (rounded to two decimals)?","o":["0.84","0.70","0.80","0.77"],"a":0,"e":"Numerator = 22,000 + 9,600 + 48,000 + 30,000 = 109,600; denominator = 130,000; mod = 0.8431, or 0.84. Dropping the ballast or the weighting changes the result. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 725.06(3), F.S., a public agency construction contract that requires a contractor to indemnify the agency beyond what subsection (2) allows is:","o":["Valid if the contractor is bonded","Valid if the agency pays the premium","Void as against the public policy of Florida","Valid for projects over $1 million"],"a":2,"e":"Outside the limited indemnity permitted in subsection (2), a public agency construction contract may not require one party to indemnify or hold harmless the other, and any such provision is void. [F.S. 725.06(3)]","r":"F.S. 725.06(3)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Float that belongs to the entire path rather than to one activity is best described as","o":["a milestone placed at the end of the path","total float, shared by the activities on that path","free float, owned only by the first activity","a negative duration assigned to the path"],"a":1,"e":"Total float is shared along a path: if the first activity uses it, the later ones lose it. Free float is the delay possible without affecting the next activity's early start. [CPM float]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.119(5)(a), F.S., where must a contractor place the registration or certification number, and what must a local building department do?","o":["On the final inspection card only; the department keeps no record","On every permit application and permit; the building department must verify it","On the certificate of occupancy only; the clerk verifies it later","On the notice of commencement only; the lender verifies the number"],"a":1,"e":"The number goes on every building permit application and every issued and recorded permit, and building departments must require proof of the contractor's number before issuing a permit. [F.S. 489.119(5)(a)]","r":"F.S. 489.119(5)(a)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A panel has 18 homerun circuits averaging 88 feet each, using 3 conductors of #12 THHN per circuit. The estimator allows 8 percent waste. THHN is bought in 500-foot reels. How many reels are needed?","o":["10","13","9","11"],"a":3,"e":"18 x 88 x 3 = 4,752 ft; x 1.08 = 5,132.2 ft; / 500 = 10.26, so 11 reels. Skipping waste gives 9.50 reels, and ordering two fewer reels leaves the job short. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What generally happens to the design wind pressure on a sign as its height above the ground increases?","o":["It decreases, because the wind is weaker higher up","It increases, because Kz rises with height above ground","It stays the same at every height above the ground","It is zero for any part of the sign above 30 ft"],"a":1,"e":"In ASCE 7 the exposure coefficient Kz grows with height above ground, so taller signs see larger pressures. [ASCE 7 Kz]","r":"ASCE 7 Kz (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contract gives 120 calendar days for completion, counted from a notice to proceed dated February 01, 2027 (day 1 is the day after the notice). What is the contract completion date?","o":["June 02, 2027","April 27, 2027","June 01, 2027","May 31, 2027"],"a":2,"e":"February 01, 2027 + 120 calendar days = June 01, 2027. Converting to 5-day weeks (about 85 working days) would give April 27, 2027, which is wrong for calendar-day contracts. [Contract time computation]","r":"Contract time (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 750 kVA, 480-V, 3-phase transformer with a 5.75% impedance is fed from an infinite bus. What is the approximate available fault current at its secondary terminals?","o":["15,689 A","902 A","9,058 A","27,174 A"],"a":0,"e":"I(FL) = 750,000 / (1.732 x 480) = 902.1 A. Isc = 902.1 / 0.0575 = 15,689 A. [NEC 110.9, 110.10]","r":"NEC 110.9, 110.10"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"According to SBA, how does invoice financing differ from factoring?","o":["Customers pay the lender directly, and the lender controls the sales ledger","In invoice financing the customers keep paying the business directly, and it keeps control of collections","The business must sell each receivable permanently to the finance company","There is no interest or fee charged in invoice financing, unlike in factoring"],"a":1,"e":"SBA describes invoice financing as borrowing against unpaid invoices with the business retaining control of collections, unlike factoring. [SBA: 3 ways to get working capital]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under the assured equipment grounding conductor program on a construction site, how often must cord sets and receptacles that are exposed to damage be tested, at a maximum?","o":["At intervals not exceeding 3 months","At intervals not exceeding 1 month","At intervals not exceeding 6 months","At intervals not exceeding 12 months"],"a":0,"e":"The general maximum test interval for cord sets, receptacles and cord-and-plug equipment is 3 months; the 6-month interval is only for fixed cord sets and receptacles not exposed to damage. [29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(E)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(E)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Workers' compensation benefits are generally paid to an injured employee","o":["regardless of who was at fault","only if the employer was negligent","only if the employee sues in court","only after a jury finds liability"],"a":0,"e":"Compensation is a no-fault system under ch. 440; it is payable without regard to fault except in limited cases listed in s. 440.09(3). [F.S. 440.10(2)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(2)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sprinkler control valve tamper switch operating at the fire alarm panel generates what type of signal?","o":["Alarm signal","Supervisory signal","Trouble signal","Pre-alarm signal"],"a":1,"e":"Valve tamper indicates an off-normal condition of a supervised system, which is a supervisory signal. A waterflow switch is an alarm signal; open circuits are trouble. [NFPA 72 (2019) 3.3 definitions]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 3, 17.17 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A bonding conductor of copper-clad aluminum is used between sign sections. What is its minimum permitted size?","o":["12 AWG","14 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Copper-clad aluminum bonding conductors must be at least 12 AWG; copper must be at least 14 AWG. [NEC 600.7(B)]","r":"NEC 600.7(B)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer is found to owe an employee $4,000 in unpaid overtime and cannot show good faith. In addition to the back wages, the employee may usually recover:","o":["Triple the back wages as a criminal fine","Interest only at 6 percent","An equal amount in liquidated damages","Nothing beyond the back wages"],"a":2,"e":"Employers who violate the minimum wage or overtime provisions are generally liable for unpaid wages plus an equal amount as liquidated damages. [FLSA Sec. 16(b)]","r":"FLSA Sec. 16(b)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In addition to other penalties, s. 489.119(5)(f), F.S., allows a local government to impose a civil fine on an unlicensed person who:","o":["Leaves a job sign posted for more than 30 days after completion","Claims in an ad to be licensed without a valid local license for the work","Runs an advertisement that is printed in a foreign language only","Hires an apprentice under age 18 without parental consent"],"a":1,"e":"A local government may fine under s. 489.127(5) a person who claims to be licensed without a valid license, or claims to be insured without general liability or workers' compensation coverage. [F.S. 489.119(5)(f)]","r":"F.S. 489.119(5)(f)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A transformer has a 6 : 1 step-down ratio. If the secondary supplies 30 A, the primary current is about (ideal)","o":["30 A","180 A","24 A","5 A"],"a":3,"e":"Current is inversely proportional to voltage: Ip = Is / ratio = 30 / 6 = 5 A. [Transformer current]","r":"Transformer current"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a plan, a branch-circuit line ends in a single arrowhead that points toward the panelboard. What does this arrowhead conventionally indicate?","o":["A dead end: the circuit is spare and not connected","A branch that returns from the panelboard to the first outlet","A feeder tap that is fused at the panelboard","A home run: the circuit continues to the panelboard that serves it"],"a":3,"e":"An arrowhead at the end of a circuit line is the usual home-run symbol; the circuit continues to the panel (circuit numbers are usually written at the arrow). It is not a spare or a tap. [Drawing practice: home runs]","r":"Drawing practice: home runs (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An employer is penalized under s. 440.10(1)(f) for misclassifying an employee as an independent contractor. What is the maximum penalty per misclassified employee?","o":["$2,500","$5,000","$10,000","$1,000"],"a":1,"e":"Section 440.10(1)(f) provides a penalty of up to $5,000 per misclassified employee, in addition to any other penalties. [F.S. 440.10(1)(f)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(f)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor registered as a dealer is required to register under which Florida statute?","o":["Section 489.119, F.S.","Section 443.131, F.S.","Section 212.18, F.S.","Section 440.10, F.S."],"a":2,"e":"Section 212.18 governs administration and dealer registration for sales and use tax; chs. 489, 443 and 440 deal with licensing, reemployment tax and workers' compensation. [F.S. 212.18]","r":"F.S. 212.18"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which cable may substitute for CL2R-listed cable under the Class 2/3 cable substitution rules?","o":["CL2P","CL2","CL2X","CL3"],"a":0,"e":"A plenum-rated cable can replace the lower-rated riser cable. CL2 and CL2X are lower fire ratings, and CL3 without an R suffix is not riser rated. [NEC Table 725.154(G)]","r":"NEC 2023 Table 725.154(G) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Maximum travel distance to an exit in a new business occupancy under NFPA 101 is 200 ft unsprinklered. With full sprinkler protection it is:","o":["250 ft","150 ft","400 ft","300 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Sprinklering a new business occupancy raises the limit from 200 ft to 300 ft. Sprinklers do not double travel distance in every occupancy. [NFPA 101, 38.2.6]","r":"NFPA 101 38.2.6 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Before conductors are installed in a conduit run, the conduit system must:","o":["Be installed complete between outlet, junction or splicing points","Have all fittings left loose for adjustment","Be energized first","Be left open at each end for ventilation"],"a":0,"e":"Raceways other than busways or exposed raceways with hinged or removable covers must be installed complete between outlet, junction or splice points before conductors are installed. [NEC 300.18(A)]","r":"NEC 300.18(A)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which of the following materials is the better conductor of electricity?","o":["Silver","Nichrome","Iron","Aluminum"],"a":0,"e":"Silver has the lowest resistivity of common metals, then copper, then gold and aluminum. [Conductor properties]","r":"Conductor properties"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"For triennial code adoption, F.S. 633.202(3) requires local governments to submit proposed amendments at least how many days before adoption (the State Fire Marshal may still process later ones)?","o":["120 days","180 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":0,"e":"The Fire Marshal gives local governments at least 180 days' notice and local governments submit amendments at least 120 days before adoption. [F.S. 633.202(3)]","r":"F.S. 633.202(3)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The energy lost as heat in a magnetic core due to the reversal of magnetization each cycle is called","o":["copper loss","hysteresis loss","corona loss","eddy loss"],"a":1,"e":"Hysteresis loss comes from domains realigning each cycle; eddy losses come from circulating currents. [Core losses]","r":"Core losses"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Retained earnings on a contractor's balance sheet represent","o":["the amount of profit the company expects to earn next year","the original amount the owner paid in when the company started","the cash balance carried in the company's bank accounts at year end","cumulative net income kept in the business after distributions"],"a":3,"e":"Retained earnings accumulate net income less dividends or distributions over the life of the company; they are not cash. [Balance sheet / equity section]","r":"Equity"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Before issuing bonds, a surety usually requires the contractor and its owners to sign","o":["a general indemnity agreement","a mechanics lien waiver","a letter of intent","an OSHA 300 log"],"a":0,"e":"The CRS report notes sureties must obtain a written indemnity agreement; the surety expects to be reimbursed by the principal and indemnitors for any loss. [CRS R42037]","r":"Surety underwriting"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Using the stated convention that a plain circuit line represents 2 current-carrying conductors and each hash mark adds 1 more current-carrying conductor (an equipment grounding conductor is implied and not counted), how many current-carrying conductors are shown by a circuit line with 2 hash marks, plus the equipment grounding conductor noted separately as 'EGC'?","o":["4 current-carrying conductors plus the EGC","2 current-carrying conductors plus the EGC","3 current-carrying conductors plus the EGC","6 current-carrying conductors including the EGC"],"a":0,"e":"Two for the plain line plus one for each of the 2 hash marks is 2 + 2 = 4 current-carrying conductors, with the EGC in addition. Always verify the convention in the legend because some sets count hashes differently. [Drawing practice: conductor hash marks]","r":"Drawing practice: conductor hash marks (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For a first-time failure to include the license number in an advertisement under s. 489.119(5)(e), F.S., the board:","o":["Issues a notice of noncompliance, and may fine or cite if not fixed in 30 days","Revokes the license immediately and imposes a fine of $10,000","Suspends the license for one year and requires a new exam","Takes no action because an omitted number is never a violation"],"a":0,"e":"A first offense draws a notice of noncompliance; the board may fine or cite a violator who fails to correct within 30 days or who commits a subsequent offense. [F.S. 489.119(5)(e)]","r":"F.S. 489.119(5)(e)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum number of conductors in a feeder to a mobile home, and how is the EGC identified?","o":["Three conductors with a bare EGC","Four conductors with a white EGC","Four insulated conductors, EGC green or green-yellow","Two conductors and a conduit ground"],"a":2,"e":"The feeder is a 4-wire run: two ungrounded, one grounded and one EGC, with the EGC green or green-yellow. [NEC 550.33]","r":"NEC 550.33"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A surety looks at total liabilities to net worth. A contractor has liabilities of $1,080,000 and net worth of $360,000. What is the ratio?","o":["0.75 to 1","2.0 to 1","0.33 to 1","3.0 to 1"],"a":3,"e":"$1,080,000 / $360,000 = 3.0 to 1. A higher ratio means more creditor financing, which generally reduces bonding appetite. [Leverage ratios; SBA/UNL]","r":"Leverage"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which factor determines whether an employee is exempt from FLSA overtime?","o":["Whether the employee regularly works more than 50 hours in the workweek","The job title the employer has assigned, such as manager or supervisor","Whether the employee has signed a written agreement to be treated as exempt","The employee's actual job duties and method of pay, not the job title"],"a":3,"e":"An exemption depends on meeting the salary level, salary basis and duties tests; titles and agreements do not control. [29 CFR 541.2]","r":"29 CFR 541.2"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"NFPA 101 limits the force to unlatch a door in an egress path to:","o":["50 lbf to release the latch","30 lbf to release the latch","15 lbf to release the latch","5 lbf to release the latch"],"a":2,"e":"The release of the latch may require no more than 15 lbf; 30 lbf is allowed to set a door in motion; and 15 lbf may be required to open the door to the minimum width. [NFPA 101, 7.2.1.4.5]","r":"NFPA 101 7.2.1.4.5 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which loads may be connected to the emergency branch circuits of an Article 700 system?","o":["Optional standby loads if the generator is large enough","Any load the owner wants backed up","Only loads classified as emergency loads","Only lighting, never power loads"],"a":2,"e":"700.15 limits emergency branch circuits to loads classified as emergency. Mixing in other loads can take capacity away from life safety loads. [NEC 700.15]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 700.15 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For an alarm system contractor I certification, s. 489.511, F.S., requires that at least what share of the applicant's work experience be in fire alarm systems?","o":["40 percent, in fire alarm systems typically used in commercial settings","10 percent, in any type of fire alarm system installed anywhere","75 percent, in residential smoke alarms and detectors only","100 percent, in monitored central station alarm systems only"],"a":0,"e":"Subsection (1)(d) requires at least 40 percent of the applicant's work experience to be in fire alarm systems typically used in commercial settings. [F.S. 489.511(1)(d)]","r":"F.S. 489.511(1)(d)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The Pregnancy Discrimination Act, an amendment to Title VII, requires an employer to treat a pregnant employee:","o":["The same as other employees similar in their ability or inability to work","As an independent contractor for the duration of the pregnancy","As exempt from all essential job duties until after childbirth","As ineligible for light duty, even if others with similar limitations get it"],"a":0,"e":"Discrimination based on pregnancy, childbirth or related conditions is sex discrimination under Title VII. [42 U.S.C. 2000e(k)]","r":"42 U.S.C. 2000e(k)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What is the maximum number of 12 AWG THHN copper conductors permitted in 3/4-in. EMT (40% fill area 0.213 sq in.; 12 AWG THHN = 0.0133 sq in.)?","o":["13","21","16","19"],"a":2,"e":"0.213 / 0.0133 = 16.02, so 16 conductors fit. (Annex C also shows 16.) [NEC Chapter 9, Tables 1, 4, 5]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Tables 1, 4, 5"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"When a sole primary qualifying agent gives notice of ending that status, the status ends when a new primary is designated or how long after satisfactory notice to the board, whichever comes first?","o":["60 days","15 days","30 days","120 days"],"a":0,"e":"Under s. 489.1195(3)(b), status ends upon designation of a new primary or 60 days after satisfactory notice, whichever is first; if none is designated, secondary agents become primary agents. [F.S. 489.1195(3)(b)-(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.1195(3)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The PO price for a panelboard is $1,420 but the invoice shows $1,486. The best practice under three-way match is to:","o":["Pay the invoice because the vendor's number always controls over the PO","Pay the lower PO price and say nothing to the vendor about the difference","Pay the invoice as billed, then adjust the purchase order afterward","Hold the invoice and resolve the price difference with the vendor before paying"],"a":3,"e":"A price variance is an exception that must be reviewed and approved or credited before payment; paying first weakens control. [Three-way match]","r":"Three-way match"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Overhead feeder conductors of 600Y/347 V pass over a residential driveway where no truck traffic is present. What is the minimum clearance?","o":["18 ft","10 ft","15 ft","12 ft"],"a":2,"e":"The 12 ft clearance is limited to circuits not over 300 V to ground. Where the voltage exceeds 300 V to ground, the same locations require 15 ft. [NEC 225.18(3)]","r":"NEC 225.18(3)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An employee suffers a work-related amputation. The employer must report it to OSHA within:","o":["30 calendar days","24 hours of learning about it","8 hours","7 calendar days"],"a":1,"e":"In-patient hospitalization of one or more employees, an amputation, or the loss of an eye must be reported within 24 hours. [29 CFR 1904.39(a)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.39(a)(2)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a hospital essential electrical system, the life safety branch must be restored within what maximum time after loss of normal power?","o":["60 seconds","10 seconds","5 seconds","30 seconds"],"a":1,"e":"NEC Article 517 requires the life safety and critical branches to be restored in 10 seconds; the equipment branch connects on a delayed schedule. [NEC 517.31]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 517.31 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"How many continuing education hours per biennium are required for alarm system contractors and specialty electrical contractors?","o":["4 hours","11 hours","14 hours","7 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Section 489.517(3)(a) sets at least 7 classroom hours of at least 50 minutes each for specialty and alarm system contractors. Electrical contractors need 11. [F.S. 489.517(3)(a)]","r":"F.S. 489.517(3)(a)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a trench 4 feet or more deep, how close must the means of egress be to employees?","o":["Located so no more than 10 feet of lateral travel is required","Located so no more than 25 feet of lateral travel is required","Located within 50 feet of the trench start","A ladder is required only for trenches over 8 feet"],"a":1,"e":"1926.651(c)(2) requires stairways, ladders, ramps or other safe means of egress in trench excavations 4 feet or more deep, positioned within 25 feet of lateral travel.","r":"29 CFR 1926.651(c)(2)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A dealer files timely electronically and owes $4,000 of sales tax for the month. Using the collection allowance of 2.5% of the first $1,200 of tax due, capped at $30, what is the allowance?","o":["$30.00","$100.00","$60.00","$0.00"],"a":0,"e":"2.5% x $1,200 = $30.00, which equals the cap; the rest of the tax earns no allowance. 2.5% of the whole $4,000 would be $100.00. [Florida DOR Sales and Use Tax tutorial; F.S. 212.12(1)]","r":"Florida DOR; F.S. 212.12"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A customer gives a contractor a $15,000 advance payment in 2026 for work to be done in 2027. For a cash-method contractor, the advance payment is generally","o":["reported as income in the year the work is completed","not income until the final invoice is issued","reported as income in the year it is received","reported half in each year"],"a":2,"e":"Generally an advance payment is income in the year received; accrual-method taxpayers may be able to defer one year under special rules, but the cash method does not defer. [IRS Pub 334 (2023), Advance payments]","r":"IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Sureties commonly evaluate a contractor on the three Cs. Which list is correct?","o":["Claims, collateral, and comfort","Cost, completion, and credit card","Character, capacity, and capital","Customer, contract, and cash"],"a":2,"e":"The CRS report on the SBA bond program says sureties evaluate credit, capacity, and character; the industry shorthand for the underwriting review is character, capacity, and capital. [CRS R42037]","r":"Surety underwriting (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 440.107, F.S., the penalty for an employer's failure to secure workers' compensation coverage is generally:","o":["1.5 times the premium it would have paid, with a cap of $10,000 per case","The premium it would have paid for the period, plus a 10 percent surcharge","2 times the premium it would have paid for the period, or $1,000, whichever is greater","A flat $5,000 per violation, regardless of the period or payroll involved"],"a":2,"e":"The base penalty is twice the premium the employer would have paid at approved manual rates for the uncovered period, with a $1,000 minimum. [F.S. 440.107]","r":"F.S. 440.107"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A motor shaft turns at 1,750 rpm. How many revolutions per second?","o":["105,000 rev/s","29.17 rev/s","14.58 rev/s","4.86 rev/s"],"a":1,"e":"1,750 / 60 = 29.17 rev/s. [Conversions]","r":"Conversions"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"In public mode, audible notification appliance sound levels are generally required to be at least how much above the average ambient sound level, and capped at what maximum?","o":["5 dB above average ambient; not more than 90 dBA","25 dB above average ambient; not more than 130 dBA","15 dB above average ambient; no maximum","15 dB above average ambient (or 5 dB above maximum sound level lasting 60 seconds or more); not more than 110 dBA"],"a":3,"e":"Public mode: 15 dB over average ambient or 5 dB over the maximum sound level lasting at least 60 s, whichever is greater, and no more than 110 dBA. [NFPA 72 (2019) 18.4.3, 18.4.1.1]","r":"NFPA 72 18.4.3 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"On a multi-employer construction job, the prime contractor is generally responsible under 29 CFR 1926.16 for:","o":["Only the work in its own trade, not that of other trades","Only its own employees, since subcontractors are responsible for theirs","Compliance of its subcontractors' work with the construction safety rules","Nothing, because subcontractors are independent businesses"],"a":2,"e":"Section 1926.16 says the prime contractor assumes responsibility for the entire contract, including the safety obligations of subcontractors, though subs also bear responsibility for their own employees. [29 CFR 1926.16(b), (c)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.16"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Weather delays that are normal for the location and season are generally","o":["compensable to the contractor in every case","always the owner's risk under the contract","excusable in every case under the contract","anticipated, and the contractor's responsibility"],"a":3,"e":"Contracts expect the contractor to plan for normal weather; only unusually severe weather beyond the normal baseline typically earns time. [Weather delay; FAR 52.249-10]","r":"Weather days"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If a general contractor holds 10% retainage on a subcontractor's $90,000 billing, the retainage payable recorded for that billing is","o":["$9,000","$81,000","$900","$10,000"],"a":0,"e":"90,000 x 10% = 9,000 withheld; the subcontractor receives 81,000 now. [IRS Construction ATG, Ch. 1]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A one-family dwelling has four fastened-in-place appliances: a 1,200 VA dishwasher, a 900 VA disposal, a 4,500 VA water heater and a 1,100 VA trash compactor (no range, dryer or HVAC among them). What is the demand load for these appliances?","o":["5,775 VA","7,700 VA","6,930 VA","3,850 VA"],"a":0,"e":"With four or more fastened-in-place appliances, 75% may be applied: (1,200 + 900 + 4,500 + 1,100) = 7,700 VA x 0.75 = 5,775 VA. [NEC 220.53]","r":"NEC 220.53"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Using the same job (earned revenue $250,000) with $290,000 billed to date, the job is","o":["underbilled by $40,000, an asset (costs and earnings in excess of billings)","overbilled by $40,000, a liability (billings in excess of costs and earnings)","overbilled by $290,000, recorded as revenue earned in the current period","underbilled by $250,000, recorded as an operating expense for the period"],"a":1,"e":"Billed 290,000 - earned 250,000 = 40,000 overbilled. Overbilling improves cash but is a liability because the work has not yet been performed. [IRS Construction ATG]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"When the disconnecting means for an Article 645 IT equipment room is operated, what else must occur?","o":["The standby generator starts","The fire alarm panel resets","HVAC shuts down and fire/smoke dampers close","Nothing else is required"],"a":2,"e":"The disconnect must also disconnect the HVAC and close required dampers. [NEC 645.10(A)]","r":"NEC 645.10(A)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For a (3)(d) retail sale plus installation contract, what must be true about title and risk of loss for the materials?","o":["Title passes only when the finished job is complete","The contractor keeps title until payment in full","The purchaser takes title and risk of loss as the materials are delivered","Title is irrelevant to the classification"],"a":2,"e":"A qualifying (3)(d) contract requires that the purchaser take title and risk of loss as materials are delivered, not only on completion of the work. [Rule 12A-1.051(3)(d), F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(3)(d)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An existing 480Y/277-V, 3-phase, 4-wire commercial service has a 12-month recorded maximum 15-minute demand of 62 kVA. A new 10 kVA noncontinuous load is to be added. Under 220.87, what is the total load used to determine whether the service has capacity?","o":["77.5 kVA","87.5 kVA","92.5 kVA","72.0 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"The existing load is 125% of the recorded maximum demand: 62 x 1.25 = 77.5 kVA. The new load at 100% brings the total to 87.5 kVA. [NEC 220.87]","r":"NEC 220.87"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The employee above has 128 hours of paid holidays and vacation, so only 1,952 of the 2,080 paid hours are productive. What is the fully burdened cost per productive hour?","o":["$36.95","$37.41","$39.38","$31.97"],"a":2,"e":"Total cost = 62,400 + 14,460.60 = 76,860.60; divided by 1,952 productive hours = 39.38. Dividing by all 2,080 paid hours gives 36.95, which under-recovers paid time off. [Labor burden]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.129(1)(k), F.S., which of these is grounds for discipline of a Part I contractor?","o":["Offering a customer a discount on the contract price for early payment","Employing a licensed subcontractor to perform part of the work","Using the company vehicle for an occasional personal errand","Falsely stating that work is bonded or that required insurance is provided"],"a":3,"e":"Paragraph (k) covers falsely stating that work is bonded, falsely stating that all subcontracted work and materials were paid for when loss results, and falsely stating that insurance is provided. [F.S. 489.129(1)(k)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(1)(k)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Direct costs for a bid are $52,000. Overhead is added at 18% of direct cost. What is the cost including overhead (before profit)?","o":["$61,360","$9,360","$63,415","$52,000"],"a":0,"e":"52,000 x 1.18 = 61,360. Dividing by (1 - 0.18) would treat overhead as a percent of price, not cost. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a parallel circuit, which quantity is the same across every branch?","o":["Resistance","Power","Voltage","Current"],"a":2,"e":"All parallel branches connect across the same two points, so each sees the same voltage. [Parallel circuits]","r":"Parallel circuits"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For a fire pump circuit, the branch circuit overcurrent device is intended to protect against:","o":["Overloads, tripping at 115%","Ground faults only","Short circuits only, carrying locked-rotor current","Overvoltage conditions"],"a":2,"e":"Fire pump circuits must carry the locked-rotor current indefinitely, and overload protection is not used. [NEC 695.4(B)]","r":"NEC 695.4(B) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A rooftop air-conditioning unit is on a flat roof. Where must the required 125-volt maintenance receptacle be located?","o":["Inside the building on the top floor","Same level, within 25 ft of the unit, not on its circuit","Anywhere on the roof within 50 ft of the unit","On the unit's own branch circuit within 6 ft"],"a":1,"e":"A 125-volt, 15- or 20-ampere receptacle must be on the same level and within 25 ft of heating, air-conditioning and refrigeration equipment, and may not be connected to the equipment's load side of its disconnect. [NEC 210.63]","r":"NEC 210.63"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A local government misses its deadline to deliver the final completion list under F.S. 218.735. What may the contractor then request?","o":["All remaining retainage, with undisputed amounts payable within 20 business days of a proper request","Only half of the retainage, with the balance held until final acceptance","A 15% late-completion bonus paid on top of the remaining retainage balance","Termination of the lien rights of every subcontractor on the project"],"a":0,"e":"If the entity misses its list deadline, the contractor may request all remaining retainage; remaining undisputed amounts are payable within 20 business days. [F.S. 218.735]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.008, F.A.C., the board audits at least what share of certificates and registrants, and how long does a selected licensee have to submit proof of coverage after the board's written request?","o":["At least 10 percent; 30 days","At least 1 percent; 10 days","At least 50 percent; 60 days","At least 100 percent; 90 days"],"a":0,"e":"The board audits at least 10 percent, and a selected licensee must provide an original certificate of insurance within 30 days of the written request. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On a Florida public job, a contractor wants to bid a particular surety only. Under F.S. 255.05(1)(a), the public entity","o":["must always require the entity's preferred surety","may require the contractor to buy the bond through the entity's agent","may require the surety to be domiciled in the same county","may not require a specific agent or bonding company"],"a":3,"e":"The statute prohibits requiring a specific agent or bonding company, though the surety must be authorized to do business in Florida. [F.S. 255.05(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)(a)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which statement correctly contrasts a certificate with a registration under F.S. Chapter 489, Part II?","o":["A certificate is statewide and not geographically limited; a registration allows work only in the jurisdictions where the contractor registered","A certificate allows work only in one county; a registration is valid anywhere in the state","A certificate is issued by a local board; a registration is issued by the Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board","A certificate is required for alarm work only; a registration is required for unlimited electrical work only"],"a":0,"e":"Certificate holders pass the state exam and may contract statewide, while registrants are limited to the local jurisdictions in which they hold registration. The local board does not issue certificates. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505(4), (17)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A patch cord with T568A on one end and T568B on the other end is best described as:","o":["A straight-through cable","A rollover console cable","A fiber duplex jumper","A crossover cable"],"a":3,"e":"Terminating the two ends with different standards swaps the transmit and receive pairs, producing a crossover. [ANSI/TIA-568.2]","r":"T568A/B (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a Class B initiating device circuit, a single open conductor results in:","o":["No effect on any device","An automatic alarm signal","Loss of the devices beyond the open, with a trouble signal at the panel","Loss of all devices including those before the open"],"a":2,"e":"A Class B circuit is a single path; the panel's supervision shows trouble, and devices beyond the open are unable to signal. [NFPA 72 (2019) 12.3]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 12 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"125 millivolts equals","o":["0.0125 V","1.25 V","0.125 V","12.5 V"],"a":2,"e":"Milli = 1/1,000, so 125 mV = 0.125 V. [Conversions]","r":"Conversions"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 20 hp, 460-volt, three-phase squirrel-cage motor (Table 430.250 full-load current 27 A) is protected by an inverse-time circuit breaker for short-circuit and ground-fault protection. What is the largest standard breaker rating normally permitted before the starting-current exceptions are used?","o":["60 A","70 A","67.5 A","90 A"],"a":1,"e":"Inverse-time breakers are limited to 250% of table FLC: 27 x 2.50 = 67.5 A. No standard size matches, so the next higher standard size, 70 A, is permitted. 67.5 A is not a standard rating. [NEC 430.52(C)(1), Table 430.52, 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 430.52(C)(1), Table 430.52, 240.6(A)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Type NM cable may NOT be used:","o":["Concealed in dwelling ceilings","As service-entrance cable","In attached garages of dwellings","Within walls of one- and two-family dwellings"],"a":1,"e":"NM and NMC cable are not permitted as service-entrance cable. [NEC 334.12]","r":"NEC 334.12"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Before ordering switchgear that is subject to a design professional's review, the contractor should:","o":["Obtain approval of the submittal data from the design professional before releasing the order","Wait until the inspection to confirm that the model is acceptable","Substitute a different brand to save money and tell no one","Place the order now and send the submittal data to the engineer later"],"a":0,"e":"Ordering before submittal approval risks buying nonconforming equipment that must be returned or replaced. [Purchasing practice]","r":"Submittals"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An emergency voice/alarm communication panel draws 0.45 A in standby and 4.8 A in alarm. Using 24 h standby plus 15 min alarm, what is the minimum battery capacity before derating?","o":["11.2 Ah","10.8 Ah","12.0 Ah","15.6 Ah"],"a":2,"e":"Standby: 0.45 x 24 = 10.8 Ah. Alarm: 4.8 x (15/60) = 1.2 Ah. Total 12.0 Ah. 11.2 Ah would use only 5 minutes of alarm. [NFPA 72 (2019) 10.6.7.2.1; arithmetic]","r":"NFPA 72 10.6.7 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A pneumatic impact tool is used on a job site. What must secure the tool to the hose?","o":["A positive means, such as a whip check or safety clip","A single wrap of duct tape around the hose and the tool body","A plastic zip tie, as long as it is rated for outdoor use","Nothing, if the air pressure supplied is below 90 psi at the tool"],"a":0,"e":"Pneumatic tools must be secured to the hose or whip by positive means to prevent accidental disconnection. [29 CFR 1926.302(b)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.302(b)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A $400,000 private contract holds 10% retainage throughout. Assuming all work is billed, how much of the contractor's money is tied up in retainage at final billing?","o":["$20,000","$60,000","$400,000","$40,000"],"a":3,"e":"400,000 x 10% = 40,000. That is cash the contractor has earned but cannot collect until retainage is released. [Retainage]","r":"IRS Construction ATG, Ch. 1"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1926.1408, within what distance of a power line must a crane employer use one of the three options (de-energize, 20-foot clearance, or Table A)?","o":["If any part of the equipment, load line or load could come closer than 20 feet","If the boom tip could come within 10 feet of the line, not counting the load","If the crane is set up within 30 feet of a pole carrying any wire","If the line is rated more than 50 kV, wherever the crane is located"],"a":0,"e":"If any part of the equipment, load line or load (including rigging) could get closer than 20 feet to a power line, the employer must follow Option 1, 2 or 3. [29 CFR 1926.1408(a)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1408(a)(2)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which injury is always recordable regardless of how long the employee stays unconscious?","o":["Loss of consciousness","A bruise that did not require treatment","A cut closed with a butterfly bandage","A minor headache"],"a":0,"e":"Any work-related case involving loss of consciousness is recordable. [29 CFR 1904.7(b)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.7(b)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A continuity test of a circuit conductor should be done:","o":["Only with the circuit de-energized and isolated","With the circuit energized, at the normal operating voltage","With the load running, so that the readings are typical","Only on live circuits, since a dead circuit gives no reading"],"a":0,"e":"Continuity and resistance functions inject a small test current from the meter's battery and are only for de-energized circuits; applying voltage can damage the meter and injure the user.","r":"Manufacturer instructions (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier extends open-account credit. The best control for a contractor is to:","o":["Pay cash in advance for every purchase regardless of the supplier's terms","Allow any employee to open accounts in the company name at any supplier","Complete a credit application with trade references and have credit limits approved before large orders","Rely on the supplier's published list price when deciding whether to buy"],"a":2,"e":"A credit application and approved credit limit give the supplier information and give the contractor accountability for who may buy on credit. [Supplier credit practice]","r":"Supplier credit"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A case requires 5 days away from work and 3 days of restricted work activity after the day of injury. How is the case classified on the 300 Log?","o":["It is not recordable, because the total is under 10 days","As a restricted work case only, since restricted days follow the days away","As a days-away-from-work case, with the days counted in the respective columns","As a first aid case, because the employee returned to the job within 10 days"],"a":2,"e":"A case involving days away is recorded as a days-away case, counting both away days and restricted days in their columns, with count stopping at the 180-day cap. [29 CFR 1904.7(b)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.7"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The load for a household electric clothes dryer in a dwelling is the nameplate rating or:","o":["3,000 VA, whichever is larger","5,000 VA, whichever is larger","1,500 VA, whichever is smaller","8,000 VA, whichever is smaller"],"a":1,"e":"A dryer load is the larger of its nameplate rating or 5,000 VA. [NEC 220.54]","r":"NEC 220.54"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"For rapid shutdown, the array boundary extends how far from the PV array in all directions?","o":["3 ft","5 ft","10 ft","1 ft"],"a":3,"e":"The array boundary is a plane 1 ft (305 mm) from the array in all directions. [NEC 690.12]","r":"NEC 690.12"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum size copper bonding conductor for the metal parts of a sign?","o":["18 AWG","14 AWG","12 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Bonding conductors must be copper not smaller than 14 AWG (or copper-clad aluminum not smaller than 12 AWG), and must comply with 250.120 and 250.122. [NEC 600.7(B)]","r":"NEC 600.7(B)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Since the 2023 amendment of s. 95.11(3)(b), F.S., the statute of repose for actions founded on the design, planning, or construction of an improvement to real property is:","o":["Four years","Seven years","Ten years","Fifteen years"],"a":1,"e":"SB 360 (2023) shortened the repose period from ten to seven years. Four years is the general limitations period, not the repose period. [F.S. 95.11(3)(b)]","r":"F.S. 95.11(3)(b)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which of these treatments makes a work-related injury recordable?","o":["A tetanus immunization given alone as a precaution","Medical treatment beyond first aid, such as stitches","Using a hot or cold compress for a minor muscle strain","Cleaning a wound with soap and water, then applying a bandage"],"a":1,"e":"Tetanus immunization, wound cleaning and bandages are first aid. Sutures and other treatment beyond the first aid list make the case recordable. [29 CFR 1904.7(b)(5)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.7(b)(5)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum number of days away from work that must be entered on the OSHA 300 log for one case?","o":["30 calendar days","180 calendar days","90 calendar days","365 calendar days"],"a":1,"e":"Employers may stop counting at 180 calendar days away; the count includes weekends and holidays when the employee would have been unable to work. [29 CFR 1904.7(b)(3)(vii)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.7(b)(3)(vii)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 10 microfarad and a 40 microfarad capacitor are connected in series. What is the total capacitance?","o":["8 microfarads","400 microfarads","25 microfarads","50 microfarads"],"a":0,"e":"Series capacitance: 1/C = 1/10 + 1/40 = 5/40, so C = 8 uF. 50 would be the parallel value. [Capacitors]","r":"Capacitors"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the 75°C-column ampacity of 350 kcmil copper conductor in Table 310.16?","o":["350 A","335 A","260 A","310 A"],"a":3,"e":"350 kcmil copper is 260 A at 60°C, 310 A at 75°C and 350 A at 90°C. [NEC Table 310.16]","r":"NEC Table 310.16"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Six current-carrying 6 AWG THHN copper conductors are installed in a conduit in a 38°C ambient. The terminals are rated 75°C. What is the maximum overcurrent device rating permitted if the conductors supply a noncontinuous fixed load and 240.4(B) may be used? (Use 90°C ampacity 75 A for derating; ambient correction 0.91; six conductors 80%.)","o":["50 A","60 A","70 A","65 A"],"a":1,"e":"Corrected ampacity = 75 x 0.91 x 0.80 = 54.6 A, which is less than the 65 A allowed at the 75°C terminals, so 54.6 A governs. That is not a standard rating, so the next higher standard rating, 60 A, may be used under 240.4(B). [NEC 240.4(B), 310.14, 310.15(B), 310.15(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 240.4(B), 310.14, 310.15(B), 310.15(C)(1)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The drywall subcontractor is scheduled to start Monday, but the electrical rough-in inspection has not passed. The best action by the electrical contractor is to","o":["cancel the pending rough-in inspection request completely","tell the GC now and ask for resequencing or an earlier inspection","wait quietly until Monday morning to see what happens","tell the drywall crew to go ahead and start work anyway"],"a":1,"e":"Prompt notice lets the GC adjust logic or float and prevents covering uninspected work. Starting anyway risks removal and rework. [Coordination with trades]","r":"Coordination with trades (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An employee was hired on June 15, 2023 by a 40-employee Florida company. Does the company have to run that employee through E-Verify under s. 448.095?","o":["No, because the employer has fewer than 50 employees and is therefore exempt","No, the requirement applies only to employees hired on or after July 1, 2023","Yes, because all current employees at covered employers must be run through E-Verify","Yes, but the employer has 5 years to complete verification of earlier hires"],"a":1,"e":"The mandate covers new hires on or after July 1, 2023; earlier hires remain under the Form I-9 requirement. [F.S. 448.095]","r":"F.S. 448.095"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Why are as-built drawings important to the electrical owner after the project is complete?","o":["They replace the permit and the final inspection for any future alteration of the system","They set the length of the warranty period that the owner receives from the manufacturer","They eliminate the need for future inspections","They document actual locations of concealed conduit and equipment for later maintenance"],"a":3,"e":"Concealed or underground wiring is hard to find later, so accurate record drawings reduce rework and risk during renovations. [Project closeout practice]","r":"Project closeout practice"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under ADAAG 4.28.4, a hotel sleeping room that has no building-connected visual alarm may instead provide:","o":["a 110 V receptacle plus a means for the building alarm to trigger an auxiliary visual alarm","a telephone with a flashing handset and no other signal","an extra audible horn mounted in the corridor outside the room","a battery-powered bell mounted inside the sleeping room"],"a":0,"e":"4.28.4 permits a visual alarm connected to the building system, or a 110 V receptacle plus a way for the building signal to trigger an auxiliary alarm visible throughout the room, with instructions. A bell or corridor horn is audible-only. [ADAAG 4.28.4]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.4"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What does the test button on a GFCI receptacle verify?","o":["That the receptacle is wired with the correct polarity and ground","That the GFCI trips and removes power, proving the sensing and tripping circuit works","That the grounding electrode resistance is within the acceptable range","That the circuit is not overloaded by the connected load at the time"],"a":1,"e":"Pressing TEST creates a small simulated ground-fault; the device should trip. A plug-in tester also verifies wiring and trip function.","r":"Manufacturer instructions; NEC 210.8 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What must be done with power tools designed to accept guards?","o":["Guards may be removed if the operator is experienced","The guards must be in place when the tool is in use","Guards are optional below 10 amperes","Guards are required only for fixed machines"],"a":1,"e":"Power tools designed to accept guards must have the guards in place when in use. [29 CFR 1926.300(b)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.300(b)(1)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor has cash of $30,000, receivables of $150,000, inventory of $60,000 and current liabilities of $200,000. What is the quick (acid-test) ratio, which excludes inventory?","o":["1.20","0.15","0.75","0.90"],"a":3,"e":"Quick ratio = (cash + receivables) / current liabilities = (30,000 + 150,000) / 200,000 = 0.90. Including inventory gives the current ratio. [Working capital]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Four arc welders have adjusted ampacities of 30, 25, 20 and 15 A. What minimum ampacity is needed for conductors supplying all four?","o":["90.0 A","82.5 A","77.5 A","60.0 A"],"a":1,"e":"630.11(B): 100% of the two largest, 85% of the third, 70% of the fourth: 30 + 25 + 17 + 10.5 = 82.5 A. [NEC 630.11(B)]","r":"NEC 630.11(B)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.505, the term \"registered residential alarm system contractor\" refers to a contractor whose registration is limited to which work?","o":["Fire alarm systems in one- and two-family dwellings anywhere in the state","Any alarm system in both residential and commercial buildings within the county","Low-voltage lighting and sound systems in residences only","Burglar alarm systems in residential settings, within the registration jurisdiction"],"a":3,"e":"The residential alarm registration is the narrowest alarm class: residential burglar alarm systems only, and only in the registered jurisdiction. Residential fire alarm work is not included. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505(23)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A 'no damages for delay' clause in a contract generally","o":["guarantees the contractor extra pay for any delay at all","limits the remedy for owner-caused delay to a time extension","requires the owner to pay the surety's premium for the job","bars the owner from collecting liquidated damages for any reason"],"a":1,"e":"Courts enforce such clauses, but not where there is fraud, bad faith, or active interference, such as a stop-work order kept in place after it was safe to resume. [Florida delay claims]","r":"No damages for delay"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under the 2010 Standards (305.3), the minimum clear floor or ground space for one wheelchair is:","o":["24 in. by 48 in.","36 in. by 48 in.","60 in. by 60 in.","30 in. by 48 in."],"a":3,"e":"305.3 sets 30 in. by 48 in. as the clear floor space size. The 60 in. by 60 in. space is a turning space or passing space, not the single-position footprint. [2010 Standards 305.3]","r":"2010 Std 305.3"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Net income is $210,000. Depreciation is $55,000. Receivables increased $80,000, inventory increased $30,000, accounts payable increased $45,000, and overbillings (billings in excess of costs) increased $60,000. What is cash from operations (indirect method)?","o":["$260,000","$150,000","$270,000","$480,000"],"a":0,"e":"$210,000 + $55,000 - $80,000 - $30,000 + $45,000 + $60,000 = $260,000. Asset increases use cash; liability increases (payables, overbillings) provide cash. [Statement of cash flows]","r":"Statement of cash flows"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor may typically be terminated for default (cause) when it:","o":["Materially breaches and fails to cure after written notice","Asks the owner for a change order for added work","Completes the punch list ahead of the contract schedule","Submits a monthly progress payment request on time"],"a":0,"e":"Termination for cause requires a material breach, such as persistent failure to supply workers or follow the plans, and usually written notice with an opportunity to cure. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A homeowner builds an addition on his own residence with hired workers and does not plan to sell or lease the property within one year after construction starts. For workers' compensation purposes, the project is:","o":["Not treated as a 'construction industry' activity","Covered by the state's guaranty fund","Exempt only if the cost is under $2,500","Treated as construction requiring full coverage"],"a":0,"e":"'Construction industry' excludes a homeowner's work on his own premises provided the property is not intended for sale, resale or lease within one year after construction begins. [F.S. 440.02]","r":"F.S. 440.02"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum force to push or pull open an interior hinged door (not fire door) under ADAAG 4.13.11?","o":["5 lbf","15 lbf","10 lbf","2 lbf"],"a":0,"e":"Interior hinged doors may require no more than 5 lbf to open. Fire doors have the minimum opening force allowed by the authority having jurisdiction. [ADAAG 4.13.11]","r":"ADAAG 4.13.11"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A surety issuing a Florida public payment and performance bond under F.S. 255.05 must be","o":["owned by the contracting public entity","an insurer authorized to do business in Florida","a bank holding company","approved by the contractor's insurance agent"],"a":1,"e":"Section 255.05(1) requires an authorized insurer; the public entity cannot require a specific bonding company. [F.S. 255.05(1)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 489.505(2), which alarm system contractor category covers all types of alarm systems for all purposes, including fire?","o":["Limited energy contractor","Residential alarm system contractor","Alarm system contractor II","Alarm system contractor I"],"a":3,"e":"Alarm system contractor I covers all alarm systems including fire; alarm system contractor II covers all types other than fire. A registered residential contractor is limited to burglar alarms in single-family homes. [F.S. 489.505(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.505(2)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Where may the overcurrent devices for the branch circuits of a dwelling NOT be installed?","o":["In a utility room","In a bathroom of the dwelling unit","In a hallway closet that is not a clothes closet","In a garage"],"a":1,"e":"Overcurrent devices may not be located in the bathrooms of dwelling units or guest rooms or guest suites of hotels and motels, or in the vicinity of easily ignitible material such as clothes closets. [NEC 240.24(E)]","r":"NEC 240.24(E)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A schedule (all finish-to-start, working days) is: P 5d (start); Q 4d after P; R 4d after P; S 6d after Q; T 6d after R; U 3d after S+T. How many critical paths does the network have?","o":["Two (P-Q-S-U and P-R-T-U)","Three (P, S, and T each form one)","One (only P-Q-S-U)","One (only P-R-T-U)"],"a":0,"e":"Both branches total 18 days (5+4+6+3 and 5+4+6+3), so both have zero float. A network can have more than one critical path. [CPM critical path]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In MasterFormat numbering, what do the first two digits of a section number such as 26 05 33 represent?","o":["The page number","The year of the edition","The division number","The level-three subject code"],"a":2,"e":"The first pair is the Level 1 division (26 Electrical), the second pair is the Level 2 group (05 common work results), and the third pair is the Level 3 section (33). [CSI MasterFormat numbering]","r":"CSI MasterFormat numbering"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1910.303, what must be marked on motor and appliance disconnects and on service, feeder and branch-circuit disconnects, unless evident?","o":["The date of the last inspection","The name of the installing contractor","Their purpose, in a legible and durable way","The available fault current at the terminals"],"a":2,"e":"Disconnecting means for motors and appliances, and each service, feeder or branch circuit at its disconnect or overcurrent device, must be legibly marked to indicate purpose unless located so the purpose is evident. [29 CFR 1910.303(f)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.303(f)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum cross slope of an accessible route under ADAAG 4.3.7?","o":["1:8","1:20","1:12","1:50"],"a":3,"e":"Cross slope on an accessible route may not exceed 1:50. A running slope of 1:20 is the point at which a route becomes a ramp. [ADAAG 4.3.7]","r":"ADAAG 4.3.7"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A load schedule for an office lists 30 duplex receptacles on single yokes. Using 180 VA per single or multiple receptacle on one strap (NEC 220.14(I)), what is the receptacle load?","o":["2,700 VA","10,800 VA","5,400 VA","45,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"30 x 180 VA = 5,400 VA. 180 VA is charged per yoke, so 360 VA would count each half of a duplex separately and 1,500 VA is the value used for small-appliance branch circuits in dwellings. [NEC 220.14(I)]","r":"NEC 220.14(I)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Class 1 circuits and NPLFA circuits are to be run in the same raceway. What is required?","o":["The Class 1 conductors must be derated by 50 percent","Both circuits must be dc only","All conductors must be insulated for the maximum voltage of any conductor in the raceway","A metal barrier must separate the two circuits"],"a":2,"e":"Class 1 and NPLFA circuits may share a cable, enclosure, or raceway whether ac or dc, provided all conductors are insulated for the maximum voltage present. [NEC 760.48(A)]","r":"NEC 2023 760.48(A)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the maximum demand, in kW, that Table 220.55 (Column C) assigns to one household electric range rated 12 kW or less?","o":["6 kW","12 kW","10 kW","8 kW"],"a":3,"e":"For one range not over 12 kW, Column C gives a maximum demand of 8 kW. [NEC Table 220.55]","r":"NEC Table 220.55"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under NEC 760.127, an overcurrent device protecting a transformer or other device supplied from power-supply conductors for a power-limited fire alarm circuit may not be rated more than:","o":["30 A","10 A","60 A","20 A"],"a":3,"e":"760.127 limits the overcurrent device for transformers or other devices supplied from power-supply conductors to 20 A. A larger breaker would not protect small power-limited conductors. [NEC 760.127]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 760.127"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A one-line shows service entrance conductors of 3/0 AWG copper. Using Table 250.66, what is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor required to connect to the grounding electrode system (not limited by a rod, pipe, or ufer special rule)?","o":["6 AWG","4 AWG","8 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.66 lists 4 AWG copper for service conductors of 2/0 or 3/0 AWG copper. 2 AWG applies to over 3/0 through 350 kcmil, and 6 AWG applies to 1 or 1/0. [NEC Table 250.66]","r":"NEC Table 250.66"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A fire pump controller is rated 480 volts. What is the lowest voltage permitted at the controller line terminals during motor starting?","o":["432 V","408 V","456 V","384 V"],"a":1,"e":"Voltage at the controller line terminals may not drop more than 15% below the controller rated voltage during starting: 480 x 0.85 = 408 V. [NEC 695.7(A)]","r":"NEC 695.7(A)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 24 V LED run uses 18 AWG copper (7.77 ohms per 1,000 ft) for a 30-ft one-way run carrying 3.0 A. What is the voltage drop and percent of 24 V?","o":["0.70 V, about 2.9 percent","2.80 V, about 11.7 percent","1.40 V, about 5.8 percent","0.23 V, about 1.0 percent"],"a":2,"e":"VD = 2 x L x R x I = 2 x 30 x 0.00777 x 3.0 = 1.40 V, which is 5.8 percent. The factor of 2 is needed for the out and return conductors. [Voltage drop calculation; NEC Chapter 9 Table 8]","r":"Voltage drop calculation; NEC Chapter 9 Table 8"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An 18 A, 240 V single-phase load is 125 ft from the panel (one way) on 8 AWG copper (16,510 circular mils, K = 12.9). What is the percent voltage drop?","o":["2.93 percent","0.73 percent","1.47 percent","3.52 percent"],"a":2,"e":"VD = 2 x 12.9 x 18 x 125 / 16,510 = 3.52 V; 3.52 / 240 = 1.47 percent. [Voltage drop]","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"For wiring of a theater in a building not required to be of fire-rated construction, which additional wiring methods may be used?","o":["Type NM cable and ENT","Type SE cable","Open wiring on insulators","Surface metal raceways only"],"a":0,"e":"In small theaters in non-fire-rated construction, Type NM and ENT may be used under the 518/520 allowance. [NEC 520.5, 518.4(B)]","r":"NEC 520.5, 518.4(B)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which financial statement reports a company's assets, liabilities, and equity at one specific date?","o":["Statement of retained earnings only","Statement of cash flows","Balance sheet","Income statement"],"a":2,"e":"The balance sheet is a snapshot at a point in time; the income statement and cash flow statement cover a period. [Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C. (statement components); SBA financial statement guidance]","r":"Rule 14-22.002, F.A.C."},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Why do contractors update a construction schedule regularly (monthly or as the contract requires)?","o":["to remove the milestones from the project schedule","to record actual progress against the plan and address delays","to reset all of the actual dates to match the planned ones","to raise the contract price whenever progress slips"],"a":1,"e":"FAR 52.236-15 requires the contractor to record actual progress on the chart as directed and submit annotated copies, and to improve progress if behind. [FAR 52.236-15]","r":"Schedule updates"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor with employees in Florida buys its workers' compensation coverage from an out-of-state policy. Under F.S. 440.10(1)(g) and 440.38(7), the policy must","o":["be issued only by a Florida-domiciled insurance company","use the rates of the state where the policy was written","be written on a claims-made form with a retro date","include a Florida endorsement using Florida class codes, rates and rules"],"a":3,"e":"Employers with Florida employees must have a Florida policy or endorsement using Florida classifications and rates. [F.S. 440.10(1)(g); 440.38(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(g); 440.38(7)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An induction motor has a synchronous speed of 1,800 rpm and a full-load rotor speed of 1,740 rpm. What is the percent slip?","o":["96.67 percent","3.45 percent","3.33 percent","1.67 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Slip = (1,800 - 1,740) / 1,800 = 3.33 percent. [Slip]","r":"Slip"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A written reminder, then a phone call, then a formal demand letter before a lien or collection action describes","o":["a method for allocating indirect overhead costs to each job in the shop","a way to compute the retainage owed on each progress billing statement","a hiring process used for adding new apprentices to the electrical crew","an escalating process for collecting overdue receivables"],"a":3,"e":"Collections typically escalate from friendly reminders to formal demand before legal steps such as a claim of lien. [Collections]","r":"Accounts receivable management (SBA/IRS Pub 334 context)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which cost would be subtracted to compute contribution margin?","o":["Annual rent on the contractor's shop and office","Materials and hourly labor for the job","The salary of the company's full-time estimator","Annual fees paid to the company's outside accountant"],"a":1,"e":"Only variable costs are subtracted; rent and salaries are fixed. [Contribution margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor misappropriates $18,500 received for a residential job in violation of F.S. 489.126. What is the penalty classification?","o":["First-degree misdemeanor","Third-degree felony","Second-degree felony","First-degree felony"],"a":1,"e":"489.126 grades the offense by amount: under $1,000 first-degree misdemeanor; $1,000 to under $20,000 third-degree felony; $20,000 to under $200,000 second-degree felony; $200,000 or more first-degree felony. [F.S. 489.126]","r":"F.S. 489.126"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A supplier adds a 6% metal surcharge to a $14,500 order of copper-based products. What is the total with the surcharge?","o":["$15,370.00","$15,425.53","$870.00","$23,200.00"],"a":0,"e":"Surcharge = $14,500 x 0.06 = $870.00; total = $15,370.00. [Purchasing math]","r":"Price adjustments"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"One horsepower is equal to","o":["550 watts","3,412 watts","746 watts","1,000 watts"],"a":2,"e":"1 hp = 746 W (550 ft-lb per second). 3,412 is BTU per kWh. [Conversions]","r":"Conversions"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contract requires substantial completion by August 14 and sets liquidated damages at $850 per calendar day. Substantial completion is reached September 2. What are the liquidated damages if no time extension was granted?","o":["$14,450","$17,000","$24,650","$16,150"],"a":3,"e":"August 14 to September 2 is 19 calendar days, and 19 x $850 = $16,150. Counting 17 days gives $14,450; counting 20 gives $17,000. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Rule 61G6-5.010, F.A.C., requires an applicant who will qualify a business organization to show responsibility for supervising:","o":["All operations of the business, including field work at all sites and finances","Only the job sites located in a single county of the state","Only the bookkeeping, payroll, and tax filings of the firm","Only the jobs for which the applicant personally pulls permits"],"a":0,"e":"The Statement of Authority rule requires the applicant to be responsible for supervising all operations, including all field work at all sites and general and job-specific financial matters. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.010]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.010"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For adult CPR, what compression rate and depth are recommended by current American Heart Association guidance?","o":["60 to 80 compressions a minute, about 1 inch deep","140 to 160 compressions a minute, about 3 inches deep","100 to 120 compressions a minute, at least 2 inches deep","90 compressions a minute, about 1.5 inches deep"],"a":2,"e":"AHA adult guidelines call for 100-120 compressions per minute at a depth of at least 2 inches (5 cm), allowing full chest recoil.","r":"AHA CPR guidelines (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A bid bond primarily guarantees that the low bidder will","o":["complete the work free of any defects for ten full years","pay all of the subcontractors on the project each month","carry workers' compensation insurance on every employee","sign the contract and furnish the final bonds if awarded"],"a":3,"e":"A bid bond protects the owner if the bidder withdraws or fails to sign and provide the final bonds. [Surety bonds]","r":"Surety bonds (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.1195(1), F.S., primary qualifying agents of a Part I business are:","o":["Responsible only for financial matters of the business entity","Responsible only for sites where they personally pulled the permits","Jointly and equally responsible for supervision, field work at all sites, and finances","Not responsible for any decision that the business owner makes"],"a":2,"e":"Primary qualifying agents share responsibility for operations, field work and finances. A joint agreement can designate one as the sole primary, with others becoming secondary. [F.S. 489.1195(1)(a), (2)]","r":"F.S. 489.1195(1)(a)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A small electrical contractor begins March with $18,000 cash. Expected March collections are $45,000; payroll $22,000, materials $14,000 and overhead $6,500 will be paid in March. What is the projected ending cash?","o":["$27,000","$20,500","$2,500","$105,500"],"a":1,"e":"Ending cash = opening + receipts - disbursements = 18,000 + 45,000 - (22,000 + 14,000 + 6,500) = 20,500. Leaving out opening cash or overhead gives a wrong total. [Cash flow forecasting]","r":"Cash flow forecast arithmetic"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which of these events requires retraining under the energy control standard?","o":["Each time a new calendar year begins, whether or not anything has changed","The employee has changed shifts but has the same job assignment and equipment","A new hazard from changes in machines or processes, or inspection findings of deviations","An employee has worked more than 40 hours in a single work week"],"a":2,"e":"Retraining is required for job assignment changes, new hazards from machines or processes, changes in procedures, and when inspections find deviations or inadequacies. [29 CFR 1910.147(c)(7)(iii)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.147(c)(7)(iii)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A surface-mounted nonmetallic cabinet is installed in a wet location on a masonry wall. Which statement about mounting airspace is correct?","o":["Airspace is required only for enclosures under 100 A","All enclosures need a 3-in. airspace","Metal enclosures need no airspace in a wet location","Nonmetallic enclosures may be mounted without the 1/4-in. airspace on a masonry surface"],"a":3,"e":"In damp or wet locations surface-type enclosures need at least 1/4 in. airspace between enclosure and wall, but nonmetallic enclosures may be installed without the airspace on concrete, masonry, tile or similar surfaces. [NEC 312.2]","r":"NEC 312.2"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A lender traditionally prefers a current ratio of what level, and what does a current ratio below 1.0 indicate?","o":["1.0 exactly; below 1.0 signals unusually high profits","0.5 or lower; below 1.0 signals a highly liquid business","5.0 or higher; below 1.0 signals excessive leverage","2.0 or higher; below 1.0 signals an illiquid business"],"a":3,"e":"The UNL guide says lenders have traditionally looked for 2.0 or greater and that below 1.0 signals an illiquid business. [UNL Extension G2009]","r":"Current ratio"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A job costs $212,000 in total direct cost and overhead. The contractor adds an 18% markup on cost. What is the selling price?","o":["$250,160","$258,536.59","$38,160","$288,320"],"a":0,"e":"Price = 212,000 x 1.18 = $250,160. Dividing by (1 - 0.18) would give a price that yields an 18% margin on price, not an 18% markup. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which conductor material is permitted for fire alarm system conductors?","o":["Copper or aluminum","Solid or stranded copper only","Copper-clad aluminum","Solid copper only; stranded is prohibited"],"a":1,"e":"Only copper conductors, solid or stranded, are permitted for fire alarm systems. [NEC 760.49(A) and (C)]","r":"NEC 2023 760.49"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1904.39, an employer must report a work-related fatality to OSHA within:","o":["48 hours","7 calendar days","24 hours","8 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Each fatality must be reported within eight hours of learning of it; in-patient hospitalizations, amputations and eye losses are reported within 24 hours. [29 CFR 1904.39(a)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.39(a)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.115(5), if a newly qualified applicant does not furnish the required insurance and financial evidence within 60 days of notification, the applicant must","o":["restart the application from scratch and pay a new fee","automatically receive a temporary license for one year","lose all exam scores permanently and retake both parts","request an extension from the department, granted for just cause"],"a":3,"e":"Section 489.115(5) requires the applicant to request an extension, which is granted for just cause. [F.S. 489.115(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(5)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An office manager is salaried and otherwise qualifies as exempt. The employer docks pay for a 3-hour personal absence in a week the manager worked. What is the main FLSA risk?","o":["Improper deductions can defeat the salary-basis test and the exemption","The employee becomes entitled to double pay","The employer must reclassify the entire company as nonexempt","None, because partial-day deductions are always allowed"],"a":0,"e":"Exempt status requires payment on a salary basis; deductions for partial-day personal absences are generally impermissible and can cost the exemption. [29 CFR 541.602]","r":"29 CFR 541.602"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What minimum property damage insurance amount does the Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board require of electrical contractors under Rule 61G6-5.008, F.A.C.?","o":["$25,000","$100,000","$1,000,000","$500,000"],"a":3,"e":"The rule requires bodily injury liability of $100,000 per person and $300,000 per occurrence, property damage of $500,000, and a minimum combined single limit of $800,000, including completed operations and products. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which cost is an equipment OWNERSHIP cost rather than an operating cost?","o":["Fuel","Depreciation","Lubricants and filters","Operator wages"],"a":1,"e":"Ownership costs (depreciation, interest, insurance, taxes) accrue whether or not the machine runs; fuel and maintenance vary with use. [Equipment cost recovery]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A covered builder's risk loss is $38,000 and the policy has a $5,000 deductible. What does the insurer pay?","o":["$38,000","$5,000","$33,000","$43,000"],"a":2,"e":"38,000 - 5,000 = $33,000. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A cash-method electrical contractor receives a customer's valid check on December 30 but does not deposit it until January 3. In which year is the income reported?","o":["The year the check was deposited at the bank (January), because that is when the funds were usable","The year the check cleared the bank, whichever date is later than receipt","The year the check was received (December), because of constructive receipt","The year the original invoice was mailed to the customer, regardless of receipt"],"a":2,"e":"Receipt of a valid check by the end of the tax year is constructive receipt in that year even if it is deposited later. [IRS Pub 334 (2023), Constructive receipt]","r":"IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A negotiated contract differs from a competitively bid contract because the price and terms are","o":["opened in public and awarded to the lowest bidder","set by the licensing board in a fixed schedule","fixed by the construction lien law","worked out directly between the owner and a selected contractor"],"a":3,"e":"Negotiated work is awarded by selection and discussion rather than by public price competition. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The same $216,000 of annual overhead is spread over 9,600 direct labor hours. What is the overhead rate per labor hour?","o":["$0.45 per hour","$45.00 per hour","$22.50 per hour","$11.25 per hour"],"a":2,"e":"216,000 / 9,600 = 22.50 per direct labor hour. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A bid is $250,000. If labor is 38% of the total price, how much of the price is labor?","o":["$155,000","$153,225.81","$95,000","$104,500"],"a":2,"e":"250,000 x 0.38 = $95,000. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor tells an owner in writing that the project is bonded when it is not, and the owner is harmed. This is","o":["a permitted marketing statement","a violation only if a lien is filed","a civil matter outside the board's authority","a ground for discipline under F.S. 489.129"],"a":3,"e":"Section 489.129(1)(k) lists false statements, including falsely indicating that the work is bonded or that workers' compensation and public liability insurance are provided. [F.S. 489.129(1)(k)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(1)(k)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A submittal is returned with the action 'Revise and Resubmit.' Can the contractor release the equipment for fabrication?","o":["Yes, because the architect has already seen the submittal","Yes, but only when the lead time on the item is long","No, it cannot be released until a revised submittal is accepted","Yes, provided the inspector raises no objection"],"a":2,"e":"'Revise and Resubmit' means the submittal does not yet conform, so the product should not be ordered or fabricated until it is accepted. [Typical submittal review actions]","r":"Typical submittal review actions"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A continuous-duty motor has a nameplate current of 21.5 A and a marked service factor of 1.15. A separate overload relay is used. What is the maximum overload trip setting permitted?","o":["24.7 A","26.9 A","30.1 A","21.5 A"],"a":1,"e":"With a service factor of 1.15 or more, the overload is set at no more than 125% of nameplate current: 21.5 x 1.25 = 26.9 A. The 24.7 A figure applies 115%, which is for motors with a service factor below 1.15. [NEC 430.32(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 430.32(A)(1)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A recorded claim of lien remains valid for how long unless an enforcement action is filed?","o":["1 year after it is recorded","90 days after recording","60 days after recording","2 years after final furnishing"],"a":0,"e":"713.22(1) states a lien does not continue longer than 1 year after recording unless an action to enforce it is commenced in that time. [F.S. 713.22(1)]","r":"F.S. 713.22"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A fast-growing electrical contractor reports record net income yet runs short of cash. Which cause is most likely?","o":["Depreciation expense recorded on the equipment fleet","Owner's equity that is larger than liabilities","Revenue recognized too early on the cash method","Receivables and retainage tied up in unpaid billings"],"a":3,"e":"Profit is not cash. Growth that is billed but not yet collected increases receivables and consumes operating cash. [Cash flow analysis]","r":"Cash flow analysis"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An action on a Florida public payment bond must generally be brought within how long after the claimant last furnished labor or materials?","o":["60 days","6 months","4 years","1 year"],"a":3,"e":"Section 255.05(10) sets a one-year limit (retainage actions have special timing rules). [F.S. 255.05(10)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(10)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A 1,000-ft reel of cable costs $430.00 and a 250-ft coil costs $118.00. How much more does each foot cost on the coil than on the reel, to the nearest tenth of a cent?","o":["$0.430","$0.042","$0.472","$0.062"],"a":1,"e":"Coil: $118.00 / 250 = $0.472 per ft; reel: $430.00 / 1,000 = $0.430 per ft; difference $0.042 per ft. Compare unit costs, not package prices. [Purchasing math]","r":"Unit price comparison"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"At most what percent of the cross-sectional area of a rigid conduit of the same trade size may the conductors occupy at a Class I seal fitting, unless the seal is identified for a higher percentage?","o":["25 percent","40 percent","53 percent","31 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors in a seal fitting are limited to 25% of the cross-sectional area of the same-size rigid metal conduit, unless the fitting is identified for a higher fill. [NEC 501.15(C)]","r":"NEC 501.15(C)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Where must the disconnecting means for an outdoor air-conditioning condensing unit be located?","o":["On or within sight of the unit, not on a removable panel","At the main service, regardless of the distance","Inside the building, next to the thermostat","Within 100 ft of the unit, with a placard"],"a":0,"e":"The disconnect must be readily accessible and within sight of the equipment; if it is mounted on the unit it may not be on a panel that must be removed for service. [NEC 440.14]","r":"NEC 440.14"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 713.23, a private-job lienor not in privity with the contractor (except laborers) must serve written notice to the contractor that it will look to the bond within how many days after starting work?","o":["45 days","5 days","20 days","75 days"],"a":0,"e":"The notice is due before starting or within 45 days after starting; a timely notice to owner under s. 713.06 also satisfies it. [F.S. 713.23(1)(c)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(1)(c)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign disconnect is installed on an exterior wall. NEC 110.26 working space requirements apply. What is the minimum clear width of working space in front of the disconnect?","o":["18 in., or the width of the equipment if less","24 in. in all cases","30 in., or the width of the equipment if greater","36 in. in all cases"],"a":2,"e":"110.26(A)(2) requires a working space at least 30 in. wide or the width of the equipment, whichever is greater. Depth is set by Table 110.26(A)(1), and headroom is at least 6-1/2 ft under 110.26(A)(3). [NEC 110.26(A)]","r":"NEC 110.26(A)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A job sells for $24,000 and costs $18,000. The markup on cost and gross margin on price are","o":["33.3% and 25%","25% and 33.3%","33.3% and 33.3%","75% and 133.3%"],"a":0,"e":"Profit = 6,000; markup = 6,000/18,000 = 33.3%; margin = 6,000/24,000 = 25%. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"When 40% of a contractor's receivables are over 90 days old, an underwriter will most likely","o":["add the old receivables back as if they were cash","ignore them since customer receivables are always good","discount or exclude the old receivables in adjusted working capital","reclassify the old receivables as part of owner's equity"],"a":2,"e":"Sureties and lenders commonly adjust balance sheets, excluding aged or disputed receivables, which lowers adjusted working capital and net worth. [Surety underwriting; Rule 14-22.002 adjusted net worth concept]","r":"Surety underwriting (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a circuit with nonlinear loads (for example variable frequency drives), the most accurate current reading comes from a","o":["true-RMS meter","average-responding meter","continuity tester","megohmmeter"],"a":0,"e":"Average-responding meters assume a sine wave and can read low on distorted waveforms. [Meters]","r":"Meters"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Nine 12 AWG THHN copper conductors (0.0133 sq in. each, Chapter 9 Table 5) are installed in EMT. What is the smallest EMT trade size permitted? (Table 4 EMT 40% areas: 1/2 in. = 0.122; 3/4 in. = 0.213.)","o":["3/4 in.","1/2 in.","1 in.","3/8 in."],"a":1,"e":"9 x 0.0133 = 0.1197 sq in., which is under the 0.122 sq in. 40% fill area of 1/2-in. EMT. [NEC Chapter 9, Table 1, Table 4, Table 5]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Table 1, Table 4, Table 5"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which type of CPA engagement gives the highest level of assurance on financial statements?","o":["Review","Bookkeeping package","Compilation","Audit"],"a":3,"e":"An audit gives the highest level of independent assurance; a review gives limited assurance; a compilation gives none. [Accountant engagement levels]","r":"Audit/review/compilation"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A public entity awards a $185,000 construction services contract. Under s. 255.078, F.S., the statutory 5 percent retainage cap in that section:","o":["Applies, because the contract exceeds $100,000 in total cost","Does not apply, because the contract cost is $200,000 or less","Applies only if the work is funded with federal money","Applies only to construction contracts over $1 million"],"a":1,"e":"Section 255.078 does not apply to construction services contracts with a total cost of $200,000 or less. A $185,000 contract is below that threshold. [F.S. 255.078(6)]","r":"F.S. 255.078(6)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An alternate in a set of bid documents is","o":["a backup bidder named in case the first bidder fails","a substitute inspector assigned by the building department","a payment term that replaces the retainage","an optional item of work the bidder prices separately as an add or deduct from the base bid"],"a":3,"e":"Alternates let the owner choose among scope options after seeing prices. They are added to or deducted from the base bid. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum ampere rating of a receptacle that supplies shore power to boats at a marina?","o":["15 amperes","20 amperes","60 amperes","30 amperes"],"a":3,"e":"Shore power receptacles must be rated not less than 30 A, and be of the locking and grounding type (pin and sleeve above 60 A). [NEC 555.33(A)]","r":"NEC 555.33(A)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 20-A branch circuit supplies several receptacles. Under Table 210.21(B)(2), what is the maximum total cord-and-plug connected load that may be placed on any one 20-A receptacle on this circuit?","o":["20 A","15 A","16 A","12 A"],"a":2,"e":"Table 210.21(B)(2) limits the cord-and-plug load on a receptacle to 80% of the circuit rating: 20 A circuit, 16 A maximum. 12 A is the figure for a 15-A circuit. [NEC 210.21(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 210.21(B)(2)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An equipment grounding conductor installed with the circuit conductors in a raceway:","o":["Is counted whenever it is larger than 10 AWG","Is counted when it is green","Is not counted as a current-carrying conductor for the adjustment factors","Is counted if it is insulated"],"a":2,"e":"Grounding and bonding conductors are not counted when applying the adjustment factors. [NEC 310.15(F)]","r":"NEC 310.15(F)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On the T568B wiring scheme, which conductor color is on pin 1 of an RJ-45 jack?","o":["White/green","Orange","White/orange","Blue"],"a":2,"e":"T568B pins 1-2 use the orange pair; T568A pins 1-2 use the green pair. [ANSI/TIA-568.2]","r":"T568B pinout (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Proceeds from a bank line of credit and owner distributions are both reported in which section of the statement of cash flows?","o":["Financing activities","Non-cash disclosures only","Operating activities","Investing activities"],"a":0,"e":"Borrowing, repaying debt, and owner contributions or distributions are financing activities. [Statement of cash flows]","r":"Statement of cash flows"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In the backward pass, the latest finish of an activity with several successors equals","o":["the largest latest start among its successors","its earliest finish","the smallest latest start among its successors","the project duration in every case"],"a":2,"e":"The backward pass takes the minimum of successors' late starts so that no successor is pushed past its own limit. [CPM backward pass; Pitt CPM notes]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor has revenue $4,200,000, net income $252,000, total assets $1,680,000, and equity $840,000 (use year-end figures). What are its net profit margin, asset turnover, and return on equity?","o":["6.0%, 0.40x, 30.0%","6.0%, 2.50x, 15.0%","15.0%, 2.50x, 30.0%","6.0%, 2.50x, 30.0%"],"a":3,"e":"Margin = $252,000/$4,200,000 = 6.0%. Asset turnover = $4,200,000/$1,680,000 = 2.50x. ROE = $252,000/$840,000 = 30.0% (equals margin x turnover x leverage 2.0 = 30.0%). [Profitability ratios, UNL Extension G2009]","r":"Return on equity"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A hermetic compressor with a rated-load current of 40 A cannot start on its 70 A protective device (175%). What is the highest rating the protective device may be raised to?","o":["90 A","80 A","100 A","125 A"],"a":0,"e":"When 175% will not carry the starting current, the rating may be increased but not above 225% of RLA: 40 x 2.25 = 90 A, a standard rating. 100 A would be 250%. [NEC 440.22(A) Exception]","r":"NEC 440.22(A) Exception"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in the space above a hung ceiling used for environmental air (plenum)?","o":["Type NM cable","Type MC cable without an overall nonmetallic covering","Type MC cable with a PVC jacket","Rigid PVC conduit"],"a":1,"e":"Plenum wiring methods are limited to metal raceways and cables such as MI, MC without overall nonmetallic covering, and AC, plus cable listed for plenum use. [NEC 300.22(C) (2026: 300.25(C))]","r":"NEC 2023 300.22(C)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sine wave has a peak value of 340 V. Its RMS (effective) value is about","o":["170 V","217 V","240 V","481 V"],"a":2,"e":"RMS = peak x 0.707 = 240 V. 0.637 gives the average, not the effective value. [RMS and peak]","r":"RMS and peak"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A feeder schedule lists: 'F-2: (2) sets of (3) 3/0 AWG Cu THWN + (1) 3 AWG Cu EGC in 2-1/2 in. conduit each set; 400 A breaker.' How many 3/0 AWG phase conductors are required to be pulled in total?","o":["3 conductors, because the two sets share their conductors","4 conductors, one for each phase plus one for the neutral","12 conductors, because each set needs its own neutral","6 conductors: three in each of the two conduits"],"a":3,"e":"Two parallel sets of three conductors gives 2 x 3 = 6 conductors of 3/0 AWG. Each set also carries its own EGC, and parallel conductors must be the same length, material, and size per NEC 310.10. [NEC 310.10 (parallel conductors); feeder schedule reading]","r":"NEC 310.10 (parallel conductors); feeder schedule reading"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Type AC cable must be secured within what distance of every outlet box and at what maximum interval between securements?","o":["12 in. of the box and every 4-1/2 ft","12 in. of the box and every 6 ft","24 in. of the box and every 10 ft","8 in. of the box and every 6 ft"],"a":0,"e":"AC cable must be secured within 12 in. of every box, cabinet or fitting and at intervals not exceeding 4-1/2 ft. [NEC 320.30]","r":"NEC 320.30"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 2-pole generator must turn at what speed to produce 60 Hz?","o":["3,600 rpm","1,800 rpm","7,200 rpm","1,200 rpm"],"a":0,"e":"N = 120 x f / P = 120 x 60 / 2 = 3,600 rpm. [Generators]","r":"Generators"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Inside a building, transmitting-station conductors must be separated from conductors of electric light, power, or signaling circuits by at least:","o":["25 mm (1 in.)","50 mm (2 in.)","300 mm (12 in.)","100 mm (4 in.)"],"a":3,"e":"Interior transmitting-station conductors must be 4 in. from other light, power, or signaling conductors unless an exception such as a firmly fixed nonconductor applies. [NEC 810.70]","r":"NEC 2023 810.70"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A certificate of insurance submitted to the Electrical Contractors' Licensing Board must show which of these?","o":["A binder number and the contractor's home address as certificate holder","A policy number and the local building department as certificate holder","A letter from the agent in place of an actual certificate","A policy number (not a binder) and the department's ECLB as certificate holder"],"a":3,"e":"The certificate must be prepared by an insurance agency and show the exact legal name of the insured, insurer, policy number (binders not accepted), effective and expiration dates, license number, DBPR's ECLB as certificate holder and a 30-day cancellation notice. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008(1), (4)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's revoked certificate is to be reissued. Under Rule 61G6-10.006(3)(b), F.A.C., the contractor must:","o":["Wait 90 days and pay a $25 reinstatement fee","Submit a letter of reprimand and nothing else to the board","Complete 11 hours of continuing education and nothing else","Pass the current licensing exam and meet all other requirements"],"a":3,"e":"Relicensing after revocation requires proof that all final-order requirements, including fines, are met, and for a certificate the contractor must pass the current exam and satisfy all other requirements. [F.A.C. 61G6-10.006(3)]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-10.006(3)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"For a private project, the Florida payment bond statute (F.S. 713.23) requires the bond amount to be at least","o":["10 percent of the contract price","the original contract price","50 percent of the contract price","the amount of the first draw"],"a":1,"e":"A contractor's payment bond under s. 713.23 must be at least the original contract price, with a copy attached to the recorded notice of commencement. [F.S. 713.23(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(1)(a)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A plan originally drawn at 1/4 in. = 1 ft-0 in. on a 24 x 36 sheet has been reduced to a half-size 11 x 17 print (50 percent). You measure a conduit run at 2.5 in. on the half-size print. What is the real length, ignoring the printed scale label?","o":["10 ft","30 ft","20 ft","40 ft"],"a":2,"e":"At 50 percent, 1/4 in. becomes 1/8 in. = 1 ft, so each printed inch is 8 ft: 2.5 x 8 = 20 ft. Measuring with the original 4 ft per inch would cut the length in half. [Scale calculation; reduced prints]","r":"Scale calculation; reduced prints"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 448.095, F.S., which private employers must use E-Verify for new hires?","o":["Private employers with 50 or more employees performing services in Florida","Private employers with 10 or more employees performing services in Florida","Only employers that hold contracts with state or local agencies","Employers with 25 or more employees in Florida"],"a":3,"e":"Since July 1, 2023, private employers with 25 or more employees performing services in Florida must verify new hires through E-Verify. [F.S. 448.095]","r":"F.S. 448.095"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor receives a Chapter 558 notice of claim from a homeowner. Within how many days of service must the contractor serve a written response with one of the options in s. 558.004(5)?","o":["15 days","30 days","75 days","45 days"],"a":3,"e":"The served party must respond within 45 days of service. The 75-day period applies only when the claimant is an association representing more than 20 parcels. [F.S. 558.004(5)]","r":"F.S. 558.004(5)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which AWG size is listed with 66,360 circular mils in NEC Chapter 9, Table 8?","o":["3 AWG","4 AWG","1 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Chapter 9 Table 8 lists 2 AWG as 66,360 CM; 4 AWG is 41,740; 1 AWG is 83,690. [NEC Chapter 9, Table 8]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Table 8"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A surety underwriter wants a contractor's WIP schedule, not just the year-end balance sheet. The main reason is that the WIP schedule","o":["It lists every employee's hourly pay rate and overtime","It records the contractor's tool and truck inventory","It replaces the need to submit any tax returns","It shows each job's contract, cost, billings, and backlog"],"a":3,"e":"A WIP schedule gives job-level profit fade, over/under billing, and backlog data that the balance sheet totals do not reveal. [Withum; SBA surety program]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Venue for enforcing a fine imposed by the Construction Industry Licensing Board under s. 489.129(6)(b), F.S., lies in which county?","o":["Leon County","Miami-Dade County","Orange County","Duval County"],"a":3,"e":"Venue for enforcement of fines is in Duval County, and the board may contract with private businesses or attorneys for collection on a contingent-fee basis. [F.S. 489.129(6)(b)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(6)(b)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which fiber type has a nominal 9-micron core and is used for long-distance links?","o":["OM1 multimode","OM2 multimode","OM4 multimode","Single-mode fiber (OS2)"],"a":3,"e":"Single-mode fiber has a very small core (~9 micron) and is used for long-haul distances; multimode cores are 50 or 62.5 micron. [TIA-568.3]","r":"TIA-568.3 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Within how many hours must an employer report a work-related fatality to OSHA?","o":["24 hours","48 hours","8 hours","7 days"],"a":2,"e":"Employers must report each work-related fatality within 8 hours; in-patient hospitalizations, amputations and eye losses must be reported within 24 hours. [29 CFR 1904.39(a)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.39(a)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 489.115(5), an applicant seeking a workers' compensation exemption submits an affidavit stating the applicant will obtain coverage within what time after the certificate is issued?","o":["30 days","1 year","10 days","60 days"],"a":0,"e":"The statute requires an affidavit that workers' compensation coverage will be obtained within 30 days after initial issuance. [F.S. 489.115(5)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(5)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A $45,000 service truck (light general-purpose truck, 5-year MACRS property) is placed in service under the half-year convention, no special depreciation or Section 179. First-year MACRS depreciation (20% table rate) is","o":["$9,000","$18,000","$14,400","$6,429"],"a":0,"e":"Table rate year 1 = 20% (200% DB x 1/5 x half-year); 45,000 x 20% = 9,000. A full-year 40% would ignore the half-year convention. [IRS Form 4562 instructions; Pub 946 tables]","r":"IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Wiring methods installed under metal-corrugated sheet roof decking must be installed to maintain what separation from the lowest surface of the roof decking?","o":["3/4 in.","1 in.","1-1/2 in.","3 in."],"a":2,"e":"A 1-1/2 in. separation is required from the lowest surface of the roof decking to the top of the cable or raceway, with exceptions such as RMC/IMC with listed fittings. [NEC 300.4(E)]","r":"NEC 300.4(E)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which county's discretionary surtax rate applies to a taxable sale of tangible personal property?","o":["The rate of the county where the seller's headquarters is located","The rate of the county where the buyer is domiciled for voting","The rate of the county where the property is delivered","The highest surtax rate in the state"],"a":2,"e":"A transaction is deemed to occur in the county where the property is delivered, and the surtax is computed at that county's rate. [F.S. 212.054(2)(a), (3)(a)]","r":"F.S. 212.054(2)(a), (3)(a)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer that paid all state unemployment contributions on time and is in a state with no federal credit reduction pays what net FUTA rate?","o":["0.6%","6.0%","5.4%","1.45%"],"a":0,"e":"The credit against FUTA is up to 5.4% of the 6.0% rate, leaving 0.6% on the first $7,000 of wages. 5.4% is the credit, not the net tax. [Form 940 instructions]","r":"Form 940 instructions"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A delay caused solely by the contractor's own poor planning or crew shortages is generally classified as","o":["non-excusable","excusable and non-compensable","excusable and compensable","a differing site condition"],"a":0,"e":"A non-excusable delay is the contractor's responsibility: it gives no extra time or money and can support liquidated damages. [Delay classification]","r":"Delay claims"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What must the written certification of a PPE hazard assessment under 29 CFR 1910.132(d)(2) identify?","o":["The names of all employees and the shoe size of each employee wearing PPE","The brand and model of each item purchased and the date it was bought","The cost, the supplier and the warranty period of each PPE item","The workplace evaluated, the person certifying, and the date(s) of the assessment"],"a":3,"e":"1910.132(d)(2) requires the certification to state the workplace evaluated, the person certifying the evaluation, and the date(s) of the assessment.","r":"29 CFR 1910.132(d)(2)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Overhead service conductors of 120/240 V cross a residential driveway. What is the minimum clearance from final grade?","o":["12 ft","18 ft","15 ft","10 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Overhead service conductors need 12 ft over residential property and driveways (voltage not exceeding 300 V to ground). [NEC 230.24(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 230.24(B)(2)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A conduit takeoff shows 187 ft of 3/4-in. EMT. EMT comes in 10-ft lengths. Ignoring waste and fittings, how many sticks must be ordered to cover the run?","o":["18 sticks","19 sticks","22 sticks","16 sticks"],"a":1,"e":"187 / 10 = 18.7, which rounds up to 19 sticks because you cannot buy part of a stick. Rounding down comes up short. [Quantity takeoff; NEC 358.30 for support spacing]","r":"Quantity takeoff; NEC 358.30 for support spacing"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A progress payment application is $84,000, and the contract holds 10% retainage. What is the net payment due this period?","o":["$8,400","$84,000","$79,800","$75,600"],"a":3,"e":"Retainage = 8,400; net = 84,000 - 8,400 = $75,600. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Type UF cable may NOT be used:","o":["As service-entrance cable","For direct burial in the earth","In wet locations with proper listing","For underground feeders to a detached garage"],"a":0,"e":"UF cable is not permitted as service-entrance cable; it is permitted for underground installation, including direct burial. [NEC 340.12]","r":"NEC 340.12"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A business continues operating after being served with a stop-work order under s. 440.107. What additional penalty applies?","o":["$1,000 per day for each day of operation in violation of the order","No additional penalty if the employee count is under 10","$100 per employee per day","$5,000 flat fee"],"a":0,"e":"The Division assesses $1,000 per day against an employer that conducts business operations in violation of a stop-work order. [F.S. 440.107]","r":"F.S. 440.107"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 60-ft pole sign is to be installed with a drilled concrete pier. Before drilling, what must the contractor do concerning underground utilities in Florida?","o":["Notify the electric utility only on the same day","Rely on the property survey instead of a locate request","Notify Sunshine 811 at least 2 full business days before digging","Notify only for trenches that are deeper than 10 ft"],"a":2,"e":"F.S. 556.105 requires notice at least 2 full business days before beginning excavation outside state waters, and the locate information is valid for 30 days. [F.S. 556.105]","r":"F.S. 556.105"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Equipment cost recovery for a contractor means","o":["recovering stolen equipment by filing a claim with the insurance company","selling used equipment to repay the loan that financed it","charging jobs an hourly or daily rate that recovers ownership and operating costs","depreciating every piece of equipment over exactly 39 years"],"a":2,"e":"An internal equipment rate spreads depreciation, interest, insurance, fuel and maintenance over expected usage so jobs carry their share. [Equipment cost recovery]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Temporary lamps for general illumination on a job site must be protected from accidental contact or breakage by:","o":["A luminaire or lampholder with a guard","Orange cord covers on the cord","Cable ties around the lamp","A caution tag on the lamp"],"a":0,"e":"Temporary lamps for general illumination must be protected from accidental contact or breakage by a suitable luminaire or lampholder with a guard. [NEC 590.4(F)]","r":"NEC 590.4(F) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"The contractor must give the owner a final payment affidavit stating that all lienors who served notice have been paid, or listing unpaid lienors, at least how many days before instituting an action to enforce a lien?","o":["30 days","15 days","5 days","1 day"],"a":2,"e":"713.06(3)(d) requires delivery of the affidavit at least 5 days before starting a lien enforcement action. [F.S. 713.06(3)]","r":"F.S. 713.06"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For a local government construction contract under $10 million, F.S. 218.735 requires the single list of items needed to complete the work (the final completion list) to be developed within how many calendar days after substantial completion?","o":["10 calendar days","60 calendar days","90 calendar days","30 calendar days"],"a":3,"e":"For projects under $10 million the list is developed within 30 days after substantial completion (or beneficial occupancy/use); for $10 million or more it can be extended by contract to 60 days. [F.S. 218.735]","r":"F.S. 218.735"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.119(5)(c), F.S., if a vehicle displays a contractor's name or text suggesting contracting, the contractor's number must be:","o":["Displayed only on the vehicle's registration paperwork on file","Displayed only if the vehicle is leased rather than owned","Clearly and legibly displayed on the vehicle with that name or text","Left off the vehicle to protect the contractor's privacy"],"a":2,"e":"Section 489.119(5)(c) requires the number to be clearly and legibly displayed with any contractor name or contracting artwork on a vehicle. Local governments may also require local license numbers. [F.S. 489.119(5)(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.119(5)(c)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under OSHA's recordkeeping rule, an employer must enter a recordable injury or illness on the OSHA 300 Log within how many calendar days of receiving information that a recordable case occurred?","o":["24 hours","7 calendar days","30 calendar days","14 calendar days"],"a":1,"e":"Section 1904.29(b)(3) requires entry on the 300 Log and the 301 incident report (or equivalent) within seven calendar days of receiving the information. [29 CFR 1904.29(b)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.29(b)(3)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 240 V series circuit has three loads. Two have voltage drops of 85 V and 62 V. What is the drop across the third?","o":["80 V","93 V","147 V","387 V"],"a":1,"e":"By KVL the drops sum to the source: 240 - 85 - 62 = 93 V. [Kirchhoff's voltage law]","r":"Kirchhoff's voltage law"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A dwelling kitchen has one unbroken 14-ft run of wall countertop (no range or sink in the run). No point along the wall line may be more than 24 in. from a receptacle. What is the minimum number of receptacle outlets for that run?","o":["5","7","3","4"],"a":3,"e":"Each receptacle can serve 48 in. of wall line (24 in. each way). The run is 14 x 12 = 168 in., and 168 / 48 = 3.5, which rounds up to 4 outlets. [NEC 210.52(C)(1)]","r":"NEC 210.52(C)(1)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Vendor A quotes $4,200 delivered. Vendor B quotes $3,950 plus $380 freight. Which choice is cheaper and by how much?","o":["Vendor B, cheaper by $250","Vendor A, cheaper by $130","Vendor B, cheaper by $130","Vendor A, cheaper by $630"],"a":1,"e":"Vendor B's total is $3,950 + $380 = $4,330, versus $4,200 for Vendor A, a difference of $130. Compare delivered cost, not item price. [Purchasing practice]","r":"Bid comparison"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Subcontractor invoices for trenching on a specific project are classified as","o":["part of the office overhead pool allocated to every job","a direct subcontract cost of that project","a depreciation expense recorded on the contractor's equipment","an owner's draw taken out of the business's profits"],"a":1,"e":"Subcontract costs for one project are directly traceable to it. [Direct vs indirect costs]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 125-volt, 20-ampere receptacle is accessible to the general public at a county fair midway. What protection is required?","o":["AFCI protection","GFCI protection for personnel","Ground-fault protection of equipment at 100 mA","A 30 mA GFPE only"],"a":1,"e":"All 125-volt, single-phase, 15-, 20- and 30-ampere receptacles in areas accessible to the public at carnivals require GFCI protection. [NEC 525.23]","r":"NEC 525.23"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A receptacle outlet is being installed for servicing a rooftop air-conditioning unit. Which statement is correct under 210.63?","o":["It may be connected to the load side of the unit's disconnect so it is de-energized when serviced","It must be a 125-V, 15- or 20-A outlet at an accessible location on the same level and within 25 ft of the equipment, and not on the load side of the equipment disconnect","It is required only for units over 5 tons","It must be installed inside the unit's control compartment"],"a":1,"e":"The service receptacle must be on the same level and within 25 ft of the HVAC equipment and may not be connected to the load side of the equipment disconnecting means. [NEC 210.63]","r":"NEC 210.63"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor buys three drills on a single invoice for a total of $2,300. Under the de minimis safe harbor election for taxpayers without an applicable financial statement, the contractor may deduct the costs if each item or invoice does not exceed:","o":["$10,000","$500","$2,500","$5,000"],"a":2,"e":"Taxpayers without an applicable financial statement may expense items costing up to $2,500 per invoice or item under Reg. 1.263(a)-1(f), if the election is made. [IRS Pub 535 / Reg. 1.263(a)-1(f)]","r":"Treas. Reg. 1.263(a)-1(f)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A feeder is protected by a 400 A overcurrent device. 10-ft feeder tap conductors leave the switchboard enclosure in a field installation and terminate in a 100 A panelboard. At minimum, the tap conductors must have an ampacity of:","o":["100 A, since it must cover the 100 A equipment rating and exceeds one-tenth (40 A) of the feeder device","400 A, equal to the feeder device","40 A, one-tenth of the feeder device only","133 A, one-third of the feeder device"],"a":0,"e":"The 10-ft tap needs ampacity at least equal to the calculated load and the rating of the equipment or OCPD it terminates in (100 A), and, because it leaves the enclosure, at least 1/10 of the feeder OCPD (40 A). The larger controls: 100 A. [NEC 240.21(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 240.21(B)(1)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A task requires 960 labor hours and must be finished in 10 working days of 8 hours. What is the minimum crew size?","o":["10 workers","12 workers","96 workers","16 workers"],"a":1,"e":"Crew size = 960 / (10 x 8) = 12.0, so 12 workers. Using hours per day without dividing by 8 gives 96. [Crew-day calculation]","r":"Crew scheduling (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Metal raceways in wet locations above grade, such as rooftop conduit exposed to rain, are treated how?","o":["The interior of the raceway is considered a wet location","The location class depends on the number of conductors","Only the exterior is considered wet","The interior is considered a dry location"],"a":0,"e":"The interior of raceways installed in wet locations above grade is considered a wet location, so insulated conductors there must be listed for wet locations. [NEC 300.9]","r":"NEC 300.9"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Depreciation of an asset begins when it is","o":["ordered from the supplier and a purchase order is issued","fully paid for in cash, with no financing outstanding","placed in service (ready and available for its specific use)","first insured under the contractor's business insurance policy"],"a":2,"e":"Pub 946 states property is placed in service when it is ready and available for a specific use, even if not yet used. [IRS Pub 946 (2025)]","r":"IRS Pub 946 (2025)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor adds a 25% markup to a $1,000 job cost. The selling price and the gross margin percentage are","o":["$1,250 and 25%","$1,333 and 25%","$1,200 and 20%","$1,250 and 20%"],"a":3,"e":"Price = 1,000 x 1.25 = 1,250; margin = (1,250 - 1,000)/1,250 = 20%. Markup is on cost, margin on price. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Practicing beyond the scope of a certification or registration is a ground for discipline under which paragraph of s. 489.533(1), F.S.?","o":["Paragraph (a)","Paragraph (e)","Paragraph (s)","Paragraph (n)"],"a":2,"e":"Paragraph (1)(s) covers practicing beyond the scope of the certification or registration. Rule 61G6-10.002 sets different penalty ranges for geographical and occupational scope violations. [F.S. 489.533(1)(s)]","r":"F.S. 489.533(1)(s)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A crew member endures repeated offensive jokes about her sex that create an abusive environment. This is called:","o":["A bona fide occupational qualification","A hostile work environment","Reverse discrimination","Disparate impact"],"a":1,"e":"Unwelcome conduct based on a protected trait that is severe or pervasive enough to alter conditions of employment is a hostile work environment. [29 CFR 1604.11(a)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1604.11(a)(3)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the inductive reactance of a 0.05 H coil at 60 Hz?","o":["18.85 ohms","6.0 ohms","0.053 ohms","3.0 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"XL = 2 x pi x f x L = 6.2832 x 60 x 0.05 = 18.85 ohms. [Inductive reactance]","r":"Inductive reactance"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On a plan drawn at 1/8 inch = 1 foot, a feeder route measures 6 3/4 inches with a scale rule. What is the actual run length?","o":["54 feet","27 feet","81 feet","108 feet"],"a":0,"e":"At 1/8 inch per foot, 1 inch = 8 feet; 6.75 x 8 = 54 feet. A 1/4-inch scale would give 27 feet. [Estimating practice, takeoff]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor requires its nonexempt employees to attend a safety training session during normal working hours. The time is:","o":["Payable only at the minimum wage","Not compensable if the training is under 2 hours","Unpaid because it benefits the employee","Hours worked that must be paid"],"a":3,"e":"Training is not compensable only if all four tests are met: outside regular hours, voluntary, not job-related, and no other work performed. Required in-hours training counts. [29 CFR 785.27-.32]","r":"29 CFR 785.27"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An applicant seeking a workers' compensation exemption under s. 440.05, F.S., must attest that the exemption will be obtained within how many days after initial issuance of the license?","o":["10 days","60 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":2,"e":"Section 489.115(5)(d) requires applicants seeking an exemption to attest they will obtain it within 30 days after initial issuance. [F.S. 489.115(5)(d)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(5)(d)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A multiwire branch circuit supplied from a 120/240-V single-phase panelboard is being used to serve a 240-V water pump and a 120-V receptacle load on the same three-wire circuit. What does 210.4(C) say?","o":["It is permitted only if the circuit is protected by a GFCI breaker","It is permitted if the neutral is one size larger than the ungrounded conductors","It is not permitted, because a multiwire branch circuit may supply only line-to-neutral loads unless it supplies a single piece of utilization equipment or all ungrounded conductors are opened together","It is permitted, because the neutral carries only the unbalanced current"],"a":2,"e":"A multiwire branch circuit is limited to line-to-neutral loads (with exceptions for a single utilization device or where all ungrounded conductors are opened simultaneously by the OCPD). Mixing a 240-V pump and 120-V receptacles fits neither exception. [NEC 210.4(C)]","r":"NEC 210.4(C)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"'Blind receiving' means that the person receiving materials:","o":["Counts and records what arrives without seeing the quantities on the PO","Receives goods only from vendors that the property owner has pre-approved","Signs for sealed packages without opening them or counting the contents","Receives deliveries only after normal business hours when the yard is closed"],"a":0,"e":"Blind receiving forces an independent count, preventing a receiver from simply confirming the ordered quantity. [Purchasing control practice]","r":"Receiving control practice"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A plug-in GFCI tester shows a lit pattern indicating 'open ground'. What does this mean?","o":["The neutral conductor is open between the receptacle and the panel","The hot and neutral conductors are reversed at the receptacle","The equipment grounding conductor is not connected to the receptacle's ground terminal","The circuit is properly wired and has a good equipment ground"],"a":2,"e":"An open ground indicates the receptacle lacks a continuous equipment grounding path. The AEGCP uses similar tests. [29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(D)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)(iii)(D)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 10 ohm resistor is in series with a parallel pair of 20 ohm and 30 ohm resistors. What is the total resistance?","o":["15 ohms","12 ohms","60 ohms","22 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"The parallel pair is 20 x 30 / 50 = 12 ohms; add the series 10 ohms to get 22 ohms. [Combination circuits]","r":"Combination circuits"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Offering a customer 'due upon receipt' terms instead of 'net 60' will most likely","o":["shorten the time to cash and reduce DSO","lengthen the collection period","eliminate the need for lien waivers","lower the contractor's receivables turnover"],"a":0,"e":"Shorter terms pull cash in sooner; net 60 lets the customer hold the money twice as long. [Payment terms]","r":"Accounts receivable management (SBA/IRS Pub 334 context)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Where must the sign disconnect be located relative to the sign it controls?","o":["Anywhere within 500 ft of the sign it controls, if the location is labeled","Only in the building's main electrical room, regardless of how far away it is","Only at the service equipment on the outside wall of the building","Within sight of the sign, or at its point of entry, or within sight of its controller"],"a":3,"e":"600.6(A) allows the disconnect at the point of entry to the sign enclosure, within sight of the sign, or within sight of the controller, and if remote it must be accessible and marked. [NEC 600.6(A)]","r":"NEC 600.6(A)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 100 kW load operates at 0.80 lagging power factor. How many kVAR of capacitors are needed to raise the power factor to 0.95?","o":["42.1 kVAR","15.0 kVAR","32.9 kVAR","75.0 kVAR"],"a":0,"e":"Q before = 100 x tan(acos 0.80) = 75.0; after = 100 x tan(acos 0.95) = 32.9; capacitors supply the difference = 42.1 kVAR. [Power factor correction]","r":"Power factor correction"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Florida employers must report each newly hired or rehired employee to the Florida New Hire Reporting Center within how many days of the hire?","o":["3 days","10 days","20 days","45 days"],"a":2,"e":"Employers must report new hires within 20 calendar days of the date of hire to support child-support enforcement and fraud prevention. [F.S. 409.2576]","r":"F.S. 409.2576"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An original contract sum is $212,000. Change orders are approved for +$8,400, -$2,150 and +$5,725. What is the current contract sum?","o":["$226,125","$220,550","$223,975","$228,275"],"a":2,"e":"$212,000 + $8,400 = $220,400; - $2,150 = $218,250; + $5,725 = $223,975. Treating the deduct as an addition gives $226,125. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Florida penalizes an erroneous, incomplete, or insufficient report at what amount?","o":["$25 for each 30 days of lateness, no cap on total","Greater of $50 or 10% of tax due, capped at $300 per report","A flat $500 penalty for each report found erroneous","2% of the tax due, with no maximum penalty amount"],"a":1,"e":"Section 443.141 sets this penalty at the greater of $50 or 10% of any tax due, but not more than $300 per report. [F.S. 443.141]","r":"F.S. 443.141"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 713.08(4)(c), F.S., a claim of lien must be served on the owner at what time?","o":["Before recording or within 15 days after recording","At least 5 days before the claim is recorded","Within 45 days after the claim is recorded","At the time the owner makes final payment"],"a":0,"e":"Service on the owner must occur before recording or within 15 days after, and late or missing service makes the lien voidable only to the extent it prejudiced someone entitled to rely on it. [F.S. 713.08(4)(c)]","r":"F.S. 713.08(4)(c)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under the ADA, an employer must provide a reasonable accommodation to a qualified applicant or employee with a disability unless doing so would cause:","o":["Any cost over $100","Minor inconvenience to coworkers","An undue hardship","A change in the employee's pay"],"a":2,"e":"Employers must accommodate known limitations of qualified individuals unless the accommodation imposes significant difficulty or expense (undue hardship). [42 U.S.C. 12112(b)(5)(A)]","r":"42 U.S.C. 12112(b)(5)(A)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In chapter 713, F.S., a 'sub-subcontractor' is a person who contracts with:","o":["The owner of the property that is being improved","The lender that is financing the improvement","The building department that issued the permit","A subcontractor, for part of the subcontractor's work"],"a":3,"e":"A subcontractor contracts with the contractor; a sub-subcontractor contracts with a subcontractor for any part of its contract. [F.S. 713.01]","r":"F.S. 713.01"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An electrical contractor's last day of work on a job (final furnishing) was March 03, 2026. What is the last day to record a claim of lien?","o":["June 01, 2026","May 02, 2026","April 17, 2026","July 01, 2026"],"a":0,"e":"Mar 03 + 90 days = Jun 01, 2026. [F.S. 713.08(5)]","r":"F.S. 713.08"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A vendor ships 70 of 100 ordered fittings and notes the remaining 30 are backordered. Proper PO control keeps the PO:","o":["Open for the 30-unit balance until it is received or cancelled","Converted into a credit memo for the value of the unshipped 30 units","Cancelled automatically, with a brand new PO opened for the balance","Closed, with the unshipped balance of 30 units simply forgotten"],"a":0,"e":"An open PO balance tracks commitments and prompts follow-up; closing it prematurely hides the pending liability. [Purchasing control practice]","r":"PO control practice"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 45 kVA, 480-V delta to 208Y/120-V transformer is a separately derived system with 1 AWG copper secondary phase conductors. What is the minimum size copper grounding electrode conductor for this system (Table 250.66)?","o":["8 AWG","6 AWG","4 AWG","2 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"The GEC for a separately derived system is sized from Table 250.66 using the largest derived ungrounded conductor. Secondary current is about 125 A, using 1 AWG copper; for 1 AWG the table requires 6 AWG copper. [NEC 250.30(A), Table 250.66]","r":"NEC 250.30(A), Table 250.66"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A one-line diagram note says the main switchboard is 'rated 42,000 A interrupting capacity at 480 V' and the engineer's fault study shows 51,000 A available at the load side of the main. What is the problem?","o":["The switchboard must be derated by 20 percent to account for the fault current","Nothing, because only the bus bracing rating matters here","The interrupting rating is below the available fault current, so the equipment does not comply","The fault study must be reduced, because the contribution of motors adds to the current"],"a":2,"e":"NEC 110.9 requires equipment intended to interrupt current at fault levels to have an interrupting rating at least equal to the available fault current. A 42 kA rating cannot be used at 51 kA unless a properly series-rated or higher-rated device is used where permitted. [NEC 110.9]","r":"NEC 110.9"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 15 horsepower motor has an output rating of approximately how many watts (1 hp = 746 W)?","o":["11,025 W","11,190 W","11,460 W","15,000 W"],"a":1,"e":"15 x 746 = 11,190 W of mechanical output. [Horsepower]","r":"Horsepower"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Which claimant is NOT qualified to recover from the fund under s. 489.141(2), F.S.?","o":["A claimant whose contractor held no valid license when the contract was made","A homeowner who obtained a final judgment against the licensee","A homeowner who exhausted all available bonds and insurance","A homeowner who filed the claim within one year of the judgment"],"a":0,"e":"Disqualifications include the spouse of the licensee, a licensee who acted as the contractor, a contract on property the licensee owned, a contractor without a valid license when contracting, and a separate business relationship with the licensee. [F.S. 489.141(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.141(2)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A stockroom's book inventory is $18,400 but the physical count totals $17,296. What is the shrinkage as a percentage of book value?","o":["6.0%","6.4%","94.0%","1.1%"],"a":0,"e":"Shrinkage = $18,400 - $17,296 = $1,104; / $18,400 = 6.0%. [Inventory control]","r":"Shrinkage"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 558.004(1)(c), F.S., a homeowner should try to serve a Chapter 558 notice within how many days of discovering a defect, and what happens if the homeowner is later?","o":["15 days; missing it bars the action","60 days; missing it voids the warranty","30 days; missing it doubles the fee","15 days; missing this does not bar an action"],"a":3,"e":"The claimant should attempt service within 15 days of discovery, but failing to do so does not bar an action. [F.S. 558.004(1)(c)]","r":"F.S. 558.004(1)(c)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A fire alarm control panel in a commercial building is fed by a 20-ampere branch circuit. Which protection must NOT be used on that circuit?","o":["A circuit breaker rated 20 amperes","Ground-fault circuit-interrupter or arc-fault circuit-interrupter protection","A red-identified disconnecting means","A lock on the breaker in the ON position"],"a":1,"e":"The fire alarm branch circuit shall not be supplied through GFCIs or AFCIs, shall supply no other loads, and the disconnect may be secured in the ON position. [NEC 760.121(B)]","r":"NEC 2023 760.121(B)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a commercial garage repair area, which receptacles must have GFCI protection for personnel?","o":["Only receptacles within 6 ft of a sink","Only outdoor receptacles","Only 250-volt receptacles","All 125-V, 15- and 20-A receptacles used with tools or lights"],"a":3,"e":"GFCI protection is required for all 125-volt, 15- and 20-ampere receptacles in areas where electrical diagnostic equipment, electrical hand tools or portable lighting equipment will be used. [NEC 511.12]","r":"NEC 511.12"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A nonmotor-generator arc welder has a rated primary current of 40 A and a 50% duty cycle (Table 630.11(A) multiplier 0.71). What is the minimum supply conductor ampacity?","o":["28.4 A","40.0 A","56.3 A","20.0 A"],"a":0,"e":"I x multiplier = 40 x 0.71 = 28.4 A. [NEC 630.11(A), Table 630.11(A)]","r":"NEC 630.11(A), Table 630.11(A)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"For construction employers, F.S. 440.02 treats an employer as covered by workers' compensation when it has","o":["four or more employees","ten or more employees","one or more employees","at least $100,000 in payroll"],"a":2,"e":"Non-construction private employers are covered at four or more employees; construction employers at one or more. [F.S. 440.02]","r":"F.S. 440.02"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which treatment is considered medical treatment (not first aid) for OSHA recording purposes?","o":["Drinking fluids for heat stress","A tetanus shot","Cleaning a surface wound","Sutures or staples to close a wound"],"a":3,"e":"Sutures, staples, prescription medications and rigid supports are medical treatment; tetanus immunization, cleaning a wound and hot or cold therapy are first aid. [29 CFR 1904.7(b)(5)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.7(b)(5)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 applies to private employers with at least how many employees?","o":["50","5","20","15"],"a":3,"e":"Title VII covers private employers, unions and agencies with 15 or more employees; the ADEA requires 20. [42 U.S.C. 2000e(b)]","r":"42 U.S.C. 2000e(b)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On an electrical drawing, the abbreviation 'NIC' beside a piece of equipment means the equipment is:","o":["Not in the contract: shown for coordination but not provided by this contractor","Normally installed by the electrical contractor under an allowance","Nonmetallic, intended for installation in corrosive conditions","Neutral isolated and then connected to the service ground"],"a":0,"e":"NIC stands for 'not in contract.' The item is shown for reference or for others to furnish, so the electrical contractor does not price it unless the notes say to make connections. [Drawing practice: abbreviations]","r":"Drawing practice: abbreviations"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A job has a contract price of $360,000 and earned revenue to date of $216,000. What is the remaining backlog on this job?","o":["$576,000","$36,000","$144,000","$216,000"],"a":2,"e":"Backlog = contract price - revenue earned to date = $360,000 - $216,000 = $144,000. [WIP schedule]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor buys 400 fixtures at $12.50 each, FOB shipping point, with $180.00 freight paid by the buyer. What is the landed cost per fixture?","o":["$192.50","$12.05","$12.95","$12.50"],"a":2,"e":"Landed cost = (400 x $12.50 + $180.00) / 400 = $5,180.00 / 400 = $12.95. Freight is part of inventory cost. [Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538 (inventory costs)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"What markup on cost is required to earn a 35% gross margin?","o":["35.00%","53.85%","65.00%","25.93%"],"a":1,"e":"Markup = margin / (1 - margin) = 0.35 / 0.65 = 53.85%. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.127(3), F.S., the department may issue a stop-work order for all unlicensed work on a project upon:","o":["A homeowner's verbal request made by telephone to the board","Probable cause that licensed work is being done without a valid credential","A jury finding of negligence in a civil case against the firm","A failure to renew a local business tax receipt on time"],"a":1,"e":"The department may issue the order on probable cause, and it may be enforced through cease-and-desist or related actions under s. 455.228. [F.S. 489.127(3)]","r":"F.S. 489.127(3)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Using 365 days, the approximate annualized cost of NOT taking a 2/10, net 30 discount (paying on day 30) is:","o":["18.4 percent","37.2 percent","24.5 percent","2.0 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Cost = (2/98) x (365/20) = 0.0204 x 18.25 = 0.3724, about 37.2%. The 2% is earned for paying 20 days early, so the annualized rate is far higher than 2%. [Terms analysis]","r":"Cash discount analysis"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1926.20(b)(2), who is responsible for frequent and regular inspections of job sites, materials and equipment?","o":["Competent persons designated by the employer","The local building official who issued the permit","The property owner or the owner's design professional","OSHA area directors, during scheduled annual inspections"],"a":0,"e":"Section 1926.20(b)(2) requires frequent and regular inspections of job sites, materials and equipment by competent persons designated by the employers. [29 CFR 1926.20(b)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.20(b)(2)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which of these must a claim of lien contain under s. 713.08(1), F.S.?","o":["The dates of first and last furnishing and the amount unpaid","A copy of the owner's most recent income tax return form","The surety's bond number and the penal sum of the bond","The building inspector's signature and the date of inspection"],"a":0,"e":"The claim must state the lienor's name and address, the person contracted with, the work and price or value, a legal description, the owner's name, first and last furnishing dates, and the unpaid amount. [F.S. 713.08(1)]","r":"F.S. 713.08(1)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contract has a $12,000 allowance for a fire alarm upgrade. The actual cost approved is $9,400. What adjustment results?","o":["An addition of $2,600","A credit to the owner of $2,600","A credit to the owner of $9,400","No adjustment; the contractor keeps the difference"],"a":1,"e":"Allowances are trued up to actual cost: 12,000 - 9,400 = 2,600 credit. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A small dealer files timely electronically and owes $800 of sales tax. Using the 2.5% of the first $1,200 of tax due (capped at $30) rule, what collection allowance applies?","o":["$30.00","$18.00","$20.00","$24.00"],"a":2,"e":"The allowance is 2.5% of the tax due up to $1,200: $800 x 2.5% = $20.00, below the $30 cap. [Florida DOR Sales and Use Tax tutorial; F.S. 212.12(1)]","r":"Florida DOR; F.S. 212.12"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A school district advertises a $380,000 construction project under F.S. 255.0525(2). What is the minimum advertising period before bid opening?","o":["10 days","30 days","45 days","21 days"],"a":3,"e":"Projects over $200,000 but not over $500,000 require advertising at least 21 days before bid opening; over $500,000 requires 30 days. [F.S. 255.0525(2)]","r":"F.S. 255.0525(2)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On a cost-plus-fee contract, the owner pays:","o":["A fixed sum regardless of costs","Only the labor hours at a stated rate","The actual reimbursable costs plus an agreed fee","The contractor's estimate with no accounting"],"a":2,"e":"A cost-plus contract reimburses documented job costs and adds a fee, either a fixed amount or a percentage. The owner carries more cost risk, so the contract should define reimbursable costs and audit rights. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What must be placed at the service equipment of a building with an on-site emergency power source?","o":["A red beacon at the meter","A site map to the utility pole","A circuit schedule only","A sign giving the type and location of the on-site source"],"a":3,"e":"A sign indicating the type and location of the on-site emergency power source must be at the service entrance. [NEC 700.7(A)]","r":"NEC 700.7(A)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A project network (all finish-to-start, durations in working days): A 3d (start); B 5d after A; C 4d after A; D 6d after B; E 2d after B+C; F 7d after D+E; G 3d after C; H 2d after F+G. What is the total float of activity C?","o":["5 days","4 days","0 days","8 days"],"a":0,"e":"C's earliest finish is 7. Its successors E (late start 12) and G (late start 18) limit its late finish to 12. Total float = LF - EF = 12 - 7 = 5 days. [CPM total float]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What voltage drop appears across a 12.5 ohm resistor carrying 18 A?","o":["225 V","112.5 V","1.44 V","243.0 V"],"a":0,"e":"E = I x R = 18 x 12.5 = 225 V. Dividing instead of multiplying gives 1.44. [Ohm's law]","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Receiving-station antenna lead-in conductors attached to a building must not be able to swing closer than what distance to power conductors of 250 volts or less?","o":["600 mm (2 ft)","300 mm (1 ft)","1.8 m (6 ft)","3.0 m (10 ft)"],"a":0,"e":"Lead-ins may not swing closer than 2 ft to circuits of 250 V or less, or 10 ft to circuits over 250 V, with limited reductions. [NEC 810.18(A)]","r":"NEC 2023 810.18(A)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which fixed-equipment condition requires exposed noncurrent-carrying metal parts to be grounded on a construction site?","o":["It is within 8 feet vertically or 5 feet horizontally of ground or grounded metal and subject to contact","It operates at less than 50 volts to ground and is installed in a dry location","It is located more than 10 feet above the floor in an unoccupied equipment room","It is fed by a nonmetallic raceway and is enclosed in a locked electrical room"],"a":0,"e":"1926.404(f)(7)(iii)(A) requires grounding where fixed equipment is within 8 feet vertically or 5 feet horizontally of ground or grounded metal and subject to employee contact.","r":"29 CFR 1926.404(f)(7)(iii)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Who must record the notice of commencement before improvements begin?","o":["The electrical contractor, once the contract has been signed and funded","The lienor's supplier, once it has first furnished materials to the job","The local building department, once the permit has been issued to the owner","The owner or the owner's authorized agent"],"a":3,"e":"Only the owner signs the notice; recording with the clerk before work begins is the owner's responsibility, and a lender must record it before disbursing construction funds. [F.S. 713.13]","r":"F.S. 713.13"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A ceiling-suspended (paddle) fan is supported by an outlet box. The box must be listed for the purpose, and the maximum fan weight the box may support is:","o":["50 lb","70 lb","35 lb","100 lb"],"a":1,"e":"Outlet boxes for sole support of ceiling-suspended fans must be listed and marked for the purpose and may not support fans weighing more than 70 lb; the marking must include the maximum weight where designed for fans over 35 lb. [NEC 314.27(C)]","r":"NEC 314.27(C)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Signs and outline lighting systems must be installed so that adjacent combustible materials are not subjected to temperatures exceeding:","o":["60 degrees C","75 degrees C","90 degrees C (194 degrees F)","105 degrees C"],"a":2,"e":"600.9(C) limits temperature on adjacent combustible materials to 90 degrees C (194 degrees F). Incandescent or HID lamps must be spaced 50 mm (2 in.) from wood or other combustibles. [NEC 600.9(C)]","r":"NEC 600.9(C)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a smooth ceiling with 30 ft spot-type smoke detector spacing, the greatest distance from a detector to a sidewall is:","o":["15 ft, which is one-half the spacing","30 ft","21 ft","7.5 ft"],"a":0,"e":"The wall-distance limit is half the listed spacing: 0.5 x 30 ft = 15 ft. [NFPA 72 (2019) 17.7.3.2.3]","r":"NFPA 72 17.7.3.2.3 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 713.20, if a lienor accepts an unsecured note for part of a claim, the lien rights","o":["are not waived unless expressly agreed in writing, and the lien deadlines are not extended","are waived automatically on the date the note is accepted by the lienor","are extended for one additional year beyond the note's due date","are replaced by a mortgage on the owner's property recorded by the clerk"],"a":0,"e":"Accepting an unsecured note does not waive the lien absent an express written agreement and does not change the 713.06/713.08 deadlines. [F.S. 713.20]","r":"F.S. 713.20"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"At the end of the required 1 1/2 hour duration, NFPA 101 allows emergency illumination to decline to an average of at least:","o":["0.06 foot-candle average, with a minimum of 0.006 foot-candle","0.6 foot-candle (6 lux), with a minimum of 0.06 foot-candle at any point","1 foot-candle, with a minimum of 0.1 foot-candle","0.1 foot-candle, with a minimum of 0.01 foot-candle"],"a":1,"e":"Illumination may decline to 0.6 fc average and 0.06 fc minimum at the end of the duration. These are 60 percent of the initial values. [NFPA 101, 7.9.2.1]","r":"NFPA 101 7.9.2.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A written change to the bidding documents issued by the owner or designer before bids are due is called a(n)","o":["change order","allowance","addendum","punch list"],"a":2,"e":"Addenda modify the bid documents during bidding and must be priced into the bid. A change order modifies the contract after award. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A network (finish-to-start, working days): A 4d (start); B 7d after A; C 6d after A; D 5d after B; E 5d after C; F 3d after D+E. To cut the project to 17 days with a single 2-day reduction, which activity should be shortened?","o":["Activity A","Activity E","Activity B","Activity C"],"a":0,"e":"Both paths (19 and 18 days) pass through A and F. Shortening A by 2 gives 17 and 16 days. Shortening B, C, or E affects only one path and leaves the other at 18 or 19. [Schedule compression]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under ADAAG 4.28.3, what is the maximum horizontal distance from any point in a room requiring visual signals to the nearest visual alarm appliance (room not exceeding 100 ft across)?","o":["25 ft","50 ft","100 ft","75 ft"],"a":1,"e":"No place in a room or common corridor may be more than 50 ft horizontally from the signal. Only very large spaces over 100 ft across may use perimeter devices spaced up to 100 ft apart. [ADAAG 4.28.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.3"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Contract revenue recognized under the percentage-of-completion method for the current year equals","o":["cumulative earned revenue to date less revenue recognized in prior years","the total contract price, recognized in full when the contract is signed","the cash received during the year from the customer","the retainage released to the contractor during the year"],"a":0,"e":"Each year's revenue is the increase in cumulative revenue earned: this year's cumulative amount less what was recognized before. [Percentage of completion]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On a floor plan, a circle with two short parallel lines through it, and the subscript GFI beside it, represents which device (using common ANSI/IEEE-style plan symbols)?","o":["A duplex receptacle wired on an isolated-ground circuit","A duplex receptacle with ground-fault circuit-interrupter protection","A single receptacle fitted with a weatherproof cover plate","A duplex receptacle that is switched by a wall switch"],"a":1,"e":"The circle with two parallel lines is the usual duplex receptacle, and the GFI subscript calls for ground-fault circuit-interrupter protection. An isolated-ground receptacle is usually marked IG, and a switched receptacle is usually marked with an S or a half-shaded circle. [Drawing practice: receptacle symbols]","r":"Drawing practice: receptacle symbols (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A fixed-temperature heat detector in the \"ordinary\" temperature classification generally operates in what range?","o":["100 to 134 degrees F","175 to 249 degrees F","250 to 324 degrees F","135 to 174 degrees F (57 to 79 degrees C)"],"a":3,"e":"NFPA 72 temperature classifications: low 100-134 F, ordinary 135-174 F, intermediate 175-249 F, high 250-324 F. [NFPA 72 (2019) Table 17.6.2.4.1]","r":"NFPA 72 Table 17.6.2.4.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"When must an entry employer review its permit-required confined space program using the canceled permits?","o":["Within 30 days after each entry","Every 5 years","Only after an injury","Within 1 year after each entry"],"a":3,"e":"The program is reviewed using canceled permits within 1 year after each entry; revisions are made as necessary. [29 CFR 1926.1204(n)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1204(n)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum cover for Schedule 40 PVC conduit (listed for direct burial) installed under a lawn, for a 240-V circuit?","o":["24 in.","12 in.","6 in.","18 in."],"a":3,"e":"Column 3 (nonmetallic raceways listed for direct burial) requires 18 in. in all locations not specified. Residential 120-V GFCI circuits may use 12 in. [NEC Table 300.5]","r":"NEC Table 300.5"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A riser sketch uses 3/16 in. = 1 ft-0 in. A run measures 2.75 in. How long is it, to the nearest tenth of a foot?","o":["0.5 ft","22.0 ft","29.3 ft","14.7 ft"],"a":3,"e":"At 3/16 in. per foot, 2.75 / (3/16) = 14.67 ft, which is about 14.7 ft (about 5.33 ft per inch). The other values apply the wrong scale or invert the fraction. [Scale calculation]","r":"Scale calculation"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"When a cable or raceway passes through bored holes in wood framing, how far must the edge of the hole be from the nearest edge of the wood member, unless protected by a steel plate?","o":["1-1/4 in.","5/8 in.","2 in.","1 in."],"a":0,"e":"The hole must be at least 1-1/4 in. from the nearest edge of the wood member; otherwise a steel plate or sleeve at least 1/16 in. thick must protect the cable. [NEC 300.4(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 300.4(A)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"What is the general Florida state sales tax rate on taxable sales of tangible personal property?","o":["6%","5%","7%","4.5%"],"a":0,"e":"The general state sales tax rate is 6%; county discretionary sales surtax may be added. [F.S. 212.05; Florida DOR Sales and Use Tax tutorial]","r":"F.S. 212.05; Florida DOR"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 255.05(8), a contractor who demands a sworn statement of account from a claimant who is not in privity gives the claimant how many days to respond?","o":["5 days","30 days","15 days","60 days"],"a":1,"e":"Failure to respond, or a false statement, forfeits the claimant's bond rights as provided in the statute. [F.S. 255.05(8)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(8)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A transformer with a rated primary current of 1.5 A is protected on the primary side only. What is the maximum rating of the primary overcurrent device as a percentage of rated primary current?","o":["125%","167%","300%","250%"],"a":2,"e":"For primary-only protection, primary current of 9 A or more is limited to 125%, 2 A to less than 9 A to 167%, and under 2 A to 300%. 1.5 A is under 2 A. [NEC Table 450.3(B)]","r":"NEC Table 450.3(B)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the rule for using the top step or top of a stepladder?","o":["It may be used if the ladder is Type IA","It may be used only for light tools","It may be used when the ladder is placed against a wall","The top or top step of a stepladder may not be used as a step"],"a":3,"e":"1926.1053(b)(13) prohibits using the top or top step of a stepladder as a step.","r":"29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(13)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Optional method (220.82), 1,800 sq ft dwelling, 120/240-V single-phase: 3 VA/sq ft lighting plus two small-appliance and one laundry circuit; nameplate loads of a 12 kW range, 5 kW dryer, 1.2 kW dishwasher and 4.5 kW water heater; a 3.6 kVA air conditioner and a 10 kW central electric heating unit (fewer than four separately controlled units) that cannot run together. What is the calculated demand current?","o":["106.4 A","121.0 A","94.3 A","162.9 A"],"a":0,"e":"General plus appliances = 9,900 + 22,700 = 32,600 VA. First 10,000 at 100% + 40% of 22,600 = 19,040 VA. Heating at 65% = 6,500 VA, which is larger than the air conditioner (3,600 VA), so it is used: 25,540 VA / 240 = 106.4 A. [NEC 220.82]","r":"NEC 220.82"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"For 2026, what is the Florida reemployment tax taxable wage base per employee?","o":["The first $7,000 of wages paid to each employee","The first $8,000 of wages","The first $9,000 of wages","All wages up to the Social Security wage base"],"a":0,"e":"Under s. 443.1217(2)(a)2, remuneration above $7,000 paid to an individual in a calendar year is not subject to contributions. [F.S. 443.1217(2)(a)2; Florida DOR]","r":"F.S. 443.1217(2)(a)2"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Remote metal parts of a section sign or outline lighting system supplied only by a remote Class 2 power supply:","o":["Must be bonded with 6 AWG copper to the service","Must be grounded to the building steel only","Must be isolated by 1/2 in. of spacing from the sign","Are not required to be bonded to an equipment grounding conductor"],"a":3,"e":"Article 600 contains an exception to the bonding requirement for remote metal parts supplied only by a remote Class 2 power supply. [NEC 600.7(B) Exception]","r":"NEC 600.7(B) Exception (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C., what is the minimum net worth for a specialty electrical contractor business organization?","o":["$5,000","$10,000","$20,000","$500"],"a":0,"e":"Unlimited electrical and alarm contractors need $10,000; specialty contractors need $5,000. [Rule 61G6-5.004]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C."},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which OSHA form is the 'Injury and Illness Incident Report' used to give details of each recordable case?","o":["Form 300","Form 3000","Form 300A","Form 301"],"a":3,"e":"OSHA Form 300 is the log, 300A the annual summary and 301 the incident report. Equivalent workers' compensation or insurance forms can substitute for the 301 if they carry the same information. [29 CFR 1904.29]","r":"29 CFR 1904.29"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A surety's guideline limits a contractor's total work on hand (backlog plus new job) to 12 times adjusted working capital. Adjusted working capital is $280,000 and current backlog on bonded jobs is $2,100,000. What is the largest new job the contractor could take under that guideline?","o":["$3,360,000","$175,000","$1,260,000","$5,460,000"],"a":2,"e":"Total capacity = 12 x $280,000 = $3,360,000. Room for a new job = $3,360,000 - $2,100,000 = $1,260,000. Backlog already uses part of the program. [Surety bonding capacity guideline]","r":"Bonding capacity (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NFPA 101 limits the ratio of maximum to minimum emergency illumination along the egress path to:","o":["40 to 1","100 to 1","10 to 1","20 to 1"],"a":0,"e":"The maximum-to-minimum illumination uniformity ratio may not exceed 40:1 throughout the egress path. A low ratio prevents bright and dark patches. [NFPA 101, 7.9.2.1]","r":"NFPA 101 7.9.2.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"F.S. 489.514 requires an applicant for certification by a registered contractor to have at least how many years of experience in the category?","o":["1 year","2 years","10 years","5 years"],"a":3,"e":"The statute requires at least five years of experience as a contractor or inspector in the category, with no revocation and no recent suspension or large fine. [F.S. 489.514(2)(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.514(2)(c)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor sells an old trencher for cash at book value. On the statement of cash flows the proceeds are reported as","o":["a reduction of accounts payable","an investing inflow","an operating inflow from revenue","a financing inflow from debt"],"a":1,"e":"Disposing of long-term assets is an investing activity. [Statement of cash flows]","r":"Statement of cash flows"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Compared with a simple bar chart, a CPM network diagram is better at showing","o":["what contract price is assigned to each activity","what weather is expected during each week of work","which activities drive the finish date and how delays pass on","how many workers are on the site on each day"],"a":2,"e":"Network logic lets the scheduler calculate float and the critical path; a plain bar chart shows durations and dates only. [Scheduling methods]","r":"Bar charts vs networks (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What is the smallest copper conductor (K = 12.9) that limits the voltage drop to 3 percent on a 240 V single-phase, 20 A load 175 ft away (one way)? Circular mils: 12 AWG 6,530; 10 AWG 10,380; 8 AWG 16,510; 6 AWG 26,240; 4 AWG 41,740.","o":["8 AWG","10 AWG","6 AWG","12 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"CM = 2 x K x I x L / VD = 2 x 12.9 x 20 x 175 / 7.2 = 12,542; the next larger standard size is 8 AWG (16,510 CM). Voltage drop is a design recommendation; ampacity must also be checked. [Voltage drop]","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Overhead 120/240-V open conductors run from a house to a detached garage and cross a private residential driveway. What is the minimum clearance above the driveway?","o":["15 ft","10 ft","18 ft","12 ft"],"a":3,"e":"For conductors of not over 300 V to ground, 12 ft is required over residential property and driveways and commercial areas not subject to truck traffic. 10 ft applies to pedestrian-only areas. [NEC 225.18(2)]","r":"NEC 225.18(2)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A circuit directory entry reads 'MISC.' for a breaker feeding loads in several rooms. Which Code requirement does that entry fail to satisfy?","o":["Each circuit needs a legible description that is clear, evident, and specific","Each circuit must list the insulation type of its conductors","Each circuit must list its available fault current in amperes","Each circuit must list the name of the installing electrician"],"a":0,"e":"NEC 408.4(A) requires each circuit and modification to have a legible, permanent description that is clear, evident, and specific, and not dependent on transient occupancy. 'MISC.' is not specific. [NEC 408.4(A)]","r":"NEC 408.4(A)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Using K = 12.9, what is the voltage drop on a 150 ft one-way run of 10 AWG copper (10,380 circular mils) carrying 24 A, single-phase?","o":["14.22 V","7.75 V","4.47 V","8.95 V"],"a":3,"e":"VD = 2 x K x I x L / CM = 2 x 12.9 x 24 x 150 / 10,380 = 8.95 V. Dropping the 2 gives half. [Voltage drop]","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 725.06(1), F.S., an indemnity clause in a private construction contract that covers damages caused partly by the indemnitee's own negligence is void unless the contract:","o":["Is notarized by both parties and then recorded with the county clerk","Is reviewed and signed by the licensed architect of record for the project","Is approved in advance by the local building official before work starts","Contains a monetary limit with a reasonable commercial relationship to the contract"],"a":3,"e":"The statute requires a monetary limitation with a reasonable commercial relationship to the contract, and it must be part of the project specifications or bid documents if there are any. Without it the indemnity is void and unenforceable. [F.S. 725.06(1)]","r":"F.S. 725.06(1)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under FAR 52.249-10, the Contracting Officer's findings on the extent of an excusable delay are","o":["void unless the contractor agrees to them in writing","final and conclusive unless appealed under the Disputes clause","advisory only and never binding on the contractor","binding only on the surety and not on the contractor"],"a":1,"e":"The clause says the Contracting Officer ascertains the facts and extent of delay, the findings are final and conclusive, but subject to the Disputes clause. [FAR 52.249-10]","r":"FAR 52.249-10"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which of these is the best reason to receive approval of a submittal BEFORE ordering a long-lead item such as a switchboard?","o":["Approval transfers all liability for the item and its performance to the architect","The design team can catch nonconformance before fabrication, avoiding rework and delay","Approval removes the need for the building inspection of the installed item","Approval waives the code requirements that would otherwise apply to the item"],"a":1,"e":"Submittals let the design team verify that the item meets the contract before purchase. They do not waive code or inspection requirements. [Submittal practice]","r":"Submittal practice"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Type UF cable is being used for a 120-V, 15-A residential outdoor circuit protected by a GFCI and a 20-A maximum overcurrent device. What is the minimum cover for the direct-buried cable?","o":["24 in.","18 in.","6 in.","12 in."],"a":3,"e":"Table 300.5 column 4 allows 12 in. for direct burial of residential branch circuits of 120 V or less with GFCI protection and a maximum 20 A overcurrent device. [NEC 300.5, Table 300.5]","r":"NEC 300.5, Table 300.5"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's cash flow statement shows operating cash flow of $145,000, investing cash flow of -$60,000, and financing cash flow of $25,000. If beginning cash was $80,000, what is ending cash?","o":["$190,000","$310,000","$225,000","$110,000"],"a":0,"e":"Net change = $145,000 - $60,000 + $25,000 = $110,000. Ending cash = $80,000 + $110,000 = $190,000. Forgetting the beginning balance gives $110,000. [Statement of cash flows]","r":"Statement of cash flows"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How must a multimeter be connected to measure current in a circuit?","o":["In parallel with the load, with the leads in the current jack","Across the supply with the selector switch set on volts","In series with the load, with the leads in the current jack","Across the circuit breaker terminals only, with the breaker on"],"a":2,"e":"Current is measured in series; connecting a meter in current mode across a voltage source creates a short circuit. [manufacturer safety instructions]","r":"Manufacturer instructions (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C., a business organization applying to qualify an unlimited electrical contractor must submit a financial statement showing a minimum net worth of","o":["$250,000","$1,000","$50,000","$10,000"],"a":3,"e":"The rule requires a minimum net worth of $10,000 for unlimited electrical and alarm contractors and $5,000 for specialty contractors. [Rule 61G6-5.004]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.004, F.A.C."},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A panel schedule shows 36 kVA of balanced load on a 208Y/120 V three-phase panel. What is the line current?","o":["100 A","173 A","300 A","43 A"],"a":0,"e":"I = 36,000 / (1.732 x 208) = 99.9 A. Using 208 V or 120 V alone ignores the three-phase relationship. [Three-phase current formula]","r":"Three-phase current formula"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A company changes the recovery period or convention of a depreciable asset it has already been depreciating. This is generally","o":["a change the taxpayer may freely make each year on the tax return","a change in accounting method that requires IRS approval (Form 3115)","a change that is reported on the quarterly Form 941 payroll return","a change that is handled by the state licensing board instead"],"a":1,"e":"Pub 946 says a change in period of recovery or convention is a change in accounting method requiring Form 3115. [IRS Pub 946 (2025)]","r":"IRS Pub 946 (2025)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A person who believes she was discriminated against in Florida employment may file a charge with the Florida Commission on Human Relations within how many days of the alleged violation?","o":["2 years","365 days","30 days","180 days"],"a":1,"e":"Section 760.11(1) allows a complaint to be filed with the FCHR within 365 days of the alleged violation. [F.S. 760.11(1)]","r":"F.S. 760.11(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Three 36 ohm resistors are connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance is","o":["36 ohms","108 ohms","72 ohms","12 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Equal resistors in parallel: R / n = 36 / 3 = 12 ohms. [Parallel circuits]","r":"Parallel circuits"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Two 120-V circuits share a neutral as a multiwire branch circuit in a dwelling panelboard fed from a 120/240-V single-phase system. Which arrangement satisfies the disconnecting requirement?","o":["Individual single-pole breakers as long as the neutral is spliced to the neutral bar with a pigtail","A 2-pole breaker, or two single-pole breakers with an identified handle tie, so both ungrounded conductors open together","Two single-pole breakers with no handle tie, because each load is only 120 V","A single-pole breaker on one ungrounded conductor and a solid link on the other"],"a":1,"e":"A multiwire branch circuit must have a means to simultaneously disconnect all ungrounded conductors at the point of origination. A handle tie or common-trip multipole breaker does this; separate unlinked single-pole breakers do not. [NEC 210.4(B)]","r":"NEC 210.4(B)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Two 50 kVA single-phase transformers are connected in open delta. What three-phase load capacity do they provide?","o":["57.7 kVA","150 kVA","100 kVA","86.6 kVA"],"a":3,"e":"Open delta delivers 86.6 percent of the installed 100 kVA, or 86.6 kVA (the same as 57.7 percent of a closed-delta bank of three 50 kVA units). It is not the full 100 kVA. [Open delta]","r":"Open delta"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Before measuring resistance with an ohmmeter, the circuit should be","o":["loaded to 80 percent","connected to ground","de-energized and isolated","energized at full voltage"],"a":2,"e":"An ohmmeter supplies its own test voltage; external voltage gives false readings and can damage the meter. [Meters]","r":"Meters"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the line-to-neutral voltage of a 480Y three-phase wye system?","o":["240 V","831 V","347 V","277 V"],"a":3,"e":"480 / 1.732 = 277 V (the familiar 277 V lighting voltage). [Wye connection]","r":"Wye connection"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The maintenance disconnecting means for pool equipment (other than lighting) must be at least how far horizontally from the inside wall of the pool, unless separated by a permanent barrier?","o":["3 ft","5 ft","10 ft","1.5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"The maintenance disconnect must be within sight of the equipment and at least 1.5 m (5 ft) horizontally from the pool unless a barrier provides that reach path. [NEC 680.12]","r":"NEC 680.12"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How is a single-gang receptacle on a strap counted for box fill?","o":["As one conductor","As two conductor allowances, based on the largest conductor connected to the device","As three conductor allowances","As zero, as devices are not counted"],"a":1,"e":"Each yoke or strap containing one or more devices counts as two conductor volume allowances based on the largest conductor connected to the device. [NEC 314.16(B)(4)]","r":"NEC 314.16(B)(4)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Beginning stock 100 units at $2.00; purchase 1 of 200 units at $2.20; purchase 2 of 150 units at $2.50; 280 units are used on jobs. Using FIFO, what is the value of the ending inventory (170 units)?","o":["$426.00","$383.44","$419.00","$354.00"],"a":2,"e":"Ending inventory is the most recent costs: 20 x $2.20 + 150 x $2.50 = $44 + $375 = $419.00. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538 (inventories)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A clamp-on ammeter placed around both conductors of a 120 V two-wire cable will read approximately","o":["double the load current because both wires are included","the normal load current of the circuit","zero, since the opposing fields cancel","the circuit voltage divided by the load resistance"],"a":2,"e":"Currents in opposite directions cancel; measure around a single conductor. [Meters]","r":"Meters"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Transfer equipment for an emergency system must be:","o":["Automatic and identified for emergency use","Manual only, with a lockout","Any listed disconnect switch","Solid-neutral type only"],"a":0,"e":"Emergency transfer equipment is automatic, listed and identified for emergency use. [NEC 700.5]","r":"NEC 700.5"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"On a construction site, which is an acceptable alternative to GFCI protection for temporary receptacles not requiring GFCI?","o":["Yellow cords with a daily glance","A single GFCI on the generator only","A written assured equipment grounding conductor program","An annual visual inspection"],"a":2,"e":"Receptacles that are not GFCI-protected must be covered by a written assured equipment grounding conductor program. [NEC 590.6(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 590.6(B)(2)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A Class 1 circuit and a power circuit are to share one raceway. Which condition is required?","o":["The Class 1 conductors must be 14 AWG or larger and the power conductors must be aluminum","The equipment powered or controlled by each circuit is functionally associated, and all conductors are insulated for the highest voltage present","The power circuit must be 120 volts or less and protected by a GFCI","They may never share a raceway under any circumstances"],"a":1,"e":"Class 1 and power conductors may be installed together when the equipment is functionally associated, with all conductors insulated for the maximum voltage in the raceway. [NEC 725.48]","r":"NEC 2023 725.48 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Questions about the bid documents that are raised BEFORE the bids are due and affect the bid are normally answered by:","o":["An addendum issued to all bidders","A private call to the bidder who asked","A change order issued to the lowest bidder","A submittal review comment"],"a":0,"e":"Pre-bid questions are answered in writing by addenda so every bidder works from the same information. RFIs are used after award. [Bidding procedures]","r":"Bidding procedures"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.128, F.S., what is the general effect of a construction contract entered into by an unlicensed contractor?","o":["It is void and unenforceable by both the contractor and the owner","It is enforceable only if the owner knew the contractor lacked a license","It is unenforceable by the unlicensed contractor in law or in equity","It is enforceable for any contract price of up to $2,500"],"a":2,"e":"Section 489.128(1) makes the contract unenforceable by the unlicensed contractor, and subsection (2) removes any lien or bond claim. The owner is not barred from enforcing it. [F.S. 489.128(1)-(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.128(1)-(2)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The NEC informational notes recommend that branch-circuit voltage drop not exceed ___ percent, and feeder plus branch circuit combined not exceed ___ percent.","o":["2; 3","3; 5","1; 3","5; 10"],"a":1,"e":"These are recommendations (informational notes), not mandatory requirements, for reasonable operating efficiency. See 210.19(A) and 215.2(A) informational notes. [NEC 210.19(A), 215.2(A) informational notes]","r":"NEC 210.19(A), 215.2(A) informational notes (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For the purpose of sizing a sign's branch circuit and overcurrent protection, the sign load is considered:","o":["A noncontinuous load","An intermittent load that may be 50 percent demand","A continuous load","A fixed appliance load subject to 75 percent demand"],"a":2,"e":"Article 600 says branch circuits supplying signs are considered continuous loads, so the conductors and overcurrent device must handle 125 percent of the load. [NEC 600.5; 210.19(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 600.5; 210.19(A)(1)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"How far must the perimeter surface to be bonded extend horizontally beyond the inside walls of the pool?","o":["1 ft","5 ft","10 ft","3 ft (1 m)"],"a":3,"e":"The perimeter surface extends 1 m (3 ft) horizontally beyond the inside walls of the pool. [NEC 680.26(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 680.26(B)(2)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A bidder discovers a math error in its own bid after submitting it but before the bid deadline. The best action is to","o":["wait and hope that the owner does not notice the error","withdraw the bid and resubmit a corrected bid before the deadline, following the bid instructions","call the owner after bid opening and ask for more money","ask the surety to pay the difference after the award"],"a":1,"e":"Most bid instructions allow withdrawal and replacement before the deadline. After opening, withdrawal is restricted and can put the bid bond at risk. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under Rule 61G6-5.008, F.A.C., which bodily injury liability limits apply to electrical contractors?","o":["$25,000 per person and $50,000 per occurrence","$300,000 per person and $100,000 per occurrence","$100,000 per person and $300,000 per occurrence","$500,000 per person and $800,000 per occurrence"],"a":2,"e":"Bodily injury is $100,000 per person and $300,000 per occurrence, with property damage at $500,000. The $300,000 and $100,000 figures are reversed in the wrong choice. [F.A.C. 61G6-5.008]","r":"F.A.C. 61G6-5.008"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Beginning stock 100 units at $2.00; purchase 1 of 200 units at $2.20; purchase 2 of 150 units at $2.50; 280 units are used on jobs. Using FIFO, what is the cost of the 280 units used?","o":["$596.00","$631.56","$661.00","$560.00"],"a":0,"e":"FIFO uses the oldest costs first: 100 x $2.00 + 180 x $2.20 = $200 + $396 = $596.00. LIFO would give $661.00. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538 (inventories)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor buys a $1,800 power tool used for years. For managerial costing, the cost is usually","o":["charged entirely to the first job on which the tool is used","ignored for costing, because tools do not cost anything to use","recovered over time through equipment or overhead rates, not only by the first job","recorded as an employer payroll tax on the crew who used it"],"a":2,"e":"Spreading the cost over expected use matches cost with the jobs that benefit. [Equipment cost recovery]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Circuit conductors for a lighting circuit are installed in the same raceway as the service conductors of a building. Which statement applies?","o":["It is allowed if the raceway is rigid metal conduit","That is prohibited, except for grounding electrode conductors, bonding conductors and load-management control conductors with overcurrent protection","It is allowed for any conductors under 30 A","It is allowed if the lighting conductors are smaller than 10 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Only service conductors may be in a service raceway, with exceptions for grounding electrode conductors, supply-side bonding jumpers, and protected load-management control conductors. [NEC 230.7]","r":"NEC 230.7"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 455.225(4), F.S., the probable cause panel must determine probable cause within how many days after receiving the final investigative report (unless extended by the secretary)?","o":["10 days","15 days","90 days","30 days"],"a":3,"e":"The panel has 30 days after receiving the final report; it may request more information within 15 days. If the panel does not act, the department determines probable cause within 10 days after the limit expires. [F.S. 455.225(4)]","r":"F.S. 455.225(4)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contract has 180 calendar days to substantial completion and liquidated damages of $750 per calendar day. Substantial completion is reached on day 196. What are the liquidated damages?","o":["$147,000","$135,000","$12,000","$12,750"],"a":2,"e":"Days late = 196 - 180 = 16; 16 x 750 = $12,000. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A phase-sequence (rotation) meter is used to","o":["measure insulation resistance of a winding","measure the power factor of a load","detect ground faults on a grounded system","determine the order of the three phases"],"a":3,"e":"It indicates ABC or CBA rotation so motors turn the correct direction. [Meters]","r":"Meters"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which load typically causes a lagging power factor?","o":["An induction motor","An incandescent lamp","A resistance heater","A bank of capacitors"],"a":0,"e":"Inductive loads such as motors and transformers make current lag voltage. Resistive loads are near unity; capacitors cause a leading PF. [Power factor]","r":"Power factor"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 4 AWG THHN copper conductor (90°C column, 95 A) is installed in a conduit in a 44°C ambient with no more than three current-carrying conductors. What is the corrected ampacity?","o":["95.0 A","65.0 A","82.7 A","76.0 A"],"a":2,"e":"Table 310.15(B)(1)(1): 41–45°C with a 90°C rating uses a 0.87 correction factor. 95 x 0.87 = 82.7 A. [NEC 310.15(B)(1), Table 310.15(B)(1)(1)]","r":"NEC 310.15(B)(1), Table 310.15(B)(1)(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under NEC 430.22, the minimum ampacity of the branch-circuit conductors to a single continuous-duty 25 hp, 230 V, three-phase induction motor (Table 430.250 full-load current 68 A) is","o":["170 A","68 A","78.2 A","85 A"],"a":3,"e":"Conductors are sized at 125 percent of the Table 430.250 full-load current: 68 x 1.25 = 85 A. [NEC 430.22, Table 430.250]","r":"NEC 430.22, Table 430.250"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In a typical construction contract, what is the owner's consideration?","o":["The promise to pay the contract price","The contractor's license number","The building permit","The notice of commencement"],"a":0,"e":"Consideration is the bargained-for exchange. The contractor promises labor, materials and completion, and the owner promises payment. A license, permit or notice of commencement is not what the owner gives in exchange. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor on the cash method pays a $9,000 supplier bill in January for materials installed in December. Under the cash method, the expense is recorded in","o":["Neither; materials are never expensed","Both months, split equally","January, when the bill is paid","December, when the materials were used"],"a":2,"e":"The cash method records expenses when paid. Accrual accounting would match the cost to December. [Cash vs. accrual]","r":"Cash vs accrual"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Adding extra water to a ready-mix truck to make the concrete easier to place in a sign footing will generally:","o":["Increase its strength by making it easier to consolidate","Have no effect on the hardened concrete if it is cured","Reduce the strength and durability of the hardened concrete","Increase its resistance to corrosion of the reinforcing"],"a":2,"e":"A higher water-cementitious materials ratio lowers strength and durability. Workability should be added with admixtures where appropriate, as approved by the engineer. [PCA, water-cementitious materials ratio]","r":"PCA, water-cementitious materials ratio (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor's annual revenue was $3,000,000 in both years. Receivables were $300,000 at the end of year 1 and $450,000 at the end of year 2. By how many days did DSO change (365-day year), and what does it do to cash?","o":["DSO fell 18.2 days, freeing $150,000 of cash","DSO rose 18.2 days (from 36.5 to 54.8), absorbing $150,000 of cash","DSO rose 18.2 days with no effect on cash","DSO rose 5.0 days, absorbing $150,000 of cash"],"a":1,"e":"Year 1 DSO = $300,000/$3,000,000 x 365 = 36.5; year 2 = $450,000/$3,000,000 x 365 = 54.8; change = 18.2 days. Receivables grew $150,000, which consumed that much operating cash. [Receivables analysis]","r":"Days sales outstanding (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The SBA guarantee fee on performance and payment bonds is 0.6% of the contract price. What is the fee for a $1,850,000 contract?","o":["$111,000","$1,110","$92,500","$11,100"],"a":3,"e":"1,850,000 x 0.006 = $11,100. [SBA Surety Bonds]","r":"SBA Surety Bonds"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"To qualify for a construction-industry workers' compensation exemption, a corporate officer must own at least what percentage of the stock?","o":["1 percent","25 percent","51 percent","10 percent"],"a":3,"e":"The statute requires at least 10 percent ownership and listing as an officer with the Division of Corporations. [F.S. 440.02]","r":"F.S. 440.02"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Where solid rock prevents the required cover depth, the wiring must be installed in:","o":["Any raceway with a 6 in. layer of gravel","A metal raceway, or nonmetallic raceway permitted for direct burial, covered by at least 2 in. of concrete extending down to rock","Direct-buried cable, covered by 2 in. of sand","Flexible metal conduit"],"a":1,"e":"Where solid rock prevents the required depth, the wiring must be in a metal raceway or a nonmetallic raceway permitted for direct burial, covered by at least 2 in. of concrete extending down to rock. [NEC Table 300.5 Note 5]","r":"NEC Table 300.5 Note 5"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"If gross margin is 20%, the cost of a job equals what percentage of the selling price?","o":["20%","120%","125%","80%"],"a":3,"e":"Cost = price x (1 - margin) = 80% of price. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A specification Part 2 lists three acceptable manufacturers and the words 'or approved equal.' If the contractor wants to use a fourth manufacturer's panelboard, what is the correct step?","o":["Install it first and then notify the inspector that the substitution was made afterward","Order it and have the owner decide at project closeout","Use it, because 'or equal' lets the contractor decide","Submit a substitution request with data showing it is equal, then wait for written approval"],"a":3,"e":"Substitutions are made under the procedures in the project (commonly 01 25 00), and the design professional decides if an item is equal. [Typical substitution procedure]","r":"Typical substitution procedure"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"If the owner's retained final payment is not enough to pay all listed and noticed lienors, the contractor has how many days to supply the shortfall before the owner distributes funds by statutory priority?","o":["10 days","3 days","30 days","60 days"],"a":0,"e":"713.06 gives the contractor 10 days to supply the shortfall; otherwise the owner distributes funds under the priority order. [F.S. 713.06(3)]","r":"F.S. 713.06"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 200 A busbar panel has a 175 A main breaker. Using the 120% rule, what is the largest inverter output current permitted (with the PV breaker rated at 125% of it)?","o":["52 A","25 A","65 A","40 A"],"a":0,"e":"Allowed sum = 120% x 200 = 240 A. 240 - 175 = 65 A for the PV breaker; 65 / 1.25 = 52 A inverter output current. [NEC 705.12(B)]","r":"NEC 705.12(B) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Adding another load in parallel across a constant-voltage source","o":["decreases total resistance and increases total current","decreases total current while voltage on each branch drops","increases total resistance and decreases total current","leaves total resistance unchanged and lowers each branch current"],"a":0,"e":"Each added branch gives current another path, so total R falls and total I rises. [Parallel circuits]","r":"Parallel circuits"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employer misclassifies workers as independent contractors and fails to secure compensation. The penalty can be up to how much per misclassified worker?","o":["$500","$5,000","$1,000","$25,000"],"a":1,"e":"Section 440.10(1)(f) authorizes a penalty of up to $5,000 for each employee misclassified as an independent contractor. [F.S. 440.10(1)(f)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(f)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Installing capacitors near an inductive load to improve power factor will","o":["raise the voltage drop in the supply conductors","increase the true power consumed by the load","convert the inductive load to a direct-current load","reduce the reactive current drawn from the source"],"a":3,"e":"Capacitors supply the reactive power locally, reducing apparent power and line current; kW is unchanged. [Power factor correction]","r":"Power factor correction"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An accounts receivable aging shows: current $210,000; 1-30 days $96,000; 31-60 days $54,000; 61-90 days $30,000; over 90 days $60,000. What percent of total receivables is more than 60 days past due?","o":["37.5%","32.0%","20.0%","13.3%"],"a":2,"e":"Total = $450,000. More than 60 days = $30,000 + $60,000 = $90,000; $90,000 / $450,000 = 20.0%. Using only the over-90 bucket gives 13.3%. [AR aging analysis]","r":"AR aging (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A mobile home park has 12 lots, each calculated at 16,000 VA. Table 550.31 allows a 27% demand factor for 12 lots. What is the demand load?","o":["192,000 VA","43,200 VA","48,000 VA","51,840 VA"],"a":3,"e":"12 x 16,000 = 192,000 VA, times 0.27 = 51,840 VA. [NEC 550.31, Table 550.31]","r":"NEC 550.31, Table 550.31"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Class E hard hats are rated to reduce the exposure to high-voltage conductors at up to:","o":["2,200 volts","440 volts","600 volts","20,000 volts"],"a":3,"e":"ANSI Z89.1 Type I/II Class E hats are proof-tested at 20,000 volts; Class G is 2,200 volts; Class C offers no electrical protection.","r":"ANSI Z89.1; 29 CFR 1926.100 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Receptacles installed for EV charging require:","o":["AFCI protection","No special protection","A padlocked enclosure","GFCI protection for personnel"],"a":3,"e":"GFCI protection is required for receptacles for EV charging. [NEC 625.54]","r":"NEC 625.54"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A sign over a loading driveway has its lowest edge at 12 ft 6 in. above grade where trucks pass. What must be done?","o":["Raise the sign to 14 ft or protect it from physical damage","Nothing, because 12 ft 6 in. is high enough for trucks that pass","Add a second disconnect at the lower edge of the sign enclosure","Convert the sign to a cord-connected sign that has a plug"],"a":0,"e":"The sign's lowest equipment is below the 14 ft minimum, so it must be raised or protected from physical damage. [NEC 600.9(A)]","r":"NEC 600.9(A)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A light switch in a public corridor is installed with its toggle 51 in. above the floor. A wheelchair user can approach it from the front. Under ADAAG 4.27.3 and 4.2.5, this mounting is:","o":["acceptable, since 51 in. is the usual standard mounting height for switches","too low, since switches in public corridors must be above 54 in.","too high, since the highest operable part may not exceed 48 in. for a forward approach","acceptable, since switches may be mounted as high as 54 in. in any case"],"a":2,"e":"The highest operable part must fall within the reach ranges of 4.2.5 and 4.2.6, and a forward approach is limited to 48 in. The 54 in. value applies only to a side approach. [ADAAG 4.27.3, 4.2.5]","r":"ADAAG 4.27.3, 4.2.5"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An electrical contractor emails an owner a priced proposal for $18,400 stating that it is good for 30 days. Legally, the proposal is best described as:","o":["An acceptance of the owner's request to bid","Consideration already paid by the contractor","A counteroffer that the owner must answer in writing","An offer that the owner can accept to create a contract"],"a":3,"e":"A definite proposal on specific terms that invites acceptance is an offer; the owner's acceptance would complete the bargain. Consideration is the exchange of value, not the proposal itself. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Splices and taps in a wireway must not fill the wireway to more than what percent of its area at that point?","o":["90%","75%","40%","60%"],"a":1,"e":"Splices and taps are permitted in wireways if accessible, with conductors, splices and taps not filling more than 75% of the wireway area at that point. [NEC 376.56(A)]","r":"NEC 376.56(A)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A direct-buried cable emerges from the ground to a meter socket. Protection must extend from the minimum cover depth below grade to a point at least how far above finished grade?","o":["6 in.","8 ft","5 ft","3 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Direct-buried conductors emerging from grade must be protected by enclosures or raceways extending from the minimum cover depth to at least 8 ft above finished grade, but no deeper than 18 in. below grade. [NEC 300.5(D)(1)]","r":"NEC 300.5(D)(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A motor draws 15 kW and delivers 13.5 kW of shaft power. How much power is lost as heat and friction, and what is its efficiency?","o":["1.5 kW lost; 111 percent","13.5 kW lost; 90 percent","1.5 kW lost; 90 percent","28.5 kW lost; 47 percent"],"a":2,"e":"Loss = 15 - 13.5 = 1.5 kW; efficiency = 13.5 / 15 = 90 percent. [Motor efficiency]","r":"Motor efficiency"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The Florida Civil Rights Act of 1992 covers employers with at least how many employees?","o":["10","50","15","25"],"a":2,"e":"The FCRA defines 'employer' as one with 15 or more employees for each working day in 20 or more calendar weeks. [F.S. 760.02(7)]","r":"F.S. 760.02(7)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"When power is supplied over Class 2 or Class 3 cabling to remote equipment, such as PoE, how is the current-carrying capability of the conductors determined?","o":["From the NEC table for current per conductor based on conductor size, bundle count, and temperature rating","It is always 1 ampere per pair regardless of bundle size","It is the same as the ampacity of 14 AWG copper","It is not limited because the circuit is Class 2"],"a":0,"e":"Section 725.144 gives ampacity tables for power over Class 2/3 cables that account for conductor size, number of cables in the bundle, and temperature rating. [NEC 725.144]","r":"NEC 2023 725.144 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A bonding conductor for metal gas piping that may become energized by a 30-A circuit is sized by which table?","o":["Table 250.102(C)(1), using the meter size","Table 250.66, using the service conductors","Table 310.16, using the pipe size","Table 250.122, using the rating of the circuit that is likely to energize the piping"],"a":3,"e":"Bonding of other metal piping that may become energized is sized per Table 250.122 using the rating of the circuit that is likely to energize it (30 A gives 10 AWG copper). [NEC 250.104(B)]","r":"NEC 250.104(B)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Where must service equipment for a mobile home be located relative to the home?","o":["Mounted on the home's exterior wall","Anywhere on the lot","Adjacent, in sight, and no more than 30 ft away","Inside the home's utility room"],"a":2,"e":"Service equipment must be located adjacent to the mobile home and not mounted in or on it; within sight and not over 30 ft away. [NEC 550.32(A)]","r":"NEC 550.32(A)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Wire priced at $412.00 per M (per 1,000 ft) is purchased in a quantity of 3,500 ft. What is the extended price?","o":["$144.20","$14,420.00","$1,236.00","$1,442.00"],"a":3,"e":"3,500 ft / 1,000 = 3.5 M; 3.5 x $412.00 = $1,442.00. 'M' means thousand. [Purchasing math]","r":"Unit pricing"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"For the fourth quarter (October through December), Form 941 is due by what date?","o":["December 31","February 15","January 31","March 31"],"a":2,"e":"The fourth-quarter Form 941 is due January 31 of the following year. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employee is killed in a work-related incident, but death occurs 20 days after the incident. For reporting, the fatality must be reported to OSHA if death occurred within how many days of the incident?","o":["7 days","30 days","180 days","60 days"],"a":1,"e":"A work-related fatality is reportable if it occurs within 30 days of the incident; in-patient hospitalization, amputation or eye loss must occur within 24 hours of the incident. [29 CFR 1904.39(b)(6)-(7)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.39(b)(6)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Convert 0.35 A to milliamperes.","o":["0.35 mA","35 mA","350 mA","3,500 mA"],"a":2,"e":"1 A = 1,000 mA, so 0.35 A = 350 mA. [Conversions]","r":"Conversions"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A monthly depositor accumulates a $104,000 employment tax liability on a Tuesday. When is the deposit due?","o":["By the next business day, becoming a semiweekly depositor","By the 15th of the following month as usual","By the Friday following the payday week","By the end of the calendar quarter in which it arose"],"a":0,"e":"The $100,000 next-day rule overrides the monthly and semiweekly schedules: deposit by the next business day, and the employer becomes a semiweekly depositor for the rest of the year and the following year. [IRS Topic 757]","r":"IRS Topic 757"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under the completed-contract method, revenue and costs on a contract are generally recognized","o":["as each invoice is paid","when the contract is completed","at the beginning of the contract","evenly over the calendar year"],"a":1,"e":"Completed-contract defers recognition until completion, unlike percentage of completion. IRC 460 limits its use for long-term contracts to exceptions such as small or home construction contracts. [IRS Construction ATG, Ch. 2]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An intersystem bonding termination (IBT) for communications systems must provide:","o":["A single terminal for one conductor only","A bonding conductor of 14 AWG","A set of terminals with the capacity for at least three intersystem bonding conductors, accessible for inspection and not interfering with opening the enclosure","A location inside the attic"],"a":2,"e":"An IBT must be accessible, have terminals for at least three bonding conductors and not interfere with opening enclosures. The bonding conductor must be not smaller than 6 AWG copper. [NEC 250.94]","r":"NEC 250.94"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 489.115(7), a board-approved 14-hour financial responsibility course may be used to satisfy what portion of the financial requirements?","o":["All of the requirements","One-quarter of the requirements","None; it is for continuing education only","Half of the requirements"],"a":3,"e":"The statute says one-half of the financial requirements can be met by completing a 14-hour board-approved course. [F.S. 489.115(7)]","r":"F.S. 489.115(7)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A series circuit has 30 ohms resistance and 40 ohms inductive reactance. What is the impedance?","o":["1,200 ohms","10 ohms","50 ohms","70 ohms"],"a":2,"e":"Z = sqrt(30 squared + 40 squared) = sqrt(2,500) = 50 ohms. Reactance and resistance are 90 degrees apart, so they add as vectors. [Impedance]","r":"Impedance"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contract pays $22 per linear foot of feeder trench. The bid form assumed 400 LF, but 520 LF were actually installed and measured. What is the payment for the trench item (no other adjustment clause applies)?","o":["$8,800","$11,440","$2,640","$12,584"],"a":1,"e":"Unit price contracts pay measured quantity: 520 x 22 = $11,440. The bid quantity of 400 was only an estimate. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A payment request under F.S. 715.12 is incomplete. How long does the obligor have to return it with written reasons and keep interest from starting on the original timeline?","o":["14 days","3 business days","30 days","7 days"],"a":0,"e":"715.12(5) lets the obligor return an incomplete or erroneous request within 14 days with written reasons; interest then runs 14 days after the corrected submission. [F.S. 715.12(5)]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has current assets of $420,000 and current liabilities of $310,000. What is its working capital?","o":["$110,000","$730,000","$135","$-110,000"],"a":0,"e":"Working capital = current assets - current liabilities = 420,000 - 310,000 = 110,000. [Working capital]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Using the aging above and estimated uncollectible rates of 1% (current), 3% (1-30), 10% (31-60), 25% (61-90), and 50% (over 90), what allowance for doubtful accounts is indicated?","o":["$45,000","$47,880","$22,500","$30,000"],"a":1,"e":"$210,000x1% = $2,100; $96,000x3% = $2,880; $54,000x10% = $5,400; $30,000x25% = $7,500; $60,000x50% = $30,000. Total = $47,880. [AR aging / allowance method]","r":"AR aging (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Using K = 12.9, what is the DC resistance of 100 ft of a single 12 AWG copper conductor (6,530 circular mils)?","o":["0.198 ohms","0.124 ohms","0.00198 ohms","0.395 ohms"],"a":0,"e":"R = K x L / CM = 12.9 x 100 / 6,530 = 0.198 ohms. (A round trip is 0.395 ohms.) [Conductor resistance]","r":"Conductor resistance"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A solid round conductor has a diameter of 0.128 inch. What is its area in circular mils?","o":["16,384 CM","0.0164 CM","128 CM","12,868 CM"],"a":0,"e":"Diameter in mils = 128; CM = 128 squared = 16,384. A mil is 0.001 inch. [Circular mils]","r":"Circular mils"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A sole primary qualifying agent ends that status by notice. Under F.S. 489.1195, the status ends when a new primary agent is designated or after how many days from notice reaching the board, whichever is first?","o":["60 days","10 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":0,"e":"Sole primary status ends at the earlier of a new designation or 60 days after notice is received by the board. [F.S. 489.1195]","r":"F.S. 489.1195"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A PIR motion detector responds to:","o":["Sound level changes","Changes in barometric pressure","A change in infrared radiation (heat) as a person moves across its field of view","Magnetic field changes"],"a":2,"e":"PIR detectors sense heat-emitting objects moving across zones in the field of view. [Burglar alarm practice]","r":"PIR (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Copper conductors of 4/0 AWG are installed in a vertical conduit. What is the maximum vertical rise before additional support is required (Table 300.19(A))?","o":["80 ft","60 ft","180 ft","100 ft"],"a":0,"e":"Table 300.19(A): copper 2/0 through 4/0 AWG, 80 ft. (180 ft is the aluminum value.) [NEC 300.19(A), Table 300.19(A)]","r":"NEC 300.19(A), Table 300.19(A)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An owner requires to be named as an \"additional insured\" on the contractor's CGL policy. This endorsement","o":["extends coverage under the contractor's policy to the owner for liability arising from the contractor's work","transfers the contractor's license to the owner for the job","gives the owner ownership and control of the policy","waives the contractor's premium for the policy year"],"a":0,"e":"The additional insured endorsement lets the owner claim under the policy, subject to its terms. A certificate alone does not do that. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Equipment with an open-circuit voltage exceeding 1000 volts may NOT be installed:","o":["On buildings with sprinklers","In a basement of a store","In or on dwelling occupancies","In outdoor locations"],"a":2,"e":"600.32: equipment with open-circuit voltage over 1000 V shall not be installed in or on dwelling occupancies. [NEC 600.32]","r":"NEC 600.32"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which conductor size has a box fill volume allowance of 5.00 cu in. per conductor in Table 314.16(B)?","o":["10 AWG","4 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 314.16(B): 14 AWG 2.00; 12 AWG 2.25; 10 AWG 2.50; 8 AWG 3.00; 6 AWG 5.00 cu in. [NEC Table 314.16(B)]","r":"NEC Table 314.16(B)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A rapidly growing contractor with strong profits often runs short of cash because","o":["profits are reported before income taxes are deducted from the contractor's net income","growth increases material, labor and receivable balances before customers pay","depreciation increases cash outflow every time the company buys more equipment","growth reduces accounts receivable balances because customers pay faster"],"a":1,"e":"More jobs mean more up-front costs funded before collections; growth consumes working capital, so a line of credit may be needed. [Working capital]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Job 214 (contract $500,000, estimated cost $400,000, cost to date $180,000) has billed $250,000 to date. Is the job overbilled or underbilled, and by how much?","o":["Underbilled by $45,000","Overbilled by $70,000","Underbilled by $25,000","Overbilled by $25,000"],"a":3,"e":"Earned revenue is $225,000. Billings $250,000 exceed earned revenue by $250,000 - $225,000 = $25,000, so the job is overbilled. [WIP schedule, over/under billing]","r":"WIP schedule"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A noncompliant employer has no payroll records. Assume the statewide average weekly wage is $1,320. Imputed weekly payroll is 1.5 times that amount per employee. For 6 employees, what is the imputed payroll for one week?","o":["$11,880","$7,920","$1,980","$15,840"],"a":0,"e":"1,320 x 1.5 = 1,980 per employee; x 6 = $11,880. [F.S. 440.107(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.107(7)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The purpose of a payment bond is to","o":["protect subcontractors, laborers and suppliers who are not paid by the contractor","protect the owner from errors in the design","guarantee that the bid price will be honored","pay the owner's attorney fees in any dispute"],"a":0,"e":"The payment bond conditions the contractor's prompt payment of labor, services and materials on the contract. [F.S. 255.05(1); F.S. 713.23(1)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(1); 713.23(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A meter movement has 10 mA full-scale deflection and 100 ohms coil resistance. What series resistor converts it to a 150 V full-scale voltmeter?","o":["1,500 ohms","15,000 ohms","14,900,000 ohms","14,900 ohms"],"a":3,"e":"Total R = 150 / 0.010 = 15,000 ohms; series multiplier = 15,000 - 100 = 14,900 ohms. [Meter ranges]","r":"Meter ranges"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A Florida excavator damages a gas line and gas is escaping. What does F.S. 556 require?","o":["File a written report within 30 days, with a copy to the property owner","Call Sunshine 811 on the next business day, and then keep the area open","Notify the operator immediately, and call 911 for an escape of gas","Backfill the damage first, and then call the operator after the repair"],"a":2,"e":"Damage to a facility must be reported immediately to the member operator, and an escape of gas or other hazardous materials requires calling 911. [F.S. 556.105]","r":"F.S. 556.105"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which transformer is exempt from the secondary-circuit ground-fault protection requirement of 600.23(B)?","o":["Any transformer that is rated 15,000 V at its secondary terminals","One with an isolated ungrounded secondary of 7,500 V or less","Any transformer that is rated 30 mA at its secondary terminals","Any electronic power supply that is listed for use with signs"],"a":1,"e":"Exempt are transformers with isolated ungrounded secondaries of 7,500 V or less, and transformers with integral porcelain or glass housing that require no field wiring of the secondary. Electronic power supplies are not exempt. [NEC 600.23(B)]","r":"NEC 600.23(B)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"At what height above a lower level must employees on a scaffold be protected from falls under 29 CFR 1926.451(g)?","o":["More than 4 feet","More than 10 feet","More than 6 feet","More than 15 feet"],"a":1,"e":"Each employee on a scaffold more than 10 feet above a lower level must be protected from falling by guardrails or a personal fall arrest system; the 6-foot trigger is for general construction. [29 CFR 1926.451(g)(1)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.451(g)(1)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A lienor receives a written demand for a sworn statement of account under s. 713.16, F.S., and does not respond for 40 days. What is the consequence?","o":["The lienor pays a $500 fine","The lien is merely delayed 30 days","The lienor loses its lien","The owner must pay the lienor double"],"a":2,"e":"A lienor that fails or refuses to furnish the sworn statement within 30 days after demand, or gives a false one, forfeits its lien. [F.S. 713.16]","r":"F.S. 713.16"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Antenna conductors for a transmitting station attached to a building must be mounted at least how far clear of the building surface on insulating supports?","o":["25 mm (1 in.)","75 mm (3 in.)","150 mm (6 in.)","600 mm (2 ft)"],"a":1,"e":"Transmitting-station antenna and lead-in conductors must be firmly mounted at least 3 in. clear of the surface on nonabsorbent insulators, unless enclosed in a grounded continuous metal shield. [NEC 810.54]","r":"NEC 2023 810.54"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A site plan shows a new pole-mounted luminaire 62 ft from a property line. What can an elevation drawing show about that luminaire that the site plan cannot?","o":["The routing of the underground conduits that serve the pole base and handhole","Vertical dimensions such as pole height, mounting height, and base elevation","The sizes of the feeders and branch circuits that serve the luminaire","The panelboard schedule and its circuit numbers"],"a":1,"e":"Plans give horizontal positions, while elevations give vertical heights. Conduit routes and feeders are on plans and riser diagrams. [Drawing practice: plans vs elevations]","r":"Drawing practice: plans vs elevations"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A job uses 640 direct labor hours. At $22.50 overhead per labor hour, how much overhead is charged to the job?","o":["$7,200","$288","$216,000","$14,400"],"a":3,"e":"640 x 22.50 = 14,400. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A transformer secondary midpoint return in a neon circuit is used to:","o":["Reduce the primary current drawn by the transformer from the supply","Provide a center tap, so each end of the tube sees half the voltage to ground","Increase the output of the transformer to as much as 30,000 V","Replace the equipment grounding conductor of the supply circuit"],"a":1,"e":"A grounded center tap divides the output so each electrode sees half the voltage to ground, helping meet the 7,500 V-to-ground limit. 600.32 allows conductors to the midpoint return. [NEC 600.32, 600.23(C)]","r":"NEC 600.32, 600.23(C)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Office furniture and equipment fall in which MACRS recovery class?","o":["3-year property","5-year property","15-year property","7-year property"],"a":3,"e":"Form 4562 instructions list office furniture and equipment as 7-year property. [IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025), Line 19]","r":"IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which OSHA general industry provision governs lockout and tagging of electrical circuits in electric utilization installations, rather than 29 CFR 1910.147?","o":["29 CFR 1910.132(h)","29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)","29 CFR 1910.269(a)(3)","29 CFR 1904.39"],"a":1,"e":"1910.147 excludes exposure to electrical hazards from work on utilization installations covered by Subpart S; lockout/tagging for electrical circuits is in 1910.333(b)(2).","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2); 1910.147(a)(1)(ii)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under accrual accounting, a contractor finishes a job and sends the invoice on December 28; the customer pays on January 15. In which period is the revenue recorded?","o":["December, when it was earned","January, when the cash was received","Split equally between December and January","The period in which the customer's check clears the bank"],"a":0,"e":"Accrual accounting recognizes revenue when earned, not when cash arrives. Cash-basis reporting would show it in January. [Accrual vs. cash basis]","r":"Accrual accounting"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 713.21(4), F.S., an interested party files a complaint to cancel a lien, and the clerk issues a summons. Within how many days after service of the summons must the lienor show cause why the lien should not be canceled?","o":["10 days","45 days","20 days","60 days"],"a":2,"e":"The summons requires the lienor to show cause within 20 days after service. The 60-day figure belongs to the notice of contest procedure in s. 713.22. [F.S. 713.21(4)]","r":"F.S. 713.21(4)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"After a smoke detector installation, sensitivity testing is required one year after installation and then:","o":["Every 5 years","Every 10 years","Monthly","Every alternate year thereafter (every two years)"],"a":3,"e":"NFPA 72 requires sensitivity testing within one year of installation and every alternate year thereafter, unless the testing shows the detector can be tested less frequently. [NFPA 72 (2019) 14.4.5.3]","r":"NFPA 72 14.4.5.3 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The primary of an LED sign power supply is 120 V and the supply is Class 2 on its output. The sign's Class 2 output wiring in a wet location should be:","o":["Ordinary indoor CL2 cable stapled to the back of the sign","Cable listed and marked for wet locations, installed per the instructions","Unlisted speaker wire run from the power supply to the modules","Lamp cord of the type used for table lamps and similar items"],"a":1,"e":"Class 2 cable in wet locations must be listed and marked for wet use (and sunlight resistant if exposed), following the sign installation instructions. [NEC Art. 600 and 725 Class 2 cable]","r":"NEC Art. 600 and 725 Class 2 cable (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor shows a profit on the income statement for the quarter but cannot meet payroll. Which explanation is most consistent with accrual accounting?","o":["Depreciation expense used up the cash that was needed to pay the crew's wages","Accrual accounting never records any revenue until the cash is actually received","Profit on the income statement and cash in the bank are always the same number","Revenue was recorded when billed, but customers have not paid yet, so the profit is sitting in receivables"],"a":3,"e":"Accrual income is recognized when earned and billed, so receivables can grow while the bank balance falls. Depreciation is a non-cash expense and does not drain cash. [IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accrual method]","r":"IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 30 hp, 460-volt, three-phase squirrel-cage motor (Table 430.250 full-load current 40 A) is protected by dual-element time-delay fuses. What is the maximum fuse size normally permitted for short-circuit and ground-fault protection before any exception?","o":["100 A","120 A","60 A","70 A"],"a":3,"e":"Dual-element (time-delay) fuses are limited to 175% of table FLC: 40 x 1.75 = 70 A, a standard size. The 100 A and 120 A values correspond to non-time-delay percentages. [NEC 430.52(C)(1), Table 430.52]","r":"NEC 430.52(C)(1), Table 430.52"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employee earns $4,000 gross in a 2026 pay period and is far below every wage base. How much total FICA tax (Social Security plus Medicare) is withheld from the employee's pay?","o":["$612.00","$250.00","$306.00","$330.00"],"a":2,"e":"Employee FICA is 7.65%: 6.2% x $4,000 = $248.00 plus 1.45% x $4,000 = $58.00, for $306.00. $612.00 would double it to include the employer share. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"With spot-type smoke detectors spaced at 30 ft on a smooth ceiling, no point on the ceiling may be farther than what distance from a detector?","o":["0.7 times the spacing, which is 21 ft","0.5 times the spacing, which is 15 ft","1.0 times the spacing, which is 30 ft","1.4 times the spacing, which is 42 ft"],"a":0,"e":"The rule is 0.7S, giving a coverage radius of about 21 ft. The 15 ft figure is the 0.5S maximum distance to a wall. [NFPA 72 (2019) 17.7.3.2.3]","r":"NFPA 72 17.7.3.2.3 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor with idle crew capacity is offered a job at a price that covers its variable costs and contributes $4,000 toward fixed costs. Fixed costs will be incurred either way. Financially, the contractor should generally","o":["reject it, because it does not include a full share of overhead allocation","reject it, because contribution margin is not the same thing as profit","accept it only if the price is at least double the variable cost","accept it, because the contribution adds to overall profit if no better work is available"],"a":3,"e":"When fixed costs are unavoidable, any positive contribution increases profit; the risk is pricing future work at the low rate. [Contribution margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign was designed for a basic wind speed of 140 mph. The design wind speed for the site increases to 170 mph. By about what percent does the velocity pressure (wind pressure) increase, all other factors equal?","o":["About 47 percent","About 21 percent","About 30 percent","About 147 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Pressure varies with the square of the speed: (170/140)^2 = 1.474, an increase of 47 percent. The speed rises only 21 percent, but the pressure rises much more. [ASCE 7 wind pressure proportional to V^2]","r":"ASCE 7 wind pressure proportional to V^2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor fabricates a custom panel enclosure in its own shop for use on a lump sum job. Under Rule 12A-1.051(10), what is the tax base for use tax on the item?","o":["The full contract price charged to the owner","Only the cost of the raw materials purchased for it","Fabricated cost, including shop labor but not installation labor","Installation labor and transportation to the site only"],"a":2,"e":"Use tax applies on the fabricated cost of items a contractor manufactures for its own use, including shop labor but excluding job-site installation and delivery. [Rule 12A-1.051(10), F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051(10)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Insulated 2/0 AWG conductors in a conduit enter a metal enclosure through a threaded hub with sharp edges. What does 300.4(G) require?","o":["A locknut on the inside only","The conductors must be protected by an identified fitting providing a smoothly rounded insulating surface, or equivalent insulating material","The conductors must be taped with two layers of friction tape","Nothing, because the conductors are insulated"],"a":1,"e":"Where raceways contain insulated conductors 4 AWG or larger entering a cabinet, box or enclosure, they must be protected from abrasion by an identified fitting with a smooth rounded insulating surface or equivalent. [NEC 300.4(G)]","r":"NEC 300.4(G)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A new employer at 2.7% pays a part-time employee $5,200 in total for the year. How much Florida reemployment tax is due on this employee?","o":["$189.00","$140.40","$378.00","$280.80"],"a":1,"e":"Because $5,200 is under the $7,000 base, all of it is taxable: $5,200 x 2.7% = $140.40. $189.00 would apply if the employee earned $7,000 or more. [F.S. 443.1217(2)(a)2; F.S. 443.131(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 443.1217; 443.131"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contract states: 'In case of conflict, the Agreement controls over the General Conditions, which control over the Drawings.' This is an example of:","o":["A liquidated damages clause that sets a daily charge for delay","A termination for convenience clause that favors the owner","An order-of-precedence clause for the contract documents","A differing site conditions clause for concealed conditions"],"a":2,"e":"An order-of-precedence (hierarchy) clause tells the parties which contract document governs when documents conflict. Without one, courts apply rules such as specific over general. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under 2010 Standards 703.4.1, the baseline of the lowest line of tactile characters on a room sign must be:","o":["exactly 60 in. to the centerline of the whole sign","at least 48 in. above the floor, and the highest line no higher than 60 in.","at least 54 in. above the floor, with the highest line at 72 in.","at least 36 in. above the floor, with the highest line at 48 in."],"a":1,"e":"The 2010 range is 48 in. minimum to the baseline of the lowest line and 60 in. maximum to the baseline of the highest line. The 1994 ADAAG used a 60 in. centerline. [2010 Standards 703.4.1]","r":"2010 Std 703.4.1"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Doors with electromagnetic locks in the means of egress must unlock upon activation of which system, among other requirements?","o":["The building fire alarm system or sprinkler waterflow","The burglar alarm system","The CCTV recorder","The telephone system"],"a":0,"e":"Egress locking arrangements must release on fire alarm or sprinkler activation and on loss of power. [NFPA 101 (2018) 7.2.1.5]","r":"NFPA 101 7.2.1.5 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A restaurant load schedule lists three kitchen appliances of 12, 8, and 6 kW. Using the NEC 220.56 commercial kitchen demand factor for three units (90 percent), what is the demand load?","o":["26 kW","23.4 kW","20.8 kW","16.9 kW"],"a":1,"e":"Connected = 12+8+6 = 26 kW. 90 percent of 26 = 23.4 kW. The other options use no demand factor or the factor for four or six or more units. [NEC Table 220.56]","r":"NEC Table 220.56"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which type of supervising station system is operated by a listed organization that receives signals and dispatches to responders on behalf of subscribers?","o":["Central station service","Proprietary supervising station","Local alarm only","Auxiliary protective system"],"a":0,"e":"A central station is a listed third-party monitoring facility serving many subscribers. A proprietary station serves one owner's premises. [NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 26]","r":"NFPA 72 Ch. 26 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Using the cost-to-cost percentage-of-completion method, a $560,000 contract has estimated total cost of $420,000 and costs to date of $168,000. What percent complete is the contract?","o":["30%","70%","250%","40%"],"a":3,"e":"Cost-to-cost % complete = costs to date / estimated total cost = 168,000/420,000 = 40%. [IRC 460(b); IRS Construction ATG]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The ADA's employment provisions (Title I) apply to employers with at least how many employees?","o":["15","50","20","10"],"a":0,"e":"Title I of the Americans with Disabilities Act covers employers with 15 or more employees. [42 U.S.C. 12111(5)]","r":"42 U.S.C. 12111(5)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For an anchor-bolt footing, why is a template used to hold the bolts while the concrete is placed?","o":["To increase the compressive strength of the surrounding concrete","To provide a grounding path through the bolts to the earth","To keep water from entering the drilled hole during the pour","To keep the bolt pattern and projection matched to the base plate"],"a":3,"e":"Anchor bolts must match the base plate holes and project the correct distance for nut and washer engagement, so a template holds them in place during the pour. [Construction practice for sign pole bases]","r":"Construction practice for sign pole bases (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A supply operation has annual cost of goods sold of $420,000, beginning inventory $70,000 and ending inventory $90,000. What is the inventory turnover?","o":["0.19 times","4.67 times","5.25 times","6.00 times"],"a":2,"e":"Average inventory = ($70,000 + $90,000)/2 = $80,000; turnover = $420,000 / $80,000 = 5.25. [Inventory management]","r":"Inventory turnover"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor plans to take a $30,000 deposit from a residential customer on a $60,000 contract. Under F.S. 489.126, because the initial payment exceeds 10 percent, the contractor must","o":["place the deposit in a trust account for one year before spending any of it","refund half of the deposit to the customer within 10 days of receiving it","apply for permits within 30 days of payment and start work within 90 days after permits issue","have the owner record a claim of lien against the contractor's own property"],"a":2,"e":"The deposit is 50% of the price, above 10%, so the permit and start-of-work timing rules apply; failure may be prima facie evidence of misappropriation. [F.S. 489.126(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.126"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.505, F.S., how does a certified electrical contractor differ from a registered electrical contractor?","o":["A certificate is limited to one county; a registration is valid in the whole state","A certificate is geographically unlimited; a registration works only in its jurisdiction","A registrant must pass the state exam, but a certificate holder need not","A certificate lasts four years, while a registration lasts only two years"],"a":1,"e":"The statute defines a certificate as a geographically unlimited certificate of competency issued by the department, and a registered electrical contractor as one who contracts only in the jurisdiction of the registration. [F.S. 489.505]","r":"F.S. 489.505"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the circular mil area of 4 AWG copper conductor in Chapter 9, Table 8?","o":["66,360 cmil","26,240 cmil","52,620 cmil","41,740 cmil"],"a":3,"e":"Table 8 gives 4 AWG as 41,740 circular mils. 6 AWG is 26,240, 3 AWG is 52,620 and 2 AWG is 66,360. [NEC Chapter 9, Table 8]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Table 8"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Which wiring method is permitted in a Class I, Division 1 location?","o":["Electrical metallic tubing","Type NM cable","Threaded RMC, threaded steel IMC, Type MI or listed MC-HL","Standard Type MC cable without a continuous gastight sheath"],"a":2,"e":"Division 1 wiring is limited to threaded RMC and IMC, Type MI with termination fittings, and listed MC-HL cable (and certain PVC underground and other listed methods). EMT and NM are never permitted. [NEC 501.10(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 501.10(A)(1)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A resistor's value stays constant while the applied voltage is doubled. What happens to the current?","o":["It increases four times","It doubles","It is cut in half","It stays the same"],"a":1,"e":"I = E / R, so current is directly proportional to voltage when R is fixed. Four times applies to power, not current. [Ohm's law]","r":"Ohm's law"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Residential rental property (80% or more of rent from dwelling units) is depreciated over","o":["27.5 years, straight-line","39 years, straight-line","7 years, declining balance","15 years, declining balance"],"a":0,"e":"The 27.5-year class applies to residential rental property; Pub 527 defines it as a building where 80% or more of gross rental income is from dwelling units. [IRS Pub 527; Form 4562 instructions]","r":"IRS Instructions for Form 4562 (2025)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Each contractor or subcontractor on a project generally pays sales tax on what?","o":["Its own purchases of materials for its portion of the work","Only the general contractor pays tax","Only the property owner pays tax","Nobody pays tax if a subcontractor is used"],"a":0,"e":"Subcontractors, like general contractors, are consumers of the materials they install and pay tax on their own purchases unless they are making a retail sale under (3)(d). [Rule 12A-1.051, F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under F.S. 556.105, how many full business days before beginning excavation not under state waters must an excavator notify the one-call notification system?","o":["At least 24 hours","At least 5 business days","At least 10 business days","At least 2 full business days"],"a":3,"e":"An excavator must notify the notification system not less than 2 full business days before excavation (10 full business days for work beneath state waters). [F.S. 556.105]","r":"F.S. 556.105"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The ordinary statute of limitations for an FLSA back wage claim, and the period if the violation was willful, are:","o":["1 year; 2 years for willful violations","2 years; 3 years for willful violations","4 years; 6 years for willful violations","3 years; 5 years for willful violations"],"a":1,"e":"Under the Portal-to-Portal Act, claims must be brought within 2 years, or 3 years for willful violations. [29 U.S.C. 255]","r":"29 U.S.C. 255"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Revenue recognized under percentage of completion differs from billings. When revenue earned exceeds billings the difference is","o":["an underbilling (an asset); the reverse is an overbilling (a liability)","a retainage payable owed to the subcontractors on the job","an increase in owner's equity recorded when the job is closed","a depreciation reserve recorded against the contractor's equipment"],"a":0,"e":"Comparing earned revenue with amounts billed identifies over- and underbilled jobs. [IRS Construction ATG]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which Florida-specific withholding is NOT required of an employer because Florida has no personal income tax?","o":["Federal income tax withholding","State income tax withholding from wages","Social Security tax","Reemployment tax contributions"],"a":1,"e":"Florida has no state personal income tax, so there is no state income tax withholding; federal withholding, FICA and Florida reemployment tax still apply. [Fla. Const. art. VII, s. 5]","r":"Fla. Const. art. VII, s. 5"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Equipment that supplies power-limited fire alarm circuits must be durably marked:","o":["Only on the shipping carton","With a yellow label reading \"CAUTION - CLASS 1\"","Where plainly visible, to indicate each circuit that is a power-limited fire alarm circuit","Only on the branch-circuit panelboard directory"],"a":2,"e":"NEC 760.124 requires durable visible marking of each PLFA circuit on the supplying equipment. [NEC 760.124]","r":"NEC 2023 760.124"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"At a Florida marina on tidal water, the electrical datum plane for land areas is located:","o":["At the highest high tide level","12 in. above the dock deck","2 ft above the highest high tide level","5 ft above mean sea level"],"a":2,"e":"For land areas subject to tidal fluctuation, the electrical datum plane is a horizontal plane 2 ft (610 mm) above the highest high tide level for the area. [NEC 555.2, 555.3]","r":"NEC 555.2, 555.3"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 15 hp motor operates at 90 percent efficiency at full load. What is the electrical input power?","o":["12,433 W","10,071 W","11,190 W","21,535 W"],"a":0,"e":"Input = output / efficiency = 11,190 / 0.90 = 12,433 W. [Motor efficiency]","r":"Motor efficiency"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a series RLC circuit at resonance,","o":["XL is zero, impedance equals XC and current is at its maximum","XC is double XL, impedance is at its maximum and current is at its minimum","XL equals XC, impedance is at its minimum and current is at its maximum","XL equals XC, impedance is at its maximum and current is at its minimum"],"a":2,"e":"Equal reactances cancel, leaving only R as impedance, so current peaks. [Resonance]","r":"Resonance"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"How is a Florida employer's contribution rate determined once it has a sufficient history?","o":["Its annual gross receipts divided by its payroll","The number of employees on its payroll only","A benefit ratio from benefits charged, plus adjustment factors","Its workers' compensation experience modification factor"],"a":2,"e":"Section 443.131 builds an experienced employer's rate from its benefit ratio (benefits charged over three years divided by taxable payroll) plus adjustment factors, with a range from 0.1% to 5.4%. [F.S. 443.131(3)]","r":"F.S. 443.131(3)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"The flash rate of a visual alarm complying with ADAAG 4.28.3 must be within which range?","o":["3 Hz to 5 Hz","5 Hz to 10 Hz","0.5 Hz to 1 Hz","1 Hz to 3 Hz"],"a":3,"e":"The visual alarm flash rate must be at least 1 Hz and no more than 3 Hz. Flashing faster than about 5 Hz is avoided because of photosensitive seizure risk. [ADAAG 4.28.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.3"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A CGL policy is rated at 4.3 per $1,000 of gross receipts. Estimated receipts are $2,400,000. What is the premium?","o":["$10,320","$103,200","$1,032","$11,352"],"a":0,"e":"2,400,000/1,000 = 2,400; x 4.3 = $10,320. Using $100 instead of $1,000 overstates the premium tenfold. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In NFPA 101, externally illuminated exit signs must be lit to what level on the face of the sign?","o":["At least 20 foot-candles","At least 5 foot-candles","At least 1 foot-candle","At least 0.1 foot-candle"],"a":1,"e":"Externally illuminated signs require at least 5 fc on the sign face. Internally illuminated signs follow their listing instead. [NFPA 101, 7.10.6]","r":"NFPA 101 7.10.6 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Kirchhoff's voltage law states that, around any closed loop,","o":["the total current in a loop equals the sum of the branch currents","the total resistance equals the sum of the reciprocals of the resistors","the voltage across any element equals its current times its power","the algebraic sum of the voltage rises and drops is zero"],"a":3,"e":"KVL: source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops in a closed loop. The current statement is Kirchhoff's current law. [Kirchhoff's laws]","r":"Kirchhoff's laws"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"After a conditional job offer, an employer may require a medical examination under the ADA if:","o":["The examination is limited to employers with fewer than 15 employees","The examination is required of all entering employees in the same job category","The examination is required only of applicants over age 40","The examination is required of one applicant who looks unwell"],"a":1,"e":"Post-offer, pre-start medical exams are permitted if applied uniformly to all entering employees in the job category, and the information is kept confidential. [29 CFR 1630.14(b)]","r":"29 CFR 1630.14(b)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"On a guaranteed maximum price (GMP) contract, what happens when the actual cost of the work plus the contractor's fee exceeds the GMP and no scope change has occurred?","o":["The owner pays the excess at cost","The excess is split 50/50 with the owner","The contractor absorbs the excess","The architect reimburses the excess"],"a":2,"e":"The GMP is a cap. Unless the owner adds scope, costs above the cap are the contractor's responsibility. Cost-plus without a cap would pass the excess to the owner. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A subcontractor schedules these activities (all finish-to-start, working days): S 4d (start); T 6d after S; U 3d after S; V 8d after T; W 5d after U+T; X 4d after W; Y 6d after V+X. Management crashes activity X by 2 days. What is the new project duration?","o":["22 days","23 days","25 days","24 days"],"a":3,"e":"Reducing X to 2 days cuts S-T-W-X-Y to 23 days, but S-T-V-Y is 24 days, so V's path becomes critical. The new duration is 24 days; compression stops helping once another path becomes critical. [Schedule compression]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 100-ft circuit uses 2 AWG THHN copper conductors. A single 8-ft section passes through a hot mechanical room where the conductors' derated ampacity is lower than in the rest of the run. Which statement is correct?","o":["The ampacity of the remainder may be used, because the hot section is not more than 10 ft and not more than 10% of the circuit length","The exception applies only to conductors smaller than 4 AWG","The ampacity of the hot section is ignored entirely","The lowest ampacity must be used for the entire length in all cases"],"a":0,"e":"Where different ampacities apply along a circuit, the lowest applies, except where the lower-ampacity section does not exceed 10 ft and 10% of the circuit length. 8 ft is under 10 ft and under 10% of 100 ft = 10 ft. [NEC 310.14(A)(2)]","r":"NEC 310.14(A)(2)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor's owner will not release the final payment because unpaid subcontractors have served notices to owner. The most direct fix is to","o":["pay the subcontractors or resolve their claims, then deliver the final affidavit with releases","ignore the notices because the prime contract controls the final payment","withhold payroll from the crew until the owner releases the final payment","record a new notice of commencement on the owner's behalf"],"a":0,"e":"Owners must protect themselves against noticed lienors (713.06); the contractor releases funds by paying noticed lienors and furnishing the affidavit and waivers. [F.S. 713.06, 713.20]","r":"F.S. 713.06"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An autotransformer starter uses the 65 percent tap. About what fraction of the full-voltage starting current is drawn from the line?","o":["35 percent","42 percent","81 percent","65 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Motor current falls to 65 percent, and line current falls with the square of the tap: 0.65 squared = 0.42, about 42 percent. [Starting methods]","r":"Starting methods"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A waiver of subrogation endorsement means the insurer","o":["waives the deductible on every claim under the policy","gives up the right to recover from a named third party after paying a claim","gives up the right to audit the premium at year end","pays covered claims twice to the same claimant"],"a":1,"e":"Contracts often require waivers so that the insurer cannot sue the owner or other parties for losses it paid. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A ground-fault protection system on a 1600 A, 480Y/277-V service is being set. What is the maximum permitted pickup setting and time delay under 230.95?","o":["600 A pickup with no time delay allowed","1600 A pickup with a 3-second delay","1200 A maximum pickup, and a 1-second maximum delay for ground-fault currents of 3000 A or more","1200 A pickup with a 5-second delay at any current"],"a":2,"e":"The maximum ground-fault pickup setting is 1200 A, and the maximum time delay is one second for ground-fault currents equal to or greater than 3000 A. [NEC 230.95(A)]","r":"NEC 230.95(A)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"In a purely inductive AC circuit, the current","o":["is 180 degrees out of phase","leads the voltage by 90 degrees","lags the voltage by 90 degrees","is in phase with the voltage"],"a":2,"e":"In an inductor the current lags voltage by 90 degrees; in a capacitor it leads by 90 degrees (ELI the ICE man). [Phase relationships]","r":"Phase relationships"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An employee's gross pay for a single 2026 paycheck is $6,500 and year-to-date wages are already above $184,500. How much Medicare tax is withheld from that check, assuming year-to-date wages remain under $200,000?","o":["$0.00","$155.00","$58.50","$94.25"],"a":3,"e":"Medicare tax has no wage base cap: $6,500 x 1.45% = $94.25. Only the Social Security tax stops at the wage base. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Selling invoices to a third party at a discount for immediate cash, with that party then collecting from the customer, is called","o":["factoring","retainage","bonding","subrogation"],"a":0,"e":"In factoring the business sells its receivables; SBA contrasts it with invoice financing, where the business keeps control of collections. [SBA working capital options]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 48-ampere EVSE is a continuous load. What is the minimum branch circuit overcurrent rating?","o":["48 A","50 A","70 A","60 A"],"a":3,"e":"EVSE is a continuous load: 48 x 1.25 = 60 A. [NEC 625.41]","r":"NEC 625.41"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The sworn notice of nonpayment on a Florida public bond claim must be served no later than how many days after the claimant's final furnishing of labor or materials?","o":["30 days","90 days","45 days","180 days"],"a":1,"e":"The notice may not be served earlier than 45 days after first furnishing and no later than 90 days after final furnishing. [F.S. 255.05(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(2)(a)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The same 150 W LED load is powered by a constant-voltage supply that the designer loads to no more than 80 percent of its rating. What is the minimum supply rating from the common sizes 150 W, 200 W, 250 W, 300 W?","o":["150 W","250 W","300 W","200 W"],"a":3,"e":"150 / 0.80 = 187.5 W, so the smallest common size that is at least that is 200 W. A 150 W supply would run at 100 percent. [Design practice for power supplies; NEC 600.5 continuous load]","r":"Design practice for power supplies; NEC 600.5 continuous load (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A new employee does not give the employer a Form W-4. For federal income tax withholding, the employer must:","o":["Treat the employee as single with no other adjustments","Wait 90 days before withholding any federal income tax","Withhold a flat 10 percent of wages until the form is received","Withhold nothing, since the employee has not claimed a filing status"],"a":0,"e":"If an employee fails to furnish a valid Form W-4, the employer withholds as if the employee were single with no adjustments. [IRS Pub 15 (2026), Sec. 9]","r":"IRS Pub 15 Sec. 9"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"For a fire alarm system used for emergency voice/alarm communications, the secondary power alarm period is:","o":["5 minutes","15 minutes","30 minutes","2 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Voice systems require 24 h standby plus 15 minutes of alarm (versus 5 minutes for tone-only systems). [NFPA 72 (2019) 10.6.7.2.1]","r":"NFPA 72 10.6.7 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An estimate has materials $84,300, labor $43,470 (920 hours at $47.25), equipment $3,800, and subcontracts $18,500. Overhead of 10% is applied to the total direct cost, then profit of 8% is applied to the sum of direct cost and overhead. What is the bid price?","o":["$177,082.60","$179,431.52","$189,088.20","$178,283.16"],"a":3,"e":"Direct = 150,070.00; + 10% overhead = 165,077.00; + 8% profit on that = 178,283.16. Adding both percentages to direct cost (18%) gives $177,082.60, which ignores that profit is applied on cost plus overhead. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 3-phase, 4-wire 480Y/277-V system supplies lighting and uses the neutral as a circuit conductor. Under 250.20, this system:","o":["Is grounded only if over 1000 V","Must be grounded","May be left ungrounded","Is grounded only on the load side"],"a":1,"e":"Three-phase, 4-wire wye systems where the neutral is used as a circuit conductor must be grounded, as must systems that can be grounded so the voltage to ground does not exceed 150 V. [NEC 250.20(B)]","r":"NEC 250.20(B)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"In the payment priority among lienors when an owner's retained funds are insufficient (s. 713.06(4)), who is paid first?","o":["The contractor, then laborers, then the suppliers","Laborers, then persons other than the contractor, then the contractor","Suppliers, then the contractor, then the laborers","All lienors share pro rata regardless of class"],"a":1,"e":"Classes are paid in order: laborers first, persons other than the contractor next, and the contractor last. Shortfalls within a class are prorated. [F.S. 713.06(4)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(4)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under the Tennessee Board of Regents weather-delay policy, a day counts as a weather delay day if weather prevents work for at least what portion of the scheduled workday?","o":["50%","25%","10%","100%"],"a":0,"e":"A weather delay day counts if weather prevents work for at least 50 percent of the scheduled workday. [TBR weather delay policy]","r":"Weather days"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 30 hp, 460-volt, three-phase motor has a Table 430.250 full-load current of 40 A. What is the minimum ampere rating of its motor-circuit disconnect (based on current)?","o":["40 A","50 A","46 A","60 A"],"a":2,"e":"The disconnect must be rated at least 115% of the motor full-load current: 40 x 1.15 = 46 A. A 40 A rating equals only 100% and is too small. [NEC 430.110(A)]","r":"NEC 430.110(A)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A sign branch circuit has a 24 A continuous load. The circuit is protected by a breaker that is NOT listed for 100 percent continuous operation. What is the minimum breaker size and the minimum conductor ampacity?","o":["25 A breaker; conductors of at least 24 A","30 A breaker; conductors of at least 30 A (24 x 1.25)","20 A breaker; conductors of at least 20 A","40 A breaker; conductors of at least 40 A"],"a":1,"e":"Continuous load times 125 percent = 30 A. The OCPD must be at least 30 A, which is a standard size. A 25 A breaker is below 30 A. [NEC 210.20(A), 210.19(A)(1)]","r":"NEC 210.20(A), 210.19(A)(1)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Neon tubing must be supported by listed supports. How close to the electrode connection must the tubing be supported?","o":["Within 6 in. (150 mm)","Within 1 in.","Within 12 in.","Within 3 ft"],"a":0,"e":"600.41: tubing is supported by listed supports within 150 mm (6 in.) of the electrode connection. [NEC 600.41]","r":"NEC 600.41"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum clearance to closet storage space for a surface-mounted fluorescent luminaire?","o":["12 in.","24 in.","3 in.","6 in."],"a":3,"e":"Surface-mounted fluorescent luminaires need at least 6 in. from the storage space; surface-mounted incandescent with an enclosed lamp needs 12 in. [NEC 410.16(C)(3)]","r":"NEC 410.16(C)(3)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"F.S. 255.05(7) allows a contractor to file, in place of a payment and performance bond, which of the following alternative forms of security?","o":["A personal promissory note signed by the contractor","A letter from the contractor's insurance agent","A lien waiver signed by each of the subcontractors","Cash, a money order, a certified check, a cashier's check, or an approved bond, note or debenture"],"a":3,"e":"Alternative security under s. 255.05(7) includes cash, money order, certified or cashier's check, or specified bonds, notes or debentures, in an amount set by the governmental entity. [F.S. 255.05(7)]","r":"F.S. 255.05(7)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A contractor has current assets of $612,000 and current liabilities of $408,000. What is the current ratio?","o":["0.50","0.60","1.50","0.67"],"a":2,"e":"Current ratio = current assets / current liabilities = $612,000 / $408,000 = 1.50. Inverting gives 0.67. [UNL Extension G2009]","r":"Current ratio"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Reinforcing steel in a drilled-pier footing is held away from the soil by:","o":["No cover, because the steel is galvanized","A coat of paint on the reinforcing steel only","About 1/4 in. of soil against the steel","Concrete cover; typical cover against earth is 3 in."],"a":3,"e":"Concrete cast against earth normally requires 3 in. of cover over reinforcement to protect against corrosion. The engineer's drawings specify the cover and reinforcing. [ACI 318 cover requirements (general)]","r":"ACI 318 cover requirements (general) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Where employee work areas have audible alarm coverage, 2010 Standards 215.3 requires the wiring system to:","o":["include visible alarms in every office at once","allow visible alarm appliances to be integrated when needed","use battery-powered strobes","be limited to Class 2 wiring"],"a":1,"e":"Employee work areas must be wired so that visible appliances can be added later for an employee who needs them, without installing strobes everywhere. [2010 Standards 215.3]","r":"2010 Std 215.3"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How much should the pairs of Cat 5e/6 cable be untwisted at the termination point?","o":["As much as 75 mm (3 in.)","No more than 13 mm (0.5 in.)","About 150 mm (6 in.)","They should be fully untwisted for 25 mm (1 in.) of the jacket"],"a":1,"e":"Excess untwist increases crosstalk. The TIA limit is 0.5 in. for Cat 5e and Cat 6. [ANSI/TIA-568.2]","r":"TIA untwist (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For recordkeeping, the total days away from work and days of restricted work for a single case are capped at:","o":["365 days","180 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":1,"e":"An employer may stop counting days away or restricted days once the total reaches 180 days. [29 CFR 1904.7(b)(3)(vii)]","r":"29 CFR 1904.7(b)(3)(vii)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A general contractor hires an electrical subcontractor that has no workers' compensation coverage. A sub's employee is hurt on the job. Under F.S. 440.10(1)(b), who must secure compensation for that employee?","o":["The general contractor","Only the injured worker","The property owner","The county building department"],"a":0,"e":"When work is sublet, the contractor and sub employees are treated as employed in one business; the contractor is liable for compensation for employees of a sub that has not secured coverage. [F.S. 440.10(1)(b)]","r":"F.S. 440.10(1)(b)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An electrician runs EMT through a 1-hour rated corridor wall, leaving a 1/2 in. gap around the conduit. According to NEC 300.21, the electrician must:","o":["seal the gap with an approved firestop system matching the wall rating","fill it with expanding foam bought at a hardware store","leave the gap alone because the conduit is metal","wrap the gap with electrical tape on both sides"],"a":0,"e":"300.21 requires approved methods that maintain the assembly's fire resistance rating. Metal conduit alone does not restore the rating, and hardware-store foam is generally not listed for it. [NEC 300.21]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) 300.21 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A company pays an employee a separately stated $3,000 bonus. Using the optional flat-rate method for supplemental wages (under $1 million for the year), how much federal income tax is withheld on the bonus?","o":["$720.00","$1,110.00","$229.50","$660.00"],"a":3,"e":"The flat rate for supplemental wages is 22%: $3,000 x 0.22 = $660.00. $720.00 would use 24% (backup withholding rate) and $229.50 would be 7.65% FICA only. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Before a claimant files a construction defect lawsuit covered by Chapter 558, F.S., how many days before filing must the notice of claim ordinarily be served on the contractor, subcontractor, supplier or design professional (for a typical homeowner, not an association of more than 20 parcels)?","o":["At least 10 days","At least 60 days","At least 30 days","At least 120 days"],"a":1,"e":"Section 558.004(1)(a) requires notice at least 60 days before filing an action, or at least 120 days if the claimant is an association representing more than 20 parcels. [F.S. 558.004(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 558.004(1)(a)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"On another job, earned revenue to date is $250,000 but only $220,000 has been billed. The job is","o":["overbilled by $30,000, a liability (billings in excess of costs and earnings)","underbilled by $220,000, recorded as a current liability on the balance sheet","underbilled by $30,000, an asset (costs and earnings in excess of billings)","properly billed, so that no adjustment is needed to the job's financial statements"],"a":2,"e":"Earned 250,000 - billed 220,000 = 30,000 underbilled. Underbilling means the contractor has done work it has not yet invoiced, which strains cash. [IRS Construction ATG]","r":"IRS Construction Industry ATG, Ch. 1-3"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A conference room has two light switches serving the same luminaires, and two redundant HVAC damper controls serving one damper. Under 2010 Standards 205.1, which statement is correct?","o":["Only one light switch must comply, and only one damper control must comply","Both light switches must comply, while one of the two redundant damper controls need not comply","Neither light switch must comply, and neither damper control must comply","Both damper controls must comply, while the light switches are exempt as redundant"],"a":1,"e":"The redundant-control exception excludes light switches, so every light switch must be within reach. For other redundant controls, one per space is not required to comply. [2010 Standards 205.1, Exc. 6]","r":"2010 Std 205.1 Exc. 6"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor expects annual overhead of $186,000 and annual direct job costs of $1,550,000. If overhead is recovered as a percentage of direct cost, how much overhead is charged to a job with $240,000 of direct cost?","o":["$25,714.29","$32,727.27","$36,000","$28,800"],"a":3,"e":"Overhead rate = 186,000/1,550,000 = 12.0%; 240,000 x 0.120 = $28,800. Dividing by total (direct + overhead) uses the wrong base. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 4-wire wye system with linear loads has phase currents of 30 A, 20 A and 10 A. What is the neutral current?","o":["60.0 A","20.0 A","10.0 A","17.3 A"],"a":3,"e":"I neutral = sqrt(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 - ab - ac - bc) = sqrt(300) = 17.3 A. Simply adding the phases (60 A) ignores their 120 degree spacing. [Neutral current]","r":"Neutral current"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 5-ft length of flexible metal conduit connects a recessed luminaire in an accessible ceiling to its junction box. Which statement is correct about securing the FMC?","o":["FMC is prohibited above ceilings","It must be secured every 3 ft","It is permitted without securing, because lengths up to 6 ft from the last point of securement to the luminaire are allowed","It must be secured every 12 in."],"a":2,"e":"FMC lengths not exceeding 6 ft from the last securing point for connections within an accessible ceiling to luminaires or equipment are permitted without further securement. [NEC 348.30]","r":"NEC 348.30"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 120/240-V single-phase 3-wire multiwire branch circuit supplies only line-to-neutral loads, and the neutral carries only the unbalanced current. How many conductors count as current-carrying for the adjustment factors?","o":["3","1","4","2"],"a":3,"e":"A neutral that carries only the unbalanced current from the other conductors of the same circuit is not counted. [NEC 310.15(E)(2)]","r":"NEC 310.15(E)(2)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under the cash method of accounting, a contractor generally reports income in the tax year in which it is","o":["actually or constructively received","earned by completing the work, whether or not paid","invoiced to the customer","entered into the signed contract"],"a":0,"e":"The cash method counts income when cash (or its equivalent) is received or made available without restriction; billing or earning it earlier does not trigger income. [IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods]","r":"IRS Pub 334 (2023), Accounting Methods"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A unit price bid form lists: Light fixture type A, 86 each at $214.50; Duplex receptacle, 240 each at $38.75; 2-inch PVC conduit, 410 LF at $11.20 per LF. What is the total bid for these items?","o":["$27,747","$13,892","$41,639","$32,339"],"a":3,"e":"86 x 214.50 = 18,447.00; 240 x 38.75 = 9,300.00; 410 x 11.20 = 4,592.00; total = $32,339. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.129(1)(r), F.S., a conviction for misapplication of construction funds under s. 713.345 requires the board to suspend all of the licensee's licenses for at least:","o":["30 days","6 months","1 year from the date of conviction","5 years"],"a":2,"e":"Misapplication of construction funds under s. 713.345 is a disciplinary ground, and on conviction the board must suspend all licenses under the chapter for at least one year, while other penalties remain available. [F.S. 489.129(1)(r)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(1)(r)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A wall-mounted visible notification appliance (strobe) in a room should have its lens mounted at what height?","o":["Between 42 in. and 48 in. above the floor","Between 60 in. and 72 in. above the floor","Between 96 in. and 120 in. above the floor","Between 80 in. and 96 in. above the floor, and at least 6 in. below the ceiling"],"a":3,"e":"Wall-mounted strobes are mounted with the lens 80 to 96 in. AFF and below the ceiling per the standard. [NFPA 72 (2019) 18.5.5]","r":"NFPA 72 18.5.5 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The labor unit for a receptacle is 0.35 hour each. At a burdened rate of $52.40 per hour, what is the labor cost for 540 receptacles?","o":["$189","$28,296","$990.36","$9,903.60"],"a":3,"e":"540 x 0.35 = 189 hours; x 52.40 = $9,903.60. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under F.S. 255.05, which type of claimant is excepted from the 45-day written notice to the contractor?","o":["A supplier of materials to a subcontractor","A sub-subcontractor who rents equipment","A material dealer that delivers to the site","A laborer who works on the project"],"a":3,"e":"Laborers are not required to give the notice to contractor; others not in privity must. [F.S. 255.05(2)(a)2.]","r":"F.S. 255.05(2)(a)2."},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Shop drawings are best described as:","o":["Drawings prepared by the contractor or supplier showing fabrication or installation for this project","The original design drawings prepared by the engineer of record for the owner's permit set","Drawings prepared by the inspector for field verification during rough-in and final inspections","The manufacturer's sales brochures for the equipment"],"a":0,"e":"Shop drawings are a kind of submittal that show fabrication, assembly, or installation details, such as switchboard layouts, for review by the design team. [AIA A201 3.12 (submittals)]","r":"AIA A201 3.12 (submittals)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under Pub 15, which of the following employer payments is NOT treated as wages?","o":["Reimbursements under an accountable plan with substantiation","Cash gift cards given to employees at holidays","Nondiscretionary production bonuses paid to staff","Tips of $20 or more reported to the employer"],"a":0,"e":"Business expense reimbursements under an accountable plan are not wages. Cash and cash-equivalent gift cards, bonuses, and reported tips are wages. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A notice to proceed (NTP) is:","o":["A recorded document that gives lien rights to the suppliers","A list of defects found by the owner at the final inspection","A certificate that the permit has been closed out by the building department","Written authorization for the contractor to begin and start the contract time"],"a":3,"e":"The NTP tells the contractor to start work, and the contract time normally runs from its date. A notice of commencement and a punch list are different documents. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Renting similar equipment costs $85/hour. Owning costs $10,820/year in fixed ownership costs plus $6.50/hour to operate. At what annual usage is owning cheaper than renting (round up)?","o":["138 hours","128 hours","1665 hours","119 hours"],"a":0,"e":"Break-even hours = fixed ownership / (rent rate - operating cost) = 10,820/(85 - 6.5) = 137.8, so 138 hours. Above that, owning is cheaper. [Equipment cost recovery]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 725.01, F.S., which of these agreements generally cannot be enforced in court unless it, or a note of it, is in writing and signed by the party to be charged?","o":["A contract to install ten receptacles in a garage this week","An agreement that by its terms cannot be performed within one year","A promise to pay a supplier's invoice for material delivered today","An agreement to replace a panel within 30 days"],"a":1,"e":"Section 725.01 lists agreements not to be performed within one year of being made, among other categories, as requiring a signed writing. The short jobs could be completed within a year. [F.S. 725.01]","r":"F.S. 725.01"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Walkways crossing an excavation need guardrails when they are how far above the lower level?","o":["3 feet or more","4 feet or more","6 feet or more","10 feet or more"],"a":2,"e":"Walkways provided where employees or equipment cross over excavations must have standard guardrails where they are 6 feet or more above lower levels. [29 CFR 1926.651(l)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.651(l)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 10 hp, 460-volt, three-phase squirrel-cage motor has a nameplate current of 13.2 A. Table 430.250 lists 14 A for this motor. What is the minimum ampacity of the branch-circuit conductors that supply only this continuous-duty motor?","o":["16.5 A","14.0 A","17.5 A","15.8 A"],"a":2,"e":"Motor branch-circuit conductors are sized at 125% of the Table full-load current, not the nameplate: 14 A x 1.25 = 17.5 A. Using the 13.2 A nameplate (16.5 A) is the tempting error. [NEC 430.22, 430.6(A)(1), Table 430.250]","r":"NEC 430.22, 430.6(A)(1), Table 430.250"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A GMP contract has a guaranteed maximum price of $1,200,000. There were no owner-directed changes, and the contractor's actual cost of the work reached $1,265,000. How much of the overrun must the contractor absorb?","o":["$0","$65,000","$32,500","$1,265,000"],"a":1,"e":"1,265,000 - 1,200,000 = $65,000. The GMP is a cap, so the contractor absorbs the amount above it. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"At what height above a lower level must construction employees on an unprotected side or edge be protected from falling?","o":["4 feet","10 feet","6 feet","15 feet"],"a":2,"e":"1926.501(b)(1) sets the trigger at an unprotected side or edge 6 feet (1.8 m) or more above a lower level; 10 feet applies to scaffolds.","r":"29 CFR 1926.501(b)(1)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Excavated or other materials and equipment must be kept at what minimum distance from the edge of an excavation?","o":["At least 2 feet","At least 1 foot","At least 5 feet","At least 10 feet"],"a":0,"e":"Materials and equipment must be kept at least 2 feet from the edge of an excavation, or restrained by retaining devices. [29 CFR 1926.651(j)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.651(j)(2)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An electrician needs to step off a ladder onto a roof landing that is 12 feet above the ground. The ladder will be set at a 4:1 angle and must extend 3 feet above the landing. The vertical height needed is 15 feet. Approximately what minimum working length ladder is needed? (Vertical height = working length x 0.968.)","o":["13 feet","14 feet","16 feet","18 feet"],"a":2,"e":"At 4:1, vertical height is about 0.968 x length; 15 / 0.968 = 15.5 feet, so a 16-foot working length is the smallest standard size that works. [29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(1), (b)(5)(i)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(1),(b)(5)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under 29 CFR 1910.269(b), for field work with two or more employees involving exposed lines or equipment of 50 volts or more, how many persons trained in first aid are required?","o":["At least two trained persons","At least one trained person, located off site","None, if a first-aid kit is carried on the truck","One trained person for every ten employees"],"a":0,"e":"Field work involving two or more employees requires at least two trained persons; for fixed locations, a trained person must be available to reach each employee within 4 minutes.","r":"29 CFR 1910.269(b)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 5,000 W heater runs 6 hours per day for 30 days at $0.145 per kWh. What is the monthly cost?","o":["$900.00","$130.50","$195.75","$4.35"],"a":1,"e":"kWh = 5 x 6 x 30 = 900; cost = 900 x 0.145 = $130.50. [Energy cost]","r":"Energy cost"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"The same nonmetallic-conduit bonding rule applies to an electronic power supply operating at 25 kHz. What spacing is required between the bonding conductor and the conduit?","o":["At least 1-1/2 in. (38 mm)","At least 1 in. (25 mm)","At least 2-1/2 in. (65 mm)","At least 1-3/4 in. (45 mm)"],"a":3,"e":"Over 100 Hz requires 45 mm (1-3/4 in.). The 38 mm value applies at 100 Hz or less, which is the case for 60 Hz magnetic transformers. [NEC 600.7(B)]","r":"NEC 600.7(B)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.129(12), F.S., the contractor under investigation may respond in writing to the complaint within how many days after service?","o":["5 days","20 days","10 days","60 days"],"a":1,"e":"The department must promptly provide a copy of the complaint stating the specific facts; the contractor has 20 days after service to respond, and the probable cause panel must consider the response. Emergency orders and criminal matters are exceptions. [F.S. 489.129(12)]","r":"F.S. 489.129(12)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An X-ray unit has a momentary rating requiring 60 A and a long-time rating requiring 40 A. What is the minimum ampacity of the supply conductors?","o":["40 A","60 A","30 A","50 A"],"a":0,"e":"Conductors are sized at 50% of the momentary rating (30 A) or 100% of the long-time rating (40 A), whichever is greater: 40 A. [NEC 660.6(A)]","r":"NEC 660.6(A) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor wants to know if overhead is under control. Revenue is $2,750,000 and general overhead is $330,000. Overhead as a percent of revenue is","o":["13.6%","12.0%","24.0%","88.0%"],"a":1,"e":"$330,000 / $2,750,000 = 12.0%. Dividing by revenue net of overhead gives 13.6%. [Overhead analysis]","r":"Overhead (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A network (finish-to-start, working days): A 4d (start); B 7d after A; C 6d after A; D 5d after B; E 5d after C; F 3d after D+E. What is the total float of activity E?","o":["0 days","1 day","2 days","5 days"],"a":1,"e":"Path A-C-E-F is 18 days against 19 for the critical path, leaving 1 day of float on C and E. [CPM float]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 100-A noncontinuous load is carried by THHN copper conductors in a conduit with 8 current-carrying conductors in a 40°C ambient. Using the 90°C column for derating (0.91 ambient factor, 0.70 adjustment factor) and the 75°C column for terminations, what is the smallest copper size?","o":["1/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","2 AWG","1 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"Required 90°C ampacity = 100 / (0.91 x 0.70) = 157.0 A. 2 AWG (130 A) is too small; 1/0 AWG (170 A) works, and its 75°C value (150 A) also covers the 100 A load. [NEC 310.14, 310.15(B)(1), 310.15(C)(1), 110.14(C)]","r":"NEC 310.14, 310.15(B)(1), 310.15(C)(1), 110.14(C)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A classroom has 1,300 net ft^2. At 20 net ft^2 per person for educational classroom use, the occupant load is:","o":["130","26","65","52"],"a":2,"e":"1,300 / 20 = 65 persons. Using 50 net ft^2 per person gives 26, which is the wrong factor. [NFPA 101, Table 7.3.1.2]","r":"NFPA 101 Table 7.3.1.2 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What current does a 75 kVA balanced load draw from a 208 V three-phase supply?","o":["120.2 A","208.2 A","360.6 A","312.3 A"],"a":1,"e":"I = kVA x 1,000 / (1.732 x E) = 75,000 / 360.2 = 208.2 A. [Three-phase power]","r":"Three-phase power"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"At which location may the grounded conductor (neutral) be connected to the equipment grounding conductor in a typical dwelling installation?","o":["At the service equipment only (not on the load side of the service disconnect)","At every subpanel","At the meter and at each branch-circuit breaker","At every receptacle"],"a":0,"e":"A grounded-to-grounding connection is made at the service (main bonding jumper) and not on the load side of the service disconnecting means, except for separately derived systems and specific exceptions. [NEC 250.24(A)(5)]","r":"NEC 250.24(A)(5)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An electrical contractor that also designs systems for owners wants coverage for errors in its design advice. The appropriate line is","o":["builder's risk insurance on the building","commercial auto insurance on the fleet","workers' compensation for the design staff","professional liability (errors and omissions)"],"a":3,"e":"CGL generally excludes professional services. E&O covers negligent design advice. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Quarterly federal employment tax returns (Form 941) are due by the end of the month following the quarter. Which list gives the 2026 due dates in Pub 15?","o":["March 15, June 15, September 15 and December 15","April 15, June 15, September 15 and January 15","February 28, May 31, August 31 and November 30","April 30, July 31, October 31 and January 31"],"a":3,"e":"Pub 15 lists Form 941 due dates as April 30, July 31, October 31 and January 31. [IRS Pub 15 (2026), Employer responsibilities]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026), Employer Responsibilities"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Materials left on a job site overnight without security are exposed mainly to which purchasing-related risk?","o":["Theft or loss that raises job cost","Lower markup","Reduced vendor terms","Higher property tax"],"a":0,"e":"Job-site loss increases material cost and shrinkage; secure storage, lock boxes and sign-out procedures lower it. [Inventory control]","r":"Job-site storage"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"The Equal Pay Act prohibits paying employees of different sexes unequal wages for:","o":["Work performed for different companies in the same industry","Jobs with different titles that involve substantially different duties","Any job within the same company, regardless of the duties performed","Equal work on jobs requiring equal skill, effort and responsibility under similar working conditions"],"a":3,"e":"The EPA applies to substantially equal jobs in the same establishment, with exceptions for seniority, merit, quantity or quality of production or a factor other than sex. [29 U.S.C. 206(d)(1)]","r":"29 U.S.C. 206(d)(1)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Materials costing $4,500 are marked up 20% on cost. The resale price and the margin on that price are","o":["$5,400 and 20.0%","$5,625 and 20.0%","$5,400 and 16.7%","$3,600 and 16.7%"],"a":2,"e":"Price = 4,500 x 1.20 = 5,400; margin = 900/5,400 = 16.7%. [Markup vs margin]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An air-conditioning unit has a compressor rated-load current of 21.2 A and a condenser fan motor of 1.8 A. What is the minimum circuit ampacity (MCA) the manufacturer would mark?","o":["23.0 A","26.5 A","28.3 A","30.1 A"],"a":2,"e":"MCA = 125% of the largest motor plus the other loads: (21.2 x 1.25) + 1.8 = 26.5 + 1.8 = 28.3 A. The 26.5 A choice leaves out the fan. [NEC 440.33, 440.35]","r":"NEC 440.33, 440.35"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which Florida statute is titled the Construction Contract Prompt Payment Law and governs written contracts to improve real property on which a lien may be claimed under chapter 713, part I?","o":["F.S. 218.735","F.S. 489.129","F.S. 715.12","F.S. 440.10"],"a":2,"e":"715.12 applies to written contracts to improve real property where a construction lien is authorized; 218.735 covers local government purchases of construction services. [F.S. 715.12]","r":"F.S. 715.12"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employee is paid semimonthly (24 pay periods) on an annual salary of $62,400. What is the gross pay per pay period?","o":["$2,400.00","$2,600.00","$5,200.00","$1,200.00"],"a":1,"e":"$62,400 / 24 = $2,600.00. $2,400.00 would divide by 26 and $5,200.00 would divide by 12. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A worker's weekly gross pay is $1,176.00. Federal income tax withheld is $98.00 and there are no other deductions. Using 6.2% Social Security and 1.45% Medicare (each rounded to the cent), what is the net pay?","o":["$1,078.00","$976.14","$988.04","$1,086.04"],"a":2,"e":"Social Security $72.91 + Medicare $17.05 = $89.96. Net = $1,176.00 - $89.96 - $98.00 = $988.04. $1,086.04 forgets the income tax withholding. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"When an employer who failed to secure coverage cannot produce payroll records, the department imputes weekly payroll for each employee equal to","o":["the minimum wage times 40","the statewide average weekly wage divided by 2","1.5 times the statewide average weekly wage","twice the statewide average weekly wage"],"a":2,"e":"Imputed weekly payroll under s. 440.107 is 1.5 times the statewide average weekly wage when records are missing. [F.S. 440.107(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.107(7)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is an INDIRECT cost (overhead) for an electrical contractor?","o":["Wire pulled on a specific job","Direct labor of the journeyman on the job","A subcontract for trenching on one project","Office staff salaries and shop rent"],"a":3,"e":"Costs that benefit several jobs or the whole business, such as office salaries and rent, are overhead and must be allocated. [Direct vs indirect costs]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"What is the effect if a lienor not in privity with the owner fails to serve a timely notice to owner under s. 713.06(2), F.S.?","o":["The lien is reduced by 10 percent of the amount claimed","Only the interest and finance charges are lost","It is a complete defense to enforcement of the lien","Nothing, if the owner actually knew about the work"],"a":2,"e":"Failure to serve, or to serve timely, is a complete defense to the lien, and the notice itself is not a lien or constructive notice. [F.S. 713.06(2)(a)]","r":"F.S. 713.06(2)(a)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For failing to secure workers' compensation, the department assesses a penalty of two times the premium that would have been paid, or","o":["$100, whichever is less","$5,000, whichever is less","$1,000, whichever is greater","$25,000, whichever is greater"],"a":2,"e":"The penalty is twice the premium at approved manual rates (usually over the preceding 12 months) or $1,000, whichever is greater. [F.S. 440.107(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.107(7)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"If a contractor treats a genuine (3)(d) job as a lump sum job and collects no tax from the customer, what exposure exists?","o":["No exposure, since materials were taxed at purchase","Assessed sales tax plus penalty and interest","Use tax assessed on the installation labor only","Loss of the owner's lien rights on the property"],"a":1,"e":"Misclassification can create assessments in either direction; a genuine (3)(d) job without tax collected can lead to assessed sales tax with penalty and interest. [Rule 12A-1.051, F.A.C.]","r":"Rule 12A-1.051"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which action is a ground for the State Fire Marshal to discipline a firesafety inspector under F.S. 633.216(5)?","o":["Attending required continuing education courses","Receiving a regular salary from the county employer","Accepting free labor or materials from a person whose work the inspector enforces","Issuing a written correction notice after an inspection"],"a":2,"e":"Accepting free or below-market labor, services, or materials from someone whose work the inspector enforces is a ground for discipline, unless that person is an immediate family member or lives with the inspector. [F.S. 633.216(5)(g)]","r":"F.S. 633.216(5)(g)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"An exit stair serves a calculated occupant load of 600 persons. If ADAAG 4.3.11.3 requires one 30 in. by 48 in. rescue space per 200 persons served, how many spaces are needed?","o":["4","6","3","2"],"a":2,"e":"600 persons / 200 persons per space = 3 spaces. Two spaces would serve only 400 persons. [ADAAG 4.3.11.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.3.11.3"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A hand conduit bender shows a cracked handle. What should the electrician do?","o":["Use it carefully, with tape wrapped around the crack in the handle","Use it only for small conduit sizes until the handle can be replaced","Use it for one last job, and then discard it at the end of that day","Tag it out of service, and repair or replace it before use"],"a":3,"e":"Tools in unsafe condition must be removed from service. [29 CFR 1926.300(a); 1926.20(b)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.300(a); 1926.20(b)(3)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"To adopt the LIFO inventory method for federal income tax, a business must:","o":["File Form 4562 with the State of Florida along with the sales tax return","Obtain prior approval of the Florida DBPR before changing the method","Limit the method to inventories that are valued under $10,000 in total","File Form 970 with its return, and generally use LIFO for financial reports too"],"a":3,"e":"Under IRC 472 the election is made on Form 970, and LIFO is subject to a conformity rule requiring its use in financial reports to owners and creditors. [IRS Pub 538; IRC 472(c)]","r":"IRS Pub 538; IRC 472"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"As AWG numbers get larger (for example from 10 to 12), the conductor diameter","o":["gets larger and resistance per foot decreases","gets smaller and resistance per foot increases","stays the same and the ampacity falls only","gets larger and resistance per foot increases"],"a":1,"e":"AWG sizes are inverse: larger numbers are thinner wire. [AWG sizes]","r":"AWG sizes"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which of these architectural scales is the LARGEST scale (shows the most detail for a given sheet size)?","o":["3/8 in. = 1 ft-0 in.","1/8 in. = 1 ft-0 in.","3/32 in. = 1 ft-0 in.","1/16 in. = 1 ft-0 in."],"a":0,"e":"A larger fraction of an inch per foot means a larger scale: 3/8 in. per foot is larger than 1/8, 3/32, or 1/16. Larger-scale drawings take precedence in many order-of-precedence clauses. [Scale conventions]","r":"Scale conventions"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which of these does Rule 61G6-5.005, F.A.C. specifically identify as a factor in the board's financial responsibility review?","o":["The applicant's gross annual contract revenue","The number of employees the applicant plans to hire","Ownership of real estate by the applicant's officers","An IRS or Florida Department of Revenue lien of record"],"a":3,"e":"Tax liens of record from the IRS or the Florida Department of Revenue against the applicant, an officer, or a 10% shareholder are a listed factor. [Rule 61G6-5.005]","r":"Rule 61G6-5.005, F.A.C."},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"In a period of rising prices, which inventory method generally results in the lowest ending inventory value and the lowest reported profit?","o":["FIFO","Average cost always","Specific identification only","LIFO"],"a":3,"e":"LIFO charges the most recent (highest) costs to cost of goods sold, leaving older, lower costs in ending inventory. FIFO does the opposite. [IRS Pub 538]","r":"IRS Pub 538"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"As the temperature of a copper conductor increases, its resistance","o":["drops to zero","increases","decreases","stays the same"],"a":1,"e":"Metals have a positive temperature coefficient of resistance. [Conductor properties]","r":"Conductor properties"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"How many full business days must a Florida excavator notify for work beneath state waters?","o":["2 full business days","5 full business days","30 full business days","10 full business days"],"a":3,"e":"Excavation beneath state waters requires at least 10 full business days' notice, and operators have 10 days to mark underwater facilities. [F.S. 556.105]","r":"F.S. 556.105"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A plan note calls for a 'NEMA 5-20R' receptacle. How can this receptacle be recognized?","o":["A T-shaped neutral slot, so it accepts both 15 A and 20 A 125-volt plugs","Two straight slots and a ground, accepting 15 A plugs only","A 250-volt, 20-ampere receptacle with a horizontal and a vertical slot","A locking (twist-lock) receptacle for generator and welder connections"],"a":0,"e":"A 5-20R has a T-shaped slot for the neutral, allowing a 20 A plug (5-20P) to fit. A 5-15R is the 15 A configuration. 250-volt receptacles are NEMA 6- series. [NEMA WD 6 configurations]","r":"NEMA WD 6 configurations (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A workers' compensation policy was issued on an estimated payroll of $500,000 at 6.4 per $100 with a mod of 0.95. The audit finds actual payroll of $562,000. What is the additional premium due?","o":["$3,769.60","$3,968","$34,169.60","$376.96"],"a":0,"e":"Additional payroll = 62,000; /100 x 6.4 = 3,968.00; x 0.95 = $3,769.60. Ignoring the mod overcharges. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"When no order-of-precedence clause exists and two contract documents conflict, which interpretation rule is most often applied?","o":["Specific provisions control over general provisions","General provisions control over specific ones","Printed text controls over handwritten changes","The lower price controls"],"a":0,"e":"Courts commonly let specific terms prevail over general ones, and handwritten or typed changes over printed boilerplate, because they better show the parties' intent. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a standard ampere rating for fuses and inverse-time circuit breakers in 240.6(A)?","o":["70 A","90 A","110 A","75 A"],"a":3,"e":"The list includes 70, 80, 90, 100, 110 and 125 A. 75 A does not appear. [NEC 240.6(A)]","r":"NEC 240.6(A)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A qualified person without insulation protection must keep how close, at minimum, to an exposed energized part of 4,160 volts (over 2 kV to 15 kV) with a conductive object under Table S-5?","o":["2 feet 0 inches","1 foot 0 inches","1 foot 6 inches","3 feet 0 inches"],"a":0,"e":"Table S-5 lists 1 ft 0 in for over 300 V to 750 V, 1 ft 6 in for over 750 V to 2 kV, and 2 ft 0 in for over 2 kV to 15 kV. [29 CFR 1910.333(c)(3)]","r":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(3)(iii)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A non-self-supporting extension ladder with a working length of 28 feet is set up at the 4:1 ratio recommended by OSHA. How far from the wall should the base be placed?","o":["4 feet","5 feet","7 feet","8 feet"],"a":2,"e":"The horizontal distance should be about one-quarter of the working length: 28 / 4 = 7 feet. [29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(5)(i)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(5)(i)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An injured employee must give the employer notice of the injury within what period to preserve the claim, subject to limited exceptions?","o":["7 days","30 days","24 hours","90 days"],"a":1,"e":"Under s. 440.185(1), the employee must advise the employer of the accident within 30 days after it occurs. [F.S. 440.185(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 440.185(1)(a)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under generally accepted accounting principles, a contractor estimates a $300,000 contract will cost $340,000 in total. How much of the expected loss should be recognized when the loss becomes apparent, even if the job is only 10% complete?","o":["The full $40,000 expected loss","$300,000 of revenue and no loss","$4,000, 10% of the loss","None until the job is complete"],"a":0,"e":"Expected total loss = $340,000 - $300,000 = $40,000. GAAP requires the entire anticipated loss on a contract to be accrued as soon as it is probable; waiting understates the loss. [GAAP loss provision, ASC 605-35]","r":"GAAP loss contracts (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"After a claim of lien is recorded, a lien action must be started within what period or the lien is generally void?","o":["3 years","60 days","90 days","1 year after recording the claim"],"a":3,"e":"Under s. 713.22(1), a lien expires 1 year after the claim of lien is recorded unless an action to enforce it is started. [F.S. 713.22(1)]","r":"F.S. 713.22(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which IRS program can give a business partial relief from federal employment taxes if it voluntarily reclassifies workers as employees?","o":["The Offer in Compromise program","The Backup Withholding Waiver","The Voluntary Classification Settlement Program (VCSP)","The Employee Retention Program"],"a":2,"e":"IRS Topic 762 points to VCSP as a program offering partial relief from federal employment taxes when a business voluntarily reclassifies workers as employees. [IRS Topic 762]","r":"IRS Topic 762"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.117(3), F.S., the board may grant an out-of-state contractor a limited registration for:","o":["One project, and it is nonrenewable","Two years, renewable","Any project in Florida for one year","All projects under $75,000"],"a":0,"e":"The limited, nonrenewable registration covers one project, and applying appoints the Department of State as agent for service of process. [F.S. 489.117(3)]","r":"F.S. 489.117(3)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A Class I, Zone 0 location is one where ignitible concentrations of flammable gases or vapors are:","o":["Likely under normal operation, but intermittent","Unlikely, and only for a short time","Present continuously or for long periods","Present only during a spill"],"a":2,"e":"Zone 0 is continuous or long-period presence. Zone 1 is likely to exist under normal operation, and Zone 2 is not likely and exists only for a short time if it does. [NEC 505.5(B)(1)]","r":"NEC 505.5(B)(1)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor has a 60-month equipment note. On the balance sheet, the principal due within the next 12 months should be shown as","o":["owner's equity","a reduction of accumulated depreciation","a current liability (current portion of long-term debt)","a noncurrent liability with the rest of the note"],"a":2,"e":"The next twelve months' principal is a current liability because it will require current assets to pay it; the remainder stays long-term. Ignoring the split overstates working capital. [Balance sheet classification]","r":"Balance sheet classification"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A surety's rate schedule is $25 per $1,000 on the first $100,000 of the contract price, $15 per $1,000 on the next $400,000, and $10 per $1,000 on the next $500,000. What is the premium for a $750,000 contract?","o":["$18,750","$7,500","$11,250","$11,000"],"a":3,"e":"100,000/1,000 x 25 = 2,500; 400,000/1,000 x 15 = 6,000; 250,000/1,000 x 10 = 2,500; total $11,000. Applying one flat rate to the whole price ignores the tiers. [Bonding practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (premium and bond arithmetic)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under ADAAG 4.28.3, what is the minimum intensity of an accessible visual alarm appliance?","o":["15 candela","110 candela","75 candela","30 candela"],"a":2,"e":"ADAAG 4.28.3 requires a minimum intensity of 75 candela. Fifteen candela is a smaller NFPA 72 value for certain room sizes, not the ADAAG figure. [ADAAG 4.28.3]","r":"ADAAG 4.28.3"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Ballasts used in signs must be:","o":["Unlisted but fused","Of the open type","Listed and thermally protected","Protected by a ground-fault circuit interrupter"],"a":2,"e":"600.22 requires ballasts to be identified for the use, listed, and thermally protected. [NEC 600.22]","r":"NEC 600.22"},{"c":"Maintaining Cash Flow","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A good use of a short-term revolving line of credit for a contractor is","o":["purchasing a commercial building that the company expects to hold for 20 years","funding the owner's long-term personal investment accounts and retirement plan","bridging the gap between payroll and material payments and customer receipts","paying off a 30-year mortgage on the contractor's shop and office building"],"a":2,"e":"Short-term working capital should finance short-term needs; matching long-term assets with long-term financing avoids repayment squeezes. [SBA working capital options]","r":"Working capital and financing"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the recommended minimum bend radius for installed four-pair UTP horizontal cable?","o":["Four times the cable outside diameter","One times the cable outside diameter","Eight times the cable outside diameter","Ten times the cable outside diameter"],"a":0,"e":"TIA recommends a 4x OD minimum for installed four-pair UTP; 8x applies to four-pair cable under pulling tension. [ANSI/TIA-568.2]","r":"TIA bend radius (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Employees are using jackhammers where the exact location of underground power lines is unknown. What protection must they be given?","o":["Rubber boots with a steel toe","Insulated protective gloves","A dielectric face shield only","Hearing protection rated for 85 dB"],"a":1,"e":"When underground power line locations are unknown and tools may contact them, employees must be provided with insulated protective gloves. [29 CFR 1926.416(a)(2)]","r":"29 CFR 1926.416(a)(2)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor has a $25,000 credit limit with a supplier, an $18,400 open balance, and wants to place a $7,200 order. What happens if the supplier enforces the limit?","o":["The order exceeds the limit by $6,600 because only the order counts","The order is within the limit, leaving $6,600 of unused credit","The order exceeds the limit by $600 and may be held until payment or limit increase","The order is within the limit, leaving $600 of unused credit"],"a":2,"e":"$18,400 + $7,200 = $25,600, which exceeds $25,000 by $600. [Supplier credit]","r":"Supplier credit"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which term describes a drawing marked up by the contractor to show the actual installed locations of conduits and equipment, delivered at project closeout?","o":["A shop drawing prepared by the supplier","A permit drawing filed with the building department","A bid drawing issued to the bidders","An as-built (record) drawing"],"a":3,"e":"As-builts or record drawings document the work as actually installed, including deviations from the design. [Project closeout practice (MasterFormat 01 78 39)]","r":"Project closeout practice (MasterFormat 01 78 39)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Type MC cable must be supported at intervals not exceeding:","o":["3 ft","6 ft","4-1/2 ft","10 ft"],"a":1,"e":"MC cable must be secured and supported at intervals not exceeding 6 ft; cables with four or fewer conductors no larger than 10 AWG must be secured within 12 in. of every box. [NEC 330.30]","r":"NEC 330.30"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"To support a delay claim, the contractor should most importantly","o":["show with updates and logs that the event delayed critical work","blame the owner for every delay, including its own","wait for final completion before documenting anything","rely on verbal notice given to the job superintendent"],"a":0,"e":"A claimant bears the burden to prove the event caused delay to project completion, and timely written notice and contemporaneous records are essential. [Delay claims; FAR 52.249-10]","r":"Delay claims"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the essential elements needed to form a valid contract?","o":["Mutual assent through offer and acceptance","Consideration exchanged by both parties","A lawful purpose and competent parties","A notarized signature from each party"],"a":3,"e":"A valid contract requires mutual assent (offer and acceptance), consideration, competent parties and a legal purpose; notarization is not an element. Notarization may matter for recording or proof, not for formation. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A guaranteed maximum price (GMP) contract is best described as:","o":["A fixed unit price quoted for each material item installed","A cost-plus contract with a ceiling on the owner's total cost for the scope","A contract that cannot be changed in any way once signed","A time-and-material contract with no limit on total cost"],"a":1,"e":"A GMP contract reimburses cost plus a fee but caps the owner's total for the defined scope; scope changes by change order can move the cap. Savings or overruns are shared as the contract states. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A sign foundation is placed in hot, dry Florida conditions. What curing practice is recommended to protect strength?","o":["Let it dry as quickly as possible in the hot Florida sun","Cure it for 1 hour and then remove the forms and load it","Keep it moist or sealed for several days, typically 7 or more","Add water to the surface only after the 28-day strength test"],"a":2,"e":"Moisture and temperature control after placing allow cement hydration to continue; rapid drying leads to cracking and low surface strength. Common practice is at least 7 days of moist curing. [PCA, curing concrete]","r":"PCA, curing concrete (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under 'FOB shipping point' terms, who generally bears freight cost and risk of loss while goods are in transit?","o":["The buyer","The carrier's insurer only","The seller","The property owner"],"a":0,"e":"FOB shipping point means risk of loss passes to the buyer when the seller delivers the goods to the carrier; FOB destination keeps the risk with the seller until delivery. [F.S. 672.319, 672.509]","r":"F.S. 672.319"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A quantity takeoff is","o":["the contractor's final invoice sent to the owner at closeout","the weekly list of workers shown on the payroll","the process of measuring and counting the materials and items shown on plans and specifications","a side-by-side comparison of two subcontractor bids"],"a":2,"e":"The takeoff produces the counts and lengths that are then priced to build the estimate. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A Florida local government construction project is under $10 million. Under s. 218.735(7), F.S., within how many calendar days after substantial completion must the final list of items be developed?","o":["10 business days","30 calendar days","60 calendar days","90 calendar days"],"a":1,"e":"For projects under $10 million the final list is developed within 30 calendar days after substantial completion. For $10 million or more the period can be extended by contract up to 60 days. [F.S. 218.735(7)]","r":"F.S. 218.735(7)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Which small metal part of a neon sign does not require bonding?","o":["Any metal part that is under 6 in. in its longest dimension or length","Metal parts of any size if they have been painted with a nonconductive coating","Parts of 2 in. or less, not likely energized, and 3/4 in. or more from the tubing","Any metal part that is within 1 in. of the transformer or power supply enclosure"],"a":2,"e":"600.7(B) exempts small metal parts that do not exceed 50 mm (2 in.) in any dimension, are not likely to be energized, and are spaced at least 19 mm (3/4 in.) from the tubing. [NEC 600.7(B)]","r":"NEC 600.7(B)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What is the minimum cover for direct-buried cables or conductors in the general location (Table 300.5 'all locations not specified')?","o":["36 in.","18 in.","24 in.","12 in."],"a":2,"e":"Column 1 of Table 300.5 requires 24 in. of cover for direct burial cables in all locations not otherwise specified. [NEC Table 300.5]","r":"NEC Table 300.5"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In a hospital, the patient care vicinity extends how far beyond the normal location of the bed and how high above the floor?","o":["6 ft beyond the bed and 7.5 ft above the floor","3 ft beyond the bed and 6 ft above the floor","10 ft beyond the bed and 9 ft above the floor","6 ft beyond the bed and 8 ft above the floor"],"a":0,"e":"The patient care vicinity is 1.8 m (6 ft) beyond the perimeter of the bed and 2.3 m (7 ft 6 in.) above the floor. [NEC 517.2]","r":"NEC 517.2"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An office building has 15,000 sq ft of floor area. Using the Table 220.12 unit load of 3-1/2 VA per sq ft for office buildings, what is the general lighting load before demand or continuous-load factors?","o":["30,000 VA","45,000 VA","52,500 VA","60,000 VA"],"a":2,"e":"15,000 sq ft x 3.5 VA = 52,500 VA. Using the 3 VA dwelling figure would give 45,000 VA. [NEC 220.12, Table 220.12]","r":"NEC 220.12, Table 220.12"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which burglar alarm sensor detects the sound frequency of breaking glass?","o":["Passive infrared (PIR) motion detector","Acoustic glass-break detector","Magnetic reed switch","Photoelectric beam"],"a":1,"e":"Acoustic glass-break detectors listen for the sound pattern of breaking glass; PIR detects heat motion. [Burglar alarm practice]","r":"Glass break detectors (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"The required sound level of audible notification appliances in sleeping areas is a minimum of:","o":["60 dBA at the pillow","75 dBA at the pillow","90 dBA at the pillow","110 dBA at the pillow"],"a":1,"e":"Sleeping area signals must be at least 75 dBA at the pillow (higher where the ambient requires). 110 dBA is the maximum permitted. [NFPA 72 (2019) 18.4.5]","r":"NFPA 72 18.4 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A three-week look-ahead schedule is mainly used to","o":["coordinate near-term work among trades, materials, and inspections","evaluate the contractor's bonding capacity this year","replace the contract schedule of values for billing","calculate the liquidated damages that may be owed"],"a":0,"e":"Short-interval look-ahead schedules turn master-schedule activities into specific weekly tasks and surface conflicts early. [Schedule coordination]","r":"Coordination with trades (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A wiring gutter's terminals each receive two 4/0 AWG copper conductors (conductors do not leave through the wall opposite the terminals). What minimum wire-bending space does Table 312.6(A) require?","o":["4 in.","8 in.","6 in.","3-1/2 in."],"a":2,"e":"For 3/0–4/0 AWG, Table 312.6(A) requires 4 in. for one wire per terminal and 6 in. for two wires per terminal. [NEC Table 312.6(A)]","r":"NEC Table 312.6(A)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Two parallel conductors carrying current in the same direction will","o":["repel each other","have no force","spin","attract each other"],"a":3,"e":"Parallel currents in the same direction attract; opposite directions repel. [Magnetism]","r":"Magnetism"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For purposes of s. 489.126, F.S., the term 'contractor' includes:","o":["Anyone who performs, contracts for, or promises the work, licensed or not","Only contractors holding a state certificate of competency","Only contractors registered with the department locally","Only business entities qualified by a qualifying agent"],"a":0,"e":"Subsection (1) extends the term beyond the licensed class to anyone who performs, contracts for or promises the work, so unlicensed persons are covered as well. [F.S. 489.126(1)]","r":"F.S. 489.126(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.127(2)(a), F.S., a first violation of the prohibited acts in subsection (1) by an unlicensed person is a:","o":["Second-degree misdemeanor","First-degree misdemeanor","Third-degree felony","Noncriminal infraction"],"a":1,"e":"The first offense is a first-degree misdemeanor. A later violation after a prior guilty finding, or any violation during a declared state of emergency, is a third-degree felony. [F.S. 489.127(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.127(2)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"On a lighting plan, the subscript 'S3' next to switch symbols in a stairwell identifies which type of switch?","o":["Single-pole switches serving a three-phase lighting circuit","Dimmer switches that have three preset light levels","Three-pole switches that open three ungrounded conductors","Three-way switches that control one load from two locations"],"a":3,"e":"S3 is the standard plan abbreviation for a three-way switch, used in pairs (with S4 four-way switches in between when needed) to control a load from multiple points. It does not mean three-phase or three-pole. [Drawing practice: switch symbols]","r":"Drawing practice: switch symbols (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A recessed luminaire that is not marked IC (insulated ceiling) is installed in an attic. How must thermal insulation be placed near it?","o":["At least 3 in. from the enclosure, none above the luminaire","Packed tightly around the can for efficiency","About 1/2 in. from the sides, none above","Laid directly on top of the luminaire housing"],"a":0,"e":"Insulation may not be installed above a recessed non-IC luminaire or within 3 in. of its enclosure, wiring compartment or ballast, so heat can escape. [NEC 410.116(B)]","r":"NEC 410.116(B) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Which is NOT one of the statutory ways an employer may secure payment of workers' compensation under F.S. 440.38?","o":["Buying insurance from an authorized carrier","Qualifying as a self-insurer","Posting a personal note payable to each employee","Joining an approved local government pool"],"a":2,"e":"The listed methods are insurance, self-insurance, an approved public utility program, a local government pool, and an individual self-insurer program. [F.S. 440.38(1)]","r":"F.S. 440.38(1)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A waterflow switch on a sprinkler system annunciates at the fire alarm control panel as which type of signal?","o":["Supervisory signal","Alarm signal","Trouble signal","Maintenance signal"],"a":1,"e":"Waterflow indicates fire conditions and is processed as an alarm; tamper is supervisory. [NFPA 72 (2019) Ch. 17 and 21]","r":"NFPA 72 17.12 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under F.S. 713.23, the surety's liability on a payment bond","o":["cannot exceed the penal sum of the bond","is unlimited","is limited to 10 percent of the penal sum","is limited to interest on unpaid claims"],"a":0,"e":"The statute provides that the surety's liability is capped at the bond's penal sum. [F.S. 713.23(1)(f)]","r":"F.S. 713.23(1)(f)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"For an electric range or clothes dryer in a new installation, the branch circuit must:","o":["Include an equipment grounding conductor (a 4-wire circuit)","Be 2-wire only","Use the neutral as the only ground","Use a grounding conductor smaller than 14 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"New range and dryer circuits must include an EGC; using the grounded conductor to ground the frame is permitted only for certain existing installations. [NEC 250.140]","r":"NEC 250.140"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the minimum on-site fuel supply for an engine-driven generator serving an emergency system?","o":["About 30 minutes of operation","At least 8 hours of operation","At least 24 hours of operation","At least 2 hours of full-demand operation"],"a":3,"e":"Emergency system fuel supply must be sufficient for at least 2 hours of full-demand operation. [NEC 700.12(B)(2)]","r":"NEC 700.12(B)(2)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Using the same bucket truck (cost $64,000, salvage $4,000, 5-year straight-line life), what is its book value at the end of year 2?","o":["$24,000","$38,400","$40,000","$52,000"],"a":2,"e":"Annual depreciation is $12,000. After 2 years accumulated depreciation is $24,000, so book value = $64,000 - $24,000 = $40,000. Using cost/5 without salvage gives $38,400. [Straight-line depreciation]","r":"Depreciation"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A subcontract requires an electrical sub to indemnify the general contractor for damage caused by another contractor on the site who has no connection to the sub. Under s. 725.06(1), F.S., this requirement is:","o":["Permitted, because an indemnity clause can cover damage by anyone on the site","Permitted if the sub carries liability insurance of at least $1 million","Not permitted, because the sub cannot be made to cover damage caused by an unrelated party","Permitted only if the subcontract is signed within 30 days of the NTP"],"a":2,"e":"Section 725.06(1) limits the damage an indemnitor may be required to cover to damage caused by the indemnitor, its contractors, subs, materialmen or agents of any tier, or the indemnitee and its people. An unrelated contractor is outside those groups. [F.S. 725.06(1)]","r":"F.S. 725.06(1)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A Notice of Commencement is recorded, but no work begins within 90 days after recording. The notice is:","o":["Still effective for lienors","Void","Converted to a claim of lien","Valid for 1 year anyway"],"a":1,"e":"Section 713.13 provides that if work does not actually commence within 90 days after recording, the notice is void. It also expires 1 year after recording unless it states otherwise. [F.S. 713.13]","r":"F.S. 713.13"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Fire alarm shop drawings are submitted to the authority having jurisdiction for plan review. Which information is customarily shown on the riser portion?","o":["The wallpaper, paint colors and furniture layout in each room of the building","Panel locations, devices per floor, circuit types and routing, and power supplies","The plumbing vents, drains and stack locations shown on the plumbing riser","The mortgage and financing arrangements that the owner has with the lender"],"a":1,"e":"Riser diagrams communicate system architecture. Plan review typically requires device layouts, riser, battery calculations, and voltage-drop calculations. [Fire alarm plan review practice]","r":"Fire alarm plan review practice (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A tone-only panel draws 0.2 A standby and 3.0 A alarm. Applying a 20 percent safety (derate) factor so that usable capacity is 80 percent of nameplate, which is the smallest of these standard batteries that is adequate: 4, 7, 12, or 18 Ah?","o":["7 Ah","4 Ah","12 Ah","18 Ah"],"a":0,"e":"Load = 0.2 x 24 + 3.0 x (5/60) = 4.8 + 0.25 = 5.05 Ah. Divide by 0.8 = 6.31 Ah, so the smallest adequate battery is 7 Ah. 4 Ah is too small; larger sizes are oversized. [NFPA 72 (2019) 10.6.7; manufacturer derating practice]","r":"NFPA 72 10.6.7 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the extent of the Class I, Division 1 area at the open end of an outdoor underground fuel tank vent pipe at a gasoline station?","o":["Within 18 in. of the vent end","Within 25 ft vertically above the vent","Within 3 ft in all directions of the open vent end","Only inside the tank itself"],"a":2,"e":"Table 514.3(B)(1): Division 1 within 3 ft (900 mm) of the open vent end; Division 2 extends from 3 ft to 5 ft. [NEC Table 514.3(B)(1)]","r":"NEC Table 514.3(B)(1) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 10 ohm resistor is in series with a 12 ohm equivalent parallel section on 120 V. What voltage appears across the parallel section?","o":["120.0 V","60.0 V","65.5 V","54.5 V"],"a":2,"e":"I = 120 / 22 = 5.455 A; E = I x 12 = 65.5 V. The remaining 54.5 V drops across the series resistor. [Combination circuits]","r":"Combination circuits"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A schedule shows an activity with negative total float. This means","o":["the crew size can be reduced on that activity now","the activity has extra time available beyond the plan","the path is behind what is needed to meet the required finish date","the activity can safely be dropped from the schedule"],"a":2,"e":"Negative float occurs when the calculated finish is later than the required completion date, signaling that recovery is needed. [CPM float]","r":"Critical path method (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A device box (3 x 2 x 3-1/2 in., 18.0 cu in.) holds only 14 AWG conductors, no devices or clamps. How many conductors can it hold?","o":["8","9","12","10"],"a":1,"e":"14 AWG requires 2.0 cu in. each. 18.0 / 2.0 = 9 conductors. [NEC 314.16(A), 314.16(B)]","r":"NEC 314.16(A), 314.16(B)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 713.015, F.S., the owner's lien-law notice must be printed in what type and signed by whom?","o":["At least 12-point, capitalized, boldface, signed and dated by the owner","At least 8-point type in any style, signed by the contractor only","Any readable type size, signed by the owner's lender or mortgage broker","At least 14-point type, notarized by a notary but not signed by the owner"],"a":0,"e":"The notice must appear in no less than 12-point capitalized boldface type on the front page of the contract or a separate page, signed and dated by the owner. The 14-point requirement is on the permit card under s. 713.135. [F.S. 713.015(1)]","r":"F.S. 713.015(1)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For how long is a Florida construction-industry certificate of election to be exempt valid after its effective date?","o":["3 years","1 year","Until the officer revokes it","2 years"],"a":3,"e":"Certificates issued on or after January 1, 2013 expire 2 years from the effective date, and the Division sends an expiration notice at least 60 days beforehand. [F.S. 440.05(6)]","r":"F.S. 440.05(6)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"For a labor-intensive electrical contractor, which allocation base is generally the most reasonable for spreading overhead to jobs?","o":["The number of permits pulled by the company during the year","The square footage of the contractor's main office","The number of customers listed in the contractor's database","Direct labor hours or direct labor dollars"],"a":3,"e":"Overhead should be allocated on a base that reflects how jobs consume resources; for labor-heavy work that is labor hours or dollars. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An injured employee had an average weekly wage of $930.00. Assuming the amount is below the maximum, what is the weekly temporary total disability benefit at 66 2/3 percent?","o":["$465.00","$930.00","$697.50","$620.00"],"a":3,"e":"$930.00 x 2/3 = $620.00 per week, subject to the statutory maximum of 100 percent of the statewide average weekly wage. [F.S. 440.15(2)]","r":"F.S. 440.15(2)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An activity has an early start of day 12, a duration of 5 days, and a late finish of day 21. What is its total float?","o":["9 days","4 days","0 days","5 days"],"a":1,"e":"Early finish = 12 + 5 = 17. Total float = late finish - early finish = 21 - 17 = 4 days (equivalently late start 16 - early start 12). [CPM float]","r":"Critical path method"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Under the 2010 ADA Standards (308.2.1 and 308.3.1), what is the range of heights for an unobstructed forward or side reach to an operable part?","o":["15 in. minimum to 48 in. maximum","15 in. minimum to 54 in. maximum","18 in. minimum to 44 in. maximum","9 in. minimum to 54 in. maximum"],"a":0,"e":"The 2010 Standards use one 15 in. to 48 in. range for both forward and unobstructed side reach. The 9 in. to 54 in. side range was in the 1994 ADAAG. [2010 Standards 308]","r":"2010 Std 308.2.1, 308.3.1"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Which of these signs is exempt from the disconnect requirement of 600.6?","o":["A roof-mounted channel-letter sign on a retail building","A pole sign with a flashing controller in a parking lot","An exit directional sign located within a building","An illuminated sign mounted on a fence along a roadway"],"a":2,"e":"Exceptions to the disconnect rule are exit directional signs inside a building and cord-connected signs with an attachment plug. [NEC 600.6 Exceptions]","r":"NEC 600.6 Exceptions"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 400-A service uses 600 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors. The grounding electrode conductor connects only to two driven ground rods. What is the minimum size copper GEC?","o":["2 AWG","4 AWG","1/0 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.66 would require 1/0 AWG, but when the GEC goes only to rod, pipe or plate electrodes it need not be larger than 6 AWG copper. [NEC 250.66(A)]","r":"NEC 250.66(A)"},{"c":"Electrical Signs, Outline Lighting, and Structural Considerations","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Splices in high-voltage secondary-circuit conductors must be made:","o":["In listed enclosures rated over 1000 V, accessible after installation","With twist-on connectors and electrical tape inside the sign body","In any junction box that is rated for 600 V and has a cover","Inside a wall cavity or sealed raceway with no means of access"],"a":0,"e":"600.32: splices in high-voltage secondary conductors are made in listed enclosures rated over 1000 V, accessible after installation and listed for the location. [NEC 600.32]","r":"NEC 600.32 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Two trades both plan to work in the same ceiling space during the same week. A coordination meeting is needed to","o":["lower the retainage being held on both of the contracts","raise the bid prices that both trades have submitted","add liquidated damages to the contract of both trades","sequence the work to avoid conflicts and delays to successors"],"a":3,"e":"Trade stacking and conflicts cause lost productivity and may extend durations; resolving the sequence preserves float. [Coordination with trades]","r":"Coordination with trades (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Using the panel schedule in which Phase A totals 5,400 VA at 120 V line-to-neutral, what is the current on phase A, assuming all loads are 120 V and the schedule values are the demand to be used?","o":["26.0 A","45.0 A","15.0 A","22.5 A"],"a":1,"e":"I = VA / V = 5,400 / 120 = 45.0 A. Dividing by 208 would apply only to line-to-line loads, and 1.732 does not belong in a single-phase line-to-neutral calculation (that gives only about 15 A). [Basic circuit calculation]","r":"Basic circuit calculation"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"After the power factor of a 100 kW, 480 V three-phase load is corrected from 0.80 to 0.95, the line current is approximately","o":["139 A","150 A","75 A","127 A"],"a":3,"e":"I = 100,000 / (1.732 x 480 x 0.95) = 127 A, down from 150 A at 0.80. [Power factor correction]","r":"Power factor correction"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Must the areas designated as hazardous (classified) locations be documented?","o":["No, only the AHJ keeps classification records","Yes, available to those who design, install, inspect or maintain","Only for Class II locations","Only when the area exceeds 1,000 sq ft"],"a":1,"e":"All areas designated as hazardous (classified) must be properly documented, with the documentation available to those authorized. [NEC 500.4(A)]","r":"NEC 500.4(A)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under Pub 15, tips received by an employee are subject to employer and employee Social Security and Medicare taxes when the tips total at least how much in a month?","o":["$100 or more in a month","$600 or more in a year","$2,000 or more in a year","$20 or more in a month"],"a":3,"e":"Tips of $20 or more in a calendar month while working for one employer are wages for FICA purposes and must be reported to the employer. [IRS Pub 15 (2026)]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.131(7)(f)1, F.S., the department generally may not pursue complaints against registered contractors when a local body has jurisdiction, unless the local body fails to act within:","o":["10 days","60 days","6 months","3 years"],"a":2,"e":"Exceptions include summary procedures and cases where the local body fails to act within 6 months. [F.S. 489.131(7)(f)1]","r":"F.S. 489.131(7)(f)1"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"What percent of the total area of a 1 in. EMT (0.864 sq in.) is filled by three 6 AWG THHN copper conductors (0.0507 sq in. each)?","o":["17.6%","25.5%","14.1%","30.0%"],"a":0,"e":"3 x 0.0507 = 0.1521 sq in.; 0.1521 / 0.864 = 17.6%, which is under the 40% limit. [NEC Chapter 9, Tables 1, 4, 5]","r":"NEC Chapter 9, Tables 1, 4, 5"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A signed contract includes a clause stating, 'This document is the entire agreement of the parties.' The owner later claims the contractor verbally promised free upgrades before signing. How does the clause most likely affect that claim?","o":["It makes any later oral promise more binding than the written terms","It voids the contract because no consideration was exchanged","It gives the owner an automatic right to cancel within three days","It generally bars proof of prior oral promises that add to the written terms"],"a":3,"e":"An integration (entire agreement) clause with the parol evidence rule generally keeps out prior or contemporaneous oral statements that vary the writing. Promises that matter should be added to the written contract. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Doubling the one-way length of a circuit while keeping the same wire size and current will make the voltage drop","o":["be cut in half","quadruple","double","stay the same"],"a":2,"e":"VD is directly proportional to length. [Voltage drop]","r":"Voltage drop"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"The abbreviation \"RFP\" in project procurement stands for","o":["required field permit","rate for payroll","request for proposal","retainage for project"],"a":2,"e":"An RFP solicits proposals that may weigh qualifications and approach as well as price. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Under NEC Article 700, wiring of emergency circuits must be installed:","o":["only in nonmetallic conduit in all occupancies","using only 12 AWG or larger conductors with a red jacket","in the same raceway as normal circuits to save material","entirely independent of all other wiring and equipment, with limited exceptions"],"a":3,"e":"Emergency circuits must be kept separate from normal wiring so a fault in the normal system cannot affect them. The exceptions cover fire-resistant enclosures, transfer equipment, and luminaires fed from both sources. [NEC Art. 700, Wiring]","r":"NFPA 70 (2023) Art. 700 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"An employee earns $30.00/hour for 2,080 paid hours ($62,400). Employer costs are: Social Security/Medicare 7.65%, FUTA $42, Florida reemployment tax $189, workers' comp $4,056, and health insurance $450/month. What is the total annual labor burden (excluding wages)?","o":["$14,460.60","$9,060.60","$76,860.60","$8,829.60"],"a":0,"e":"FICA 4,773.60 + FUTA 42 + RT 189 + WC 4,056 + health 5,400 = 14,460.60. [Labor burden; Pub 15, Topic 759, FL DOR]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A conductor is spliced in a small box whose opening is 4 in. in its largest dimension. How far must the conductor extend outside the opening of the box?","o":["At least 8 in.","At least 6 in.","At least 3 in.","At least 1 in."],"a":2,"e":"If the opening is less than 8 in. in any dimension, each conductor must have 6 in. of free length and extend at least 3 in. outside the opening. [NEC 300.14]","r":"NEC 300.14"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"Direct-buried service conductors buried 18 in. or more below grade (not concrete-encased) must have what identification?","o":["A buried copper tracer at 6 in. depth","A red paint stripe on the conduit","A warning ribbon placed in the trench at least 12 in. above the underground installation","No identification is required"],"a":2,"e":"Direct-buried service conductors and raceways not encased in concrete and buried 18 in. or more must be identified by a warning ribbon at least 12 in. above the installation. [NEC 300.5(D)(3)]","r":"NEC 300.5(D)(3)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A cost-plus-fixed-fee contract has a fee of $38,000. The job's reimbursable costs finish at $296,500. What is the total payment to the contractor?","o":["$296,500","$334,500","$364,150","$38,000"],"a":1,"e":"296,500 + 38,000 = $334,500. A fixed fee does not change with cost. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (quantity, labor, markup arithmetic)"},{"c":"Management Accounting","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Why should the overhead rate be reviewed at least annually?","o":["The IRS requires the rate to change on January 1 of each year","The rate is set by the state licensing board and published annually","Overhead costs and work volume change, so an old rate may under- or over-recover costs","The rate must always equal the state sales tax rate for the year"],"a":2,"e":"Rates are based on forecast costs and volume; if either changes the rate must be updated or bids will be mispriced. [Overhead allocation]","r":"Management accounting (figures invented; formulas standard)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A 15 kW resistance heater produces approximately how many BTU per hour (1 kW = 3,412 BTU/h)?","o":["51,180 BTU/h","180,000 BTU/h","54,000 BTU/h","11,190 BTU/h"],"a":0,"e":"15 x 3,412 = 51,180 BTU/h. [Conversions]","r":"Conversions"},{"c":"Contract Scheduling","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under FAR 52.249-10, a contractor must notify the Contracting Officer in writing of a cause of delay within","o":["48 hours of the end of the project","30 days after final payment","1 year of substantial completion","10 days from the beginning of the delay"],"a":3,"e":"Paragraph (b) requires written notice within 10 days from the beginning of any delay unless the Contracting Officer extends that period. [FAR 52.249-10(b)]","r":"FAR 52.249-10"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"At each outlet, junction and switch point, at least how much free conductor must be left, measured from where it emerges from its cable sheath or raceway?","o":["8 in.","12 in.","3 in.","6 in."],"a":3,"e":"At least 6 in. of free conductor must be available for splices or device connections, and where the box opening is less than 8 in., each conductor must extend at least 3 in. outside the opening. [NEC 300.14]","r":"NEC 300.14"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"A contractor buys a bucket truck for $64,000 with an estimated salvage value of $4,000 and a 5-year life. Using straight-line depreciation, what is annual depreciation expense?","o":["$800","$24,000","$12,800","$12,000"],"a":3,"e":"($64,000 - $4,000) / 5 years = $12,000 per year. Forgetting to subtract salvage gives $12,800. [Straight-line depreciation]","r":"Depreciation"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the synchronous speed of an 8-pole motor on 60 Hz?","o":["1,200 rpm","900 rpm","1,800 rpm","600 rpm"],"a":1,"e":"Ns = 120 x 60 / 8 = 900 rpm. [Synchronous speed]","r":"Synchronous speed"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under an occurrence-form liability policy, coverage applies when","o":["the claim is first made during the policy period, no matter when the injury occurred","the contractor is first sued, no matter when the injury occurred","the policy is renewed after the injury","the bodily injury or property damage happens during the policy period"],"a":3,"e":"An occurrence policy is triggered by when the injury or damage occurs, even if the claim is filed years later. A claims-made policy is triggered by when the claim is first made. [Insurance practice]","r":"Insurance and bonding practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"Under NEC 110.16, which equipment requires an arc-flash hazard warning label?","o":["Only equipment rated above 600 volts, in industrial plants","All lighting fixtures in commercial buildings and in stores","Only panelboards that are installed in dwelling units","Equipment in other than dwelling units, likely to need examination while energized"],"a":3,"e":"Equipment in other than dwelling units that is likely to require examination, adjustment, servicing or maintenance while energized must be field or factory marked to warn qualified persons of the potential arc-flash hazard.","r":"NEC 110.16(A) (2023) (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Purchasing Control","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which practice best controls loss of portable power tools?","o":["Buy replacement tools whenever one is reported missing from the truck","Leave tools unlocked at the job site so that crews can reach them easily","A sign-out log or check-in/check-out system with tool numbers assigned to individuals","Let crews borrow tools from one another without keeping any records"],"a":2,"e":"Marking tools and tracking who has each one creates accountability and reduces shrink. [Tool control practice]","r":"Tool control"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Surety premiums commonly run 1.5% to 3.0% of the contract price. A contractor is bonded on a $400,000 job at a 2.0% rate. Under the SBA program the surety fee is 20% of the premium charged. What is the surety fee to SBA?","o":["$8,000","$6,400","$1,600","$2,400"],"a":2,"e":"Premium = $400,000 x 2.0% = $8,000. Surety fee = 20% x $8,000 = $1,600. The contractor fee (0.6% = $2,400) is a separate charge. [CRS R42037]","r":"SBA Surety Bond Guarantee"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 0.5 m conductor moves at 10 m/s perpendicular to a 0.8 tesla field. What EMF is induced (E = B x L x v)?","o":["4.0 V","16.0 V","8.0 V","0.04 V"],"a":0,"e":"E = 0.8 x 0.5 x 10 = 4.0 V. [Electromagnetic induction]","r":"Electromagnetic induction"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.132, F.S., an uncertified or unregistered person associated with a contracting firm may be fined up to what amount, plus costs, and within how many days is the fine due after the final order is filed?","o":["$500; 10 days","$10,000; 90 days","$25,000; 60 days","$5,000; 30 days"],"a":3,"e":"The department may impose a fine of up to $5,000 plus investigative and legal costs, due within 30 days after the final order is filed (paused during an appeal). [F.S. 489.132(3)]","r":"F.S. 489.132(3)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"An employing unit is an employer subject to Florida's reemployment tax if it paid at least how much in wages in a calendar quarter of the current or preceding year?","o":["$500","$7,000","$1,500","$10,000"],"a":2,"e":"Under s. 443.1215(1)(a), an employing unit qualifies if it paid wages of at least $1,500 in a calendar quarter, or employed at least one individual in each of 20 calendar weeks. [F.S. 443.1215(1)(a)]","r":"F.S. 443.1215(1)(a)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.533(2), F.S., the board may impose an administrative fine of not more than what amount per count or separate offense for Part II violations?","o":["$500","$1,000","$10,000","$25,000"],"a":2,"e":"Section 489.533(2) lists denial, revocation or suspension, reprimand, probation, restriction of scope and restitution, plus an administrative fine not to exceed $10,000 per count or offense. [F.S. 489.533(2)]","r":"F.S. 489.533(2)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A scaffold is erected near an insulated line carrying 240 volts. What is the minimum clearance under the scaffold rule for insulated lines of less than 300 volts?","o":["10 feet","6 feet","3 feet","1 foot"],"a":2,"e":"The table in 1926.451(f)(6) allows 3 feet for insulated lines under 300 volts and 10 feet for insulated lines from 300 volts to 50 kV.","r":"29 CFR 1926.451(f)(6)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"PVC conduit used in exposed locations where it is subject to physical damage must be:","o":["Identified for the use, such as Schedule 80 PVC","Schedule 40 PVC","Thin-wall PVC tubing","Any PVC with a 1/4 in. wall"],"a":0,"e":"PVC exposed to physical damage must be identified for the use; Schedule 80 PVC is the standard answer. Schedule 40 is acceptable only where not subject to physical damage. [NEC 352.10(F)]","r":"NEC 352.10(F)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"NM cable is run to a single-gang nonmetallic box that has no internal cable clamps. The cable must be secured:","o":["Within 18 in. of the box","Within 4-1/2 ft of the box","Within 8 in. of the box, with the sheath extending at least 1/4 in. into the box","At the box only, using the cover plate"],"a":2,"e":"For single-gang nonmetallic boxes without cable clamps, the cable must be secured within 8 in. of the box and the sheath must extend at least 1/4 in. inside the box. [NEC 314.17, 334.30]","r":"NEC 314.17, 334.30"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A contractor is already bound to finish a project for $90,000. Halfway through, the contractor refuses to continue unless the owner signs a promise to pay an extra $6,000 for the same scope. The owner signs. A court would most likely find the added promise:","o":["Unenforceable, because the contractor gave no new consideration for it","Enforceable, because the owner signed the promise in writing","Enforceable, because the contractor is a licensed professional","Enforceable, because $6,000 is less than a 10 percent increase"],"a":0,"e":"Under the pre-existing duty rule, a promise to do what one is already obligated to do is not new consideration. A true change in scope with a written change order would be different. [General contract law]","r":"General contract law (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Wiring and Protection","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"An electrician installs a 20-A, 125-V receptacle serving a washing machine in a dwelling laundry area. Which protection applies?","o":["Neither, because it is a dedicated appliance circuit","GFCI protection for personnel, and AFCI protection because laundry areas are listed in 210.12(A)","GFCI only","AFCI only"],"a":1,"e":"Laundry areas of dwelling units require GFCI protection for 125-V receptacles and are among the areas listed for AFCI protection. [NEC 210.8(A), 210.12(A)]","r":"NEC 210.8(A), 210.12(A)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 443.1217(1), which of the following counts as wages subject to Florida reemployment tax?","o":["Only hourly wages paid in cash by payroll check","Only amounts paid above the $7,000 per-person base","Only wages paid to full-time permanent employees","Commissions, bonuses, and tips reported to the employer"],"a":3,"e":"Wages include all remuneration for employment, such as commissions, bonuses, back pay awards, and reported tips; the $7,000 cap limits how much is taxed. [F.S. 443.1217(1)]","r":"F.S. 443.1217(1)"},{"c":"Interpretation of Financial Statements and Reports","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Net income after taxes is $63,000. Total assets were $780,000 at the beginning of the year and $920,000 at the end. What is return on assets using average total assets?","o":["7.4%","3.7%","6.8%","8.1%"],"a":0,"e":"Average assets = ($780,000 + $920,000) / 2 = $850,000. ROA = $63,000 / $850,000 = 7.4%. [UNL Extension G2009]","r":"Return on assets"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A 3 AWG THW copper conductor (75°C column, 100 A) runs in a 38°C ambient with no more than three current-carrying conductors. What is the corrected ampacity?","o":["88 A","82 A","100 A","91 A"],"a":0,"e":"For a 75°C rating, the correction factor for 36–40°C is 0.88: 100 x 0.88 = 88 A. (0.91 is the 90°C factor.) [NEC 310.15(B)(1), Table 310.15(B)(1)(1)]","r":"NEC 310.15(B)(1), Table 310.15(B)(1)(1)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under s. 489.117(4)(a)1, F.S., local governments generally may NOT require a contractor license for which scope?","o":["Electrical service changes for one- and two-family homes","Replacement of plumbing supply lines in older buildings","Low-risk scopes such as painting, flooring, and handyman services","Reroofing of buildings taller than three stories"],"a":2,"e":"Persons whose job scope does not substantially match a registered or certified category need not register, and local governments cannot require licenses for listed low-risk scopes such as painting, flooring and handyman services. [F.S. 489.117(4)(a)1]","r":"F.S. 489.117(4)(a)1"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A plan note reads 'EC to provide all conduit and wire, GC to provide 3/4-in. plywood backboard.' What does 'EC' stand for and what is the practical effect?","o":["Engineer of record; the engineer supplies the conduit and wire","Electrical contractor; the EC's price includes conduit and wire but not the plywood","Existing conductors; the old conductors in the building are to be reused as shown","Emergency circuit; the conductors are to be connected to the generator"],"a":1,"e":"EC is the electrical contractor and GC the general contractor. Responsibility notes like this define scope so the bid includes the right items. [Drawing practice: scope notes]","r":"Drawing practice: scope notes"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What is the time constant (RC) of a 10,000 ohm resistor and a 100 microfarad capacitor in series?","o":["1.0 s","10 s","100 s","0.1 s"],"a":0,"e":"tau = R x C = 10,000 x 0.0001 = 1.0 s. The capacitor reaches about 63 percent of the supply in one time constant. [Time constants]","r":"Time constants"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Which contract type reimburses the contractor for the actual cost of the work and then adds a fee?","o":["Lump sum","Cost-plus","Unit price","Competitive sealed bid"],"a":1,"e":"In a cost-plus arrangement the owner pays documented costs plus an agreed fee, so the owner carries most of the cost risk. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"Personnel Management","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"A 15-year-old helper works 3 hours on each of four school days (Monday through Thursday) in a school week. Under federal child labor rules, how many more hours may she work that week, on Saturday, a non-school day?","o":["3 hours","8 hours","6 hours","18 hours"],"a":2,"e":"The weekly school-week cap is 18 hours; she already worked 4 x 3 = 12, leaving 18 - 12 = 6, which is less than the 8-hour non-school-day limit. [29 CFR 570.35]","r":"29 CFR 570.35"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"Under current s. 95.11(3)(b), F.S., the seven-year repose period runs from the earliest of several events. A temporary certificate of occupancy was issued on March 2, the certificate of occupancy on June 15, and the owner took possession on April 20. Which event starts the repose clock?","o":["The certificate of occupancy issued on June 15","The owner's date of possession, April 20","The date the final payment was made to the contractor","The temporary certificate of occupancy on March 2"],"a":3,"e":"The clock runs from the earliest of the temporary certificate of occupancy, certificate of occupancy, certificate of completion, or abandonment. Owner possession is not one of the listed events under the current text. [F.S. 95.11(3)(b)]","r":"F.S. 95.11(3)(b)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"What does a phase rotation meter indicate?","o":["The sequence in which the phases reach their peaks, ABC or CBA","The voltage drop between the phases under the full rated load","The insulation resistance of each phase conductor to ground","The total harmonic distortion of the three-phase supply system"],"a":0,"e":"Phase rotation is critical before connecting three-phase motors; wrong rotation reverses the direction of the motor.","r":"Phase rotation meter operation (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Plan and Specification Reading and Interpretation","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"While doing a takeoff for a conduit run, the estimator measures 90 ft horizontal on the plan, then must also add the vertical conduit from the ceiling to a switch box 48 in. above the floor in a room with a 10-ft ceiling. How much vertical conduit does that drop add?","o":["4 ft, the mounting height of the switch box","10 ft, the full floor-to-ceiling height of the room, as shown on the section","6 ft, from the 10-ft ceiling to a box mounted 4 ft above the floor","14 ft, the ceiling height plus the mounting height of the box"],"a":2,"e":"Ceiling height minus mounting height is 10 ft - 4 ft = 6 ft. The plan view does not show vertical runs, so they must be added from the elevation or notes. [Quantity takeoff; plan vs elevation]","r":"Quantity takeoff; plan vs elevation"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Unless the notice of commencement states a later expiration date, when does it expire as to conveyances, mortgages and creditors under s. 713.13, F.S.?","o":["1 year after recording","90 days after recording","6 months after the certificate of occupancy","30 days after final payment"],"a":0,"e":"The default term is one year after recording. If the contract runs longer, the notice should state the longer period or payments after expiration can be improper. [F.S. 713.13]","r":"F.S. 713.13"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"Under s. 489.521(7)(c), F.S., the board must assess a fine of at least what amount, or issue a citation, to a contractor who omits the number from an advertisement?","o":["$25","$100","$500","$1,000"],"a":1,"e":"The board must fine at least $100 or issue a citation for an omitted number. Rule 61G6-10.002 sets first-offense fines at $100 to $250. [F.S. 489.521(7)(c)]","r":"F.S. 489.521(7)(c)"},{"c":"Complying with Payroll and Sales Tax Laws","s":"biz","d":3,"q":"For one quarter, a contractor's covered wages are $90,000 (all below the wage bases), federal income tax withheld is $9,000, and no tips or credits apply. What is the total tax liability reported on Form 941 for the quarter?","o":["$15,885.00","$20,160.00","$22,770.00","$13,770.00"],"a":2,"e":"Social Security 12.4% x $90,000 = $11,160; Medicare 2.9% x $90,000 = $2,610; plus $9,000 income tax withheld = $22,770. $15,885 counts only the employee share of FICA ($6,885) plus withholding, and $20,160 leaves out Medicare. [IRS Pub 15 (2026); Form 941]","r":"IRS Pub 15 (2026)"},{"c":"Special Occupancies and Situations","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"What bonding conductor is used for equipotential bonding of pool structural elements?","o":["8 AWG or larger solid copper","10 AWG stranded copper","12 AWG insulated","6 AWG aluminum"],"a":0,"e":"Equipotential bonding uses solid copper, insulated, covered or bare, not smaller than 8 AWG. [NEC 680.26(B)]","r":"NEC 680.26(B)"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"When the recovery fund pays a claim that settles a judgment, award or order against a licensee, what happens to the licensee under s. 489.143(9), F.S.?","o":["The license is suspended until the licensee repays the fund plus interest","The license is revoked for life with no chance of reinstatement","The licensee must complete 14 hours of continuing education only","Nothing happens, because the payment comes from state funds"],"a":0,"e":"Subsection (9) provides automatic suspension on the payment date and reinstatement only upon full repayment plus interest, and bankruptcy does not relieve these penalties. [F.S. 489.143(9)]","r":"F.S. 489.143(9)"},{"c":"Wiring Methods and Materials","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"A conduit that terminates underground where direct-buried conductors emerge from it must have:","o":["A galvanized elbow only","A bare opening","A coupling cap","A bushing or terminal fitting with an integral bushed opening (or equivalent seal)"],"a":3,"e":"A bushing, or terminal fitting with an integral bushed opening, must be used at the end of a conduit that terminates underground where conductors emerge as a direct burial wiring method. [NEC 300.5(H)]","r":"NEC 300.5(H)"},{"c":"Life Safety and Americans with Disabilities Act","s":"tech","d":2,"q":"In NFPA 101, emergency lighting must provide an initial illumination level, averaged along the path of egress at floor level, of not less than:","o":["5 foot-candles (54 lux), with a minimum of 1 foot-candle","1 foot-candle (10 lux), with a minimum of 0.1 foot-candle at any point","0.1 foot-candle (1 lux), with a minimum of 0.01 foot-candle","10 foot-candles (108 lux) at the doors only"],"a":1,"e":"Initial emergency illumination averages at least 1 fc (10 lux), with no point below 0.1 fc (1 lux). The maximum-to-minimum ratio may not exceed 40 to 1. [NFPA 101, 7.9.2.1]","r":"NFPA 101 7.9.2.1 (concept check: written from widely documented provisions, not checked line by line against the code book)"},{"c":"Estimating and Bidding a Job","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Which condition would most likely require the estimator to increase the standard labor unit for installing conduit?","o":["Receiving materials on pallets at the job site","Using a crew that has installed the same system before","Working in a new, unoccupied warehouse at floor level","Working at ceiling heights above 15 feet in a congested area"],"a":3,"e":"Height, congestion and difficult access reduce productivity, so a difficulty factor is applied to the base labor units. The other choices help or are neutral. [Estimating practice]","r":"Estimating practice (Florida Contractors Manual, Business content area)"},{"c":"General Theory and Electrical Principles","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"Reversing the rotation of a three-phase induction motor is done by","o":["interchanging all three of the line leads at once","reversing the neutral conductor connection to the motor","interchanging any two of the three line leads","changing the frequency of the supply to the motor"],"a":2,"e":"Swapping two leads reverses the phase sequence and the rotating field. [Phase rotation]","r":"Phase rotation"},{"c":"Interpretation of Contracts and Agreements","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"A hold-harmless (indemnity) clause generally means that one party agrees to:","o":["Finish the work in half of the time stated in the schedule","Lend money to the other party to carry the project costs","Protect the other party from specified third-party claims","Provide a performance bond for the full contract amount"],"a":2,"e":"Indemnity shifts responsibility for covered claims, and often defense costs, from one party to another. In Florida construction contracts, s. 725.06 limits how far it may go. [F.S. 725.06]","r":"F.S. 725.06"},{"c":"Complying with Contracting Laws and Rules","s":"biz","d":2,"q":"Under s. 489.503, F.S., which of these may be done without an electrical license?","o":["Installing a 200-ampere residential service for a fee","Wiring a fire alarm system in an office building","Installing or repairing lightning rods or related systems","Installing a commercial lighting retrofit for a customer"],"a":2,"e":"Subsection (22) exempts installing or repairing lightning rods and related systems. Services, fire alarms and commercial lighting for customers require a license. [F.S. 489.503(22)]","r":"F.S. 489.503(22)"},{"c":"OSHA, Safety, Procedures for Testing and Use of Tools and Equipment","s":"tech","d":1,"q":"A top rail of a guardrail system must withstand what minimum force applied in any downward or outward direction within 2 inches of its top edge?","o":["50 pounds","100 pounds","200 pounds","300 pounds"],"a":2,"e":"1926.502(b)(3) requires the top rail to withstand at least 200 pounds; midrails and similar members must withstand at least 150 pounds.","r":"29 CFR 1926.502(b)(3)"},{"c":"Obtaining Insurance and Bonding","s":"biz","d":1,"q":"An employer that continues to operate under a stop-work order is subject to a penalty of","o":["$100 per day","$1,000 per day","$500 per week","$10,000 flat"],"a":1,"e":"Section 440.107 provides a $1,000 per day penalty for operating in violation of a stop-work order. [F.S. 440.107(7)]","r":"F.S. 440.107(7)"},{"c":"Alarms/Limited Energy","s":"tech","d":3,"q":"A Class 2 ac power source that is NOT inherently limited has an output voltage of 20 volts or less. What is the maximum overcurrent protection the Table 11(A) lists for it?","o":["8.0 amperes","1.0 ampere","5.0 amperes","100 divided by Vmax"],"a":2,"e":"For a not-inherently-limited Class 2 source in the 0-20 V range, Table 11(A) lists 5.0 A maximum overcurrent protection. 8.0 A is the Imax of an inherently limited source, so it is the tempting wrong choice. [NEC Ch. 9 Table 11(A)]","r":"NEC Ch. 9 Table 11(A)"}];